// Rep Max Learn lessons: one per topic. Fields: s, t, kind (method, idea or writing), why, hook (idea topics only), chunks, example (task, steps, answer), selfx, faded, trap, recap. Written by Sonnet, checked by Opus.
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.1.3.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Osmosis questions on Paper 1 often use potato cylinders from required practical 3, so you need to explain which way water moves and why mass changes.", "hook": {"q": "A shrivelled raisin is left in a glass of pure water for several hours. What happens to it?", "o": ["It shrinks, because water moves out of the raisin into the pure water", "It stays the same size, because water moves in and out equally", "It swells, because water moves into the raisin from the pure water", "It swells, because the sugar inside moves out into the water"], "a": 2, "f": ["Not quite. The raisin contains dissolved sugar, so it is more concentrated than pure water. Water moves towards the more concentrated side, which is into the raisin.", "Not quite. Water would only move in and out equally if the two sides had the same concentration. Pure water is far more dilute than the inside of a raisin.", "Right. The raisin's contents are more concentrated than pure water, so water moves into the raisin and it swells. This is osmosis.", "The outcome is right but the reason is not. It is water that moves, not sugar, and the water moves into the raisin."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What osmosis is", "b": "**Osmosis** is the movement of water from a **dilute** solution to a more concentrated solution, through a **partially permeable membrane**. The membrane lets small water molecules through but blocks larger solute molecules. Water passes both ways, but more goes from the dilute side. So the net movement is towards the concentrated side."}, {"h": "Effect on plant tissue", "b": "Potato cells have a partially permeable membrane. Compare the outside solution with the cell contents. If the outside is **more dilute**, water enters and the mass increases. If it is **more concentrated**, water leaves and the mass decreases. If both are equal, there is **no net movement** and no change in mass."}, {"h": "Required practical 3", "b": "Cut potato cylinders of equal size. Put each in a different sucrose concentration, including pure water. Blot dry and weigh each cylinder before, and again after the same time. Calculate **percentage change in mass** = change ÷ starting mass × 100. Keep **control variables** the same: volume, temperature, time and cylinder size."}, {"h": "Finding the zero point", "b": "Plot percentage change (y) against concentration (x). Where the line **crosses 0%**, there is no net water movement. The solution then has the **same concentration** as the cells. Read the crossing from the graph, not the midpoint. Example: +3% at 0.2 and −2% at 0.4 mol/dm³ crosses at 0.32 mol/dm³. Press Next step.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "steps", "x": [0.16, 0.44], "y": [-3, 4], "xstep": 0.04, "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "Sucrose concentration in mol/dm³", "ylabel": "Percentage change in mass", "lines": [{"c": 0, "step": 3, "pts": [[0.2, 3], [0.4, -2]]}, {"dash": true, "c": 3, "label": "No net change", "step": 4, "pts": [[0.16, 0], [0.44, 0]]}, {"dash": true, "c": 1, "step": 5, "pts": [[0.32, 0], [0.32, -3]]}], "points": [{"at": [0.2, 3], "label": "+3%", "step": 1}, {"at": [0.4, -2], "label": "−2%", "step": 2}, {"at": [0.32, 0], "label": "0.32", "step": 5}], "steps": ["At 0.2 mol/dm³ the potato gains 3% in mass. Water has moved in by osmosis.", "At 0.4 mol/dm³ the potato loses 2% in mass. Water has moved out.", "Join the points. The line falls from gain to loss, so it must cross 0% somewhere between.", "The 0% line is where there is no change in mass, so no net water movement.", "The line crosses 0% at 0.32 mol/dm³. This is the same concentration as the cells. Read it from the graph."]}}], "example": {"task": "A potato cylinder is left in pure water for 30 minutes. Its mass increases. Explain why.", "steps": [{"do": "Pure water is more dilute than the liquid inside the potato cells.", "why": "Direction depends on comparing concentrations, so you state this first."}, {"do": "The cell membrane is partially permeable, so water can pass through but dissolved solutes cannot.", "why": "Osmosis only happens across a partially permeable membrane."}, {"do": "Water moves into the cells by osmosis, from the dilute solution to the more concentrated one.", "why": "This is the direction rule for osmosis."}, {"do": "More water enters than leaves, so the cells gain water and the cylinder's mass increases.", "why": "It links the water movement to the mass change the question asks about."}], "answer": "Pure water is more dilute than the potato cell contents. The cell membrane is partially permeable, so water can pass but solutes cannot. Water moves into the cells by osmosis, from dilute to concentrated. There is a net gain of water, so the mass increases."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer begin by comparing the water with the cell contents?", "o": ["Because osmosis needs energy, which depends on how concentrated the cell contents are", "Because water moves towards the more concentrated solution, so this sets the direction", "Because sugar moves out of the cell until both concentrations are the same", "Because the cell contents are always more dilute than any solution outside"], "a": 1, "f": ["Osmosis is passive and needs no energy. Mixing it up with active transport is the slip here.", "Right. Water moves from the more dilute solution to the more concentrated one. You must compare the two before you can say which way it goes.", "Solutes cannot cross a partially permeable membrane. In osmosis only water moves, so this describes the wrong process.", "Cell contents can be more dilute or more concentrated than the outside. Only pure water is certain to be more dilute than them."]}, "faded": {"task": "A potato cylinder is left in a concentrated sucrose solution. Its mass decreases. Explain why.", "given": ["The sucrose solution is more concentrated than the liquid inside the potato cells.", "The cell membrane is partially permeable, so water can pass through but sucrose cannot."], "q": "What is the next point in the answer?", "o": ["Sucrose moves out of the cells to balance the concentrations, so the mass falls.", "Water moves from the concentrated solution into the cells, so the mass falls.", "Water is pulled out of the cells by active transport, so the mass falls.", "Water moves out of the cells into the solution by osmosis, so the mass falls."], "a": 3, "f": ["Solutes do not cross a partially permeable membrane, as the last step said. It is water that moves in osmosis.", "The direction is reversed. Water moves towards the more concentrated solution, which is outside the cells. Water entering the cells would also raise the mass.", "Osmosis is passive, so it does not use active transport. Name osmosis and give the direction of water movement.", "Right. The cells are more dilute than the solution, so water moves out by osmosis. There is a net loss of water, so the mass falls."]}, "trap": "Writing that sugar or other solutes move, or that water moves from concentrated to dilute. Only water crosses the membrane, and it moves from the dilute solution to the concentrated one. Check which side is more dilute first.", "recap": "Water moves by osmosis from the more dilute solution to the more concentrated one, through a partially permeable membrane."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.2.2.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Heart and blood vessel questions appear in Paper 1 and often ask you to name vessels, trace blood flow or link structure to function.", "hook": {"q": "Blood goes from the heart to the lungs, then back to the heart before going round the body. What is the most likely reason?", "o": ["The heart filters the blood after the lungs have added oxygen to it", "Blood can only reach one organ at a time, so it has to take turns", "Blood loses pressure in the narrow lung vessels, so the heart pumps it again", "The lungs make new blood cells that the heart must then collect"], "a": 2, "f": ["Filtering is not the heart's job. The heart is a pump, and the lungs add oxygen by gas exchange.", "Blood flows to many organs at once. The real reason for the second pump is pressure, not turn-taking.", "Right. Lung capillaries are very narrow, so pressure drops. The heart pumps the blood again to push it round the body.", "New blood cells are made in bone marrow, not the lungs. The lungs exchange gases with the blood."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Double circulation", "b": "Blood passes through the heart twice on each full trip. This is a **double circulatory system**. One loop goes from the heart to the **lungs** and back, so blood picks up oxygen. The other loop goes from the heart to the **body** and back."}, {"h": "Chambers and valves", "b": "The heart has four chambers. The two **atria** (upper) receive blood. The two **ventricles** (lower) pump blood out. **Valves** stop blood flowing backwards. The left ventricle has a thicker wall than the right, because it pumps blood at higher pressure all round the body."}, {"h": "Route through the heart", "b": "Deoxygenated blood: vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle → **pulmonary artery** → lungs. Oxygenated blood: **pulmonary vein** → left atrium → left ventricle → **aorta** → body. The right side serves the lungs. The left side serves the body."}, {"h": "Three types of vessel", "b": "**Arteries** carry blood away from the heart, at high pressure. Their walls are thick, with muscle and elastic fibres, and the lumen is narrow. **Veins** carry blood towards the heart, at low pressure. They have thinner walls, a wider lumen and valves. **Capillaries** have walls one cell thick, so substances diffuse quickly."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how oxygenated blood travels from the lungs to the rest of the body. Name the vessels and the heart chambers it passes through. (5 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Blood leaves the lungs in the pulmonary vein.", "why": "This vessel carries oxygenated blood towards the heart, so it is a vein."}, {"do": "The blood enters the left atrium.", "why": "The pulmonary vein joins the left side of the heart, and atria receive blood."}, {"do": "It passes through a valve into the left ventricle.", "why": "The valve keeps blood moving one way. Ventricles are the chambers that pump."}, {"do": "The left ventricle contracts and pumps the blood at high pressure into the aorta.", "why": "The thick wall gives high pressure. The aorta is the artery that leaves the left side."}, {"do": "The aorta and its branches carry the blood to the organs, where capillaries let oxygen diffuse out.", "why": "Arteries take blood away from the heart. Capillary walls are one cell thick."}], "answer": "Oxygenated blood flows from the lungs along the pulmonary vein to the left atrium. It passes through a valve into the left ventricle. The ventricle contracts and pumps it at high pressure into the aorta, which carries it in arteries to the body's organs."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta, not the pulmonary artery?", "o": ["The pulmonary artery only carries blood back towards the heart", "The aorta leads to the body; the pulmonary artery leads to the lungs", "The aorta is a vein, so it is the vessel that can carry oxygenated blood", "The right ventricle is the only chamber that can pump blood into an artery"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Arteries always carry blood away from the heart. The pulmonary artery carries blood from the right ventricle to the lungs.", "Right. The left ventricle serves the body through the aorta. The right ventricle serves the lungs through the pulmonary artery.", "Wrong. The aorta is an artery, since it carries blood away from the heart. Oxygen content does not decide the vessel type.", "Wrong. Both ventricles pump blood into arteries: the left into the aorta, the right into the pulmonary artery."]}, "faded": {"task": "Describe how deoxygenated blood travels from the body to the lungs. Name the vessels and chambers it passes through.", "given": ["Blood returns from the body in the vena cava.", "It enters the right atrium."], "q": "What is the next step in the answer?", "o": ["It flows straight into the pulmonary artery, which takes it to the lungs.", "It passes through a valve into the left ventricle.", "It enters the pulmonary vein, which takes it to the lungs.", "It passes through a valve into the right ventricle, which pumps it out."], "a": 3, "f": ["This skips a chamber. Blood must go through the right ventricle before it can enter the pulmonary artery.", "The left side holds oxygenated blood. Deoxygenated blood stays on the right side until it reaches the lungs.", "The pulmonary vein carries blood from the lungs to the heart. Blood going to the lungs travels in the pulmonary artery.", "Right. The right atrium passes blood through a valve to the right ventricle. The ventricle then pumps it into the pulmonary artery."]}, "trap": "Do not say veins always carry deoxygenated blood. Use direction instead: arteries carry blood away from the heart, veins towards it. The pulmonary artery is deoxygenated and the pulmonary vein is oxygenated.", "recap": "Arteries carry blood away from the heart, veins towards it; the right side pumps to the lungs, the left to the body."}
{"s": "business", "t": "1.2.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often give you a business and ask which type of market research it should use and why, so you need to know what each type gives and costs.", "hook": {"q": "A new bakery wants to know roughly how much bread people in its town buy. What is the quickest, cheapest way to start?", "o": ["Stop shoppers outside and interview each one", "Look up existing local population and spending figures", "Hold a focus group with eight local families", "Watch customers at a rival bakery all week"], "a": 1, "f": ["Slow and costly, and it only covers the people who happen to pass by.", "Right. The figures already exist, so nothing needs collecting. Using data someone else gathered is secondary research.", "A focus group takes time to set up and gives opinions from a few people, not town-wide totals.", "Watching takes a week and only shows one rival's customers, not the whole town."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Primary research", "b": "**Primary research** is new data you collect yourself, for your own question. Methods include surveys, questionnaires, **focus groups** and **observation**. It is up to date and fits your product exactly. But it takes time and money, and a small sample can be biased."}, {"h": "Secondary research", "b": "**Secondary research** uses data that already exists, collected by someone else. Examples are **market reports**, government statistics and websites. It is quick and cheap, and shows the wider market, such as its size. But it may be out of date or not answer your exact question."}, {"h": "Using both together", "b": "Many firms start with **secondary research** for cheap background. Then they use **primary research** to fill gaps about their own product. Data is more **reliable** if the sample is large and random, and comes from the target market. The graph shows a made-up survey. Press play and watch it settle as replies grow.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "draw", "pen": true, "read": ["replies", "% yes"], "dp": 1, "ms": 6000, "x": [0, 100], "xstep": 20, "y": [0, 100], "ystep": 20, "xlabel": "Number of replies", "ylabel": "Running % who would buy", "lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 2, "label": "Whole town 60%", "pts": [[0, 60], [100, 60]]}, {"c": 0, "label": "Survey result", "step": 1, "pts": [[5, 100], [10, 80], [20, 50], [30, 40], [40, 45], [50, 50], [60, 55], [80, 57.5], [100, 60]]}], "points": [{"at": [100, 60], "label": "60%"}], "cap": "With 5 replies the survey says 100%. With 100 replies it is at the true 60%. A bigger sample is more reliable."}}], "example": {"task": "Aisha plans to start a business selling handmade dog treats. Explain how she could use both secondary and primary research.", "steps": [{"do": "Aisha could first read an online market report on UK spending on pet food. This is secondary research, because someone else collected the data.", "why": "Names the type correctly and links it to her market."}, {"do": "The report is cheap and quick. It shows how big the market is and whether it is growing, so she can see if the market is worth entering.", "why": "Gives a benefit of secondary research, applied to her business."}, {"do": "However, the report cannot tell her what local dog owners think of her recipes or what price they would pay.", "why": "Shows the gap, which is why primary research is needed."}, {"do": "So she could give a questionnaire to a random sample of dog owners at a local park. This is primary research, as she collects new data first-hand.", "why": "Names primary research and a method, aimed at her target market."}, {"do": "Together, the report shows the market is worth entering and the questionnaire shows what to sell and at what price. This lowers her risk of a poor decision.", "why": "Links both types to a decision, which earns the final mark."}], "answer": "Aisha could use a market report (secondary) to find the size of the pet food market cheaply. She could then use a questionnaire (primary) with local dog owners to test her recipes and price. Together they reduce risk."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Aisha use the market report before the questionnaire?", "o": ["Secondary data is always more reliable than primary data", "Market reports count as primary data because she bought them", "It cheaply shows if the market is worth entering before she pays for primary", "The exam board says secondary research must always be done first"], "a": 2, "f": ["Reliability depends on the sample and the source, not the type. Secondary data can be out of date.", "A report is secondary however it is obtained. Someone else collected the data first.", "Right. Cheap background first shows whether the market is worth entering. Then she spends time and money only on the gaps.", "There is no such rule. Starting with secondary research is a sensible choice, not a requirement."]}, "faded": {"task": "A new gym wants to know which fitness classes people in its town would use. It has the figures below. What should it do next?", "given": ["A government report shows 12,000 adults live in the town. This is secondary research.", "It is cheap, but it does not show which classes people would use."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["Use a report on classes at gyms in another city", "Ask the eight gym staff which classes they like", "Read more government population statistics", "Survey a random sample of local adults on class choices"], "a": 3, "f": ["Still secondary, and about another city. It does not show what people in this town want.", "Staff are a tiny, biased group. They are not the target market.", "More population figures are secondary data and still will not show class preferences.", "Right. New first-hand data from a random sample of local people fills the gap the report leaves."]}, "trap": "Do not call data primary just because you found it yourself online. Primary means you collected it first-hand for your own question. If someone else collected it first, it is secondary.", "recap": "Primary data is collected by you, first-hand; secondary data already exists. Secondary gives cheap background, primary answers your own questions."}
{"s": "business", "t": "1.3.2", "kind": "method", "why": "Paper 1 often has a break-even or profit calculation, worth several marks, and you may be asked to read a break-even diagram.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Revenue, costs and profit", "b": "**Revenue** is the money from sales: $\\text{price} \\times \\text{quantity sold}$. **Fixed costs** stay the same whatever you sell, such as rent. **Variable costs** rise with output: cost per unit $\\times$ units. Total costs $=$ fixed $+$ variable. Profit $=$ total revenue $-$ total costs."}, {"h": "Interest on loans", "b": "**Interest** is the extra you pay back when you borrow. You find it as a percentage: $\\frac{\\text{total repayment} - \\text{amount borrowed}}{\\text{amount borrowed}} \\times 100$. If you borrow £10,000 and repay £10,500, that is $\\frac{500}{10{,}000} \\times 100 = 5\\%$. Interest is an extra **cost**, so it lowers profit."}, {"h": "Break-even and margin of safety", "b": "**Break-even** is the output where total revenue equals total costs: no profit, no loss. Each sale leaves price − variable cost per unit to pay off fixed costs. So break-even = fixed costs ÷ that amount. **Margin of safety** = actual output − break-even output. On the graph, fixed costs are £9,000, variable £4 a case. Drag the price slider.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [0, 1500], "y": [0, 24000], "xstep": 375, "ystep": 4000, "xlabel": "Output (cases per month)", "ylabel": "Costs and revenue (£)", "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "lines": [{"label": "Total costs", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 9000], [1500, 15000]]}, {"dash": true, "c": 2, "pts": [[0, 9000], [1500, 9000]]}], "points": [], "frames": [{"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [1500.0, 15000.0]], "label": "Revenue"}, {"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[1500.0, 0.0], [1500.0, 15000.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1500.0, 15000.0], "label": "Break-even"}], "read": ["Price £10 − £4 = £6 left per case", "Break-even = 9,000 ÷ 6 = 1500 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [1500.0, 18000.0]], "label": "Revenue"}, {"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[1125.0, 0.0], [1125.0, 13500.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1125.0, 13500.0], "label": "Break-even"}], "read": ["Price £12 − £4 = £8 left per case", "Break-even = 9,000 ÷ 8 = 1125 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [1500.0, 21000.0]], "label": "Revenue"}, {"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[900.0, 0.0], [900.0, 12600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [900.0, 12600.0], "label": "Break-even"}], "read": ["Price £14 − £4 = £10 left per case", "Break-even = 9,000 ÷ 10 = 900 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [1500.0, 24000.0]], "label": "Revenue"}, {"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[750.0, 0.0], [750.0, 12000.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [750.0, 12000.0], "label": "Break-even"}], "read": ["Price £16 − £4 = £12 left per case", "Break-even = 9,000 ÷ 12 = 750 cases"]}], "ctl": {"type": "slider", "label": "p", "vals": ["10", "12", "14", "16"], "start": 1}}}, {"h": "Reading a break-even diagram", "b": "On the graph, output is along the bottom and £ up the side. The revenue line starts at zero. The **total cost** line starts at the fixed-cost level. They cross at the **break-even point**. Right of it revenue is above costs: profit. Left: loss. Drag the slider to move the gap. Read the margin of safety along the output axis.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [0, 1500], "y": [0, 18000], "xstep": 375, "ystep": 4500, "xlabel": "Output (cases per month)", "ylabel": "Costs and revenue (£)", "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "lines": [{"label": "Revenue", "c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1500, 18000]]}, {"label": "Total costs", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 9000], [1500, 15000]]}, {"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[0, 9000], [1500, 9000]]}], "points": [{"at": [1125, 13500], "label": "Break-even"}], "frames": [{"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[0.0, 9000.0], [0.0, 0.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [0.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 0 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £−9000", "Output − break-even = −1125 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[125.0, 9500.0], [125.0, 1500.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [125.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 125 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £−8000", "Output − break-even = −1000 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[250.0, 10000.0], [250.0, 3000.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [250.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 250 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £−7000", "Output − break-even = −875 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[375.0, 10500.0], [375.0, 4500.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [375.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 375 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £−6000", "Output − break-even = −750 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[500.0, 11000.0], [500.0, 6000.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [500.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 500 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £−5000", "Output − break-even = −625 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[625.0, 11500.0], [625.0, 7500.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [625.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 625 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £−4000", "Output − break-even = −500 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[750.0, 12000.0], [750.0, 9000.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [750.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 750 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £−3000", "Output − break-even = −375 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[875.0, 12500.0], [875.0, 10500.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [875.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 875 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £−2000", "Output − break-even = −250 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[1000.0, 13000.0], [1000.0, 12000.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1000.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 1000 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £−1000", "Output − break-even = −125 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[1125.0, 13500.0], [1125.0, 13500.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1125.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 1125 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £0", "Output − break-even = 0 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[1250.0, 14000.0], [1250.0, 15000.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1250.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 1250 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £1000", "Output − break-even = 125 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[1375.0, 14500.0], [1375.0, 16500.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1375.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 1375 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £2000", "Output − break-even = 250 cases"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 2, "pts": [[1500.0, 15000.0], [1500.0, 18000.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1500.0, 0], "label": "Output"}], "read": ["Output = 1500 cases", "Profit (− is a loss) = £3000", "Output − break-even = 375 cases"]}], "ctl": {"type": "slider", "label": "q", "vals": ["0", "125", "250", "375", "500", "625", "750", "875", "1000", "1125", "1250", "1375", "1500"], "start": 6}}}], "example": {"task": "Press Next step to build the graph. A firm making phone cases: fixed costs £9,000 a month, price £12, variable costs £4 a case, sales of 1,500 a month. Find break-even output, margin of safety and profit.", "steps": [{"do": "Price minus variable cost per unit: £12 − £4 = £8.", "why": "This is what each case sells for after its own variable cost, so it is what goes towards fixed costs."}, {"do": "Break-even = fixed costs ÷ £8 = 9,000 ÷ 8 = 1,125 cases.", "why": "This is how many £8 amounts are needed to cover £9,000 of fixed costs."}, {"do": "Margin of safety = actual output − break-even = 1,500 − 1,125 = 375 cases.", "why": "It shows how far sales could fall before the firm makes a loss."}, {"do": "Revenue = £12 × 1,500 = £18,000. Variable costs = £4 × 1,500 = £6,000. Total costs = £9,000 + £6,000 = £15,000.", "why": "Profit needs both total revenue and total costs, and total costs include fixed and variable."}, {"do": "Profit = £18,000 − £15,000 = £3,000.", "why": "Check: 375 cases above break-even × £8 = £3,000. Both methods agree."}], "answer": "Break-even is 1,125 cases a month. The margin of safety is 375 cases. Profit is £3,000 a month.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "steps", "x": [0, 1500], "y": [0, 18000], "xstep": 375, "ystep": 4500, "xlabel": "Output (cases per month)", "ylabel": "Costs and revenue (£)", "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "lines": [{"label": "Fixed costs", "dash": true, "c": 2, "step": 1, "pts": [[0, 9000], [1500, 9000]]}, {"label": "Total costs", "c": 1, "step": 2, "pts": [[0, 9000], [1500, 15000]]}, {"label": "Revenue", "c": 0, "step": 3, "pts": [[0, 0], [1500, 18000]]}, {"dash": true, "c": 3, "step": 5, "pts": [[1500, 0], [1500, 15000]]}, {"label": "Margin of safety", "c": 3, "step": 5, "pts": [[1500, 0], [1125, 0]]}, {"c": 2, "step": 6, "pts": [[1500, 15000], [1500, 18000]]}], "points": [{"at": [1125, 13500], "label": "Break-even", "step": 4}], "steps": ["Fixed costs are £9,000 a month whatever the output, so the line is flat.", "Total costs start at £9,000 and rise £4 for every case made. At 1,500 cases: £9,000 + £6,000 = £15,000.", "Revenue starts at zero and rises £12 for every case sold. At 1,500 cases it is £18,000.", "The lines cross at 1,125 cases, where revenue = total costs = £13,500. That is break-even.", "Actual sales are 1,500 cases. Margin of safety = 1,500 − 1,125 = 375 cases, read along the output axis.", "At 1,500 cases revenue (£18,000) is above total costs (£15,000). The gap is the profit: £3,000."]}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 1 take the £4 variable cost off the £12 price before dividing?", "o": ["Because variable costs are paid only once, at the start of the month", "Because the £12 price is too large a number to divide into fixed costs", "Because each case sold covers its own £4 cost, leaving £8 towards fixed costs", "Because profit is always found by taking variable costs off the price"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Variable costs are paid on every unit made. It is fixed costs that stay the same whatever the output.", "Wrong. The size of the price is not the issue. Using the full price ignores that each unit has its own cost to cover.", "Right. Only the £8 left after variable costs can pay off fixed costs, so that is the amount you divide by.", "Wrong. This confuses profit with the break-even step. Profit is revenue minus total costs, and fixed costs are included."]}, "faded": {"task": "The graph shows a firm with fixed costs of £4,800 a month. Each unit sells for £9 and has variable costs of £5. It makes 1,500 units a month. Find the margin of safety.", "given": ["Price − variable cost per unit = £9 − £5 = £4", "Break-even = 4,800 ÷ 4 = 1,200 units"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Fixed costs ÷ actual output: 4,800 ÷ 1,500 = 3.2 units", "Actual output − break-even: 1,500 − 1,200 = 300 units", "Break-even − actual output: 1,200 − 1,500 = −300 units", "Actual output × £4: 1,500 × £4 = £6,000"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. This divides the wrong way round and gives a cost per unit, not a number of units.", "Right. Margin of safety is actual output minus break-even output, so 300 units.", "Wrong. The subtraction is the wrong way round. A negative margin of safety would mean a loss, but 1,500 is above break-even.", "Wrong. This is the total left after variable costs. It is not the margin of safety, which is a gap in units."], "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 1800], "y": [0, 16200], "xstep": 300, "ystep": 1800, "xlabel": "Output (units per month)", "ylabel": "Costs and revenue (£)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [1800, 16200]], "label": "Revenue", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 4800], [1800, 13800]], "label": "Total costs", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 4800], [1800, 4800]], "label": "Fixed costs", "dash": true, "c": 2}, {"pts": [[1500, 0], [1500, 16200]], "label": "Actual sales", "dash": true, "c": 3}], "points": [{"at": [1200, 10800], "label": "Break-even"}], "xnums": true, "ynums": true}}, "trap": "Dividing fixed costs by the selling price instead of by price minus variable cost per unit. Work out the amount left per unit first, then divide. Margin of safety is usually in units.", "recap": "Break-even = fixed costs ÷ (price − variable cost per unit); margin of safety = actual output − break-even output."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.1.1.7", "kind": "idea", "why": "Electronic structure explains the periodic table, reactivity and bonding later in the course, and short questions on it appear in Paper 1.", "hook": {"q": "A carbon atom has 6 electrons. The first shell, nearest the nucleus, can hold only 2. Where do the other 4 most likely go?", "o": ["Into the first shell, because a shell can hold any number", "Into the next shell out, the second shell", "Into a shell far from the nucleus, to keep them apart", "Nowhere, because only 2 electrons can be in an atom"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. The first shell is full at 2 electrons, so any extra electrons cannot fit there.", "Right. When the first shell is full, electrons go into the next shell out. This idea of filling from the inside is how all electronic structures are built.", "Wrong. Electrons do not skip shells. They fill the lowest energy level first, then the next one out.", "Wrong. An atom can have many more than 2 electrons. They are spread over several shells."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Electrons sit in shells", "b": "Electrons move around the nucleus in **shells**, also called **energy levels**. The first shell is closest to the nucleus and has the lowest energy. Electrons always go into the **lowest available energy level** first."}, {"h": "How many each shell holds", "b": "The **first shell** holds up to 2 electrons. The **second shell** holds up to 8. For the first 20 elements, the third also holds up to 8. A new shell only starts once the shell inside it is **full**."}, {"h": "Writing the structure", "b": "Write the number in each shell as a list, from the inside outwards, with commas between. In a neutral atom, the **number of electrons** equals the **atomic number**. The numbers in your list must add up to it."}, {"h": "What the numbers tell you", "b": "The number of electrons in the **outer shell** matches the **group number** for Groups 1 to 7. The number of occupied shells matches the **period number**. So 2,8,3 is in Group 3, Period 3."}], "example": {"task": "Chlorine has atomic number 17. Explain how to find its electronic structure and use it to say which group and period chlorine is in.", "steps": [{"do": "Atomic number 17 means 17 protons. A neutral atom has 17 electrons.", "why": "Neutral atoms have equal numbers of protons and electrons, so this is the total to place."}, {"do": "Put 2 electrons in the first shell. That leaves 15.", "why": "The lowest energy level fills first, and it holds a maximum of 2."}, {"do": "Put 8 electrons in the second shell. That leaves 7.", "why": "The second shell holds 8 and must be full before the third shell starts."}, {"do": "Put the remaining 7 in the third shell. The structure is 2,8,7. Check: 2 + 8 + 7 = 17.", "why": "7 is fewer than 8, so the third shell is not full. The total check shows nothing is missing."}, {"do": "The outer shell has 7 electrons, so chlorine is in Group 7. Three shells are used, so it is in Period 3.", "why": "Outer electrons give the group. Occupied shells give the period."}], "answer": "Chlorine has 17 electrons. They fill from the inside: 2 in the first shell, 8 in the second, 7 in the third, giving 2,8,7. The outer shell has 7 electrons, so it is in Group 7. It has three shells, so it is in Period 3."}, "selfx": {"q": "In the chlorine example, why are 8 electrons put in the second shell before any go in the third?", "o": ["Every shell in every atom must contain exactly 8 electrons", "The third shell is nearest the nucleus, so it has to fill last", "The second shell has lower energy than the third and holds 8, so it fills first", "The group number tells you how many electrons go in the second shell"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Only the second and third shells hold 8 here. The first holds 2, and outer shells can have fewer than 8 electrons.", "Wrong. The first shell is nearest the nucleus, then the second, then the third. The nearest shells fill first, not last.", "Right. Electrons fill the lowest energy level first. The second shell is lower than the third, and it holds 8, so it must be full first.", "Wrong. The group number matches the outer shell electrons, not the second shell. It is a result of the structure, not a rule for filling."]}, "faded": {"task": "Calcium has atomic number 20. Work out its electronic structure.", "given": ["A neutral calcium atom has 20 electrons.", "Shell 1 takes 2 electrons, leaving 18.", "Shell 2 takes 8 electrons, leaving 10."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Put all 10 in the third shell, giving 2,8,10", "Put 8 in the third shell, then the last 2 in a fourth shell: 2,8,8,2", "Put 2 in the third shell and 8 in the fourth shell: 2,8,2,8", "Put 8 in the third shell and stop, giving 2,8,8"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. For the first 20 elements the third shell holds only 8. Putting 10 in it breaks the rule.", "Right. The third shell holds 8, leaving 2 for the fourth shell. This gives 2,8,8,2, which adds up to 20.", "Wrong. This fills the fourth shell before the third is full. Each shell must be full before the next one starts.", "Wrong. 2 + 8 + 8 = 18, not 20. Two electrons are missing, so check that your total matches the atomic number."]}, "trap": "Starting a new shell before the one inside is full, or not checking that the numbers add up to the atomic number. Fill 2, then 8, then 8, then add your numbers.", "recap": "Fill shells from the inside (2, 8, 8), write the numbers in order, and check they add up to the atomic number."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.3.1.2", "kind": "method", "why": "You need relative formula mass in almost every calculation in this unit, including moles, and it often appears as a short calculation question on its own.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Adding up the Mr", "b": "The **relative formula mass (Mr)** of a compound is the sum of the **relative atomic masses (Ar)** of all the atoms in its formula. Take each Ar from the periodic table or the question. Multiply by the number of atoms of that element, then add."}, {"h": "Brackets in formulae", "b": "A small number (a **subscript**) just after a **bracket** multiplies everything inside it. In Mg(OH)₂ there are 2 O atoms and 2 H atoms. Work out the bracket first, then multiply. Missing the number after a bracket is a common way to lose marks."}, {"h": "Percentage by mass", "b": "The **percentage by mass** of an element tells you how much of the compound's mass is that element. Use $\\frac{A_r \\times \\text{number of atoms}}{M_r \\text{ of compound}} \\times 100$. Find the **Mr of the compound** first. Count every atom of the element, not just one."}, {"h": "Check your answer", "b": "The percentages of all the elements in a compound add up to **100%**. Your answer for one element must be between 0 and 100. Round only at the end, and give the number of **significant figures** the question asks for."}], "example": {"task": "Calculate the percentage by mass of oxygen in calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂. Ar: Ca = 40, O = 16, H = 1. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.", "steps": [{"do": "Count the atoms: 1 Ca, 2 O, 2 H.", "why": "The 2 after the bracket multiplies both O and H inside it."}, {"do": "Mr = 40 + (2 × 16) + (2 × 1) = 40 + 32 + 2 = 74.", "why": "Mr is the sum of the Ar of every atom in the formula."}, {"do": "Mass of oxygen in one formula unit = 2 × 16 = 32.", "why": "There are two O atoms, so both count, not just one."}, {"do": "Percentage = 32 ÷ 74 × 100 = 43.243...%", "why": "This is the oxygen's share of the whole Mr, written as a percentage."}, {"do": "Round to 3 significant figures: 43.2%.", "why": "The question asks for 3 significant figures, so round only now."}], "answer": "The percentage of oxygen in Ca(OH)₂ is 43.2% by mass."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the mass of oxygen divided by the Mr of the whole compound in step 4?", "o": ["Because the Mr is always larger than 100, so the answer comes out as a percentage", "Because the percentage is oxygen's share of the total mass of the compound", "Because the Ar of calcium and hydrogen are not needed in the calculation", "Because dividing by the Mr removes the units from the Ar of oxygen"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. The Mr of a compound can be any value, and here it is 74. You divide by it to compare with the whole, not to reach 100.", "Right. A percentage compares a part with the whole. The part is the mass of oxygen and the whole is the Mr of the compound.", "Wrong. Ca and H are needed, because they make up the Mr that you divide by. Leaving them out would give the wrong total.", "Wrong. Ar values have no units. You divide by Mr to find the fraction of the mass that is oxygen."]}, "faded": {"task": "Calculate the percentage by mass of oxygen in magnesium nitrate, Mg(NO₃)₂. Ar: Mg = 24, N = 14, O = 16.", "given": ["Ar values: Mg = 24, N = 14, O = 16.", "Mr = 24 + 2 × (14 + 48) = 24 + 124 = 148."], "q": "What is the mass of oxygen to put on the top of the fraction?", "o": ["3 × 16 = 48", "2 × 16 = 32", "6 × 16 = 96", "1 × 16 = 16"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. This counts only the 3 O atoms in one NO₃ group. The 2 outside the bracket doubles this to 6 O atoms.", "Wrong. This uses the 2 outside the bracket but ignores the 3 in NO₃. Each nitrate group has 3 O atoms.", "Right. Each NO₃ has 3 O atoms and there are 2 NO₃ groups, so 6 O atoms. 6 × 16 = 96. Then 96 ÷ 148 × 100 = 64.9%.", "Wrong. This uses the Ar of just one oxygen atom. You must multiply by the number of O atoms in the formula."]}, "trap": "Using the Ar of just one atom, or ignoring the number outside a bracket. Count every atom of the element, and multiply anything inside a bracket by the number after it.", "recap": "Find the Mr by adding every atom, then divide (Ar × number of atoms) by the Mr and multiply by 100."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.1.5", "kind": "method", "why": "The exam often asks for the speed, direction or lever order of a mechanism, and gear and pulley calculations are quick marks if you follow the same method each time.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Types of movement", "b": "**Rotary** movement goes round in a circle. Linear goes one way in a straight line. **Reciprocating** goes back and forth in a straight line. **Oscillating** swings back and forth along an arc. A cam turns rotary movement into reciprocating movement. In the graph, the follower rests, rises, then returns. A bell crank linkage turns movement through 90°.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 360], "y": [0, 20], "xstep": 60, "ystep": 5, "xlabel": "cam rotation / °", "ylabel": "follower displacement / mm", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [120, 0], [240, 20], [360, 0]], "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [60, 0], "label": "Dwell"}, {"at": [180, 10], "label": "Rise"}, {"at": [300, 10], "label": "Return"}]}}, {"h": "Gears: direction and ratio", "b": "Meshing gears turn opposite ways. An **idler** gear between driver and output makes them turn the same way. **Gear ratio** = driven teeth ÷ driver teeth. Speed out = speed in ÷ ratio. A larger driven gear turns slower but gives more turning force."}, {"h": "Pulleys and belts", "b": "Pulleys use the same ratio as gears, but with **diameters** instead of teeth: ratio = driven diameter ÷ driver diameter. An **open belt** keeps the direction the same. A **crossed belt** reverses it. A larger driven pulley turns slower."}, {"h": "Levers: three orders", "b": "Every lever has an effort, a load and a pivot. In a **first order** lever the pivot is in the middle, like a seesaw. In a **second order** lever the load is in the middle, like a wheelbarrow. In a **third order** lever the effort is in the middle, like tweezers."}], "example": {"task": "Gear A (driver, 20 teeth) turns clockwise at 240 rpm. It meshes with idler B (40 teeth), which meshes with gear C (output, 60 teeth). Find the speed and direction of C.", "steps": [{"do": "Find the direction. A is clockwise, so B is anticlockwise, so C is clockwise.", "why": "Each meshing pair turns opposite ways. Two reversals bring C back to the same direction as A."}, {"do": "Ignore the idler. Use the driver (20 teeth) and the output (60 teeth) only.", "why": "The idler only changes direction. Only the first and last gears set the speed."}, {"do": "Ratio = driven ÷ driver = 60 ÷ 20 = 3, so the ratio is 3:1.", "why": "This is the gear ratio formula, with the output gear as the driven gear."}, {"do": "Speed out = 240 ÷ 3 = 80 rpm.", "why": "Speed out = speed in ÷ ratio."}, {"do": "Check: C is bigger than A, so it should turn slower. 80 rpm is less than 240 rpm.", "why": "A quick sense check catches an upside-down ratio."}], "answer": "Gear C turns at 80 rpm clockwise."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked example leave out the idler's 40 teeth when it works out the ratio?", "o": ["The idler does not turn, so it cannot change the speed", "The idler's effect cancels out, so only the first and last gears set the speed", "The idler has the most teeth, so it is the largest gear", "Idlers are never counted because they are always the middle gear"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. The idler does turn. Its teeth drive C and are driven by A, so its effect cancels out, and this is why it only changes direction.", "Right. The idler is driven by A and then drives C, so its tooth count cancels out. It changes direction only.", "Wrong. Having the most teeth is not the reason. Even a tiny idler would be left out, because only the driver and output set the speed.", "Wrong. Being in the middle is not the reason. The idler is left out because its effect on speed cancels out, not because of where it sits."], "a_note": null}, "faded": {"task": "Driver gear P (48 teeth) turns anticlockwise at 100 rpm. Idler Q has 16 teeth. Output gear R has 12 teeth. Find the speed and direction of R.", "given": ["Direction: P anticlockwise, Q clockwise, so R is anticlockwise.", "Ignore the idler. Use driver 48 teeth and output 12 teeth."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Ratio = 48 ÷ 12 = 4, so speed out = 100 ÷ 4 = 25 rpm", "Ratio = 16 ÷ 48 = 1/3, so speed out = 100 ÷ (1/3) = 300 rpm", "Ratio = 12 ÷ 48 = 0.25, so speed out = 100 ÷ 0.25 = 400 rpm", "Ratio = 12 ÷ 48 = 0.25, so speed out = 100 × 0.25 = 25 rpm"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. This turns the ratio upside down. The ratio is driven ÷ driver, which is 12 ÷ 48. Here the small output gear should turn faster, not slower.", "Wrong. This puts the idler's 16 teeth into the ratio. Only the driver (48) and the output (12) are used.", "Right. The ratio is 12 ÷ 48 = 0.25. Dividing 100 rpm by 0.25 gives 400 rpm. The small output gear turns faster.", "Wrong. The ratio is correct, but speed out = speed in ÷ ratio. Multiplying makes the small output gear turn slower, which fails the sense check."]}, "trap": "Putting the idler's teeth into the ratio. Only the driver and the output gear set the speed. The idler only changes direction. Cross out the idler before you calculate.", "recap": "Ratio = driven ÷ driver, speed out = speed in ÷ ratio, and the idler changes direction only."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.2.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "The written paper often shows a product and asks you to name the forces on it, then say how to stiffen it. This lesson covers both.", "hook": {"q": "You clamp a plastic ruler flat on a desk at one end, sticking out over the edge, and press down on the free end. Which surface is stretched?", "o": ["The bottom surface, because it is nearest the desk", "Neither surface. The ruler only gets shorter overall", "The top surface, on the outside of the curve", "Both surfaces are stretched by the same amount"], "a": 2, "f": ["Nearest the desk is not what matters. The bottom is on the inside of the curve, so it is squashed, not stretched.", "The ruler does not simply shorten. Bending stretches one surface and squashes the other.", "The top is on the outside of the curve, so it has further to go and is stretched. This is tension.", "Bending does not treat both sides alike. One side is stretched and the other is squashed."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Tension and compression", "b": "**Tension** is a pulling force that tries to stretch a material, like the cable holding up a swing. **Compression** is a pushing force that tries to squash it, like the legs of a stool when you sit on it. Tension pulls apart. Compression pushes together."}, {"h": "Bending", "b": "A **bending** force makes a beam curve under a load. On a **cantilever**, which is fixed at one end only, a load pushes the free end down. The top surface is stretched, so it is in tension. The bottom surface is squashed, so it is in compression."}, {"h": "Torsion and shear", "b": "**Torsion** is a twisting force along the length of an object, like turning a screwdriver or wringing out a cloth. **Shear** is when two forces act in opposite directions very close together and try to slide one part past another, like scissors cutting paper or a bolt in a hinge."}, {"h": "Stiffening and reinforcing", "b": "**Triangulation** uses diagonal braces, because a triangle cannot change shape without its sides changing length. **Ribs** add depth along a thin part. Folding or corrugating sheet material does the same. **Laminating** bonds thin layers together to make a stiffer, stronger board."}], "example": {"task": "A wooden shelf is fixed to a wall at one end only. A heavy box sits on its free end. Explain the forces in the shelf and how to stiffen it.", "steps": [{"do": "The box pushes the free end down, so the shelf acts as a cantilever and is under a bending force.", "why": "Name the overall force first. A load on a beam fixed at one end causes bending."}, {"do": "The top surface is on the outside of the curve. It is stretched, so it is in tension.", "why": "The outside of a curve gets longer. Stretching means tension."}, {"do": "The bottom surface is on the inside of the curve. It is squashed, so it is in compression.", "why": "The inside of a curve gets shorter. Squashing means compression."}, {"do": "To stiffen it, fix a triangular bracket under the shelf, from the wall to the shelf, so the angle cannot change.", "why": "Triangulation stops the shelf bending down. The answer links the method to the problem."}], "answer": "The box makes the shelf bend like a cantilever. The top is in tension and the bottom is in compression. A triangular bracket under the shelf would stiffen it by triangulation, stopping the shelf bending down."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the top surface of the shelf is in tension?", "o": ["Tension is the name for any force that pushes down from above", "Bending puts the top on the outside of the curve, so it gets longer and is stretched", "The wall holds the shelf, so the top is pulled towards the wall", "The box twists the shelf, and twisting always puts the top in tension"], "a": 1, "f": ["Tension is a pulling force that stretches, not a downward push. The downward load causes the bending, but the stretch is what makes it tension.", "The outside of a curve is longer, so the top is stretched. Stretching is tension.", "The wall holds the shelf in place. It is not what stretches the top surface. The curve of the bend does that.", "A load on the end of a shelf causes bending, not torsion. Torsion is twisting along the length."]}, "faded": {"task": "A flat steel strip is clamped at one end. A weight hangs from its free end. Explain the forces in the strip and how to stiffen it.", "given": ["The weight makes the strip bend downwards, so it acts as a cantilever.", "The top surface is stretched, so it is in tension.", "The bottom surface is squashed, so it is in compression."], "q": "Which point completes the answer by saying how to stiffen the strip?", "o": ["Make the strip thinner so that it carries less of the load", "Paint the strip, because a coating stops the force reaching the metal", "Drill a row of holes along it, because a lighter strip bends less", "Fold the long edges down into a channel, adding depth to resist bending"], "a": 3, "f": ["A thinner strip is easier to bend. The load is the same, and less material means less stiffness.", "Paint protects against rust but does not change how stiff the strip is. Forces act through a coating.", "Holes remove material, so the strip is less stiff and bends more. Its own weight is tiny compared with the hanging load.", "Folding the edges adds depth to a flat strip. A deeper shape resists bending far better than a flat one."]}, "trap": "Swapping tension and compression on a bent beam, or calling every twist 'bending'. Picture the load pushing the free end down: the top stretches, the bottom squashes. Twisting along the length is torsion.", "recap": "A loaded cantilever has tension on top and compression underneath; torsion twists, shear slides, and triangles, ribs, folds and laminating stiffen."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p1-q2", "kind": "writing", "why": "Q2 is worth 8 marks on Paper 1, and most of them come from explaining the effects of the writer's specific word choices.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Quote, name, explain", "b": "Make a **point** about what the writer creates, such as danger or sadness. Give a short **quotation** of a few words. Then explain what those words suggest. Use **subject terminology**, such as 'verb' or 'personification', where it helps, but naming a technique earns little on its own."}, {"h": "Zoom in on one word", "b": "Choose the most powerful word, often a verb or adjective. Ask what it suggests: its **connotations**. 'Slithered' links to snakes, so a person seems sly. This close look at a **word choice** is where higher marks are earned."}, {"h": "Finish with the effect", "b": "End each point with the **effect on the reader**. What do they picture or feel? Use phrases such as 'this suggests' and 'this makes the reader'. Link it to what the writer is creating, such as **tension** or sympathy."}], "example": {"task": "Read: \"The wind clawed at her coat. She stumbled on, the dark lane swallowing her footsteps.\" How does the writer use language to create a threatening setting?", "steps": [{"do": "The writer presents the wind as hostile through personification: \"The wind clawed at her coat.\"", "why": "Makes a clear point, names the technique and quotes briefly. This is the starting point, not the whole answer."}, {"do": "The verb \"clawed\" suggests sharp claws, so the wind seems like an aggressive animal.", "why": "Zooms in on one word and explains what it suggests. This is the analysis the marks reward."}, {"do": "This makes the reader feel she is in danger, creating tension.", "why": "Links the word to the effect on the reader, which is what the question asks for."}, {"do": "Similarly, \"swallowing her footsteps\" suggests the dark devours sound, making the lane threatening and isolating.", "why": "Adds a second, different quotation and explains its effect, which widens the range of the answer."}], "answer": "The writer presents the wind as hostile through personification: \"The wind clawed at her coat.\" The verb \"clawed\" suggests sharp claws, so the wind seems like an aggressive animal. This makes the reader feel she is in danger, creating tension. Similarly, \"swallowing her footsteps\" suggests the dark devours sound, making the lane threatening and isolating."}, "selfx": {"q": "In the second step, the answer focuses on the single word \"clawed\". Why does it do this?", "o": ["Only verbs can be analysed, so the other words in the quotation must be ignored", "One word always earns more marks than a longer quotation, whatever is said about it", "That word carries the meaning, so exploring what it suggests shows close analysis", "One word speaks for itself, so no explanation of its effect is needed"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Other word types can be analysed too. Verbs are often a good choice, but this is not a rule that bars the rest.", "Wrong. Marks come from the quality of the explanation, not from the length of the quotation. A short quote with no analysis earns little.", "Right. 'Clawed' links to animals and pain, so exploring it shows what the writer's choice suggests. That detail is what earns the marks.", "Wrong. No word explains itself. You must always say what it suggests and how it affects the reader."]}, "faded": {"task": "Read: \"Rain hammered the roof. Inside, the kitchen was a cage of silence.\" How does the writer use language to create atmosphere? Choose the best next sentence.", "given": ["The writer creates a violent, uneasy atmosphere. The storm is shown through the verb in \"Rain hammered the roof.\""], "q": "Which sentence should come next to earn the most marks?", "o": ["'Hammered' is a verb, a doing word, so it shows what the rain does to the roof.", "'Hammered' suggests heavy, repeated blows, so the rain seems forceful and relentless.", "The writer says the rain hammered the roof to describe the weather in a lot of detail.", "'Hammered' is onomatopoeia, which makes the writing more interesting for the reader."], "a": 1, "f": ["This only gives the word class. It does not say what the word suggests, so there is no analysis.", "Right. It zooms in on 'hammered' and explains what it suggests (heavy, repeated blows). The effect on the reader can follow.", "This repeats the quotation in other words. It says nothing about what the word suggests, so it is description, not analysis.", "This names a technique, then gives a vague effect ('more interesting'). It does not explain what these particular words suggest."]}, "trap": "Naming a technique, such as personification, and stopping. Always add the specific words and what they suggest. Test yourself: have you written 'this suggests…' or 'this makes the reader…'?", "recap": "Quote a few words, zoom in on one, then explain what it suggests and how it affects the reader."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p2-q5a", "kind": "writing", "why": "Question 5 is worth 40 marks, half of Paper 2, and asks you to argue a view for a stated audience in a form such as a letter, article or speech.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Fix audience, purpose, form", "b": "Before you write, decide three things. The **audience** is who reads it. The **purpose** is usually to argue or persuade. The **form** is a letter, article or speech. Together they set your tone. A headteacher needs formal Standard English. A speech to students can be warmer and more direct."}, {"h": "State and develop points", "b": "Say your **viewpoint** clearly in the opening. Then give each point its own paragraph. Do not stop at the opinion. **Develop** it with a reason, an example or a result. \"Homework is too long\" is only a claim. Add what it causes, and the point starts to persuade."}, {"h": "Answer the other side", "b": "A **counter-argument** is a view that opposes yours. Raise it fairly: \"Some may argue... but...\". Then **answer** it with a reason. This shows you have thought about both sides, and it makes your own case stronger. One short paragraph is enough."}, {"h": "Techniques and accuracy", "b": "Use techniques on purpose, not in every sentence: **direct address**, a rhetorical question, a list of three, a short fact or statistic. End with a strong closing line. Marks also go on **technical accuracy**: spelling, punctuation and varied sentences."}], "example": {"task": "Write a letter to your headteacher, Mr Hughes, arguing that the school library should stay open until 5 pm. A model answer is built below in four parts.", "steps": [{"do": "Dear Mr Hughes, I am writing to urge you to keep the library open until 5 pm.", "why": "Formal greeting suits the audience. It states the purpose and the writer's view straight away."}, {"do": "Many of us share noisy homes, so quiet study is hard. A calm library lets us revise properly.", "why": "The point is developed: a reason (noisy homes) and a result (better revision), not just an opinion."}, {"do": "Some may argue it costs too much. But staff are already on site, so the cost is small.", "why": "It raises the opposing view fairly, then answers it with a reason. This shows balance and strengthens the case."}, {"do": "Surely better exam results are worth it?", "why": "A short rhetorical question links back to the revision point and ends firmly on the writer's view."}], "answer": "Dear Mr Hughes, I am writing to urge you to keep the library open until 5 pm. Many of us share noisy homes, so quiet study is hard. A calm library lets us revise properly. Some may argue it costs too much. But staff are already on site, so the cost is small. Surely better exam results are worth it?"}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 add \"But staff are already on site\" after the opposing view?", "o": ["It repeats the opening request in different words.", "It answers the opposing view with a reason, so the case looks stronger.", "It switches the audience from the headteacher to the staff.", "It agrees the library should close, to seem fair."], "a": 1, "f": ["The sentence gives a new reason (staff are already there). It does not repeat the request from the opening.", "Right. Raising a counter-argument is not enough. You must answer it with a reason, which makes your own view more convincing.", "The audience is still Mr Hughes. The staff are mentioned as evidence, not addressed.", "The writer still argues for the library staying open. Being fair means admitting the other view exists, not giving in to it."]}, "faded": {"task": "Write a speech for Year 10 arguing that students should volunteer in their community.", "given": ["Opening: \"Good morning, everyone. Today I want to persuade you that volunteering is worth your time.\"", "Point 1: \"It builds skills. Organising a charity event teaches teamwork that no lesson can match.\""], "q": "Which sentence should come next to raise and answer a counter-argument?", "o": ["Volunteering is also good fun, and it makes you feel happy and proud.", "Some people say they are too busy, but honestly they are just being lazy.", "Of course, nobody has time for volunteering, so I understand if you disagree.", "Some may argue that we are too busy. But one hour a week is easy to fit in."], "a": 3, "f": ["This is another opinion in support. It does not raise the opposing view or answer it.", "It raises the other side but only insults it. There is no reason, and the tone could put off the audience.", "It raises the other side but gives in to it. The writer must answer the objection, not agree with it.", "Right. It states the opposing view fairly with \"Some may argue\", then answers it with a reason: one hour a week is small."]}, "trap": "Writing a list of opinions (\"It's unfair. It's boring.\") without reasons. Fix: after each point, add \"because\" and a result or example. Match your tone to the reader: formal for a headteacher, no slang.", "recap": "Fix audience, purpose and form first, then state your view, develop every point, and answer a counter-argument in a tone that suits the reader."}
{"s": "english_lit", "t": "lit-nv-cc", "kind": "writing", "why": "Paper 1 Section B tests Dickens's ideas and methods, and a tight paragraph built on a short quotation is the core of every answer.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Start with a clear point", "b": "Begin with a **point** that answers the question about Dickens's **ideas**. Say what he presents and what it shows about society. Do not retell the plot. 'Dickens presents Scrooge as…' beats 'Then the ghost takes Scrooge to…'."}, {"h": "Quote, method, effect", "b": "Use a short **quotation**, only a few words. Name the **method**, such as an imperative, a simile, personification or a symbol. Then explain the **effect** on the reader. All three together make analysis."}, {"h": "Link to context and purpose", "b": "Add **context** only when it explains the idea. The novella came out in 1843. Many poor children had little food or schooling, and the 1834 Poor Law pushed the poor towards workhouses. End on Dickens's **purpose**: what he wants readers to think or do."}], "example": {"task": "Explore how Dickens presents poverty in A Christmas Carol. Write one analytical paragraph about the novella as a whole.", "steps": [{"do": "Dickens presents poverty as a danger the rich can still prevent.", "why": "A clear point answers the question directly and sets the idea you will prove."}, {"do": "The Ghost's imperative, 'Beware them both', about Ignorance and Want, two personified children, makes the warning urgent and speaks to readers as well as Scrooge.", "why": "A short quotation is evidence. Naming the method and its effect shows how Dickens's language works."}, {"do": "Many poor Victorian children lacked food and schooling, so the warning felt real.", "why": "Context earns marks when it explains why the idea mattered to Dickens's readers."}, {"do": "Scrooge's final generosity shows the rich can act, as Dickens urges.", "why": "Linking to the whole novella and Dickens's purpose shows you understand the book as a whole."}], "answer": "Dickens presents poverty as a danger the rich can still prevent. The Ghost's imperative, 'Beware them both', about Ignorance and Want, two personified children, makes the warning urgent and speaks to readers as well as Scrooge. Many poor Victorian children lacked food and schooling, so the warning felt real. Scrooge's final generosity shows the rich can act, as Dickens urges."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model paragraph name the 'imperative' when it quotes the Ghost?", "o": ["The mark scheme demands one technical term in every sentence", "It shows how Dickens's language creates an effect on readers", "It makes the quotation look longer and more impressive", "It proves the quotation has been copied out accurately"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. No such rule exists. A term earns marks only when you use it to explain an effect.", "Right. Naming the method and saying what it does shows you are analysing Dickens's choices, not just quoting.", "Wrong. Length earns nothing. The quotation is kept short so that your comment on it can carry the marks.", "Wrong. Accuracy matters, but naming a method has nothing to do with checking a quotation."], "x": null}, "faded": {"task": "Explore how Dickens presents Scrooge at the start of the novella. Write one analytical paragraph.", "given": ["Dickens presents Scrooge as cold and isolated by choice.", "He is described as 'solitary as an oyster'."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to earn the most marks?", "o": ["Dickens uses a simile here, which is a good technique to use.", "Scrooge is very unkind and the reader dislikes him a lot.", "The simile suggests he shuts himself away, like a closed shell, by choice.", "Later, the Ghost of Christmas Past shows him sitting alone at school."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. It names the method but gives no effect. Say what the simile suggests about Scrooge.", "Wrong. This is vague. It names no method and does not link to the quotation.", "Right. It names the simile, explains the closed-shell image and links it to Scrooge choosing isolation.", "Wrong. This retells the plot and leaves the quotation. Analyse the evidence you have just given."]}, "trap": "Retelling the plot instead of analysing. After every quotation, name Dickens's method and explain its effect on the reader. If a sentence only says what happens, rewrite it to say what Dickens shows.", "recap": "Make a point about Dickens's ideas, support it with a short quotation, analyse the method and effect, then link to context and purpose."}
{"s": "english_lit", "t": "lit-sh-mac", "kind": "writing", "why": "Section A of Paper 1 is a Macbeth question worth 34 of the 64 marks, and it asks you to write about an extract and the whole play.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What earns marks", "b": "Examiners reward three things. First, a clear **personal response** to the question, backed by quotations. Second, analysis of Shakespeare's **methods**: language, structure and form. Third, **context**, used only where it explains an idea. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are marked too."}, {"h": "Build each paragraph", "b": "Start with a **point** that answers the question. Add a short **quotation**. Then pick out a key word or method and explain its **effect** on the audience. Quotations of a few words work best, because you can analyse every word."}, {"h": "Use the whole play", "b": "The question covers an extract and the play as a whole. Begin with the extract, then link to a moment earlier or later. A **contrast** or a **change** between two moments shows that you understand the **structure** of the play."}, {"h": "Weave in context", "b": "Context is a short link, not a separate paragraph. Many in **Jacobean** England, including **King James I**, believed a king was chosen by God. So killing one was a terrible sin. Use this to explain how an audience would react."}], "example": {"task": "Starting with Act 5 Scene 1, where she sleepwalks, explore how Shakespeare presents Lady Macbeth's guilt. Write about this scene and the play as a whole.", "steps": [{"do": "Shakespeare presents Lady Macbeth's guilt as a punishment she cannot escape, as her early confidence collapses into torment.", "why": "A clear argument answers the question straight away."}, {"do": "In Act 2 she dismisses guilt: \"A little water clears us of this deed.\" The short, simple words suggest she believes murder can be washed away easily.", "why": "A short quotation from earlier in the play, with the effect of the language explained."}, {"do": "By Act 5 she sleepwalks, rubbing her hands and crying \"Out, damned spot!\" The imperative \"Out\" shows her trying to command away a stain that is only imagined, so the torment is in her mind.", "why": "The contrast with Act 2 shows change. A method is named and its effect explained."}, {"do": "A Jacobean audience, who saw killing a king as a sin against God, would see her breakdown as deserved punishment, not just sadness.", "why": "Context explains the audience's reaction and is tied to the argument."}], "answer": "Shakespeare shows that Lady Macbeth's belief that guilt is easily washed away is false. The \"little water\" of Act 2 becomes the stain she can never remove in Act 5."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer quote from Act 2 before the quote from Act 5?", "o": ["Quotations must always appear in the order they are printed in the text.", "It lets the answer show how her attitude changes across the play.", "Quotations from early in the play earn more marks than later ones.", "It makes the answer longer, and longer answers always score higher."], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Order is not a rule. Here the order works because it traces a change, and you could use a different order if your argument needed it.", "Right. The Act 2 confidence sets up a contrast with the Act 5 breakdown. That shows development across the whole play, which the question asks for.", "Wrong. The mark scheme does not reward quotations by position in the play. It rewards how well they support and develop your argument.", "Wrong. Length alone earns nothing. Marks go for the quality of ideas and analysis, not the number of words."]}, "faded": {"task": "Starting with Act 3 Scene 1, explore how Shakespeare presents Macbeth's fear of Banquo. Write about this scene and the play as a whole.", "given": ["Opening: Shakespeare presents Banquo as a threat that stops Macbeth enjoying being king.", "Point: In Act 3 Scene 1, Macbeth is king but feels unsafe because of Banquo."], "q": "Which sentence should come next?", "o": ["Next, Macbeth hires murderers to kill Banquo, but Fleance escapes.", "Shakespeare uses powerful language here, which makes the audience feel scared.", "He says \"To be thus is nothing\", showing his crown feels worthless without safety.", "He says \"To be thus is nothing\", which is a good quote about Banquo and fear."], "a": 2, "f": ["This retells the plot. It moves on without a quotation or any analysis of Shakespeare's methods, so it earns little.", "This is vague. It names no technique, gives no quotation and does not say which words create the effect, so there is nothing to analyse.", "Right. It adds a short quotation and explains it. The word \"nothing\" shows that being king feels worthless while Banquo makes Macbeth feel unsafe.", "This has a quotation but no analysis. Calling it \"good\" explains nothing. You need to say what the words show and how they do it."]}, "trap": "Retelling what happens (\"then she sleepwalks\") instead of analysing. Marks go for how Shakespeare presents ideas. After every quotation, pick out a key word or method and explain its effect on the audience.", "recap": "Make a clear point, support it with a short quotation, analyse a key word or method, and link to another moment or to context."}
{"s": "history", "t": "el-1", "kind": "idea", "why": "OCR questions ask how far Elizabeth controlled her government and country, so you need specific evidence of her power and its limits.", "hook": {"q": "Elizabeth had no police force and only a tiny permanent army. How was she most likely to get her orders carried out in the counties?", "o": ["By sending royal officials from London to run every county", "By relying on local landowners to act for her, without pay", "By letting each county choose its own ruler", "By asking Parliament to run local affairs for her"], "a": 1, "f": ["She did not have enough paid officials or money for this. Most local work was done by people who already lived in the counties.", "Right. The crown depended on local gentry to enforce her laws and keep order. This is the idea behind Justices of the Peace.", "Counties did not choose their own rulers. Authority came from the queen, and local leaders acted in her name.", "Parliament met rarely and was not a body that ran local affairs. Local government was done by gentry on her behalf."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Court and Privy Council", "b": "The **court** was the queen's household and the centre of power. Nobles competed there for **patronage**: offices, land and monopolies. The **Privy Council** was a small group of advisers she chose, such as William Cecil. It met often and carried out government business. Elizabeth made the final decisions."}, {"h": "Parliament and Puritans", "b": "Only Elizabeth could call or dismiss **Parliament**, and she could veto its laws. It met rarely, mainly to vote taxes. **Puritan** MPs pressed for further religious reform. She refused to let MPs debate religion or the succession, and jailed critics such as Peter Wentworth in 1593. She made concessions over monopolies in 1601."}, {"h": "Local rule and propaganda", "b": "She had no police force. **Justices of the Peace** (JPs), unpaid local gentry, enforced laws in the counties. **Propaganda** built loyalty. Portraits showed her as a strong Virgin Queen, and summer progresses let ordinary people see her. Her hosts paid much of the cost."}, {"h": "Essex's rebellion, 1601", "b": "The Earl of **Essex** lost favour after failing in Ireland, and his monopoly on sweet wines was not renewed. He blamed Robert **Cecil** and rose in London on 8 February 1601. Almost nobody joined him. He was executed on 25 February. It showed court rivalry was dangerous, but she still held control."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why Elizabeth was able to keep control of England in the 1590s.", "steps": [{"do": "Elizabeth chose her own Privy Council, including the Cecils, and rewarded loyal nobles with offices, land and monopolies. The Council advised her, but she made the final decisions.", "why": "Shows power at the centre came from her, and that favour kept nobles loyal."}, {"do": "Parliament could not seriously challenge her. Only she could call or dismiss it, and she could veto its laws. She jailed critics such as Peter Wentworth in 1593.", "why": "Shows how she contained challenges from MPs, a second source of her control."}, {"do": "In the counties, she relied on JPs. These unpaid gentry enforced her laws, because she had no police force. They wanted to keep her favour.", "why": "Explains how her orders reached the whole country cheaply."}, {"do": "Propaganda gave her support. Portraits showed a strong Virgin Queen, and progresses let people see her. Loyal subjects were less likely to rebel, and Essex found almost no support in 1601.", "why": "Links loyalty to a real event as evidence."}], "answer": "Elizabeth kept control because she held the key powers. She chose her councillors and gave out patronage. She decided when Parliament met. Gentry as JPs enforced her rule locally, and propaganda built loyalty, so Essex's rebellion in 1601 failed."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention that only she could call or dismiss Parliament and could veto laws?", "o": ["It shows Parliament had no part in government at all", "It shows Parliament met every year to check her power", "It shows she ruled mainly through laws made by the Commons", "It shows Parliament depended on her and could not overrule her"], "a": 3, "f": ["Too strong. Parliament did vote taxes and pass laws. The point is that it worked under her control, not that it did nothing.", "Parliament met rarely, and only when she chose to call it. It was not a regular check on her.", "Wrong emphasis. She governed through her Council and royal power. Parliament met rarely, and she could veto its laws.", "Right. Her control of when Parliament met, and of which laws passed, shows it could not overrule her. This supports the point about her power."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how Elizabeth's government kept control of the counties in the 1590s.", "given": ["Point 1: Elizabeth had no police force or large standing army, so she relied on local gentry to carry out her rule."], "q": "Which is the best next point?", "o": ["Point 2: Parliament chose the JPs and ran each county for her.", "Point 2: JPs were elected by local people and paid wages by the crown.", "Point 2: JPs came from the local gentry, were unpaid and enforced her laws in their county.", "Point 2: The Council sent soldiers from London to garrison every county town."], "a": 2, "f": ["This is the trap. Parliament did not choose JPs or run the counties. The crown appointed them, and the Privy Council supervised them.", "JPs were appointed by the crown, not elected. They were not paid wages. They served for the status and to keep the queen's favour.", "Right. This follows from Point 1 and names who did the work. JPs tried minor crimes and enforced laws, and they served without pay.", "She had no large paid army to station in towns. The point is that the government relied on local gentry rather than on soldiers."]}, "trap": "Saying Parliament chose the Privy Council or ran the country. Elizabeth chose her councillors, decided when Parliament met and made the final decisions. Avoid this by naming who held each power.", "recap": "Elizabeth held final power: she chose her Council, controlled Parliament, and relied on nobles and gentry to carry out her rule locally."}
{"s": "history", "t": "ph-1", "kind": "writing", "why": "The People's Health paper often asks you to explain why people acted as they did, which needs their beliefs plus specific evidence.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Living conditions", "b": "Medieval towns were **crowded**, with narrow streets. People dumped rubbish, human waste and animal guts in the streets and rivers. Rivers such as the Thames and Fleet became **polluted**. Nobody knew about **germs**, so few saw the danger."}, {"h": "Beliefs about causes", "b": "People explained disease in three main ways. **Miasma**: bad air from rotting waste. The **four humours**: an imbalance of body fluids. **God's punishment**: sin made God angry. Each belief led to different actions, so reactions to the Black Death varied."}, {"h": "Public health efforts", "b": "Towns ordered streets cleaned, built **conduits** to bring in spring water, and in 1388 Parliament banned dumping waste in ditches and rivers. These laws were often poorly enforced. **Monasteries** often did better, with piped water and latrines built over streams."}, {"h": "Building an explanation", "b": "Each paragraph needs a **point** that answers the 'why'. Back it with **evidence**: a named belief, group or action. Finish with a **link** back to the question. Name the belief, then show the action it led to."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why people reacted to the Black Death of 1348–49 in so many different ways.", "steps": [{"do": "People reacted in different ways because nobody knew the real cause of the plague, so they blamed different things.", "why": "It answers 'why' straight away and gives your whole answer a line of argument."}, {"do": "Many believed God sent the plague to punish sin. So flagellants whipped themselves in public to win God's forgiveness, and others prayed.", "why": "A named group and belief explain the action. 'To win God's forgiveness' shows it was not a cure."}, {"do": "Others believed in miasma, bad air from rotting waste. So towns ordered streets cleaned and people carried sweet-smelling herbs to cover bad smells.", "why": "It names the belief, then shows the actions that followed from it."}, {"do": "Doctors followed the four humours, so they used bloodletting to remove extra blood and restore balance.", "why": "A third belief gives a third kind of reaction, so the answer covers a range."}, {"do": "All of these made sense at the time. Fleas carried by rats spread the plague and nobody knew it, so different beliefs led to different actions.", "why": "The link ties the evidence back to the question and gives the underlying reason."}], "answer": "A full answer gives an overall reason, then three beliefs (God's punishment, miasma, humours), each with the action it caused, and ends by linking to ignorance of the true cause."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 say flagellants whipped themselves 'to win God's forgiveness'?", "o": ["Whipping was a medical treatment meant to drain the plague from the body", "Flagellants were doctors who whipped patients to remove bad air", "It shows their action came from the belief that plague was God's punishment", "Whipping was ordered by town councils as a way to clean the streets"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Whipping was not a medical treatment. That mixes flagellants up with bloodletting and the humours.", "Wrong. Flagellants whipped themselves, not patients, and they were not doctors. Bad air belongs to miasma.", "Right. The purpose, forgiveness, links the action to the belief in God's anger. That explains the reaction and earns the mark.", "Wrong. Flagellants acted on their own for religious reasons. Street cleaning was a separate response based on miasma."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why monasteries were often cleaner than medieval towns.", "given": ["Point: Monasteries had the money and organisation to plan their water and waste."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to give specific evidence for this point?", "o": ["Monks cleaned because they knew germs caused disease, so they boiled all their water.", "Many had piped fresh water and latrines built over a stream that carried waste away.", "Monasteries were very old, and many monks lived there for their whole lives.", "Monks never caught the plague because God protected them for living holy lives."], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Germs were not understood until much later. Monks cleaned for other reasons, such as order and comfort.", "Right. It gives specific evidence of water and waste systems, which supports the point and explains the cleaner conditions.", "Wrong. This does not explain cleanliness. It is detail with no link to the question.", "Wrong. This is inaccurate, since monks died of plague too. It also drifts from cleanliness to belief."]}, "trap": "Writing that flagellants tried to cure the sick, or that medieval people did nothing about health. Flagellants did penance for sin. Towns and monasteries did act, but without knowing the real causes.", "recap": "Explain reactions by naming the belief, such as miasma, humours or God's punishment, then the action it caused, because nobody knew about fleas or germs."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A4", "kind": "method", "why": "Expanding and factorising appear on all three papers and are needed for solving quadratics, and surd simplifying appears in non-calculator questions.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Expanding two brackets", "b": "Multiply **every term** in the first bracket by every term in the second. $(x+a)(x+b)$ gives four terms. Then collect the **like terms**, usually the two $x$ terms in the middle. Take care with signs: $3\\times(-5)=-15$."}, {"h": "Factorising $x^2+bx+c$", "b": "Find two numbers that **multiply** to give $c$ and **add** to give $b$. Call them $p$ and $q$. Then $x^2+bx+c=(x+p)(x+q)$. List the factor pairs of $c$ and test each pair's sum."}, {"h": "Which signs to use", "b": "If $c$ is **positive**, both numbers have the same sign as $b$. If $c$ is **negative**, one number is positive and one is negative. The sum is then the difference of the two, and the larger number takes the sign of $b$."}, {"h": "Surds and checking", "b": "For **surds**, $\\sqrt{a}\\times\\sqrt{a}=a$, and you simplify by taking out a **square number**: $\\sqrt{12}=\\sqrt{4}\\times\\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}$. Always check a factorisation by expanding it. You should get the original expression back."}], "example": {"task": "Factorise $x^2+2x-24$.", "steps": [{"do": "Look at $c=-24$. It is negative, so the two numbers have opposite signs.", "why": "A negative product needs one positive and one negative number."}, {"do": "List the factor pairs of 24: $1\\times24$, $2\\times12$, $3\\times8$, $4\\times6$.", "why": "The two numbers must multiply to 24 in size, so they come from this list."}, {"do": "The numbers have opposite signs, so the sum is their difference. Look for a difference of 2. That is 4 and 6.", "why": "We need them to add to $b=+2$. Only the pair 4 and 6 differs by 2."}, {"do": "The larger number, 6, takes the sign of $b$, which is $+$. So the numbers are $+6$ and $-4$.", "why": "Check: $6\\times(-4)=-24$ and $6+(-4)=2$. Both conditions hold."}, {"do": "Write the brackets: $(x+6)(x-4)$.", "why": "The numbers $p=6$ and $q=-4$ go straight into $(x+p)(x+q)$."}, {"do": "Check by expanding: $x^2-4x+6x-24=x^2+2x-24$.", "why": "Getting the original expression back shows the factorisation is correct."}], "answer": "$x^2+2x-24=(x+6)(x-4)$"}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is 6 made positive and 4 made negative in the worked example?", "o": ["The bigger number always goes in the first bracket, so it must be positive", "The numbers must add to $+2$, so the larger one has to be positive", "The product is $-24$, so both numbers must be negative", "The factor 6 is even, so it takes the plus sign"], "a": 1, "f": ["Order of brackets does not decide signs. The sum of $+2$ does, so the larger number must be positive.", "Right. A sum of $+2$ means the larger number wins the sign. So $+6$ and $-4$ add to $+2$.", "Two negatives would multiply to a positive, not $-24$. One number must be positive and one negative.", "Being even has no effect on a sign. The sign comes from the product and the sum."]}, "faded": {"task": "Factorise $x^2-2x-15$.", "given": ["$c=-15$ is negative, so the numbers have opposite signs.", "Factor pairs of 15: $1\\times15$ and $3\\times5$. The pair with difference 2 is 3 and 5."], "q": "Which numbers should go in the brackets next?", "o": ["$-3$ and $+5$", "$+3$ and $+5$", "$+3$ and $-5$", "$-3$ and $-5$"], "a": 2, "f": ["These multiply to $-15$ but add to $+2$. We need $b=-2$, so the larger number, 5, must be negative.", "These multiply to $+15$, not $-15$. Opposite signs are needed because $c$ is negative.", "Right. $3\\times(-5)=-15$ and $3+(-5)=-2$. So $x^2-2x-15=(x+3)(x-5)$.", "These multiply to $+15$ and add to $-8$. Two negatives cannot give a product of $-15$."]}, "trap": "Picking numbers with the right product but the wrong sum, such as $+5$ instead of $-5$. Test both the product and the sum, then expand your brackets to check.", "recap": "To factorise $x^2+bx+c$, find two numbers that multiply to $c$ and add to $b$, then check by expanding."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "R9", "kind": "method", "why": "Percentage change questions can appear on any of your three Edexcel papers, and reverse percentages are a common place to lose marks.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Percentage change", "b": "To find a **percentage change**, work out the change first. Then divide by the **original** value and multiply by 100.\n\n$\\text{percentage change}=\\dfrac{\\text{change}}{\\text{original}}\\times100$\n\nThe original is the value before the change. A rise from £50 to £62 is £12, so $12\\div50\\times100=24\\%$."}, {"h": "Multipliers and over 100%", "b": "A **multiplier** does a whole percentage change in one step.\n\nIncrease by 15%: $100\\%+15\\%=115\\%$, so the multiplier is $1.15$.\nDecrease by 15%: $100\\%-15\\%=85\\%$, so the multiplier is $0.85$.\n\nA **percentage over 100%** just means bigger than the original. 130% of a number is $1.3$ times it."}, {"h": "Finding the original value", "b": "New value $=$ original $\\times$ multiplier. To go back, **divide by the multiplier**.\n\nDo not find a percentage of the new value and add or subtract it. The percentage was taken of the **original**, which you do not know yet."}, {"h": "Simple interest", "b": "**Simple interest** is the same amount every year. It is worked out from the **original sum** only.\n\nInterest per year $=$ original sum $\\times$ rate $\\div100$. Multiply by the number of years.\n\nExample: £400 at 3% for 5 years. $400\\times0.03=12$, so £12 a year. The interest is $12\\times5=60$, so £60."}], "example": {"task": "In a sale, all bikes are reduced by 35%. One bike has a sale price of £260. Work out the original price of the bike.", "steps": [{"do": "The original price is 100%. After a 35% reduction, the sale price is $100\\%-35\\%=65\\%$ of the original.", "why": "This tells you what percentage of the original the known £260 is."}, {"do": "Write 65% as the multiplier $0.65$. So original $\\times\\,0.65=260$.", "why": "A multiplier turns the original price into the sale price."}, {"do": "Divide by the multiplier: original $=260\\div0.65$.", "why": "Dividing undoes multiplying by 0.65."}, {"do": "Calculate: $260\\div0.65=26000\\div65=400$.", "why": "Multiplying both numbers by 100 removes the decimal and makes it easier without a calculator."}, {"do": "Check: 35% of 400 is 140, and $400-140=260$.", "why": "Going forward from the original should give the sale price you were told."}], "answer": "The original price was £400."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 divide £260 by 0.65 instead of taking 35% off £260?", "o": ["Dividing by a multiplier is always the way to answer any percentage question", "The 35% should be taken off £260 first, and then 0.65 is used to finish", "£260 is 65% of the original, so dividing by 0.65 undoes the reduction", "Dividing by 0.65 is the same as adding 35% of £260 to £260"], "a": 2, "f": ["Not always. You multiply by the multiplier to find a new value from a known original. You divide only when the new value is known and the original is not.", "There is no first step of taking 35% off £260. The 35% belongs to the unknown original, so the multiplier does the whole job alone.", "Right. The sale price is original × 0.65. Dividing the sale price by 0.65 reverses this and gives the original.", "This is a common slip. Adding 35% of £260 gives £351, not £400. A percentage of the new value is not the same as the same percentage of the original."]}, "faded": {"task": "A monthly phone plan rises by 12% to £56. Work out the price before the rise.", "given": ["An increase of 12% means the new price is 100% + 12% = 112% of the original.", "The multiplier is 1.12, so original × 1.12 = 56."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Work out $56\\times1.12$", "Work out $56\\div1.12$", "Work out 12% of 56 and subtract it", "Work out $56\\div0.12$"], "a": 1, "f": ["This multiplies the new price by 1.12, which would add 12% again. You need to undo the increase, so you divide.", "Correct. Dividing by the multiplier undoes the increase: $56\\div1.12=50$. The original price was £50.", "This takes 12% of the new price. The 12% was of the original, so this gives £49.28, which is wrong.", "0.12 is only the 12% on its own. The multiplier for a 12% increase is 1.12, because the original 100% is included."]}, "trap": "Taking the percentage off the new value, for example finding 12% of £56 and subtracting it. The percentage was of the original. Write the multiplier, then divide the new value by it.", "recap": "To find the original value, divide the new value by the multiplier. Never take a percentage of the new value."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.1.1", "kind": "method", "why": "Energy questions appear on Paper 1, and kinetic energy and power are often combined in one multi-mark calculation.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Energy stores", "b": "Energy is held in **stores**. The stores are kinetic, gravitational potential, elastic potential, thermal, chemical, nuclear, magnetic and electrostatic. A moving object has energy in its **kinetic** store. A raised object has energy in its **gravitational potential** store. A hot object has energy in its thermal store."}, {"h": "Energy transfers", "b": "Energy moves between stores by heating, by waves, by an electric current, or by a force doing **work**. In a closed system the total energy is **conserved**. It is never created or destroyed. Energy not usefully transferred mostly ends up in the thermal store of the surroundings. This is **wasted** energy."}, {"h": "Two store equations", "b": "**Kinetic energy**: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$. **Gravitational potential energy**: $E_p = mgh$. Use mass in kg, speed in m/s and height in m. Here g = 9.8 N/kg. Energy is in **joules** (J). Check every unit before you substitute."}, {"h": "Power", "b": "**Power** is how fast energy is transferred: $P = \\frac{E}{t}$. Use E in joules and t in **seconds**. The unit is the **watt** (W). 1 W means 1 joule transferred every second. A 60 W bulb transfers 60 J each second."}], "example": {"task": "A sprinter of mass 60 kg accelerates from rest to 8.0 m/s in 4.0 s. Assume all the energy transferred goes to her kinetic store. Calculate the power of the transfer.", "steps": [{"do": "List what you know: m = 60 kg, v = 8.0 m/s, t = 4.0 s. The store gaining energy is kinetic.", "why": "This shows which equation to use. The units are already kg, m/s and s."}, {"do": "Write the equation: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$.", "why": "Kinetic energy depends on mass and speed."}, {"do": "Square the speed first: $8.0^2 = 64$.", "why": "Only v is squared, so deal with it before anything else."}, {"do": "Multiply by the mass, then halve: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 60 \\times 64 = 1920$ J.", "why": "She starts at rest, so all of this is the energy transferred."}, {"do": "Write $P = \\frac{E}{t}$ and substitute: $P = \\frac{1920}{4.0}$.", "why": "Power is energy transferred per second. The time is already in seconds."}, {"do": "Calculate: $P = 480$ W.", "why": "Joules per second are watts, so 480 W means 480 J each second."}], "answer": "Energy transferred = 1920 J. Power = 480 W."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer square 8.0 before multiplying by 60?", "o": ["Because the whole of ½ × 60 × 8.0 must be squared at the end", "Because squaring turns metres per second into joules", "Because in the equation only the speed is squared, not the ½ or the mass", "Because the power equation also needs the speed squared"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Squaring the whole product gives (240)² = 57 600, which is far too big. The square belongs to v only.", "Wrong. Squaring does not change units. The joules come from kg × (m/s)², all together.", "Right. In ½mv², the power 2 applies only to v. Square v first, then multiply by m and halve.", "Wrong. P = E/t has no speed in it. The square belongs to the kinetic energy equation."]}, "faded": {"task": "A 4.0 kg trolley is pushed from rest to 3.0 m/s in 2.5 s. All the energy goes to its kinetic store. Calculate the power.", "given": ["Kinetic energy: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$", "Substitute: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4.0 \\times 3.0^2$"], "q": "What is the correct next step?", "o": ["$E_k = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4.0 \\times 6.0 = 12$ J", "$3.0^2 = 9.0$, so $E_k = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4.0 \\times 9.0 = 18$ J", "$E_k = (\\frac{1}{2} \\times 4.0 \\times 3.0)^2 = 36$ J", "$E_k = 4.0 \\times 9.0 = 36$ J"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. This doubles the speed (3.0 × 2) instead of squaring it. Squaring means 3.0 × 3.0 = 9.0.", "Right. Square the speed first, then multiply by the mass and halve. Power is then 18 ÷ 2.5 = 7.2 W.", "Wrong. This squares after multiplying by ½ and m. Only the speed should be squared.", "Wrong. Squaring is correct here, but the ½ has been left out. Kinetic energy needs the half."]}, "trap": "Squaring the wrong thing, or not at all. Write the square of v as its own line first. Also keep time in seconds: convert minutes before using P = E/t.", "recap": "Square v, multiply by m, halve to get kinetic energy in joules; divide by the time in seconds to get power in watts."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.2.1", "kind": "method", "why": "Calculations with $V = IR$ come up in Paper 1, often for 2 or 3 marks, and you also need to know how resistors, lamps and diodes behave.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Setting up the circuit", "b": "Connect the **ammeter** in **series** with the component, so the same current flows through it. Connect the **voltmeter** in parallel, directly across the component only. Read both meters, then use them to find the resistance."}, {"h": "Using V = IR", "b": "The equation is $V = IR$, so $R = \\frac{V}{I}$ and $I = \\frac{V}{R}$. Use **volts** (V), amps (A) and **ohms** (Ω). Convert **milliamps** to amps by dividing by 1000. For resistance, V always goes on top."}, {"h": "Resistor, lamp and diode", "b": "Watch the graph draw each line. A fixed **resistor** at constant temperature gives a straight line through the origin; its resistance stays the same. In a **filament lamp** the wire heats up as current rises, so resistance increases and the graph curves. A **diode** lets current flow in one direction only; its resistance is very high the other way.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "draw", "pen": true, "read": ["V (V)", "I (A)"], "dp": 2, "ms": 7000, "x": [-4, 4], "y": [-0.6, 0.6], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 0.2, "xlabel": "potential difference / V", "ylabel": "current / A", "lines": [{"label": "Resistor", "c": 0, "step": 1, "pts": [[-4, -0.4], [4, 0.4]]}, {"label": "Lamp", "c": 1, "step": 2, "pts": [[-4, -0.2], [-3, -0.18], [-2, -0.14], [-1, -0.08], [0, 0], [1, 0.08], [2, 0.14], [3, 0.18], [4, 0.2]]}, {"label": "Diode", "c": 2, "step": 3, "pts": [[-4, 0], [0.6, 0], [0.8, 0.15], [1, 0.6]]}], "cap": "Resistor: straight line. Lamp: curve flattens as it heats. Diode: almost no current in reverse, then a sharp rise."}}], "example": {"task": "A student sets up a circuit to test a fixed resistor. The voltmeter reads 6.0 V and the ammeter reads 25 mA. Calculate the resistance of the resistor.", "steps": [{"do": "Write down what you know: V = 6.0 V across the resistor, I = 25 mA through it.", "why": "The voltmeter gives the potential difference. The ammeter gives the current."}, {"do": "Convert the current to amps: 25 mA = 25 ÷ 1000 = 0.025 A.", "why": "The equation needs amps to give an answer in ohms."}, {"do": "Write the equation: $R = \\frac{V}{I}$.", "why": "Resistance is wanted, so V goes on top and I underneath."}, {"do": "Substitute and calculate: $R = \\frac{6.0}{0.025} = 240$.", "why": "Both values are now in the correct units."}, {"do": "State the unit: R = 240 Ω.", "why": "Resistance is measured in ohms. A missing unit can lose a mark."}], "answer": "R = 240 Ω"}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 change 25 mA into 0.025 A?", "o": ["The voltmeter reading is in volts, so the current must be changed to volts too.", "The equation R = V ÷ I only gives ohms when the current is in amps.", "An ammeter can only ever show amps, so milliamps must be converted.", "Dividing by 1000 makes the answer smaller, which is always needed."], "a": 1, "f": ["Volts and amps are different quantities. A current is never converted into volts. The point is to use the unit the equation is built on.", "Right. V in volts and I in amps give R in ohms. Using 25 instead of 0.025 would give an answer 1000 times too small.", "Ammeters can show milliamps. The conversion is needed because the equation is defined with current in amps, not because of the meter.", "The conversion is not about the size of the answer. Skipping it gives a wrong answer, and it would not always make the answer smaller."]}, "faded": {"task": "A 470 Ω resistor has a current of 12 mA through it. Calculate the potential difference across it.", "given": ["Current: I = 12 mA = 0.012 A (divide by 1000)", "Equation: V = IR (V is wanted, so multiply I by R)"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["V = 470 ÷ 0.012 = about 39 000 V", "V = 12 × 470 = 5640 V", "V = 0.012 × 470 = 5.64 V", "V = 0.012 ÷ 470 = 0.000 026 V"], "a": 2, "f": ["This divides R by I, which is the wrong rearrangement. V = IR means multiply, so the answer would be far too large.", "This uses 12 instead of 0.012. The mA conversion was ignored, so the answer is 1000 times too big.", "Right. Multiply the current in amps by the resistance: 0.012 × 470 = 5.64 V.", "This divides I by R. The equation V = IR multiplies them, so the answer would be tiny and wrong."]}, "trap": "Leaving the current in mA, or putting I on top when finding R. Convert mA to A first, then use $R = \\frac{V}{I}$ with V on top.", "recap": "Convert current to amps, then use $V = IR$ or $R = \\frac{V}{I}$, and give resistance in ohms (Ω)."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.1.2.1a", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 1 questions on Christian beliefs often ask you to explain a belief or to show that Christians hold different views.", "hook": {"q": "A person loves a child and has the power to stop the child being hurt. The child is hurt and the person does nothing. What would most people conclude?", "o": ["They have done nothing wrong, because loving people never step in", "Either they could not stop it, or they did not care enough to", "The child must be imagining it, as a loving person would act", "Love and power are unrelated, so nothing can be said about it"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Most people expect someone who loves a child and can help to step in. This assumes the opposite, and it misses the tension.", "Right. Love and power are both needed to stop the harm. If it happens anyway, one seems missing. This is the shape of the problem of evil.", "Wrong. This denies that the harm happened. The problem of evil starts from the fact that suffering is real.", "Wrong. Love and power are exactly what decide whether someone would help. The problem of evil depends on linking the two."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The problem of evil", "b": "Christians believe God is **omnipotent** (all-powerful) and **omnibenevolent** (all-loving). The **problem of evil** asks why such a God allows suffering. Many Christians reply that humans have free will, which explains moral evil, such as cruelty. Natural evil, such as earthquakes, is harder to explain."}, {"h": "The Trinity", "b": "Christians believe in **one God** who exists as three **persons**: Father, Son (Jesus) and Holy Spirit. The Father is linked with creating, the Son with saving, and the Spirit with God's power and presence today. The three are equal. They are not three separate gods."}, {"h": "Creation", "b": "**Genesis** 1 teaches that God created the universe over six days and called it good. Some Christians read this literally. Others read it as a symbolic story about God's purpose. John 1 says the **Word** (Jesus) was there at creation, and Genesis 1:2 mentions God's Spirit."}, {"h": "Life after death", "b": "Christians believe death is not the end, because Jesus rose from the dead. Many believe in **judgement**, then **heaven** or hell. Many Catholics believe in **purgatory**, where souls are made ready for heaven. Most Protestants reject this idea. Christians also differ on what hell is."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the problem of evil challenges some Christians' belief in God.", "steps": [{"do": "State the Christian belief about God: God is all-powerful and all-loving.", "why": "The problem only exists because of these two beliefs, so the answer must start there."}, {"do": "Link each quality to suffering: an all-loving God would want to stop it, and an all-powerful God would be able to.", "why": "This shows why these qualities seem to clash with suffering."}, {"do": "Give the evidence: suffering exists, for example an earthquake that kills thousands.", "why": "A concrete example shows the clash is real, not just theory."}, {"do": "Draw the conclusion: so God seems either not all-powerful or not all-loving, which challenges belief.", "why": "This answers 'why it challenges' directly and completes the chain of reasoning."}, {"do": "Add a Christian response: some reply that free will explains moral evil.", "why": "It shows awareness that Christians hold different views."}], "answer": "Christians believe God is all-powerful and all-loving. An all-loving God would want to stop suffering, and an all-powerful God could. Yet suffering exists, such as earthquakes that kill thousands. So either God is not all-powerful, or not all-loving, which challenges belief. Some Christians reply that free will explains moral evil."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention both 'all-powerful' and 'all-loving' together?", "o": ["Because Christians believe these are two separate gods", "Because every answer needs two key terms to earn marks", "Because the problem only arises if God has both qualities", "Because all-loving means God causes the suffering"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Christians believe in one God. Treating qualities as separate gods is the same slip as thinking the Trinity means three gods.", "Wrong. Marks come from reasoning, not from counting terms. The terms are used because the argument depends on them.", "Right. If God lacked power or love, suffering would be less puzzling. The problem comes from God having both qualities.", "Wrong. All-loving means God cares for people. The problem is that a loving God seems to allow suffering, not cause it."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why many Christians believe in life after death.", "given": ["Point: Many Christians believe death is not the end and that God will judge people.", "Reason: They believe Jesus rose from the dead after the crucifixion."], "q": "Which is the best next step to develop the answer?", "o": ["Link: Christians believe in reincarnation, so people return to Earth in new bodies.", "Link: Protestants believe in purgatory, so everyone gets a second chance after death.", "Link: The Trinity means three gods, so there are three separate afterlives.", "Link: Christians hope to share in Jesus' new life, so they look forward to heaven."], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Reincarnation belongs to other religions. Christians believe in judgement and heaven or hell, not return to Earth.", "Wrong. Most Protestants reject purgatory, and it is not a second chance for everyone. It is a Catholic idea about preparing for heaven.", "Wrong. The Trinity is one God in three persons, not three gods, and it does not mean three afterlives.", "Right. It links the resurrection back to the question: Christians hope to share in Jesus' new life, so it gives them hope of heaven."]}, "trap": "Saying the Trinity means three gods. Christians believe in one God who exists as three persons. Also do not mix up moral evil (human choices) with natural evil (earthquakes): free will mainly explains the first.", "recap": "Christians believe in one God in three persons, all-loving and all-powerful, which makes suffering a problem; they also believe in judgement after death."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.1.5.1a", "kind": "writing", "why": "In 4-mark 'explain two' questions on Muslim beliefs, each point must be developed, not just stated, to earn both of its marks.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "How the 4 marks work", "b": "'Explain two ways…' gives 2 marks for each way. You earn one mark for a clear **point**. You earn the second mark when you **develop** it. To develop, say how or why, using words like 'so' or 'which means'."}, {"h": "Choose beliefs you know", "b": "Sunni Muslims hold six articles of faith, including **Tawhid**, angels (malaikah), al-Qadr and akhirah (life after death). Shi'a Muslims hold five roots: Tawhid, **Adalat** (justice), Nubuwwah (prophethood), **Imamah** (leadership by Imams) and Mi'ad (life after death). Pick the belief the question names."}, {"h": "Keep your two points apart", "b": "Your two points must be **different**. Write 'Muslims believe…' so you state beliefs as beliefs held by followers. Use the right terms, such as Allah, Jannah and shirk. Then show the **effect** on how Muslims live or think."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two ways in which belief in life after death (akhirah) may affect the life of a Muslim today. [4 marks]", "steps": [{"do": "Belief in akhirah encourages Muslims to live by Allah's rules.", "why": "This is a clear first point. It names one effect of the belief."}, {"do": "They believe angels record their deeds and Allah will judge them, so they pray, give zakat and are honest, hoping for Jannah.", "why": "This develops the point. It explains why, using 'so', and gives real examples of actions."}, {"do": "It can also give comfort.", "why": "This is a second, different point. It is not a repeat of the first."}, {"do": "Muslims believe death is not the end, so a grieving family can trust that a good person will be rewarded and suffering is temporary.", "why": "This develops the second point. It shows how the belief affects feelings and hope."}], "answer": "Belief in akhirah encourages Muslims to live by Allah's rules. They believe angels record their deeds and Allah will judge them, so they pray, give zakat and are honest, hoping for Jannah. It can also give comfort. Muslims believe death is not the end, so a grieving family can trust that a good person will be rewarded."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer add 'so they pray, give zakat and are honest' after the angels sentence?", "o": ["It repeats the point so that the answer looks longer", "It lists Pillars of Islam, which earn marks whatever the question asks", "It develops the point by showing how the belief changes what Muslims do", "It proves the belief is true, which the question requires"], "a": 2, "f": ["Repeating earns nothing extra. This part adds new information: the actions Muslims take because of the belief.", "Zakat is a Pillar, but it only earns marks here because it is linked to the belief. Unlinked lists do not gain marks.", "Right. The 'so' links the belief to its effect on behaviour. That link is what turns a point into a developed point.", "The question asks for effects on Muslims, not proof. RS answers state beliefs as beliefs held by followers; they do not prove them."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Tawhid is important to Muslims. [4 marks]", "given": ["Point 1: Tawhid is the most basic Muslim belief: Muslims believe Allah is one, with no partners.", "Development 1: The Shahadah, said to become a Muslim, begins 'There is no god but Allah'."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to start a different second reason?", "o": ["Tawhid is important because it is the foundation of Islam, as the Shahadah shows.", "Tawhid is also important because Muslims must worship Allah alone and avoid shirk.", "Tawhid is important because Muslims believe Allah is one and has no partners.", "Tawhid is also important because it teaches that Allah sends angels such as Jibril."], "a": 1, "f": ["This repeats reason 1. Being the foundation and the Shahadah were already used, so it earns no new marks.", "Right. This is a new reason: it affects worship. 'Avoid shirk' leaves room to develop it, for example by saying Muslims pray only to Allah.", "This only restates what Tawhid means. It gives no reason why it matters, and it duplicates the first point.", "Angels are a separate belief, not part of Tawhid. It does not answer why Tawhid is important."]}, "trap": "Writing two points that are really the same, or stating a belief with no development. After each point, add 'so' or 'which means' and say what Muslims do, think or feel because of it.", "recap": "For each 'explain two' answer, give a clear Muslim belief, then develop it with 'so' to show its effect on Muslims."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.1.1", "kind": "method", "why": "Magnification calculations are a regular Paper 1 question, and they often need a unit conversion between mm and µm.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "The magnification formula", "b": "**Magnification** tells you how many times bigger the image is than the real object. Magnification = **image size** ÷ **real size**. Rearranged, real size = image size ÷ magnification. Image size is what you measure on the drawing or photo."}, {"h": "Match the units", "b": "Image size and real size must be in the **same unit**. **1 mm = 1000 µm**. To go from mm to µm, multiply by 1000. To go from µm to mm, divide by 1000. Do the conversion at the start or the end, but never skip it."}, {"h": "Total magnification", "b": "With a light microscope, **total magnification** = **eyepiece lens** × **objective lens**. A ×10 eyepiece and a ×40 objective give ×400. Use the total in the formula, not one lens on its own."}, {"h": "Check your answer", "b": "Measure the drawing with a **ruler**, in mm, in a straight line across the cell. The magnification is above 1, so the real size must be **smaller** than the image size. A real size bigger than the image means you multiplied instead of dividing."}], "example": {"task": "A student draws a plant cell seen with a light microscope. The drawing is 36 mm long. The magnification is ×400. Calculate the real length of the cell in µm.", "steps": [{"do": "Write real size = image size ÷ magnification.", "why": "You want the real size, so you rearrange the formula to make it the subject."}, {"do": "Substitute: real size = 36 mm ÷ 400 = 0.09 mm.", "why": "The image size is 36 mm and the magnification is 400. The answer is still in mm."}, {"do": "Convert to µm: 0.09 × 1000 = 90 µm.", "why": "The question asks for µm. 1 mm = 1000 µm, so you multiply by 1000."}, {"do": "Check: 90 µm is much smaller than the 36 mm drawing.", "why": "Real size should be smaller than image size. This confirms you divided rather than multiplied."}], "answer": "The real length of the cell is 90 µm."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 multiply 0.09 by 1000?", "o": ["Multiplying by 1000 cancels out the ×400 magnification", "1 µm = 1000 mm, so you multiply to change mm into µm", "A µm is 1000 times smaller than a mm, so you need more of them", "Real sizes in cell biology must always be multiplied by 1000"], "a": 2, "f": ["The magnification was already dealt with in step 2 by dividing. Step 3 is only a change of unit.", "This reverses the units. It is 1 mm = 1000 µm. A mm is the bigger unit.", "Right. A µm is a smaller unit, so the same length needs a bigger number when written in µm.", "There is no fixed multiplier for real sizes. You multiply by 1000 here only because you are converting mm to µm."]}, "faded": {"task": "A drawing of an animal cell is 8 mm across. The microscope has a ×10 eyepiece and a ×40 objective. Find the real width of the cell in µm.", "given": ["Total magnification = 10 × 40 = ×400", "Real size = image size ÷ magnification"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["8 × 400 = 3200 µm", "8 ÷ 40 = 0.2 mm", "8 ÷ 400 = 0.02 µm", "8 ÷ 400 = 0.02 mm, then convert to µm"], "a": 3, "f": ["This multiplies by the magnification. That gives the image size from a real size. Here you must divide to find the real size.", "This uses only the objective lens. You already found the total magnification, ×400, and must use that.", "The division is right, but 8 mm ÷ 400 gives 0.02 mm, not µm. You still need to convert.", "Right. 8 mm ÷ 400 = 0.02 mm. Then × 1000 gives 20 µm."]}, "trap": "Multiplying by the magnification instead of dividing, or leaving the answer in mm when µm is asked for. Check that the real size is smaller than the image, then check the unit asked for.", "recap": "Real size = image size ÷ magnification, with both in the same unit, and 1 mm = 1000 µm."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.1.1.6", "kind": "method", "why": "Required practical 2 is in Paper 1, where you may describe safe technique or calculate and compare clear zone areas from data.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Growing bacteria safely", "b": "Unwanted microorganisms must not get in. **Sterilise** the Petri dish and agar jelly first. Pass the **inoculating loop** through a flame before it transfers bacteria. Tape the lid on, so air microbes stay out and culture microbes stay in. In school, incubate at about **25°C**, which makes harmful pathogens less likely to grow."}, {"h": "Testing antibiotics", "b": "Spread bacteria over the agar to make a **lawn**. Place paper discs soaked in different antibiotics on it. Add a **control** disc with no antibiotic. After incubation, a **clear zone** appears around a disc where bacteria could not grow. A bigger clear zone means a more effective antibiotic."}, {"h": "Making it a fair test", "b": "Only the type of antibiotic should change. Keep the rest the same: the **concentration** of each antibiotic, disc size, type of bacteria, temperature and incubation time. Otherwise you cannot tell what caused a difference between the **clear zones**."}, {"h": "Calculating clear zone area", "b": "The clear zone is a circle. You measure its **diameter**, straight across the middle. **Radius** = diameter ÷ 2. Area = $\\pi r^2$, in mm². Compare **areas**, not diameters. Doubling the diameter makes the area four times bigger."}], "example": {"task": "Discs soaked in antiseptics X and Y are placed on a bacterial lawn. The clear zones are 10 mm (X) and 16 mm (Y) across. Which is more effective, and how many times larger is its clear zone area?", "steps": [{"do": "Halve each diameter. X: 10 ÷ 2 = 5 mm. Y: 16 ÷ 2 = 8 mm.", "why": "The formula $\\pi r^2$ needs the radius, not the diameter."}, {"do": "Area of X = $\\pi \\times 5^2 = 25\\pi = 78.5$ mm² (3 s.f.).", "why": "Square the radius first, then multiply by $\\pi$."}, {"do": "Area of Y = $\\pi \\times 8^2 = 64\\pi = 201$ mm² (3 s.f.).", "why": "Same method with Y's radius. The unit is mm² because it is an area."}, {"do": "Divide: 201 ÷ 78.5 = 2.56. Or use the ratio of the squared radii, 64 ÷ 25 = 2.56.", "why": "Dividing the larger area by the smaller gives 'how many times larger'."}, {"do": "State the conclusion: Y is more effective.", "why": "A bigger clear zone means more bacteria were stopped from growing."}], "answer": "Y is more effective. Its clear zone area (201 mm²) is about 2.56 times larger than X's (78.5 mm²), so Y stopped more bacteria growing."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked example halve each diameter in the first step?", "o": ["Only half of the clear zone contains dead bacteria, so only half is measured", "$\\pi r^2$ uses the radius, which is half the diameter, measured from the disc's centre", "The paper disc takes up half of the width, so it must be removed", "Areas in mm² are always found by halving the length in mm"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. The whole circle is a clear zone. Halving finds the radius. It does not remove part of the zone.", "Right. The radius runs from the centre to the edge of the circle, so it is half the diameter. The formula needs this value.", "Wrong. The disc is small and is not subtracted here. The step is about turning a diameter into a radius.", "Wrong. There is no such rule. You halve because the formula needs the radius, then you square it."]}, "faded": {"task": "Antibiotic P gives a clear zone 6 mm across. Antibiotic Q gives one 10 mm across. Find how many times larger Q's area is than P's.", "given": ["Radius of P = 6 ÷ 2 = 3 mm", "Radius of Q = 10 ÷ 2 = 5 mm"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Area of P = $\\pi \\times 6^2 = 113$ mm²", "Compare the diameters: 10 ÷ 6 = 1.7 times larger", "Area of P = $\\pi \\times 3^2 = 28.3$ mm²", "Area of P = $\\pi \\times 3 = 9.4$ mm²"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. This uses the diameter as the radius, even though the radius was just found. Always square the radius.", "Wrong. This compares diameters. The question asks about area, so you must work out both areas first.", "Right. Square the radius, 3² = 9, then multiply by $\\pi$. Then do the same for Q: $\\pi \\times 5^2 = 78.5$ mm².", "Wrong. The radius has not been squared. The formula is $\\pi r^2$, which means $\\pi \\times r \\times r$."]}, "trap": "Comparing diameters instead of areas, or putting the diameter into $\\pi r^2$. Always halve the diameter first, then square the radius. Check your answer ends in mm².", "recap": "Grow bacteria with aseptic technique, then compare antibiotics by clear zone area, $\\pi r^2$ with r = diameter ÷ 2, not by diameter."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.1.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 1 asks short describe and explain questions on cell division and stem cells, usually linking mitosis to growth and stem cells to medical treatment.", "hook": {"q": "A cell is about to divide into two new cells. What must happen to its DNA first?", "o": ["It is split in half, so each new cell gets half of it", "It is destroyed, and each new cell then makes brand new DNA", "It is copied, so each new cell can receive a full set", "Nothing. Each new cell makes its own DNA later on"], "a": 2, "f": ["Halving the DNA is a slip. Body cells must keep a full set, so the DNA is copied first and then shared out.", "The DNA is not destroyed. The original is copied, and the copies are what get shared between the new cells.", "Right. The DNA is copied first, so there are two sets. One full set can then go to each new cell.", "If the DNA were not copied first, the new cells would have no full set. They cannot make DNA from nothing."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Chromosomes and copying DNA", "b": "The nucleus of a body cell holds **chromosomes**. Each is a long, coiled DNA molecule carrying many genes. Human body cells have 46, in 23 pairs. Before dividing, the cell grows and makes more ribosomes and mitochondria. It also **replicates** its DNA, so each chromosome has two identical copies."}, {"h": "What happens in mitosis", "b": "In **mitosis**, one copy of each chromosome is pulled to each end of the cell. The nucleus divides. Then the cytoplasm and cell membrane divide. This makes two **genetically identical** cells, each with 46 chromosomes. Organisms use mitosis for growth and development."}, {"h": "Stem cells", "b": "A **stem cell** is **undifferentiated**. It can divide to make more cells, and it can differentiate into specialised cells. Embryonic stem cells can become most cell types. Adult stem cells, such as those in bone marrow, form fewer types, for example blood cells. Plants have **meristems** that do this throughout life."}, {"h": "Stem cells in medicine", "b": "Stem cells may help treat conditions such as diabetes and paralysis. In **therapeutic cloning**, an embryo with the patient's own genes is made, so the body does not **reject** the cells. Risks: infected stem cells could pass a virus to the patient. Some people have ethical or religious objections to using embryos."}], "example": {"task": "Describe what happens to a body cell from when it starts to prepare for division until two new cells have formed.", "steps": [{"do": "The cell grows and makes more ribosomes and mitochondria.", "why": "The two new cells will each need enough sub-cellular structures to work."}, {"do": "The cell replicates its DNA, so each chromosome has two identical copies.", "why": "This happens before mitosis, so there is a full set of chromosomes to share out."}, {"do": "In mitosis, one copy of each chromosome is pulled to each end of the cell.", "why": "This shares the copies equally, so each end gets one full set."}, {"do": "The nucleus divides, then the cytoplasm and cell membrane divide.", "why": "This is the point where one cell becomes two separate cells."}, {"do": "Two genetically identical cells form, each with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell.", "why": "The chromosome copies were identical, so the new cells match each other and the parent."}], "answer": "The cell grows and makes more ribosomes and mitochondria. It replicates its DNA, so each chromosome has two identical copies. In mitosis, one copy of each chromosome is pulled to each end. The nucleus divides, then the cytoplasm and membrane divide. Two genetically identical cells form."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer put DNA replication before the chromosomes are pulled to opposite ends?", "o": ["Each chromosome needs two copies, so one copy can go to each new cell", "DNA is copied during mitosis itself, so both are the same stage", "So the two new cells end up with half the chromosomes of the parent", "So the new cells can differ from each other and add variation"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Replication makes two copies of every chromosome. Mitosis then pulls one copy to each end, so each new cell gets a full set.", "Replication happens before mitosis, while the cell is growing. Mitosis only shares out the copies that already exist.", "Halving the number is a mix-up with making gametes. In mitosis each new cell keeps all 46 chromosomes.", "Mitosis makes identical cells. The copies are identical, so there is no variation. Variation comes from other processes."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why doctors might treat a patient with stem cells from a cloned embryo that has the patient's own genes.", "given": ["Stem cells are undifferentiated, so they can differentiate into the type of cell the patient needs.", "The cloned embryo has the same genes as the patient."], "q": "What is the next point that completes the explanation?", "o": ["Using the patient's own genes means there is no risk of any kind, such as infection", "The cloned cells have half the patient's chromosomes, so they fit in more easily", "The cells are copied by meiosis, so they are all different and one will match", "The patient's body does not see the cells as foreign, so it does not reject them"], "a": 3, "f": ["Therapeutic cloning still has risks. A virus in the cells could infect the patient. Same genes only help with rejection.", "Cloned cells have the same full set of genes as the patient, not half. Halving is a mix-up with gametes.", "Stem cells divide by mitosis, which makes identical cells. Meiosis makes gametes and is not used here.", "Right. The cells have the same genes as the patient, so the body does not treat them as foreign and does not reject them."]}, "trap": "Thinking DNA is copied during mitosis itself. It is copied earlier, while the cell is growing. Mitosis only shares out the copies, and each new cell keeps the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell.", "recap": "The cell grows and copies its DNA first, then mitosis makes two identical cells. Stem cells can differentiate into other cell types."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.1.3.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Diffusion and active transport come up in short-answer questions on exchange of substances, often asking you to explain which process is used and why.", "hook": {"q": "Perfume is sprayed in one corner of a still room. Soon it can be smelt everywhere. What best explains this?", "o": ["Warm air always pushes the particles towards the colder parts of the room", "A draught from a door or window carries the particles to every part of the room", "Particles move randomly and spread from where there are many to where there are few", "The particles use energy to travel towards the people who are smelling them"], "a": 2, "f": ["Warm air does not steer particles to cold areas. The spreading happens even in still air at an even temperature.", "The room is still, so there is no draught. Particles spread out without any outside push.", "Random movement means more particles leave the crowded area than enter it, so they spread out. This is diffusion.", "Particles are not drawn towards people and do not use energy to spread. Diffusion is passive."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Diffusion: down the gradient", "b": "**Diffusion** is the **net movement** of particles from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration. It is **passive**, so it needs no energy. It happens with particles in a gas or in a solution. Oxygen, carbon dioxide and urea cross cell membranes this way."}, {"h": "What speeds diffusion up", "b": "Diffusion is faster with a **steeper concentration gradient** (a bigger difference), a **higher temperature** (particles move faster) and a **larger surface area** of membrane. A bigger gradient means more particles cross each second."}, {"h": "Surface area to volume ratio", "b": "**SA:V** = surface area ÷ volume. A 1 cm cube is 6:1. A 2 cm cube is 24:8 = 3:1. Bigger objects have a **smaller ratio**, because volume grows faster than surface area. A small **single-celled organism** has a large ratio, so diffusion across its surface meets its needs."}, {"h": "Active transport", "b": "**Active transport** moves substances from a lower to a higher concentration, **against the gradient**. It needs **energy from respiration**. Root hair cells take in mineral ions from dilute soil water this way. Gut cells absorb glucose this way when the gut has less glucose than the cell."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a small single-celled organism can get enough oxygen by diffusion, but a large animal cannot rely on diffusion across its body surface.", "steps": [{"do": "Oxygen enters the organism by diffusion, from a higher concentration outside to a lower concentration inside. Respiration uses oxygen up inside, which keeps the concentration there low.", "why": "This names the process and its direction. The gradient is what drives diffusion."}, {"do": "A single-celled organism has a large surface area to volume ratio.", "why": "It is small, so it has plenty of membrane for its tiny volume."}, {"do": "So enough oxygen crosses the surface each second to meet the needs of the whole cell.", "why": "This links the ratio to the rate: supply across the surface matches what the cell uses."}, {"do": "A large animal has a much smaller surface area to volume ratio.", "why": "As size increases, volume grows faster than surface area, so the ratio falls."}, {"do": "So its body surface is too small to supply oxygen to all its cells by diffusion alone. It needs specialised exchange surfaces and a transport system.", "why": "This concludes the comparison: supply across the surface cannot meet demand from the large volume."}], "answer": "Oxygen diffuses in down a concentration gradient. A single-celled organism has a large SA:V ratio, so enough oxygen crosses its surface. A large animal has a small SA:V ratio, so its surface cannot supply all its cells by diffusion alone."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 1 say that respiration uses up oxygen inside the organism?", "o": ["Because oxygen needs energy from respiration to be pulled in by active transport", "Because using up oxygen keeps the concentration inside low, so the gradient continues", "Because respiration warms the cell, which makes oxygen diffuse faster", "Because using up oxygen increases the surface area of the cell membrane"], "a": 1, "f": ["Oxygen moves in by diffusion, which needs no energy. Respiration uses oxygen, but it does not power the uptake.", "Correct. If oxygen built up inside, the gradient would flatten and diffusion would slow. Using it up keeps the difference steep.", "Respiration does release some heat, but the point here is the gradient, not temperature.", "Using up a substance does not change membrane area. Surface area is a separate factor."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why cells with a folded cell membrane absorb digested food molecules faster by diffusion than cells with a smooth membrane.", "given": ["Digested food molecules are at a higher concentration in the gut than inside the cells.", "So they move into the cells by diffusion, down the concentration gradient."], "q": "What is the next point in the answer?", "o": ["The folds increase the volume of the cell, so more molecules fit inside it.", "The folds pump the molecules in by active transport, which is faster than diffusion.", "The folds give a larger surface area, so more molecules can cross the membrane each second.", "The folds raise the temperature of the membrane, so the molecules move faster."], "a": 2, "f": ["Folds add membrane area, not volume. The rate depends on the surface area for diffusion, not on how much room is inside.", "The movement is down the gradient, so it is diffusion. Folds do not change the process to active transport.", "Correct. A larger surface area means more molecules cross at once, so the rate of diffusion is higher.", "Folds do not change the temperature. The factor they change is surface area."]}, "trap": "Check direction first: down the gradient is diffusion (no energy); against it is active transport (energy from respiration). Also, bigger objects have a smaller SA:V ratio, not a bigger one.", "recap": "Diffusion moves particles down a concentration gradient with no energy; active transport moves them against it using energy from respiration; larger SA:V speeds exchange."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to name a level of organisation or explain why something is an organ, and later Organisation topics build on this.", "hook": {"q": "Your stomach churns food and also makes digestive juices. Why might it need more than one type of tissue?", "o": ["Because one tissue can only be made from a single cell", "Churning and making juices are different jobs, so different tissues do each", "Because tissues only work when there are two of them together", "Because the stomach is an organ system, and systems need many tissues"], "a": 1, "f": ["A tissue is a group of similar cells, not a single cell. This confuses the levels of organisation.", "Right. Different jobs need different specialised cells. Grouping each type into its own tissue lets one organ do several jobs.", "Tissues do not need to be paired. The number of tissues depends on the jobs the organ has to do.", "The stomach is an organ, not an organ system. Organ systems are made of several organs."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Cells and tissues", "b": "A **cell** is the basic unit of a living thing. A **tissue** is a group of similar cells that work together to do one job. Examples are muscular tissue, which contracts, and glandular tissue, which makes substances such as enzymes."}, {"h": "Tissues make organs", "b": "An **organ** is a group of different tissues working together to do one job. The stomach has muscular tissue to churn food, glandular tissue to make digestive juices, and **epithelial tissue** to cover its inside and outside surfaces."}, {"h": "Organs make systems", "b": "An **organ system** is a group of organs working together to do one job. The digestive system digests and absorbs food. Its organs include the salivary glands, stomach, liver, pancreas and small intestine. Organ systems together make the whole **organism**."}, {"h": "Keeping the levels apart", "b": "The **levels of organisation** in order are cell, tissue, organ, organ system, organism. Each level is made from the one below. A tissue is **similar cells**. An organ is **different tissues**. An organ system is several organs. Check what a thing is made of."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the stomach is an organ, and how it is part of a larger system in the body.", "steps": [{"do": "The stomach is made of many cells. Cells of the same type are grouped together as tissues.", "why": "Starts at the lowest level. Tissues are the level above cells."}, {"do": "It has muscular tissue to churn food, glandular tissue to make digestive juices, and epithelial tissue to cover its surfaces.", "why": "Names three different tissues and gives each a job. Both earn marks."}, {"do": "These different tissues work together to digest food, so the stomach is an organ.", "why": "This uses the definition: an organ is several tissues doing one job."}, {"do": "The stomach works with the small intestine and liver in the digestive system, which is an organ system.", "why": "An organ system is organs working together, so this links the organ to the next level."}], "answer": "The stomach is made of different tissues: muscular tissue churns food, glandular tissue makes digestive juices and epithelial tissue covers surfaces. Together they digest food, so it is an organ. It works with other organs in the digestive system."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer call the stomach an organ rather than a tissue?", "o": ["It contains several different tissues working together to do one job", "It is larger than most tissues, and only large structures are organs", "It makes digestive juices, which only an organ can do", "It is part of the digestive system, so it must be an organ"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. An organ is made of different tissues working together. A tissue is only one group of similar cells.", "Size does not decide the level. What it is made of does.", "Glandular tissue alone can make juices, so this does not make the stomach an organ.", "Being part of an organ system does not define an organ. The system is the next level up."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why the digestive system is called an organ system.", "given": ["The digestive system contains several organs, such as the stomach, pancreas and small intestine."], "q": "Which sentence should come next?", "o": ["All these organs are one type of tissue, so together they form a bigger tissue.", "Together they form one large organ, because each of them contains tissues.", "These organs work together to digest and absorb food, which is one overall job.", "Each organ is a single cell, so together the cells make up the system."], "a": 2, "f": ["Organs are made of several different tissues, not one. This mixes up tissue and organ.", "Several organs together make an organ system, not a bigger organ. This is a common slip between the two levels.", "Right. An organ system is a group of organs working together to do one job. Here that job is digesting and absorbing food.", "Organs are made of many cells grouped into tissues, not a single cell. This skips the tissue and organ levels."]}, "trap": "Calling the stomach a tissue or an organ system. A tissue is one group of similar cells. An organ contains several tissues. An organ system is several organs. Check what each is made of.", "recap": "A tissue is similar cells, an organ is different tissues working together, and an organ system is organs working together."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.2.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Digestion and the amylase–iodine practical are tested in Paper 1, so you need to recall what enzymes and bile do and explain the results.", "hook": {"q": "Chew a piece of bread for a minute and it starts to taste sweet. What is the best explanation?", "o": ["Saliva contains amylase, which breaks the starch in the bread down into sugars.", "Saliva contains sugar, which mixes into the bread as you chew.", "Chewing alone turns starch into sugar by grinding it into small pieces.", "Saliva contains lipase, which breaks the bread down into sweet fatty acids."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Amylase in saliva digests starch into sugars, and you taste the sugars. This is a chemical change, not just chewing.", "Saliva is mostly water and enzymes. It does not add sugar. The sweetness comes from sugar made from starch.", "Chewing only cuts food into smaller pieces. It does not change starch into sugar. An enzyme is needed for that chemical change.", "Lipase digests lipids (fats), not starch, and it makes glycerol and fatty acids. Starch is digested by amylase."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Enzymes digest food", "b": "Starch, protein and fat are large and **insoluble**. **Digestive enzymes** break them into small **soluble** molecules that can pass into the blood. Amylase breaks starch into sugars. Protease breaks protein into amino acids. Lipase breaks lipids into glycerol and fatty acids."}, {"h": "What bile does", "b": "The liver makes **bile**. The gall bladder stores it, then it flows into the small intestine. Bile is alkaline, so it neutralises acid from the stomach and gives enzymes the right pH. It also **emulsifies** fat into droplets. This gives a larger **surface area** for lipase."}, {"h": "Food tests", "b": "**Benedict's** solution, heated in a water bath, goes from blue to green, yellow, orange or brick-red if sugars are present. **Iodine** goes from orange-brown to blue-black for starch. **Biuret** goes from blue to purple for protein. Ethanol then water gives a cloudy white layer for lipids."}, {"h": "Amylase and pH practical", "b": "Mix amylase, starch and a **buffer** of known pH in a water bath. Every 30 seconds, drop a sample into iodine on a spotting tile. Time how long until the iodine stays orange-brown, which means all the starch is digested. Repeat at other pHs. The pH with the shortest time is closest to the **optimum pH**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how bile and lipase work together to digest the fat in a cheese sandwich.", "steps": [{"do": "Bile is made in the liver, stored in the gall bladder and released into the small intestine.", "why": "This says where bile comes from and where it acts, so the rest of the answer makes sense."}, {"do": "Bile is alkaline, so it neutralises the acid that comes from the stomach.", "why": "Enzymes in the small intestine need a suitable pH. Acid would slow them."}, {"do": "Bile emulsifies the fat, breaking large fat globules into many small droplets.", "why": "This is the job of bile. It is a physical change, not a chemical one."}, {"do": "The droplets have a larger surface area, so lipase can reach more fat at once.", "why": "This links emulsification to faster digestion. It is the key mark."}, {"do": "Lipase breaks the lipids down into glycerol and fatty acids.", "why": "This names the enzyme's products, which are small and soluble enough to be absorbed."}], "answer": "Bile is made in the liver, stored in the gall bladder and released into the small intestine. It is alkaline, so it neutralises stomach acid and gives enzymes a suitable pH. It emulsifies fat into droplets, giving a larger surface area. Lipase then digests the fat faster, making glycerol and fatty acids."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say bile neutralises the acid from the stomach?", "o": ["Neutralising the acid is how bile turns fat into small droplets.", "Bile is an enzyme, and stomach acid would stop it working.", "Enzymes in the small intestine need a suitable pH, and the acid would slow them.", "Alkaline bile is what breaks the fat down into glycerol and fatty acids."], "a": 2, "f": ["Emulsifying fat is a separate job of bile. Neutralising acid is about pH, not about making droplets.", "Bile is not an enzyme. It is a liquid that neutralises acid and emulsifies fat.", "Right. Stomach acid enters the small intestine. Bile raises the pH so the digestive enzymes there can work well.", "Bile does not digest fat chemically. Lipase does that. Bile only helps by neutralising acid and emulsifying."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the protein in an egg is digested so that it can be absorbed into the blood.", "given": ["Protein molecules are large and insoluble, so they cannot be absorbed into the blood as they are.", "A protease enzyme acts on the protein in the stomach and the small intestine."], "q": "Which point completes the explanation?", "o": ["It breaks protein into glucose, which is small and soluble enough to be absorbed.", "It breaks protein into glycerol and fatty acids, small and soluble enough to be absorbed.", "It emulsifies protein into droplets, giving a larger surface area so it is absorbed.", "It breaks protein into amino acids, which are small and soluble enough to be absorbed."], "a": 3, "f": ["Glucose comes from digesting starch with amylase, not protein. Link each enzyme to its own substrate and products.", "Glycerol and fatty acids are the products of lipase acting on lipids. Protease makes amino acids from protein.", "Emulsifying is done by bile and only applies to fat. Protease is an enzyme that breaks protein down chemically.", "Right. Protease digests protein into amino acids. These are small and soluble, so they can pass into the blood."]}, "trap": "Thinking blue-black means digestion has finished. Blue-black means starch is still there. Digestion is finished when the iodine stays orange-brown.", "recap": "Enzymes break large insoluble food molecules into small soluble ones; in the amylase practical, orange-brown iodine means all the starch has gone."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.2.2.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to match each part of the blood to its job, or to link a feature of a blood cell to what it does.", "hook": {"q": "You graze your knee. The bleeding stops after a few minutes and a scab forms. What in the blood helps most?", "o": ["Red blood cells, which carry oxygen to the wound", "White blood cells, which engulf pathogens", "Platelets, which help the blood to clot", "Plasma, which is the liquid that carries the cells"], "a": 2, "f": ["Red cells carry oxygen. They do not seal wounds, so this mixes up the jobs of the blood cells.", "White cells defend against pathogens. They do not stop the bleeding itself, so this is the wrong job.", "Correct. Platelets help the blood to clot at a wound. The clot dries to form a scab.", "Plasma carries dissolved substances and the cells around the body. It does not seal a wound."], "a_note": null}, "chunks": [{"h": "Blood is a tissue", "b": "**Blood** is a **tissue**. It has a liquid part called **plasma**. Red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets are suspended in it. Plasma carries carbon dioxide from the organs to the lungs, soluble products of digestion from the small intestine to other organs, and urea from the liver to the kidneys."}, {"h": "Red blood cells", "b": "**Red blood cells** carry oxygen from the lungs to all the body's cells. They contain **haemoglobin**, which binds to oxygen. Mature red cells have no **nucleus**, so there is more room for haemoglobin. They are a biconcave disc shape, which gives a large surface area."}, {"h": "White blood cells", "b": "White blood cells defend the body against pathogens. They do this in three ways. They engulf and digest pathogens, which is **phagocytosis**. They produce **antibodies**. They also produce **antitoxins**, which neutralise toxins made by pathogens."}, {"h": "Platelets", "b": "**Platelets** are small fragments of cells. They have no **nucleus**. They help the blood to **clot** at a wound. The clot dries to form a scab. This stops blood loss and helps to keep pathogens out."}], "example": {"task": "A girl grazes her knee on a path. Explain how her blood helps to stop blood loss and to prevent infection.", "steps": [{"do": "Platelets help the blood to clot at the graze.", "why": "Platelets are the part of blood that clots, so they deal with the blood loss."}, {"do": "The clot dries to form a scab, which stops more blood leaving and stops pathogens getting in.", "why": "This links the clot to both parts of the question: blood loss and infection."}, {"do": "Some pathogens may still get in. White blood cells engulf and digest them (phagocytosis).", "why": "White cells are the defenders. Name phagocytosis as the process."}, {"do": "White blood cells also produce antibodies and antitoxins.", "why": "This gives the other ways white cells defend the body, so it earns extra marks."}], "answer": "Platelets help the blood to clot at the graze. The clot dries to a scab, which stops blood loss and stops pathogens entering. Any pathogens that get in are engulfed and digested by white blood cells. White cells also produce antibodies and antitoxins."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say white blood cells, not red blood cells, deal with pathogens that get in?", "o": ["Red blood cells have no nucleus, so they are too small to reach the graze", "White cells engulf pathogens and make antibodies. Red cells carry oxygen using haemoglobin", "Red blood cells are found only in the lungs, so they never reach a graze", "White cells are larger than red cells, so they block the graze like a scab"], "a": 1, "f": ["Red cells travel all through the blood. Having no nucleus gives room for haemoglobin. It does not stop them reaching a wound.", "Correct. Defence against pathogens is the job of white cells. Red cells are adapted to carry oxygen.", "Red cells are in the blood all over the body, not only the lungs. The reason is about their job, not their position.", "Scabs come from clotting, which platelets help with. White cells defend by engulfing pathogens and making antibodies."], "a_note": null}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the blood helps muscle cells during a race. Include how it supplies what they need and removes waste.", "given": ["Red blood cells carry oxygen from the lungs to the muscle cells. The oxygen is bound to haemoglobin."], "q": "Which sentence is the correct next point, about removing the waste carbon dioxide?", "o": ["White blood cells carry carbon dioxide from the muscles to the lungs.", "Platelets carry carbon dioxide from the muscles to the lungs.", "Plasma carries carbon dioxide from the muscles to the lungs.", "Plasma carries carbon dioxide from the lungs to the muscles."], "a": 2, "f": ["White cells defend the body against pathogens. Transporting carbon dioxide is a job of the plasma.", "Platelets help blood to clot. They do not carry gases.", "Correct. Plasma carries carbon dioxide from the organs, here the muscles, to the lungs.", "The direction is wrong. Carbon dioxide is a waste made in the muscles, so it goes from the muscles to the lungs."], "a_note": null}, "trap": "Writing only that red cells carry more oxygen. Say the space left by no nucleus is for haemoglobin, which binds oxygen. Also remember that plasma carries carbon dioxide and urea.", "recap": "Plasma carries dissolved substances, red cells carry oxygen using haemoglobin, white cells defend against pathogens, and platelets help blood to clot at wounds."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.2.2.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 1 often asks short 'describe' or 'explain' questions on heart disease, lifestyle risk factors and cancer, and most marks go to precise key terms.", "hook": {"q": "Heart disease cannot be passed from one person to another. What is the best reason?", "o": ["It is not caused by a pathogen; it is linked to things like diet, smoking and genes", "It is caused by a pathogen that can only survive inside the heart", "It only affects older people, whose immune systems are weaker", "It is caused by a pathogen, but the body always destroys it before it spreads"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Heart disease is non-communicable: no pathogen is involved. Risk factors such as diet, smoking and inherited genes raise the chance of it.", "Wrong. Heart disease is not caused by any pathogen. Pathogens such as bacteria and viruses cause communicable diseases only.", "Wrong. Age can raise the risk, but the reason it cannot be passed on is that no pathogen causes it. Immune strength is not the point.", "Wrong. This assumes a pathogen is involved. There is none, so there is nothing to spread in the first place."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Not caught from others", "b": "**Non-communicable diseases** are not caused by pathogens. You cannot catch them from another person. Examples are coronary heart disease, cancer and type 2 diabetes. They usually have several causes. A **risk factor** is something that raises the chance of getting a disease. It does not make the disease certain."}, {"h": "Coronary heart disease", "b": "The **coronary arteries** supply the heart muscle with blood. In coronary heart disease, layers of **fatty material** build up inside them and make them narrower. Less blood flows, so less oxygen reaches the heart muscle. A **stent** holds an artery open. Statins lower blood cholesterol. Faulty heart valves can be replaced."}, {"h": "Lifestyle risk factors", "b": "Smoking raises the risk of lung disease, heart disease and some cancers. Obesity is a risk factor for **type 2 diabetes**. Alcohol can damage the liver and brain. Smoking and alcohol in pregnancy can harm the unborn baby. **Carcinogens** and **ionising radiation** raise the risk of cancer. Some cancers also run in families through inherited genes."}, {"h": "How cancer starts and spreads", "b": "Cancer starts when changes in cells make them divide in an uncontrolled way. The cells form a **tumour**. A **benign** tumour stays in one place, often inside a membrane. In a **malignant** tumour, cells invade neighbouring tissues and spread in the blood to other parts of the body. They form secondary tumours."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how a malignant tumour differs from a benign tumour, and explain why a malignant tumour is more dangerous. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "A tumour of either type is a mass of abnormal cells. It forms when cells divide in an uncontrolled way.", "why": "This shared starting point shows you know what a tumour is before comparing."}, {"do": "A benign tumour stays in one place, often inside a membrane. Its cells do not invade other tissues.", "why": "This is the key feature of benign. It can grow, but it does not spread."}, {"do": "Cells of a malignant tumour invade neighbouring tissues.", "why": "This is the first thing that makes a tumour malignant, so it earns a mark."}, {"do": "Malignant cells break away and travel in the blood to other parts of the body.", "why": "Spread in the blood is the precise point examiners look for. It separates malignant from benign."}, {"do": "They form secondary tumours in new places, so malignant tumours are harder to treat than a tumour that stays in one place.", "why": "This links the spread to the danger, which is what 'explain' asks for."}], "answer": "Both are masses of abnormal cells from uncontrolled division. A benign tumour stays in one place. A malignant tumour's cells invade neighbouring tissues and spread in the blood to other parts of the body, forming secondary tumours. This makes it more dangerous and harder to treat."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say malignant cells travel in the blood?", "o": ["Because blood makes tumour cells divide faster than normal", "Because benign cells also travel in blood, but more slowly", "Because blood carries the cells to other organs to form secondary tumours", "Because the blood destroys the cells before they reach another organ"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Blood does not make cells divide faster. Its role here is transport. Uncontrolled division comes from changes inside the cells.", "Wrong. Benign cells do not spread in the blood at all. Spread is exactly what separates malignant from benign.", "Right. Travel in the blood is how cancer cells reach other parts of the body. There they form secondary tumours.", "Wrong. Blood does not stop the spread. If it did, cancer would not spread and malignant tumours would not be more dangerous."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why narrowing of the coronary arteries is a problem for the heart. (3 marks)", "given": ["The coronary arteries supply the heart muscle with blood.", "In coronary heart disease, fatty material builds up inside them and makes them narrower."], "q": "What is the next point in the explanation?", "o": ["The heart muscle gets more blood than it needs, so it swells", "Less blood reaches the heart muscle, so it gets less oxygen", "Less blood reaches the lungs, so less oxygen enters the blood", "Blood flows backwards through the valves into the atria"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Narrowed arteries reduce blood flow. They do not increase it, so the muscle receives less, not more.", "Right. A narrower artery lets less blood through, so the heart muscle gets less oxygen. That is the effect the question asks for.", "Wrong. This mixes up the coronary arteries with the pulmonary artery. The coronary arteries supply the heart muscle itself, not the lungs.", "Wrong. Backflow is a problem with faulty valves, not with narrowed coronary arteries. The link here is blood supply to the heart muscle."]}, "trap": "Writing that a malignant tumour is 'bigger' or 'grows faster'. Benign tumours can grow too. Always say what makes it malignant: its cells invade neighbouring tissues and spread in the blood, forming secondary tumours.", "recap": "Risk factors raise the chance of non-communicable disease; a malignant tumour is cancer because its cells spread in the blood and form secondary tumours."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.2.3.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Short explain questions on transpiration, stomata, and xylem versus phloem come up regularly in Paper 1, and they build on diffusion and osmosis.", "hook": {"q": "A celery stalk stands in red dye. After a few hours, thin red lines run up the stalk into the leaves. What does this suggest?", "o": ["Water soaks evenly up the stalk, like water into a sponge", "The leaves make the dye and send it down to the base", "The stalk has narrow tubes that carry water up to the leaves", "Water only moves up the stalk when the leaves are in the dark"], "a": 2, "f": ["The dye makes thin lines, not an even patch. That points to set tubes, not general soaking.", "The dye started in the water at the base. It moved up into the leaves, not down from them.", "Thin red lines show water travelling in narrow tubes. You will meet these tubes as the xylem.", "The lines show where water travels, not when. Nothing here links the movement to darkness."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Plant organs and tissues", "b": "Roots, stems and leaves are **organs**. Each is made of several tissues. In a leaf, the epidermis covers the surface. **Palisade mesophyll** does most of the photosynthesis. Spongy mesophyll has air spaces for gas exchange. **Meristem** tissue at shoot and root tips keeps making new cells."}, {"h": "Xylem and phloem", "b": "**Xylem** carries water and mineral ions from the roots up to the shoots. It is made of dead, hollow cells strengthened with lignin. **Phloem** carries dissolved sugars from the leaves to growing and storage parts. This is **translocation**. Phloem is made of living cells, and sugars can move up or down."}, {"h": "Transpiration", "b": "**Transpiration** is the loss of water vapour from leaves. Water evaporates from mesophyll cells inside the leaf. The vapour diffuses out through the **stomata**. Losing water pulls more up the xylem from the roots. This is the **transpiration stream**. It is faster when it is hotter, brighter, windier or less humid."}, {"h": "Guard cells control stomata", "b": "Each stoma (pore) lies between two **guard cells**. When the guard cells are turgid, the stoma is open. This lets in carbon dioxide for photosynthesis. When the plant is short of water, the guard cells lose water and go **flaccid**. The stomata close. Less water is lost, but gas exchange slows."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how water is lost from a leaf, and how the plant reduces this loss when it is short of water. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Water evaporates from the surface of the mesophyll cells inside the leaf.", "why": "This is where transpiration starts: liquid water becomes water vapour."}, {"do": "The water vapour diffuses out through the stomata, from a high concentration inside to a lower one outside.", "why": "Diffusion is movement down a concentration gradient. Stomata are the openings."}, {"do": "More water is pulled up the xylem from the roots to replace what was lost.", "why": "This links the loss to the transpiration stream. Xylem carries water."}, {"do": "When water is short, the guard cells lose water and go flaccid, so the stomata close.", "why": "Closed stomata stop vapour escaping, so less water is lost."}], "answer": "Water evaporates from mesophyll cells and the vapour diffuses out through the stomata. This pulls more water up the xylem. When water is short, the guard cells go flaccid and the stomata close, so less water is lost."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the lost water is replaced through the xylem and not the phloem?", "o": ["Xylem carries water and mineral ions up from the roots. Phloem carries sugars.", "Phloem carries water up the plant. Xylem carries dissolved sugars down.", "Xylem cells are living and pump water up. Phloem cells are dead tubes.", "Both carry water, but xylem is closer to the stomata so it is used."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. The xylem moves water and mineral ions from roots to leaves. The phloem is for dissolved sugars (translocation).", "This swaps the two tissues. Xylem carries water up. Phloem carries dissolved sugars.", "This reverses the cell types. Xylem is dead, hollow tubes. Phloem is made of living cells. Neither pumps water in transpiration.", "Only the xylem carries water up from the roots. Phloem carries dissolved sugars. Closeness to the stomata does not decide this."], "d": 0}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how sugars made in a leaf reach a growing root tip.", "given": ["Sugars are made in the leaf by photosynthesis.", "The dissolved sugars move into the phloem."], "q": "What is the next point in the explanation?", "o": ["The transpiration stream carries the sugars up the xylem to the root tip.", "The sugars travel in the xylem, which carries dissolved food both up and down.", "The sugars leave through the stomata and are taken in again by root hairs.", "Translocation carries the sugars through the phloem to the root tip for use or storage."], "a": 3, "f": ["The transpiration stream is water moving up the xylem. Sugars are not carried in it, and the root tip is below the leaf.", "This gives the xylem the phloem's job. Xylem carries water and mineral ions upwards only.", "Stomata let gases and water vapour pass. Sugars do not leave the leaf this way. Roots do not take them back in.", "Right. Translocation in the phloem moves dissolved sugars to growing or storage parts such as the root tip."]}, "trap": "Thinking stomata open when the plant is short of water. Losing water makes guard cells flaccid, so the stomata close. Remember: turgid means open, flaccid means closed.", "recap": "Water moves up the xylem and leaves as vapour through stomata; flaccid guard cells close stomata, and phloem carries sugars."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.3.1.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 1 often asks how a named pathogen causes illness, how it spreads, and how to stop it, so you need to link all three in a few clear points.", "hook": {"q": "A person with a stomach bug does not wash their hands after using the toilet. Why could this make others ill?", "o": ["Dirty hands make the person's own illness worse", "Pathogens on their hands can pass to other people or food", "Stomach bugs only ever spread through the air", "Pathogens always die as soon as they leave the body"], "a": 1, "f": ["Washing hands is about stopping spread to others, not about the person's own illness getting worse.", "Right. Pathogens can pass from hands to food or to other people by direct contact. Washing hands breaks that route.", "Stomach bugs can spread by contact and in food or water. Air is only one possible route, not the only one.", "Many pathogens survive for a time outside the body, on hands, surfaces, food or water. That is how they spread."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What pathogens are", "b": "**Pathogens** are microorganisms that cause infectious disease. They can be bacteria, viruses, protists or fungi. They can infect plants and animals. A disease that can pass from one organism to another is **communicable**."}, {"h": "How they make you ill", "b": "**Bacteria** are small cells that reproduce rapidly inside the body. They can release **toxins** that damage tissues. **Viruses** live and reproduce inside your cells. This damages the cells, and that makes you ill."}, {"h": "How they spread", "b": "Pathogens spread by **direct contact** (touching, body fluids), by water (drinking or washing in contaminated water) and by **air** (droplets from coughs and sneezes). A **vector** is another organism, such as a mosquito, that carries a pathogen between hosts."}, {"h": "Reducing the spread", "b": "Match the control to the route. **Hygiene**, such as washing hands, stops contact spread. Isolating infected people stops them passing it on. **Destroying vectors** stops carried pathogens. **Vaccination** protects people from catching the disease."}], "example": {"task": "A bacterial disease spreads in droplets from coughs and sneezes. Explain how the bacteria make a person ill and how the spread of the disease can be reduced.", "steps": [{"do": "The bacteria enter the body and reproduce rapidly.", "why": "Bacteria are small cells that divide quickly, so their numbers build up fast."}, {"do": "They release toxins that damage the person's tissues.", "why": "Toxins are the bacterial way of causing harm. This damage makes the person feel ill."}, {"do": "The bacteria spread through the air in droplets from coughs and sneezes, which other people breathe in.", "why": "This names the route of spread, so the control can be matched to it."}, {"do": "Infected people should cover their mouths when coughing and be isolated from others until they recover.", "why": "Both actions stop droplets reaching other people, which blocks the route of spread."}], "answer": "The bacteria reproduce rapidly in the body and release toxins that damage tissues, which makes the person ill. They spread in droplets in the air. Covering coughs and sneezes and isolating infected people stops the droplets reaching others."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say to cover the mouth when coughing and sneezing?", "o": ["It kills the bacteria already inside the person's body", "It stops the bacteria from making toxins", "It blocks the droplets that carry the bacteria through the air", "It makes other people immune to the disease"], "a": 2, "f": ["Covering the mouth does nothing to bacteria already inside the body. It acts on how they leave and spread.", "Toxin production happens inside the body. Covering the mouth only affects how the bacteria travel.", "Right. The route of spread is droplets in the air. Covering the mouth blocks that route, so fewer people breathe them in.", "Immunity comes from vaccination or from having had the disease. Covering the mouth only reduces how much pathogen is spread."]}, "faded": {"task": "A virus is spread when mosquitoes bite an infected person and then bite other people. Explain how the spread of this disease could be reduced.", "given": ["Step 1: Mosquitoes are vectors, so they carry the virus from one person to another.", "Step 2: The route of spread is the mosquito bite."], "q": "Which is the best next step?", "o": ["Tell people to cover their mouths when they cough", "Tell people to wash their hands before eating", "Give people antibiotics to kill the virus in their cells", "Destroy the mosquitoes and their breeding sites"], "a": 3, "f": ["Covering coughs blocks droplets in the air. This virus does not spread that way, so the control does not match the route.", "Hand washing stops contact and food spread. This virus is carried by bites, so it would not block the route.", "Antibiotics do not work on viruses, and they do not stop mosquitoes spreading it. The control must target the route of spread.", "Right. The vector spreads the virus. Destroying the mosquitoes or their breeding sites breaks the route of spread."]}, "trap": "Saying viruses release toxins. Toxins are the classic bacterial way of causing illness, while viruses damage the cells they reproduce in. Also match each control to the route of spread.", "recap": "Bacteria release toxins and viruses damage the cells they reproduce in; reduce spread by blocking the route: contact, water, air or vector."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.3.1.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to name a disease's pathogen, how it spreads, its symptoms and how to control it, often for a case described in the question.", "hook": {"q": "Measles spreads in droplets from coughs and sneezes. What would best stop it spreading through a school?", "o": ["Putting mosquito nets over every pupil's bed", "Making sure all school food is cooked thoroughly", "Vaccinating the pupils against the disease", "Spraying the classroom plants with fungicide"], "a": 2, "f": ["Nets block mosquito bites. Measles is not spread by a vector, so nets do nothing here.", "Cooking food kills bacteria such as Salmonella. Measles spreads in droplets, not through food.", "Right. A vaccine prepares the immune system, so pupils are protected when droplets carrying the virus reach them.", "Fungicide kills fungi on plants. Measles is a virus that infects people, so this does not match."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Match disease to pathogen", "b": "Each disease is caused by one type of **pathogen**. Viruses: measles, HIV, tobacco mosaic virus. Bacteria: Salmonella, gonorrhoea. Fungus: rose black spot. **Protist**: malaria. Tobacco mosaic virus and rose black spot infect plants. The others infect people."}, {"h": "How each one spreads", "b": "Measles: **droplets** from coughs and sneezes. HIV: blood and other body fluids, and sexual contact. Salmonella: contaminated food, such as raw eggs or poultry. Gonorrhoea: sexual contact. Rose black spot: spores carried by wind and water. Malaria: a mosquito **vector** carries the protist between people."}, {"h": "Symptoms show the damage", "b": "Measles: fever and red rash. HIV: flu-like illness, then damaged **immune cells**; the graph shows them falling as the virus rises, and AIDS follows. Salmonella: fever, cramps, vomiting, diarrhoea. Gonorrhoea: thick yellow or green discharge and pain when urinating. Malaria: repeated fevers. Tobacco mosaic virus and black spot damage leaves, so there is **less photosynthesis** and poor growth.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "xstep": 2, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "time since infection / years", "ylabel": "relative level (arbitrary units)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [0.5, 8], [1, 3], [2, 2], [6, 2], [8, 2.5], [9, 5], [10, 8]], "label": "Virus", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 8], [0.5, 6], [1, 7.5], [4, 7], [6, 6], [8, 4], [9, 3], [10, 2]], "label": "Immune cells", "c": 1}], "points": [{"at": [8, 4], "label": "Other infections"}, {"at": [10, 2], "label": "AIDS"}]}}, {"h": "Match control to spread", "b": "Each control targets the pathogen or its route. Measles: **vaccination**. HIV: antiretroviral drugs. Salmonella: vaccinate poultry and cook food well. Gonorrhoea: condoms, and antibiotics (some strains are resistant). Black spot: **fungicide** and removing affected leaves. Malaria: stop mosquitoes breeding and biting, for example with nets."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how HIV is spread, what it does in the body and how it is treated. (5 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "HIV is a virus. It spreads in body fluids, such as blood when needles are shared, and through sexual contact.", "why": "Names the type of pathogen and the route of spread, which questions usually ask for."}, {"do": "At first, it causes a flu-like illness.", "why": "Gives the early symptom."}, {"do": "The virus attacks the body's immune cells.", "why": "States what the virus damages, which links the cause to the later effects."}, {"do": "If it is not controlled, the immune system becomes so badly damaged that it cannot fight other infections or cancers. This is late-stage HIV, called AIDS.", "why": "Explains the serious consequence of the damage to immune cells."}, {"do": "Antiretroviral drugs control the virus, so the infection is less likely to reach AIDS.", "why": "Names the treatment. It controls HIV rather than curing it."}], "answer": "HIV is a virus spread by body fluids, such as shared needles, and sexual contact. It causes a flu-like illness, then attacks immune cells. Untreated, it leads to AIDS, when other infections cannot be fought. Antiretroviral drugs control it."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say a person with AIDS gets other infections and cancers?", "o": ["HIV passes into other pathogens and makes them more harmful", "The immune system is so damaged that it cannot deal with them", "Antiretroviral drugs weaken the body's defences against infection", "HIV digests the gut lining, letting other pathogens enter the blood"], "a": 1, "f": ["HIV does not pass into other pathogens. It infects the body's own immune cells.", "Right. HIV damages immune cells. In late-stage HIV the immune system can no longer deal with other infections or cancers.", "The drugs control HIV and protect the immune system. The weakness comes from the virus damaging immune cells.", "HIV targets immune cells, not the gut lining. The problem is a damaged immune system."]}, "faded": {"task": "A rose bush has purple-black spots on its leaves. The leaves turn yellow and fall early. Explain the cause and how to treat it.", "given": ["The pathogen is a fungus, which causes rose black spot.", "The spots damage the leaves, so there is less photosynthesis and poor growth."], "q": "Which point about treatment comes next?", "o": ["Give the rose antibiotics, which kill fungi inside the leaves", "Cover the rose with a net to stop mosquitoes spreading the fungus", "Vaccinate the other roses so the fungus cannot infect them", "Spray with a fungicide and remove and destroy the affected leaves"], "a": 3, "f": ["Antibiotics kill bacteria, not fungi. Using them here matches the wrong pathogen type.", "Black spot spreads by spores carried in wind and water. Mosquitoes are not involved, so nets do not match.", "Plants are not vaccinated against black spot. This borrows a control used for diseases such as measles in people.", "Right. A fungicide kills the fungus. Removing and destroying affected leaves also stops spores spreading to healthy plants."]}, "trap": "Choosing a control that does not match the pathogen or its route of spread, such as antibiotics for a virus or nets for droplets. Name the pathogen and route first, then choose.", "recap": "For each disease, link the pathogen type, how it spreads, its symptoms and a control that targets the pathogen or its route."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.3.1.6", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions on this topic ask you to explain how the body stops and destroys pathogens, often naming the three jobs of white blood cells.", "hook": {"q": "You breathe in microbes all day, yet most never make you ill. What is the best reason?", "o": ["Your lungs absorb the microbes and then breathe them out as carbon dioxide", "Your airways make sticky mucus that traps many of them before they reach your lungs", "Your nose filters out all of them, so none ever get past it into your body", "Microbes in the air are almost never able to cause any disease at all"], "a": 1, "f": ["Lungs do not absorb microbes and turn them into carbon dioxide. Gas exchange moves oxygen and carbon dioxide only.", "Right. Mucus in the airways traps microbes. Cilia then move it up to the throat, where it is swallowed.", "Nose hairs and mucus trap many particles, but not all. Some pathogens do get through, so the body needs more defences.", "Many airborne microbes can cause disease, such as those that cause colds or flu. Your defences stop most of them."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Barriers that keep pathogens out", "b": "Your body has barriers that stop most pathogens getting in. The **skin** is a physical barrier. The nose has hairs and **mucus** that trap particles. The trachea and bronchi make mucus too. **Cilia** (tiny hairs) move it to the throat to be swallowed. The stomach makes hydrochloric acid, which kills pathogens."}, {"h": "Phagocytosis", "b": "If pathogens get inside, **white blood cells** attack them. One type, the phagocyte, carries out **phagocytosis**. A phagocyte engulfs a pathogen, taking it inside the cell. Enzymes then digest the pathogen and destroy it. Phagocytes are a type of white blood cell, so this is one of the ways **white blood cells** fight pathogens."}, {"h": "Antibodies", "b": "Every pathogen has **antigens** on its surface. **Lymphocytes** detect antigens that are foreign. This stimulates them to make **antibodies**. Each antibody is specific: its shape fits one type of antigen only. Antibodies bind to the antigens and help to destroy the pathogen."}, {"h": "Antitoxins", "b": "Some bacteria release **toxins**, which are poisons. White blood cells make **antitoxins**. Antitoxins **neutralise** the toxins. They do not engulf or kill the pathogen. Antibodies target antigens on the pathogen. Antitoxins target the toxins it releases."}], "example": {"task": "Bacteria enter the body through a cut and release a toxin. Explain how white blood cells defend the body against them. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Phagocytes engulf the bacteria and digest them (phagocytosis).", "why": "This destroys bacteria directly, and naming the process earns a mark."}, {"do": "Lymphocytes detect the antigens on the surface of the bacteria.", "why": "Antigens mark the bacteria as foreign. Detection comes before antibodies are made."}, {"do": "This stimulates the lymphocytes to make antibodies. Each antibody has a shape that fits only the antigens of these bacteria.", "why": "Antibodies are specific. Saying this shows why they work against one type of pathogen."}, {"do": "The antibodies bind to the antigens and help to destroy the bacteria.", "why": "Binding is what antibodies do. Write 'bind', not 'engulf'."}, {"do": "White blood cells also make antitoxins, which neutralise the toxin released by the bacteria.", "why": "The question mentions a toxin, so antitoxins must be named. They target toxins, not bacteria."}], "answer": "Phagocytes engulf and digest the bacteria. Lymphocytes detect the bacteria's antigens and make specific antibodies, which bind to the antigens and help destroy the bacteria. Antitoxins neutralise the toxin."}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 3, why does the answer say the antibody fits only the antigens of these bacteria?", "o": ["Antibodies are made by phagocytes, which can only recognise bacteria", "Antibodies dissolve the cell wall, which is different in every type of bacterium", "Antibodies are specific: each has a shape that fits one type of antigen only", "Antibodies are only made when a toxin is present, so they match that toxin"], "a": 2, "f": ["Antibodies are made by lymphocytes, not phagocytes. Phagocytes engulf pathogens instead.", "Antibodies do not dissolve cell walls. They bind to antigens and help destroy the pathogen.", "Right. Antibody shape matches one antigen, like a key in a lock. That is why antibodies for one pathogen do not work on another.", "Antibodies target antigens on the pathogen, not toxins. Toxins are neutralised by antitoxins."]}, "faded": {"task": "Some bacteria get past the body's barriers and enter the blood. Explain how white blood cells defend the body against them. (3 marks)", "given": ["Phagocytes engulf some of the bacteria and digest them.", "Other bacteria are dealt with by a second type of white blood cell."], "q": "Which point should come next?", "o": ["Antitoxins bind to the antigens on the bacteria and kill them", "Antibodies engulf the bacteria in the same way as phagocytes do", "Lymphocytes make antibodies that fit any antigen, so they work on all pathogens", "Lymphocytes detect the bacteria's antigens and make antibodies that fit them"], "a": 3, "f": ["Antitoxins neutralise toxins. They do not bind to antigens or kill bacteria. That is the job of antibodies.", "Engulfing is phagocytosis, done by phagocytes. Antibodies bind to antigens and do not engulf anything.", "Antibodies are specific. Each one fits only one type of antigen, so they cannot work on all pathogens.", "Right. Detection of antigens by lymphocytes comes first. It stimulates the making of specific antibodies."]}, "trap": "Saying antitoxins or antibodies engulf or kill pathogens. Engulfing is phagocytosis by phagocytes. Antibodies bind to antigens. Antitoxins neutralise toxins. Match each name to its target.", "recap": "Phagocytes engulf pathogens, antibodies bind to a pathogen's antigens, and antitoxins neutralise toxins; skin, mucus and stomach acid stop pathogens entering."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.3.1.7", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to explain how vaccination protects both the vaccinated person and the wider community, for 3 or 4 marks.", "hook": {"q": "A virus spreads when infected people meet others. In one town, most people cannot catch this virus. What is most likely to happen to its spread?", "o": ["It spreads just as quickly as it did before", "It spreads faster, because the virus becomes stronger", "It stops completely everywhere within a single day", "It slows down, as few people around can catch it"], "a": 3, "f": ["Spread depends on how many people can be infected. If most people cannot catch it, each infected person passes it on less often.", "A virus does not get stronger because people are protected. Fewer people to infect means slower spread, not faster.", "Spread slows, but it does not vanish at once. A few people can still catch it and pass it on.", "Right. Each infected person mostly meets people who cannot catch it, so the pathogen is passed on less and spreads less."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What a vaccine contains", "b": "A vaccine contains a small amount of a dead or inactive form of a pathogen. It carries the pathogen's **antigens** but cannot make you ill. Your **white blood cells** detect the antigens and make **antibodies** against them."}, {"h": "Protection later on", "b": "If the same live pathogen enters your body later, your white blood cells **respond quickly**. They make the **correct antibodies** fast, as the graph shows: a quicker, higher rise the second time. The pathogen is destroyed before you become ill. Antibodies are **specific**, so each vaccine protects only against its own pathogen. Press Replay to watch it.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "draw", "pen": true, "read": ["time", "antibody"], "dp": 1, "ms": 6000, "x": [0, 16], "xstep": 2, "y": [0, 10], "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "time (arbitrary units)", "ylabel": "antibody concentration (arbitrary units)", "lines": [{"c": 0, "step": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [2, 0], [3, 0.2], [4, 0.8], [5, 1.6], [6, 2], [7, 1.4], [8, 0.7], [9, 0.2], [10, 0]]}, {"c": 1, "step": 2, "pts": [[10, 0], [10.5, 3], [11, 6.5], [12, 9], [13, 8.2], [14, 6], [16, 4]]}], "points": [{"at": [2, 0], "label": "vaccination"}, {"at": [10, 0], "label": "pathogen enters"}], "cap": "First rise: slow and low. After the live pathogen enters at 10, the rise is quicker and higher."}}, {"h": "Herd immunity", "b": "If a large proportion of a population is vaccinated, the pathogen has **few people to infect**. It spreads much less. Unvaccinated people are then less likely to meet it. This is **herd immunity**."}, {"h": "Who herd immunity helps", "b": "Some people cannot be vaccinated, such as very young babies. They are **not immune**. They are protected only because fewer infected people are around. If the **proportion vaccinated** falls, the pathogen can spread again."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how vaccinating most of a population against measles protects people who have not been vaccinated. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Vaccinated people have been given a dead or inactive form of the virus. Their white blood cells make antibodies against its antigens.", "why": "This is how the vaccine makes each vaccinated person immune."}, {"do": "If they meet live measles virus later, their white blood cells quickly make the correct antibodies. The virus is destroyed before it makes them ill.", "why": "Fast antibody production stops infection, so they do not pass the virus on."}, {"do": "With most people vaccinated, there are very few infected people in the population.", "why": "Fewer infected people means fewer sources of the pathogen."}, {"do": "The virus spreads much less, so an unvaccinated person is less likely to meet it and catch it.", "why": "This links reduced spread to the protection of the unvaccinated."}, {"do": "This is herd immunity. The unvaccinated person is still not immune. They are just less likely to be infected.", "why": "It shows you know the protection comes from reduced spread, not from antibodies."}], "answer": "Vaccinated people's white blood cells quickly make the correct antibodies, so the virus is destroyed before they can spread it. With most people protected, there are few infected people. The virus spreads much less, so unvaccinated people are less likely to meet it. This is herd immunity."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 5 say the unvaccinated person is still not immune?", "o": ["Unvaccinated people make antibodies slowly, so they are only partly immune", "Herd immunity passes antibodies from vaccinated people to unvaccinated people", "Their protection comes from fewer infected people around, not from their own antibodies", "Unvaccinated people can never be immune to any disease, even after catching it"], "a": 2, "f": ["Unvaccinated people have not been exposed to the antigens, so their white blood cells have not made the antibodies. The idea of 'partly immune' is a slip.", "Antibodies are not passed between people through herd immunity. Each person's own white blood cells make antibodies after seeing antigens.", "Right. Herd immunity works by reducing spread. The unvaccinated person has no extra antibodies; they just meet the pathogen less often.", "People can become immune by catching a disease and recovering. The point here is that herd immunity itself does not make anyone immune."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the polio vaccine protects a vaccinated child from becoming ill with polio.", "given": ["The vaccine contains a dead or inactive form of the polio virus, which has its antigens.", "The child's white blood cells detect the antigens and make antibodies."], "q": "What is the best next point?", "o": ["Antibodies from the vaccine stay in the blood and kill any live virus that arrives.", "If live virus enters later, white blood cells quickly make the correct antibodies.", "The vaccine releases antibiotics, which kill any live polio virus that enters later.", "The vaccine blocks the virus from entering the body, so no response is needed."], "a": 1, "f": ["A vaccine does not contain antibodies. The child's own white blood cells made them, and a fast new response is what protects.", "Right. Antibodies are made quickly if the live virus enters later, so the virus is destroyed before it causes illness.", "Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, and vaccines do not release them. This mixes up antibiotics with antibodies.", "The virus can still enter the body. Protection comes from the fast antibody response, not from blocking entry."]}, "trap": "Do not write that unvaccinated people become immune through herd immunity. They stay non-immune but are less likely to meet the pathogen. Also, vaccines do not contain antibodies: your white blood cells make them.", "recap": "Vaccines let white blood cells quickly make the correct antibodies later; when most people are vaccinated, the pathogen spreads less, protecting unvaccinated people too."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.3.1.8", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to explain why an antibiotic or painkiller suits an illness, or to describe how a new drug is tested before patients can use it.", "hook": {"q": "A company has just made a new drug. What should be checked first, before it is given to people?", "o": ["How much it will cost to make each tablet", "What colour and shape the tablet should be", "Whether it is toxic, meaning harmful to the body", "How many shops are willing to sell the drug"], "a": 2, "f": ["Cost matters to the company, but it is not the first safety check. A harmful drug must be spotted before anything else.", "Appearance has no effect on safety. The first checks are about harm and whether the drug works.", "Right. Toxicity is checked first, in the lab and then in animals, so that people are not put at risk.", "Sales are a business matter. Safety testing comes before any thought of selling the drug."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Antibiotics and painkillers", "b": "**Antibiotics** such as penicillin kill **bacteria**. They do not work on viruses, which live inside your cells. **Painkillers** only ease symptoms such as pain. They do not kill any pathogen. Each antibiotic works only on certain types of bacteria."}, {"h": "Where new drugs come from", "b": "Many drugs were first found in **plants** and **microorganisms**. Digitalis, a heart drug, comes from foxglove. Aspirin was first taken from willow. Penicillin comes from a mould. Today chemists often make (**synthesise**) drugs in a lab, but the starting point may still be a plant chemical."}, {"h": "Testing in the lab", "b": "New drugs are tested for **toxicity** (is it harmful?), **efficacy** (does it work?) and the right dose. This is called preclinical testing. It uses cells and tissues first, then live animals, because a whole body works differently from cells. Only then do **clinical trials** on people begin."}, {"h": "Clinical trials on people", "b": "Trials start with healthy volunteers on a very low dose to check safety. If the drug is safe, patients try different doses to find the **optimum dose**. In a **double blind trial**, some patients get a **placebo**. Neither patients nor doctors know who has what until the end."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how a new drug is tested before it is used to treat patients. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "The drug is first tested in a laboratory on human cells and tissues.", "why": "This checks toxicity and efficacy without risking a living animal or person."}, {"do": "It is then tested on live animals.", "why": "Cells cannot show how a whole body reacts to the drug."}, {"do": "Clinical trials begin with healthy volunteers given a very low dose.", "why": "This checks that the drug is safe for humans before higher doses are used."}, {"do": "If it is safe, patients are given different doses to find the optimum dose.", "why": "The best dose works well without causing harm."}, {"do": "In a double blind trial, some patients get a placebo and nobody knows who has which.", "why": "This makes the comparison fair, so hopes about the drug cannot change the results."}], "answer": "The drug is tested on cells, tissues and live animals for toxicity and efficacy. Clinical trials then start with healthy volunteers on a very low dose. Patients are used to find the optimum dose. Double blind trials with a placebo keep the results fair."}, "selfx": {"q": "In the last step, why are neither the patients nor the doctors told who has the placebo?", "o": ["So the trial is quicker, as nobody needs to check the results", "So expectations cannot bias what patients or doctors report", "So the placebo can act as a real drug and kill bacteria", "So the drug is shown to be safe for healthy volunteers"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Being double blind does not save time. It makes the results fairer. The results are still checked carefully.", "Right. If people knew who had the drug, their expectations could change how they feel or how results are judged. Hiding it removes that bias.", "Wrong. A placebo contains no active drug, so it cannot kill bacteria. It is there as a comparison.", "Wrong. Safety in healthy volunteers is checked earlier, at a very low dose. Hiding the placebo is about fair results."]}, "faded": {"task": "A doctor gives a patient with a bacterial chest infection both an antibiotic and a painkiller. Explain why.", "given": ["The antibiotic kills the bacteria that cause the infection, so it treats the cause."], "q": "What is the next point to make about the painkiller?", "o": ["The painkiller kills the bacteria more slowly, so it backs up the antibiotic", "The painkiller stops the bacteria from becoming resistant to the antibiotic", "The painkiller removes any viruses that the antibiotic cannot kill", "The painkiller eases the pain, but does not kill the bacteria"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Painkillers do not kill pathogens at all, fast or slow. This mixes up painkillers with antibiotics.", "Wrong. Painkillers have no effect on resistance. Resistance comes from bacteria changing, helped by antibiotic overuse.", "Wrong. Painkillers do not kill viruses either. The infection here is bacterial anyway.", "Right. Painkillers ease symptoms such as pain. They do not kill bacteria, so the antibiotic is still needed to treat the cause."]}, "trap": "Saying antibiotics treat any infection, or that painkillers cure disease. Antibiotics kill bacteria only. Painkillers ease symptoms only. Always say which one you mean and what it acts on.", "recap": "Antibiotics kill bacteria, painkillers ease symptoms, and a new drug is tested for toxicity, efficacy and dose in the lab, then in trials."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.3.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Higher tier papers ask you to describe how monoclonal antibodies are made, and to explain how they target particular cells in tests and treatments.", "hook": {"q": "You need a large supply of one exact antibody. A lymphocyte makes it but cannot divide much in a dish. What would help most?", "o": ["Inject many different antigens and collect all the antibodies made", "Combine it with a cell that divides fast, so one cell gives many identical cells", "Mix in lymphocytes from many animals, each making its own antibody", "Warm the lymphocyte in the dish so it makes its antibody faster than usual"], "a": 1, "f": ["This gives a mixture of many antibodies, not one exact type. You want identical copies, which needs a single clone of cells.", "Right. A fast-dividing partner cell lets one antibody-making cell form a large clone, and every cell in it makes the same antibody.", "Different lymphocytes make different antibodies. That gives a mixture, not a supply of one exact antibody.", "Warming does not turn one cell into many. Too much heat denatures proteins and kills cells, so the supply would not grow."], "x": null}, "chunks": [{"h": "What they are", "b": "**Monoclonal antibodies** are identical antibodies. They are all made by a single **clone** of cells. Each has a binding site that is complementary to one **specific** antigen. It will not bind to other antigens."}, {"h": "Making them", "b": "Inject a mouse with the antigen. Its **lymphocytes** make the matching antibody. Fuse one of these lymphocytes with a tumour cell. This makes a **hybridoma** cell, which can both divide and make the antibody. Clone a single hybridoma, then collect and purify the antibody."}, {"h": "Tests and research", "b": "Attach a **fluorescent dye** to the antibodies. They bind only to their target molecule, so the dye shows where it is in a cell or tissue. They also detect hormones or pathogens in blood samples, and are used in **pregnancy tests**. This helps **diagnosis**."}, {"h": "Treating disease", "b": "A drug, a radioactive substance or a chemical that stops cell division can be attached to the antibody. In cancer, the antibody binds to antigens on **cancer cells**. The attached substance acts there, with less harm to healthy cells. They cause more **side effects** than expected, so they are not yet as widely used as hoped."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how monoclonal antibodies against a cancer antigen are made in the lab. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Inject a mouse with the cancer antigen.", "why": "This stimulates the mouse's lymphocytes to make antibodies that fit that specific antigen."}, {"do": "Collect lymphocytes from the mouse that make the wanted antibody.", "why": "Only these cells make the one antibody type needed."}, {"do": "Fuse a lymphocyte with a tumour cell to make a hybridoma cell.", "why": "The lymphocyte gives the specific antibody. The tumour cell gives rapid, repeated division."}, {"do": "Culture the hybridoma cells so they divide into a clone, then collect and purify the antibody they make.", "why": "Every cell in the clone makes identical antibody, so large amounts can be harvested."}], "answer": "A mouse is injected with the antigen, so its lymphocytes make antibody. A lymphocyte is fused with a tumour cell to make a hybridoma. The hybridoma divides to form a clone making identical antibodies, which are collected and purified."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the mouse injected with the cancer antigen in the first step?", "o": ["So the mouse's tumour cells can grow, ready to fuse with lymphocytes later", "So the mouse becomes immune to the cancer and its lymphocytes divide for ever", "So the mouse makes antibodies that fit every antigen, not just the wanted one", "So its lymphocytes make antibodies with a binding site that fits that antigen"], "a": 3, "f": ["The tumour cells used for fusion are not grown inside the mouse. The antigen's job is to trigger antibody production.", "Immunity does not make lymphocytes divide for ever. Fast division comes later, from the tumour cell in the hybridoma.", "Antibodies are specific. Injecting one antigen makes lymphocytes that produce antibodies for that antigen, not for every antigen.", "Right. The antigen stimulates lymphocytes to make antibody with a complementary binding site, which is the antibody you want to copy."], "x": null}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a monoclonal antibody can deliver a radioactive substance to a tumour.", "given": ["Make a monoclonal antibody whose binding site is complementary to an antigen found on the tumour cells.", "Attach the radioactive substance to the antibody."], "q": "What is the next point in the explanation?", "o": ["After injection, it binds to antigens on every body cell, so the radiation spreads evenly", "After injection, the radioactive substance finds the tumour itself and the antibody protects it", "After injection, it binds only to the tumour's antigen, so radiation reaches those cells", "After injection, the antibody destroys the tumour cells itself, so the radiation is not needed"], "a": 2, "f": ["This ignores specificity. The antibody fits only the tumour antigen, so it does not bind to every body cell.", "The antibody does the targeting. The radioactive substance has no way of finding the tumour on its own.", "Right. The antibody is specific, so it carries the radioactive substance to the tumour cells and the radiation acts there, harming fewer healthy cells.", "The antibody is only a carrier here. The attached radioactive substance is what damages the tumour cells."], "x": null}, "trap": "Saying the tumour cell makes the antibody or chooses the target. The lymphocyte makes the specific antibody. The tumour cell only gives fast division. Name both cells and their jobs in your answer.", "recap": "Monoclonal antibodies are identical copies from one hybridoma clone; each binds one specific antigen, so it can carry a dye or drug there."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.3.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Short-answer questions often ask you to link a symptom to its cause or name a type of plant defence, which are quick marks if you know the terms.", "hook": {"q": "A plant is grown for weeks in pure water with no minerals added. It stays small and its leaves turn yellow. What is the most likely reason?", "o": ["It is getting too much water, so its roots cannot work", "Its leaves have lost their waxy cuticle", "Its roots cannot take in mineral ions that it needs", "It has been attacked by a poison made by another plant"], "a": 2, "f": ["Too much water can harm roots, but pure water with no minerals points to a missing nutrient, not an excess of water.", "The cuticle protects against microbes and water loss. It does not explain small growth and yellow leaves in this situation.", "Right. Plants need mineral ions from the soil or water. Without them, growth is poor and leaves lose their green colour.", "Nothing in the question suggests a poison. The only thing missing is minerals."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Spotting plant disease", "b": "Look for **symptoms**: stunted growth, spots on leaves, areas of decay, abnormal growths, malformed stems or leaves, discolouration, or pests on the plant. To identify the cause, use a gardening manual or website, send a sample to a **laboratory**, or use a testing kit with **monoclonal antibodies**."}, {"h": "Mineral ion shortages", "b": "Roots take up mineral ions from the soil. **Nitrate** ions are needed to make proteins, so a shortage causes stunted growth. **Magnesium** ions are needed to make chlorophyll, so a shortage causes **chlorosis**: yellow leaves."}, {"h": "Physical and mechanical", "b": "**Physical** defences are barriers that block microbes: cellulose cell walls, the waxy cuticle on leaves, and bark made of dead cells. **Mechanical** defences stop animals feeding: thorns, hairs, and leaves that droop or curl when touched."}, {"h": "Chemical defences", "b": "**Chemical** defences are substances the plant makes. Some are **antibacterial chemicals** that kill microbes. Others are **poisons** that deter herbivores, the animals that eat plants. A thorn or a waxy layer is not a chemical defence."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how plants defend themselves against microbes and against animals that eat them.", "steps": [{"do": "Plants have physical barriers: cellulose cell walls and a waxy cuticle on the leaves.", "why": "These block microbes from getting in. Barriers count as physical defences."}, {"do": "Trees have bark, which is layers of dead cells on the stem.", "why": "Bark is another physical barrier that microbes find hard to get through."}, {"do": "Mechanical adaptations such as thorns and hairs stop animals eating the plant. Some leaves droop or curl when touched.", "why": "Mechanical defences are structures or movements that deter animals."}, {"do": "Plants also make chemicals. Antibacterial chemicals kill microbes and poisons deter herbivores.", "why": "Chemical defences are substances the plant itself makes."}], "answer": "Physical barriers such as cell walls, a waxy cuticle and bark block microbes. Thorns, hairs and drooping leaves are mechanical adaptations against animals. Plants also make chemicals: antibacterial substances kill microbes and poisons deter herbivores."}, "selfx": {"q": "In the worked example, why is the waxy cuticle not described as a chemical defence?", "o": ["It acts as a barrier, but chemical defences are poisons or antibacterial substances", "It is made of cellulose, which is not a chemical", "It only protects the plant from animals, not from microbes", "It is made by the plant, but chemical defences come from the soil"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. The cuticle works by blocking entry. Chemical defences are substances the plant makes that kill microbes or deter herbivores.", "Cellulose is the material of cell walls, not the cuticle. Everything is made of chemicals, so that is not the reason.", "The cuticle helps against microbes, not just animals. The mistake here is mixing up who the defence acts against.", "Chemical defences are made by the plant itself, not taken from the soil."]}, "faded": {"task": "A tomato plant has yellow leaves. Explain how a shortage of magnesium ions in the soil causes this.", "given": ["Plants take up magnesium ions from the soil through their roots.", "Magnesium ions are needed to make chlorophyll."], "q": "Which is the next point in the explanation?", "o": ["Less protein is made, so the plant is stunted and the leaves turn yellow", "With less chlorophyll the leaves turn yellow (chlorosis) and absorb less light", "The cell walls become weak, so microbes get in and cause yellow spots", "The yellow colour is a poison that stops herbivores eating the leaves"], "a": 1, "f": ["That is the nitrate link. Nitrate is needed for protein and growth. Magnesium is needed for chlorophyll.", "Right. Chlorophyll makes leaves green and absorbs light for photosynthesis. Less of it means chlorosis.", "Magnesium is not used for cell walls here, and this describes an infection, not a mineral shortage.", "Yellow leaves from a shortage are not a defence. Chemical defences are substances such as poisons."]}, "trap": "Counting thorns, a waxy cuticle or bark as chemical defences. Chemical means a substance the plant makes, such as a poison or an antibacterial chemical. Barriers are physical. Thorns are mechanical.", "recap": "Match each symptom to its cause, and sort defences correctly: barriers are physical, thorns and drooping are mechanical, poisons and antibacterials are chemical."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.4.1.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exams often ask for the photosynthesis equation and how plants use glucose, including the nitrate ions needed to make proteins.", "hook": {"q": "Glucose contains only carbon, hydrogen and oxygen. Which of these could a plant NOT make from glucose alone?", "o": ["Starch, for storing energy", "Cellulose, for cell walls", "Protein, for growth and repair", "Fats and oils, for storage"], "a": 2, "f": ["Starch is made only from glucose, so it contains carbon, hydrogen and oxygen. Glucose alone is enough.", "Cellulose is a long chain of glucose units. It needs nothing else.", "Right. Proteins contain nitrogen, and glucose has none. The plant needs another source of nitrogen.", "Fats and oils contain only carbon, hydrogen and oxygen, so glucose alone can supply what is needed."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The photosynthesis reaction", "b": "Plants take in **carbon dioxide** and water. They make **glucose** and oxygen.\n\ncarbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen\n\n6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂\n\nThe reaction happens in **chloroplasts**. Chlorophyll absorbs light energy to drive it."}, {"h": "Energy is taken in", "b": "Photosynthesis is an **endothermic** reaction. It takes in energy from the surroundings.\n\nThe energy comes from **light**. It is absorbed by chlorophyll in the chloroplasts. Some of it ends up stored in the glucose that the plant makes."}, {"h": "Uses of glucose", "b": "Plants use glucose for **respiration**, which releases energy for their cells. They also change it into other substances. **Cellulose** strengthens cell walls. **Starch** is an insoluble store. Fats and oils are stored too, for example in seeds."}, {"h": "Making proteins", "b": "Proteins contain **nitrogen**. Glucose has none. Plants take in **nitrate ions** from the soil through their roots.\n\nGlucose combines with nitrate ions to make **amino acids**. Amino acids are joined together to make proteins."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how a plant makes glucose, and explain how it uses the glucose to grow.", "steps": [{"do": "The plant takes in carbon dioxide and water. Chlorophyll in the chloroplasts absorbs light energy.", "why": "These are the reactants and the energy source, so the answer starts with what goes in."}, {"do": "They react to make glucose and oxygen. The reaction is endothermic because it takes in light energy.", "why": "This gives the reaction and the products, with the correct term for the energy change."}, {"do": "Some glucose is used in respiration, which releases energy for the cell's processes.", "why": "This is the first use of glucose, and it links growth to energy."}, {"do": "Some glucose is changed into cellulose, which strengthens the cell walls of new cells.", "why": "Growth means making new cells, and each one needs a cell wall."}, {"do": "Glucose combines with nitrate ions from the soil to make amino acids, which are joined to make proteins.", "why": "Proteins need nitrogen. Nitrate ions supply it, because glucose has none."}], "answer": "Leaves take in carbon dioxide and water and use light energy absorbed by chlorophyll to make glucose and oxygen. Glucose is used in respiration for energy and to make cellulose for cell walls. With nitrate ions it makes amino acids, then proteins."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the last step of the answer mention nitrate ions?", "o": ["Glucose contains no nitrogen, but proteins need it", "Nitrate ions give the energy for making amino acids", "Nitrate ions are a reactant in photosynthesis", "Nitrate ions are needed to turn glucose into cellulose"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Amino acids and proteins contain nitrogen. Glucose has none, so nitrate ions from the soil supply it.", "Nitrate ions do not supply energy. That comes from respiration. Nitrate ions supply nitrogen, a building material for amino acids.", "The reactants in photosynthesis are carbon dioxide and water. Nitrate ions are used later, in making amino acids.", "Cellulose is made from glucose alone. Nitrate ions are needed only for substances containing nitrogen, such as proteins."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a potato plant uses the glucose it makes to store energy and to build strong cell walls.", "given": ["Photosynthesis in the leaves makes glucose from carbon dioxide and water.", "Some glucose is changed to starch, an insoluble store of energy in the tuber."], "q": "What is the next point in the answer?", "o": ["Some glucose is made into cellulose by respiration, which releases energy.", "Some glucose is changed to starch, which is also used to build the cell walls.", "Glucose alone is turned into amino acids, which strengthen the cell walls.", "Some glucose is changed to cellulose, which strengthens the cell walls."], "a": 3, "f": ["Respiration releases energy from glucose. It does not make cellulose. Mixing up the two uses of glucose loses the mark.", "Starch is a storage substance. The substance that strengthens cell walls is cellulose, so this repeats the last point wrongly.", "Amino acids need nitrate ions as well as glucose, and they make proteins. They are not what strengthens cell walls.", "Right. Cellulose is made from glucose and strengthens the cell walls, which answers the second half of the question."]}, "trap": "Forgetting that proteins need nitrogen. Glucose alone has none. Always say that nitrate ions from the soil are combined with glucose to make amino acids, which are joined to make proteins.", "recap": "Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen, using light energy; glucose is used for respiration, cellulose, starch, fats and oils, and with nitrate ions for proteins."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.4.1.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to read limiting-factor graphs and explain why the rate of photosynthesis rises, levels off or falls, often using the pondweed practical.", "hook": {"q": "A plant in bright light is given even brighter light, but its rate of photosynthesis stays the same. What is the best explanation?", "o": ["The plant has stopped needing light to photosynthesise", "The extra light has damaged the chlorophyll straight away", "Something else, such as carbon dioxide, is now in short supply", "The plant has stopped making glucose and started respiring"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Light is still the energy source for photosynthesis. The rate stays the same because something else is holding it back.", "Wrong. Chlorophyll is not damaged just by brighter light in this situation. The flat rate points to another factor running short.", "Right. If more light changes nothing, light is not the problem any more. Another factor, such as carbon dioxide or temperature, is now limiting.", "Wrong. Plants respire all the time, but respiration does not replace photosynthesis. A flat rate means another factor is limiting."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What a limiting factor is", "b": "Photosynthesis needs **light**, **carbon dioxide** and a suitable temperature. Water is also needed, but it is rarely short. The factor in shortest supply is the **limiting factor**. It sets the rate. Raising any other factor changes nothing until the limiting one is raised."}, {"h": "Reading a light graph", "b": "The graph shows rate against light intensity at three carbon dioxide levels. Drag the slider. On the rising part, light is the **limiting factor**: more light gives a faster rate. On the flat part (the **plateau**), more light changes nothing, so carbon dioxide or temperature is limiting. More carbon dioxide raises the plateau. Watch the gain readout.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 14], "xstep": 2, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "light intensity / arbitrary units", "ylabel": "rate of photosynthesis / arbitrary units", "lines": [{"label": "low CO₂", "c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [2, 4], [10, 4]]}, {"label": "medium CO₂", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [4, 8], [10, 8]]}, {"label": "high CO₂", "c": 2, "pts": [[0, 0], [6, 12], [10, 12]]}], "points": [], "frames": [{"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [0.0, 0.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [0.0, 0.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["Light intensity = 0", "Rate (medium CO₂) = 0", "Gain from 1 more light unit = 2"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[2.0, 0.0], [2.0, 4.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [2.0, 4.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["Light intensity = 2", "Rate (medium CO₂) = 4", "Gain from 1 more light unit = 2"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[4.0, 0.0], [4.0, 8.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [4.0, 8.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["Light intensity = 4", "Rate (medium CO₂) = 8", "Gain from 1 more light unit = 0"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[6.0, 0.0], [6.0, 8.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [6.0, 8.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["Light intensity = 6", "Rate (medium CO₂) = 8", "Gain from 1 more light unit = 0"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[8.0, 0.0], [8.0, 8.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [8.0, 8.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["Light intensity = 8", "Rate (medium CO₂) = 8", "Gain from 1 more light unit = 0"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[10.0, 0.0], [10.0, 8.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [10.0, 8.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["Light intensity = 10", "Rate (medium CO₂) = 8", "Gain from 1 more light unit = 0"]}], "ctl": {"type": "slider", "label": "s", "vals": ["0", "2", "4", "6", "8", "10"], "start": 3}}}, {"h": "Temperature and carbon dioxide", "b": "More **carbon dioxide** raises the rate until another factor limits it. Warmer conditions speed up the enzymes, so the rate rises. Above the **optimum** temperature the enzymes **denature** and the rate falls. At low temperatures, temperature can be the limiting factor."}, {"h": "Required practical 6", "b": "Put **pondweed** in water, with a lamp at a measured **distance**. Count the oxygen bubbles per minute. Move the lamp to change the light intensity. Keep temperature, carbon dioxide and the pondweed the same. Repeat each distance and find a mean."}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows rate of photosynthesis against light intensity at 20 °C. The line rises, then levels off. Explain the shape of the graph. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "On the rising part, as light intensity increases, the rate of photosynthesis increases.", "why": "Light is the limiting factor here. It supplies the energy for photosynthesis."}, {"do": "On the flat part, the rate stops increasing even though light intensity keeps rising.", "why": "This describes the plateau using the graph evidence."}, {"do": "So light is no longer the limiting factor on the plateau.", "why": "More light makes no difference, so light is not in short supply."}, {"do": "Another factor is now limiting, such as carbon dioxide concentration or temperature.", "why": "These are the other factors that can limit the rate."}, {"do": "Increasing that factor would raise the rate, and the plateau would be higher.", "why": "This shows you understand that the limiting factor controls the rate."}], "answer": "The rate rises because light is the limiting factor. It levels off because light is no longer limiting. Carbon dioxide concentration or temperature is now in shortest supply and limits the rate.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "xstep": 2, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "light intensity / arbitrary units", "ylabel": "rate of photosynthesis / arbitrary units", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [4, 8], [10, 8]], "label": "20 °C", "c": 0}], "xnums": false, "ynums": false}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say light is no longer the limiting factor on the plateau?", "o": ["Because the plant cannot absorb any more light at all", "Because the light has all been used up by the plant", "Because more light gives no extra rate, so another factor must be limiting", "Because the plateau shows photosynthesis has stopped"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. This guesses a cause that the graph does not show. The evidence is that the rate does not change when light increases.", "Wrong. Light is not used up like a reactant. The point is that raising it no longer changes the rate.", "Right. The rate does not respond to more light. So light is not in shortest supply, and another factor is limiting.", "Wrong. The rate on the plateau is still above zero. Photosynthesis is carrying on, just at a steady rate."]}, "faded": {"task": "The graph shows a grower raising the carbon dioxide concentration around tomato plants in bright light at a constant warm temperature. The rate rises, then levels off. Explain this.", "given": ["On the rising part, carbon dioxide concentration is the limiting factor, so more carbon dioxide gives a faster rate.", "On the flat part, more carbon dioxide no longer increases the rate."], "q": "What is the next point to make?", "o": ["So the plant has stopped photosynthesising completely", "So carbon dioxide is still limiting, but only at a lower level", "So the enzymes must have denatured in the warm conditions", "So carbon dioxide is no longer limiting; light or temperature now is"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. The rate on the plateau is still above zero. It has stopped increasing, not stopped.", "Wrong. If carbon dioxide were still limiting, more of it would raise the rate. A flat line means it is no longer limiting.", "Wrong. Denaturing makes the rate fall, not level off. The temperature was constant and warm, so nothing suggests this.", "Right. More carbon dioxide makes no difference, so it is not limiting. Light intensity or temperature is now in shortest supply."], "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "xstep": 2, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "CO₂ concentration / arbitrary units", "ylabel": "rate of photosynthesis / arbitrary units", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [4, 8], [10, 8]], "label": "bright light", "c": 0}], "xnums": false, "ynums": false}}, "trap": "Saying light is still the limiting factor on the plateau. Light limits only on the rising part of the graph. On the flat part, name another factor: carbon dioxide concentration or temperature.", "recap": "The factor in shortest supply limits the rate, so on a plateau the factor on the x-axis is no longer limiting."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.4.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 1 often asks you to explain what happens in the body during and after exercise, and you need respiration to answer it.", "hook": {"q": "After a sprint you keep panting for a minute or two. What is the most likely reason?", "o": ["Your lungs have been damaged by the hard exercise", "Your body needs extra oxygen to deal with something built up in your muscles", "Your body is making new oxygen to replace what it used up", "Your muscles have run out of glucose and cannot get any more"], "a": 1, "f": ["Exercise does not damage healthy lungs. Panting is the body's normal response, so this is not the reason.", "Right. Hard exercise leaves a substance in the muscles that needs oxygen to remove it. Panting supplies that oxygen.", "The body cannot make oxygen. It only takes it in from the air and carries it in the blood.", "Glucose is carried in the blood, so supply continues. Panting is about oxygen, not a lack of glucose."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Aerobic respiration", "b": "**Aerobic respiration** uses oxygen. It happens in the **mitochondria** of cells and releases energy from glucose.\n\nglucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water\n\nIt is the main way cells release energy. It releases much more energy per glucose than anaerobic respiration."}, {"h": "Anaerobic respiration", "b": "**Anaerobic respiration** happens without oxygen. In human muscles: glucose → **lactic acid**.\n\nGlucose is only partly broken down, so much less energy is released.\n\nYeast and plant cells make ethanol and carbon dioxide instead: glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide."}, {"h": "Response to exercise", "b": "Exercising muscles respire faster. They need more oxygen and glucose, and must lose more carbon dioxide.\n\nSo your **heart rate**, **breathing rate** and breath volume increase. Blood then carries more oxygen and glucose to the muscles and takes carbon dioxide away."}, {"h": "Oxygen debt", "b": "If oxygen cannot reach the muscles fast enough, they also respire anaerobically. **Lactic acid** builds up and causes muscle fatigue.\n\nAfter exercise, extra oxygen is needed to react with the lactic acid. This is the **oxygen debt**: on the graph, the uptake above resting level after exercise ends. Blood flow removes the lactic acid. Press Next step.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "steps", "x": [0, 12], "y": [0, 3.5], "xstep": 2, "ystep": 0.5, "xlabel": "time / min", "ylabel": "oxygen uptake / dm³ per min", "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "lines": [{"dash": true, "label": "resting", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0.5], [12, 0.5]]}, {"label": "oxygen uptake", "c": 0, "step": 1, "pts": [[0, 0.5], [2, 0.5], [3, 2], [4, 2.5], [6, 1.5], [8, 0.8], [10, 0.5], [12, 0.5]]}, {"label": "oxygen needed", "c": 3, "step": 2, "pts": [[0, 0.5], [2, 0.5], [2, 3], [4, 3]]}, {"label": "oxygen shortfall", "c": 3, "step": 3, "pts": [[4, 2.5], [4, 3], [3.5, 3], [3.5, 2.25], [3, 2], [3, 3], [2.5, 3], [2.5, 1.25], [2, 0.5], [2, 3]]}, {"label": "oxygen debt", "c": 2, "step": 4, "pts": [[4, 0.5], [4, 2.5], [5, 2], [5, 0.5], [6, 0.5], [6, 1.5], [7, 1.15], [7, 0.5], [8, 0.5], [8, 0.8], [9, 0.65], [9, 0.5], [10, 0.5]]}], "points": [{"at": [2, 0.5], "label": "Exercise starts", "step": 1}, {"at": [4, 2.5], "label": "Exercise ends", "step": 1}, {"at": [10, 0.5], "label": "Debt repaid", "step": 5}], "steps": ["Oxygen uptake: resting at 0.5. It rises after exercise starts at 2 min and peaks at 2.5 when exercise ends at 4 min. Then it falls.", "The oxygen the muscles need jumps up as soon as exercise starts (drawn as an example).", "Uptake lags behind the need. The gap is oxygen not delivered in time, so muscles also respire anaerobically and make lactic acid.", "After exercise, uptake stays above resting (shaded). This extra oxygen reacts with the lactic acid: it is the oxygen debt.", "Uptake is back to 0.5 at 10 min, so the debt is repaid. That is why you keep breathing hard after you stop."]}}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a runner's breathing rate and heart rate stay high for a few minutes after a sprint. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "During the sprint, the muscles respire very fast. Oxygen cannot be delivered quickly enough, so the muscles also respire anaerobically.", "why": "This explains where the problem starts: oxygen supply is too slow for the demand."}, {"do": "Anaerobic respiration makes lactic acid, which builds up in the muscles.", "why": "Glucose is only partly broken down, and lactic acid is the product in human muscles."}, {"do": "After the sprint, extra oxygen is needed to react with the lactic acid. This is the oxygen debt.", "why": "This gives the reason the body still needs extra oxygen once the sprint has stopped."}, {"do": "Breathing rate and heart rate stay high so that more oxygenated blood reaches the muscles, and blood flow removes the lactic acid.", "why": "This links the high rates to the job they do, which earns the final mark."}], "answer": "Muscles used anaerobic respiration in the sprint and made lactic acid. Extra oxygen is needed to react with it (oxygen debt). Fast breathing and heart rate supply oxygenated blood to the muscles and remove the lactic acid."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say lactic acid builds up during the sprint?", "o": ["Oxygen arrives too slowly, so glucose is only partly broken down in the muscles", "Aerobic respiration makes lactic acid when it happens too fast", "Fast breathing forces the muscles to make lactic acid", "The muscles stop respiring, and lactic acid is what is left over"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. When oxygen supply cannot keep up, muscles also respire anaerobically. Glucose is only partly broken down, and the product is lactic acid.", "Aerobic respiration makes carbon dioxide and water, whatever its speed. Lactic acid only comes from anaerobic respiration.", "Breathing does not make anything in the muscles. It only supplies oxygen. Lactic acid is made by respiration in muscle cells.", "The muscles keep respiring. They respire both aerobically and anaerobically. Lactic acid is a product of anaerobic respiration, not a leftover from stopping."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a cyclist's heart rate increases during a long ride. (3 marks)", "given": ["Exercising muscles respire faster.", "So they need more oxygen and glucose, and produce more carbon dioxide."], "q": "What is the next point in the answer?", "o": ["The heart beats faster to release energy from glucose for the muscles", "The heart beats faster to make oxygen that the muscles can use", "The heart beats faster, so blood delivers more oxygen and glucose and removes more CO₂", "The heart beats faster so that the muscles can switch to anaerobic respiration"], "a": 2, "f": ["Energy is released by respiration in cells, not by the heart. The heart only pumps blood to supply and remove substances.", "The body cannot make oxygen. Blood carries oxygen taken in by the lungs, and a faster heart rate carries it round faster.", "Right. A faster heart rate moves more blood through the muscles. This delivers oxygen and glucose and takes carbon dioxide away.", "The aim is to supply enough oxygen so aerobic respiration can continue. Anaerobic respiration starts only when the oxygen supply cannot keep up."]}, "trap": "Saying breathing or the heart makes the energy. Energy is released by respiration in cells. Breathing and blood flow only supply oxygen and glucose and remove carbon dioxide. Name respiration as the process that releases energy.", "recap": "Muscles respire faster in exercise; if oxygen is short they make lactic acid, and the oxygen debt is repaid afterwards."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.4.2.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask what a molecule is built from or what happens to excess protein, so these are quick recall marks.", "hook": {"q": "Building a large molecule from small ones needs energy. Which process in cells transfers that energy?", "o": ["Osmosis, the movement of water across a membrane", "Respiration, which transfers energy from glucose", "Digestion, which breaks food into small molecules", "Diffusion, the spreading out of particles"], "a": 1, "f": ["Osmosis moves water. It does not transfer energy for building molecules.", "Right. Respiration transfers energy, and cells use it to power the reactions that build molecules.", "Digestion breaks food down in the gut. It does not supply energy for building molecules inside cells.", "Diffusion is passive movement of particles. It does not transfer energy for building molecules."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What metabolism means", "b": "**Metabolism** is the sum of all the reactions in a cell or in the whole body. These reactions are controlled by **enzymes**. Many of them need energy. This energy is transferred by **respiration**."}, {"h": "Building carbohydrates", "b": "Cells join many **glucose** molecules to make larger carbohydrates. Plants make starch as a store and **cellulose** for cell walls. Animals make **glycogen** as a store. All of this uses energy from respiration."}, {"h": "Building lipids and proteins", "b": "A **lipid** is made from one glycerol molecule and three fatty acid molecules. Plants use glucose and **nitrate ions** to make **amino acids**. Cells then join amino acids together to make proteins."}, {"h": "Breaking down molecules", "b": "Metabolism also breaks molecules down. Respiration breaks down glucose to transfer energy. The body cannot store spare protein. **Excess protein** is broken down and **urea** is made. Urea is removed from the body by excretion."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a cell makes a lipid molecule, and why respiration is needed. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "A lipid is made from small molecules: one glycerol molecule and three fatty acid molecules.", "why": "This names the correct building blocks and the correct numbers. Both are needed for the mark."}, {"do": "These molecules are joined together in a chemical reaction to make one larger lipid molecule.", "why": "This shows that a big molecule is being built up from small ones."}, {"do": "The reaction is controlled by an enzyme.", "why": "Metabolic reactions in cells are enzyme-controlled. Examiners look for this link."}, {"do": "The energy needed to join the molecules is transferred by respiration.", "why": "This links building molecules to the energy from respiration, as the specification does."}, {"do": "All the reactions in the cell, including this one, are called metabolism.", "why": "This uses the key term correctly and shows what the topic is about."}], "answer": "A lipid is built from one glycerol and three fatty acid molecules. They are joined in an enzyme-controlled reaction. Respiration transfers the energy for this. These reactions together are metabolism."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention respiration when explaining how a lipid is made?", "o": ["Respiration is the enzyme that joins the molecules together", "Respiration makes the three fatty acids from glycerol", "Respiration transfers the energy needed for the joining reaction", "Respiration breaks the lipid down as soon as it forms"], "a": 2, "f": ["Respiration is a process, not an enzyme. The enzyme controls the reaction. Respiration supplies the energy.", "Fatty acids are not made from glycerol in respiration. Respiration transfers energy from glucose. It does not make the building blocks.", "Right. Building a lipid needs energy. Respiration transfers it, so it is named as the energy source.", "Respiration is named because it supplies energy for building. It does not undo the lipid that has just been made."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a plant cell makes proteins using glucose and nitrate ions from the soil.", "given": ["Step 1: The plant uses glucose and nitrate ions to make amino acids."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["The amino acids are joined together in long chains to make protein molecules.", "The amino acids are broken down to make urea, which is excreted.", "The amino acids are joined to one glycerol molecule to make a protein.", "The amino acids are converted to starch, which is stored in the cell."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Amino acids are joined together to make proteins. This is a building reaction in metabolism, powered by respiration.", "Urea is made when excess protein is broken down. That is the reverse direction and does not make proteins.", "Glycerol is used with fatty acids to make lipids. Proteins are built only from amino acids.", "Starch is made by joining glucose molecules. Amino acids build proteins, not starch."]}, "trap": "Mixing up building blocks. A lipid is 1 glycerol + 3 fatty acids, not 3 glycerol + 1 fatty acid. Amino acids build proteins. Glucose builds starch, glycogen and cellulose.", "recap": "Metabolism is all the reactions in cells; respiration transfers energy to build molecules such as lipids (1 glycerol + 3 fatty acids) and proteins."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.5.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 often asks you to describe the pathway of a reflex, and to handle reaction-time results from required practical 7.", "hook": {"q": "You touch a hot pan and your hand jerks away before you feel any pain. What is the best explanation?", "o": ["Your hand muscles sensed the heat themselves and moved away", "Your brain weighed up the danger and chose to move your hand", "The response happened automatically, without you having to think", "Hormones in your blood carried a message to your arm muscles"], "a": 2, "f": ["Muscles are effectors. They carry out a response but do not detect heat. Receptors in the skin do that.", "A decision made by the brain is a conscious response. That would be too slow to stop the burn.", "Right. A reflex is automatic and rapid, so it happens before the brain has time to make a conscious choice.", "Hormones travel in the blood, which is much slower. Reflexes use fast electrical impulses in neurones."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The signal pathway", "b": "A stimulus is a change in your surroundings. **Receptors** detect it and make electrical impulses. A sensory neurone carries them to the **central nervous system** (CNS: brain and spinal cord). A motor neurone carries impulses out to an **effector**, a muscle or gland, which produces the response."}, {"h": "Synapses", "b": "Neurones do not touch. A **synapse** is a tiny gap between two neurones. The electrical impulse cannot cross it. Instead, the first neurone releases a **chemical**. It **diffuses** across the gap and sets off a new impulse in the next neurone."}, {"h": "Reflex actions", "b": "A **reflex** is automatic and rapid, and does not involve conscious thought. It follows a **reflex arc**: receptor, sensory neurone, **relay neurone** in the CNS, motor neurone, effector. The brain does not have to decide first. Reflexes protect you from harm."}, {"h": "Measuring reaction time", "b": "In the ruler drop test, a partner drops a ruler without warning and you catch it. The shorter the distance it falls, the quicker your **reaction time**. Use the same hand, ruler and conditions each time. Repeat several times and calculate a **mean** to reduce random errors."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how a reflex arc lets a person pull their hand away from a hot pan before they have time to think. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Receptors in the skin detect the heat (the stimulus) and start electrical impulses.", "why": "Every response starts with a receptor detecting a stimulus."}, {"do": "The impulses travel along a sensory neurone to the spinal cord, which is part of the CNS.", "why": "The sensory neurone carries the message in to the CNS."}, {"do": "At a synapse, a chemical is released and diffuses across the gap. It starts impulses in a relay neurone, then in a motor neurone.", "why": "Impulses cannot cross synapses, so a chemical passes the message on."}, {"do": "The motor neurone carries impulses to the effector, a muscle in the arm. The muscle contracts and pulls the hand away.", "why": "Effectors carry out the response."}, {"do": "The brain is not needed to start this response, so it is automatic and very fast.", "why": "This explains why a reflex protects you from damage."}], "answer": "Receptors detect the heat and send impulses along a sensory neurone to the spinal cord. A chemical crosses the synapse to a relay neurone, then a motor neurone. This carries impulses to a muscle, which contracts. It is automatic and fast."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say a chemical is released at the synapse?", "o": ["The impulse cannot cross the gap, so a chemical diffuses across to start a new one", "The chemical carries the message in the blood to the muscle in the arm", "The chemical makes the impulse travel faster along the neurone", "The chemical joins the two neurones so the impulse flows straight through"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. There is a gap between neurones. The impulse stops there, and a chemical diffuses across to trigger a new impulse.", "This mixes up synapses with hormones. The chemical only crosses the tiny gap. It is not carried in the blood.", "The chemical does not speed up impulses along a neurone. Its job is to pass the message across the gap.", "Neurones do not join up. The gap stays, which is why a chemical is needed."]}, "faded": {"task": "A person steps on a drawing pin and pulls their foot away. Describe the reflex pathway.", "given": ["Receptors in the skin of the foot detect the pin (the stimulus).", "A sensory neurone carries impulses to the spinal cord."], "q": "What is the next step in the pathway?", "o": ["Impulses go along the sensory neurone straight to the leg muscle, which contracts", "A chemical crosses a synapse to a relay neurone, then a motor neurone carries impulses on", "Impulses travel to the brain, which decides to move the foot and sends a message back", "A hormone is released into the blood and carried to the muscles in the leg"], "a": 1, "f": ["This misses out the CNS. The message must pass through a relay neurone and a motor neurone before it reaches the effector.", "Right. In a reflex arc, a chemical passes the message across synapses to a relay neurone, then to a motor neurone.", "This describes a conscious, voluntary response. In a reflex the brain does not decide first, so it is quicker.", "Hormones work slowly in the blood. Reflexes use neurones and impulses, which are much faster."]}, "trap": "Using one result, or the fastest, as the reaction time. Take a mean of the repeats. In reflex answers, do not say the brain decides: the response is automatic, through the CNS.", "recap": "A reflex goes receptor, sensory neurone, relay neurone, motor neurone, effector; for reaction time, repeat the test and take a mean."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.5.2.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 often asks you to explain how the lens changes shape or what a brain region does, and exact wording earns the marks.", "hook": {"q": "A magnifying glass is thick and strongly curved. Compared with a thin, flatter lens, how much does it bend (refract) light?", "o": ["Less, because thicker glass absorbs more of the light", "More, because a thicker, more curved lens refracts light more strongly", "The same, because all glass bends light by the same amount", "More, but only when the light is very bright"], "a": 1, "f": ["Absorbing light is not the same as bending it. Thickness and curve change how much light is refracted, not just how much gets through.", "Correct. A thicker, more curved lens refracts light more. The eye uses this idea to focus on near objects.", "Glass bends light by an amount that depends on the shape of the lens. A more curved lens bends light more.", "Brightness does not change the amount of refraction. The shape of the lens does."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Main brain regions", "b": "The **cerebral cortex** is the wrinkled outer layer. It controls consciousness, intelligence, memory and language. The **cerebellum** is at the back and coordinates movement and balance. The **medulla** controls unconscious activities such as heartbeat and breathing."}, {"h": "Studying the brain", "b": "Scientists link brain regions to jobs in three ways. **MRI scans** show structure and activity in a living brain without surgery. **Electrical stimulation** of a region shows what it controls, but the brain must be exposed. Studying patients with **brain damage** shows what is lost when a region fails. The brain is delicate, so disorders are hard to treat."}, {"h": "Focusing the eye", "b": "The cornea and lens refract light onto the retina. The lens changes shape to focus. This is **accommodation**.\n\nNear object: **ciliary muscles** contract, **suspensory ligaments** slacken, lens thicker and more curved, so it refracts more.\n\nDistant object: muscles relax, ligaments tighten, lens thinner, so it refracts less."}, {"h": "Eye defects", "b": "In **myopia** (short sight), distant objects look blurred. Light focuses in front of the retina. A concave lens corrects it. In **hyperopia** (long sight), near objects look blurred. Light focuses behind the retina. A convex lens corrects it. Other treatments: contact lenses, laser surgery on the cornea, or a replacement lens."}], "example": {"task": "A student has myopia (short sight). Explain why distant objects look blurred to her and how glasses can correct this. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "In myopia, light from distant objects is focused in front of the retina.", "why": "This states the fault. A sharp image needs the focus to fall exactly on the retina."}, {"do": "This happens because the eyeball is too long, or the lens and cornea refract the light too strongly.", "why": "This gives a cause for the focus falling short of the retina."}, {"do": "The light spreads out again before it reaches the retina, so the image on the retina is blurred.", "why": "This links the focus position to what she sees: a blurred image."}, {"do": "Glasses with a concave lens spread the light rays out slightly before they enter the eye. The focus moves back onto the retina.", "why": "This names the lens type and says how it changes the focus."}], "answer": "In myopia, light from distant objects focuses in front of the retina, because the eyeball is too long or the lens refracts too much. The image on the retina is blurred. A concave lens spreads the rays slightly, so they focus on the retina."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer use a concave lens for myopia?", "o": ["It makes the eyeball shorter, so the retina moves closer to the lens.", "It brings the rays together, so the light focuses sooner.", "It makes the ciliary muscles contract, so the lens gets thicker.", "It spreads the rays out slightly, so the focus moves back onto the retina."], "a": 3, "f": ["Glasses sit outside the eye and cannot change the length of the eyeball. They change the direction of the light entering it.", "That is what a convex lens does. In myopia the light already focuses too soon, so bringing it together would make things worse.", "Glasses do not control the ciliary muscles. A thicker lens would refract even more, pushing the focus further in front of the retina.", "Correct. The focus is too far forward. Spreading the rays slightly before they enter the eye moves it back onto the retina."]}, "faded": {"task": "A man has hyperopia (long sight). Explain why a phone held close looks blurred and how glasses help. (4 marks)", "given": ["In hyperopia, light from near objects is focused behind the retina.", "This is because the eyeball is too short, or the lens cannot become thick enough.", "The light has not come to a focus when it reaches the retina, so the image is blurred."], "q": "What should the final step say?", "o": ["A concave lens in glasses spreads the light rays out, so they focus on the retina.", "A convex lens in glasses makes the eyeball longer, so the image falls on the retina.", "A convex lens in glasses brings the light rays together, so they focus on the retina.", "A convex lens in glasses makes the ciliary muscles relax, so the lens becomes thinner."], "a": 2, "f": ["A concave lens is the correction for myopia. Here the focus is too far back, so the rays need to be brought together, not spread out.", "Glasses cannot change the length of the eyeball. They change how the light is bent before it enters the eye.", "Correct. A convex lens brings the rays together slightly before they enter the eye. The focus moves forward onto the retina.", "Glasses do not act on the ciliary muscles. A thinner lens would also refract less, moving the focus even further behind the retina."]}, "trap": "Mixing up near and distant. Near: ciliary muscles contract, lens thick. Distant: muscles relax, lens thin. Also, myopia blurs distant objects and hyperopia blurs near ones. Link each name to what looks blurred.", "recap": "Near objects: ciliary muscles contract, ligaments slacken and the lens thickens; myopia focuses light in front of the retina, hyperopia behind it."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.5.2.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Body temperature comes up in Paper 2 as short explain questions about what happens when someone is too hot or too cold, and why it helps.", "hook": {"q": "After a hard run on a warm day, your skin looks red and you sweat. What is your body most likely trying to do?", "o": ["Store extra energy in the skin so that you stay warm", "Stop respiration in the muscles so that less energy is released", "Transfer energy to the surroundings so that you cool down", "Keep blood away from the skin so that less energy is lost"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. The body is too hot, so it needs to lose energy, not store it. Storing energy in the skin would make overheating worse.", "Wrong. Respiration does not stop. Muscles keep releasing energy while you run. Cooling happens by losing energy to the surroundings.", "Right. Red skin means more blood flows near the surface. Sweat evaporating also takes energy away. Both transfer energy from you to the surroundings.", "Wrong. This is what happens in the cold, to reduce energy loss. A red face and sweating mean the opposite, because more blood is sent to the skin."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Detecting temperature changes", "b": "The **thermoregulatory centre** in the brain has receptors that monitor the temperature of the blood. **Receptors in the skin** send impulses to the centre about skin temperature. The centre then sends impulses to **effectors**, which correct the change. This keeps the body at about 37°C."}, {"h": "When you are too hot", "b": "Blood vessels supplying the skin capillaries **dilate** (vasodilation). More blood flows near the skin surface, so more energy is transferred to the surroundings. Sweat glands release **sweat**. When sweat **evaporates**, it transfers energy from the skin to the environment, which cools you."}, {"h": "When you are too cold", "b": "Blood vessels supplying the skin capillaries **constrict** (vasoconstriction). Less blood flows near the surface, so less energy is transferred to the surroundings. **Sweating stops**. Muscles contract rapidly in **shivering**, and the respiration in them releases energy that warms the body."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how the body responds when a person walks from a warm room into freezing air.", "steps": [{"do": "Receptors in the skin detect the fall in skin temperature. They send impulses to the thermoregulatory centre in the brain.", "why": "Detection comes first. The brain is the control centre and needs the information."}, {"do": "The centre also monitors blood temperature. It sends impulses to effectors, which are the skin blood vessels, sweat glands and muscles.", "why": "The centre coordinates the response by telling the effectors what to do."}, {"do": "Blood vessels supplying the skin capillaries constrict. Less blood flows near the skin surface, so less energy is transferred to the surroundings.", "why": "This links the response (vasoconstriction) to its effect (less energy lost)."}, {"do": "Sweating stops, so less energy is transferred from the skin by evaporation of sweat.", "why": "Sweat only cools when it evaporates, so stopping it reduces energy loss."}, {"do": "Muscles contract rapidly (shivering). Respiration in the muscle cells releases energy, which warms the body.", "why": "Linking shivering to respiration releasing energy is what earns the mark."}], "answer": "Skin receptors and the thermoregulatory centre detect the cold. The centre signals effectors. Skin blood vessels constrict, so less energy is lost, and sweating stops. Muscles shiver, and respiration in them releases energy that warms the body."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does vasoconstriction reduce energy loss from the body?", "o": ["Less blood flows near the skin surface, so less energy is transferred to the surroundings", "The vessels move away from the skin, so the blood is further from the cold air", "Constriction stops the blood from carrying any energy around the body", "Constriction makes sweat glands release more sweat, which insulates the skin"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Blood carries energy to the skin. With narrower vessels, less blood reaches the surface, so less energy passes to the surroundings.", "Wrong. Blood vessels do not move. The vessels supplying the skin capillaries narrow, which changes how much blood flows near the surface.", "Wrong. Blood still carries energy around the body. Constriction only reduces the flow of blood to the skin surface.", "Wrong. Sweating stops in the cold. Sweat does not insulate. When it evaporates, it removes energy from the skin."]}, "faded": {"task": "A runner's blood temperature rises on a hot day. Explain how her body brings it back to normal.", "given": ["Receptors in the thermoregulatory centre in the brain detect that her blood temperature is above normal.", "Skin receptors also detect the higher skin temperature and send impulses to the centre."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Impulses go to effectors: sweat glands make sweat and skin blood vessels constrict", "Skin receptors release a hormone that makes blood vessels move towards the skin", "Impulses go to effectors: sweat glands make sweat and skin blood vessels dilate", "Impulses go to effectors: sweating stops and her muscles begin to shiver"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Sweating is correct, but constriction is the response to cold. In the heat the vessels dilate, so more blood flows near the skin.", "Wrong. The centre uses nervous impulses to effectors here, not a hormone. Blood vessels also do not move. They dilate or constrict.", "Right. Impulses go to effectors. Sweat glands release sweat, and vessels supplying the skin capillaries dilate, so more energy is transferred to the surroundings.", "Wrong. This is the response to being too cold. Stopping sweating and shivering would raise her temperature further."]}, "trap": "Saying blood vessels move towards or away from the skin. They stay in place. The vessels supplying the skin capillaries dilate (widen) or constrict (narrow), which changes how much blood flows near the surface.", "recap": "The brain's thermoregulatory centre detects temperature changes; in heat, vessels dilate and you sweat; in cold, vessels constrict, sweating stops and you shiver."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.5.3.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Blood glucose control and diabetes come up in Paper 2, usually as short explain questions or as a comparison of type 1 and type 2 diabetes.", "hook": {"q": "After a big meal, glucose from your food enters your blood. What must your body do to keep the level steady?", "o": ["Make more glucose so the level stays the same as before", "Move the extra glucose out of the blood and store it", "Do nothing, because blood glucose is not controlled", "Remove all the glucose from the blood through the kidneys"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. The problem is too much glucose, not too little. Making more would push the level even higher.", "Right. The body takes the extra glucose out of the blood and stores it, which keeps the level steady. You will see which hormone does this.", "Wrong. Blood glucose is tightly controlled. If it were not, the level would swing too high and too low.", "Wrong. The body needs glucose for respiration. It keeps it and stores the extra, rather than losing it all in urine."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Glands and hormones", "b": "The endocrine system is made of glands. They release **hormones** straight into the blood. The blood carries them to target organs. The **pituitary gland** is the 'master gland'. Its hormones control other glands, such as the thyroid, adrenal glands, ovaries and testes. The **pancreas** controls blood glucose."}, {"h": "Insulin lowers glucose", "b": "After a meal, blood glucose rises. The **pancreas** detects this and releases **insulin** into the blood. Insulin makes cells take up glucose from the blood. In the liver and muscles, excess glucose is converted to **glycogen** for storage. Blood glucose falls back to normal."}, {"h": "Glucagon raises glucose", "b": "If blood glucose falls too low, the pancreas releases **glucagon**. Glucagon makes the liver convert stored glycogen back into glucose. The glucose is released into the blood. Insulin and glucagon each reverse a change in glucose level. This is **negative feedback**."}, {"h": "Two types of diabetes", "b": "In **type 1 diabetes** the pancreas makes little or no insulin, so blood glucose can rise too high. It is treated with insulin injections. In **type 2 diabetes** body cells stop responding to insulin, even if insulin levels are normal. Obesity is a risk factor. Diet and exercise help control it. Drag the slider on the graph to see.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [0, 6], "xstep": 1, "y": [0, 12], "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "time after meal / hours", "ylabel": "blood glucose / mmol/dm³", "lines": [{"dash": true, "label": "normal level", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 4], [6, 4]]}, {"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 4.0], [1.0, 8.0]]}], "points": [], "frames": [{"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[1.0, 8.0], [2.0, 10.0]]}, {"c": 0, "pts": [[2.0, 10.0], [3.0, 10.0]]}, {"c": 0, "pts": [[3.0, 10.0], [6.0, 10.0]], "label": "blood glucose"}], "points": [{"at": [4, 10.0], "label": "4 hours"}], "read": ["Insulin working: 0%", "Glucose at 2 h: 10 mmol/dm³", "Glucose at 4 h: 10 mmol/dm³"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[1.0, 8.0], [2.0, 9.0]]}, {"c": 0, "pts": [[2.0, 9.0], [3.0, 8.5]]}, {"c": 0, "pts": [[3.0, 8.5], [6.0, 8.5]], "label": "blood glucose"}], "points": [{"at": [4, 8.5], "label": "4 hours"}], "read": ["Insulin working: 25%", "Glucose at 2 h: 9 mmol/dm³", "Glucose at 4 h: 8.5 mmol/dm³"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[1.0, 8.0], [2.0, 8.0]]}, {"c": 0, "pts": [[2.0, 8.0], [3.0, 7.0]]}, {"c": 0, "pts": [[3.0, 7.0], [6.0, 7.0]], "label": "blood glucose"}], "points": [{"at": [4, 7.0], "label": "4 hours"}], "read": ["Insulin working: 50%", "Glucose at 2 h: 8 mmol/dm³", "Glucose at 4 h: 7 mmol/dm³"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[1.0, 8.0], [2.0, 7.0]]}, {"c": 0, "pts": [[2.0, 7.0], [3.0, 5.5]]}, {"c": 0, "pts": [[3.0, 5.5], [6.0, 5.5]], "label": "blood glucose"}], "points": [{"at": [4, 5.5], "label": "4 hours"}], "read": ["Insulin working: 75%", "Glucose at 2 h: 7 mmol/dm³", "Glucose at 4 h: 5.5 mmol/dm³"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[1.0, 8.0], [2.0, 6.0]]}, {"c": 0, "pts": [[2.0, 6.0], [3.0, 4.0]]}, {"c": 0, "pts": [[3.0, 4.0], [6.0, 4.0]], "label": "blood glucose"}], "points": [{"at": [4, 4.0], "label": "4 hours"}], "read": ["Insulin working: 100%", "Glucose at 2 h: 6 mmol/dm³", "Glucose at 4 h: 4 mmol/dm³"]}], "ctl": {"type": "slider", "label": "s", "vals": ["0", "0.25", "0.5", "0.75", "1"], "start": 4}}}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows a person's blood glucose concentration after a meal rich in carbohydrate. Explain how the body lowers blood glucose concentration after the meal.", "steps": [{"do": "Digested carbohydrate enters the blood, so blood glucose concentration rises above the normal level.", "why": "This is the change that starts the response. Say what is detected."}, {"do": "The pancreas detects the rise and releases insulin into the blood.", "why": "Names the gland and the hormone. The blood carries the hormone."}, {"do": "Insulin makes body cells take up glucose from the blood.", "why": "Shows how the hormone actually lowers the level."}, {"do": "In the liver, excess glucose is converted into glycogen for storage.", "why": "Explains where the extra glucose goes. Glycogen is the storage molecule."}, {"do": "Blood glucose concentration falls back to the normal level.", "why": "Links back to the start. This is negative feedback."}], "answer": "After a meal, blood glucose rises. The pancreas releases insulin into the blood. Insulin makes body cells take up glucose. The liver converts excess glucose into glycogen for storage. So blood glucose falls back to normal.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 6], "y": [0, 10], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "time after meal / hours", "ylabel": "blood glucose / mmol/dm³", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 4], [1, 8], [2, 6], [3, 4], [4, 4], [5, 4], [6, 4]], "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [6, 4]], "dash": true, "label": "normal level", "c": 1}]}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the liver converts excess glucose into glycogen?", "o": ["Glycogen is the hormone that tells the pancreas to stop releasing insulin", "Glycogen is the form in which glucose leaves the body in the urine", "Glycogen makes body cells respond better to insulin", "Glycogen is a store, so glucose leaves the blood without being lost"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Glycogen is a storage carbohydrate, not a hormone. Insulin and glucagon are the hormones here.", "Wrong. In a healthy person glucose is not lost in urine. It is stored as glycogen and used later.", "Wrong. Glycogen does not change how cells respond to insulin. That idea belongs to type 2 diabetes, which is a different point.", "Right. Glycogen is a storage form. Converting glucose to glycogen lowers blood glucose and keeps the glucose for later."]}, "faded": {"task": "The graph shows blood glucose after a meal for a healthy person and a person with type 1 diabetes. Explain why the person with type 1 diabetes has high blood glucose after a meal.", "given": ["After a meal, glucose from digested carbohydrate enters the blood and blood glucose rises.", "In type 1 diabetes the pancreas makes little or no insulin."], "q": "Which point comes next in the answer?", "o": ["The liver releases glucagon, which turns glucose into glycogen, so the level falls.", "Cells stop responding to insulin, so glucose stays in the blood and stays high.", "With little insulin, cells take up little glucose, so blood glucose stays high.", "The kidneys make extra insulin, so glucose moves into cells and the level falls."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. The pancreas, not the liver, releases glucagon. Glucagon turns glycogen into glucose and raises the level, so the hormones are swapped.", "Wrong. This describes type 2 diabetes. In type 1 the problem is too little insulin, not cells that ignore it.", "Right. With little insulin in the blood, cells do not take up glucose, so it stays in the blood and the level stays high.", "Wrong. The kidneys do not make insulin. The pancreas does, and in type 1 it makes very little."], "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 6], "y": [0, 12], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "time after meal / hours", "ylabel": "blood glucose / mmol/dm³", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 4], [1, 8], [2, 6], [3, 4], [4, 4], [5, 4], [6, 4]], "label": "healthy", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [1, 8], [2, 10], [3, 10], [4, 10], [5, 10], [6, 10]], "label": "type 1", "c": 1}]}}, "trap": "Mixing up glucagon and glycogen, or type 1 and type 2. Glucagon is a hormone. Glycogen is the store. Type 1: little insulin made. Type 2: cells do not respond to insulin.", "recap": "Insulin lowers blood glucose (stored as glycogen); glucagon raises it. Type 1: little insulin made. Type 2: cells do not respond."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.5.3.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 often asks you to explain what happens to water, glucose and urea in the kidney, how ADH changes urine, and how dialysis works.", "hook": {"q": "On a hot day you sweat a lot and drink very little. What will your urine be like?", "o": ["Pale and large in volume", "Dark and small in volume", "Dark and large in volume", "Pale and small in volume"], "a": 1, "f": ["Pale, large volumes of urine come after drinking a lot. Here you have lost water, so the body must save it.", "Right. Your body saves water, so you make a small amount of concentrated, dark urine.", "Saving water means less water leaves in urine, so the volume is small, not large.", "Volume and colour go together here. Saving water gives a small volume that is dark, not pale."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Filtering the blood", "b": "Your **kidneys** filter the blood under pressure. Small molecules pass into the kidney tubules as filtrate: water, glucose, ions and **urea**. Blood cells and proteins are too large, so they stay in the blood. Urea is made in the liver from excess amino acids."}, {"h": "Selective reabsorption", "b": "Next, **selective reabsorption** puts useful substances back into the blood. All the glucose is reabsorbed. Ions and water are only partly reabsorbed, in the amounts the body needs. Urea is not reabsorbed. Urea, extra ions and extra water leave as **urine**, which is stored in the bladder."}, {"h": "ADH controls water", "b": "The brain monitors the water content of the blood. When it is low, the pituitary gland releases **ADH** into the blood. ADH makes the kidney tubules reabsorb more water. This is **negative feedback**: the change is reversed. High water content means less ADH and more dilute urine."}, {"h": "Dialysis for kidney failure", "b": "If the kidneys fail, a **dialysis** machine cleans the blood. Blood flows past a **partially permeable membrane**. The dialysis fluid on the other side has normal blood levels of glucose and ions, so these stay in the blood. Urea diffuses out into the fluid."}], "example": {"task": "A girl runs in hot weather and does not drink. Explain how her body keeps its water level right and why her urine is dark and small in volume.", "steps": [{"do": "She loses water in sweat, so the water content of her blood falls.", "why": "This is the change that her body must detect and correct."}, {"do": "The brain detects the low water content. The pituitary gland releases more ADH into the blood.", "why": "The pituitary gland is where ADH is released. The brain is the monitor."}, {"do": "ADH travels in the blood to the kidney tubules. It makes them reabsorb more water back into the blood.", "why": "This names the target of ADH and what it does."}, {"do": "Less water is lost, so she makes a small volume of urine. Urea still leaves, so the urine is concentrated and dark.", "why": "The same urea is dissolved in less water."}, {"do": "Her blood water content rises back to normal and ADH release falls.", "why": "This is negative feedback: the change is reversed."}], "answer": "Sweating lowers blood water content. The brain detects this and the pituitary gland releases more ADH. ADH makes the kidney tubules reabsorb more water. Less water is lost, so the urine is small in volume and dark. Blood water content then returns to normal."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is her urine darker after ADH has acted (step 4)?", "o": ["ADH makes the kidneys produce extra urea", "ADH stops glucose being reabsorbed, so it colours the urine", "More water is reabsorbed but urea still leaves, so urea is in less water", "ADH makes the kidneys filter out blood cells into the urine"], "a": 2, "f": ["The kidneys do not make urea. The liver does. ADH changes only how much water is reabsorbed.", "ADH acts on water reabsorption, not glucose. All glucose is reabsorbed anyway, so none is in urine.", "Right. Urea is not reabsorbed, so it still leaves. With less water in the urine, it is more concentrated and darker.", "Blood cells are too large to be filtered out at all. ADH does not change this."]}, "faded": {"task": "A boy drinks a large amount of water quickly. Explain how his body returns the water content of his blood to normal.", "given": ["His blood water content rises.", "The brain detects this and the pituitary gland releases less ADH."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["The kidney tubules reabsorb less water, so a large volume of dilute urine is made", "The kidney tubules reabsorb more water, so a large volume of dilute urine is made", "The kidneys stop filtering the blood, so no urine is made until the water is gone", "The kidneys make extra urea, which carries the water out of the body"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Less ADH means less water is reabsorbed. More water stays in the urine, so it is large in volume and dilute.", "This gets the effect of ADH backwards. Less ADH means less water is reabsorbed. More reabsorption would give a small volume of urine.", "The kidneys keep filtering all the time. The body changes how much water is reabsorbed, not whether filtering happens.", "The kidneys do not make urea. The liver does. Urea does not carry water out."]}, "trap": "Thinking everything small is lost in urine. All glucose is reabsorbed, water only partly, and protein is never filtered out. Only urea, extra ions and extra water leave in urine.", "recap": "Kidneys filter blood, then reabsorb all glucose and the water and ions you need; urea and the excess leave as urine, with ADH controlling water."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.5.3.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 often asks which hormone does which job in the menstrual cycle and how each type of contraceptive works.", "hook": {"q": "A hormone is made in a gland in the brain but must act on the ovaries. How does it most likely get there?", "o": ["Along nerve cells, like a reflex message", "In the blood, as it flows round the body", "Through the digestive system", "Along the fallopian tubes"], "a": 1, "f": ["Nerves carry fast electrical impulses. Hormones are chemical messengers and do not use nerves.", "Right. Hormones are released into the blood. They travel to target organs, such as the ovaries, and change how they work.", "The digestive system handles food. Hormones in the menstrual cycle do not travel through it.", "The fallopian tubes carry the egg to the uterus. They are not a transport route for hormones."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Before ovulation", "b": "The **pituitary gland** in the brain makes **FSH**. It travels in the blood to the ovaries. FSH makes an egg mature in an ovary. It also stimulates the ovaries to make **oestrogen**. Oestrogen makes the lining of the uterus grow thick, ready for a possible embryo."}, {"h": "Ovulation and after", "b": "The **pituitary gland** also makes **LH**. On the graph, LH peaks around day 14 of a 28-day cycle and causes **ovulation**, the release of the egg. The ovaries make **progesterone**, which maintains the lining and rises after ovulation. If no embryo implants, the lining breaks down and the period begins. Press Replay to watch it draw.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "draw", "ms": 6000, "x": [0, 28], "y": [0, 10], "xstep": 7, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "time / days", "ylabel": "hormone level / arbitrary units", "lines": [{"label": "FSH", "c": 0, "step": 1, "pts": [[0, 5], [6, 5], [12, 3], [14, 5], [16, 2], [22, 2], [28, 3]]}, {"label": "LH", "c": 1, "step": 2, "pts": [[0, 1], [10, 1], [12, 3], [14, 10], [16, 2], [28, 1]]}, {"label": "oestrogen", "c": 2, "step": 3, "pts": [[0, 2], [6, 2], [13, 8], [16, 4], [21, 6], [28, 2]]}, {"label": "progesterone", "c": 3, "step": 4, "pts": [[0, 1], [14, 1], [21, 8], [28, 0]]}], "points": [{"at": [14, 10], "label": "ovulation", "step": 2}, {"at": [28, 0], "label": "period begins", "step": 4}], "cap": "FSH and LH come from the pituitary gland; oestrogen and progesterone from the ovaries. LH peaks on day 14: ovulation."}}, {"h": "Hormonal contraceptives", "b": "The **oral contraceptive pill** contains hormones that **inhibit FSH** production, so no eggs mature. An injection, implant or skin patch gives **slow-release progesterone**. This stops eggs maturing and being released for months or years."}, {"h": "Other methods", "b": "**Barrier methods**, such as condoms and diaphragms, stop sperm reaching the egg. Condoms also reduce the spread of STIs. An **intrauterine device** (IUD) stops an embryo implanting; some release a hormone. **Spermicides** kill or disable sperm. Sterilisation is surgery that stops eggs and sperm meeting."}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows hormone levels in the first 16 days of a menstrual cycle. Describe how hormones from the pituitary gland and the ovaries control the first 14 days, up to the release of an egg.", "steps": [{"do": "The pituitary gland releases FSH into the blood. The blood carries it to the ovaries.", "why": "Names the gland and the route. This is where the control starts."}, {"do": "FSH makes an egg mature in an ovary.", "why": "This is the main job of FSH. It gets the egg ready, but does not release it."}, {"do": "FSH also stimulates the ovaries to make oestrogen. Oestrogen makes the lining of the uterus thicken.", "why": "Links FSH to oestrogen, then oestrogen to its effect on the lining."}, {"do": "Around day 14, the pituitary gland releases LH. LH causes ovulation, so the egg is released from the ovary.", "why": "LH triggers release. This finishes the sequence the question asks for."}], "answer": "The pituitary gland releases FSH, which travels in the blood to the ovaries. FSH matures an egg and stimulates oestrogen production. Oestrogen thickens the uterus lining. Around day 14, LH from the pituitary causes ovulation.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 16], "y": [0, 12], "xstep": 2, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "time / days", "ylabel": "hormone level / arbitrary units", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 4], [4, 6], [8, 4], [12, 2], [14, 4], [16, 2]], "label": "FSH", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [4, 2], [8, 4], [12, 8], [14, 6], [16, 4]], "label": "oestrogen", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [10, 2], [12, 4], [14, 10], [16, 2]], "label": "LH", "c": 2}]}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer name LH, not FSH, as the cause of the egg being released?", "o": ["FSH is made in the ovary, so it cannot reach the egg", "FSH is only made after the egg has already been released", "LH is the hormone that causes ovulation. FSH only matures the egg", "LH is made in the ovary and so acts faster than FSH"], "a": 2, "f": ["FSH is made in the pituitary gland, not the ovary. It does reach the ovary through the blood.", "FSH is made before ovulation. It is the hormone that starts the egg maturing.", "Right. Each hormone has its own job. FSH matures the egg, and LH causes ovulation. Mixing them up loses the mark.", "LH is made in the pituitary gland, not the ovary. Speed is not why it is named."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a progesterone implant under the skin reduces the chance of pregnancy.", "given": ["Point 1: The implant slowly releases progesterone into the blood."], "q": "What is the next point in the explanation?", "o": ["Progesterone is a barrier that physically blocks sperm, like a condom.", "Progesterone kills sperm as soon as they enter the vagina.", "Progesterone cuts the tube that carries the egg to the uterus.", "Progesterone stops eggs maturing and being released from the ovaries."], "a": 3, "f": ["Condoms and diaphragms are barriers. A hormone is not a physical barrier. It works by changing the body.", "Killing sperm describes spermicides. Progesterone does not do this.", "Cutting the tubes is surgical sterilisation. An implant is not surgery on the tubes.", "Right. Slow-release progesterone inhibits the maturation and release of eggs, so there is no egg to fertilise."]}, "trap": "Choosing FSH for egg release, or saying progesterone releases the egg. FSH matures the egg, LH releases it, and progesterone maintains the lining. Also, hormonal contraceptives give no protection against STIs.", "recap": "FSH matures the egg, LH releases it, oestrogen and progesterone build and maintain the lining; contraceptives stop eggs maturing, sperm reaching them, or embryos implanting."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.5.3.6", "kind": "idea", "why": "Higher tier papers can ask you to describe how IVF works and explain what FSH and LH do, so learn the steps in order.", "hook": {"q": "A woman's ovaries do not mature her eggs, because her body makes too little of the hormone that does this. What could a doctor sensibly give her?", "o": ["A drug that blocks the hormones made by her pituitary gland", "A drug containing testosterone to speed up egg growth", "A drug containing the hormones that make eggs mature", "A drug containing a hormone that keeps the uterus lining thick"], "a": 2, "f": ["Blocking pituitary hormones would make things worse. The pituitary gland makes FSH and LH, which the ovaries need to mature and release eggs.", "Testosterone is mainly a male sex hormone. It does not make eggs mature in the ovary.", "Right. If her own hormone level is too low, giving the missing hormones can make her eggs mature. This is the idea behind fertility drugs.", "A hormone that thickens the uterus lining does not mature eggs. The problem here is in the ovary, not the uterus."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Roles of FSH and LH", "b": "The pituitary gland makes **FSH** and **LH**. FSH makes eggs mature in the ovary. LH stimulates **ovulation**, the release of a mature egg. If a woman makes too little FSH, her eggs do not mature. A fertility drug containing FSH and LH can help."}, {"h": "IVF: hormones and eggs", "b": "**IVF** starts with hormones. The woman is given FSH and LH for several days. This stimulates **several eggs** to mature, not just one. A doctor then collects the eggs from her ovaries. More eggs give more chances of an embryo."}, {"h": "IVF: fertilisation and embryos", "b": "The collected eggs are mixed with sperm from the father in a lab. This is where **fertilisation** happens. The fertilised eggs grow into **embryos**. When they are tiny balls of cells, one or two are put into the woman's **uterus**."}, {"h": "Benefits and drawbacks", "b": "IVF can help couples who cannot conceive naturally to have a child. It has drawbacks. It is **not always successful**. It can be stressful, emotionally and physically. Putting back several embryos risks a **multiple birth**, which is risky for the mother and babies."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how IVF is used to help a woman who cannot become pregnant naturally. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "The woman is given FSH and LH for several days.", "why": "These hormones stimulate several eggs to mature in her ovaries, so more eggs are available."}, {"do": "The mature eggs are collected from her ovaries.", "why": "The eggs must be taken out of her body because fertilisation will happen in a lab."}, {"do": "The eggs are mixed with sperm from the father in a lab, so they are fertilised.", "why": "This is the 'in vitro' fertilisation, which is the core idea of IVF."}, {"do": "The fertilised eggs develop into embryos. When they are tiny balls of cells, one or two are placed in her uterus.", "why": "Embryos can then implant. Only one or two are used to limit multiple births."}], "answer": "The woman is given FSH and LH to mature several eggs. The eggs are collected from her ovaries and fertilised by the father's sperm in a lab. The fertilised eggs develop into embryos. One or two of these tiny balls of cells are put into her uterus."}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 2, why are the mature eggs collected from her ovaries before they are fertilised?", "o": ["The hormones only work once the eggs have left the ovaries", "IVF fertilises eggs in a lab, so they must first be removed from the ovaries", "LH can only make eggs mature once they are outside the woman's body", "Removing the eggs stops the ovaries releasing any more FSH into her blood"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. FSH and LH act on the ovaries, which is why they are given before the eggs are collected. They do not need the eggs to be outside her body.", "Right. In IVF, fertilisation happens in a lab, outside the body. So the mature eggs have to be taken out of her ovaries first.", "Wrong. This mixes up the hormones and where they act. FSH matures eggs inside the ovaries; LH triggers egg release. The eggs are collected only after maturing.", "Wrong. Collecting eggs does not control her FSH. Her FSH is raised by the fertility drug, and the eggs are collected for fertilisation."]}, "faded": {"task": "A woman's oviducts are blocked, so sperm cannot reach her eggs. Describe how IVF can help her to have a child.", "given": ["She is given FSH and LH so that several eggs mature.", "The eggs are collected and mixed with the father's sperm in a lab, where they are fertilised."], "q": "What is the next step in IVF?", "o": ["All the embryos are put into the uterus so that at least one survives", "Embryos are grown in the lab until they are babies, then put in the uterus", "Sperm are added again in her oviduct so that fertilisation can finish naturally", "When the embryos are tiny balls of cells, one or two are placed in her uterus"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Putting back many embryos raises the risk of a multiple birth, which is dangerous for the mother and babies. Only one or two are used.", "Wrong. Embryos are put back when they are still tiny balls of cells. They cannot grow into babies in a lab dish.", "Wrong. Fertilisation has already happened in the lab. There is no second fertilisation in the oviduct in IVF.", "Right. The fertilised eggs have become embryos. At the tiny ball of cells stage, one or two are placed in the uterus to implant."]}, "trap": "Do not write that FSH and LH cause fertilisation, or that fertilisation happens inside the woman. In IVF the hormones only mature eggs, and fertilisation happens in a lab.", "recap": "In IVF, FSH and LH mature several eggs, which are collected and fertilised in a lab, then one or two embryos go into the uterus."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.5.3.7", "kind": "idea", "why": "Higher tier questions ask you to explain what adrenaline does and how thyroxine is kept steady, in linked points that use the terms TSH, pituitary and thyroid.", "hook": {"q": "A room heater switches off when the room gets too warm and on when it gets too cold. What does this do to any change in temperature?", "o": ["It makes each change bigger, so the room warms or cools faster", "It ignores each change, so the temperature drifts up or down freely", "It reverses each change, bringing the temperature back to the set level", "It stops changes happening at all, so the temperature never varies"], "a": 2, "f": ["A heater that made changes bigger would push the temperature further away. A thermostat does the opposite.", "The heater does respond. It switches on or off depending on the temperature, so the temperature does not drift.", "Right. The response works against the change. Your body uses the same idea, called negative feedback.", "The temperature still varies a little. The heater only brings it back towards the set level each time."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Adrenaline: fight or flight", "b": "When you are frightened or stressed, the **adrenal glands** release **adrenaline** into the blood. It travels to target organs, such as the heart. Adrenaline prepares your body to fight or to run away, called '**fight or flight**'."}, {"h": "What adrenaline does", "b": "Adrenaline makes the **heart rate** increase. More blood is pumped each minute, so more **oxygen** and **glucose** reach the brain and muscles. The muscles can then respire faster and release more energy for fighting or running."}, {"h": "Thyroxine and metabolism", "b": "The **thyroid gland** makes **thyroxine**. It controls the **basal metabolic rate**, the rate of chemical reactions in the body at rest. It also helps growth and development. Too much or too little upsets the body, so the level must stay steady."}, {"h": "Thyroxine feedback loop", "b": "The **pituitary gland** releases **TSH**, which stimulates the thyroid to release thyroxine. If thyroxine is low, the pituitary releases more TSH, so thyroxine rises. If thyroxine is high, TSH release is reduced, so thyroxine falls. This is **negative feedback**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how negative feedback brings the blood thyroxine level back to normal after it rises above normal. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "The blood thyroxine level rises above normal. This high level acts on the pituitary gland and inhibits it.", "why": "Say what the change is. The response is triggered by the level being too high."}, {"do": "The inhibited pituitary gland releases less TSH into the blood.", "why": "TSH comes from the pituitary. A high thyroxine level reduces its release."}, {"do": "With less TSH, the thyroid gland is stimulated less, so it releases less thyroxine.", "why": "TSH stimulates the thyroid. Less stimulation means less thyroxine is made and released."}, {"do": "The thyroxine level falls back to normal. The response reversed the original rise, so this is negative feedback.", "why": "Link the outcome back to the change. Negative feedback works against the change."}], "answer": "High thyroxine inhibits the pituitary gland, so it releases less TSH. The thyroid gland is stimulated less, so it releases less thyroxine. The level falls back to normal. The response reverses the rise, so it is negative feedback."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the pituitary releases LESS TSH in step 2?", "o": ["The pituitary has used up its TSH and must wait to make more", "The high thyroxine level inhibits the pituitary, so the response works against the rise", "TSH breaks down thyroxine in the blood, so less TSH is needed to lower it", "Adrenaline tells the pituitary to stop making TSH when thyroxine is high"], "a": 1, "f": ["TSH is not used up. Its release is controlled by the thyroxine level in the blood.", "Right. High thyroxine reduces TSH release. That is the feedback, and it works against the original rise.", "TSH does not break down thyroxine. It stimulates the thyroid gland to release thyroxine.", "Adrenaline is not part of this loop. Thyroxine itself acts on the pituitary."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how negative feedback brings the blood thyroxine level back to normal after it falls below normal.", "given": ["The blood thyroxine level falls below normal.", "The pituitary gland is no longer inhibited, so it releases more TSH into the blood."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["TSH stimulates the thyroid gland to release less thyroxine, so the level falls further", "TSH is detected by the adrenal glands, which release adrenaline to raise thyroxine", "TSH stimulates the thyroid gland to release more thyroxine, so the level rises to normal", "TSH makes the pituitary release more thyroxine until the level is normal"], "a": 2, "f": ["This reverses the effect of TSH. Negative feedback raises a low level, it does not push it further down.", "The adrenal glands release adrenaline, not thyroxine. They are not part of this loop.", "Right. TSH stimulates the thyroid, so more thyroxine is released and the level rises back to normal.", "Thyroxine is made by the thyroid, not the pituitary. The pituitary releases TSH."]}, "trap": "Thinking negative feedback makes the change bigger, or mixing up the glands. Remember: the pituitary releases TSH, the thyroid releases thyroxine, and the response always works against the change.", "recap": "Adrenaline raises heart rate to deliver more oxygen and glucose; low thyroxine raises TSH release and high thyroxine lowers it."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.5.4.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 often asks you to explain why a shoot or root bends, including in required practical 8, so you need the full chain of reasoning.", "hook": {"q": "A plant on a windowsill leans towards the glass. Which part of the stem is growing faster?", "o": ["The side facing the window, because it gets more light", "The side away from the window, in the shade", "Both sides grow at the same rate and the stem just tips over", "Neither side, because stems only grow at the very bottom"], "a": 1, "f": ["Lit-side growth would bend the stem away from the window. Plants lean towards light, so the lit side cannot be the faster one.", "Right. The shaded side grows faster, which pushes the stem over towards the light. You will see why auxin causes this.", "Equal growth gives a straight stem. Bending needs one side to grow faster than the other.", "Stems grow along their length, mainly near the tip. Growth on one side being faster is what makes it lean."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Shoots and light", "b": "**Auxin** is a plant hormone made in the shoot tip. When light shines from one side, auxin moves to the shaded side. There it makes cells **elongate** more. The shaded side grows faster than the lit side, so the shoot bends towards the light. This is **phototropism**."}, {"h": "Shoots and gravity", "b": "Plants also respond to gravity. This is **gravitropism**. In a shoot lying on its side, auxin collects on the lower side. Auxin makes shoot cells elongate, so the lower side grows faster. The shoot bends upwards, away from gravity. This is **negative gravitropism**."}, {"h": "Roots do the opposite", "b": "In roots, auxin also collects on the lower side. But in roots, a high level of auxin **inhibits** elongation. So the lower side grows more slowly than the upper side. The root bends downwards, in the direction of gravity. This is **positive gravitropism**."}, {"h": "Required practical 8", "b": "Use **seedlings** of the same species and age. Give light from one side only (for example a box with a slit), or lay them on their side in the dark. Measure the **angle** of bending with a protractor. Use several seedlings and calculate the **mean**."}], "example": {"task": "A seedling is laid on its side in the dark. After two days the shoot has bent upwards. Explain why.", "steps": [{"do": "Gravity makes auxin move to the lower side of the shoot.", "why": "Gravity is the stimulus. Unequal auxin distribution is what causes the response."}, {"do": "Auxin makes shoot cells elongate, so cells on the lower side elongate more.", "why": "In shoots, auxin promotes cell elongation. More auxin means more elongation."}, {"do": "The lower side grows faster than the upper side.", "why": "Bending only happens when one side grows faster than the other."}, {"do": "The shoot bends upwards, away from gravity. This is negative gravitropism.", "why": "Faster growth on the lower side pushes the tip up. Naming the response shows you know the correct term."}], "answer": "Auxin collects on the lower side of the shoot. It makes those cells elongate more. The lower side grows faster than the upper side, so the shoot bends upwards. This is negative gravitropism."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the lower side of the shoot grows faster?", "o": ["In shoots, auxin makes cells elongate, and the lower side has more auxin", "In shoots, auxin stops cells elongating, so the upper side is held back", "Gravity pulls cells towards the lower side and makes them larger", "The lower side receives more light, so it grows faster"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Auxin promotes elongation in shoots. More auxin on the lower side means more elongation there, so it grows faster.", "This is the effect in roots, not shoots. In shoots, auxin promotes elongation, so the side with more auxin grows faster.", "Gravity does not move cells or make them bigger. It makes auxin collect on the lower side, and auxin causes the growth.", "There is no light in this experiment because the seedling is in the dark. The stimulus is gravity."]}, "faded": {"task": "A seedling's root is laid horizontally in the dark. After two days it has bent downwards. Explain why.", "given": ["Gravity makes auxin move to the lower side of the root.", "In roots, a high level of auxin inhibits cell elongation."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["So the lower side elongates more than the upper side, as in a shoot", "So the upper side has more auxin and elongates less than the lower side", "So cells on the lower side divide more and make the root heavier", "So the lower side elongates less than the upper side, and the root bends down"], "a": 3, "f": ["This treats roots like shoots. In roots, auxin inhibits elongation, so the lower side grows less, not more.", "Auxin collects on the lower side, not the upper side. The upper side has less auxin, so it elongates more.", "Bending comes from unequal cell elongation, not from cells dividing or the root becoming heavier.", "Right. The lower side has more auxin, which inhibits elongation. The upper side grows faster, so the root bends downwards."]}, "trap": "Saying auxin builds up on the lit side, or that the shoot bends away from light. Auxin collects on the shaded side, away from the light. Check which side has more auxin first.", "recap": "Auxin collects on the shaded or lower side, speeding elongation in shoots and slowing it in roots, so unequal growth causes bending."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.5.4.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Higher tier questions ask you to match auxin, ethene and gibberellins to how growers use them, and to explain their effects on plants.", "hook": {"q": "Bananas are picked green, long before they reach UK shops. Why do growers do this?", "o": ["Green bananas contain more vitamins, which would be lost if they were picked ripe", "Green fruit is firmer, so it is damaged less and lasts longer on the journey", "Bananas cannot be picked once they have started to ripen on the plant", "Ripe bananas weigh more, so they cost more to ship across the world"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Bananas are not picked green to save vitamins. The real reason is about how well the fruit survives the journey.", "Right. Unripe fruit is firmer, so it bruises less and keeps longer. Growers ripen it later using ethene.", "Wrong. Fruit can be picked ripe. Growers pick it green by choice, to protect it in transport.", "Wrong. Ripening does not make fruit noticeably heavier. The reason is damage and shelf life, not weight."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Auxin: rooting powder", "b": "A cutting is a piece of stem cut from a plant. Dipped in **rooting powder**, which contains **auxin**, it grows roots faster. Growers can then make many new plants quickly, and each is a copy of the parent. Auxin is also used in **tissue culture** to promote growth."}, {"h": "Auxin: weedkillers", "b": "Weedkillers contain **synthetic auxins**. They are **selective**. Broad-leaved weeds, such as daisies, respond with fast, **uncontrolled growth** and die. Narrow-leaved plants such as grass and cereals do not respond in the same way, so the crop survives."}, {"h": "Ethene: ripening fruit", "b": "**Ethene** is a gas that makes fruit **ripen**. Growers pick fruit **unripe**, because it is firmer and bruises less. They store it cool, which slows ripening. Near the shops they add ethene, so the fruit ripens at the right time."}, {"h": "Gibberellins: seeds and fruit", "b": "**Gibberellins** have three uses. They **end seed dormancy**, so seeds germinate when sown. They promote flowering. They also **increase fruit size**, so growers have larger fruit to sell."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how growers use plant hormones to (a) get cuttings to grow roots, (b) kill weeds in a wheat field and (c) have ripe fruit in shops. (6 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Cuttings are dipped in rooting powder that contains auxin.", "why": "Names the hormone and its use. Auxin stimulates root growth from the cut stem."}, {"do": "So the cuttings grow roots quickly and develop into new plants.", "why": "States the result: many new plants from one parent, fast."}, {"do": "A synthetic auxin weedkiller is sprayed over the wheat field.", "why": "Names the hormone for weeds. It is selective: it affects broad-leaved weeds, not cereals."}, {"do": "The broad-leaved weeds grow fast in an uncontrolled way and die, but the narrow-leaved wheat survives.", "why": "Links the hormone to its effect, instead of just saying it kills weeds."}, {"do": "Fruit is picked unripe and stored cool. Near the shops it is treated with ethene, so it ripens on time.", "why": "Ethene triggers ripening. Unripe fruit is firmer, so it travels without damage."}], "answer": "Cuttings are dipped in rooting powder containing auxin, which makes roots grow so new plants form quickly. Synthetic auxin weedkiller makes broad-leaved weeds grow out of control and die, while wheat survives. Fruit is picked unripe, then ethene is added to ripen it just before sale."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer say the wheat survives the weedkiller?", "o": ["Wheat makes ethene, which cancels out the effect of the synthetic auxin", "Wheat has no auxin of its own, so the weedkiller cannot affect it", "The weedkiller is selective: narrow-leaved wheat does not respond as the weeds do", "The weedkiller only touches the weeds because it is sprayed on them one by one"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Ethene is the ripening hormone. It does not cancel auxin, and this is not why wheat survives.", "Wrong. All plants make their own auxin. Wheat survives because it responds differently, not because it lacks auxin.", "Right. Synthetic auxin weedkillers are selective. Broad-leaved weeds respond with abnormal growth and die. Narrow-leaved cereals do not respond in the same way.", "Wrong. The whole field is sprayed. The weedkiller works because of how the plants respond, not careful aiming."]}, "faded": {"task": "A grower sows seeds that stay dormant, then wants large fruit. Explain how gibberellins can be used.", "given": ["Gibberellins are applied to the seeds.", "They end seed dormancy, so the seeds germinate when sown."], "q": "Which point should come next to explain how gibberellins help with fruit?", "o": ["Gibberellins sprayed on the plants later make the fruit ripen, which makes it bigger.", "Gibberellins sprayed on the plants later stop the fruit growing, so it stores longer.", "Ethene sprayed on the plants later increases fruit size, so the crop is bigger.", "Gibberellins sprayed on the plants later increase fruit size, so the crop is bigger."], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. This mixes up ripening with size. Ripening is controlled by ethene, and ripe fruit is not bigger because of it.", "Wrong. Gibberellins do not stop fruit growing. They increase fruit size, which is what this grower wants.", "Wrong. This swaps the hormones. Ethene controls ripening. Gibberellins are the ones that increase fruit size.", "Right. Gibberellins increase fruit size. This continues the answer by linking the same hormone to a second use."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the hormones, or saying weedkiller auxin stops growth. It makes weeds grow too fast and abnormally. Link them: auxin = roots and weeds, ethene = ripening, gibberellins = seeds and fruit size.", "recap": "Auxin roots cuttings and kills broad-leaved weeds, ethene ripens fruit, and gibberellins end seed dormancy, promote flowering and enlarge fruit."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.6.1.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 often asks short explain or compare questions on reproduction that link meiosis, chromosome numbers and variation.", "hook": {"q": "A human body cell has 46 chromosomes. If an egg and a sperm each had 46 too, how many would the fertilised egg have?", "o": ["23", "46", "92", "69"], "a": 2, "f": ["23 is what a gamete actually has. But if gametes carried 46, the total after fusing would be 46 + 46, not 23.", "46 would only work if the gametes had 23 each. Here each gamete has 46, so the two sets add together.", "Right: 46 + 46 = 92. The number would double every generation, so something must halve it when gametes are made. That is meiosis.", "69 is half of 138 or 1.5 times 46. It has no link to the sum. Fertilisation adds the two gametes' chromosomes: 46 + 46."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Two types of reproduction", "b": "**Sexual reproduction** needs two parents. Their gametes (sex cells) join at fertilisation, so the offspring are genetically different from each other and from the parents.\n\n**Asexual reproduction** needs one parent. There are no gametes and no fusion. The offspring are **clones**, genetically identical to the parent."}, {"h": "Meiosis makes gametes", "b": "**Meiosis** is cell division that makes gametes. In humans it happens only in the ovaries and testes.\n\nThe cell copies its genetic information, then divides **twice**. This gives **four** gametes. Each has a single set of chromosomes (23 in humans). Each gamete is genetically different from the others."}, {"h": "Fertilisation restores the number", "b": "At **fertilisation**, an egg and a sperm fuse: 23 + 23 = 46. The full number is restored.\n\nThe new cell then divides by **mitosis** many times and develops into an embryo. Mitosis makes identical cells with 46 chromosomes. Genes from two parents are mixed, which gives **variation**."}, {"h": "Pros and cons", "b": "**Sexual:** variation means some offspring may survive if the environment changes or a disease arrives. Humans can use it for selective breeding. But it needs two parents and is slower.\n\n**Asexual:** only one parent is needed. It is faster and makes many offspring. But there is no variation, so all are at risk from the same change or disease."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how meiosis and fertilisation keep the human chromosome number at 46 and also make offspring different from their parents.", "steps": [{"do": "Meiosis happens in the ovaries and testes. The cell copies its genetic information, then divides twice.", "why": "This names where meiosis happens and what it does. It is the start of the process."}, {"do": "This makes four gametes, each with 23 chromosomes, which is half the normal number.", "why": "The halving is the key idea. It is what stops the number doubling."}, {"do": "Each gamete is genetically different from the others.", "why": "This is the first source of variation, and it comes before fertilisation."}, {"do": "At fertilisation, the egg (23) and sperm (23) fuse to give a cell with 46 chromosomes.", "why": "This shows the full number is restored, using the numbers."}, {"do": "The offspring gets a mixture of genes from two parents, so it differs from both of them.", "why": "This links fertilisation to variation, which answers the second part of the question."}], "answer": "Meiosis in the ovaries and testes makes four genetically different gametes, each with 23 chromosomes. At fertilisation two gametes fuse, restoring 46. The offspring has a mixture of genes from both parents, so it is different from each of them."}, "selfx": {"q": "In the worked example, why does the answer say meiosis gives gametes only 23 chromosomes?", "o": ["So that all the gametes are identical to the parent cell", "So that fertilisation restores 46 rather than doubling it", "So that the embryo only needs half of the genes to grow", "So that mitosis does not need to happen after fertilisation"], "a": 1, "f": ["Meiosis gives gametes that are genetically different, not identical to the parent cell. Identical cells come from mitosis.", "Right: two gametes each with 23 join to make 46. Without halving, the number would double in every generation.", "The embryo needs a full set of genes, and fertilisation supplies it. Halving happens so the two halves can add up to a whole.", "Mitosis is still needed after fertilisation, because the fertilised egg must divide many times to form the embryo."]}, "faded": {"task": "A farmer grows a field of plants that were all produced by asexual reproduction. Explain why a new disease could kill the whole field.", "given": ["Asexual reproduction uses one parent and mitosis.", "So all the plants are genetically identical clones."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["So meiosis makes a few plants different, and those few survive the disease", "So they have half the chromosomes of the parent, which makes them weak against disease", "So the plants grow too fast to make enough seeds, and the disease spreads quickly", "So if one plant is not resistant to the disease, none of the plants are resistant"], "a": 3, "f": ["Asexual reproduction does not use meiosis. Meiosis makes gametes for sexual reproduction, which is where the variation comes from.", "Asexual offspring have the full number of chromosomes, copied by mitosis. Halving only happens in meiosis.", "Growing fast is not the reason. The point is that identical genes mean the same weakness to the disease.", "Right: with no variation, there are no resistant plants. A disease that can infect one plant can infect all of them."]}, "trap": "Saying meiosis makes two identical cells, or that a sperm has 46 chromosomes. Meiosis makes four different cells, each with 23. Mitosis makes two identical cells with 46.", "recap": "Meiosis makes four genetically different gametes with half the chromosomes; fertilisation restores the full number and mixes genes, giving variation."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.6.1.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 asks short explain questions linking DNA, genes, proteins and mutations, and you earn marks by building a chain of linked points.", "hook": {"q": "DNA has four bases (A, T, C, G). Cells need about 20 different amino acids. What problem would there be if one base coded for one amino acid?", "o": ["The DNA would be too long to fit inside the nucleus", "The proteins made would all be far too large", "Only four different amino acids could be coded for", "The bases could not stay together in the double helix"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. The length of DNA is not the issue here. The issue is how many different codes four bases can make on their own.", "Wrong. Protein size depends on how many amino acids are joined, not on how many bases code for each one.", "Right. Four bases would give only four codes, so only four amino acids. Groups of three bases give enough different codes for all of them.", "Wrong. The double helix holds two strands together. It has nothing to do with how many bases code for one amino acid."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "DNA structure", "b": "DNA is a **polymer** made of repeating units called **nucleotides**. Each nucleotide has a sugar, a phosphate group and one base: A, T, C or G. The sugar and phosphate groups alternate to form a backbone. Two strands twist together as a **double helix**. Bases pair across the strands: A with T, C with G."}, {"h": "Genes and chromosomes", "b": "DNA is found in **chromosomes**. A **gene** is a small section of DNA that codes for one protein. The order of bases sets the order of amino acids. A **triplet** of three bases codes for one amino acid. So 90 bases code for 30 amino acids."}, {"h": "Making a protein", "b": "Proteins are made on **ribosomes**, using a template copied from the gene. **Carrier molecules** bring specific amino acids, which are added in the correct order. The finished chain folds into a **unique shape**. This shape lets it work as an enzyme, hormone or structural protein."}, {"h": "Mutations and the genome", "b": "A **mutation** is a change in the base sequence of DNA. In a gene it may change the amino acids, so the protein may fold differently and not work. A mutation in **non-coding** DNA can change how much protein is made. The **genome** is all of an organism's genetic material."}], "example": {"task": "A mutation changes one base in a gene. The enzyme made from this gene stops working. Explain how the mutation could stop the enzyme working. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "State that the mutation changes the base sequence, so one triplet of bases in the gene is different.", "why": "Start from the DNA. The mutation is a change in the bases."}, {"do": "Say the changed triplet may code for a different amino acid, so the order of amino acids in the protein changes.", "why": "Base order sets amino acid order. This links DNA to the protein."}, {"do": "Say the chain of amino acids then folds into a different shape.", "why": "The amino acid order decides how the chain folds."}, {"do": "Say the active site no longer fits the substrate, so the enzyme cannot work.", "why": "Enzymes work only if the active site matches the substrate. This links shape to function."}], "answer": "The mutation changes a triplet of bases in the gene. This may change an amino acid, so the order of amino acids changes. The protein chain then folds into a different shape. The enzyme's active site no longer fits the substrate, so the enzyme cannot work."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the protein folds into a different shape?", "o": ["The changed base is built into the protein chain and bends it", "Amino acid order decides folding, so a new order can give a new shape", "The mutation adds extra chromosomes, which squash the protein", "Ribosomes change the shape of every protein, whatever the gene says"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Bases are part of DNA, not of the protein. Proteins are chains of amino acids only.", "Right. The amino acid order controls the folding. A changed order can give a changed shape, which is why the mutation matters.", "Wrong. A single-base mutation does not add chromosomes. It changes the base sequence within one gene.", "Wrong. Ribosomes build the chain by following the gene's code. The code, not the ribosome, decides the final shape."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the order of bases in a gene decides the structure of the protein it codes for. (3 marks)", "given": ["A gene is a section of DNA. Each triplet of bases codes for one amino acid.", "So the order of bases sets the order of amino acids in the protein."], "q": "Which point should come next in the answer?", "o": ["The chain folds into its unique shape first, and then the amino acids are added.", "Each base codes for one amino acid, so the protein has as many amino acids as bases.", "The ribosome copies the gene to make a new DNA strand, which becomes the protein.", "Carrier molecules bring amino acids to a ribosome in the order the triplets specify."], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. The order is reversed. The amino acids are joined into a chain first, and the finished chain then folds.", "Wrong. It is a triplet of three bases, not one base, that codes for one amino acid. There are fewer amino acids than bases.", "Wrong. A protein is made of amino acids, not DNA. The ribosome does not make DNA when it builds a protein.", "Right. Carrier molecules bring specific amino acids to the ribosome in the order the triplets code for. This links the base order to the amino acid order."]}, "trap": "Writing that a mutation 'changes the protein' and stopping. Complete the chain: base order changes, amino acid order may change, shape changes, function is lost. Some mutations change nothing, so write 'may'.", "recap": "Three bases code for one amino acid; base order sets amino acid order, which sets protein shape, so a mutation can change function."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.6.1.6", "kind": "method", "why": "Paper 2 often asks you to complete a genetic cross and give a chance as a fraction, ratio or percentage, usually for a few marks.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Dominant and recessive", "b": "You have two **alleles** of each gene, one from each parent. A **dominant** allele (capital letter) shows even with one copy. A **recessive** allele (small letter) shows only when both alleles are recessive. The pair of alleles is the genotype. What you can see is the phenotype."}, {"h": "Drawing a cross", "b": "Write the parents' genotypes. Split each into **gametes**, one allele each. Put one parent's gametes along the top and the other's down the side. Fill each box of the **Punnett square** with two alleles. Heterozygous means two different alleles. Then count the boxes that match the question."}, {"h": "Sex determination", "b": "Human females are **XX** and males are **XY**. Every egg carries an X. Half of the sperm carry X and half carry Y. So the **sperm** decides the sex of the baby. Each baby has a 50% chance of being a boy and a 50% chance of being a girl."}, {"h": "Inherited disorders", "b": "Some disorders are inherited. Polydactyly is caused by a dominant allele. Cystic fibrosis is caused by a recessive allele. A **carrier** has one recessive allele and no symptoms. Two carriers have 1 in 4 affected, 2 in 4 carriers (50%), 1 in 4 neither. Each child's chances are **independent**."}], "example": {"task": "Polydactyly is caused by a dominant allele, D. A father has polydactyly, but his own mother does not. His partner does not have it. What is the chance their child has polydactyly?", "steps": [{"do": "Set the key: D = polydactyly (dominant), d = no polydactyly (recessive).", "why": "A capital letter is the dominant allele, so the key makes the later genotypes clear."}, {"do": "Partner: dd. Father: Dd.", "why": "She is unaffected, so dd. His mother is dd, giving him d. He is affected, so his other allele is D."}, {"do": "Gametes: father D or d. Partner d only.", "why": "Each gamete carries one allele from the pair."}, {"do": "Put D and d along the top. Put d and d down the side. Fill in the boxes: Dd, dd, Dd, dd.", "why": "Each box joins one gamete from each parent."}, {"do": "Count the boxes with a D allele: 2 out of 4.", "why": "One D allele is enough for polydactyly, because D is dominant."}, {"do": "Write the chance: 2 in 4 = 50%.", "why": "The question asks for a chance, so give a fraction or percentage."}], "answer": "50% (1 in 2). Dd gives polydactyly and dd does not. The same chance applies to every child they have."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the partner's genotype written as dd?", "o": ["Recessive alleles are the most common, so she must have two of them", "She has no polydactyly, and one D would show, so she has no D allele", "She is a carrier, so she has one d allele and one D allele", "Women always have two recessive alleles for this gene"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. How common an allele is does not decide a genotype. Her phenotype does.", "Right. D is dominant, so even one copy would give polydactyly. No condition means both alleles are d.", "Wrong. With a dominant allele, one D shows. A Dd person would have polydactyly, so she would not be unaffected.", "Wrong. Sex does not decide which alleles a gene has. Only the X and Y chromosomes differ."]}, "faded": {"task": "Black fur (B) is dominant to brown fur (b) in mice. Two black mice, both Bb, are crossed. Find the chance of a brown baby.", "given": ["Parents: Bb × Bb", "Gametes: B or b from each parent"], "q": "What are the four offspring genotypes in the Punnett square?", "o": ["BB, BB, bb, bb", "Bb, Bb, Bb, Bb", "BB, Bb, Bb, bb", "BB, Bb, bb, bb"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. This pairs like with like. Each top gamete must join each side gamete, which gives BB, Bb, Bb, bb.", "Wrong. Four Bb would need one parent to be BB and the other bb. Both parents here are Bb.", "Right. B+B gives BB, b+B and B+b give Bb twice, and b+b gives bb. Brown is bb: 1 in 4, or 25%.", "Wrong. This has bb twice, but only one box has two b gametes meeting. Fill each box from its row and column."]}, "trap": "Giving 25% for a carrier because bb or cc is 25%. Count the boxes that match the question. Also, an affected child does not make the next one safe. Each child has the same chances.", "recap": "Write the genotypes, split them into gametes, fill the Punnett square, then count the boxes that match the question."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.6.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to explain how natural selection changes a population of an unfamiliar animal or plant, and the same steps work every time.", "hook": {"q": "Green and brown beetles live on dark brown soil. Birds hunt them by sight. Which beetles are more likely to survive long enough to breed?", "o": ["Green beetles, because there are fewer of them", "Both colours equally, because birds eat at random", "Brown beetles, because birds find them harder to see", "Green beetles, because they will change colour to hide"], "a": 2, "f": ["Rarity does not protect an individual. Birds see green beetles easily, so they are more likely to be eaten.", "Birds hunt by sight, so colour matters. Beetles that match the soil are less likely to be spotted.", "Brown beetles blend in with the dark soil, so they are less likely to be eaten and more likely to breed.", "Beetles cannot change colour on purpose to suit their surroundings. Their colour is set by the alleles they inherit."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why individuals differ", "b": "Differences between individuals of the same species are called **variation**. It can come from the **genes** they inherit, from the **environment** they grow up in, or from both. For example, your height depends on your genes and on your diet. The graph (example data, 100 students) shows a spread of heights (continuous variation), not just two types.", "fig": {"type": "bar", "cats": ["150-154", "155-159", "160-164", "165-169", "170-174", "175-179", "180-184"], "vals": [5, 10, 20, 30, 20, 10, 5], "y": [0, 35], "ystep": 5, "xlabel": "Height in cm", "ylabel": "Number of students"}}, {"h": "Mutations make new alleles", "b": "A **mutation** is a random change in DNA. Mutations happen continuously. Most have no effect on the **phenotype**. Very rarely, one gives a new phenotype. If the environment changes, that new phenotype may suit it better and give an advantage."}, {"h": "How natural selection works", "b": "Living things compete for food, space and mates. Individuals with features better suited to the environment are more likely to **survive and breed**. They pass on their **alleles**. Over generations, those alleles become more common in the population. This is **natural selection**."}, {"h": "Evolution and new species", "b": "Natural selection changes the inherited features of a population over time. This is **evolution**. If populations become so different that they can no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring, they are different **species**."}], "example": {"task": "Foxes in a cold region now mostly have thick fur. Their ancestors had a mix of thick and thin fur. Explain how natural selection led to this change. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Foxes in the population varied in fur thickness, because they had different alleles. These alleles came from mutations.", "why": "Natural selection needs variation to exist first. The environment does not create it."}, {"do": "In cold winters, thick-furred foxes lost less heat, so they were more likely to survive.", "why": "This links the feature to the environment and gives the advantage."}, {"do": "The surviving foxes were more likely to breed, so they had more offspring.", "why": "Survival only matters for evolution if the individual reproduces."}, {"do": "These offspring inherited the alleles for thick fur from their parents.", "why": "Alleles are the thing passed on, not the fur itself."}, {"do": "Over many generations, the thick-fur allele became more common, so most foxes now have thick fur.", "why": "Repeated selection changes the whole population over time."}], "answer": "Foxes varied in fur thickness because of different alleles. In cold winters, thick-furred foxes were more likely to survive and breed. They passed on the allele for thick fur. Over many generations it became more common."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why must the answer say that thick-furred foxes breed, not just survive?", "o": ["Breeding makes the parents' fur grow thicker in their lifetime", "Thick fur makes foxes more fertile than thin-furred foxes", "Survival alone automatically changes the alleles in the next generation", "Alleles are only passed on if the survivors reproduce"], "a": 3, "f": ["Breeding does not change a parent's fur. Features gained in life are not passed on. Only alleles are inherited.", "Fur thickness is not linked to fertility here. The advantage is surviving the cold, then reproducing.", "A survivor with no offspring passes on nothing. Alleles only spread through reproduction.", "Alleles reach the next generation only through offspring. Without breeding, selection changes nothing."]}, "faded": {"task": "In a dry region, some plants have longer roots than others. Explain how most plants in the population came to have long roots.", "given": ["Plants in the population varied in root length because they had different alleles.", "In dry years, plants with longer roots reached more water, so they were more likely to survive."], "q": "What is the next point in the answer?", "o": ["The plants grew longer roots because they needed water, then passed this on", "Plants with short roots mutated on purpose so that their roots grew longer", "Farmers chose the long-rooted plants to breed, so the allele spread", "The survivors reproduced, passing the long-root allele to their offspring"], "a": 3, "f": ["This says features gained in life are inherited. The roots were already long because of alleles. Needing water does not change them.", "Mutations are random, not on purpose. The variation was already there, as the first step says.", "This describes selective breeding, where humans choose. Here the dry environment does the selecting.", "Survivors breed and pass on the allele. Repeating this over generations makes it common."]}, "trap": "Do not write that individuals change on purpose to fit their environment. The variation already exists, from random mutations. Selection only changes which alleles become common, so write \"survive, breed, pass on alleles\".", "recap": "Variation already exists; individuals suited to the environment survive, breed and pass on their alleles, so those alleles become more common over generations."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.6.2.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Selective breeding comes up in Paper 2 as a short 'describe' or 'explain' question, often about a crop or farm animal you have not met before.", "hook": {"q": "A farmer wants sheep that grow more wool. Which plan is most likely to work over many years?", "o": ["Give all the sheep extra food so their lambs are born with more wool", "Breed the best wool producers together, then keep breeding from their best lambs", "Breed sheep at random and shear them more often so the wool grows thicker", "Breed sheep with the least wool, because they will pass on more variation"], "a": 1, "f": ["Extra food changes how the sheep grow, not the alleles they pass on. Lambs are not born with features their parents gained during life.", "Right. The farmer chooses parents with the wanted feature and repeats this with the best offspring each time.", "Random breeding does not favour wool. Shearing does not change alleles either, so nothing is passed on.", "Choosing sheep with the least wool moves the flock away from the wanted feature. Selection must be for the feature you want."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What it means", "b": "**Selective breeding** (artificial selection) means humans choose which organisms breed, so that wanted features are passed on. Examples: **disease resistance** in crops, animals that give more meat or milk, dogs with a gentle nature, and large or unusual flowers."}, {"h": "The four steps", "b": "1. Choose **parents** with the wanted feature.\n2. Breed them together.\n3. Pick the best **offspring** and breed from them.\n4. Repeat over many **generations**.\n\nEach generation, the feature becomes stronger or more common."}, {"h": "Why it is slow", "b": "Features are inherited through **alleles**. Only offspring with the wanted alleles are chosen to breed, so those alleles become more common. One round is not enough. It takes many **generations**. Humans choose the parents. They do not change genes directly."}, {"h": "The risks", "b": "Breeding from a few chosen animals or plants reduces **variation**. Related individuals may breed together. This is **inbreeding**. Harmful alleles are then more likely to be inherited from both parents, causing inherited defects. A new disease could also harm many, as few may be resistant."}], "example": {"task": "A dairy farmer wants cows that give more milk. Describe how she could use selective breeding to get them, and give one risk.", "steps": [{"do": "Choose the cows that give the most milk. Breed them with a bull whose mother was a high milk producer.", "why": "Parents with the wanted feature are chosen. This is the start of selective breeding."}, {"do": "From the calves, pick the ones that later give the most milk, and breed from those.", "why": "Only the best offspring are kept, so alleles for high yield are passed on."}, {"do": "Repeat this process over many generations.", "why": "Each generation the wanted alleles become more common, so milk yield keeps rising."}, {"do": "Risk: the chosen cows may be closely related, so this is inbreeding.", "why": "Inbreeding reduces variation in the herd."}, {"do": "Harmful alleles are more likely to be inherited from both parents, so more calves have inherited diseases.", "why": "This links the risk to its effect, which earns the mark."}], "answer": "Choose the highest-yielding cows and breed them. Select the best calves and breed from them. Repeat over many generations. A risk is inbreeding, which reduces variation and makes inherited diseases more likely."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the process is repeated over many generations?", "o": ["Mutations are needed each time to change the genes the farmer wants", "Cows that eat more in one generation pass on a bigger milk yield", "One round of breeding makes all the calves identical clones", "Each round keeps the best offspring, so wanted alleles become more common"], "a": 3, "f": ["Selective breeding uses alleles already in the population. It does not rely on mutations, and farmers cannot make them happen.", "This is the wrong idea that features gained in life are inherited. Only alleles are passed on, not effects of diet.", "Breeding two cows does not give clones. Offspring still vary, which is why the farmer must pick the best each time.", "Right. Repeating selection each generation raises the proportion of high-yield alleles, so yield increases step by step."]}, "faded": {"task": "A grower wants sweet peas with larger flowers. Complete the selective breeding method.", "given": ["Choose parent plants with the largest flowers.", "Cross-pollinate them and collect their seeds."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Take cuttings from the best plant to grow copies of it", "Pick the offspring with the smallest flowers to keep variation high", "Grow the seeds, then breed from the offspring with the largest flowers", "Add fertiliser to the seeds so the flowers grow larger, then stop"], "a": 2, "f": ["Cuttings are cloning, not selective breeding. Cloning makes identical plants and does not select from offspring.", "Choosing small flowers moves away from the aim. The grower must select offspring that show the wanted feature.", "Right. The best offspring are selected and bred, and this is then repeated over many generations.", "Fertiliser affects growth, not the alleles passed on. Stopping after one round would not make the feature stronger."]}, "trap": "Do not say selective breeding is fast or changes genes directly. It takes many generations, and humans only choose which organisms breed. Inbreeding can make inherited diseases more likely.", "recap": "Selective breeding means choosing parents with the wanted feature and repeating over many generations, which reduces variation and risks inbreeding and disease."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.6.2.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to describe how a gene is moved into a cell, or to give an example and a concern about GM crops.", "hook": {"q": "Bacteria cannot normally make human insulin. What must a bacterium be given so that it can?", "o": ["Some human insulin to store inside it", "A copy of the human gene that codes for insulin", "A whole human cell to live inside it", "Extra ribosomes to build insulin faster"], "a": 1, "f": ["Insulin given to the bacterium would just sit there. The bacterium needs the instructions to make the protein itself, and a gene is those instructions.", "Right. A gene is a section of DNA that codes for a protein. With the insulin gene, the bacterium can make insulin itself.", "A whole cell contains thousands of genes and does not become part of the bacterium. Only the one gene needed is transferred.", "Bacteria already have ribosomes. Without the insulin gene they have no instructions for insulin, so ribosomes are not what is missing."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What it means", "b": "**Genetic engineering** changes the **genome** of an organism. A gene from another organism is added to give a desired characteristic. A gene is a section of DNA that codes for a protein. The new organism then makes that protein."}, {"h": "Moving the gene", "b": "First, **enzymes** isolate the gene by cutting it out of the chromosome. Next, the gene is inserted into a **vector**, usually a bacterial **plasmid** or a virus. Finally, the vector carries the gene into the new cells. The order matters. (Higher Tier detail.)"}, {"h": "Examples", "b": "Bacteria are engineered to make **human insulin** to treat diabetes. **GM crops** can be resistant to disease or insect attack, or give bigger, better fruit. Genes are added at an early stage, such as an embryo, so every cell that grows from it carries the gene."}, {"h": "Concerns", "b": "Some people worry that GM crops could harm populations of **wild flowers** and **insects**. Others feel the effects of eating GM crops on **human health** have not been fully explored. Good answers weigh these concerns against benefits such as bigger, better fruit."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how bacteria can be genetically engineered to make human insulin. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Enzymes cut the insulin gene out of a human chromosome.", "why": "This isolates only the gene needed, not the rest of the DNA."}, {"do": "The gene is inserted into a vector, a bacterial plasmid.", "why": "The vector is used to get the gene into the bacterial cell."}, {"do": "The plasmid carries the gene into a bacterial cell.", "why": "The bacterium now contains the human gene."}, {"do": "The bacteria divide, so every new cell has a copy of the gene.", "why": "This gives many bacteria that can all make insulin."}, {"do": "The bacteria use the gene to make human insulin, which is collected.", "why": "The gene codes for the protein. The insulin treats diabetes."}], "answer": "Enzymes cut out the human insulin gene. It is inserted into a plasmid vector. The plasmid carries the gene into bacteria. The bacteria divide and use the gene to make insulin, which is collected to treat diabetes."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why are enzymes used in the first step, before the gene goes into the plasmid?", "o": ["They make the insulin gene longer so it fits the plasmid", "They make the bacterium divide so more copies are made", "They cut out and isolate just the gene that is needed", "They turn the gene into the insulin protein directly"], "a": 2, "f": ["Enzymes do not lengthen genes. They cut DNA. Isolating the gene is what lets it be put into a vector.", "Cell division comes later, after the gene is inside the bacterium. Enzymes here are for cutting, not for making cells divide.", "Right. Enzymes cut the required gene out of the chromosome, so that only this gene is put into the vector.", "Proteins are made by cells using the gene, not by enzymes acting on the gene itself. Enzymes at this step only cut the DNA."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a gene for insect resistance can be put into a crop plant. Steps 1 and 2 are done.", "given": ["Enzymes isolate the insect-resistance gene by cutting it out of the other organism's chromosome.", "The gene is inserted into a vector, such as a plasmid or a virus."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["The vector carries the gene into plant cells at an early stage of development", "The gene is injected straight into the leaves of a fully grown plant", "Enzymes cut the gene out of the vector again to isolate it", "The plants with the gene are cross-bred over many generations"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. The vector carries the gene into the plant cells. At an early stage, every cell that grows from them gets a copy of the gene.", "A fully grown plant has millions of cells. Only a few would receive the gene, so the whole plant would not be resistant. The gene should go in early.", "The gene has just been put into the vector. Cutting it out again would undo the step. The vector's job is to carry the gene in.", "Cross-breeding over generations is selective breeding, not genetic engineering. Here the gene is moved directly into the cells."]}, "trap": "Putting the gene straight into the bacterium, or inserting it before it has been cut out. First isolate the gene with enzymes, then put it into a vector, then the vector carries it in.", "recap": "Enzymes cut out the gene, a vector such as a plasmid carries it into the new cell, and the cell then makes the protein."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.6.2.5", "kind": "idea", "why": "Cloning questions usually ask you to describe how one named method makes identical organisms, so you need the steps in the right order and the reason for each.", "hook": {"q": "A gardener cuts a shoot off a rose bush and plants it in compost. It grows roots and becomes a new plant. How do its genes compare with the parent's?", "o": ["Half are from the parent and half come from the compost", "They are exactly the same, as it grew from the parent's own cells", "They are different, because the new plant is in a different pot", "They are mixed, because the shoot must be pollinated first"], "a": 1, "f": ["Compost does not give a plant genes. Genes come only from the cells of the organism.", "Right. The shoot's cells divide by mitosis, so the new plant has the same genes as the parent. It is a clone.", "Surroundings can change how a plant looks, such as its size. They do not change its genes.", "Pollination joins gametes from two parents, which mixes genes. A cutting involves no gametes."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Clones and cuttings", "b": "A **clone** is an organism with exactly the same genes as another. Clones come from one parent, so there is no mixing of genes from two gametes. A **cutting** is a piece of stem cut from a parent plant and planted. It grows roots and becomes a clone of the parent."}, {"h": "Tissue culture", "b": "**Tissue culture** grows new plants from small groups of cells taken from a parent plant. The cells are placed in a sterile dish with a nutrient medium and **hormones**. They grow into plantlets. It makes many identical plants quickly and can save rare species."}, {"h": "Embryo transplants", "b": "In an **embryo transplant**, sperm and egg from chosen animals are joined in a lab. The embryo is split into single cells while they are still **unspecialised**. Each cell grows into a new embryo. These are placed in host mothers. The young are clones of each other, not of their parents."}, {"h": "Adult cell cloning", "b": "In **adult cell cloning**, the **nucleus** is removed from an unfertilised egg. A nucleus from an adult body cell is put in its place. An electric shock makes the cell divide to form an embryo. The embryo is implanted in a womb. The clone has the genes of the nucleus donor."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how adult cell cloning could produce a lamb that is genetically identical to a prize-winning ram.", "steps": [{"do": "Take the nucleus from a body cell of the ram.", "why": "A body cell has a full set of chromosomes, so it carries all the ram's genes."}, {"do": "Remove the nucleus from an unfertilised egg cell taken from a ewe.", "why": "This leaves no ewe genes in the egg, so only the ram's genes will be used."}, {"do": "Insert the ram's nucleus into the empty egg cell.", "why": "The nucleus carries the genes, so the new cell now has the ram's genes."}, {"do": "Give the cell an electric shock so it divides to form an embryo.", "why": "The shock stimulates division. No sperm fertilises the egg."}, {"do": "Implant the embryo into the womb of a surrogate ewe, where it grows into a lamb.", "why": "The embryo needs a womb to develop. The lamb is a clone of the ram."}], "answer": "A nucleus is taken from a ram's body cell. The nucleus is removed from an unfertilised egg cell, and the ram's nucleus is put in. An electric shock makes the cell divide into an embryo. The embryo is implanted into a surrogate ewe's womb. The lamb has the ram's genes, so it is his clone."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer remove the egg cell's own nucleus in step 2?", "o": ["So that a sperm cell can fertilise the egg instead", "So that the egg cell is able to divide after the shock", "So that the lamb will carry genes from both ram and ewe", "So that the lamb's genes come only from the ram's nucleus"], "a": 3, "f": ["No sperm is used in adult cell cloning. The egg is not fertilised, and the electric shock starts division.", "Removing the nucleus is not what makes division happen. The electric shock does that. The nucleus is removed to control which genes the embryo has.", "This is the reverse of the aim. A clone must have genes from one donor only, not a mix of two.", "Right. The nucleus holds the genes. With the ewe's nucleus gone, the lamb has only the ram's genes."]}, "faded": {"task": "Describe how tissue culture can be used to produce many identical orchid plants from one parent plant.", "given": ["Take small groups of cells from the parent orchid.", "Place them in a sterile dish of agar jelly containing nutrients."], "q": "Which step comes next?", "o": ["Fertilise the cells with pollen from another orchid so that seeds form", "Remove each cell's nucleus and put in a nucleus from another orchid", "Add plant hormones so the cells grow into small plantlets with roots and shoots", "Give the cells an electric shock so that they start to divide"], "a": 2, "f": ["Fertilising would mix genes from two parents, so the plants would not be identical. Tissue culture uses no gametes.", "This is adult cell cloning, an animal method. Tissue culture uses whole cells from the parent plant.", "Right. Hormones make the cells grow and develop into plantlets. These are clones, and can then be planted in compost.", "An electric shock belongs to adult cell cloning in animals. Plant cells in tissue culture are given nutrients and hormones."]}, "trap": "Thinking the egg is fertilised by sperm in adult cell cloning. Its own nucleus is removed, so the clone matches the adult nucleus donor. In embryo transplants, clones match each other, not the host mother.", "recap": "Clones are genetically identical because their genes come from one source: in adult cell cloning, that source is the nucleus put into the empty egg."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.6.3.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 often asks how a feature became common or a new species formed, and marks depend on using Darwin's steps, not Lamarck's.", "hook": {"q": "Some birds in a population have slightly longer beaks. Their food is deep inside flowers. What do you expect over many generations?", "o": ["Birds stretch their beaks while feeding, so every bird's beak grows longer", "Longer-beaked birds feed better, survive and breed more, so long beaks become common", "All birds slowly grow longer beaks because the population needs them", "Short-beaked birds turn into long-beaked birds as they get older"], "a": 1, "f": ["Stretching during life is Lamarck's idea. Features gained like this are not usually inherited, so the young would not have longer beaks.", "Right. The variation already existed. Better-fed birds survive and breed, so their long-beak alleles spread through the population.", "Populations do not change because they need to. Change happens because some existing individuals survive and breed better.", "Individual birds do not change type as they age. A population changes over generations, as better-suited individuals breed more."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Darwin's natural selection", "b": "Individuals in a species show **variation**. Those best suited to their environment are more likely to **survive and breed**. They pass helpful features on to their young. Over many generations these features become more common. This is **natural selection**. Alfred Russel Wallace reached the same idea independently."}, {"h": "Lamarck's different idea", "b": "Lamarck suggested that features an animal **gained during its life**, by using a body part a lot, were passed on to its young. The evidence does not support this: such changes are not usually inherited. Natural selection is supported by evidence, including our knowledge of **genes**."}, {"h": "How new species form", "b": "A **species** is a group that can breed to produce **fertile offspring**. A new species can form if a population is split by **isolation**, such as a river or sea. Conditions differ on each side, so natural selection favours different features. Eventually the groups cannot breed to produce fertile offspring."}, {"h": "Mendel and genes", "b": "Mendel bred pea plants in the 1860s. He showed that features are passed on as separate **inherited units**, not blended. His work was not accepted until after his death. In the early 1900s the units were linked to **chromosomes** and called **genes**. This explained the inheritance Darwin could not."}], "example": {"task": "Explain, using Darwin's theory, how a population of antelope became faster over many generations when lions hunted them.", "steps": [{"do": "The antelope in the population varied in running speed.", "why": "Natural selection starts with variation. Some of it is due to different alleles."}, {"do": "Lions caught the slower antelope more easily, so the faster antelope were more likely to survive.", "why": "This links the feature to survival in this environment."}, {"do": "The faster antelope lived long enough to breed.", "why": "Survival only matters for evolution if it leads to breeding."}, {"do": "They passed on their alleles for speed to their offspring.", "why": "Alleles carry the feature to the next generation. It is not a skill gained during life."}, {"do": "Over many generations, more of the population had alleles for speed, so average speed rose.", "why": "This shows gradual change across the whole population."}], "answer": "Antelope varied in speed. Lions caught slower ones, so faster ones survived and bred. They passed on alleles for speed. Over many generations these alleles became more common, so the population became faster."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 4 say the antelope passed on alleles for speed, not that they trained to run faster?", "o": ["Faster antelope grow new alleles during each chase, which they then pass on", "Alleles are passed on only if the antelope has trained hard enough to be fast", "Only inherited alleles reach offspring; a skill gained in life is not usually passed on", "Natural selection changes the alleles of each antelope as it runs from lions"], "a": 2, "f": ["Alleles do not grow in response to chases. This is Lamarck's idea in disguise. Speed is passed on through alleles already present.", "Training is not needed for alleles to be passed on. Alleles in gametes are inherited whatever the animal did in life.", "Right. Offspring inherit alleles from gametes. Features gained during life, such as fitness from training, are not usually inherited.", "Natural selection does not change any one animal's alleles. It changes how common alleles are in the population over generations."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a deer population in a cold region became mostly thick-furred over many generations.", "given": ["Deer varied in fur thickness, partly due to different alleles.", "In harsh winters, thicker-furred deer lost less heat and were more likely to survive."], "q": "Which is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Deer grew thicker fur by feeling cold, then passed the thicker fur to their young", "All deer in the population mutated to get thick fur once winter arrived", "Thin-furred deer changed their alleles during their lives to match the cold", "Thick-furred survivors bred and passed on their alleles, so thick fur became more common"], "a": 3, "f": ["This is Lamarck's idea. Fur thickness gained from feeling cold is not inherited. The variation already existed in the population.", "Mutations are random and do not happen to a whole population because conditions change. The variation already existed.", "Individual deer do not change their alleles to suit the weather. Selection changes which alleles are common in the population.", "Right. Survivors breed and pass on alleles for thick fur. Over generations these alleles become more common in the population."]}, "trap": "Writing that animals changed because they needed to, or that features gained in life were passed on. That is Lamarck. Write instead: variation already existed, the best suited survived and bred, and passed on alleles.", "recap": "Natural selection: variation exists, the best suited survive, breed and pass on alleles; features gained during life are not inherited."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.6.3.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to explain gaps in the fossil record, to say what fossils show, or to name a cause of extinction.", "hook": {"q": "A jellyfish and a shark's tooth both sink to the sea floor and are buried in mud. Which is more likely to become a fossil?", "o": ["The jellyfish, because soft bodies are pressed neatly into the mud", "Both equally, because anything buried in mud turns into rock", "The tooth, because hard parts decay slowly and can be preserved", "Neither, because fossils only form from whole animals"], "a": 2, "f": ["Soft bodies rot quickly, so the jellyfish is usually gone before it can be preserved. Being soft makes fossilising harder, not easier.", "Not everything buried becomes a fossil. Most remains rot away. Only some are preserved, and hard parts are much more likely to survive.", "A tooth is a hard part. It decays slowly, so it lasts long enough to be preserved or replaced by minerals.", "Fossils do not need a whole animal. Single teeth, bones and shells are common fossils."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three ways fossils form", "b": "Fossils are the remains of organisms found in rock. They form in three main ways. Decay is stopped because a **condition for decay** is missing, such as oxygen or warmth. Hard parts are replaced by **minerals** as they decay. **Traces** are preserved, such as footprints, burrows and rootlet traces."}, {"h": "Gaps in the record", "b": "Many early life forms were **soft-bodied**. Soft bodies decay quickly, so they rarely became fossils. The few traces they left were often **destroyed** by geological activity, such as rock being crushed or heated. So the **fossil record** is incomplete."}, {"h": "Evidence for evolution", "b": "Fossils are found in layers of rock. Older rock usually lies below newer rock. Fossils from different layers show how organisms **changed over time**. This is evidence for **evolution**. Bacteria give evidence we can watch: **antibiotic-resistant** strains, such as MRSA, have evolved by natural selection."}, {"h": "Why species die out", "b": "A species is **extinct** when no individuals are left alive. Causes include a new predator, a new disease, or a new competitor. They also include **environmental change** over a long time. A single **catastrophic event**, such as a huge volcanic eruption or an asteroid collision, can also cause extinction."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the fossil record of early life forms is incomplete. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Many early life forms were soft-bodied.", "why": "This gives the starting fact. Soft bodies do not have the hard parts that usually fossilise."}, {"do": "Soft bodies decay quickly, so they usually rotted away before they could become fossils.", "why": "This links soft bodies to decay. Rotting removes the body before it can be preserved."}, {"do": "The few traces that did form were often destroyed later by geological activity, such as rock being crushed or heated.", "why": "This is a second, separate reason, so it earns its own mark."}, {"do": "So many early organisms left no fossils, which leaves gaps in the record.", "why": "This links the reasons back to the question, which asks why the record is incomplete."}], "answer": "Many early life forms were soft-bodied. Their bodies decayed quickly, so they rarely formed fossils. Any traces they did leave were often destroyed by geological activity. So many organisms left no fossils, and the fossil record has gaps."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 link soft bodies to decay?", "o": ["Soft organisms were rare, so few of them could be fossilised", "Heat in the rocks causes decay, which destroyed the traces", "Decay turns soft bodies into minerals, which then form fossils", "Rotting removes the body before it can be preserved or replaced by minerals"], "a": 3, "f": ["The organisms were not rare. The gap exists because their bodies rotted, not because there were few of them.", "Heat and crushing belong to step 3, destroying traces. Decay is rotting by microbes, which is a different cause.", "Decay does not make fossils. Minerals replace hard parts that survive long enough, but a body that rots quickly is gone first.", "Fossils need some part of the organism to last. If a soft body rots first, nothing remains to be preserved or replaced by minerals."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the shell of a sea animal can become a fossil. (3 marks)", "given": ["The shell is a hard part, so it decays slowly.", "The animal sinks and is buried in layers of mud."], "q": "What is the next point in the answer?", "o": ["The shell is heated by the Sun until it hardens into rock", "Soft parts of the animal turn into minerals and make the shell bigger", "As the shell slowly decays, minerals replace it, forming a rock copy of the shell", "Footprints form in the mud around the shell and replace it"], "a": 2, "f": ["Fossils form buried in the ground, not by heat from the Sun. The shell is not simply hardened.", "The soft parts rot away. They do not turn into minerals or add to the shell.", "Minerals replace the shell as it decays. This leaves a rock copy of the shell, which is one of the three ways fossils form.", "Footprints are traces, a different type of fossil. They do not replace a shell. Keep the ways of forming fossils separate."]}, "trap": "Do not say soft-bodied organisms were rare. They were not. The record is incomplete because their bodies rotted quickly and the few traces were destroyed.", "recap": "Fossils mostly come from hard parts, so the record has gaps; species die out through new threats, changed environments or catastrophes."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.6.3.7", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to explain how resistance evolves by natural selection and why it threatens health.", "hook": {"q": "A course of antibiotic kills nearly all the bacteria causing an infection. A few survive. What is the best explanation?", "o": ["They got used to the antibiotic during the treatment", "They were the biggest and strongest bacteria present", "They already had a feature that let them survive it", "The antibiotic could not reach them inside the body"], "a": 2, "f": ["Bacteria do not adapt during treatment. Individual bacteria do not get used to a drug. This is the common 'getting used to it' slip.", "Size and strength are not what matters. The survivors differ in one feature, resistance, not in general toughness.", "Right. A few bacteria already carried a resistance feature, from a chance mutation. The antibiotic did not create it. It only removed the others.", "The antibiotic spreads through the body in the blood. Survivors are not simply hidden. They have a different feature that protects them."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "New strains by mutation", "b": "Bacteria reproduce very fast, so there are huge numbers of them. **Mutations** in their DNA happen by chance. These create new **strains**. Some mutations make a strain **resistant** to an antibiotic, so the drug no longer kills it."}, {"h": "Antibiotics select", "b": "An antibiotic kills the non-resistant bacteria. A resistant bacterium **survives** and **reproduces**. It passes the gene for resistance to its offspring. Over time most of the population is resistant. This is **natural selection**. More antibiotic use means stronger selection."}, {"h": "Why it is a threat", "b": "Infections by a resistant strain, such as **MRSA**, are hard to treat. The strain can **spread** between people, who have no immunity to it. If no antibiotic works, patients stay ill for longer or may die. New antibiotics are slow and costly to develop."}, {"h": "Slowing resistance", "b": "Doctors should **not prescribe** antibiotics for viral infections or mild illness. Patients should **complete the course** so that all the bacteria are killed. Farmers should restrict antibiotic use on animals. All of this reduces selection for resistant strains."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a population of bacteria can become resistant to an antibiotic. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "A random mutation in a bacterium's DNA gives it resistance to the antibiotic.", "why": "Variation comes from chance mutation, not from the antibiotic. This is the starting point."}, {"do": "When the antibiotic is used, it kills the non-resistant bacteria, but the resistant bacterium survives.", "why": "This is the selection step. The antibiotic favours the resistant bacteria."}, {"do": "The surviving bacterium reproduces and passes the gene for resistance to its offspring.", "why": "Inheritance is needed for natural selection. Bacteria reproduce quickly, so numbers rise fast."}, {"do": "Over time, more of the population is resistant, so the antibiotic no longer works.", "why": "This links the process to the outcome the question asks about."}], "answer": "A chance mutation makes a bacterium resistant. The antibiotic kills the non-resistant bacteria, but the resistant one survives. It reproduces and passes on the resistance gene. Over time the population is mostly resistant."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 1 say the mutation is random, rather than caused by the antibiotic?", "o": ["The antibiotic makes the bacteria mutate so that they survive it", "Mutations happen by chance. The antibiotic only selects which bacteria survive", "Bacteria choose which mutations to keep once they meet the antibiotic", "Mutations only happen in bacteria that have met the antibiotic before"], "a": 1, "f": ["This is a common misconception. The antibiotic does not direct mutation. A mutation for resistance arises by chance, and it helps only if the drug is present.", "Right. Mutations happen at random, with or without the antibiotic. The antibiotic then kills the non-resistant bacteria, so resistant ones are selected.", "Bacteria cannot choose mutations. This treats evolution as a deliberate act, but it is chance followed by selection.", "Mutations can occur in any bacterium, whether or not it has met the drug. Past exposure is not needed."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a strain of bacteria resistant to penicillin becomes common in a hospital where penicillin is used a lot. (4 marks)", "given": ["A mutation gives a few bacteria resistance to penicillin.", "Penicillin kills the non-resistant bacteria."], "q": "Which is the correct next point?", "o": ["The surviving bacteria get used to penicillin and pass on this habit", "The resistant bacteria mutate again, which makes them stronger", "Penicillin changes the survivors' DNA, so their offspring are resistant", "The resistant bacteria survive, reproduce and pass on the gene for resistance"], "a": 3, "f": ["Bacteria do not get used to a drug. Resistance is a gene inherited from a chance mutation, not a habit.", "A second mutation is not part of the process. The same mutation is passed on, and selection raises its frequency.", "The drug does not change DNA to make resistance. The mutation was already there, and penicillin only selects.", "Right. Survival, reproduction and passing on the gene complete the natural selection link. Resistant bacteria then become more common."]}, "trap": "Do not write that bacteria or patients 'get used to' the antibiotic. Say a chance mutation gave resistance, the antibiotic killed the others, and the resistant bacteria survived and reproduced.", "recap": "Resistance arises by chance mutation, then antibiotics select for it, so resistant bacteria survive, reproduce and spread."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.6.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Classification questions often turn up as short 2 to 4 mark questions, such as reading a binomial name or an evolutionary tree.", "hook": {"q": "A cat, a dog and an oak tree. Which two would a scientist put in the same large group?", "o": ["Cat and oak, because both are living things found on land", "Cat and dog, because both are animals sharing many features", "Dog and oak, because both are often found in the same parks", "None of them, because each one needs a group of its own"], "a": 1, "f": ["Living on land is not how scientists group organisms. A cat is an animal and an oak is a plant, so they are in different kingdoms.", "Right. Organisms are grouped by shared features. Cats and dogs are both animals and share many features, so they are in the same kingdom.", "Where an organism is found does not decide its group. A dog is an animal and an oak is a plant.", "Classification puts organisms into groups because they share features. Cats and dogs share far more with each other than with a tree."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Groups from big to small", "b": "Scientists sort living things into groups. Linnaeus's system has seven levels, from largest to smallest: **kingdom**, phylum, class, order, family, **genus**, **species**. Each group is split into smaller groups. Organisms in the same group share features. The smaller the group, the more they share."}, {"h": "Two-word names", "b": "The **binomial system** gives every species a two-word name. The first word is the **genus**, with a capital letter. The second word is the **species**, in lower case. In Homo sapiens, Homo is the genus and sapiens is the species. Printed names are in italics, or underlined if handwritten."}, {"h": "Three domains", "b": "Better microscopes and chemical analysis of cells led to new models of classification. Carl Woese's **three-domain system** puts living things into Archaea, Bacteria or **Eukaryota**. These domains sit above kingdom. Plants, animals, fungi and protists are all in Eukaryota."}, {"h": "Evolutionary trees", "b": "An **evolutionary tree** shows how scientists believe organisms are related. It uses classification data for living species and **fossil** data for extinct ones. Each split is a **common ancestor**. The more recent the shared ancestor, the more closely related the species are."}], "example": {"task": "Lions are Panthera leo and tigers are Panthera tigris. Explain what these names show about how they are classified and how closely related they are. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "The names are binomial names. The first word is the genus and the second word is the species.", "why": "This shows you know how to read the name before using it."}, {"do": "Both animals have Panthera as the first word, so they are in the same genus.", "why": "Matching first words means a shared genus."}, {"do": "The second words, leo and tigris, are different, so they are different species.", "why": "A species has its own second word. Same genus does not mean same species."}, {"do": "Sharing a genus means they also share the larger groups above it, such as the same family, order and class.", "why": "Each genus sits inside one family, so the larger groups are shared too."}, {"do": "On an evolutionary tree, lions and tigers would branch from a recent common ancestor, so they are closely related.", "why": "A recent shared ancestor means a close relationship."}], "answer": "The names are binomial. Both are in genus Panthera, so they share all the larger groups. The species names differ, so they are different species. They would split from a recent common ancestor on an evolutionary tree, so they are closely related."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say lions and tigers share a family as well as a genus?", "o": ["Family is smaller than genus, so a family sits inside the genus", "The species words leo and tigris both name the same family", "All big cats have the same species word in their names", "Each genus sits inside one family, so all larger groups are shared"], "a": 3, "f": ["This reverses the order. Family is larger than genus, so the genus sits inside the family.", "The species words leo and tigris differ, and species words do not name a family. The shared genus is the link.", "The species words differ between big cats. The shared first word, the genus, is the link.", "Right. Groups nest inside each other, so organisms in the same genus must also be in the same family, order, class, phylum and kingdom."]}, "faded": {"task": "Wolf: Canis lupus. Golden jackal: Canis aureus. Red fox: Vulpes vulpes. Explain which two are most closely related.", "given": ["The names are binomial: first word genus, second word species.", "The wolf and jackal share the genus Canis."], "q": "What is the next point in the answer?", "o": ["The fox has a different species name, so it must be in a different kingdom.", "Wolf and jackal have different second words, so they are in different genera.", "The fox is in genus Vulpes, so it is less closely related than the two Canis species.", "Vulpes vulpes repeats a word, so the fox is the same species as the wolf."], "a": 2, "f": ["Species names do not decide the kingdom. All three animals are in the animal kingdom.", "The first word gives the genus. The wolf and jackal share Canis, so they are in the same genus. Only the species differ.", "Right. A different genus means fewer shared groups. The wolf and jackal share a genus, so they are the closest pair.", "A repeated word in a name does not make it the same species. The wolf is lupus and the fox is vulpes, so the species differ."]}, "trap": "Mixing up genus and species in a name, or giving both words capitals. The genus comes first with a capital. The species comes second in lower case. Species at the ends of tree branches are not ancestors of each other.", "recap": "A binomial name is genus then species, and species that share a more recent common ancestor are more closely related."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.7.1.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Ecology is on Paper 2, where you may be asked to name factors, explain how species depend on each other, or link an adaptation to its habitat.", "hook": {"q": "Frogs in a pond eat flies. A disease kills most of the frogs. What happens to the number of flies at first?", "o": ["It stays the same, because the disease only affects frogs", "It rises, because fewer flies are eaten", "It falls, because the disease spreads to the flies", "It falls, because flies need frogs to survive"], "a": 1, "f": ["Species in a community depend on each other. Removing the frogs changes what happens to the flies, so the number does not stay the same.", "Frogs eat flies. With fewer frogs, fewer flies are eaten, so fly numbers rise at first. This is interdependence.", "The question says the disease kills frogs. Pathogens usually affect only certain species, so you cannot assume it spreads to flies.", "This gets the link the wrong way round. Frogs eat flies, so flies do not need frogs to survive. Losing the predator helps the prey."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Abiotic and biotic factors", "b": "**Abiotic** factors are non-living. Examples: light intensity, temperature, moisture, soil pH and mineral content, wind, carbon dioxide for plants, oxygen for water animals. **Biotic** factors are living. Examples: food supply, predators, pathogens and competition. Both kinds affect which organisms live in a **community**."}, {"h": "Interdependence", "b": "Species in a community are **interdependent**. Each may rely on others for food, shelter, pollination or seed dispersal. In a **stable community**, the species and abiotic factors are balanced, so population sizes stay fairly constant. Removing one species can change the numbers of many others. Press Play on the graph: predator numbers peak after prey.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "ms": 150, "x": [0, 16], "xstep": 2, "y": [0, 100], "ystep": 20, "xlabel": "Time / years (idealised model)", "ylabel": "Population (arbitrary units)", "lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 50.0], [0.1, 52.3538], [0.2, 54.693], [0.3, 57.0034], [0.4, 59.2705], [0.5, 61.4805], [0.6, 63.6197], [0.7, 65.675], [0.8, 67.6336], [0.9, 69.4834], [1.0, 71.2132], [1.1, 72.8122], [1.2, 74.2705], [1.3, 75.5792], [1.4, 76.7302], [1.5, 77.7164], [1.6, 78.5317], [1.7, 79.1711], [1.8, 79.6307], [1.9, 79.9075], [2.0, 80.0], [2.1, 79.9075], [2.2, 79.6307], [2.3, 79.1711], [2.4, 78.5317], [2.5, 77.7164], [2.6, 76.7302], [2.7, 75.5792], [2.8, 74.2705], [2.9, 72.8122], [3.0, 71.2132], [3.1, 69.4834], [3.2, 67.6336], [3.3, 65.675], [3.4, 63.6197], [3.5, 61.4805], [3.6, 59.2705], [3.7, 57.0034], [3.8, 54.693], [3.9, 52.3538], [4.0, 50.0], [4.1, 47.6462], [4.2, 45.307], [4.3, 42.9966], [4.4, 40.7295], [4.5, 38.5195], [4.6, 36.3803], [4.7, 34.325], [4.8, 32.3664], [4.9, 30.5166], [5.0, 28.7868], [5.1, 27.1878], [5.2, 25.7295], [5.3, 24.4208], [5.4, 23.2698], [5.5, 22.2836], [5.6, 21.4683], [5.7, 20.8289], [5.8, 20.3693], [5.9, 20.0925], [6.0, 20.0], [6.1, 20.0925], [6.2, 20.3693], [6.3, 20.8289], [6.4, 21.4683], [6.5, 22.2836], [6.6, 23.2698], [6.7, 24.4208], [6.8, 25.7295], [6.9, 27.1878], [7.0, 28.7868], [7.1, 30.5166], [7.2, 32.3664], [7.3, 34.325], [7.4, 36.3803], [7.5, 38.5195], [7.6, 40.7295], [7.7, 42.9966], [7.8, 45.307], [7.9, 47.6462], [8.0, 50.0], [8.1, 52.3538], [8.2, 54.693], [8.3, 57.0034], [8.4, 59.2705], [8.5, 61.4805], [8.6, 63.6197], [8.7, 65.675], [8.8, 67.6336], [8.9, 69.4834], [9.0, 71.2132], [9.1, 72.8122], [9.2, 74.2705], [9.3, 75.5792], [9.4, 76.7302], [9.5, 77.7164], [9.6, 78.5317], [9.7, 79.1711], [9.8, 79.6307], [9.9, 79.9075], [10.0, 80.0], [10.1, 79.9075], [10.2, 79.6307], [10.3, 79.1711], [10.4, 78.5317], [10.5, 77.7164], [10.6, 76.7302], [10.7, 75.5792], [10.8, 74.2705], [10.9, 72.8122], [11.0, 71.2132], [11.1, 69.4834], [11.2, 67.6336], [11.3, 65.675], [11.4, 63.6197], [11.5, 61.4805], [11.6, 59.2705], [11.7, 57.0034], [11.8, 54.693], [11.9, 52.3538], [12.0, 50.0], [12