onstant. The amounts are not necessarily equal."}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows the rates of the forward and reverse reactions in a sealed container: N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃. The forward reaction is exothermic. Explain how equilibrium is reached and give the reverse energy change.", "steps": [{"do": "At the start, only nitrogen and hydrogen are present, so the forward reaction is at its fastest.", "why": "Rate depends on concentration, and the reactants have their highest concentration at the start."}, {"do": "As ammonia forms, the reverse reaction starts and speeds up. The forward reaction slows down because nitrogen and hydrogen are used up.", "why": "Concentration of ammonia rises and concentration of reactants falls, so the two rates move towards each other."}, {"do": "Eventually the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the reverse reaction. The system is at equilibrium.", "why": "This is what equilibrium means, and the mark scheme looks for 'equal rates'."}, {"do": "Both reactions continue, but the concentrations of N₂, H₂ and NH₃ no longer change. This is possible because the container is closed.", "why": "This is why it is called dynamic. Nothing can escape, so the amounts stay constant."}, {"do": "The forward reaction is exothermic, so the reverse reaction is endothermic. It takes in the same amount of energy as the forward reaction releases.", "why": "The energy change is equal in size and opposite in type in the reverse direction."}], "answer": "At the start, only N₂ and H₂ are present, so the forward reaction is fastest. As NH₃ builds up, the reverse reaction speeds up and the forward reaction slows. At equilibrium the rates are equal, so the concentrations stay constant. Both reactions continue. The reverse reaction is endothermic.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "xstep": 2, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "Time", "ylabel": "Rate of reaction", "lines": [{"pts": [[0.0, 10.0], [0.0625, 9.815399], [0.125, 9.636478], [0.1875, 9.46306