5, 3.970399], [9.875, 3.97131], [9.9375, 3.972193], [10.0, 3.973048]], "c": 1, "label": "reverse"}], "xnums": false, "ynums": false}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the concentrations stay constant even though both reactions continue?", "o": ["The reverse reaction stops, so ammonia cannot be broken down", "Ammonia is made at the same rate as it is broken down", "The container holds equal amounts of N₂, H₂ and NH₃", "The reactions stop after a short time and only restart if disturbed"], "a": 1, "f": ["If the reverse reaction stopped, ammonia would keep building up. At equilibrium the reverse reaction is still happening.", "Correct. Because the two rates are equal, ammonia is broken down as fast as it is made, so the amounts do not change.", "Equilibrium does not mean equal amounts. The amounts are constant, and they can be very different from each other.", "The reactions do not stop. Particles keep reacting in both directions, which is why it is called dynamic."]}, "faded": {"task": "Hydrogen and iodine react in a sealed flask: H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI. Explain what happens as the system reaches equilibrium.", "given": ["At the start only H₂ and I₂ are present, so the forward reaction is fastest.", "As HI forms, the reverse reaction starts and speeds up. The forward reaction slows as H₂ and I₂ are used up."], "q": "Which point should come next in the explanation?", "o": ["The forward reaction stops, so no more HI is made and the amounts stay constant", "Both reactions stop once H₂, I₂ and HI are present in equal amounts", "The reverse reaction overtakes the forward one until all the HI has changed back", "The forward and reverse rates become equal, so the concentrations stop changing"], "a": 3, "f": ["The forward reaction does not stop. HI is still being made, but it is broken down at the same rate, so the amounts stay constant.", "Reactions do not stop, and the amounts do not have to be equal. This mixes up 'constant' with 'equal'.", "If the reverse reaction kept winning, there would be no equilibrium. The two rates meet and stay equal.", "Correct. Equal forward and reverse rates in a closed system give dynamic equilibrium, with constant concentrations."]}, "trap": "Writing that the reactions stop at equilibrium, or that the amounts are equal. Both reactions carry on at the same rate. The amounts are constant but can be very different. Write 'same rate', not 'same amount'.", "recap": "At dynamic equilibrium in a closed system, forward and reverse reactions continue at the same rate, so amounts stay constant but not necessarily equal."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.6.2.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Higher Paper 2 asks you to predict and explain how changing conditions affects the yield of a reversible reaction, including industrial examples such as the Haber process.", "hook": {"q": "A reversible reaction is at equilibrium in a closed flask. You add more of one reactant. What do you expect to happen?", "o": ["Nothing changes, because the reaction is already at equilibrium", "More product forms, using up some of the extra reactant", "The reaction stops, because the reactants are now unbalanced", "More of that reactant forms, because the system makes what was added"], "a": 1, "f": ["Equilibrium is dynamic, not frozen. A change in conditions moves the position until a new equilibrium is reached.", "Right. The system works against the change. Extra reactant is partly used up, so more product forms.", "Both reactions keep going. The system adjusts and settles at a new equilibrium. It does not stop.", "This is backwards. The system opposes the change, so it removes some of the added reactant rather than making more."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Opposing a change", "b": "At **equilibrium** in a closed system, the forward and reverse reactions go at the same rate. If you change a condition, the **position of equilibrium** moves. **Le Chatelier's principle** says it moves to oppose the change. Moving right gives more products. Moving left gives more reactants."}, {"h": "Concentration", "b": "Increase the **concentration** of a reactant and the system tries to use it up. The position shifts right, so more product forms. Increase a product's concentration and the position shifts left. Removing a product as it forms shifts the position right, so the **yield** rises. Press Next step to see the graph of the rates.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "steps", "x": [0, 30], "xstep": 5, "y": [0, 10], "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "Time", "ylabel": "Rate (arbitrary units)", "lines": [{"c": 0, "step": 1, "pts": [[0, 4], [10, 4]]}, {"c":