 added. The forward rate jumps up to 8 straight away. The reverse rate has not changed yet.", "Reactant is used up, so the forward rate falls. More product forms, so the reverse rate rises. The position shifts right.", "The rates meet again at 6. Equal rates mean a new equilibrium, with more product than before."]}}, {"h": "Temperature", "b": "Raise the **temperature** and the system opposes it by taking in heat. That is the **endothermic** direction. Lower the temperature and it favours the **exothermic** direction, which gives out heat. The two directions have opposite energy changes, so check which one is exothermic."}, {"h": "Pressure and catalysts", "b": "Pressure only matters for gases. Raising the **pressure** favours the side with fewer gas molecules. Lowering it favours the side with more. Count the balancing numbers. If both sides have equal numbers, there is no shift. A **catalyst** does not move the position. It only gets you to equilibrium faster."}], "example": {"task": "CO(g) + 2H₂(g) ⇌ CH₃OH(g). Explain why increasing the pressure increases the yield of methanol.", "steps": [{"do": "State that increasing the pressure is a change, so the equilibrium shifts to oppose it. It shifts to the side that lowers the pressure.", "why": "This is Le Chatelier's principle. Every answer starts from it."}, {"do": "Count the gas molecules on each side. Left: 1 CO + 2 H₂ = 3 molecules. Right: 1 CH₃OH = 1 molecule.", "why": "Gas pressure depends on the number of gas molecules, so the count decides the direction."}, {"do": "Say the position shifts right, towards the side with fewer gas molecules.", "why": "Fewer molecules means lower pressure, which opposes the increase."}, {"do": "Conclude that more CH₃OH forms, so the yield of methanol increases.", "why": "Link the shift back to the question: the product is made in greater amounts."}], "answer": "Increasing the pressure shifts the equilibrium to the side with fewer gas molecules. The left has 3 molecules and the right has 1. So the position shifts right, which lowers the pressure, and more methanol forms. The yield increases."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer count the gas molecules on each side?", "o": ["More molecules means a faster reaction, so the larger side wins", "The side with more molecules has more mass, so it is favoured", "Pressure comes from gas molecules, so fewer molecules means lower pressure", "The equilibrium always shifts to the side with one molecule"], "a": 2, "f": ["This mixes up rate and position. Pressure changes the position of equilibrium, which is a different question from how fast the reaction goes.", "Mass is the same on both sides in any balanced equation. The count of gas molecules is what matters for pressure.", "Right. Gas pressure comes from the number of gas molecules. Shifting to the side with fewer molecules lowers the pressure and opposes the rise.", "This only works when pressure is increased and that side has fewer gas molecules. Lowering the pressure favours the side with more."]}, "faded": {"task": "2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g). The forward reaction is exothermic. Explain why raising the temperature lowers the yield of SO₃.", "given": ["Raising the temperature is a change, so the equilibrium shifts to oppose it by absorbing heat.", "The forward reaction is exothermic, so the reverse reaction is endothermic."], "q": "What is the next point in the explanation?", "o": ["So the equilibrium shifts right, because that direction is exothermic and releases heat", "So the rate of the forward reaction falls, which leaves less SO₃ at equilibrium", "So the equilibrium shifts right, since there are fewer gas molecules on that side", "So the equilibrium shifts left, favouring the endothermic reverse reaction, so less SO₃"], "a": 3, "f": ["Releasing heat would add to the rise in temperature. The system needs to absorb heat, so it favours the endothermic direction.", "This confuses rate with position. Raising the temperature speeds up both reactions. The yield falls because the position shifts, not because the forward rate drops.", "This is a pressure argument. The question is about temperature, so the energy change decides the direction.", "Right. The system opposes the rise by favouring the endothermic reverse reaction. The position shifts left, so less SO₃ forms."]}, "trap": "Saying a higher temperature always favours the forward reaction. It favours the endothermic direction. Check whether the forward reaction is exothermic or endothermic first. The reverse reaction has the opposite energy change.", "recap": "Equilibrium shifts to oppose a change: raised temperature favours the endothermic direction, raised pressure favours fewer gas molecules."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.7.1.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions on this often ask you to explain how fractional distillation works or how chain length changes a fuel's properties, so you need clear cause-and-effect sentences.", "hook": {"q": "Petrol pours easily and catches fire quickly. Bitumen (road tar) is thick and hard to light. What is the main difference between their molecules?", "o": ["Petrol molecules are longer hydrocarbon chains than bitumen molecules", "Petrol molecules are shorter hydrocarbon chains than bitumen molecules", "Petrol molecules contain oxygen, but bitumen molecules do not", "Petrol molecules contain more carbon atoms than hydrogen atoms"], "a": 1, "f": ["Reversed. Longer chains make a liquid thicker and harder to ignite, which describes bitumen, not petrol.", "Right. Petrol has short chains, so it is runny and ignites easily. Bitumen has very long chains, so it is thick and hard to light.", "Both are hydrocarbons, so neither contains oxygen. The difference is chain length, not the elements present.", "Alkanes have more hydrogen atoms than carbon atoms (CₙH₂ₙ₊₂). The difference between the two is chain length."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Crude oil is a mixture", "b": "**Crude oil** is a mixture of many **hydrocarbons**, which contain only hydrogen and carbon. Most are **alkanes**, with the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, such as C₃H₈. The molecules are not chemically joined, so each keeps its own properties. Crude oil is finite: it formed over millions of years from buried plankton."}, {"h": "Fractional distillation", "b": "Crude oil is heated until it evaporates. The vapour enters a **fractionating column**, which is hot at the bottom and cooler at the top. Each **fraction**, a group of hydrocarbons with similar **boiling points**, condenses where the temperature drops below its boiling point. It is drawn off at that level."}, {"h": "Chain length and properties", "b": "As chains get longer, the **boiling point** rises, the **viscosity** rises (thicker, flows less easily) and **flammability** falls (harder to ignite). Longer molecules have stronger forces between them, so more energy is needed to separate them. Short chains are gases or runny liquids. Long chains are thick liquids or solids."}, {"h": "Uses and burning", "b": "Fractions are used as **fuels**: LPG, petrol, kerosene, diesel and heavy fuel oil. They are also **feedstock** for the petrochemical industry, making solvents, lubricants, polymers and detergents. In plenty of air, hydrocarbons undergo **complete combustion**: carbon forms CO₂, hydrogen forms H₂O, and energy is released."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how fractional distillation separates crude oil, and why the fraction collected at the top of the column is the most flammable.", "steps": [{"do": "Crude oil is heated so that it evaporates, and the vapour passes into a fractionating column.", "why": "Hydrocarbons with different boiling points can only be separated once they are vapours."}, {"do": "The column is hot at the bottom and gets cooler towards the top.", "why": "This temperature gradient lets different hydrocarbons condense at different heights."}, {"do": "Each hydrocarbon condenses where the column is cooler than its boiling point. Long chains have high boiling points, so they condense near the bottom. Short chains have low boiling points, so they rise to the top.", "why": "Boiling point depends on chain length, so this sorts the molecules into fractions."}, {"do": "The top fraction contains the shortest chains, so it is the most flammable.", "why": "Flammability falls as chain length rises, so short chains ignite most easily."}], "answer": "Crude oil is heated and its vapour enters a column that is hotter at the bottom. Hydrocarbons condense at different heights by boiling point: long chains low down, short chains at the top. Short chains ignite most easily."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why do long-chain hydrocarbons condense near the bottom of the column?", "o": ["They are heavier, so they sink to the bottom before they can condense", "Their boiling points are low, so they condense before any other fraction", "Their boiling points are high, so they condense while the column is still hot", "They react with the hot air at the bottom, which turns them into liquids"], "a": 2, "f": ["This confuses condensing with sinking. Position depends on the temperature at which each fraction condenses, not on weight.", "Reversed. Long chains have high boiling points, not low. Low boiling points belong to short chains, which rise to the top.", "Right. A high boiling point means the vapour turns back to liquid even at a high temperature, so it condenses low down where it is hottest.", "Fractional distillation is a physical separation. No reaction takes place, and the hydrocarbons condense by cooling."]}, "faded": {"task": "Fraction X has 8 carbon atoms per molecule. Fraction Y has 20 carbon atoms per molecule. Compare the properties of X and Y.", "given": ["Y has longer molecules than X, so Y has a higher boiling point.", "So Y condenses lower down the fractionating column than X."], "q": "Which sentence completes the comparison correctly?", "o": ["Y is less viscous and more flammable than X, because it has more atoms.", "Y is more viscous and less flammable than X, because its chains are longer.", "Y is more viscous and more flammable than X, because it has a higher boiling point.", "Y is less viscous and less flammable than X, because it condenses lower down."], "a": 1, "f": ["Both trends are reversed. More atoms in the chain mean a thicker liquid that is harder to ignite, not runnier and easier to light.", "Right. Longer chains give higher viscosity and lower flammability, so Y is thicker and harder to ignite than X.", "Longer chains raise viscosity but lower flammability. A higher boiling point goes with being harder to ignite, not easier.", "Viscosity is wrong here. Longer chains make Y more viscous, not less. Only the lower flammability is correct."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the trend. Shorter chains have lower boiling points, are runnier and ignite more easily. Longer chains are the opposite. Test your answer by picturing petrol (short) and bitumen (long).", "recap": "Fractional distillation separates crude oil by boiling point. Shorter chains boil at lower temperatures, flow more easily and ignite more easily."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.7.1.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 often asks you to explain why oil is cracked, complete a cracking equation, or describe the bromine water test for alkenes.", "hook": {"q": "Crude oil contains many long molecules that make thick, sticky liquids. People want more petrol, which has shorter molecules. What could an oil company do?", "o": ["Use the thick liquid as petrol without changing it", "Break the long molecules into shorter ones", "Join the long molecules into even longer chains", "Cool the crude oil until it turns into petrol"], "a": 1, "f": ["Liquids made of long molecules are thick, flow badly and ignite poorly. Using them unchanged would not meet the demand for petrol.", "Right. Splitting long molecules into shorter ones gives more of the useful, runny fuels. This is cracking.", "Longer chains would make even thicker, less useful liquids. Joining small molecules into long chains is polymerisation, which goes the wrong way here.", "Cooling only changes the state of a substance. The molecules stay the same size, so it would not become petrol."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why crack?", "b": "Fractional distillation gives more **long-chain hydrocarbons** than are needed. Short chains are more useful as fuels, because they ignite more easily and flow more easily. **Cracking** breaks large hydrocarbons into smaller, more useful molecules. The products include alkenes."}, {"h": "Two cracking methods", "b": "In **catalytic cracking**, the hydrocarbon is vaporised and passed over a hot **catalyst**. In **steam cracking**, it is mixed with steam at a high temperature. Both give a mixture of shorter alkanes and alkenes. The alkanes are used as fuels. The alkenes are used to make polymers and other chemicals."}, {"h": "What is an alkene?", "b": "**Alkenes** are hydrocarbons with a **C=C double bond**. They are **unsaturated**. Alkanes have only single bonds. Alkenes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ, so ethene is C₂H₄ and propene is C₃H₆. The double bond makes alkenes more reactive than alkanes."}, {"h": "The bromine water test", "b": "Shake the sample with orange **bromine water**. An **alkene** turns it **colourless**, because bromine adds across the C=C bond. An alkane leaves it orange. A colour change shows that at least one alkene is present. It does not show that every product is an alkene."}], "example": {"task": "Decane, C₁₀H₂₂, is cracked to make octane and ethene. Describe how this is done, give the equation, and say how to show that an alkene forms.", "steps": [{"do": "Decane is vaporised and passed over a hot catalyst, or mixed with steam at a high temperature.", "why": "These are the conditions for catalytic cracking and steam cracking."}, {"do": "The long decane molecules break into shorter molecules.", "why": "Cracking means breaking large hydrocarbons into smaller ones."}, {"do": "C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄. Octane is an alkane and ethene is an alkene.", "why": "The equation balances: 10 C and 22 H on each side. One product has a C=C bond."}, {"do": "Shake the products with orange bromine water. It turns colourless, so an alkene is present.", "why": "Only alkenes, with C=C bonds, decolourise bromine water. Alkanes leave it orange."}, {"do": "Octane is used as a fuel. Ethene is used to make polymers.", "why": "This links the products to why cracking is done: more useful molecules."}], "answer": "Decane is vaporised and passed over a hot catalyst (or mixed with steam at high temperature). Its molecules break into shorter ones: C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄. Ethene has a C=C double bond, so it turns orange bromine water colourless. Octane is used as a fuel and ethene to make polymers."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄ a correct way to complete the cracking equation for decane?", "o": ["Both products are alkanes, so both fit the formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂", "The catalyst adds the extra hydrogen atoms needed to make ethene", "There are 10 C and 22 H atoms on each side, and one product is an alkene", "Alkenes always have two carbon atoms, so ethene must form"], "a": 2, "f": ["C₂H₄ fits CₙH₂ₙ, not CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. It is an alkene, not an alkane. Cracking always makes at least one alkene.", "A catalyst is not used up and adds no atoms. The atoms in the products all come from the decane molecule.", "Right. Atoms are conserved, so C and H balance. Cracking also needs an alkene among the products, and C₂H₄ is one.", "Alkenes can have any number of carbons from two upwards. Cracking decane could give other alkenes; this split simply makes ethene."]}, "faded": {"task": "Dodecane, C₁₂H₂₆, is cracked into hexane, C₆H₁₄, and one other product. Explain what type of compound the other product is.", "given": ["Cracking splits a long-chain hydrocarbon into shorter molecules.", "C₁₂H₂₆ → C₆H₁₄ + ?, so the missing product must be C₆H₁₂."], "q": "What is the next point in the explanation?", "o": ["C₆H₁₂ fits CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, so it is another alkane called hexane", "C₆H₁₂ is hydrogen gas, because the extra hydrogen atoms form H₂", "C₆H₁₂ is hexane, because the name depends only on the number of carbons", "C₆H₁₂ fits CₙH₂ₙ, so it is an alkene (hexene) with a C=C double bond"], "a": 3, "f": ["For n = 6, CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ gives C₆H₁₄, not C₆H₁₂. C₆H₁₂ has two fewer hydrogens, so it is not an alkane.", "The product still has 6 carbon atoms. Hydrogen gas is H₂, and no hydrogen is released in this equation.", "The name also depends on the type of compound. Hexane is C₆H₁₄. C₆H₁₂ has a double bond and is hexene.", "Right. C₆H₁₂ fits CₙH₂ₙ, the alkene formula. So the other product is hexene, which has a C=C double bond."]}, "trap": "Writing that bromine water goes 'clear'. Write 'colourless': orange to colourless. Clear only means see-through, and a coloured liquid can be clear. With an alkane, it stays orange.", "recap": "Cracking breaks long alkanes into shorter alkanes and alkenes; only alkenes, which have C=C, turn orange bromine water colourless."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.7.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Alkene questions ask you to name products, complete equations and describe the bromine water test, and one pattern explains them all.", "hook": {"q": "Ethene, C₂H₄, and ethane, C₂H₆, are both hydrocarbons. Which one can take in extra atoms without losing any of its own?", "o": ["Ethane, because it already has more hydrogen atoms", "Ethene, because its double bond can open up to join new atoms", "Both can, because they have the same number of carbons", "Neither can, because carbon always makes exactly four bonds"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Ethane has only single bonds, so every carbon already has its four bonds filled. Extra atoms could only join by replacing something.", "Right. The C=C double bond can open to a single bond. That frees up bonds on each carbon, so new atoms can join on.", "Wrong. The number of carbons is not what matters. The type of bond between them decides whether atoms can add on.", "Wrong. Carbon does make four bonds. But in ethene two of them are used on the double bond, and that bond can open."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What alkenes are", "b": "**Alkenes** are hydrocarbons with a **C=C double bond**. Their general formula is CₙH₂ₙ. The first four are ethene C₂H₄, propene C₃H₆, butene C₄H₈ and pentene C₅H₁₀. They are **unsaturated**: they have two fewer hydrogen atoms than the alkane with the same number of carbons."}, {"h": "Addition reactions", "b": "The C=C bond is the reactive part. In an **addition reaction** it opens up to a single bond. A small molecule adds on, with one part joining each carbon. Nothing is lost, so there is only **one product**. Alkenes react this way with hydrogen, steam and halogens."}, {"h": "Hydrogen and steam", "b": "With hydrogen and a catalyst, an alkene becomes an **alkane**: C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆. With steam and a catalyst, it becomes an **alcohol**: C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH. So ethene and steam make ethanol. The alkane product is **saturated**."}, {"h": "Halogens and the test", "b": "Alkenes add halogens to make **haloalkanes**: C₂H₄ + Br₂ → C₂H₄Br₂. This gives the **bromine water** test. Shake an alkene with orange bromine water and it turns colourless. An alkane leaves it orange, because it has no C=C bond to react with."}], "example": {"task": "Ethene is shaken with orange bromine water. Describe what you see and explain it. Give the equation.", "steps": [{"do": "Say that the orange bromine water turns colourless.", "why": "This is the observation. It is the result the question asks you to describe."}, {"do": "Say that ethene is unsaturated because it has a C=C double bond.", "why": "The double bond is what lets the reaction happen. Alkanes lack it."}, {"do": "Say that the double bond opens and one bromine atom bonds to each carbon.", "why": "This explains what happens to the molecules in an addition reaction."}, {"do": "Say that only one product forms, 1,2-dibromoethane: C₂H₄ + Br₂ → C₂H₄Br₂.", "why": "Nothing is lost, which is the key feature of addition. The equation balances."}], "answer": "The orange bromine water turns colourless. Ethene is unsaturated because it has a C=C double bond. In an addition reaction the double bond opens and one bromine atom bonds to each carbon. Only one product forms, 1,2-dibromoethane: C₂H₄ + Br₂ → C₂H₄Br₂."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the orange colour of the bromine water disappear?", "o": ["The bromine dissolves in the ethene and is diluted", "The bromine evaporates because the reaction gives out heat", "The bromine reacts with ethene and becomes a colourless compound", "Bromine replaces hydrogen and a colourless gas is given off"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Dilution would only make the colour paler. The colour vanishes because the bromine is used up in a chemical reaction.", "Wrong. The bromine does not evaporate. It reacts, and its atoms end up in a new compound.", "Right. Bromine adds across the C=C bond to make 1,2-dibromoethane, which is colourless. So the orange colour is lost.", "Wrong. This describes substitution. In an addition reaction nothing is replaced and no gas is given off. There is one product."]}, "faded": {"task": "Ethene, C₂H₄, reacts with chlorine, Cl₂. Describe the reaction and give the equation.", "given": ["Ethene has a C=C double bond, so it is unsaturated.", "Chlorine is a halogen, so this is an addition reaction."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["C₂H₄ + Cl₂ → C₂H₄Cl₂; both Cl atoms join, one to each carbon", "C₂H₄ + Cl₂ → C₂H₃Cl + HCl; one H is replaced by Cl", "C₂H₄ + Cl₂ → C₂H₄Cl; one Cl atom joins and the other is lost", "C₂H₄ + Cl₂ → C₂Cl₄ + H₂; all the hydrogen atoms are replaced"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. The double bond opens and one Cl atom joins each carbon. Nothing is lost, so there is one product, 1,2-dichloroethane, C₂H₄Cl₂.", "Wrong. This is a substitution reaction, with HCl as a second product. Alkenes add halogens, so there is only one product.", "Wrong. Both atoms of Cl₂ add on. No atoms are lost in addition, and C₂H₄Cl would not balance.", "Wrong. Nothing is replaced and no hydrogen gas forms. All the atoms of the reactants end up in one product."]}, "trap": "Thinking atoms get replaced or lost. In addition, all the atoms of the small molecule add across C=C, giving one product. Ethene and steam make ethanol, not ethane or ethanoic acid.", "recap": "Alkenes are unsaturated, so their C=C bond opens and adds H₂, steam or halogens, giving one product."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.7.2.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 asks short recall questions on alcohols and carboxylic acids: their formulas, names and reactions, and how ethanol is made by fermentation.", "hook": {"q": "A bottle of wine is left open for weeks and tastes like vinegar. What has happened to the ethanol?", "o": ["It has been reduced to methanol", "It has evaporated, leaving only water", "It has burned to carbon dioxide and water", "It has been oxidised to ethanoic acid"], "a": 3, "f": ["Methanol is a different alcohol. Nothing here would turn ethanol into methanol. The sour taste points to an acid.", "Some ethanol may evaporate, but that would not make the wine sour. Vinegar's sourness comes from an acid.", "Burning needs a flame and gives no sour taste. Nothing is burning in an open bottle.", "Right. Oxygen from the air and microbes oxidise ethanol to ethanoic acid. Ethanoic acid is the acid in vinegar."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Alcohols: the –OH group", "b": "Alcohols contain the **–OH** group. The first four are methanol CH₃OH, ethanol CH₃CH₂OH, propanol and butanol. They dissolve in water to give neutral solutions. They react with sodium to give **hydrogen** gas, seen as fizzing. They burn in air to make carbon dioxide and water."}, {"h": "Making and using ethanol", "b": "**Fermentation** makes ethanol. Yeast acts on glucose in warm, **anaerobic** conditions (no oxygen). C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂. Ethanol is used in drinks, as a solvent and as a fuel. With oxygen and microbes, ethanol is **oxidised** to ethanoic acid."}, {"h": "Carboxylic acids: –COOH", "b": "Carboxylic acids contain **–COOH**. Count every carbon, including the one in –COOH: methanoic 1, ethanoic 2, propanoic 3, butanoic 4. Propanoic acid is CH₃CH₂COOH. They are **weak acids**: they only partly ionise in water, so their pH is higher than that of a strong acid of the same concentration."}, {"h": "Reactions of carboxylic acids", "b": "Carboxylic acids react with **carbonates** to make a salt, water and carbon dioxide. With an alcohol and an acid catalyst they make an **ester**. Esters have fruity smells and are used in perfumes and flavourings. Ethanoic acid + ethanol → ethyl ethanoate + water."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how ethanol is made from glucose by fermentation, and explain why air must be kept out of the mixture.", "steps": [{"do": "Yeast is added to a solution of glucose.", "why": "Enzymes in yeast catalyse the change from glucose to ethanol."}, {"do": "The mixture is kept warm, but not hot.", "why": "Warmth makes the enzymes work quickly. Too much heat would damage them."}, {"do": "Glucose changes to ethanol and carbon dioxide: C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂.", "why": "This is the reaction, so the products must be named correctly."}, {"do": "Oxygen is kept out, so the conditions are anaerobic.", "why": "With air present, the ethanol is oxidised to ethanoic acid and the ethanol is lost."}], "answer": "Yeast is added to a glucose solution. Its enzymes change glucose into ethanol and carbon dioxide. The mixture is kept warm but not hot. Air is kept out because oxygen would oxidise the ethanol to ethanoic acid."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say oxygen must be kept out of the mixture?", "o": ["Oxygen would denature the enzymes in the yeast straight away", "Oxygen would use up the carbon dioxide that the yeast needs", "Oxygen would let microbes oxidise the ethanol to ethanoic acid", "Oxygen would dehydrate the ethanol and turn it into ethene"], "a": 2, "f": ["Enzymes are denatured by high temperature, not by oxygen. This mixes up two different conditions.", "Carbon dioxide is a product of fermentation, not something the yeast needs. It appears on the right of the equation.", "Right. In air, ethanol is oxidised to ethanoic acid. That spoils the product, which is why fermentation is anaerobic.", "Oxidising an alcohol makes a carboxylic acid, not an alkene. Oxygen does not dehydrate ethanol. Ethene is made by cracking."]}, "faded": {"task": "Describe how ethyl ethanoate, an ester, is made in the laboratory from ethanoic acid.", "given": ["Mix ethanoic acid (a carboxylic acid) with ethanol (an alcohol)."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Add sodium carbonate, which acts as the catalyst", "Add a few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid as a catalyst", "Add yeast and keep oxygen out of the mixture", "Add a piece of sodium so the alcohol reacts first"], "a": 1, "f": ["Sodium carbonate would react with the ethanoic acid to make carbon dioxide. It is not a catalyst for making esters.", "Right. Esters form from a carboxylic acid and an alcohol with an acid catalyst. Ethyl ethanoate and water are made.", "Yeast is for fermentation, which makes ethanol from glucose. It does not make esters.", "Sodium reacts with alcohols to make hydrogen. That would use up the ethanol, not make an ester."]}, "trap": "Not counting the carbon inside –COOH, so writing propanoic acid as C₃H₇COOH. That is butanoic acid. Count every carbon in the molecule, including the –COOH carbon, then check the formula.", "recap": "Alcohols have –OH, carboxylic acids have –COOH; ethanol is made by anaerobic fermentation and oxidised to ethanoic acid."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.7.3.1", "kind": "method", "why": "Paper 2 often asks you to draw a polymer's repeat unit from its monomer, or the reverse, and these are quick marks if you follow the steps.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Monomers have C=C", "b": "A **monomer** is a small molecule that joins to many others. In addition polymerisation, the monomers are **alkenes** or molecules like them. Each has a C=C double bond. Ethene, propene and chloroethene are all examples."}, {"h": "The double bond opens", "b": "In **addition polymerisation**, one bond of each C=C breaks. Each carbon then bonds to a carbon in the next monomer. This builds a long chain. Only the polymer forms, and no atoms are lost. The chain has only **single bonds** between its carbons."}, {"h": "Drawing the repeat unit", "b": "The **repeat unit** is the short section that repeats along the chain. Draw the two carbons joined by a single bond. Keep every atom or group that was on them. Put the unit in brackets, with the bonds going through the brackets. Write **n** at the bottom right."}, {"h": "Working backwards", "b": "To find the **monomer** from a repeat unit, do the reverse. Take the two carbons in the chain and put the **C=C** back between them. Keep the side groups where they are. Check that each carbon has four bonds."}], "example": {"task": "Chloroethene, CH₂=CHCl, is the monomer for poly(chloroethene). Draw the repeat unit of the polymer.", "steps": [{"do": "Write out the monomer with its C=C. One carbon has H and H. The other carbon has H and Cl.", "why": "You need to see what is attached to each carbon of the double bond."}, {"do": "Change the C=C to a single bond, C–C. Each carbon now has a free bond pointing outwards.", "why": "One bond of the double bond breaks so each carbon can join to the next monomer."}, {"do": "Keep every atom where it was: –CH₂–CHCl–.", "why": "No atoms are lost, and side groups stay on the same carbon."}, {"do": "Put brackets round this section so the outer bonds go through them. Write n at the bottom right.", "why": "This shows that the unit repeats n times along the chain."}, {"do": "Check: only single bonds in the chain, two carbons, and one Cl.", "why": "A C=C left in the repeat unit would lose the mark."}], "answer": "[–CH₂–CHCl–]ₙ (brackets with bonds going through them, n at the bottom right, only C–C single bonds)"}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the C=C in the monomer drawn as C–C in the repeat unit?", "o": ["The hydrogen atoms leave, so the carbons only need one bond between them", "Chlorine takes one bond from the carbons, so the double bond cannot stay", "One bond of the double bond breaks so each carbon can join to the next monomer", "A small molecule such as water is lost when the monomers join"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. No atoms leave in addition polymerisation. The hydrogens stay on the carbons. The bond opens to link to other monomers.", "Wrong. Chlorine stays as a side group and does not take a bond. The double bond opens so the carbon atoms can link to the next monomer.", "Right. One of the two bonds in C=C breaks, leaving a free bond on each carbon. These bonds join to the next monomers in the chain.", "Wrong. This describes condensation polymerisation, where a small molecule is lost. In addition polymerisation the polymer is the only product."], "a_note": null}, "faded": {"task": "Tetrafluoroethene, CF₂=CF₂, is the monomer for poly(tetrafluoroethene), PTFE. Draw the repeat unit.", "given": ["Each carbon of C=C has two F atoms attached.", "Open the double bond: C=C becomes C–C, with a free bond on each carbon."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Remove two F atoms to make room for the chain, leaving –CF–CF–", "Keep all four F atoms and write [–CF₂–CF₂–]ₙ with n at the bottom right", "Keep the double bond and write [–CF₂=CF₂–]ₙ so the monomer is unchanged", "Move the F atoms into the chain and write [–C–F–C–F–]ₙ"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. No atoms are lost in addition polymerisation. All four fluorine atoms stay on the carbons.", "Right. The atoms stay on the same carbons, the bond is single, and the brackets with n show the repeating unit.", "Wrong. The C=C opens during polymerisation. A repeat unit with a double bond is the commonest error.", "Wrong. Fluorine atoms stay as side atoms on the carbons. Only the two carbons form the backbone."]}, "trap": "Leaving C=C in the repeat unit, or choosing a saturated molecule as the monomer. Monomers have C=C. The repeat unit has only C–C, with every side atom kept.", "recap": "The C=C in the monomer opens to C–C, every side atom stays, and the repeat unit goes in brackets with n."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.7.3.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Higher tier questions ask you to explain how a polyester or polypeptide forms, naming the groups that react, the link made and the small molecule lost.", "hook": {"q": "Monomers join in a long chain. At every join, a small molecule such as water leaves. How does the polymer's mass compare with the total mass of the monomers?", "o": ["The polymer has the same mass as all the monomers added together", "The polymer has a greater mass, because new bonds add mass", "The polymer has a smaller mass, because the small molecules have left", "The two cannot be compared, because polymers are too big to weigh"], "a": 2, "f": ["Mass is only the same if nothing leaves. Here a small molecule is lost at every join, so it is not all in the polymer.", "Bonds do not add mass. Atoms leave as water, so the polymer has less mass than the monomers used.", "Right. The water (or other small molecule) leaves the polymer, so the polymer's mass is less than the monomers' total.", "The masses can be compared. Each monomer has a relative formula mass, and the lost molecules can be counted."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Two groups, one small molecule", "b": "In **condensation polymerisation**, each monomer has two **functional groups**, one at each end. A group on one monomer reacts with a group on the next. This joins them. At each join, a small molecule is lost, usually **water**. Addition polymerisation is different: it uses C=C and loses nothing."}, {"h": "Polyesters", "b": "A **dicarboxylic acid** has –COOH at both ends. A **diol** has –OH at both ends. A –COOH and an –OH react to make an **ester link**, –COO–, and lose H₂O. The two monomers alternate along the chain. The product is a polyester."}, {"h": "Amino acids and polypeptides", "b": "An **amino acid** has an amine group, –NH₂, and a carboxylic acid group, –COOH. The –COOH of one molecule reacts with the –NH₂ of the next. Water is lost and a **peptide link**, –CONH–, forms. A long chain of amino acids is a **polypeptide**. Glycine is NH₂CH₂COOH."}, {"h": "One monomer or two?", "b": "A monomer with two different groups, such as an **amino acid**, can join to copies of itself. A monomer with two identical groups, such as a **diol** or a **dicarboxylic acid**, cannot. It needs a partner with the other group. So a polyester from a diol and a diacid needs two monomers."}], "example": {"task": "Ethane-1,2-diol, HO–CH₂CH₂–OH, reacts with a dicarboxylic acid to make a polyester. Explain how the polymer forms.", "steps": [{"do": "State that the diol has an –OH group at each end and the dicarboxylic acid has a –COOH group at each end.", "why": "Condensation polymerisation needs monomers with two functional groups. Naming them shows you know this."}, {"do": "Say that an –OH group on the diol reacts with a –COOH group on the acid.", "why": "The reacting groups must be named. Ester links come from –OH with –COOH."}, {"do": "Say that this forms an ester link, –COO–, and a molecule of water is lost.", "why": "Marks are given for the link and for the small molecule lost. Both make it a condensation reaction."}, {"do": "Say that the joined molecule still has a reactive group at each end, so the reaction repeats.", "why": "This explains why a long chain forms, not just a pair of molecules."}, {"do": "Say that diol and acid units alternate, giving a polyester and many water molecules.", "why": "This links the repeating pattern to the final product."}], "answer": "Each monomer has two functional groups: –OH on the diol, –COOH on the acid. An –OH reacts with a –COOH, forming an ester link and losing water. Both ends can still react, so the chain grows, alternating diol and acid, to make a polyester."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the chain keep growing after the first diol and acid have joined?", "o": ["The water that is lost acts as a catalyst for the next join", "The joined molecule still has a reactive group at each end", "The ester link opens up and bonds to the next monomer", "A C=C bond in the acid opens to let more monomers join"], "a": 1, "f": ["Water is just a product that leaves. Chain growth does not depend on it acting as a catalyst.", "Right. The diol still has a free –OH at one end and the acid a free –COOH at the other, so more monomers can join.", "The ester link is stable and stays as part of the chain. New monomers join at the free ends, not by opening this link.", "This mixes up addition and condensation polymerisation. Polyesters grow through –OH and –COOH groups, not C=C bonds."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how molecules of glycine, NH₂CH₂COOH, join to form a polypeptide.", "given": ["Each glycine molecule has two functional groups: –NH₂ at one end and –COOH at the other.", "So glycine can react with other glycine molecules."], "q": "What is the next point in the explanation?", "o": ["The –COOH groups react with –OH groups, forming ester links and losing water", "The C=C bonds open and the molecules join, with no small molecule lost", "The –NH₂ groups of two molecules react together, forming a peptide link and losing water", "The –COOH of one reacts with the –NH₂ of the next, losing water and forming a peptide link"], "a": 3, "f": ["Glycine has no –OH alcohol group. Ester links are for polyesters. Amino acids make peptide links.", "Glycine has no C=C bond. This describes addition polymerisation, which loses nothing.", "Two –NH₂ groups do not react with each other. The –NH₂ reacts with a –COOH on the next molecule.", "Right. The –COOH of one reacts with the –NH₂ of the next, giving a peptide link, –CONH–, and water."]}, "trap": "Saying nothing is lost, as in addition polymerisation. Every link in a condensation polymer loses a small molecule, usually H₂O. Always name the link (ester or peptide) and the water lost.", "recap": "Condensation polymerisation joins monomers with two functional groups, losing water at each link: an ester link in polyesters, a peptide link in polypeptides."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.7.3.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions on this topic ask you to name the monomers of DNA, proteins, starch and cellulose, and to describe the structure of DNA.", "hook": {"q": "A code stores information using a few different symbols in a chosen order. What must DNA's monomers be like for DNA to store a code?", "o": ["All identical, repeated again and again, like the monomers in poly(ethene)", "Four different kinds, which can be joined in any order along the chain", "One single huge unit that is not repeated, so there is no chain", "Many small alkenes, each with a C=C double bond that opens up to join"], "a": 1, "f": ["Identical units repeated give no code. Poly(ethene) works this way, but DNA does not.", "Right. Four different monomers in a varied order can store a code, just as letters in a chosen order spell out words.", "DNA is a polymer, so it is a chain of many monomers, not one unit.", "Alkenes are the monomers of addition polymers such as poly(ethene). DNA's monomers are not alkenes."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "DNA is a polymer", "b": "**DNA** is a very large molecule that carries the genetic instructions for how living things develop and work. Most DNA is made of two polymer chains twisted together in a **double helix**. Each chain is a long line of repeating units called **nucleotides**."}, {"h": "Four different nucleotides", "b": "Each **nucleotide** has three parts: a sugar, a **phosphate group** and one **base**. There are four different bases, so there are four different nucleotides. They are joined in a chain in a varied order. The order of the nucleotides is the code."}, {"h": "Proteins", "b": "**Proteins** are natural polymers made from **amino acids**. Many different amino acids are used. Each protein has its own order of amino acids, so proteins have different shapes and jobs. Do not mix them up with DNA, which is made of nucleotides."}, {"h": "Starch and cellulose", "b": "**Starch** and **cellulose** are natural polymers made from **sugar** units (glucose). Plants use starch to store energy. Cellulose forms the strong walls of plant cells. Both are made of sugar units, but the units are joined differently, so their properties differ."}], "example": {"task": "Describe the structure of DNA. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "DNA is a polymer. It is a very large molecule made of many repeating units joined in long chains.", "why": "A polymer is many monomers joined. Naming DNA as a polymer gives the starting point."}, {"do": "The repeating units are nucleotides. Each nucleotide contains a sugar, a phosphate group and a base.", "why": "This names the monomer correctly and gives its three parts. Do not call the monomers amino acids."}, {"do": "There are four different nucleotides, which differ in their base. They are joined in a chain in a varied order.", "why": "This shows DNA has four kinds of monomer, not one, and that the order carries the code."}, {"do": "Most DNA has two of these chains twisted together to make a double helix.", "why": "This is the overall shape. State that there are two chains, not just that it is a helix."}], "answer": "DNA is a polymer made of many nucleotides. Each nucleotide has a sugar, a phosphate group and a base. There are four different nucleotides, joined in a varied order. Most DNA is two chains twisted together as a double helix."}, "selfx": {"q": "Step 3 says the four nucleotides differ in their base. Why is that the part that differs?", "o": ["The bases are the monomers, and the sugar and phosphate are joined on afterwards", "Each nucleotide has a different phosphate group, and the base is the same in all four", "The sugar and phosphate group are the same in all four, so only the base can vary", "The base is a C=C double bond, and each nucleotide has a different number of them"], "a": 2, "f": ["The nucleotide is the monomer. The base is only one of its three parts.", "This is the wrong way round. The phosphate group is the same in each nucleotide. The base is the part that varies.", "Right. The sugar and phosphate group are the same in every nucleotide. Only the base changes, and there are four types.", "Bases are not C=C double bonds. That feature belongs to alkene monomers, not to DNA."]}, "faded": {"task": "Describe the structure of starch. (3 marks)", "given": ["Starch is a natural polymer.", "It is a very large molecule made of many repeating units."], "q": "Which statement should come next to name the repeating units correctly?", "o": ["The repeating units are amino acids, joined in a long chain.", "The repeating units are nucleotides, each with a phosphate group and a base.", "The repeating units are alkenes, each with a C=C double bond.", "The repeating units are sugar (glucose) units, joined in long chains."], "a": 3, "f": ["Amino acids are the monomers of proteins, not starch.", "Nucleotides are the monomers of DNA, not starch.", "Alkenes are the monomers of addition polymers such as poly(ethene). Starch is a natural polymer made from sugar units.", "Right. Starch is made of sugar (glucose) units joined in long chains."]}, "trap": "Swapping the monomers: calling DNA's monomers amino acids or bases. Learn the pairs: DNA from nucleotides, proteins from amino acids, starch and cellulose from sugar units.", "recap": "DNA is made of four different nucleotides, proteins of amino acids, and starch and cellulose of sugar units."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.8.1.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often give melting or boiling point data and ask whether a sample is pure, or ask you to recall what a formulation is.", "hook": {"q": "Salt is spread on icy roads. The ice then melts even when the air is below 0 °C. What is the best explanation?", "o": ["Salt releases heat as it dissolves, so it warms the ice above 0 °C", "Salt mixes with the ice and lowers its melting point, so it melts below 0 °C", "Salt raises the melting point of ice, so the ice melts faster", "Salt reacts with ice to make a new solid that cannot freeze"], "a": 1, "f": ["Salt does not warm the ice. The ice melts because its melting point has changed, not because heat is added.", "Right. The salt is an impurity in the ice. Impurities lower the melting point, so the ice melts below 0 °C.", "A higher melting point would make ice harder to melt. Impurities lower the melting point, not raise it.", "No new solid forms. Salt and ice make a mixture, and the impurity lowers the melting point."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What 'pure' means", "b": "In chemistry, a **pure substance** is a single **element** or a single **compound**. Nothing else is mixed in. Everyday language is looser. 'Pure orange juice' only means nothing has been added. It is still a mixture of many substances."}, {"h": "Melting and boiling points", "b": "A pure substance melts at one **sharp**, fixed temperature. It boils at one fixed temperature too. An **impure** sample melts over a **range** of temperatures, and it starts melting lower than the pure value. Impurities usually raise the boiling point, and it boils over a range."}, {"h": "Using the data", "b": "Compare the sample's reading with the **data-book value** for the pure substance. Sharp and matching means probably pure. A range, or a lower melting point, means **impure**. A sharp match cannot tell you whether the substance is an element or a compound."}, {"h": "What a formulation is", "b": "A **formulation** is a mixture designed as a useful product. It is made by mixing components in carefully **measured quantities**, so the product has the properties needed. Examples are fuels, paints, medicines, cleaning agents, alloys, fertilisers and foods. Formulations are mixtures, so they are not pure substances."}], "example": {"task": "Pure naphthalene melts at 80 °C. Sample P melts at 80 °C. Sample Q melts from 72 °C to 77 °C. The graph shows heating curves for P and Q. Explain which sample is likely to be pure.", "steps": [{"do": "A pure substance melts at one sharp, fixed temperature.", "why": "This is the rule the whole answer uses."}, {"do": "Sample P melts at 80 °C. This matches the pure value, so P is likely to be pure.", "why": "A sharp melting point that matches the data shows purity."}, {"do": "Sample Q melts over a range of 5 °C (72 °C to 77 °C), so Q is not pure.", "why": "A pure substance does not melt over a range."}, {"do": "All of Q's values are below 80 °C. Impurities lower the melting point.", "why": "A second piece of evidence supports the same conclusion."}], "answer": "P is likely to be pure because it melts at 80 °C, the sharp value for pure naphthalene. Q melts over a range and below 80 °C, so it is impure.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 8], "y": [71, 80], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "Time", "ylabel": "Temperature in °C", "lines": [{"pts": [[0.2, 71], [2, 80], [6, 80]], "label": "P", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0.8, 71], [1, 72], [6, 77], [6.6, 80]], "label": "Q", "c": 1}]}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention that Q's values are below 80 °C as well as being a range?", "o": ["Pure substances can only melt below their data-book value, so Q is pure", "The data-book value is unreliable, so a lower value is expected", "A lower melting point shows Q is a different element from naphthalene", "Impurities widen the range and lower the melting point, so both signs agree"], "a": 3, "f": ["Pure substances melt at their fixed value, not below it. A value below it points to impurity.", "The data-book value is the reliable reference. The lower reading is the evidence, not an error.", "A lower value does not show a different element. It shows the naphthalene is mixed with impurities.", "Right. Impurities cause both effects at once. Giving the range and the lower values makes the conclusion stronger."]}, "faded": {"task": "Pure compound Y melts at 95 °C. A sample melts from 88 °C to 93 °C. Explain whether the sample is pure.", "given": ["A pure substance melts at one sharp, fixed temperature.", "The sample melts over a range of 5 °C, so it is not melting at one fixed temperature."], "q": "Which point should come next?", "o": ["The range is below 95 °C, so the sample is a different pure compound", "Impurities raise the melting point, so the sample must be impure", "All the values are below 95 °C, and impurities lower the melting point, so it is impure", "The range is narrow, so the sample is pure enough to count as pure"], "a": 2, "f": ["A different pure compound would also melt sharply. A range shows a mixture, not a new pure compound.", "The conclusion is right but the fact is wrong. Impurities lower the melting point, not raise it.", "Right. The temperatures are all below the pure value of 95 °C. That is a second sign of impurity.", "A narrow range is still a range. Any range shows the sample is impure."]}, "trap": "Thinking a melting range means pure. A range, or a value below the pure melting point, shows impurity. Only a sharp point matching the data-book value suggests purity.", "recap": "Pure substances melt and boil at sharp, fixed temperatures; impure samples melt over a lower range; a formulation is a designed mixture."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.8.1.3", "kind": "method", "why": "Chromatography is a required practical, so Paper 2 can ask you to measure distances, calculate an Rf value and use it to name a substance.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "How the substances separate", "b": "The **solvent** is the **mobile phase**. It moves up the paper, which is the **stationary phase**. Each substance spends time dissolved in the solvent and time stuck to the paper. Substances that dissolve better in the solvent travel further up the paper, so the mixture separates."}, {"h": "Measuring the distances", "b": "Measure from the **start line** to the centre of each spot. Measure the solvent distance from the same start line, up to the **solvent front**. Mark the solvent front in pencil as soon as you take the paper out, because the solvent evaporates. Use the same unit for both."}, {"h": "Calculating and matching Rf", "b": "$R_f = \\frac{\\text{distance moved by substance}}{\\text{distance moved by solvent}}$. It has no units and is never more than 1. In the same solvent, a substance always has the same **Rf value**. So you can match a spot to a table of known values. A **pure substance** gives one spot."}], "example": {"task": "An ink spot moved 4.5 cm from the start line. The solvent front moved 7.5 cm from the same line. Known Rf values in this solvent: red 0.30, blue 0.60, green 0.80. Calculate the Rf and name the dye.", "steps": [{"do": "Note the distances: substance 4.5 cm, solvent 7.5 cm. Both are measured from the start line.", "why": "Rf compares the two distances, so both must start at the same place."}, {"do": "Write the formula: $R_f = \\frac{\\text{distance moved by substance}}{\\text{distance moved by solvent}}$.", "why": "The substance distance goes on top, because the solvent always travels furthest."}, {"do": "Substitute the values: $R_f = \\frac{4.5}{7.5}$.", "why": "The distances are in the same unit, so you can use them as they are."}, {"do": "Calculate: $R_f = 0.60$. Write no unit.", "why": "The cm cancel. A value below 1 is a sensible check."}, {"do": "Compare 0.60 with the table. It matches blue.", "why": "The table is for the same solvent, so the values can be compared."}], "answer": "Rf = 0.60 (no units). This matches blue, so the dye is blue."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why can you compare the Rf of 0.60 with the table in the last step?", "o": ["A different solvent changes the start line, so the distances could not be measured", "Rf has units that depend on which solvent is used, so they must match the table", "The table values were found in the same solvent, and a substance's Rf changes with solvent", "Only one solvent can separate a mixture, so other solvents give no spots"], "a": 2, "f": ["You draw the start line yourself, in pencil. The real issue is that a substance's Rf value changes when the solvent changes.", "Rf has no units in any solvent, because the distances cancel. The solvent affects the value, not the units.", "Correct. A substance's Rf depends on the solvent. Only values from the same solvent can be matched fairly.", "Other solvents also separate mixtures. They just give different Rf values, so you cannot compare across solvents."]}, "faded": {"task": "A spot moved 3.6 cm from the start line. The solvent front moved 9.0 cm from the same line. Find the Rf value.", "given": ["Distances are both from the start line: substance 3.6 cm, solvent 9.0 cm.", "$R_f = \\frac{\\text{distance moved by substance}}{\\text{distance moved by solvent}}$"], "q": "What is the next step and result?", "o": ["Divide solvent by substance: $\\frac{9.0}{3.6} = 2.5$", "Divide substance by solvent: $\\frac{3.6}{9.0} = 0.40$, with no units", "Subtract the distances: $9.0 - 3.6 = 5.4$", "Divide substance by solvent and give the unit: $0.40$ cm"], "a": 1, "f": ["This is the wrong way up. It gives a value above 1, which Rf can never be. The substance distance goes on top.", "Correct. The substance distance goes on top, so 3.6 ÷ 9.0 = 0.40. Both distances are in cm, so the units cancel.", "Rf is a ratio, so you divide. Subtracting gives the gap between the spot and the solvent front, which is not Rf.", "The division is right, but Rf has no unit. Both distances are in cm, so the cm cancel."]}, "trap": "Dividing the wrong way up, or measuring from the bottom of the paper instead of the start line. Substance goes on top, both distances come from the start line, and Rf is never above 1.", "recap": "Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent, both from the start line, and it has no units and is never above 1."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.8.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask for a gas test and its positive result, and you lose marks if either the test or the result is missing.", "hook": {"q": "A splint with a glowing end, but no flame, is put into a test tube of pure oxygen. What do you think happens?", "o": ["The glow goes out straight away", "The splint relights", "The splint makes a squeaky pop", "The splint turns cloudy"], "a": 1, "f": ["Oxygen helps things burn, so it does not put out a glowing end. The glow gets hotter instead.", "Right. Oxygen makes things burn better, so the glowing splint bursts back into flame.", "A squeaky pop is the test for hydrogen with a lit splint. It is not what oxygen does.", "Cloudy is a result for limewater with carbon dioxide. A splint cannot turn cloudy."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Splint tests: H₂ and O₂", "b": "For hydrogen, hold a **lit splint** at the mouth of the test tube. Hydrogen burns quickly with oxygen in the air and gives a **squeaky pop**. For oxygen, light a splint, then blow out the flame so the end only glows. Put the **glowing splint** in the tube. Oxygen makes it relight."}, {"h": "Limewater test for CO₂", "b": "Bubble the gas through **limewater**. This is a colourless solution of calcium hydroxide. If the gas is **carbon dioxide**, the limewater turns **cloudy** (milky). A white solid, calcium carbonate, forms and does not dissolve. If the limewater stays clear, the gas is not carbon dioxide."}, {"h": "Litmus test for chlorine", "b": "Hold a piece of **damp litmus paper** in the gas. **Chlorine** dissolves in the water on the paper and **bleaches** it white. The paper must be damp. Chlorine is toxic, so the test is done in a fume cupboard."}], "example": {"task": "Calcium carbonate reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid and makes a gas. Describe how to test that the gas is carbon dioxide and give the result.", "steps": [{"do": "Pour some limewater into a test tube.", "why": "Limewater is the test reagent for carbon dioxide. It starts as a clear, colourless solution."}, {"do": "Bubble the gas from the reaction through the limewater.", "why": "The gas must touch the solution so that the two can react."}, {"do": "Watch the limewater. It turns cloudy (milky).", "why": "Cloudy is the positive result. If nothing changes, the gas is not carbon dioxide."}, {"do": "Explain the change: a white solid, calcium carbonate, has formed.", "why": "This insoluble solid is what makes the limewater look cloudy."}], "answer": "Bubble the gas through limewater. If the gas is carbon dioxide, the limewater turns cloudy because insoluble white calcium carbonate forms."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the limewater turns cloudy?", "o": ["Carbon dioxide burns in the limewater and makes smoke", "Carbon dioxide dissolves and makes a coloured solution", "The limewater loses its water and becomes thick", "Carbon dioxide reacts with it to make insoluble calcium carbonate"], "a": 3, "f": ["Carbon dioxide does not burn. The cloudiness is a solid forming in the liquid, not smoke.", "Carbon dioxide does not give a coloured solution here. The limewater turns cloudy, not a new colour.", "The limewater does not lose its water. A new white solid appears in it.", "Right. The reaction makes calcium carbonate, which does not dissolve. Tiny solid particles make the limewater look cloudy."]}, "faded": {"task": "A student thinks a gas given off in an experiment is chlorine. Describe how to test for chlorine and give the result.", "given": ["Work in a fume cupboard, because chlorine is toxic.", "Dampen a piece of litmus paper with a little water."], "q": "What is the next step and the result that shows the gas is chlorine?", "o": ["Hold a lit splint in the gas; it makes a squeaky pop", "Hold the dry litmus paper in the gas; it is bleached white", "Hold the damp litmus paper in the gas; it is bleached white", "Bubble the gas through limewater; it turns cloudy"], "a": 2, "f": ["A lit splint and a squeaky pop is the test for hydrogen, not chlorine.", "The paper was dampened in the step before, and it must stay damp. Chlorine needs the water to bleach the paper.", "Right. Chlorine dissolves in the water on the paper and bleaches it white. This is the positive result.", "Limewater turning cloudy is the test for carbon dioxide. Chlorine is tested with damp litmus paper."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the splint tests, or writing a test without its result. Hydrogen: lit splint, squeaky pop. Oxygen: glowing splint relights. Always write the test and the result together.", "recap": "Learn each gas as test plus result: H₂ lit splint pops, O₂ glowing splint relights, CO₂ clouds limewater, Cl₂ bleaches damp litmus."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.8.3.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to identify an unknown compound from test results, or to describe the tests from the required practical.", "hook": {"q": "Two clear solutions are mixed and the mixture turns cloudy white. What has most likely formed?", "o": ["A tiny solid that does not dissolve in the liquid", "A gas that has dissolved in the liquid", "A stronger, more concentrated solution", "A new layer of a different liquid"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. An insoluble solid forming from two solutions is called a precipitate. It makes the mixture cloudy. Most ion tests rely on this.", "A dissolved gas would not make the liquid cloudy. Gases show as bubbles or fizzing, not a white cloud.", "A more concentrated solution stays clear. Cloudiness means tiny solid particles are scattered in the liquid.", "A separate liquid layer would show a clear boundary, not a white cloudiness. Cloudy white means small solid particles."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Flame tests", "b": "Dip a clean wire loop in the sample and hold it in a blue Bunsen flame. The **flame colour** shows the **metal ion**: lithium crimson, sodium yellow, potassium lilac, calcium orange-red, copper(II) green. In a mixture, one colour can **mask** another."}, {"h": "Sodium hydroxide test", "b": "Add **sodium hydroxide solution** to the metal ion solution. Coloured **precipitate**s: Cu²⁺ blue, Fe²⁺ green, Fe³⁺ brown. White precipitates: Al³⁺, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺. Only aluminium hydroxide dissolves in **excess** sodium hydroxide, giving a colourless solution. Calcium and magnesium stay as precipitates."}, {"h": "Carbonates and halides", "b": "**Carbonates**: add dilute acid. It fizzes. Bubble the gas through limewater, which turns cloudy, showing CO₂. **Halides**: add dilute nitric acid, then **silver nitrate** solution. Chloride gives a white precipitate, bromide cream, iodide yellow."}, {"h": "Sulfate test", "b": "Add dilute **hydrochloric acid**, then **barium chloride** solution. A white precipitate of barium sulfate means **sulfate** ions are present. The acid goes first because it removes carbonate ions, which would also give a white precipitate with barium ions."}], "example": {"task": "A white solid is potassium sulfate. Describe the tests that show it contains potassium ions and sulfate ions. Give the result of each test.", "steps": [{"do": "Flame test: dip a clean wire loop in the solid and hold it in a blue Bunsen flame. A lilac flame shows K⁺.", "why": "Each metal ion gives its own flame colour. Lilac means potassium."}, {"do": "Dissolve some of the solid in distilled water.", "why": "The sulfate test works on a solution, so the ions can react."}, {"do": "Add dilute hydrochloric acid to the solution.", "why": "The acid removes any carbonate ions, which would also give a white precipitate with barium ions."}, {"do": "Add barium chloride solution. A white precipitate of barium sulfate forms.", "why": "Barium ions and sulfate ions make insoluble barium sulfate. The white precipitate shows sulfate ions."}, {"do": "Conclude: the solid is potassium sulfate.", "why": "Name both ions. The flame test gives the metal, and the barium chloride test gives the sulfate."}], "answer": "A lilac flame test shows potassium ions. Dissolve the solid, add dilute hydrochloric acid, then barium chloride solution. A white precipitate of barium sulfate shows sulfate ions. So the solid is potassium sulfate."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is dilute hydrochloric acid added before the barium chloride solution?", "o": ["It reacts with sulfate ions to make them insoluble", "It removes carbonate ions that would also give a white precipitate", "It helps the solid dissolve faster in the water", "It supplies the barium ions that form the precipitate"], "a": 1, "f": ["The acid does not make sulfate insoluble. Barium ions do that, forming barium sulfate. The acid has a different job.", "Right. Carbonate ions would form white barium carbonate. Acid reacts with them first, so a white precipitate can only come from sulfate.", "The solid is already dissolved by this step. The acid is added to prevent a false result, not to speed up dissolving.", "The barium ions come from the barium chloride solution, not from the acid. Hydrochloric acid contains hydrogen and chloride ions."]}, "faded": {"task": "Describe tests to show that a solution is potassium bromide. Give the result of each test.", "given": ["Flame test: a lilac flame shows K⁺ is present.", "Add dilute nitric acid to a sample of the solution."], "q": "What is the next step for the bromide ions?", "o": ["Add barium chloride solution; a white precipitate shows bromide ions", "Add silver nitrate solution; a white precipitate shows bromide ions", "Add silver nitrate solution; a yellow precipitate shows bromide ions", "Add silver nitrate solution; a cream precipitate shows bromide ions"], "a": 3, "f": ["Barium chloride is the sulfate test. Halides are tested with silver nitrate solution.", "Silver nitrate is correct, but a white precipitate shows chloride, not bromide.", "Silver nitrate is correct, but a yellow precipitate shows iodide. Bromide gives cream.", "Right. Silver nitrate with halide ions gives a precipitate, and bromide gives a cream one. Chloride is white and iodide is yellow."]}, "trap": "Naming only one ion. The flame test gives only the metal ion, and the anion needs its own test. Name the full compound, such as potassium sulfate, and give each result.", "recap": "Each ion has its own test: a flame test or sodium hydroxide for metal ions, then acid plus a reagent for carbonate, halide or sulfate."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.8.3.6", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to give advantages of instrumental methods, or to describe and interpret flame emission spectra, usually for 2 to 4 marks.", "hook": {"q": "A forensic scientist has one tiny speck of unknown powder. She needs a reliable result quickly. Which method suits best?", "o": ["A test tube test that needs several grams of the powder", "A test judged by eye from a colour change", "An instrument that detects tiny amounts and works quickly", "A test that needs no equipment, so it must be the most accurate"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong: a test needing several grams cannot be done on one tiny speck. Instrumental methods are sensitive, so they work on very small samples.", "Wrong: judging a colour by eye depends on the person and is less accurate. Instruments give more reliable results.", "Right: instrumental methods are sensitive (they detect tiny amounts), accurate and rapid. That suits a tiny sample needed fast.", "Wrong: having no equipment does not make a test accurate. Simple chemical tests are usually less accurate and less sensitive than instruments."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why use instruments?", "b": "Instrumental methods use machines to detect and identify elements and compounds. Compared with chemical tests, they are **accurate**, **sensitive** (they can detect very small amounts) and **rapid**. Results do not depend on judging a colour by eye."}, {"h": "Drawbacks", "b": "Instruments have drawbacks. The equipment is **expensive** and the operator needs **training**. A chemical test in a test tube is cheap and simple, but it is slower and less sensitive."}, {"h": "How the method works", "b": "In **flame emission spectroscopy**, a solution of the sample is put into a flame. The light given out passes through a **spectroscope**. This produces a **line spectrum**: a set of separate coloured lines. Each metal ion gives its own pattern of lines."}, {"h": "Reading a line spectrum", "b": "The **positions** of the lines are matched to known spectra to identify the ions. A mixture shows lines from each ion present. The **intensity** (brightness) of the lines shows the concentration: brighter lines mean a more concentrated ion. On the graph (illustrative values), a sample's intensity is read across to the line, then down to its concentration.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 20], "xstep": 2, "ystep": 4, "xlabel": "concentration of ion in mg/dm³", "ylabel": "line intensity in arbitrary units", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 20]], "label": "standards", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 12], [6, 12], [6, 0]], "dash": true, "c": 1}], "points": [{"at": [6, 12], "label": "sample"}], "xnums": true, "ynums": true}}], "example": {"task": "A water sample may contain metal ions. Describe how flame emission spectroscopy can identify the ions and show how much of each is present.", "steps": [{"do": "Put a solution of the sample into a flame.", "why": "The metal ions in the solution give out light in the flame."}, {"do": "Pass the light given out through a spectroscope.", "why": "This produces a line spectrum from the sample."}, {"do": "Compare the positions of the lines with known spectra of metal ions.", "why": "Each ion has its own pattern of lines, so a match identifies the ion. Lines from two ions mean both are present."}, {"do": "Compare the intensity of the lines, or measure it.", "why": "Brighter lines mean a higher concentration of that ion, so this gives how much is present."}], "answer": "The solution is put into a flame and the light given out passes through a spectroscope to give a line spectrum. Line positions are matched to known spectra to identify the ions. Line intensity shows concentration."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer compare the line positions with known spectra?", "o": ["Known spectra show how concentrated the sample is", "Each metal ion has its own pattern of lines, so a match identifies it", "It checks that the flame was hot enough", "It shows how much of the sample was used"], "a": 1, "f": ["This swaps the two ideas. Concentration comes from the intensity of the lines. Line positions identify the ion.", "Right: every metal ion gives its own set of lines, like a fingerprint. Matching the pattern to a known spectrum identifies the ion.", "Flame temperature is not what the comparison checks. The line positions are matched to identify the ions present.", "The amount of sample used is not shown by line positions. Matching positions identifies which ions are present."]}, "faded": {"task": "A river water sample is tested by flame emission spectroscopy. Its spectrum shows lines from more than one metal ion. You must describe how the ions are identified.", "given": ["The sample solution is put into a flame.", "The light given out is passed through a spectroscope to give a line spectrum."], "q": "What is the next step to identify the ions?", "o": ["Measure the pH of the solution to find which metals are present", "Add sodium hydroxide solution and note the colour of any precipitate", "Use only the brightness of the lines to decide which ions are present", "Compare the positions of the lines with known spectra of metal ions"], "a": 3, "f": ["pH does not identify metal ions in this method. The line spectrum is what identifies them.", "This is a chemical test, not part of flame emission spectroscopy. The spectrum is already produced, so you now analyse it.", "Brightness (intensity) shows concentration, not which ion it is. This swaps the two ideas. Line positions identify the ions.", "Right: each ion has its own line pattern. Matching the line positions to known spectra identifies every ion in the mixture."]}, "trap": "Saying instruments are cheap or simple. They are usually expensive and need training. Their advantages are speed, accuracy and sensitivity. Also do not swap: line positions show which ion; intensity shows concentration.", "recap": "Instrumental methods are rapid, accurate and sensitive; flame emission gives a line spectrum whose line positions identify metal ions and whose intensity shows concentration."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.9.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to describe the air today and to explain how photosynthesis and other processes changed the early atmosphere into the one we have now.", "hook": {"q": "Today about 20% of the air is oxygen, but the early atmosphere had little or none. Where did most of this oxygen come from?", "o": ["Erupting volcanoes", "Photosynthesis by algae and plants", "Rain splitting water in the clouds", "Rocks slowly giving it off"], "a": 1, "f": ["Volcanoes mainly released carbon dioxide, water vapour and nitrogen. They were not the main source of oxygen.", "Right. Algae, then plants, made oxygen as a product of photosynthesis. It built up in the air over billions of years.", "Rain does not split water into oxygen. That would need a lot of energy, and it is not how the oxygen formed.", "Rocks did not release the oxygen. Living things made it by photosynthesis."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Dry air today", "b": "The graph shows dry air: about **80% nitrogen** and about **20% oxygen** (AQA specification values). The rest is small amounts of other gases: **carbon dioxide** (about 0.04%), argon and other noble gases. Water vapour is also in air, varying from place to place. These proportions have stayed roughly the same for the last 200 million years.", "fig": {"type": "bar", "cats": ["Nitrogen", "Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide"], "vals": [80, 20, 0.04], "y": [0, 100], "ystep": 20, "xlabel": "Gas in dry air", "ylabel": "Percentage of dry air (%)"}}, {"h": "The early atmosphere", "b": "In the first billion years there was intense **volcanic activity**. Volcanoes released mainly **carbon dioxide**, plus water vapour and nitrogen, with small amounts of methane and ammonia. There was little or no oxygen. As the Earth cooled, the water vapour **condensed** and formed the oceans."}, {"h": "Oxygen builds up", "b": "**Algae** in the oceans began **photosynthesis**, and plants followed. They take in carbon dioxide and release **oxygen**: carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen. Over billions of years oxygen slowly rose to about 20% of the air. This allowed animals to evolve."}, {"h": "Carbon dioxide falls", "b": "Carbon dioxide fell in several ways. Algae and plants **used it up** in photosynthesis. Some **dissolved** in the oceans and formed carbonate sediments. Carbon was also **locked away** in sedimentary rocks such as limestone, and in fossil fuels made from dead plants and sea organisms."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how the amount of oxygen in the atmosphere increased over billions of years. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "The early atmosphere had little or no oxygen. It was mainly carbon dioxide, released by volcanoes.", "why": "This gives the starting point, so the change in oxygen can be shown."}, {"do": "Algae evolved in the oceans and carried out photosynthesis: carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen.", "why": "This names the process that makes oxygen and the reactants it uses."}, {"do": "The oxygen was released and gradually built up in the atmosphere over a very long time.", "why": "It shows the change was slow, not sudden."}, {"do": "Plants later evolved and added more oxygen, until it reached about 20% of the air.", "why": "This links the process to the final value in the air today."}], "answer": "At first there was little or no oxygen. Algae, then plants, carried out photosynthesis, using carbon dioxide and water to make glucose and oxygen. The oxygen was released and slowly built up over billions of years. It reached about 20% of the air today."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer explain the oxygen increase using photosynthesis and not volcanoes?", "o": ["Volcanoes only began to erupt after algae had evolved", "Volcanoes released oxygen, but the oceans absorbed all of it", "Photosynthesis makes nitrogen, which then changes into oxygen", "Early volcanoes released mainly carbon dioxide, not oxygen"], "a": 3, "f": ["Volcanic activity was intense in the first billion years, long before algae. Timing is not the reason.", "Volcanoes did not release oxygen in any large amount, so there was nothing for the oceans to absorb.", "Photosynthesis makes glucose and oxygen. It does not make nitrogen, and nitrogen does not change into oxygen.", "Right. Volcanic gases were mainly carbon dioxide, water vapour and nitrogen, so only photosynthesis can explain the rise."]}, "faded": {"task": "Describe how the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere decreased over billions of years. (4 marks)", "given": ["Point 1: The early atmosphere was mainly carbon dioxide from volcanoes.", "Point 2: Algae and plants took in carbon dioxide for photosynthesis."], "q": "Which is the best third point for the answer?", "o": ["Some dissolved in the oceans and formed carbonate sediments.", "Some was changed into nitrogen by bacteria in the oceans.", "Some escaped into space as the Earth cooled down.", "Some changed into oxygen when the volcanoes stopped erupting."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Dissolved carbon dioxide formed carbonate sediments, which were locked away in sedimentary rocks such as limestone.", "Carbon dioxide is not turned into nitrogen. The nitrogen came from volcanoes and built up gradually.", "The carbon was not lost to space. It was locked in rocks and fossil fuels.", "Carbon dioxide does not turn into oxygen when volcanoes stop. In photosynthesis the carbon goes into glucose."]}, "trap": "Saying oxygen came from volcanoes. Early volcanoes released mainly carbon dioxide, water vapour and nitrogen. Oxygen came from photosynthesis by algae and plants, so name that process in your answer.", "recap": "Algae and plants used photosynthesis to raise oxygen to about 20% and lower carbon dioxide, which was also locked in rocks and fossil fuels."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.9.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 questions ask you to explain how greenhouse gases warm the Earth, the human causes and the effects, often using data.", "hook": {"q": "A clear, dry winter night is usually colder than a humid, cloudy one. What does this suggest about water vapour?", "o": ["Water vapour makes heat of its own during the night", "Water vapour absorbs infrared from the ground and sends some back down", "Water vapour forms a solid lid that stops all heat escaping", "Water vapour reflects sunlight onto the ground at night"], "a": 1, "f": ["Water vapour does not make heat. It only affects how much of the ground's heat escapes.", "Right. Water vapour is a greenhouse gas. It absorbs infrared from the ground and re-radiates some of it back down, so humid nights stay warmer.", "Not a lid. Nothing solid forms. The gas absorbs and re-radiates infrared, so some heat still escapes.", "There is no sunlight at night to reflect. The effect is on infrared given out by the ground."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "How the Earth stays warm", "b": "Sunlight passes through the atmosphere and warms the Earth's surface. The warm surface gives out **infrared radiation**. **Greenhouse gases** (carbon dioxide, methane, water vapour) **absorb** some of it. They then re-radiate it in all directions. Some goes back down, so the surface stays warmer than it otherwise would."}, {"h": "Human causes", "b": "Human activity adds greenhouse gases. Burning **fossil fuels** releases carbon dioxide. **Deforestation** removes trees that take in CO₂, and burning or rotting wood releases it. **Methane** comes from cattle farming, rice growing and landfill sites. More of these gases means more infrared is absorbed and returned. The graph shows CO₂ rising since 1960 (Mauna Loa, NOAA).", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [1960, 2020], "y": [300, 420], "xstep": 10, "ystep": 20, "xlabel": "Year", "ylabel": "CO₂ concentration / ppm", "lines": [{"pts": [[1960, 317], [1970, 326], [1980, 339], [1990, 354], [2000, 370], [2010, 390], [2020, 414]], "label": "CO₂", "c": 0}]}}, {"h": "Effects of climate change", "b": "A rise in the Earth's average temperature is **global warming**. Melting ice on land and expanding warmer water cause **sea level rise**, so coasts flood and erode. Weather may become more **extreme**, and rainfall patterns change. Wildlife may move or die out as habitats change. Climate models are complex, so predictions are uncertain."}, {"h": "Cutting carbon footprints", "b": "A **carbon footprint** is the total CO₂ and other greenhouse gases given out over the life of a product, service or event. You can cut it with **renewable energy**, saving energy, and **carbon capture and storage**. Barriers include cost, changes to lifestyle and the need for countries to agree."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how burning fossil fuels could lead to global warming.", "steps": [{"do": "Burning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.", "why": "This starts the chain by linking the human activity to a greenhouse gas."}, {"do": "Sunlight warms the Earth's surface, and the surface gives out infrared radiation.", "why": "Infrared is the energy that would otherwise escape to space."}, {"do": "Carbon dioxide absorbs some of this infrared and re-radiates it in all directions.", "why": "Absorbing and re-radiating is what makes it a greenhouse gas."}, {"do": "Some of the re-radiated infrared goes back down to the surface, so less escapes to space.", "why": "This shows why the surface ends up warmer."}, {"do": "More carbon dioxide means more infrared is returned, so the average temperature rises.", "why": "This links the extra gas to global warming."}], "answer": "Burning fossil fuels releases CO₂. Sunlight warms the Earth's surface, which emits infrared radiation. CO₂ absorbs some of it and re-radiates it in all directions. Some returns to the surface, so less escapes to space. More CO₂ means more is returned, so the Earth warms."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say 'some' of the infrared goes back down, not all of it?", "o": ["CO₂ reflects sunlight, and only part of the sunlight is reflected", "The ozone layer blocks the rest of the infrared from escaping", "The gas re-radiates in all directions, so only part is sent downwards", "CO₂ forms a lid with gaps in it, so some infrared leaks out"], "a": 2, "f": ["CO₂ does not reflect sunlight here. It absorbs infrared from the surface. Mixing up reflection and absorption is a common slip.", "The ozone layer is a separate topic. It absorbs ultraviolet and plays no part in this infrared effect.", "Right. Re-radiation goes in every direction. Only the part heading downwards warms the surface, and the rest goes off into space.", "Greenhouse gases do not form a lid of any kind. Gas molecules absorb and re-emit infrared. They do not make a barrier."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how landfill sites and cattle farming could increase global warming.", "given": ["Rotting waste in landfill and cattle digestion release methane into the atmosphere.", "Methane is a greenhouse gas, so it absorbs infrared radiation from the Earth's surface."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["It re-radiates the infrared in all directions, so some returns to the surface", "It reflects sunlight back down onto the surface, so the Earth warms", "It forms a solid lid that traps all the infrared below it", "It destroys the ozone layer, so more infrared reaches the Earth"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Re-radiation in all directions sends some infrared back to the surface. Less escapes to space, so the Earth warms.", "Methane acts on infrared from the Earth, not on sunlight. Saying 'reflects sunlight' is a common error.", "Gases do not make a solid lid. Some infrared still escapes. The mechanism is absorbing and re-radiating.", "Ozone depletion is a different issue. It involves ultraviolet radiation and does not explain global warming."]}, "trap": "Do not say greenhouse gases reflect sunlight, form a lid or make a hole in the ozone layer. Say they absorb infrared radiation from the Earth and re-radiate it, some back down.", "recap": "Greenhouse gases absorb infrared radiation from the Earth's surface and re-radiate it in all directions, some back down, so more of them means more warming."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.9.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to name pollutants from burning fuels, say how each forms and explain its effects, often for 2 to 4 marks.", "hook": {"q": "Homes with gas boilers should have a carbon monoxide alarm. Why can you not rely on your nose to spot the gas?", "o": ["Carbon monoxide smells, but only at very low levels that people ignore", "Carbon monoxide smells the same as the gas supply, so the two are confused", "Carbon monoxide is only made when the boiler is switched off", "Carbon monoxide has no colour and no smell, so people cannot detect it"], "a": 3, "f": ["Carbon monoxide has no smell at any level. It is not just faint, and that is the danger.", "Carbon monoxide has no smell at all, so it cannot be confused with the smell of the gas supply.", "It forms when fuel burns with too little oxygen, so it forms while the boiler is running, not when it is off.", "Right. Carbon monoxide is colourless and odourless, so only an alarm can warn you. Later you will see why it is poisonous."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Where pollutants come from", "b": "Burning fuel with plenty of oxygen is **complete combustion**. It makes carbon dioxide and water. With too little oxygen you get **incomplete combustion**. This makes **carbon monoxide** and soot (carbon particles). Some fuel may also escape unburnt."}, {"h": "Sulfur and nitrogen", "b": "Many fuels contain sulfur as an impurity. It burns to make **sulfur dioxide**. In hot engines, nitrogen and oxygen from the **air** react to make **oxides of nitrogen**. The nitrogen comes from the air, not from the fuel."}, {"h": "Effects of the pollutants", "b": "**Sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen** cause breathing problems. They also dissolve in rain water and cause **acid rain**. Soot particles cause **global dimming**, because they reduce the sunlight reaching Earth. They can also cause lung problems."}, {"h": "Why carbon monoxide is toxic", "b": "**Carbon monoxide** is colourless and odourless, so you cannot detect it. It binds to **haemoglobin** in red blood cells. Haemoglobin then cannot carry oxygen, so the blood carries less oxygen around the body."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how burning fuels can cause acid rain. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Some fuels contain sulfur impurities. When they burn, the sulfur reacts with oxygen to make sulfur dioxide.", "why": "This names the first pollutant and how it forms."}, {"do": "In hot engines, nitrogen and oxygen from the air react together to make oxides of nitrogen.", "why": "This is the second pollutant. Mention the air and the high temperature."}, {"do": "These gases are released into the air and dissolve in water droplets in clouds.", "why": "This links the gases to rain. Both form acidic solutions."}, {"do": "The rain that falls is acidic. It can damage buildings and harm plants and living things in lakes.", "why": "This finishes the chain with acid rain and a consequence."}], "answer": "Fuels with sulfur impurities make sulfur dioxide when burnt. Hot engines make oxides of nitrogen from nitrogen and oxygen in the air. These gases dissolve in rain water in clouds, making acid rain. It damages buildings and harms plants and aquatic life."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say oxides of nitrogen form in hot engines, using nitrogen from the air?", "o": ["Because petrol is mostly nitrogen, which burns when the engine is hot", "Because incomplete combustion always makes oxides of nitrogen", "Because nitrogen and oxygen in the air only react at very high temperatures", "Because sulfur impurities in the fuel turn into oxides of nitrogen"], "a": 2, "f": ["Fuels are mainly hydrocarbons. The nitrogen in these oxides comes from the air, not from the fuel.", "Incomplete combustion makes carbon monoxide and soot. Oxides of nitrogen need the high temperature of the engine.", "Right. Nitrogen is very unreactive. It only reacts with oxygen when the engine is hot enough.", "Sulfur impurities make sulfur dioxide, not oxides of nitrogen. Do not mix up the two sources."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a faulty gas heater in a closed room can be dangerous to the people inside. (3 marks)", "given": ["The heater has a poor air supply, so the gas burns by incomplete combustion.", "This makes carbon monoxide, which is colourless and odourless, so people cannot detect it."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to complete the explanation?", "o": ["Carbon monoxide dissolves in water in the lungs and makes acid rain.", "Carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin, so the blood carries less oxygen.", "Carbon monoxide uses up the oxygen in the lungs, so people cannot breathe in.", "Carbon monoxide forms soot, which causes global dimming in the room."], "a": 1, "f": ["Acid rain is caused by sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen. Carbon monoxide is dangerous for a different reason.", "Right. Carbon monoxide combines with haemoglobin in red blood cells, which stops them carrying oxygen.", "Carbon monoxide does not use up oxygen in the lungs or stop you breathing in. It stops haemoglobin carrying oxygen.", "Soot is a separate pollutant from incomplete combustion. Global dimming is not the danger to people in the room."]}, "trap": "Saying carbon monoxide stops you breathing in or uses up oxygen in your lungs. Always say it binds to haemoglobin, so the blood carries less oxygen.", "recap": "Match each pollutant to its source and effect: incomplete combustion gives carbon monoxide, which stops haemoglobin carrying oxygen."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.1.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "The written exam often asks you to explain how new technology affects industry, people, culture or the environment, and a good answer links cause to effect.", "hook": {"q": "A phone's battery is sealed in and fades after about three years. Who gains most from this?", "o": ["The environment, because fewer phones are made", "The owner, because the phone is cheaper to repair", "The maker, because owners are pushed to buy a new phone sooner", "Nobody, because sealed batteries always last longer"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. A short life means people replace phones sooner, so more are made and more are thrown away.", "Wrong. A sealed battery makes repair harder and often dearer, so it does not help the owner.", "Right. Limiting the product's life so people replace it sooner is planned obsolescence, and it brings the maker more sales.", "Wrong. A sealed battery does not make a phone last longer. Here it fades after three years, so the owner loses out."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Industry: automation and CAD/CAM", "b": "**Automation** uses machines or robots to do tasks with little human input. In **CAD/CAM**, a computer design (CAD) is sent to a machine such as a CNC router (CAM), which makes the part. Parts come out fast, accurate and identical, and machines can run for long hours. The drawbacks are high costs to buy and set up."}, {"h": "People and culture", "b": "Automation removes many repetitive manual jobs. It creates jobs in programming, maintenance and design, so workers need **retraining**. **Culture** means shared tastes and habits. Social media spreads **trends** fast, so people want new products sooner and often replace things that still work."}, {"h": "Environment and obsolescence", "b": "**Planned obsolescence** means the maker deliberately limits a product's life, for example with a sealed battery or a new model every year. People replace products sooner, so there is more **waste** and more energy and materials are used. Accurate CAM cutting can reduce scrap, so technology can help the environment too."}], "example": {"task": "A furniture company buys a CNC router and robot arms for its factory. Explain how this may affect the company, its workers and the environment. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "The CNC router cuts every part from the same CAD file, so parts are identical and accurate.", "why": "Names the technology and links it to a clear effect on quality."}, {"do": "The machines work quickly and without breaks, so the company makes more units in less time and the cost of each unit falls.", "why": "Links automation to industry benefits: output and cost."}, {"do": "Fewer workers are needed for repetitive tasks, but new jobs appear in programming and maintenance, so staff need retraining.", "why": "Shows the effect on people: jobs are lost and the work changes."}, {"do": "Accurate cutting from a file wastes less material, but making and running the machines uses energy.", "why": "Gives an environmental gain and an environmental cost."}], "answer": "The router cuts identical, accurate parts from a CAD file. Automation makes more units faster, lowering cost per unit. Fewer manual jobs are needed, but programming and maintenance jobs appear, so workers need retraining. Less material is wasted, but the machines use energy."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer mention new jobs as well as fewer jobs?", "o": ["Automation always creates more jobs than it removes", "The type of work changes, so a good answer shows both the loss and the gain", "Every step in an exam answer must have a positive and a negative", "New jobs are the only effect on workers that examiners accept"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Automation does not always create more jobs than it removes. The point is that the kind of work changes.", "Right. Technology removes some jobs and creates others, so a balanced point covers both and earns more credit.", "Wrong. No rule says every step needs both sides. This point mentions both because both really happen.", "Wrong. Job losses are also a valid effect. The answer includes both because the situation has both."]}, "faded": {"task": "A clothing brand brings out new designs every few weeks and promotes them on social media. Many customers throw last month's clothes away. Explain the effect on the environment.", "given": ["Culture: social media spreads trends quickly, so people feel they must buy new items to keep up.", "Industry: CAD and automated cutting let the brand make new designs quickly."], "q": "What is the best next point to complete the environmental effect?", "o": ["Robots cut the cloth, so fewer workers are needed and waste falls", "Customers save money, so the effect on the environment is positive", "Automated cutting uses no energy, so the effect on the environment is zero", "Clothes are binned early, so waste rises and replacements use more energy and materials"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Fewer workers is an effect on people. Any small saving in cutting scrap is outweighed by clothes being thrown away early.", "Wrong. Money saved is not an environmental effect. The rapid buying and throwing away is what harms the environment.", "Wrong. Machines use energy, and making many extra clothes uses materials as well. The effect is not zero.", "Right. Clothes thrown away early add to waste, and making replacements uses more energy and materials."]}, "trap": "Treating obsolescence as a product simply wearing out. It is the maker's deliberate choice. Ask: did the designer limit the product's life, or make it go out of date, on purpose?", "recap": "New technology changes industry, people, culture and the environment, and a good answer gives an effect and a reason for each."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.1.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to explain how power stations and energy storage work, give their pros and cons, and link them to real situations.", "hook": {"q": "A wind farm makes more electricity than the country needs at 3 am. What is the best use of the spare energy?", "o": ["Use it to create extra energy, so less fuel is needed later", "Nothing can be done, because electricity cannot be stored in any form", "Store it, for example by pumping water uphill, and use it later", "Burn it off as waste heat so the grid is not overloaded"], "a": 2, "f": ["Energy cannot be created. Storage only saves energy that was already generated, so you get back some of it later, not extra.", "Electricity itself is hard to store, but its energy can be stored in other forms, such as water at height or chemical energy in batteries.", "Right. Spare energy can be changed into a store, such as water raised to a high reservoir, and turned back into electricity when demand is high.", "Wasting the energy is the worst option. Storage lets you keep it for peak times instead of losing it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Fossil fuel power stations", "b": "Coal, oil or gas is burned to heat water into **steam**. The steam turns a **turbine**, which drives a generator. Advantages: reliable, and output can be raised quickly. Disadvantages: burning releases **CO₂**, which adds to global warming, and the fuels are non-renewable, so they will run out."}, {"h": "Nuclear power stations", "b": "In **nuclear fission**, uranium atoms split and release heat. The heat makes steam, which turns turbines and generators. A small amount of fuel gives a very large amount of energy, and little CO₂ is released while running. Disadvantages: dangerous **radioactive waste**, very high building costs, and uranium is non-renewable."}, {"h": "Renewable energy", "b": "**Renewable** sources are replaced naturally: wind, solar, hydroelectric, tidal and wave. They release no CO₂ while generating. Disadvantages: wind and solar depend on the weather, so output is **not constant**. Building them can be costly and can affect landscapes and wildlife."}, {"h": "Storing energy", "b": "Supply does not always match demand, so energy is stored. In **pumped storage**, spare electricity pumps water to a high reservoir. At peak demand the water flows down through turbines. A **battery** stores energy chemically. Rechargeable lithium-ion cells store a lot of energy for their mass."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a pumped storage system helps a country that gets much of its electricity from wind power.", "steps": [{"do": "Wind turbines only make electricity when the wind blows, so output can be high when demand is low and low when demand is high.", "why": "This states the problem. Storage is needed because supply and demand do not match."}, {"do": "When demand is low, the spare electricity powers pumps that lift water up to a high reservoir.", "why": "This is the charging stage. The spare energy is stored as the energy of water at height."}, {"do": "When demand is high, the water is released and flows down through turbines.", "why": "This is the discharging stage. The stored energy is turned back into movement."}, {"do": "The turbines drive generators, which send electricity to the grid when it is needed most.", "why": "This links the stored energy back to meeting demand."}, {"do": "So the wind energy is saved, not wasted. Less fossil fuel needs to be burned at peak times.", "why": "This gives the benefit, in terms of CO₂ and fuel use."}], "answer": "Wind output depends on the weather and may not match demand. At quiet times, spare electricity pumps water to a high reservoir. At peak demand, the water flows down through turbines and generators. This returns the stored energy to the grid, so less fossil fuel is needed."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the water is pumped up when demand is low?", "o": ["Spare electricity is available then, so energy that might be wasted can be stored", "Pumping the water creates extra energy, which can be used later", "Water can only be pumped uphill at night, when the pumps are allowed to run", "Wind turbines only generate electricity at times when demand is low"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. At quiet times there is more electricity than needed. Using it to pump water stores the energy for when demand is high.", "Energy cannot be created. Pumped storage only saves energy that was already generated, and some is lost in the process.", "Pumps can work at any time. The reason is that spare electricity is available when demand is low, not the time of day.", "Wind turbines work whenever the wind blows. Their output is not tied to low demand, which is why storage is needed."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a nuclear power station helps a country cut its CO₂ emissions.", "given": ["Uranium atoms split in nuclear fission and release heat.", "The heat boils water to make steam, which turns turbines connected to generators."], "q": "Which point should come next to finish the explanation?", "o": ["Uranium is a renewable fuel, so the power station never needs refuelling.", "The waste is not radioactive, so it can be thrown away with ordinary rubbish.", "Burning uranium releases heat but gives out no gases, so it makes no waste.", "No fossil fuel is burned, so very little CO₂ is released while it runs."], "a": 3, "f": ["Uranium is not renewable, and it does not answer the CO₂ question. The link is that no fossil fuel is burned.", "Nuclear waste is radioactive and dangerous. It must be stored carefully, so this is factually wrong.", "Uranium is not burned. Fission splits atoms. It also makes radioactive waste, so this is wrong.", "Right. Fission releases heat without burning fossil fuels, so little CO₂ is released while running. This links directly to the question."]}, "trap": "Reversing pumped storage. Water is pumped up when demand is low and released when demand is high. Storage saves energy that was already generated. It does not create extra energy.", "recap": "Fossil fuel and nuclear stations make steam to turn turbines; each source has pros and cons; storage saves spare energy for peak demand."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.1.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to match a smart, composite or technical material to a product and explain why it suits the job.", "hook": {"q": "Some glasses lenses darken when you step into bright sunshine and clear again indoors. What are the lenses reacting to?", "o": ["The temperature of your skin", "The amount of UV light", "The sound around you", "The pressure of the frame"], "a": 1, "f": ["Temperature changes would be a thermochromic effect. These lenses respond to light, not to warmth.", "Right. The lenses react to UV light in sunshine. That is a photochromic effect, a type of smart behaviour.", "Sound has no effect on lens materials like these. The trigger is something in the environment that the lens can sense.", "Pressure from the frame does not change the colour. The change comes from UV light reaching the lens."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Smart materials", "b": "A **smart material** reacts to a change in its surroundings, such as heat, light or electricity. The change is usually reversible. **Thermochromic** pigments change colour with temperature. **Photochromic** pigments change colour in UV light. Shape memory alloys such as Nitinol return to a set shape when heated. Piezoelectric materials produce electricity when squeezed."}, {"h": "Composite materials", "b": "A **composite** joins two or more materials to get properties that neither has alone. In **CFRP**, carbon fibres give strength and a polymer resin holds them together. It is strong, stiff and light, but costly. **GRP** uses glass fibres in resin. It is cheaper and common in boat hulls. Plywood and MDF are composites too."}, {"h": "Technical textiles", "b": "**Technical textiles** are chosen for performance, not looks. **Kevlar** is very strong and resists cuts. Nomex resists flames and heat. **Gore-Tex** is waterproof but breathable. Conductive fabrics carry electric current. Microencapsulated fabrics hold tiny capsules that burst to release a scent or other substance when rubbed."}, {"h": "Modern, not always smart", "b": "**Modern materials** are newly developed or newly practical to use. **Graphene** is a single layer of carbon atoms. It is very strong, very light and conducts electricity well. But it does not react to its surroundings, so it is not **smart**. Only call a material smart if it reacts to a change."}], "example": {"task": "A baby's feeding spoon contains thermochromic pigment that changes colour when food is too hot. Explain why this is a smart material and how it helps.", "steps": [{"do": "A smart material reacts to a change in its surroundings.", "why": "This gives the definition, which is the first mark."}, {"do": "Here the change is temperature. The pigment changes colour when the food is hot.", "why": "It links the definition to this product."}, {"do": "The colour change is reversible. When the food cools, the original colour returns.", "why": "Reversible change is typical of smart materials, and it lets the spoon be reused."}, {"do": "A carer can see the colour change and know the food is too hot to feed the baby.", "why": "It states the benefit to the user: it helps prevent burns."}], "answer": "It is smart because it reacts to a change in its surroundings. The pigment changes colour when the food gets hot, and changes back as it cools. A carer sees the colour and knows the food is too hot, which helps prevent burns."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the colour change is reversible?", "o": ["Because the pigment is a composite of two different materials", "Because changing back shows the pigment reacts to UV light", "Because the exam always expects the cost to be mentioned", "Because smart materials change back, so the spoon can be reused"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Reversibility is not about being a composite. A composite is two or more materials combined, which is a different idea.", "Wrong. This pigment reacts to heat, not light. Reacting to light would be photochromic, which is a different pigment.", "Wrong. Cost is not the point here. Reversibility is a feature of how smart materials behave and why this spoon is reusable.", "Right. Smart materials usually change back when the surroundings return to normal. That lets the spoon be used again and again."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why photochromic lenses in glasses are a smart material and how they help the wearer.", "given": ["Smart materials react to a change in their surroundings.", "Here the change is the amount of UV light reaching the lenses."], "q": "Which sentence should come next?", "o": ["The lenses darken when they get hot, so the wearer's eyes stay cool.", "The lenses are glass fibres in resin, so they are strong and light.", "The lenses darken in bright sunshine and clear indoors, so one pair works in both places.", "The lenses are one layer of carbon atoms, so they conduct electricity."], "a": 2, "f": ["This mixes up photochromic with thermochromic. The trigger was already given as UV light, not heat.", "This describes a composite such as GRP. It does not say how the lenses react to the change in light.", "Right. It says what the lenses do when the light changes and gives the benefit: no need to swap between glasses and sunglasses.", "This describes graphene, a modern material. It has nothing to do with how the lenses react to UV light."]}, "trap": "Calling any high-tech material smart. Carbon fibre, Kevlar and graphene are not smart, because they do not react to changes. Before writing 'smart', check what the material reacts to.", "recap": "Smart materials react to a change in their surroundings; composites combine materials for properties neither has alone; technical textiles are chosen for performance."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.1.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often give you a product and ask you to name its input, process or output, so splitting any product into three stages earns quick marks.", "hook": {"q": "An automatic shop door opens as you walk towards it. Which of these must happen first?", "o": ["A motor pulls the door open", "Something detects that you are there", "The door decides to close again", "The power supply is switched off"], "a": 1, "f": ["The motor moves the door, but only after the door knows you are there. It acts last, not first.", "Right. The door must sense you before anything else can happen. That sensing is the first stage of a system.", "Closing again comes after the door has opened. Nothing can be decided until you have been detected.", "The door needs power to work. Switching it off would stop the system, not start it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three stages", "b": "A **system** takes something in, deals with it and does something useful. Any electronic product can be split into three stages, written in order as **input → process → output**. Ask what each part does, not where it sits."}, {"h": "Inputs sense", "b": "An **input** detects something and sends a signal onwards. A **sensor** does this automatically. A PIR sensor detects movement. An LDR detects light level. A thermistor detects temperature. A **switch** is an input too, because you operate it."}, {"h": "Processes decide", "b": "The **process** stage decides what to do with the signal. In most modern products this is a **microcontroller**: a small chip you program. It compares the input with rules in its program, or counts time. Then it decides which output to switch on."}, {"h": "Outputs act", "b": "An **output** turns the signal into something you can see, hear or feel. A lamp or LED gives light. A **buzzer** gives sound. A motor gives movement. The power supply is none of the three stages. It just powers the system."}], "example": {"task": "A night light has an LDR, a microcontroller and an LED. It comes on when the room is dark. Explain how the system works, using input, process and output.", "steps": [{"do": "Name the three parts. The LDR is the input, the microcontroller is the process and the LED is the output.", "why": "This sets out the structure and shows you know each part's job."}, {"do": "The LDR senses the light level in the room and sends a signal to the microcontroller.", "why": "An input detects a change and passes it on as a signal."}, {"do": "The microcontroller compares this signal with the value in its program. When the room gets dark enough, it decides to switch the LED on.", "why": "The process is the decision. The microcontroller does the deciding."}, {"do": "The microcontroller sends a signal to the LED, which gives out light.", "why": "The output does the job the user wants: it lights the room."}], "answer": "The LDR is the input. It senses how light the room is and sends a signal. The microcontroller is the process. It compares the signal with its program and decides to switch the LED on when it is dark enough. The LED is the output. It lights up the dark room."}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 3, why is the microcontroller described as the process and not the output?", "o": ["It is the largest part and sits in the middle of the circuit", "It uses more power than the LDR or the LED", "It compares the signal with its program and decides, but does not light the room", "It is connected to the LED, and the part connected to the LED is the output"], "a": 2, "f": ["Size and position do not decide the stage. Always ask what the part does.", "Power use is not how you tell the stages apart. The stage depends on the part's job.", "Right. It makes the decision, which is the process. The LED is the part that actually lights the room.", "Controlling the LED does not make it the output. The output is the part that does the job, which is the LED."]}, "faded": {"task": "A greenhouse heater has a thermistor, a microcontroller and a heater. It switches on when the air is cold. Explain how the system works.", "given": ["Input: the thermistor senses the air temperature and sends a signal to the microcontroller."], "q": "Which statement correctly gives the next step, the process?", "o": ["The thermistor decides that the air is too cold and switches the heater on", "The heater is the process because it changes the temperature of the air", "The microcontroller is the output because it controls the heater", "The microcontroller compares the signal with its set value and decides to switch the heater on"], "a": 3, "f": ["A sensor only detects and sends a signal. It does not make decisions. The microcontroller decides.", "The heater warms the air, so it is the output. It acts on the decision and does not make it.", "This is the common slip. Controlling the heater does not make it an output. Deciding is the process.", "Right. Comparing the signal with a set value and deciding to switch the heater on is the process stage."]}, "trap": "Do not call the microcontroller the output because it controls the lamp or buzzer. It decides, so it is the process. The output is the part that does the job: light, sound or movement.", "recap": "Input senses, process decides and output acts, so name each part by its job: the microcontroller is the process."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.1.6.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "The written exam often asks you to place a material in the right category and give a reason, and later topics on properties build on this.", "hook": {"q": "A magnet sticks to a steel bookcase but not to an aluminium drinks can. Which element is in the steel but not in the aluminium?", "o": ["Copper", "Zinc", "Iron", "Tin"], "a": 2, "f": ["Copper is not attracted by a magnet. Copper pipes are non-ferrous, so it cannot explain why the magnet sticks to steel.", "Zinc is non-ferrous and is not magnetic. It is often used to coat steel, but it is not what the magnet is sticking to.", "Correct. Steel contains iron, and a magnet is attracted to iron. Metals with iron in them are called ferrous.", "Tin is non-ferrous and not magnetic. Some food cans are steel with a thin tin coating, but the magnet is attracted to the steel."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Five material families", "b": "Materials are sorted into five **families**: papers and boards, timbers, metals, polymers and textiles. Each family is then split into smaller **categories**. Paper and board come from wood fibres. Timber comes from trees. Metals come from ores. Most polymers come from crude oil. Textiles are made from fibres."}, {"h": "Metals: ferrous or not", "b": "**Ferrous** metals contain iron: mild steel, cast iron and high-carbon steel. **Non-ferrous** metals contain no iron: aluminium, copper, zinc and tin. An **alloy** is a metal mixed with other elements. Steel is a ferrous alloy. Brass is a non-ferrous alloy of copper and zinc."}, {"h": "Timbers", "b": "**Hardwoods** come from broad-leaved trees, such as oak and beech. **Softwoods** come from conifers, such as pine and spruce. The names describe the tree, not how hard the wood feels. **Manufactured boards**, such as plywood, MDF and chipboard, are made from layers, fibres or chips of wood. These are glued and pressed into sheets."}, {"h": "Polymers and textiles", "b": "**Thermoforming** polymers soften when heated and can be reshaped, such as acrylic. **Thermosetting** polymers set hard and cannot be reshaped, such as epoxy resin. Fabrics are natural (cotton, wool) or synthetic (polyester, nylon). A **blended** fabric has different fibres spun into one yarn. A mixed fabric weaves different yarns together."}], "example": {"task": "A table has an oak frame, a pine shelf and an MDF back panel. Explain how these three timbers are categorised.", "steps": [{"do": "Oak is a hardwood. It comes from a broad-leaved tree.", "why": "The category is decided by the type of tree. Naming the tree type earns the mark."}, {"do": "Pine is a softwood. It comes from a conifer, a tree with needles.", "why": "This uses the same rule as step 1, so the two categories are clearly contrasted."}, {"do": "MDF is a manufactured board. It is made from wood fibres, glued and pressed into sheets.", "why": "It is not sawn from one kind of tree. That is why it has its own category."}, {"do": "The names hardwood and softwood describe the tree, not how hard the wood feels.", "why": "This closes the answer by avoiding the common mistake of judging by hardness."}], "answer": "Oak is a hardwood because it comes from a broad-leaved tree. Pine is a softwood because it comes from a conifer. MDF is a manufactured board, made from wood fibres glued and pressed into sheets. The names describe the tree, not the hardness."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer call MDF a manufactured board and not a hardwood?", "o": ["MDF is too soft to count as a hardwood", "It is wood fibres glued into sheets, not cut from a tree", "MDF contains no real wood, only plastic", "MDF is cheaper than oak, so it is a lower category"], "a": 1, "f": ["Hardwood and softwood name the tree, not the softness. Softness is not how timber is categorised.", "Correct. MDF is made by gluing and pressing wood fibres into sheets. It is not sawn from one tree type, so it is a manufactured board.", "MDF is made from wood fibres. It does contain wood. It is a manufactured board because of how it is made.", "Price does not decide a material's category. The category depends on what it is made from and how."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how these metals are categorised: a cast iron pan, a copper pipe and a bronze statue. (Bronze is copper mixed with tin.)", "given": ["Cast iron is ferrous because it contains iron.", "Copper is non-ferrous because it contains no iron."], "q": "What is the next step, for bronze?", "o": ["Bronze is a non-ferrous alloy: copper mixed with tin, and it contains no iron.", "Bronze is a ferrous alloy because all alloys contain some iron.", "Bronze is a pure non-ferrous metal because copper is non-ferrous.", "Bronze is ferrous because it is hard and dark, like cast iron."], "a": 0, "f": ["Correct. Bronze is a mixture of metals, so it is an alloy. Neither copper nor tin is iron, so it is non-ferrous.", "Not all alloys contain iron. Brass and bronze are alloys with no iron. Check the ingredients, not the word alloy.", "Bronze is a mixture of copper and tin, so it is not pure. Mixing metals makes an alloy.", "Looks and hardness do not decide the category. Only iron content does, and bronze has none."]}, "trap": "Judging a material by how it looks or feels. Brass is an alloy but non-ferrous. Balsa is soft but a hardwood. Ask what the metal contains (iron?) or which tree the timber comes from.", "recap": "Sort a material by what it is made of: iron means ferrous, tree type means hardwood or softwood, and mixed metals are alloys."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.1.6.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often name a product and ask which property makes a material suitable. Marks go to the exact property word and a link to the job.", "hook": {"q": "A cycle helmet shell is hit hard in a crash. What does the shell material most need to do?", "o": ["Conduct heat away from the head quickly", "Be drawn out into thin wire", "Absorb the impact without breaking", "Take in water and swell slowly"], "a": 2, "f": ["Heat conduction does not protect the head in a crash. The shell's job is to deal with the impact.", "Being drawn into wire is ductility. A helmet is not made by drawing, and it must not stretch out.", "Right. This is toughness: absorbing impact energy without cracking or shattering. It protects the rider's head.", "Absorbing water would weaken the shell. Absorbency is a different property and not wanted here."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Shaping properties", "b": "A **malleable** material can be hammered, rolled or pressed into shape without cracking. A **ductile** material can be drawn out into wire. Copper and aluminium are both. Match the process to the word: pressing a panel needs malleability, drawing wire needs ductility."}, {"h": "Impact and wear", "b": "**Toughness** is absorbing impact without breaking. **Hardness** is resisting scratches, dents and wear. A **brittle** material, such as glass, shatters with little bending. Hard does not mean tough: a very hard material can still be brittle."}, {"h": "Heat and electricity", "b": "A material with high **thermal conductivity** lets heat pass through quickly. High **electrical conductivity** lets current flow easily. Metals are usually good at both. An **insulator** conducts poorly. Most plastics are insulators, so they are used to stop heat or current."}, {"h": "Other physical properties", "b": "**Density** is mass for a given volume. Low density means light for its size, which suits aircraft parts. **Fusibility** is how easily a material melts. **Absorbency** is how easily a material takes in liquid, as wood and cotton do."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a saucepan has a copper base and a plastic handle.", "steps": [{"do": "The base is made of copper, which has high thermal conductivity.", "why": "Names the exact property and the material it belongs to."}, {"do": "This lets heat pass quickly from the hob through the base into the food.", "why": "Links the property to the job the base has to do."}, {"do": "The handle is made of plastic, which is a thermal insulator because it has low thermal conductivity.", "why": "A different part needs the opposite property, so name it."}, {"do": "This stops heat travelling up the handle, so it stays cool enough to hold safely.", "why": "Links the property to the user's need."}], "answer": "Copper has high thermal conductivity, so heat passes quickly into the food. Plastic has low thermal conductivity, so it insulates. The handle stays cool and the user can hold it safely."}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 3, why does the answer choose a plastic with low thermal conductivity for the handle?", "o": ["So heat passes quickly into the cook's hand", "So the plastic can be drawn into wire", "So the handle melts easily when it is moulded", "So the handle stays cool enough to hold safely"], "a": 3, "f": ["This is the opposite of what is needed. Fast heat flow suits the base, not the handle.", "Drawing into wire is ductility. It has nothing to do with keeping a handle cool.", "That confuses fusibility with insulation. Easy melting is not the reason, and a handle that melted would be unsafe.", "Right. Low thermal conductivity means little heat travels along the handle, so it is safe to touch."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why the blade of a wood chisel is made from hardened steel.", "given": ["The blade edge has to cut into wood and keep cutting without wearing away."], "q": "What should the next point say?", "o": ["It needs ductility, so the steel can be drawn into thin wire.", "It needs hardness, so the edge resists wear and stays sharp.", "It needs to be malleable, so the edge can be easily pressed out of shape.", "It needs low density, so the blade is light to hold."], "a": 1, "f": ["Ductility is about being drawn into wire. A chisel is not stretched, so it does not explain a lasting edge.", "Right. Hardness is resisting wear, scratching and denting, so the cutting edge stays sharp.", "A malleable edge would bend or flatten under pressure. That is the opposite of what a cutting edge needs.", "Low density only makes the blade light. It does not explain why the edge stays sharp."]}, "trap": "Mixing up malleable (shaped by hammering or pressing), ductile (drawn into wire) and tough (survives impact). Match the word to the process or the job, and do not use 'hard' to mean 'tough'.", "recap": "Name the exact property, then link it to the job: malleable shapes, ductile draws into wire, tough absorbs impact, hard resists wear."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to justify a material, and you score by naming the right factor and linking it to the product.", "hook": {"q": "A garden chair stays outside all year. Which property matters most when choosing its material?", "o": ["A smooth, shiny surface", "Being very light to lift", "Resistance to rain and rot", "Being sold in many colours"], "a": 2, "f": ["Looks matter to buyers, but a shiny chair that rots in one winter fails its job.", "Low weight helps when moving the chair, but it is not the main need for something left outside.", "Right. The chair must survive rain all year, so resistance to water and rot is the key function.", "Colour choice is about looks. It does not decide whether the chair lasts outdoors."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Function, looks and cost", "b": "**Function** means the material's properties suit the job, such as strength, hardness or weather resistance. **Aesthetics** covers colour, texture and finish. **Cost** includes the price of the material and the cost of making each product, which falls when you make large quantities."}, {"h": "Availability", "b": "**Availability** asks whether you can get the material in the right form, size and amount, and on time. Stock sizes such as sheets, bars and planks are easier to buy. Rare or distant materials can cause **delays** and raise the price."}, {"h": "Environmental factors", "b": "**Environmental** factors look at the material's effect on the planet. Is it **renewable**, recycled or recyclable? How much energy does it take to make and transport? The **FSC** logo shows timber from a responsibly managed forest where trees are replanted."}, {"h": "Social, cultural, ethical", "b": "**Social** factors affect people and communities, such as safe, affordable products. **Cultural** factors cover beliefs, customs and the meaning of colours or symbols. **Ethical** factors are about fair treatment, such as Fairtrade pay and safe working conditions."}], "example": {"task": "A company sells a reusable water bottle. The body is made from recycled aluminium. Explain why the company chose this material.", "steps": [{"do": "Function: recycled aluminium is light and resists corrosion, so a bottle carried every day is easy to carry and does not rust.", "why": "It names a property and links it to how the bottle is used."}, {"do": "Looks: aluminium can be anodised or painted in many colours, so the bottle looks attractive to buyers.", "why": "Appearance affects sales, so aesthetics is a valid factor."}, {"do": "Environmental: making aluminium from recycled scrap needs far less energy than extracting it from ore, so carbon emissions fall.", "why": "It names the factor and gives a clear reason."}, {"do": "Environmental again: the bottle can be recycled at the end of its life, so the metal stays in use.", "why": "This links to the whole life of the product."}], "answer": "Recycled aluminium is light and resists corrosion, so the bottle is easy to carry and lasts. It can be coloured to look attractive. Recycling uses far less energy than making new metal, cutting emissions, and the bottle can be recycled again."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 1 include the words 'carried every day'?", "o": ["It adds length, and longer answers always earn more marks", "It links the property to how the product is used", "It shows that cost is the real reason for the choice", "It proves aluminium is the cheapest metal available"], "a": 1, "f": ["Length alone earns nothing. Marks go for relevant reasons, not extra words.", "Right. A property only earns marks when you tie it to the product's use. 'Light' matters because the bottle is carried.", "The step is about function, not cost. Mixing up factors loses marks.", "The step makes no claim about price. Aluminium is not the cheapest metal, so this would be wrong anyway."]}, "faded": {"task": "A firm sells tote bags made from Fairtrade cotton. Explain why the firm chooses this material.", "given": ["Function: cotton is soft, strong and washable, so the bag copes with heavy daily use."], "q": "Which point should come next to earn the ethical mark?", "o": ["Fairtrade cotton is the cheapest cotton, so cost is the main reason.", "Cotton comes from a plant, so this is only an environmental choice.", "Fairtrade cotton is easy to get, so availability is the key factor.", "Fairtrade means growers get fair pay and safe conditions, which is an ethical factor."], "a": 3, "f": ["Fairtrade cotton usually costs more, not less. The logo points to ethics, not low cost.", "Cotton being a plant does not explain the Fairtrade logo. It signals fair treatment of growers, which is ethical.", "Availability is not what the logo shows. Fairtrade is about how workers are treated.", "Right. It names the factor and gives a reason: fair pay and safe conditions for the people who grew the cotton."]}, "trap": "Naming a factor without a reason, or picking the wrong factor. FSC and Fairtrade logos point to environmental or ethical factors, not cost. Add 'because' and link the factor to the product.", "recap": "Name the factor, then link it to the product with a reason: function, looks, cost, availability, or environmental, social, cultural or ethical."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.2.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 1 can ask you to explain how making a product harms the environment or people, and how designers can cut that harm.", "hook": {"q": "A phone is made, shipped abroad, used for years, then thrown away. When can it harm the environment?", "o": ["Only in the factory, where it is made", "Only at the end, when it is thrown away", "At every stage, from making it to throwing it away", "Only while it is being shipped abroad"], "a": 2, "f": ["Factories do cause harm, but this is not the only stage. Shipping, use and disposal also use energy or create waste.", "Disposal does cause harm, but it is not the only stage. Making and moving the phone also use resources and energy.", "Right. Making uses materials and energy, shipping burns fuel, use needs power, and disposal creates waste. Harm can happen at every stage.", "Shipping burns fuel, but it is only one stage. Making the phone and throwing it away also harm the environment."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Two kinds of footprint", "b": "A product's **ecological footprint** is the harm it does to the environment. This covers resources used, energy used, pollution and waste. Its **social footprint** is its effect on people, such as pay, hours and safety of the workers who make it, and the communities nearby."}, {"h": "Refuse, reduce, reuse", "b": "**Refuse**: do not buy or use a harmful product or material, such as a single-use plastic bag. **Reduce**: use less material or energy, such as a thinner bottle. **Reuse**: use the same product again, such as a glass jar for storing food."}, {"h": "Repair, recycle, rethink", "b": "**Repair**: fix a broken product instead of throwing it away. **Recycle**: break a material down and make it into new items, such as melting aluminium cans. **Rethink**: ask whether a product is needed, and redesign it to do less harm."}, {"h": "Working conditions", "b": "Some products are made with **low pay**, long hours, unsafe machinery or **child labour**. These harm people, so they are part of the social footprint. The **Fairtrade** mark shows that producers get a fair price and better working conditions."}], "example": {"task": "A company makes plastic water bottles. Explain how it could use the six Rs to reduce the ecological footprint of the bottle. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Reduce: remove the extra plastic label sleeve and use a smaller cap, so less plastic is used.", "why": "Names an R and applies it to this product. Less plastic means less oil used and less waste."}, {"do": "Reuse: make the bottle strong enough to refill many times, so fewer new bottles need making.", "why": "A second R, applied to the bottle. It gives a reason: fewer bottles made."}, {"do": "Recycle: make it from one clearly marked plastic, so it can be recycled into new items at the end of its life.", "why": "Links a design choice to recycling. Less plastic goes to landfill."}, {"do": "Together, these cut the oil, energy and waste involved, so the ecological footprint is smaller.", "why": "Links the points back to the question by saying how the footprint falls."}], "answer": "The company could reduce plastic by removing the label sleeve and using a smaller cap. It could make the bottle refillable so fewer are made. It could use one clearly marked plastic so it can be recycled. Together these use less oil and energy and cut waste, so the ecological footprint is smaller."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer end with the sentence about oil, energy and waste?", "o": ["It adds marks for length, because longer answers always score more", "It links the Rs back to the question by saying how the footprint falls", "It changes the focus to the social footprint of the factory workers", "It shows the bottle will cost less to make, which the question asks about"], "a": 1, "f": ["Marks are for relevant points, not length. A longer answer with no link to the question earns nothing extra.", "Right. The question asks about reducing the ecological footprint. This sentence shows the effect of the Rs on the environment, which earns the final mark.", "Oil, energy and waste are ecological harms. Workers' pay and conditions are the social footprint, which is a different thing.", "The question asks about ecological footprint, not cost. Cost may change, but this sentence is about environmental harm."]}, "faded": {"task": "A phone company wants to cut its ecological footprint. Explain how it could use the six Rs. (4 marks)", "given": ["Repair: design the phone so a cracked screen or worn battery can be replaced.", "This keeps the phone in use for longer, so fewer new phones are made."], "q": "Which point should come next to add a different R with a reason?", "o": ["Reduce: pay factory workers more so they can afford a better life.", "Repair: replace broken parts so the phone lasts longer.", "Recycle: phones are recycled, which is good for the environment.", "Recycle: take back old phones and recover metals, so less new metal is mined."], "a": 3, "f": ["Better pay is a social footprint point, not an ecological one. It is also not what reduce means, which is using less material or energy.", "This repeats Repair, which is already given. The question asks for a different R.", "This names an R but gives no reason. 'Good for the environment' is too vague to earn a mark.", "Right. It names a new R, says what happens, and gives a reason linked to the environment: less mining."]}, "trap": "Mixing up refuse with reduce or recycle. Refuse means not buying or using it at all. Reduce means using less. Recycle means making the material into something new. Keep ecological (environment) and social (people) apart too.", "recap": "Ecological footprint is harm to the environment, social footprint is effects on people, and the six Rs (refuse, reduce, reuse, repair, recycle, rethink) cut harm."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.2.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask where materials come from and how their environmental impact is judged, with LCA and recycling appearing in both short and longer comparison answers.", "hook": {"q": "A plastic bottle is made, filled, delivered to shops, used, then thrown away. At which stages does it affect the environment?", "o": ["Only when it is being made in the factory", "Only when it is thrown away at the end", "At every stage, from raw materials to disposal", "Only while it is being driven to the shops"], "a": 2, "f": ["Making the bottle does have an impact, but so do the other stages. Looking at one stage alone misses the rest.", "Disposal matters, but the bottle also uses resources and energy before that. Focusing only on the end is a common slip.", "Right. The bottle has an impact from getting the raw materials to throwing it away. An LCA looks at all of these stages.", "Transport adds emissions, but it is only one stage. Raw materials, making, use and disposal count too."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Natural and finite sources", "b": "Products start as **raw materials**. Timber comes from trees and cotton from plants. These are **renewable** if they are replaced by planting. Metals come from ores dug out of the ground. Most polymers and synthetic fibres, such as polyester, come from crude oil. Ores and oil are **finite**: once used, they cannot be replaced."}, {"h": "Turning it into stock", "b": "Raw materials are processed into **stock forms** such as sheet, bar or board. Logs are sawn into planks, then **seasoned** to dry them. Metal is extracted from its ore, for example iron is smelted in a furnace. Crude oil is refined and the chemicals are made into polymers, often sold as granules."}, {"h": "Life cycle assessment", "b": "An **LCA** measures a product's environmental impact at every stage: raw materials, manufacture, transport, use and disposal. At each stage it records things like energy used, emissions and waste. Designers use it to compare products and to find the **stage with the biggest impact**."}, {"h": "Recycling at end of life", "b": "At the end of its life a product can be **reused**, **recycled** or sent to landfill. Recycling saves raw materials and usually energy. Metals can be melted and cast again. **Thermoforming polymers** can be reheated and reshaped, but thermosetting polymers cannot be remelted. Mixed materials are hard to separate."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how an LCA could be used to compare a steel water bottle with a polymer water bottle. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "State that an LCA looks at every stage of each bottle's life: raw materials, manufacture, transport, use and disposal.", "why": "This shows you know an LCA covers the whole life, not one stage."}, {"do": "Compare raw materials: steel comes from mined iron ore, the polymer from crude oil. Both are finite and need energy to extract.", "why": "The first stage compares where each material comes from."}, {"do": "Record the energy used and emissions made in manufacturing and transporting each bottle.", "why": "These are the measurements an LCA collects at the middle stages."}, {"do": "Compare use: a steel bottle may be refilled for years, while a polymer bottle may be thrown away sooner.", "why": "Longer use means fewer replacement bottles, which lowers the total impact."}, {"do": "Compare disposal: steel can be melted and recycled, so check what happens to the polymer bottle. Then add up the stages to see which has less impact.", "why": "End of life is the last stage, and the totals allow a fair comparison."}], "answer": "An LCA checks every stage: raw materials, manufacture, transport, use and disposal. For each bottle you record the energy, emissions and waste at each stage, including how long it is used and whether it is recycled. Comparing the totals shows which bottle has the lower environmental impact."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked answer compare how long each bottle is used?", "o": ["Because an LCA only looks at the use stage of a product", "Because longer use means fewer new bottles to make and dispose of", "Because reusing a bottle makes its raw materials renewable", "Because the use stage always has the biggest impact"], "a": 1, "f": ["An LCA covers all five stages, not only use. This is the trap of picking one stage.", "Right. A bottle used for years means fewer new bottles to make, transport and dispose of, so the total impact falls.", "Reusing a product does not make its raw materials renewable. Steel and oil stay finite.", "The biggest impact is not always in the use stage. For many products it is in raw materials or manufacture."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how an LCA could be used to compare a cotton carrier bag with a polythene carrier bag. (4 marks)", "given": ["An LCA looks at each stage of the life of both bags.", "Raw materials: cotton is grown on farms, while polythene is made from crude oil."], "q": "What is the best next step in the answer?", "o": ["Count only the waste left after the bags are thrown away", "Say cotton is natural, so its bag has no environmental impact", "Choose the cheaper bag, since the LCA result is the cost", "Compare the energy and emissions from making and moving each bag"], "a": 3, "f": ["The LCA covers every stage, not just disposal. Counting only the end of life is the commonest slip.", "Natural materials still need land, water and energy, and the bag must be made and moved. Natural does not mean no impact.", "An LCA measures environmental impact, not price. Cost may matter to a designer, but it is not the LCA result.", "Right. Manufacture and transport are the next stages in order. An LCA records energy and emissions for each."]}, "trap": "Thinking an LCA covers only making the product or only throwing it away. It covers the whole life, so name every stage: raw materials, manufacture, transport, use and disposal.", "recap": "An LCA judges a product's environmental impact across its whole life, from raw materials to disposal, so different products can be compared fairly."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.2.5", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to explain why a material is treated before or during making, such as why timber is seasoned or why metal is annealed.", "hook": {"q": "You bend a paper clip back and forth in the same spot, again and again. What happens to the metal there?", "o": ["It gets softer and bends more easily each time", "It gets harder and more brittle, then snaps", "It stays exactly the same until it is heated", "It gets longer and thinner until it is stretched out"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Cold bending does the opposite. It makes the metal harder, which is why the clip gets stiffer in that spot.", "Right. Repeated cold bending makes metal harder and more brittle. This is work hardening, and eventually the metal cracks or snaps.", "Wrong. Metal changes when it is worked cold. Its structure is distorted, so it becomes harder even without any heating.", "Wrong. This confuses bending with stretching. The clip keeps its length and snaps at the bend because it has become brittle."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What seasoning does", "b": "Timber from a freshly felled tree holds a lot of water. **Seasoning** dries it to a suitable **moisture content** before use. In air seasoning, planks are stacked outdoors under cover, with gaps for air. This takes months or years. **Kiln seasoning** uses a heated chamber. It takes days or weeks and the moisture level can be controlled."}, {"h": "Why unseasoned timber fails", "b": "As wet timber dries, it **shrinks**. The outside dries before the middle, so it shrinks unevenly. This builds up stress. The wood can **warp**, twist or **split**. Furniture would change shape, doors would jam and joints would open. Seasoned timber has already lost most of this water, so it stays far more stable."}, {"h": "Work hardening and annealing", "b": "Bending, hammering or rolling metal when cold causes **work hardening**. The metal becomes harder and more brittle, and it can crack. **Annealing** reverses this. You heat the metal (copper to red heat), then let it cool. It becomes soft and **ductile** again, so you can keep shaping it."}, {"h": "Link process to problem", "b": "Each process fixes a specific problem. Timber holds water and moves as it dries. Cold-worked metal loses its ductility. In an answer, name the problem, name the process, then say what it changes. Neither is a **coating** or **surface finish**. Seasoning removes water. Annealing uses heat to change the metal's inner structure."}], "example": {"task": "A joiner makes a kitchen door from oak that was not seasoned. Explain what is likely to happen to the door over the next few months, and why.", "steps": [{"do": "Unseasoned oak still holds a lot of water from the living tree.", "why": "This is the starting cause. The moisture content is too high for a finished product."}, {"do": "In a warm, dry kitchen the oak slowly loses this water and shrinks.", "why": "Timber shrinks as it dries, so its size changes after the door is made."}, {"do": "The outside dries faster than the middle, so the shrinking is uneven and stress builds up.", "why": "Uneven shrinking is what pulls the wood out of shape."}, {"do": "The door may warp, twist or split, so it no longer fits its frame and the joints may open.", "why": "This links the cause to the effect on the finished product."}, {"do": "Seasoning the oak first, to a suitable moisture content, would avoid this.", "why": "It names the process that solves the problem."}], "answer": "Unseasoned oak holds a lot of water. In a warm kitchen it dries and shrinks. The outside dries faster than the middle, so it shrinks unevenly and builds up stress. The door may warp, twist or split and stop fitting. Seasoning first avoids this."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the outside dries faster than the middle?", "o": ["Oak is a hardwood, and hardwoods always dry from the middle outwards", "The middle of the door soaks up the water that the outside loses", "Uneven drying causes uneven shrinking, which builds stress that bends or splits the wood", "The outside has a coating, which makes the timber lose water faster"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Hardwoods do not dry from the middle outwards. The outer wood dries first, and the type of timber is not the point of this step.", "Wrong. The middle does not take water from the outside. Both parts lose water, but the outside loses it sooner.", "Right. The outside dries and shrinks before the middle. This uneven shrinking creates stress, which is what causes warping, twisting and splitting.", "Wrong. A coating would slow moisture loss, not speed it up. Seasoning is also a drying process, not a coating."]}, "faded": {"task": "A plumber bends a copper pipe several times while fitting it. The bend becomes stiff and starts to crack. Explain how annealing helps.", "given": ["Bending cold copper causes work hardening.", "The copper becomes harder and more brittle, so it starts to crack."], "q": "What is the next point in the answer?", "o": ["Annealing: heat to red heat, then cool, so the copper is soft and ductile again.", "Seasoning: leave it to dry for weeks so the extra moisture leaves the copper.", "Add a lacquer coat so the surface is protected while it is being bent.", "Bend it faster, so the heat from the work makes the copper soft again."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Heating copper to red heat and letting it cool removes the hardness. It becomes soft and ductile, so the plumber can carry on bending it.", "Wrong. Seasoning is for timber. Copper does not hold water in its structure, and drying it would not make it softer.", "Wrong. A coating only changes the surface. The hardness is inside the metal, so a lacquer would not stop the cracking.", "Wrong. Cold bending is what causes the work hardening. Bending more would make the copper harder and more likely to crack."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the two processes, or calling seasoning a finish. Seasoning dries timber. Annealing heats metal to soften it. Neither adds a layer. Ask yourself: is water being removed, or is metal being softened?", "recap": "Seasoning dries timber so it stays stable, and annealing heats and cools work-hardened metal so it is soft and ductile again."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.2.6", "kind": "method", "why": "Written-paper questions often ask how many parts you can cut from a stock sheet or length, or how much is wasted, and your NEA needs this planning too.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Stock forms and sizes", "b": "**Stock forms** are the shapes materials are sold in, such as sheet, board, bar, tube and angle. Each comes in **standard sizes**, so you cannot ask for any size you like. Sheet materials such as MDF and acrylic come as large rectangles. Metals and timber also come as lengths. You plan your parts around these sizes."}, {"h": "Whole pieces from a sheet", "b": "Take one side of the sheet and the piece side that lies along it. Divide, then **round down**, because part of a piece is no use. Do the same for the other side. **Multiply** the two whole numbers. For a length: $2000 \\div 450 = 4.4$, so 4 pieces and a 200 mm **offcut**."}, {"h": "Working out waste", "b": "**Waste** = sheet area − area of pieces used. **Percentage waste** = $\\text{waste} \\div \\text{sheet area} \\times 100$. To cut waste, plan the layout first. Turn pieces through 90° only if grain or pattern allows it. Keep offcuts for small parts. Ignore saw cuts unless the question includes them."}], "example": {"task": "A 1000 mm × 600 mm acrylic sheet is cut into 300 mm × 250 mm pieces, all the same way round. Ignore saw cuts. How many pieces fit, and what percentage is waste?", "steps": [{"do": "Divide the 1000 mm side by the 300 mm piece side: $1000 \\div 300 = 3.33$. Round down to 3.", "why": "Only whole pieces count. A 4th piece would run off the end of the sheet."}, {"do": "Divide the 600 mm side by the 250 mm piece side: $600 \\div 250 = 2.4$. Round down to 2.", "why": "Same rule on the other side. The leftover 100 mm strip is too narrow for a piece."}, {"do": "Multiply the two whole numbers: $3 \\times 2 = 6$ pieces.", "why": "3 pieces in each row and 2 rows gives the total."}, {"do": "Sheet area: $1000 \\times 600 = 600\\,000$ mm². Pieces used: $6 \\times (300 \\times 250) = 6 \\times 75\\,000 = 450\\,000$ mm².", "why": "You need both areas to find the waste."}, {"do": "Waste: $600\\,000 - 450\\,000 = 150\\,000$ mm². Then $150\\,000 \\div 600\\,000 \\times 100 = 25\\%$.", "why": "Percentage waste is always compared with the whole sheet, not with the pieces."}], "answer": "6 pieces fit, and 25% of the sheet is wasted."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is 3.33 rounded down to 3 in step 1?", "o": ["Rounding always goes down in cutting questions, so the rule never needs checking", "Only whole pieces can be cut, and a 4th piece would not fit on the sheet", "It leaves a gap for the saw cut between each piece", "Because 0.33 is less than 0.5, so it rounds down as it normally would"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Down is the right direction, but this gives no reason. You round down because a part piece cannot be used, and you should check that it fits.", "Right. Three pieces use 900 mm of the 1000 mm. The extra 100 mm cannot hold a 300 mm piece, so the 0.33 is discarded.", "Wrong. The question says to ignore saw cuts. The 3 comes from whole pieces fitting, not from allowing for the blade.", "Wrong. This is normal rounding. It would round 3.6 up to 4, but 3.6 pieces still means only 3 fit. Always round down."], "a_note": null}, "faded": {"task": "A 1200 mm × 900 mm sheet is cut into 350 mm × 200 mm pieces, none turned. Find the greatest number of whole pieces.", "given": ["Along 1200 mm: $1200 \\div 350 = 3.43$, round down to 3."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Divide 900 by 350 to get 2.57, so 2 along the width", "Divide 900 by 200 to get 4.5, and round up to 5", "Divide 900 by 200 to get 4.5, and round down to 4", "Divide the areas: $1\\,080\\,000 \\div 70\\,000 = 15.43$, so 15"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. The 350 mm side is already lying along the 1200 mm side. The 900 mm side must be divided by the 200 mm side.", "Wrong. Rounding up gives a 5th row that would not fit. Half a piece is no use, so always round down.", "Right. The 200 mm side lies along the 900 mm side. $900 \\div 200 = 4.5$, so 4 rows fit. Then $3 \\times 4 = 12$ pieces.", "Wrong. Dividing areas ignores the offcut strips, which cannot make whole pieces. It gives 15, but only 12 fit."]}, "trap": "Dividing the sheet area by the piece area and rounding gives too many pieces. The leftover strips cannot make whole pieces. Divide side by side, round each down, then multiply.", "recap": "Divide each sheet side by the piece side along it, round each down, then multiply; waste is sheet area minus area used."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.2.7", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to name the scale of production for a product and explain why it suits, usually in terms of numbers made and cost per item.", "hook": {"q": "A potter makes one bowl by hand. A factory makes 10,000 identical bowls. Which is likely cheaper per bowl, and why?", "o": ["The potter's, because one person working alone needs no costly machines", "The same, because every bowl uses about the same amount of clay", "The factory's, because the cost of machines and moulds is shared by 10,000 bowls", "The potter's, because making by hand is always quicker than using machines"], "a": 2, "f": ["Not usually. One-off work takes a lot of skilled time, and nothing is shared. Each bowl carries the whole cost of the potter's time.", "Materials cost about the same per bowl, but they are not the whole cost. Machine and mould costs matter more.", "Right. Set-up costs are fixed, so spreading them over 10,000 bowls makes each bowl cheaper.", "Hand work is usually slower than automated machines. This is why one-off products cost more per item."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "One-off and prototype", "b": "**One-off production** makes a single product, often to a customer's order, such as a bespoke wedding dress. A **prototype** is a first model, made to test a design before more are made. Both are usually made by skilled workers using flexible tools. Cost per item is high, but the design is easy to change."}, {"h": "Batch production", "b": "**Batch production** makes a set number of identical products, then the machines are reset for a different product. Bread, limited-edition clothing and posters are made this way. It is flexible, and output can match demand. The **set-up** time between batches costs money."}, {"h": "Mass and continuous", "b": "**Mass production** makes very large numbers of identical products on a dedicated line, often with automation, such as cars. **Continuous production** runs non-stop, 24 hours a day, to make huge amounts of one product, such as sheet steel or chemicals. Both need costly set-up and are hard to change."}, {"h": "Cost per item", "b": "Tooling, jigs and machine set-up cost the same whatever number is made. Larger runs share this **set-up cost** between more products, so **cost per item** falls from one-off to batch to mass and continuous. But higher volume is harder to change. Drag the slider on the graph (example costs): batch becomes cheapest at P (20), mass at Q (60).", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [0, 80], "y": [0, 1600], "xstep": 10, "ystep": 200, "xlabel": "number of products made", "ylabel": "total cost / £", "lines": [{"label": "One-off", "c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [80, 1600]]}, {"label": "Batch", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 200], [80, 1000]]}, {"label": "Mass", "c": 2, "pts": [[0, 500], [80, 900]]}], "points": [{"at": [20, 400], "label": "P"}, {"at": [60, 800], "label": "Q"}], "frames": [{"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[5.0, 0.0], [5.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [5.0, 100.0]}, {"at": [5.0, 250.0]}, {"at": [5.0, 525.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £50 per item", "Mass: £105 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[10.0, 0.0], [10.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [10.0, 200.0]}, {"at": [10.0, 300.0]}, {"at": [10.0, 550.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £30 per item", "Mass: £55 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[15.0, 0.0], [15.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [15.0, 300.0]}, {"at": [15.0, 350.0]}, {"at": [15.0, 575.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £23.33 per item", "Mass: £38.33 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[20.0, 0.0], [20.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [20.0, 400.0]}, {"at": [20.0, 400.0]}, {"at": [20.0, 600.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £20 per item", "Mass: £30 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[25.0, 0.0], [25.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [25.0, 500.0]}, {"at": [25.0, 450.0]}, {"at": [25.0, 625.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £18 per item", "Mass: £25 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[30.0, 0.0], [30.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [30.0, 600.0]}, {"at": [30.0, 500.0]}, {"at": [30.0, 650.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £16.67 per item", "Mass: £21.67 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[35.0, 0.0], [35.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [35.0, 700.0]}, {"at": [35.0, 550.0]}, {"at": [35.0, 675.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £15.71 per item", "Mass: £19.29 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[40.0, 0.0], [40.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [40.0, 800.0]}, {"at": [40.0, 600.0]}, {"at": [40.0, 700.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £15 per item", "Mass: £17.5 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[45.0, 0.0], [45.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [45.0, 900.0]}, {"at": [45.0, 650.0]}, {"at": [45.0, 725.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £14.44 per item", "Mass: £16.11 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[50.0, 0.0], [50.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [50.0, 1000.0]}, {"at": [50.0, 700.0]}, {"at": [50.0, 750.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £14 per item", "Mass: £15 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[55.0, 0.0], [55.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [55.0, 1100.0]}, {"at": [55.0, 750.0]}, {"at": [55.0, 775.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £13.64 per item", "Mass: £14.09 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[60.0, 0.0], [60.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [60.0, 1200.0]}, {"at": [60.0, 800.0]}, {"at": [60.0, 800.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £13.33 per item", "Mass: £13.33 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[65.0, 0.0], [65.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [65.0, 1300.0]}, {"at": [65.0, 850.0]}, {"at": [65.0, 825.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £13.08 per item", "Mass: £12.69 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[70.0, 0.0], [70.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [70.0, 1400.0]}, {"at": [70.0, 900.0]}, {"at": [70.0, 850.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £12.86 per item", "Mass: £12.14 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[75.0, 0.0], [75.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [75.0, 1500.0]}, {"at": [75.0, 950.0]}, {"at": [75.0, 875.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £12.67 per item", "Mass: £11.67 per item"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "pts": [[80.0, 0.0], [80.0, 1600.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [80.0, 1600.0]}, {"at": [80.0, 1000.0]}, {"at": [80.0, 900.0]}], "read": ["One-off: £20 per item", "Batch: £12.5 per item", "Mass: £11.25 per item"]}], "ctl": {"type": "slider", "label": "n", "vals": ["5", "10", "15", "20", "25", "30", "35", "40", "45", "50", "55", "60", "65", "70", "75", "80"], "start": 0}}}], "example": {"task": "A company will make 100,000 identical plastic toy cars. Explain why mass production suits this product. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Mass production suits this because the toy cars are all identical and a very large number, 100,000, is needed.", "why": "Name the scale and link it to the number and sameness of the product."}, {"do": "A dedicated assembly line can make only toy cars, so the machines never need resetting for a different product.", "why": "This separates mass from batch production: the machines are not changed."}, {"do": "A costly injection mould and automated machines are worth buying, because their cost is shared across 100,000 cars, so each car costs very little.", "why": "Links set-up cost to the number made. This is the main reason for a low cost per item."}, {"do": "Automation needs fewer workers per car and gives fast, consistent output, so demand is met and every car is the same quality.", "why": "A second advantage, linked to the high demand and identical products."}], "answer": "Mass production suits 100,000 identical toy cars. A dedicated line makes only one product, so machines are never reset. The costly mould and automated machines are shared across all the cars, so each costs little. Automation also gives fast, consistent output to meet demand."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 say a costly injection mould is worth buying for this product?", "o": ["The mould gets cheaper every time it is used, so the price drops", "A mould means the company can pay its workers less for each car", "The mould always uses less plastic per car than any other method", "Its fixed cost is split across 100,000 cars, so each car carries very little"], "a": 3, "f": ["A mould costs the same however often it is used. Only the share of its cost per car falls, as more cars are made.", "Mould cost has nothing to do with pay rates. Labour per car may fall through automation, but that is a different point.", "Material use is not the reason. The saving comes from sharing a fixed cost, not from using less plastic.", "Right. The mould is a fixed cost. Over 100,000 cars the share per car is tiny, so cost per item is low."]}, "faded": {"task": "A clothing firm makes 300 red hoodies, then resets its machines to make 300 blue ones. Explain why batch production suits this. (3 marks)", "given": ["The firm makes a set number (300) of identical hoodies at a time, which is what batch production means.", "The cutting and sewing machines can be reset for the next colour or style."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to complete the explanation?", "o": ["Output can be matched to orders for each colour, so the firm avoids a costly line for one design.", "The hoodies are made non-stop, 24 hours a day, so each one costs the least possible.", "Each hoodie is made to one customer's measurements, so the cost per hoodie is low.", "A dedicated line making only one design is justified, as it is never reset."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Batch production is flexible and can match demand for each product, without the cost of a dedicated line.", "This describes continuous production. A batch stops while machines are reset, so it is not non-stop.", "This describes one-off production. Batch hoodies are identical within the batch, and cost per item is not low for one-offs.", "This describes mass production. The hoodie firm does reset its machines, so a dedicated line does not fit."]}, "trap": "Choosing mass production just because the number is big. A batch can be hundreds or thousands. Look for the clue: machines reset for a different product means batch; a dedicated line for one product means mass.", "recap": "A single item is one-off or prototype; machines reset between products is batch; very large numbers of one product is mass or continuous."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.2.8", "kind": "idea", "why": "Short-answer questions ask you to explain how jigs, templates, tolerances and quality checks help a factory make many identical parts accurately.", "hook": {"q": "A factory must drill the same four holes in 500 wooden panels. Which method is most likely to put the holes in the same places every time?", "o": ["Measure and mark each panel with a ruler, then drill by hand", "Draw round a card shape on each panel and drill where you think best", "Clamp each panel in a fixture that guides the drill to the hole positions", "Mark the first panel carefully, then copy its holes by eye on the rest"], "a": 2, "f": ["Measuring each panel separately lets small errors creep in, so the holes will not match exactly.", "A card shape only helps with marking. It does not hold the panel or guide the drill, so the holes can still vary.", "Right. A fixture that holds the panel and guides the drill is a jig. It fixes the hole positions for every panel.", "Copying by eye drifts further from the first panel each time. The holes will not be identical."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Templates and jigs", "b": "A **template** is a shape you draw round or mark out from. It saves measuring each time. A **jig** is different. It holds the workpiece, or guides the tool, so every part is made the same way. A drilling jig fixes the hole positions."}, {"h": "Tools and machines", "b": "Hand tools suit one-off work. In industry, **machines** such as a pillar drill or lathe cut faster and more accurately. A **CNC machine** follows a program of instructions, so each part it makes is identical. Machines often work together with jigs."}, {"h": "Working to tolerances", "b": "A **tolerance** is the amount a size may vary while the part still works. 50 mm ±0.2 mm means anything from 49.8 mm to 50.2 mm is accepted. A smaller tolerance needs more **accuracy** and usually costs more. Parts outside it are rejected."}, {"h": "Checking quality", "b": "**Quality control** means checking parts against the standard, for example by measuring a sample with a gauge. Faulty parts are removed. **Quality assurance** is the planned system of procedures that aims to stop faults happening in the first place."}], "example": {"task": "A company makes 1000 identical wooden shelf brackets. Explain how a drilling jig helps the company make them.", "steps": [{"do": "The jig holds each bracket firmly in exactly the same position.", "why": "The work cannot move, so every bracket sits in the same place relative to the drill."}, {"do": "Guide holes in the jig set where the drill bit goes, so the hole positions are the same on every bracket.", "why": "The positions are built into the jig. This is what makes the parts identical."}, {"do": "The operator does not need to measure or mark out each bracket, so each one is made faster.", "why": "Saving time per part lowers production cost across 1000 parts."}, {"do": "Holes stay within the tolerance, so fewer brackets are rejected and the screws line up when fitted.", "why": "This links accuracy to a result for the company: less waste and parts that fit."}], "answer": "The jig holds each bracket in the same position and guides the drill to the same hole positions every time. No marking out is needed, so it is quicker. The holes stay within tolerance, so fewer parts are rejected."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say no marking out is needed for each bracket?", "o": ["The hole positions are built into the jig, so it sets them without new measuring", "The jig draws the outline of the bracket on the wood, so the operator can skip measuring", "The drill finds the correct position by itself, so the jig is only there to hold the wood", "Jig-made parts have no tolerance, so the hole positions do not need to be checked"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. The jig fixes the positions, so each bracket needs no separate measuring or marking out.", "This describes a template, which is drawn round. A jig holds the work and guides the tool.", "A drill does not find positions by itself. The guide holes in the jig steer the bit.", "Jig-made parts still have a tolerance. Quality checks still happen, because even jigs wear."]}, "faded": {"task": "A factory makes 5000 metal pins that must fit into holes in another part. Explain why a tolerance such as 10.00 mm ±0.10 mm is given.", "given": ["A tolerance is the range of sizes a part can have and still work.", "Here, pins from 9.90 mm to 10.10 mm are accepted."], "q": "Which point should come next to explain why the tolerance matters?", "o": ["A smaller tolerance, such as ±0.5 mm, would make the pins more accurate.", "Pins outside the range are rejected, so every accepted pin fits any hole.", "Every pin must measure exactly 10.00 mm, because machines never vary.", "The tolerance is the extra metal added to each pin to make it stronger."], "a": 1, "f": ["±0.5 mm is a larger tolerance, which allows more variation. A smaller tolerance means more accuracy.", "Right. Rejecting parts outside the range means every accepted pin fits, so parts are interchangeable.", "No machine makes every part exactly the same size. That is why a tolerance is needed.", "A tolerance is an allowed variation in size. It is not extra material or a strength measure."]}, "trap": "Saying a jig draws the shape, or that a template holds the work. A template is marked round. A jig holds or guides. Ask: does it only help you mark, or does it physically hold or guide?", "recap": "A jig holds the work or guides the tool so identical parts are accurate and quick; tolerances set how much each part may vary."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.2.9", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to name a finish, say how it works and explain why it suits a material, so you need all three for each finish.", "hook": {"q": "A plain steel bolt is left outside through a wet winter. What most likely happens to it?", "o": ["It stays shiny, because steel is a hard metal", "It turns orange-brown as it reacts with oxygen and water", "It turns white as a layer of zinc builds up on it", "It rots as fungi grow deep inside the metal"], "a": 1, "f": ["Hardness does not stop corrosion. Steel is an iron alloy, and iron reacts with oxygen and water.", "Right. Steel rusts when oxygen and water reach it. Finishes exist to stop this happening.", "Zinc only appears if someone galvanises the steel. Bare steel has no zinc to build up.", "Rot is a timber problem caused by fungi. Metals corrode instead of rotting."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why finish a product?", "b": "A **surface finish** protects a product or improves how it looks. Steel and iron suffer **corrosion**: they rust when oxygen and water reach them. Rusting is a type of **oxidation**, which means reacting with oxygen. Timber suffers rot and insect attack. Finishes also add colour or a smooth feel."}, {"h": "Barrier coatings on metal", "b": "Painting puts a coloured layer on metal. **Powder coating** sprays dry plastic powder on, then heats it so it melts into a tough, even layer. **Dip coating** dips heated metal into plastic powder for a thick plastic coat. All three act as a **barrier** against oxygen and water. If scratched through, the metal underneath can rust."}, {"h": "Zinc, plating and anodising", "b": "**Galvanising** coats steel with zinc. Zinc corrodes first, so it protects the steel even when scratched. **Electroplating** uses an electric current to add a thin layer of another metal, such as chrome, for looks and protection. **Anodising** thickens the oxide layer on aluminium, making it harder wearing and able to take dye."}, {"h": "Finishes for timber", "b": "**Pressure treatment** forces preservative deep into timber. It gives long-lasting protection against rot and insects. **Tanalising** is an example. It suits fences and decking. Paint, varnish, stain and oil mainly treat the surface. They improve the look and add some protection, but they can wear or crack and need reapplying."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why decking boards are pressure treated rather than only painted. [4 marks]", "steps": [{"do": "Pressure treatment forces preservative deep into the timber under pressure. Tanalising is an example.", "why": "This names the process and shows you know how it works."}, {"do": "The preservative protects the boards against rot and insect attack.", "why": "This states what the treatment protects against. Damp outdoor timber is at risk of rot."}, {"do": "Paint only coats the surface of the wood.", "why": "The question says 'rather than', so you must compare the two finishes."}, {"do": "If the paint wears or cracks, moisture gets in and rot can start.", "why": "This is the consequence. It shows why a surface coating is the weaker choice."}, {"do": "Treated boards are protected throughout, so they last longer outdoors.", "why": "This links back to the question and gives the reason pressure treatment is better."}], "answer": "Pressure treatment forces preservative deep into the wood, protecting it from rot and insects. Paint only coats the surface. If it wears or cracks, moisture gets in and the wood rots. Treated boards are protected throughout, so they last longer outdoors."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention that paint only coats the surface?", "o": ["Paint is not allowed on outdoor timber, so it must be ruled out", "Paint is a preservative that soaks deep, so it needs mentioning", "The examiner wants every finish for timber listed in the answer", "The question says 'rather than', so the answer must compare the two finishes"], "a": 3, "f": ["Paint can be used outdoors. It is just less protective than pressure treatment, so the answer compares them.", "Paint does not soak deep. Treating it as a deep preservative is the mix-up that gets this question wrong.", "Listing finishes earns little. The mark comes from explaining why one is better than the other.", "Right. 'Rather than' asks for a comparison. Saying what paint does, and its weakness, shows why pressure treatment is better."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a steel garden chair is powder coated. [3 marks]", "given": ["Bare steel rusts when oxygen and water reach it.", "Powder coating covers the steel with a melted layer of plastic."], "q": "What is the next point to make?", "o": ["The coating is more reactive than steel, so it rusts first and the steel is spared", "Powder coating makes the steel itself harder, so oxygen and water cannot rust it", "The layer is a barrier that keeps oxygen and water away from the steel", "The bright colour improves the look, and this is what stops the steel from rusting"], "a": 2, "f": ["That describes sacrificial protection, which is galvanising with zinc. A plastic coating does not work this way.", "Powder coating does not change the steel. It adds a layer on top of it.", "Right. The plastic layer is a barrier. With no oxygen or water reaching the steel, rusting is stopped or slowed.", "Colour improves appearance, but it does not stop rust. The protection comes from the layer acting as a barrier."]}, "trap": "Saying paint or varnish protects timber as well as pressure treatment. They only coat the surface and can crack. Name pressure treatment, say it goes deep, and match each finish to its material.", "recap": "Name the finish, say what it protects against, and say how it works: a surface barrier, deep preservative, or sacrificial zinc."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.3.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to tell primary from secondary data, name research methods, and write a brief or a specification point that can be measured and tested.", "hook": {"q": "A designer is creating a new school bag for Year 7 pupils. Which source gives information most directly from the real users?", "o": ["A magazine article about the best school bags", "A short questionnaire filled in by 20 Year 7 pupils", "A shop website listing bag sizes and prices", "A book about how school bags have changed"], "a": 1, "f": ["Someone else wrote this earlier, for a different purpose. It is secondary data, not first-hand from the users.", "Right. The designer collects answers first-hand from the actual users. This is primary data.", "A shop website already exists and was made by someone else. It is useful, but it is secondary data.", "A book is existing information collected by someone else. It gives background, not first-hand answers from Year 7 pupils."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Primary data", "b": "**Primary data** is collected first-hand, by you, for your own project. Methods include **questionnaires**, interviews, observing users, and testing or measuring products. It is up to date and fits your target users. It can take time to collect."}, {"h": "Secondary data", "b": "**Secondary data** already exists because someone else collected it earlier. Examples are websites, books, magazines and reports. It is quick and cheap, but it can be old, biased or about different users. Check it against your own **primary data**."}, {"h": "Brief and design specification", "b": "A **design brief** is a short statement of what you will design and who it is for. A **design specification** is a list of requirements the product must meet. Each point should be measurable and based on your data, such as a size range or a test."}, {"h": "Manufacturing specification", "b": "A **manufacturing specification** describes how the product will be made. It covers materials, processes, sizes with **tolerances**, quantity and quality checks. The design specification says what the product must do. The manufacturing specification says how it will be made."}], "example": {"task": "A designer must create a storage box for children's cycle helmets at a primary school. Describe how primary and secondary data can be used to write the design specification. [4 marks]", "steps": [{"do": "Give a questionnaire to pupils and parents asking how helmets are carried and stored now.", "why": "This is primary data: collected first-hand from the real users."}, {"do": "Measure a sample of real child helmets. The longest is 270 mm.", "why": "Measuring is primary data and gives sizes the box must fit."}, {"do": "Read online safety guidance and reviews of existing helmet storage products.", "why": "This is secondary data. It already exists, so it is quick and covers many products."}, {"do": "Check the secondary findings against the questionnaire results.", "why": "Secondary data may be old or about other users. Primary data confirms it suits these pupils."}, {"do": "Write a specification point: 'Internal length must be at least 280 mm to fit all measured helmets.'", "why": "It is measurable, testable and linked directly to the data."}], "answer": "Use a questionnaire and measurements of real helmets as primary data. Use online guidance and reviews as secondary data. Check one against the other. Then write measurable points, such as an internal length of at least 280 mm, in the design specification."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the designer check the secondary findings against the questionnaire results?", "o": ["Secondary data is always wrong, so it must be replaced", "The exam board says every project needs two questionnaires", "Secondary data may be old or about other users, so it needs checking", "Primary data is always right, so secondary data is not needed"], "a": 2, "f": ["Secondary data is not always wrong. It is useful, but it needs checking to see whether it fits your users.", "Checking is not about the number of questionnaires. It is about whether existing data suits your target users.", "Right. Someone else collected it, maybe years ago or for a different group. Primary data from your users shows whether it still applies.", "Both types are useful. Primary data fits your users, while secondary data adds wider background. Using both is stronger."]}, "faded": {"task": "A designer is making a water bottle for primary school children. Use the data to write one design specification point.", "given": ["Primary: checked 20 pupils' bags at lunch. 8 bottles had leaked inside the bags.", "Secondary: online reviews say leaking lids are the most common complaint."], "q": "Which is the best next step, a specification point based on this data?", "o": ["'The bottle should look cool and feel nice to hold.'", "'Design a leak-proof bottle for primary school children.'", "'The bottle will be injection moulded in batches of 5000.'", "'The lid must not leak when held upside down for 10 seconds.'"], "a": 3, "f": ["'Cool' and 'nice' are opinions. They cannot be measured or tested, and they do not use the leak data.", "This is a design brief: what to design and for whom. A specification needs measurable requirements.", "This is a manufacturing specification point about how to make it. It does not use the leak data or say what the bottle must do.", "Right. It is measurable, can be tested, and answers the leaking problem found in both the primary and secondary data."]}, "trap": "Calling internet or book research primary because you found it yourself. Ask who collected the data. If you collected it for your project, it is primary. If someone else collected it earlier, it is secondary.", "recap": "Primary data is collected first-hand for your project, secondary already exists; use both to write a brief, then a measurable design specification."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.3.10", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to choose a tool or machine for a job, explain why it suits that job, and say how to use it safely.", "hook": {"q": "You try to cut a tight curve with a saw whose wide blade is held stiff by a bar along its top edge. What happens?", "o": ["The blade turns easily and follows the curve", "The stiff, wide blade cannot turn, so it jams or wanders", "The bar makes the blade bend into the curve", "The saw cuts the curve faster than a thin blade would"], "a": 1, "f": ["A wide blade held rigid cannot twist round a tight bend. That is why curves need a different saw.", "Right. A wide, rigid blade has no room to turn, so it binds in the cut or runs off the line.", "The bar is there to stop the blade bending. It keeps cuts straight, so it works against curves.", "Width and stiffness make turning harder, not faster. Speed is not the point. Following the line is."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Match hand tool to job", "b": "A **coping saw** has a thin blade that turns tight curves in thin timber. A **fret saw** does the same for finer, more delicate work. A **tenon saw** has a stiff back, so it cuts straight lines. A hacksaw is for cutting metal."}, {"h": "What machines add", "b": "Machines give speed and accuracy. A **pillar drill** drills holes at right angles to the work, at a set depth. A **laser cutter** cuts or engraves sheet material from a drawing file. Both give repeatable results with less effort than hand tools."}, {"h": "Digital design and making", "b": "**CAD** is drawing the design on a computer. A **CNC machine** follows coded instructions made from that file. It repeats the same cut, so every part is **identical**. A 3D printer is additive: it builds layers. Milling and routing are subtractive: they cut material away."}, {"h": "Using tools safely", "b": "Wear **PPE**, such as safety glasses. **Clamp** the work; never hold small pieces by hand. Remove the chuck key before switching on. Keep **guards** in place. Tie back long hair and loose sleeves. Do not wear gloves near rotating parts."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a CNC router is more suitable than hand tools for making 200 identical wooden signs.", "steps": [{"do": "A CNC router follows a CAD/CAM file, so it cuts the same path every time.", "why": "This names how the machine is controlled. The link to CAD/CAM is a key term."}, {"do": "All 200 signs are therefore identical in size and shape.", "why": "This links the feature to its benefit: accuracy and consistency across the batch."}, {"do": "Once set up, it cuts quickly and needs little supervision.", "why": "Speed matters in batch production. Cutting 200 signs by hand would take far longer."}, {"do": "Hand tools would be slower, and the results would vary with the user's skill.", "why": "The question says 'more suitable than', so you must compare."}, {"do": "The work is clamped and guards are used, so hands stay clear of the cutter.", "why": "Safer than 200 rounds of hand sawing, and it shows you know how to use the machine."}], "answer": "A CNC router follows a CAD/CAM file, so it cuts the same path every time. All 200 signs are identical and accurate. Once set up, it works quickly with little supervision. Hand tools would be slower and vary with the user's skill. The work is clamped and guards are used, so hands stay clear."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the signs are 'identical' straight after mentioning the CAD/CAM file?", "o": ["Because a CAD file makes the wood stronger, so all the signs match", "Because a CNC router needs no setup, so each sign starts the same", "Because the same coded path is repeated for every sign, so the results match", "Because hand tools can never cut accurately, so only a router can match them"], "a": 2, "f": ["CAD does not change the strength of the wood. It stores the design that the machine follows.", "A CNC machine does need setting up. The match comes from the repeated instructions, not from skipping setup.", "Right. The machine runs the same coded path each time, so every sign has the same size and shape.", "Skilled people can cut accurately by hand. The point is that a machine repeats the cut without variation."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a tenon saw is suitable for cutting a straight joint in a piece of oak.", "given": ["A tenon saw has a thin blade with a stiff steel or brass back along its top edge.", "The thin blade makes a narrow cut, so little wood is wasted."], "q": "Which point should come next in the explanation?", "o": ["The narrow blade can turn tight curves, so it follows the joint outline", "The coarse teeth are made to cut through thick metal bars quickly", "The back is flexible, so the blade can bend to follow the line", "The stiff back keeps the blade straight, so the cut is straight and accurate"], "a": 3, "f": ["That describes a coping saw. A tenon saw's rigid back stops it turning, which suits straight cuts.", "A hacksaw is for metal. A tenon saw is a fine-toothed wood saw, so this ties it to the wrong job.", "It is the opposite. The back is stiff. A flexible back would let the blade wander off the line.", "Right. The stiff back stops the blade twisting, so the cut stays straight. That is why it suits a joint."]}, "trap": "Choosing a tenon saw or hacksaw for curves. Their blades are wide or held rigid. For curves, pick a coping saw or fret saw, and say the blade is thin.", "recap": "Choose the tool for its job: thin blades for curves, stiff backs for straight cuts, CNC for identical parts, and always clamp work."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.3.11", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to name a shaping process, say which type it is and describe how it is carried out safely and accurately.", "hook": {"q": "You press a lump of modelling clay flat into a disc. Compared with the lump, what has happened to the clay?", "o": ["Some clay has been removed", "Some clay has been added", "The same clay, just reshaped", "Some added and some removed"], "a": 2, "f": ["Nothing was cut or scraped off. Removing material is wasting, and pressing does not do that.", "Nothing was stuck on. Adding material means joining extra material to the work.", "Right. The amount of clay is unchanged and only its shape differs. Processes like this are called deforming.", "Neither happened. The disc has exactly the same amount of clay as the lump."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Wasting: taking material away", "b": "A **wasting** process removes material to leave the shape you want. Examples are sawing, drilling, filing, milling and turning on a lathe. The removed material is waste, such as shavings, dust or **swarf** (metal chips). You start with more material than you need."}, {"h": "Adding: building up", "b": "An **addition** process joins or builds up material to make the shape. Examples are gluing, soldering, welding, laminating and 3D printing. You end with more material than you started with. Joining parts with an **adhesive** is adding, not removing or bending."}, {"h": "Deforming and reforming", "b": "**Deforming** changes the shape of a solid by force or heat, with nothing added or removed. Examples are bending, forging and vacuum forming. **Reforming** melts or liquefies the material so it flows into a mould and sets. Examples are casting and injection moulding."}, {"h": "Finishes: safe and accurate", "b": "A **finish** protects a surface and improves its appearance, for example paint, varnish, oil, or polishing. Prepare the surface first, then apply thin, even coats. Work in a ventilated area and wear gloves and eye protection. Check that the finish suits the **material** you are using."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how a pewter badge is made by casting, and name the type of shaping process it is.", "steps": [{"do": "Make a mould with a cavity in the shape of the badge.", "why": "The cavity gives the molten metal its shape. The mould must be accurate, or the badge will be too."}, {"do": "Melt the pewter in a crucible until it is liquid.", "why": "Reforming needs the material to flow. Pewter has a low melting point, so it is practical to melt."}, {"do": "Pour the molten pewter carefully into the mould, wearing PPE.", "why": "Hot metal can burn. Safety equipment is part of using the process safely."}, {"do": "Leave it to cool and set. Then remove the badge and file off any excess metal.", "why": "Cooling makes the metal solid in the cavity shape. Filing removes spare metal."}, {"do": "State that casting is a reforming process.", "why": "The metal is melted and set into a new shape. It is not cut away or joined."}], "answer": "A mould with the badge shape is made. Pewter is melted in a crucible and poured into the mould, wearing PPE. It cools and sets in the cavity shape. The badge is removed and filed. Casting is reforming, because the metal is melted and set in a new shape."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer call casting reforming rather than adding?", "o": ["The metal is cooled after it is poured in", "The metal is melted and set, not joined on", "The metal is cut away from a larger block", "The mould is made of a different material"], "a": 1, "f": ["Cooling happens in many processes. It does not decide the type. What matters is that the material was melted and reshaped.", "Right. Reforming turns the material liquid so it flows and sets in a new shape. Adding joins extra material to a part.", "Cutting away from a block is wasting. Casting pours the metal into a mould, so it does not do this.", "The mould material does not decide the process type. The type depends on what happens to the metal being shaped."]}, "faded": {"task": "Describe how a plastic bottle top is made by injection moulding.", "given": ["Plastic granules are fed into a hopper and heated until they are molten.", "A screw pushes the molten plastic under pressure into a closed mould."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["A heated sheet is pulled down over the mould by a vacuum", "The plastic is bent in a jig while hot and held until it cools", "The top is cut out of a solid block of plastic on a lathe", "The plastic cools and sets, then the mould opens to eject it"], "a": 3, "f": ["That describes vacuum forming. Injection moulding forces molten plastic into a closed mould, not a sheet over it.", "That describes line bending, which is deforming. Here the plastic has been melted and injected into a mould.", "Turning a block on a lathe is wasting. Injection moulding is reforming, and no material is cut away.", "Right. The plastic hardens in the cavity shape. The mould then opens and the finished part is ejected."]}, "trap": "Mixing up deforming and reforming. Bending, forging and vacuum forming keep the material solid. Reforming melts it so it flows into a mould. Ask: was it removed, added, bent, or melted and reset?", "recap": "Name the type first: wasting removes, adding joins on, deforming reshapes a solid, and reforming melts then sets in a mould."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.3.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "AQA papers often ask you to explain how an issue such as deforestation, CO₂ or Fairtrade affects a design choice, usually for 2 to 4 marks.", "hook": {"q": "Trees take in carbon dioxide as they grow. A large forest is cut down and not replanted. What happens to CO₂ in the air over time?", "o": ["It falls, because living trees give out CO₂ and fewer trees are left", "It rises, because fewer trees are left to absorb it and cleared wood can release it", "It stays the same, because the cut wood still holds all its carbon", "It falls, because trees produce CO₂ as they grow and fewer trees means less"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Trees absorb CO₂ as they grow; they are not a net source. Removing them removes an absorber.", "Right. Fewer trees absorb less CO₂. Burnt or rotting wood also releases the carbon it stored.", "Wrong. Wood can hold carbon, but burning or rotting releases it, and fewer trees means less is absorbed.", "Wrong. This reverses the process. Trees take CO₂ in from the air, so clearing them lets CO₂ build up."], "hook_note": null}, "chunks": [{"h": "Deforestation and timber", "b": "**Deforestation** is clearing forest for farming, building or timber. Fewer trees means less CO₂ absorbed and habitats lost. Opportunity: a designer can specify **FSC-certified** timber from well-managed forests where trees are replanted. Limit: certified timber can cost more and be harder to source."}, {"h": "Carbon dioxide and warming", "b": "Burning fossil fuels to make and transport products releases **carbon dioxide** (CO₂). Extra CO₂ traps heat, which causes **global warming**. Opportunity: designers can use recycled materials, cut energy in manufacture and source materials nearby. Limit: some processes still need high energy, and customers may not pay more."}, {"h": "Finite resources and mining", "b": "Metals and plastics come from mining and drilling. Ores and oil are **finite resources**: they will run out. Extraction also damages land and causes pollution. Opportunity: design for **recycling** and use less material. Limit: recycling needs collection and sorting, and recycled material may not suit every product."}, {"h": "Fairtrade: people and cost", "b": "The **Fairtrade** mark shows producers in developing countries get a fair price and decent working conditions. This is a **social** issue. Opportunity: an ethical selling point. Limit: materials cost more, which is an **economic** effect. The mark does not mean organic, UK-made or recycled."}], "example": {"task": "A company makes cotton tote bags. It is thinking of using Fairtrade cotton. Explain one opportunity and one limit of this choice for the designer. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Fairtrade cotton means the farmers got a fair price and decent working conditions.", "why": "States what the mark shows, so the later points rest on a correct meaning."}, {"do": "This means farmers in developing countries can earn a living and work safely.", "why": "Links the mark to its social effect on people."}, {"do": "Opportunity: the designer can sell the bag as ethical to customers who care about this.", "why": "Turns the social benefit into a gain for the designer or business."}, {"do": "Limit: the cotton costs more, so the bag's price rises or the company's profit falls.", "why": "Gives an economic limit and says what it causes."}, {"do": "The mark covers pay and conditions, so it does not show the bag is recycled or low in carbon.", "why": "Shows you know what the mark does not claim."}], "answer": "Fairtrade cotton means farmers get a fair price and decent conditions, so they can earn a living safely. This lets the designer sell the bag as ethical to customers who care. However, the cotton costs more, so the price rises or profit falls. The mark covers people, not recycling or carbon."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the last step say the mark does not show the bag is recycled or low in carbon?", "o": ["Because organic cotton is always Fairtrade, so the two terms mean the same", "Because Fairtrade applies only to farms in the UK, so it cannot judge imported cotton", "Because the mark means the bag can be recycled at the end of its life", "Because the mark covers pay and conditions only, so green claims need other evidence"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Organic and Fairtrade are different. One is about farming methods, the other about pay and conditions.", "Wrong. Fairtrade is for producers in developing countries, not the UK.", "Wrong. The mark says nothing about what happens to the bag at the end of its life.", "Right. Fairtrade is about fair pay and conditions. A claim about recycling or carbon needs a different mark or evidence."], "hook_note": null}, "faded": {"task": "A designer is making a wooden chair. Explain how the designer can reduce the effect of deforestation.", "given": ["Deforestation means forests are cleared faster than they regrow, so fewer trees absorb CO₂.", "The designer can specify FSC-certified timber for the chair."], "q": "Which point should come next to explain why this choice helps?", "o": ["FSC timber is Fairtrade, so the tree growers are paid a fair price for it.", "FSC timber is recycled wood, so no new trees need to be cut down at all.", "FSC timber comes from managed forests where trees are replanted, so tree cover is kept.", "Hardwood grows back within a year, so cutting it never reduces the number of trees."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. This mixes up two marks. Fairtrade is about producers' pay; FSC is about well-managed forests.", "Wrong. FSC timber is not necessarily recycled. New trees may be cut, but they are replaced.", "Right. Replanting keeps tree cover, so CO₂ absorption and habitats are protected. This links FSC directly to deforestation.", "Wrong. Many hardwoods grow slowly, and this claim is false. The explanation should name replanting."], "hook_note": null}, "trap": "Treating marks as if they mean the same thing. Fairtrade is about pay and conditions; FSC is about well-managed forests. Neither automatically means recycled, organic or low-carbon. Say what each mark actually shows.", "recap": "Every material choice has an environmental, social or economic effect; name the issue, then give one opportunity and one limit for the designer."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.3.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to describe or explain the work of designers and companies such as Dyson, Apple or Alessi, linking each feature to its effect.", "hook": {"q": "A vacuum cleaner with a bag loses suction as the bag fills with dust. Which design change would best fix this?", "o": ["Fit a much bigger bag so it takes longer to fill", "Fit a stronger motor to pull harder through the bag", "Spin the air so dust is thrown out of the airflow into a bin", "Use a thinner bag material so air passes through more easily"], "a": 2, "f": ["A bigger bag only delays the problem. Dust still blocks the pores in the bag, so suction still drops over time.", "A stronger motor uses more energy but does not stop dust clogging the bag. The cause of the lost suction is still there.", "Right. Spinning the air throws dust outwards into a bin, so there is no bag to clog. This is the idea behind Dyson's cleaner.", "A thinner bag still fills with fine dust and clogs. It may also let dust escape, which is a worse result."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Dyson: engineering solutions", "b": "**James Dyson** founded the company. It is known for the **bagless** vacuum cleaner, which uses **cyclonic separation**. Air spins at high speed and flings dust out into a bin. With no bag to clog, suction stays strong. Dyson's work starts from a problem and solves it with new engineering."}, {"h": "Apple: simple and user-friendly", "b": "Apple is known for electronics such as the iPhone. Designer **Jonathan Ive** led a **minimal** style: clean forms, smooth surfaces and few buttons. The aim is **user-friendly** products that people can use at once, with little need for instructions."}, {"h": "Alessi: stylish homeware", "b": "**Alessi** is an Italian company making kitchen and homeware products. It works with well-known designers, such as Philippe Starck, who designed the Juicy Salif lemon squeezer. Its products are **stylish**, bold and often **playful**. Many are meant to be talked about and displayed, not just used."}, {"h": "Describe, then explain", "b": "For each company, remember three things: who or what it is known for, the main **design feature**, and the **effect** of that feature on the user. Marks come from the link. For example, 'no bag, so suction stays strong' scores. 'Dyson makes vacuums' does not."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how the work of Dyson has influenced the design of vacuum cleaners. [4 marks]", "steps": [{"do": "James Dyson developed the bagless vacuum cleaner.", "why": "This names the designer and the specific innovation, so it is accurate and relevant."}, {"do": "It uses cyclonic separation, which spins the air so dust is flung out into a bin.", "why": "This describes how the feature works, using the correct term."}, {"do": "This means there is no bag to clog, so suction stays strong for longer.", "why": "This links the feature to a benefit for the user. It is the key explain mark."}, {"do": "Many other makers now sell bagless cleaners, and a clear bin shows when it needs emptying.", "why": "This shows the wider influence on the market and adds a second user benefit."}], "answer": "James Dyson developed the bagless vacuum cleaner. It uses cyclonic separation, spinning the air so dust is flung out into a bin. With no bag to clog, suction stays strong for longer. A clear bin shows when it needs emptying, and many other makers now sell bagless cleaners."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say 'With no bag to clog, suction stays strong for longer' after describing cyclonic separation?", "o": ["It repeats the name of the company to make the answer longer", "It links the feature to a user benefit, which earns the explain mark", "It lists the materials used, which examiners always want to see", "It compares the cleaner's price with other cleaners"], "a": 1, "f": ["Repeating the name adds no marks. Marks come from new, relevant points, not length.", "Right. A feature only earns full marks when you say what difference it makes to the user or the product.", "The answer does not mention materials. Materials are not what this sentence is about.", "Price is not mentioned here. The sentence explains performance, not cost."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how Apple's design approach makes the iPhone easy to use. [3 marks]", "given": ["Point 1: Apple designs products with minimal forms and very few buttons on the front."], "q": "Which sentence is the best next point?", "o": ["Apple was founded in California and sells its products all over the world.", "The iPhone is expensive, so it must be a high-quality product.", "Apple uses bright colours and bold decoration, in the same way as Alessi.", "With so few controls to learn, a new user can work the phone quickly by touch."], "a": 3, "f": ["This is a fact about the company, not about the design. It does not explain why the iPhone is easy to use.", "Price does not explain ease of use. Cost and quality are different ideas from usability.", "This mixes up the companies. Bold decoration is more like Alessi. Apple's approach is minimal and simple.", "Right. It links the few buttons to a benefit for the user: fewer controls to learn, so quicker, easier use."]}, "trap": "Mixing up companies. Dyson is cyclonic engineering, Apple is minimal and user-friendly, and Alessi is stylish and playful homeware. Name the company's key feature, then link it to its effect.", "recap": "Name the designer's or company's key feature, then say what effect it has on the user: Dyson, cyclones; Apple, simplicity; Alessi, style."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.3.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to describe or explain one of these strategies, or to say how a designer can avoid design fixation, usually for 2 to 4 marks.", "hook": {"q": "You must design a new torch. Which approach is most likely to give you an original design?", "o": ["Study one popular torch closely and improve it slightly", "Sketch lots of different ideas before choosing one to develop", "Pick your first idea and spend your time perfecting it", "Ask one friend to choose the design for you"], "a": 1, "f": ["Staying close to one existing product keeps you stuck on it. That is design fixation, so the result is unlikely to be original.", "Right. Many varied ideas give you choice and comparison. That widens your thinking, so the final design is more likely to be original.", "Committing to the first idea early is a common cause of fixation. You never see better alternatives.", "One person's choice limits the ideas to one view. Getting views helps, but only alongside many ideas of your own."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "User-centred design", "b": "**User-centred design** puts the people who will use the product first. The designer finds out their needs and wants, for example with interviews or questionnaires. Users then try **prototypes** and give feedback. The design changes to suit them, not just the designer's taste."}, {"h": "Iterative design", "b": "**Iterative design** is a cycle: **model**, test, **evaluate**, improve. Then you go round again. Each version should be better than the last. Models can be sketches, card models or CAD. Problems found early are cheaper to fix than problems found in the final product."}, {"h": "Collaboration", "b": "**Collaboration** means working with others, such as a team, users or experts. Each person brings different skills and **views**. Others can spot faults you missed and suggest ideas you would not have thought of alone. Good ideas can be shared and combined."}, {"h": "Avoiding design fixation", "b": "**Design fixation** is getting stuck on one idea or one existing product. It blocks new ideas. To avoid it, **research widely**, including sources such as nature. Sketch **many varied ideas** before choosing. Ask other people for their views on your sketches."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a designer can avoid design fixation when designing a new cycle helmet. [4 marks]", "steps": [{"do": "Say what design fixation is: sticking to one idea, or copying one existing helmet, so that new ideas are blocked.", "why": "This shows you know the term the question depends on."}, {"do": "Research widely. Look at many different helmets and at other sources, such as the hard shell of a tortoise.", "why": "A wide range of research gives more starting points than one product does."}, {"do": "Sketch many varied ideas before choosing one to develop.", "why": "Comparing several ideas stops the first one being picked without a good reason."}, {"do": "Ask users and other designers for their views on the sketches.", "why": "Other people see options the designer missed. This is collaboration."}, {"do": "Link it to the result: the final helmet is more original and suits its users better.", "why": "Linking the method to its result turns a statement into an explanation."}], "answer": "Design fixation is sticking to one idea. A designer avoids it by researching widely, including nature, and sketching many varied ideas before choosing one. They then ask users and others for views. This gives a wider choice, so the final helmet is more original and suits users better."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 say to sketch many ideas before choosing one?", "o": ["So the first idea can be developed fully before any others are looked at", "So the designer can copy the best existing helmet more accurately", "So several ideas can be compared and the first one is not picked automatically", "So the designer does not need to ask users for feedback later"], "a": 2, "f": ["This describes design fixation. Developing the first idea straight away is what the strategy avoids.", "Copying one existing product is also fixation. The aim is new and varied ideas, not a copy.", "Right. Having several ideas to compare means the choice is made for a reason. This avoids fixing on the first idea.", "Sketching ideas does not replace feedback. Asking others is a separate step, and it is still needed."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how iterative design could improve a child's school bag. [4 marks]", "given": ["The designer makes a first model of the bag.", "Children test the model by carrying books. The strap keeps slipping off their shoulders."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Decide the bag is finished, because the model has now been made", "Copy a popular bag and stop testing the first model", "Ask only one adult for an opinion and make no changes", "Evaluate the result, change the strap, then make and test a new model"], "a": 3, "f": ["A first model is rarely finished. Iterative design means improving it after testing, not stopping after one attempt.", "Copying another bag drops the cycle and risks fixation. Iterative design improves your own design using test results.", "One opinion with no changes is not a cycle. The test results must lead to a change and a retest.", "Right. After testing, you evaluate the results, improve the design and test again. Going round the cycle again is what makes it iterative."]}, "trap": "Do not confuse design fixation with iterative design. Iterative design deliberately repeats model, test, evaluate and improve. Fixation is being stuck on one idea, so it blocks change.", "recap": "Avoid design fixation by researching widely, sketching many varied ideas and asking others' views. Iterative design is the repeated cycle of model, test, evaluate, improve."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.3.5", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to pick a drawing type or complete a third angle drawing, so you need to know what each way of showing ideas is for.", "hook": {"q": "A maker must cut a part from acrylic to exact sizes. What should the designer give them?", "o": ["A quick freehand sketch with notes", "A scaled drawing with sizes in mm", "A mood board of similar products", "A perspective drawing of the finished part"], "a": 1, "f": ["A sketch is quick but not accurate. The maker would have to guess sizes.", "Right. A scaled drawing with dimensions gives the exact sizes the maker needs to cut the part.", "A mood board shows style and inspiration. It has no sizes, so the maker cannot cut from it.", "A perspective drawing looks realistic but is not to scale. Measurements cannot be taken from it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Sketches, models and diagrams", "b": "A **sketch** is a quick freehand drawing, with notes, used for early ideas. A **model** is a 3D version made from card, foam or CAD. It shows form and size. A **diagram**, such as a flow chart, explains a process or system."}, {"h": "Drawings that show form", "b": "**Isometric** drawings use 30° lines and no face is a true shape. **Oblique** drawings show the front face as a true shape, with depth lines usually at 45°. **Perspective** lines meet at a vanishing point, so it looks realistic. An exploded view pulls parts apart to show assembly."}, {"h": "Working drawings and scale", "b": "A **working drawing** gives a maker everything needed to make the product. It is drawn accurately, by hand or with CAD, to **scale**. A scale of 1:2 means the drawing is half real size. **Dimensions** in mm always give the real size, not the drawn size."}, {"h": "Third angle layout", "b": "In **third angle orthographic projection**, the plan goes directly above the front view, lined up with it. An end view goes beside the front view, on the side it is viewed from. Edges hidden from a view are drawn as **dashed lines**."}], "example": {"task": "A designer is passing a chair design to a manufacturer. Explain why a scaled third angle working drawing is more useful than a freehand sketch.", "steps": [{"do": "A sketch is freehand, so its proportions may be wrong. A third angle drawing is drawn to scale, so every part is in proportion.", "why": "Accurate proportion means the manufacturer makes the chair as designed."}, {"do": "The drawing has dimensions in mm, so the maker can read the exact size of each part.", "why": "Dimensions remove guesswork, so parts are cut to the correct size."}, {"do": "It shows separate views: plan, front and end. Faces seen straight on show their true shape, and dashed lines show hidden edges.", "why": "Detail that one view hides is shown in another, so nothing is missed."}, {"do": "The layout follows standard conventions, so any manufacturer reads it the same way.", "why": "Shared rules reduce misunderstandings and costly mistakes."}], "answer": "A third angle drawing is to scale, so proportions are accurate. Dimensions in mm give exact sizes. Separate views and dashed lines show every feature, including hidden ones. Standard conventions mean any maker reads it the same way, so there are fewer errors."}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 3, why does the answer mention dashed lines?", "o": ["They show the real sizes of the parts, written in mm along the edges", "They show the scale, so the maker knows how much to enlarge it", "They show hidden edges, such as a drilled hole, so no detail is left out", "They show the centre line through a hole or a round part of the chair"], "a": 2, "f": ["Sizes are given by dimension lines and numbers. Dashed lines are a different convention.", "Scale is written in the title block, for example 1:2. Dashed lines do not show it.", "Right. Dashed lines show edges that cannot be seen from that view, so the drawing still shows all the features.", "Centre lines are thin chain lines of long and short dashes. The dashed lines in step 3 show hidden edges."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a designer might make a card model of a chair before it is manufactured.", "given": ["A model is a 3D version of the design, so the client can look at it from every side."], "q": "Which is the best next point?", "o": ["It tests size and proportion, so errors are fixed before costly materials are used.", "It is made from the final materials, so it can be sold to the customer straight away.", "It gives the exact sizes in mm, so the maker does not need a working drawing.", "It shows the order of assembly using lines pulled apart, like an exploded view."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. A cheap model lets the designer test the design and make changes before expensive materials are committed.", "A card model is made from cheap materials to test the idea. It is not a finished product for sale.", "A model does not replace a working drawing. Exact dimensions and scale come from the drawing.", "That describes an exploded view, which is a drawing. A model is a 3D object, not a set of lines."]}, "trap": "Drawing the plan view below the front view. That is first angle projection. In third angle, the plan goes above the front view and lines up with it.", "recap": "A sketch shows early ideas quickly; a scaled third angle drawing gives exact sizes and views, with the plan above the front view."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.3.6", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions on prototypes can appear in the written paper, and your own NEA project needs you to make, test and improve one before you finish.", "hook": {"q": "A firm plans to make 10 000 bike bottle holders. Why might it first make one working prototype?", "o": ["To sell that single holder to its first customer", "To find faults and fix them before costly mass production", "To avoid having to write a specification", "To prove the design is perfect, so no testing is needed"], "a": 1, "f": ["A prototype is made to be tested, not sold. Selling one holder does not help the firm improve the design for 10 000.", "Right. Faults are cheap to fix on one prototype. Finding them after 10 000 are made would be very costly.", "A prototype is tested against the specification, so the specification is still needed first.", "A prototype is made because the design may not be perfect. Testing is its whole purpose."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What a prototype is", "b": "A **prototype** is a working model of a design. You make it to **test** the design, not just to look at. It may use cheaper materials or simpler methods than the final product. It is never the finished product."}, {"h": "Test against the specification", "b": "Each test should check one point of the **specification**. Use measurements where you can, such as load, size or time. Also collect **user feedback** from the people the product is for. They notice problems that measurements miss."}, {"h": "Modify and retest", "b": "Test results show faults. You **modify** the design, make the change, then **retest**. This repeating cycle is **iterative design**. Retesting checks that the fix works and has not caused a new fault. Stop when every specification point is met."}, {"h": "Could it be sold?", "b": "A good prototype also shows **commercial viability**. Can it be made in quantity at a cost that allows a profit? Is it safe? Will the **target market** want to buy it? A product that works but costs too much to make will not sell."}], "example": {"task": "A designer makes a prototype child's step stool. Explain how making and testing the prototype helps improve the design before manufacture. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "The designer makes a full-size working stool from low-cost material, following the specification.", "why": "It can be tested for real without paying for final materials."}, {"do": "The designer tests it against the specification by loading it with the required mass. It holds the load but wobbles.", "why": "A measured result shows clearly whether a requirement is met."}, {"do": "Children try the stool and say the edges feel sharp.", "why": "User feedback finds problems that measurements do not show."}, {"do": "The designer rounds the edges and widens the legs, then retests the stool.", "why": "Iterative design: each fix is checked to make sure it works."}, {"do": "The designer checks the cost of making it in quantity and whether it appeals to buyers.", "why": "This shows whether it could be sold at a profit."}], "answer": "A working prototype lets the designer test the stool against the specification, such as load and stability, which shows it wobbles. User feedback shows faults like sharp edges. The designer widens the legs, rounds the edges and retests. Faults are cheaper to fix now than after mass production."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the designer retest the stool after widening the legs?", "o": ["To check the change fixed the fault without causing a new one", "Because the first test results are always wrong", "To make the prototype look more like a finished product", "Because retesting is only needed if the client asks for it"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. A modification may not work, or may cause a new fault. Retesting proves the specification is now met.", "The first results are usually correct. They are what showed the fault. The point of retesting is to check the fix.", "Retesting checks performance, not appearance. A prototype is for testing, not for looking finished.", "Retesting is part of the iterative design cycle. It is not optional, and it does not wait for the client."]}, "faded": {"task": "A prototype lunch box must not leak and must be easy for a 7-year-old to open. Explain how it should be tested and improved.", "given": ["Make a full-size prototype in the planned plastic.", "Test: fill with 200 ml of water, turn upside down for 1 minute. Result: water leaks at the lid corner."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["Accept the leak as minor and move on to test only how easily it opens", "Change the colour of the lid, then test whether it opens easily", "Add a better seal to the lid, then repeat the same leak test", "Record the result and send the design to be manufactured"], "a": 2, "f": ["Leaking breaks a specification point. Every point must be met, so moving on with a known fault is a mistake.", "Colour has no effect on leaking. Changing an unrelated feature does not fix the fault found.", "Right. The failed test shows the fault. You modify the lid seal, then repeat the same test to check the fix works.", "Manufacturing a design that has failed a test wastes money. Faults must be fixed and retested first."]}, "trap": "Treating the prototype as the finished product, or as a model only to look at. Its main purpose is testing. Always link each test to a specification point, then modify and retest.", "recap": "A prototype is a working model you test against the specification, then modify and retest until it meets the brief and could be sold."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.3.7", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to justify a choice of material or component, and marks go to specific reasons linked to the product, not opinions.", "hook": {"q": "A screw goes missing from a shelf bracket. It is a standard size. Where could you most easily get an identical one?", "o": ["Only from the company that made the shelf", "From any hardware shop, because it is a standard size", "A machinist would have to make one from scratch", "You could not replace it without buying a new bracket"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Standard parts are made to set sizes by many suppliers, so you do not depend on the original maker.", "Right. A standard size means any shop's screw of that size fits. That makes the part easy to get and easy to replace.", "Wrong. Making one from scratch is only needed for a special part. A standard screw is already mass produced.", "Wrong. A standard part can be swapped alone. Replacing the whole bracket would waste money and time."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Match needs to properties", "b": "Start with the **function**. What must the part do? A pan handle must resist heat, so choose a thermal **insulator**. A bridge beam must resist bending, so choose a strong, stiff material. Then check **aesthetics**: colour, texture and finish. The material must do the job and suit the user."}, {"h": "Cost and availability", "b": "**Cost** covers the material price and the cost of working it. Buying in bulk usually lowers the price per item. **Availability** means a supplier has the material in stock in the form you need. Materials come in **stock forms**, such as sheets, bars and tubes, in standard sizes."}, {"h": "Standard components", "b": "**Standard components** are mass produced to set sizes, such as screws, hinges and bearings. They are cheap and easy to get. They are **interchangeable**, so any part of that size fits and a broken one is easy to replace. The designer saves design time and manufacturing cost. The graph (example prices) shows a special screw always costs more each.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [200, 1000], "y": [0, 1.2], "xstep": 100, "ystep": 0.2, "xlabel": "Quantity made", "ylabel": "Cost per screw (£)", "lines": [{"pts": [[200.0, 1.2], [205.0, 1.17561], [210.0, 1.152381], [215.0, 1.130233], [220.0, 1.109091], [225.0, 1.088889], [230.0, 1.069565], [235.0, 1.051064], [240.0, 1.033333], [245.0, 1.016327], [250.0, 1.0], [255.0, 0.984314], [260.0, 0.969231], [265.0, 0.954717], [270.0, 0.940741], [275.0, 0.927273], [280.0, 0.914286], [285.0, 0.901754], [290.0, 0.889655], [295.0, 0.877966], [300.0, 0.866667], [305.0, 0.855738], [310.0, 0.845161], [315.0, 0.834921], [320.0, 0.825], [325.0, 0.815385], [330.0, 0.806061], [335.0, 0.797015], [340.0, 0.788235], [345.0, 0.77971], [350.0, 0.771429], [355.0, 0.76338], [360.0, 0.755556], [365.0, 0.747945], [370.0, 0.740541], [375.0, 0.733333], [380.0, 0.726316], [385.0, 0.719481], [390.0, 0.712821], [395.0, 0.706329], [400.0, 0.7], [405.0, 0.693827], [410.0, 0.687805], [415.0, 0.681928], [420.0, 0.67619], [425.0, 0.670588], [430.0, 0.665116], [435.0, 0.65977], [440.0, 0.654545], [445.0, 0.649438], [450.0, 0.644444], [455.0, 0.63956], [460.0, 0.634783], [465.0, 0.630108], [470.0, 0.625532], [475.0, 0.621053], [480.0, 0.616667], [485.0, 0.612371], [490.0, 0.608163], [495.0, 0.60404], [500.0, 0.6], [505.0, 0.59604], [510.0, 0.592157], [515.0, 0.58835], [520.0, 0.584615], [525.0, 0.580952], [530.0, 0.577358], [535.0, 0.573832], [540.0, 0.57037], [545.0, 0.566972], [550.0, 0.563636], [555.0, 0.56036], [560.0, 0.557143], [565.0, 0.553982], [570.0, 0.550877], [575.0, 0.547826], [580.0, 0.544828], [585.0, 0.54188], [590.0, 0.538983], [595.0, 0.536134], [600.0, 0.533333], [605.0, 0.530579], [610.0, 0.527869], [615.0, 0.525203], [620.0, 0.522581], [625.0, 0.52], [630.0, 0.51746], [635.0, 0.514961], [640.0, 0.5125], [645.0, 0.510078], [650.0, 0.507692], [655.0, 0.505344], [660.0, 0.50303], [665.0, 0.500752], [670.0, 0.498507], [675.0, 0.496296], [680.0, 0.494118], [685.0, 0.491971], [690.0, 0.489855], [695.0, 0.48777], [700.0, 0.485714], [705.0, 0.483688], [710.0, 0.48169], [715.0, 0.47972], [720.0, 0.477778], [725.0, 0.475862], [730.0, 0.473973], [735.0, 0.472109], [740.0, 0.47027], [745.0, 0.468456], [750.0, 0.466667], [755.0, 0.464901], [760.0, 0.463158], [765.0, 0.461438], [770.0, 0.45974], [775.0, 0.458065], [780.0, 0.45641], [785.0, 0.454777], [790.0, 0.453165], [795.0, 0.451572], [800.0, 0.45], [805.0, 0.448447], [810.0, 0.446914], [815.0, 0.445399], [820.0, 0.443902], [825.0, 0.442424], [830.0, 0.440964], [835.0, 0.439521], [840.0, 0.438095], [845.0, 0.436686], [850.0, 0.435294], [855.0, 0.433918], [860.0, 0.432558], [865.0, 0.431214], [870.0, 0.429885], [875.0, 0.428571], [880.0, 0.427273], [885.0, 0.425989], [890.0, 0.424719], [895.0, 0.423464], [900.0, 0.422222], [905.0, 0.420994], [910.0, 0.41978], [915.0, 0.418579], [920.0, 0.417391], [925.0, 0.416216], [930.0, 0.415054], [935.0, 0.413904], [940.0, 0.412766], [945.0, 0.41164], [950.0, 0.410526], [955.0, 0.409424], [960.0, 0.408333], [965.0, 0.407254], [970.0, 0.406186], [975.0, 0.405128], [980.0, 0.404082], [985.0, 0.403046], [990.0, 0.40202], [995.0, 0.401005], [1000.0, 0.4]], "c": 0, "label": "Bespoke"}, {"pts": [[200.0, 0.2], [205.0, 0.2], [210.0, 0.2], [215.0, 0.2], [220.0, 0.2], [225.0, 0.2], [230.0, 0.2], [235.0, 0.2], [240.0, 0.2], [245.0, 0.2], [250.0, 0.2], [255.0, 0.2], [260.0, 0.2], [265.0, 0.2], [270.0, 0.2], [275.0, 0.2], [280.0, 0.2], [285.0, 0.2], [290.0, 0.2], [295.0, 0.2], [300.0, 0.2], [305.0, 0.2], [310.0, 0.2], [315.0, 0.2], [320.0, 0.2], [325.0, 0.2], [330.0, 0.2], [335.0, 0.2], [340.0, 0.2], [345.0, 0.2], [350.0, 0.2], [355.0, 0.2], [360.0, 0.2], [365.0, 0.2], [370.0, 0.2], [375.0, 0.2], [380.0, 0.2], [385.0, 0.2], [390.0, 0.2], [395.0, 0.2], [400.0, 0.2], [405.0, 0.2], [410.0, 0.2], [415.0, 0.2], [420.0, 0.2], [425.0, 0.2], [430.0, 0.2], [435.0, 0.2], [440.0, 0.2], [445.0, 0.2], [450.0, 0.2], [455.0, 0.2], [460.0, 0.2], [465.0, 0.2], [470.0, 0.2], [475.0, 0.2], [480.0, 0.2], [485.0, 0.2], [490.0, 0.2], [495.0, 0.2], [500.0, 0.2], [505.0, 0.2], [510.0, 0.2], [515.0, 0.2], [520.0, 0.2], [525.0, 0.2], [530.0, 0.2], [535.0, 0.2], [540.0, 0.2], [545.0, 0.2], [550.0, 0.2], [555.0, 0.2], [560.0, 0.2], [565.0, 0.2], [570.0, 0.2], [575.0, 0.2], [580.0, 0.2], [585.0, 0.2], [590.0, 0.2], [595.0, 0.2], [600.0, 0.2], [605.0, 0.2], [610.0, 0.2], [615.0, 0.2], [620.0, 0.2], [625.0, 0.2], [630.0, 0.2], [635.0, 0.2], [640.0, 0.2], [645.0, 0.2], [650.0, 0.2], [655.0, 0.2], [660.0, 0.2], [665.0, 0.2], [670.0, 0.2], [675.0, 0.2], [680.0, 0.2], [685.0, 0.2], [690.0, 0.2], [695.0, 0.2], [700.0, 0.2], [705.0, 0.2], [710.0, 0.2], [715.0, 0.2], [720.0, 0.2], [725.0, 0.2], [730.0, 0.2], [735.0, 0.2], [740.0, 0.2], [745.0, 0.2], [750.0, 0.2], [755.0, 0.2], [760.0, 0.2], [765.0, 0.2], [770.0, 0.2], [775.0, 0.2], [780.0, 0.2], [785.0, 0.2], [790.0, 0.2], [795.0, 0.2], [800.0, 0.2], [805.0, 0.2], [810.0, 0.2], [815.0, 0.2], [820.0, 0.2], [825.0, 0.2], [830.0, 0.2], [835.0, 0.2], [840.0, 0.2], [845.0, 0.2], [850.0, 0.2], [855.0, 0.2], [860.0, 0.2], [865.0, 0.2], [870.0, 0.2], [875.0, 0.2], [880.0, 0.2], [885.0, 0.2], [890.0, 0.2], [895.0, 0.2], [900.0, 0.2], [905.0, 0.2], [910.0, 0.2], [915.0, 0.2], [920.0, 0.2], [925.0, 0.2], [930.0, 0.2], [935.0, 0.2], [940.0, 0.2], [945.0, 0.2], [950.0, 0.2], [955.0, 0.2], [960.0, 0.2], [965.0, 0.2], [970.0, 0.2], [975.0, 0.2], [980.0, 0.2], [985.0, 0.2], [990.0, 0.2], [995.0, 0.2], [1000.0, 0.2]], "c": 1, "label": "Standard"}], "points": [[200, 1.2], [500, 0.6], [1000, 0.4], [200, 0.2], [1000, 0.2]]}}, {"h": "Environmental and ethical", "b": "Also weigh **environmental** factors. Can the material be recycled? Does it come from a managed source, such as FSC timber? **Ethical** factors include fair working conditions for the people who make it. Name the factor, then link it to the product."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a designer would use standard screws rather than make special ones for a product made in large numbers. [4 marks]", "steps": [{"do": "Standard screws are mass produced to set sizes, so the designer can simply choose one from a catalogue.", "why": "This defines 'standard' and starts the chain of reasoning."}, {"do": "Because so many are made, each one is cheap. This lowers the manufacturing cost of every product.", "why": "Cost is a key reason. Linking it to mass production earns the mark."}, {"do": "Suppliers keep them in stock, so they are easy to get. The factory does not wait for special parts to be made.", "why": "This covers availability and the time saved."}, {"do": "Any screw of that size is interchangeable, so a broken or lost screw is easy to replace for the user.", "why": "This covers easy replacement, a benefit after the product is sold."}], "answer": "Standard screws are mass produced to set sizes, so they are cheap and lower manufacturing cost. They are easy to get from suppliers, so no time is lost making special parts. They are interchangeable, so a lost screw is easily replaced."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 link low cost to the screws being mass produced?", "o": ["Making huge numbers spreads the production cost, so each screw costs little", "Mass produced parts are lower quality, so they cost less", "Cost is the only factor a designer ever needs to consider", "Mass production makes every part stronger than a special one"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Large batches spread the setup and production costs over many parts, so the price of each one falls.", "Wrong. Low cost comes from the large quantity, not from poor quality. Standard parts can be perfectly strong.", "Wrong. Designers weigh many factors, including function and environment. Cost is just one reason here.", "Wrong. Mass production does not make parts stronger. Never give 'stronger' as the reason without evidence."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a designer would use a standard bulb fitting, not design a new one, for a desk lamp made in large numbers. [4 marks]", "given": ["Standard bulb fittings are mass produced to set sizes.", "They are cheap to buy, so they lower the manufacturing cost of the lamp."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to earn further marks?", "o": ["They are better than other fittings, so customers will prefer the lamp.", "They are stronger than a fitting made specially for the lamp.", "They are easy to get, and standard bulbs fit, so blown bulbs are easy to replace.", "They are the only kind of fitting that a designer is allowed to use."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. 'Better' is vague and earns nothing. Give a specific reason such as availability or replacement.", "Wrong. Standard parts are not automatically stronger. This is the 'stronger' trap with no real reason.", "Right. This adds two new specific points: availability, and standard bulbs fit, so a blown bulb is easy to replace.", "Wrong. Designers can design their own parts. Standard parts are chosen for reasons such as cost, not because of a rule."]}, "trap": "Writing only that standard components are 'better' or 'stronger'. Give specific reasons instead: lower cost, easy to get, interchangeable, easy to replace. Link each one to the product.", "recap": "Choose materials and components by function, cost and availability; standard components are cheap, easy to get and easy to replace."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.3.8", "kind": "method", "why": "Exam questions can ask you to work out size limits from a drawing, and to explain why accurate limits matter when making parts.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What a tolerance is", "b": "No part can be made to exactly the stated size every time. A **tolerance** is the variation from the stated size that is allowed. A part is acceptable if its size falls between two limits: the **upper limit** (largest allowed) and the **lower limit** (smallest allowed)."}, {"h": "Finding the limits", "b": "Add the **plus value** to the size to get the upper limit. Take the **minus value** off the size to get the lower limit. Example: 60 mm, +0.3 and −0.1 gives an upper limit of 60.3 mm and a lower limit of 59.9 mm."}, {"h": "Equal tolerances with ±", "b": "The sign **±** means the plus and minus values are the same. 10 mm ± 0.1 mm means +0.1 and −0.1, so the limits are 9.9 mm and 10.1 mm. Work out each limit separately, then check a **measured size** against them."}, {"h": "Why the limits matter", "b": "Parts must **fit** together, and parts made in different batches must be **interchangeable**. A smaller tolerance is a tighter limit. It usually needs better machines, slower work and more checking, so the **cost** goes up."}], "example": {"task": "A hole in a panel is drawn as 18 mm, +0.4 mm and −0.2 mm. Work out the limits. Then decide whether a hole measuring 18.5 mm is acceptable.", "steps": [{"do": "Read the drawing: size 18 mm, plus value 0.4 mm, minus value 0.2 mm.", "why": "You need all three numbers before you start, and you must not mix up the two values."}, {"do": "Upper limit = 18 + 0.4 = 18.4 mm.", "why": "The plus value is added to the size to give the largest allowed size."}, {"do": "Lower limit = 18 − 0.2 = 17.8 mm.", "why": "The minus value is taken off the size to give the smallest allowed size."}, {"do": "State the range: the hole must be between 17.8 mm and 18.4 mm.", "why": "Writing the range makes the checking step easy and clear."}, {"do": "Compare 18.5 mm with the range. 18.5 is bigger than 18.4, so it is outside the limits.", "why": "A size is only acceptable if it is no more than the upper limit and no less than the lower limit."}], "answer": "Limits are 17.8 mm (lower) and 18.4 mm (upper). A hole of 18.5 mm is above the upper limit, so it is not acceptable."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the lower limit found by doing 18 − 0.2 in the worked example?", "o": ["The lower limit is always found by taking the smaller number from the larger one", "The minus value says how far below the stated size the hole may be", "The tolerance must be taken off both ends of the size", "The 0.2 is smaller than 0.4, so it must be subtracted"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong: both limits start from the size. The minus sign tells you to subtract; which number is larger does not decide it.", "Correct. The −0.2 allows the hole to be up to 0.2 mm smaller than 18 mm, so you take it off to find the smallest allowed size.", "Wrong: the plus value is added for the upper limit. Only the minus value is taken off, and only for the lower limit.", "Wrong: size of the number does not decide. If the minus value were 0.5 you would still subtract it for the lower limit."]}, "faded": {"task": "A rod is drawn as 50 mm, +0.1 mm and −0.3 mm. Find the upper and lower limits of its length.", "given": ["Size 50 mm, plus value 0.1 mm, minus value 0.3 mm.", "Upper limit = 50 + 0.1 = 50.1 mm."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Lower limit = 50 − 0.1 = 49.9 mm", "Lower limit = 50 − 0.1 − 0.3 = 49.6 mm", "Lower limit = 50 − 0.3 = 49.7 mm", "Lower limit = 50 + 0.3 = 50.3 mm"], "a": 2, "f": ["This uses the plus value (0.1) by mistake. The lower limit uses the minus value, which is 0.3 mm here.", "This takes off both values. Each limit uses only one value: the lower limit uses just the minus value.", "Correct. The minus value 0.3 mm is taken off the size, so the lower limit is 49.7 mm. The range is 49.7 mm to 50.1 mm.", "This adds the minus value, which would give a size above 50 mm. A minus value always makes the lower limit smaller than the size."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the signs and getting the wrong range, such as 59.7 to 60.1 for 60 mm, +0.3 and −0.1. Add the plus value for the upper limit. Subtract the minus value for the lower limit.", "recap": "Upper limit = size + plus value; lower limit = size − minus value; a part passes only if its size is between them."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.3.9", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to explain how accurate marking out, templates, jigs and nesting cut waste and cost when a product is made, in school or in industry.", "hook": {"q": "You must cut ten identical shapes from one sheet of card. Which layout leaves the most usable card for other jobs?", "o": ["Spread the shapes out so they cover the whole sheet evenly", "Put the shapes in the middle of the sheet and leave the edges blank", "Fit the shapes close together, starting in one corner of the sheet", "Scatter the shapes at random, whichever way they happen to land"], "a": 2, "f": ["Spreading them out leaves many small gaps. These scraps are too small to use again, so more card is wasted.", "Shapes in the middle leave a ring of card round the edge. That is awkward to reuse, so more is wasted.", "Right. Close shapes leave one big usable piece of card. This is the idea behind nesting.", "Random placing leaves uneven gaps and small scraps. It has no plan to save material."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Mark out accurately", "b": "Accurate **marking out** means fewer spoiled parts. Measure every point from one **reference point** (a datum edge) so errors do not add up. Use a sharp pencil, scriber or marking knife, and a try square for right angles. Cut on the **waste side** of the line so the part keeps its size."}, {"h": "Templates and jigs", "b": "A **template** is a shape you draw around. It gives matching outlines without measuring each one. A **jig** holds the work and guides the tool, so every part is cut or drilled in the same place. Both suit batch production: parts match, there are fewer mistakes, and less material is scrapped."}, {"h": "Nesting shapes", "b": "**Nesting** means fitting shapes close together on the sheet, so less is left over as **waste**. Leave a small gap for the **width of the cut**. Less waste means fewer sheets bought, so the cost falls. Plan the cuts so that large offcuts stay usable."}], "example": {"task": "A workshop makes 200 identical plywood key fobs from large sheets. Explain how nesting and a template help to reduce waste and cost. [4 marks]", "steps": [{"do": "Nesting means the fob outlines are marked close together across the sheet.", "why": "Little sheet is left unused between shapes, so less material becomes waste."}, {"do": "A small gap is left between the outlines for the width of the saw cut.", "why": "The blade removes some material, so each fob stays full size and no neighbour is cut into."}, {"do": "More fobs fit on each sheet, so fewer sheets are needed for 200 fobs.", "why": "This links less waste to the money saved: lower material cost."}, {"do": "A card template is drawn around for every outline.", "why": "All the fobs match, with fewer marking-out errors, so fewer parts are scrapped and wasted."}], "answer": "Nesting fits the fob outlines close together, leaving a small gap for the saw cut. This leaves less waste, so more fobs fit on each sheet and fewer sheets are bought, which lowers cost. A template makes every outline identical, so fewer parts are scrapped."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say to leave a small gap between nested shapes?", "o": ["So the layout looks neater when it is marked out", "So the blade has room to cut without damaging the next shape", "So that more waste is made for the school to recycle", "So each shape can be cut slightly larger than the template"], "a": 1, "f": ["Neatness is not the reason. The gap exists because the cut removes material, and a neat look earns no marks here.", "Right. The saw removes a strip of material. The gap stops the cut eating into a neighbouring shape.", "Making more waste is the opposite of the aim. Nesting is used to reduce waste.", "Shapes should match the template exactly. The gap is for the cut and does not change the size."]}, "faded": {"task": "A school cuts 40 identical round MDF coasters from one sheet. Explain how to reduce waste when marking out.", "given": ["Step 1: Make a card template of one coaster, so every outline is the same size."], "q": "What is the best next step to reduce waste?", "o": ["Draw circles near the sheet edges and leave the middle empty for later", "Draw circles touching exactly, with no gap, so nothing is lost in cutting", "Draw circles well apart so the saw never gets near the next coaster", "Draw circles close together in rows, leaving a small gap for the saw cut"], "a": 3, "f": ["This leaves a large, awkward piece in the middle. Nesting means packing shapes close together in one area.", "Touching shapes forget the width of the cut. The blade would remove part of a coaster and spoil it.", "Wide gaps create more waste, so more material and more cost. The gap only needs to cover the cut.", "Right. Close rows cut waste, and the small gap allows for the width of the saw cut."]}, "trap": "Saying nesting is used because it is quicker or looks neater. The main reason is less waste and lower material cost. Also remember the small gap for the cut.", "recap": "Nesting fits shapes close together, with a small gap for the cut, so there is less waste and a lower material cost."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "mat-tb", "kind": "idea", "why": "Timber questions often ask you to choose a timber or board for a product and justify it, and the marks come from linking a property to the use.", "hook": {"q": "A wide table top is made from one solid plank. The room's air keeps changing from damp to dry. What is most likely to happen?", "o": ["It stays exactly the same size and shape", "It swells and shrinks, mostly across the grain, and may warp", "It shrinks equally in every direction and stays flat", "Nothing, because cut wood is no longer a living material"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wood is not fixed in size. It takes in and loses moisture as the air changes, so its size changes.", "Right. Solid timber moves with moisture, mostly across the grain. This is why wide solid planks can warp, and why boards were developed.", "Timber does not move equally. It moves much more across the grain than along it, which is what causes warping.", "Cut timber still takes in and loses moisture. Being 'dead' does not stop it moving."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Hardwood and softwood", "b": "**Hardwoods** come from broad-leaved trees, such as oak, ash and beech. **Softwoods** come from conifers, such as pine, spruce and larch. The tree decides the group, not how hard the wood feels. Many hardwoods grow slowly and cost more. Softwoods grow faster and are usually cheaper."}, {"h": "Natural timber properties", "b": "Natural timber has **grain**. It is strong along the grain and weaker across it. It takes in and loses moisture, so it can **warp** or split. Oak is hard, tough and durable. Pine is light, easy to work and cheap, but needs a **finish** outdoors."}, {"h": "Manufactured boards", "b": "**Manufactured boards** are made from veneers, fibres or chips, in large flat sheets. **Plywood**: thin layers glued with each layer's grain at 90° to the next, so it is strong and resists warping. **MDF**: fine fibres bonded with resin, no grain, smooth, takes paint well, swells if wet. Chipboard: chips and resin, cheap."}, {"h": "Matching timber to use", "b": "Link a **property** to the use. Outdoors, choose a timber that resists rot, such as a hardwood with natural oils, or a **treated** softwood. In dry rooms indoors, boards such as MDF are fine. Large flat panels suit manufactured boards. State the property, then say why it helps."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why MDF is a suitable material for the painted doors of a flat-pack bedroom wardrobe. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "MDF is made from fine wood fibres bonded with resin and pressed into sheets.", "why": "It starts with what the material is, which the later points rely on."}, {"do": "This means it has no grain or knots, so its surface is smooth and the same everywhere.", "why": "It links the way MDF is made to a property."}, {"do": "So paint gives an even finish, with no grain pattern showing through.", "why": "It ties the property to the use in the question: painted doors."}, {"do": "It comes in large flat sheets of even thickness, so a wide door can be cut from one piece, and it costs less than hardwood.", "why": "A second property, linked to the product and to cost."}, {"do": "A dry bedroom suits MDF, because MDF swells if it gets wet.", "why": "It shows the weakness does not matter here, which supports the choice."}], "answer": "MDF is made of wood fibres and resin, so it has no grain. Its smooth surface takes paint evenly. It comes in large flat sheets, so wide doors are cut from one piece cheaply. A dry bedroom avoids its weakness to water."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say MDF has no grain?", "o": ["Because no grain makes MDF waterproof, so it suits any room", "Because no grain makes MDF a hardwood, so it is very strong", "Because no grain can show through paint, so the finish is smooth and even", "Because no grain lets MDF be bent along the grain into curves"], "a": 2, "f": ["MDF is not waterproof. It swells if it gets wet. Having no grain does not change that.", "MDF is not a tree type, so it is neither hardwood nor softwood. Grain absence does not make it strong.", "Right. With no grain pattern or knots, paint sits on a uniform surface. This links the property to painted doors.", "With no grain there is no 'along the grain' to bend. MDF is not suited to bending into tight curves."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why pine is a suitable choice for the frame of a low-cost garden shed. (4 marks)", "given": ["Pine is a softwood, from conifers that grow quickly.", "So it is cheap and easy to buy in standard sizes."], "q": "Which point should come next?", "o": ["It is easy to cut and join, and preservative or paint protects it outdoors", "It is a hardwood, so it is hard and resists rot without any treatment", "It has no grain, so it cannot warp or split in wet weather", "It is made of layers glued at 90°, so it is strong in all directions"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. This gives a second property (easy to work) and deals with the outdoor use by naming a protective finish.", "This contradicts the answer so far. Pine is a softwood, and untreated softwood can rot outdoors.", "Having no grain describes MDF, not pine. Pine is natural timber, so it has grain and can warp or split.", "This describes plywood. Pine is solid timber and is not built up from layers."], "x": ""}, "trap": "Judging hardwood or softwood by how hard the timber feels. The tree type decides it. Balsa is a hardwood and yew is a softwood, so name the tree type.", "recap": "Hardwoods come from broad-leaved trees, softwoods from conifers; MDF has no grain, plywood layers cross at 90°, and justify choices by linking property to use."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "nea-A", "kind": "writing", "why": "Part A is worth 10 of the 100 NEA marks, and your whole project depends on the user need and design opportunity you find here.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Choose a challenge", "b": "AQA releases several **contextual challenges**. Each is a broad context, not a product to copy. You choose one. Pick a context where you can reach real people to talk to and watch. Then narrow it down to one **user** and a problem they face."}, {"h": "Investigate your user", "b": "Find out your user's **needs** (what the product must do) and **wants** (what they would like). Use **primary research**, which you collect yourself: interviews, surveys, observing the user, or testing. Record what you actually found, with numbers or quotes where you can."}, {"h": "Study existing products", "b": "Use **secondary research** too, such as websites, books and reviews. Look at **existing products** that already try to solve the problem. For each one, say what works and what fails for your user. Link every comment back to the user's needs."}, {"h": "End with an opportunity", "b": "Finish by pulling your findings together into one **design opportunity**: a gap that a new product could fill, backed by your **evidence**. Stop there. The brief and specification are part B, and ideas come later."}], "example": {"task": "A student chooses a challenge about helping people at home. Build a model Part A write-up for their user: their grandfather, who has arthritis in his hands.", "steps": [{"do": "I chose the home-living challenge because I can visit my user easily. My user is my grandfather, aged 76. He has arthritis in both hands and lives alone.", "why": "It names the challenge, gives a reason for the choice, and picks one specific user rather than \"people\"."}, {"do": "I interviewed him and watched him make a drink. He struggles to twist jar lids. He said he wants to stay independent and does not want equipment that looks medical.", "why": "This is primary research. It shows a need (low grip strength) and a want (ordinary look) from the user."}, {"do": "I tested three jar openers. A rubber pad is cheap but needs a hard squeeze. A wall-mounted one needs little grip but cannot be moved. An electric one needs no grip but is bulky. None suits weak hands, fits a drawer and looks ordinary.", "why": "It analyses strengths and weaknesses of existing products, and links each one to the user's needs."}, {"do": "My research shows a gap: a jar opener that needs very little grip, is small enough for a drawer, and does not look medical. This is my design opportunity.", "why": "It joins the evidence into one clear opportunity. It does not drift into a brief or design ideas."}], "answer": "A strong Part A names a challenge and one specific user, uses your own research, analyses existing products against the user's needs, and ends with one evidence-based design opportunity. It stops before the brief and designs."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 include that he does not want equipment that looks medical?", "o": ["It lists a material that the final product must be made from", "It shows the research was secondary, because it came from a source", "It records a want as well as a need, using evidence from the user", "It proves the finished jar opener will sell well in shops"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Materials belong in the specification and design work later. This comment records what the user feels, not a material.", "Wrong. The grandfather was interviewed by the student, so this is primary research. Secondary research is information collected by other people.", "Right. A need is what the product must do. A want is what the user would like. Here it is a want, taken from the user himself.", "Wrong. Part A finds user needs and opportunities. It does not predict sales, and nothing has been made yet."]}, "faded": {"task": "A student chose a challenge about school life. Their user is their brother, aged 11, who walks to school with a heavy bag. Finish the Part A write-up.", "given": ["I chose the school-life challenge. My user is my brother, aged 11, who walks 1 km to school each day.", "I interviewed him and watched him pack. His bag is heavy and he cannot find small items such as his pencil case."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to earn marks in Part A?", "o": ["I will design a bag with a light frame and four labelled pockets for his small items.", "I compared three bags. One has many pockets but is heavy, so it meets only one need.", "Most school bags are made of polyester or nylon and have two shoulder straps.", "The bag must weigh less than 1 kg and cost under £30 to make."], "a": 1, "f": ["This jumps to a design idea. Ideas come later in the project, after the brief and specification. Part A is for investigating.", "Right. It examines existing products and links their strengths and weaknesses to the user's needs: weight and finding small items.", "This is general information. It does not judge any product against your user's needs, so it earns little.", "This is a specification point. Writing the specification is part B, and it should come after the investigation is finished."]}, "trap": "Jumping ahead to design ideas, a brief or making. Part A is only for choosing a challenge, investigating the user and products, and ending with a design opportunity backed by evidence.", "recap": "Part A: choose a challenge, investigate one real user and existing products with your own research, then state an evidence-based design opportunity."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "nea-B", "kind": "writing", "why": "Your design brief and specification earn up to 10 marks in your NEA, and you test your prototype against the specification in your evaluation.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What a design brief does", "b": "A **design brief** is a short statement of what you will design, who it is for and where it will be used. It comes from a problem found in your research. It does not name the final solution, so you still have room for **design ideas**."}, {"h": "Measurable specification points", "b": "A **specification** is a list of requirements your prototype must meet. Make each point **measurable** so you can test it. Use numbers, units and tolerances. 'Looks good' cannot be tested. 'Weighs under 50 g' can."}, {"h": "Add a reason", "b": "Add a **reason** to each point, starting 'because' or 'so'. Link it to your **research**: a survey, measurements or existing products. The reason shows why you chose that number. Points with reasons are stronger than bare numbers."}, {"h": "Cover a spread of areas", "b": "Cover a spread of areas: **function**, size and weight, **safety**, materials, cost, and appearance. Even appearance can be measured, for example 'uses no more than two colours'. Do not write six points about size alone."}], "example": {"task": "Research shows Year 7 pupils' wired earphones tangle in school bags. Write a design brief and a specification with three points, each with a reason.", "steps": [{"do": "Brief: 'Design and make a holder for Year 7 pupils to keep wired earphones untangled in a school bag, so they can be used quickly between lessons.'", "why": "It names the user, setting and problem from research. It does not fix the solution."}, {"do": "Function: 'The holder must store at least 1.2 m of cable without knots, because most pupils in the survey use wired earphones with a cable about that long.'", "why": "A length can be measured, so it can be tested. The reason comes from research."}, {"do": "Size and weight: 'It must be no larger than 60 × 60 × 20 mm and weigh under 50 g, so it fits in a pencil case.'", "why": "Numbers with units can be checked with a rule and scales. The reason links to where users keep it."}, {"do": "Safety: 'All edges must be rounded to a radius of at least 2 mm, because 11-year-olds will handle it daily and keep it in a bag with other items.'", "why": "A radius can be checked. The reason names the user and how it will be used."}], "answer": "The brief names the product, user, setting and problem without fixing the solution. Each specification point is measurable (1.2 m, 60 × 60 × 20 mm, under 50 g, 2 mm radius) and has a 'because' or 'so' reason from research."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the size point in step 3 end with 'so it fits in a pencil case'?", "o": ["It makes the point measurable, because a pencil case is a standard size", "It gives a reason from the users' habits, showing why those numbers were chosen", "It replaces the need for numbers, as the pencil case sets the limit", "It tells the maker which material and tools to use for the holder"], "a": 1, "f": ["The numbers and units make the point measurable. Pencil cases vary in size, so they cannot act as the test.", "The reason links the numbers to how users will carry the product, which makes the point justified, not just measurable.", "The numbers are still needed. Without them you cannot check the prototype with a rule. The reason adds to the numbers.", "A reason explains why a requirement matters. It does not choose materials or tools. Those come later, in design development."]}, "faded": {"task": "A survey shows older users struggle to open screw-top jars because small lids are hard to grip. Write a design brief and a specification with three points, each with a reason.", "steps": [{"do": "Brief: 'Design and make a product that helps older users open screw-top jars in their kitchen, because many in the survey find small lids hard to grip.'", "why": "It names the user, setting and problem, and leaves room for different design ideas."}, {"do": "Function: 'It must open lids from 40 mm to 80 mm in diameter, because these were the jar sizes users said they struggle with.'", "why": "A diameter range can be measured and tested on real jars."}], "q": "Which is the best size and weight point to write next?", "o": ["It must be light and small enough for older users to hold easily", "It must weigh under 150 g and be no longer than 200 mm in total", "It must weigh under 150 g, so users with weak hands can hold it in one hand", "It must be a rubber grip pad, as rubber is the best material for this"], "a": 2, "f": ["'Light and small' has no numbers or units, so it cannot be tested against the prototype.", "The numbers can be tested, but there is no reason linked to research, so the point is weaker.", "It gives a measurable limit with a unit, plus a reason linked to the users from the research.", "This fixes the solution and the material too early, and 'best' cannot be measured or tested."]}, "trap": "Writing vague points such as 'looks good' or 'is cheap', or bare numbers with no reason. Vague points cannot be tested against your prototype. Points without reasons show no link to your research.", "recap": "A clear brief states the user, setting and problem. Each specification point is measurable, can be tested on the prototype and is justified by research."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "nea-C", "kind": "writing", "why": "Generating design ideas is worth 20 of the 100 NEA marks, awarded for how varied and creative your ideas are and how well you explain them.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "A range, not a repeat", "b": "Marks go to a **range** of ideas that are genuinely different. Change the **form**, how it works, the materials or how it is made. A new colour or a slightly larger size is not a new idea. Aim for ideas that a user could really choose between."}, {"h": "Use more than one method", "b": "Show ideas through different **communication** methods: quick 2D sketches, 3D sketches, different views, CAD, or a simple card **model**. Use colour or shading to show materials. Pick the method that best shows each idea. A model can check size or shape before you choose."}, {"h": "Annotate against the spec", "b": "Notes must explain, not just label. Link a feature to a **specification** point or a **user need**, and give a reason. Use measurements, materials and making methods. Words like \"because\" and \"so that\" turn a label into an explanation."}, {"h": "Show your thinking", "b": "Good annotation also shows judgement. Note one possible **weakness**, or say how an idea could be **developed** or tested. This shows you are thinking critically. It also helps you choose the best idea later, with evidence."}], "example": {"task": "Write the annotations for one idea: a desk tidy for a Year 7 student. Spec: holds pens and a phone, fits a 150 mm wide shelf, made in a school workshop.", "steps": [{"do": "Idea B: a wedge-shaped tidy with a sloping slot at the front for a phone and three pen tubes behind.", "why": "It names the idea and its form, so the reader sees how it differs from the other ideas."}, {"do": "At 140 mm wide, it fits the 150 mm shelf with room to spare.", "why": "It links a feature to a spec point using a measurement as evidence."}, {"do": "The slope keeps the screen facing the user, so they can read it without picking the phone up.", "why": "It explains the benefit to the user, using \"so\" to give a reason."}, {"do": "It is made from 6 mm plywood, laser cut and slot-jointed. Ply is stiff and cuts cleanly.", "why": "It shows the idea can be made in school and gives a reason for the material."}, {"do": "The pen tubes may be too shallow, so a card model could test the depth.", "why": "It shows critical thinking and a way to develop the idea."}], "answer": "Idea B: a wedge-shaped tidy with a sloping phone slot and three pen tubes. At 140 mm wide, it fits the 150 mm shelf. The slope keeps the screen facing the user, so it can be read without lifting. Laser-cut, slot-jointed 6 mm ply is stiff and cuts cleanly. A card model could test tube depth."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the second annotation include the measurement \"140 mm\"?", "o": ["It makes the annotation longer, and longer notes always earn more marks.", "It is the size every desk tidy must be, so the examiner expects it.", "A measurement shows the idea meets a spec point, so the claim is backed by evidence.", "It proves this idea is better than the other ideas on the sheet."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Length earns nothing by itself. Marks come from useful explanation, and here the number is useful because it supports a claim.", "Wrong. There is no standard size for desk tidies. The 140 mm comes from this spec's 150 mm shelf, not from a rule.", "Right. The number connects the idea to the 150 mm shelf in the spec. It turns a vague claim into evidence.", "Wrong. One measurement does not compare ideas. It only shows this idea meets one spec point."]}, "faded": {"task": "Annotate Idea C: a wall-mounted rack for a teenager's bedroom. Spec: saves floor space, holds 1 kg, easy to clean.", "given": ["Idea C: a wall rack with one shelf and two hooks, so it takes up no floor space.", "The smooth, wipe-clean varnished surface makes it easy to clean."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to show the idea can carry a 1 kg load?", "o": ["It would look modern painted blue, a colour many teenagers say they like.", "9 mm ply is stiff enough to hold 1 kg, and two wall-plugged screws fix it to the wall.", "It will be strong because it is made from a strong material.", "Wall racks like this cost about £10 in shops, so mine will be cheap too."], "a": 1, "f": ["This is about appearance. It does not address the 1 kg spec point, so it earns nothing here.", "Right. It names a material and thickness, and the fixing method. It gives a reason that links directly to the 1 kg spec point.", "This is vague and circular. It says \"strong\" without naming the material or giving a reason.", "This is about cost and research, not the load. It does not show how this idea meets the 1 kg spec point."]}, "trap": "Drawing one idea again with small changes, or labelling a sketch (\"handle\", \"base\") without explaining it. Instead, make ideas truly different and explain each feature with a reason linked to the spec or user.", "recap": "Show several genuinely different ideas, and annotate each one to explain how it meets the specification and the user's needs."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "nea-D", "kind": "idea", "why": "Developing design ideas is worth 20 marks of your NEA, awarded for evidence that you tested your idea, made changes and can explain why.", "hook": {"q": "You have chosen a phone stand design. What is the quickest, cheapest way to check its angle and size before cutting wood?", "o": ["Cut the final stand from hardwood and see whether it works", "Make a card mock-up and rest a real phone on it", "Colour in the drawing so the design looks finished", "Write the evaluation of the stand before making it"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. This is realising, not developing. Mistakes would cost time and material, and a fault would mean starting again.", "Right. A cheap model lets you check angle and size, and you can change it in minutes.", "Wrong. A neat drawing is presentation. It cannot show whether a real phone sits at a good angle.", "Wrong. An evaluation comes after making. It does not test anything, so it cannot improve the design."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Model your idea", "b": "Developing means trying your chosen idea out before you make it. Build a **model**: a card mock-up, a foam shape or a **CAD** model. Each one answers a question about size, shape, fit or feel. Models are quick, cheap and easy to change."}, {"h": "Test it fairly", "b": "Test your model with **target users** and against your specification. Change **one thing at a time**, so you know which change made the difference. Use measurements where you can, such as **anthropometric data** for grip or reach. Record every result."}, {"h": "Refine and explain", "b": "To **refine** is to change the design because of a test. Each change needs a chain: result, problem, change, reason. Add **annotations** and photos of your models. A change with no reason earns few marks."}, {"h": "End with a makeable design", "b": "Keep developing until the design is **fit to be made**. Sizes, materials and parts are decided and backed by your tests. Early sketches are **generating ideas**. The final prototype is **realising**. Developing sits between them."}], "example": {"task": "A student has chosen a design for a desk tidy to hold pens. Explain how they should develop this idea before making the final product. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Make a full-size card mock-up of the desk tidy.", "why": "A cheap model shows size and shape before any expensive material is used."}, {"do": "Ask three target users to put pens in and take them out. Pens tip over because the tidy is only 60 mm deep.", "why": "Testing with real users gives evidence, not just opinion."}, {"do": "Record the result with a photo and a note: 'Pens tip out. Tidy too shallow.'", "why": "Recording links the later change to evidence."}, {"do": "Change only the depth, from 60 mm to 90 mm, because the pens are 140 mm long and need more support.", "why": "One change, with a reason, shows a justified refinement."}, {"do": "Make a new card model and retest it with the same users.", "why": "Retesting shows whether the change worked."}], "answer": "Make a full-size card model, test it with target users, and record the result. The pens tipped over, so the tidy was too shallow. Increase the depth from 60 mm to 90 mm and give the reason. Retest a new model to check the fault is fixed."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer retest a new card model after deepening the tidy?", "o": ["Because every project must show at least three models", "Because the first test was invalid and has to be repeated", "To check the change fixed the fault and did not cause a new one", "Because the final prototype cannot be made until the card model is thrown away"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. There is no rule about a number of models. Each model is made because a test showed a problem to fix.", "Wrong. The first test was valid and found a real fault. Retesting checks the change, not the original test.", "Right. A change is only a good refinement if testing shows it worked. A deeper tidy might, for example, be too bulky.", "Wrong. Models are not thrown away. Keep them as evidence. Retesting is about checking the new design."]}, "faded": {"task": "A student models a child's storage box in foam. Three children from the target group try to open it. Two cannot lift the lid.", "given": ["Step 1: Make a full-size foam model of the box.", "Step 2: Ask three children from the target group to open the lid.", "Step 3: Record the result: 'Lid too tight. 2 of 3 children could not lift it.'"], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["Make the final box in plywood now, as the foam model has done its job", "Redraw the box in colour and add a title block to the drawing", "Remove the lid from the specification, since the children could not open it", "Add a finger recess to the lid, note why, then test a new foam model"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. A known fault would be carried into the final product. Developing means fixing faults and retesting first.", "Wrong. Presentation does not solve the problem. A neat drawing shows no refinement.", "Wrong. Changing the specification to fit a fault avoids the problem. Fix the design to meet the need.", "Right. It makes a justified change linked to the test result, then retests it. This is the full chain that earns marks."]}, "trap": "Confusing developing with generating or realising. Early sketches are generating ideas, and the final prototype is realising. A card or foam mock-up that tests size and shape is developing, as long as you record the changes.", "recap": "Developing means model, test fairly, record the result, make a reasoned change, and retest until the design can be made."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "nea-E", "kind": "method", "why": "Your teacher marks this NEA section out of 20 for safe, skilful tool use and accurate work within tolerances, backed up by your making record.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Prepare yourself first", "b": "Before you go near a machine, **tie back hair** and remove anything loose, such as ties, jewellery and dangling sleeves. Put on **eye protection**. A spinning bit can catch loose items and pull them in. Chips can also fly up."}, {"h": "Set up the tool and work", "b": "Check the **guards** are in place. Fit the right bit, tighten the chuck, remove the chuck key and set a suitable speed. Then **clamp** the work in a vice or clamp. Never hold it by hand, because the bit can catch it and spin it."}, {"h": "Work to tolerances", "b": "A **tolerance** is the variation in size you can accept. 30 mm ± 1 mm means anything from 29 mm to 31 mm. Mark out carefully, work steadily, then measure. A **template** or jig helps you make identical parts."}, {"h": "Check and record as you go", "b": "Measure the part against your drawing during making, not only at the end. These **quality checks** catch errors early. If a part is outside the tolerance, correct it if you can. Photograph the checks in your **making record** as evidence of accuracy."}], "example": {"task": "Drill an 8 mm hole in a 100 mm × 60 mm piece of pine, 30 mm ± 1 mm from one edge, using a pillar drill. Set out how you prepare, set up, drill and check.", "steps": [{"do": "Tie back long hair. Remove your tie, jewellery and anything loose. Roll up sleeves and put on eye protection.", "why": "Loose items can be pulled into the spinning bit, and chips can fly into your eyes."}, {"do": "Mark the hole 30 mm from the edge with a rule and try square. Draw a cross, then make a small dent at its centre with a bradawl.", "why": "The cross shows the exact centre. The dent stops the bit wandering at the start."}, {"do": "Fit the 8 mm bit and tighten the chuck. Remove the chuck key. Set a slower speed than you would for a small bit.", "why": "A loose bit slips. A key left in the chuck can be thrown out. Larger bits need slower speeds."}, {"do": "Clamp the pine in a machine vice with scrap wood underneath. With the machine off, lower the bit to line it up with the dent. Check the guard is in place.", "why": "Clamping stops the work spinning. Lining up puts the hole in the right place. Scrap wood gives a clean exit hole."}, {"do": "Switch on. Lower the bit steadily, keeping your hands clear. Switch off and wait for the bit to stop before you touch anything.", "why": "A steady feed gives a cleaner, more accurate hole. Hands stay away from moving parts."}, {"do": "Unclamp the wood. Measure from the edge of the wood to the near side of the hole, then add 4 mm (half the 8 mm hole). Check the total is between 29 mm and 31 mm.", "why": "This checks the hole against the tolerance. Photograph the check for your making record."}], "answer": "Prepare yourself, set up the drill, clamp the work and check the guard. Drill steadily, then measure the hole from the edge. It must be 29 mm to 31 mm away to be within tolerance."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example clamp the pine in a vice instead of holding it by hand?", "o": ["Clamping makes the hole bigger, so it is easier to keep within tolerance", "The bit can catch the wood and spin it, which could injure your hand", "Once the wood is clamped, you no longer need the guard or eye protection", "Hand-holding is safe if the piece is large and you wear eye protection"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Clamping does not change the hole size. It holds the work still so the position stays accurate. The size comes from the bit you choose.", "Correct. A drill bit can grab the work and spin it. A clamp or vice holds it still and keeps your hands away from the bit.", "Wrong. Clamping is one safety step, not a replacement for others. You still need the guard and eye protection every time you use the machine.", "Wrong. Size does not make hand-holding safe. A large piece can still be spun by the bit, and goggles do not protect your hands."]}, "faded": {"task": "You will drill a 6 mm hole in a piece of plywood on a pillar drill. Choose the next step.", "given": ["Tie back hair, remove loose items, put on eye protection.", "Mark the hole centre with a cross and make a small dent with a bradawl.", "Fit the 6 mm bit and tighten the chuck."], "q": "What should you do next, before switching the machine on?", "o": ["Hold the plywood flat with one hand and switch on, as the hole is small", "Switch on to test the speed, then clamp the plywood while the bit spins", "Remove the guard so that you can see the mark, then switch on", "Remove the chuck key, set a suitable speed, clamp the plywood and check the guard"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. A small hole is just as risky. The bit can catch the plywood and spin it, so it must be clamped, not held.", "Wrong. Never reach near a spinning bit. Clamp the work and set up everything while the machine is off and has stopped.", "Wrong. The guard is there to protect you. Check it is in place and working. Do not remove it to see better.", "Correct. Remove the chuck key, set the speed, clamp the work and check the guard, all before switching on."]}, "trap": "Holding the workpiece by hand instead of clamping it. The bit can catch it and spin it. Clamp the work in a vice or clamp every time, even for a quick, small hole.", "recap": "Before using any machine, tie back hair, wear eye protection, check settings and guards and clamp the work, then measure your result against the tolerance."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "nea-F", "kind": "writing", "why": "Analysing and evaluating is worth 20 marks in your NEA, and high marks need test evidence, user feedback and specific improvements.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Test every spec point", "b": "Go through your **specification** one point at a time. For each point, choose a **test** that gives evidence: measure it, count, load it, time it or use it. Write what you did and what happened. Use numbers where you can."}, {"h": "Gather user feedback", "b": "Ask your **client** or target users to try the prototype. Give each person the same questions and record what they say. Feedback shows things tests miss, such as comfort or looks. Treat it as **evidence** alongside your own test results."}, {"h": "Judge each point", "b": "Decide whether each point is **met**, **partly met** or **not met**. Base the decision on your results, not on how you feel. If a test fails some of the time, the point is not fully met, even if most users were happy."}, {"h": "Suggest improvements", "b": "Each **improvement** should be specific, linked to a test result, practical to make and **retested**. Say what you would change, by how much and how you would check it worked. 'Make it better' earns no marks."}], "example": {"task": "Write an evaluation paragraph for one specification point of your prototype desk tidy: 'It must not tip over when full of pens and knocked lightly.' Use test results and feedback.", "steps": [{"do": "Specification point 4: the tidy must stay upright when knocked. I filled it with six pens and gave the top edge five light pushes.", "why": "It names the spec point and the test, so the marker can see exactly what is being checked."}, {"do": "It tipped twice out of five pushes.", "why": "This is a result, not an opinion. Counting makes it clear and checkable."}, {"do": "My client said it feels top-heavy. This point is only partly met.", "why": "User feedback supports the test. The judgement follows from the evidence."}, {"do": "I would widen the base from 90 mm to 120 mm, then repeat the test.", "why": "The change is specific, linked to the fault, and includes a retest to check it worked."}], "answer": "Specification point 4: the tidy must stay upright when knocked. I filled it with six pens and gave the top edge five light pushes. It tipped twice. My client said it feels top-heavy. This point is only partly met. I would widen the base from 90 mm to 120 mm, then repeat the test."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer say the point is 'only partly met'?", "o": ["Three out of five passed, and a majority is enough to meet a point", "It tipped in two tests, so the point is not met every time", "The client's comment alone decides whether a point is met", "An evaluation must always say that a point is only partly met"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. A spec point needs to be met reliably. A majority of passes still leaves failures, so the point is not fully met.", "Right. The test shows it failed some of the time. A point that fails sometimes cannot be called fully met.", "Wrong. The client's view is useful evidence, but it supports the test results. It does not replace them.", "Wrong. Judgements must come from the evidence. A point can be fully met if every test passes."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate a card phone stand against this spec point: 'It must hold a phone at 60° to 70° from flat.' Choose the next sentence of your evaluation.", "given": ["Spec point 2: the stand must hold a phone at 60° to 70° from flat.", "I measured the angle with a protractor, with the phone in place, three times."], "q": "Which sentence should come next?", "o": ["The stand looks good and the phone sits at a nice angle.", "I think most users would find the angle fine, so it meets the point.", "The angle was 64°, 65° and 64°, which is inside the 60° to 70° range.", "I would improve the stand by making the angle better for the user."], "a": 2, "f": ["This is opinion. 'Looks good' and 'nice' give no measured result, so it earns little credit.", "This is a guess about users, not evidence. The next step needs the results of your protractor tests.", "Right. It gives the measured results and compares them with the spec range, so the judgement is backed by evidence.", "This jumps to a vague improvement before stating any result. 'Better' is not specific or linked to a test."]}, "trap": "Writing only 'it works well' or 'I am pleased with it'. That is opinion. Name the spec point, quote a test result or user comment, then give a specific improvement.", "recap": "For each spec point, give test evidence and user feedback, judge whether it is met, then suggest a specific improvement you would retest."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p1-q1", "kind": "method", "why": "Question 1 opens Paper 1 and is worth 4 marks, and it only tests whether you can find what the opening lines clearly say.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What Q1 tests", "b": "Q1 tests whether you can find **explicit information**. That means facts the text states directly. It only uses the **opening lines** of the extract. You do not need to analyse anything or explain what the writer means."}, {"h": "Check every option", "b": "Take each option and look for the **exact words** that match it. If you cannot point to them, the option is wrong. Wrong options often contain **one changed detail**, such as a time, a number, a place or who did what."}, {"h": "Facts, not guesses", "b": "An **inference** is a sensible guess about a feeling or a reason. Q1 does not reward it. Words like \"nervous\", \"angry\" and \"because\" are warning signs. Also watch for small changes, such as \"nearly empty\" becoming \"empty\". Choose only what is **clearly stated**."}], "example": {"task": "Extract: \"The lift was broken, so Omar carried the box up six flights of stairs. At the top he stopped, breathing hard. Behind the door, someone was laughing.\" Which is true?", "steps": [{"do": "Underline the facts: the lift was broken; Omar carries a box up six flights; he stops at the top, breathing hard; someone is laughing behind the door.", "why": "These are the only things you are allowed to use."}, {"do": "Test A, \"Omar takes the lift to the top\". The text says the lift \"was broken\", so A is false.", "why": "It contradicts the exact words."}, {"do": "Test B, \"Omar is scared of whoever is laughing\". The text never mentions fear, so B is false.", "why": "Fear is a guess. Q1 rewards only what is stated."}, {"do": "Test C, \"Omar carries the box up six flights of stairs\". The text says exactly this.", "why": "You can point to the exact words, so C is true."}, {"do": "Test D, \"Someone is laughing in front of the door\". The text says \"behind the door\", so D is false.", "why": "One changed detail makes an option false."}], "answer": "C. Omar carries the box up six flights of stairs. It is the only option that matches the extract's exact words."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is option B rejected, even though Omar might well feel scared?", "o": ["Laughter is a happy sound, so Omar cannot be scared", "Breathing hard shows he is tired, not scared", "Fear can only be shown in the middle of a text", "The extract never says he is scared, and Q1 only rewards stated facts"], "a": 3, "f": ["This is still a guess, just the opposite one. The text does not tell you how Omar reacts to the laughter.", "This is another inference. The text says he is breathing hard but does not say why.", "There is no such rule. B is rejected because the text does not state fear anywhere.", "Correct. B is an inference. Q1 only accepts what the text clearly states, so you need words you can point to."]}, "faded": {"task": "Extract: \"Ella pushed open the café door with her elbow, her hands full of paper cups. Rain dripped from her hood onto the floor. Mr Hale waited by the till, arms folded.\" Which is true?", "given": ["Underline the facts: Ella opens the door with her elbow; her hands are full of paper cups; rain drips from her hood; Mr Hale waits by the till, arms folded.", "Rule out anything that is only a guess about feelings or reasons."], "q": "Next, check each option against the underlined facts. Which option matches them exactly?", "o": ["Mr Hale is angry because Ella is late", "Ella's hands are full of paper cups", "Ella pushes the café door open with her shoulder", "Rain is dripping from Mr Hale's hood"], "a": 1, "f": ["Anger and lateness are both guesses. The text only says Mr Hale waited with his arms folded.", "Correct. The text says \"her hands full of paper cups\", so you can point to the exact words.", "The detail is changed. The text says she used her elbow, not her shoulder.", "The detail is swapped. The rain drips from Ella's hood, not Mr Hale's."]}, "trap": "Choosing an option that sounds likely, such as a character feeling nervous or angry, when the text never says it. Ask: which exact words prove this? If you cannot find any, reject it.", "recap": "Choose only the option you can prove with the exact words of the extract, and reject guesses and changed details."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p1-q3", "kind": "writing", "why": "Q3 is worth 8 marks on Paper 1 and asks how the whole extract is built, which needs a different kind of answer from Q2.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What Q3 is asking", "b": "**Structure** means how the writer orders the **whole extract**: the beginning, the middle and the end. You are asked what the writer focuses on first, how that focus changes, and why. Name the effect on the reader, such as tension, curiosity or unease."}, {"h": "Spot the shifts", "b": "A **shift of focus** is where the writer moves your attention. Look for wide to **close-up**, place to person, outside to inside, calm to action, or past to present. Check the ending too. A sudden stop or a final detail is a structural choice."}, {"h": "Link each shift to effect", "b": "For each shift, write three things: where it happens, what moves to what, and the **effect** on the reader. Try: \"The focus moves from ... to ..., which makes the reader ...\". Move through the extract **in order**, so you cover the start, the middle and the end."}, {"h": "Stay on structure", "b": "Do not retell the **plot**. Do not pick out single words or similes, because that is **language analysis** (Q2). Short quotations can show where a shift happens. Your comment should still be about the order and the movement of the focus."}], "example": {"task": "Q3: How does the writer structure the extract to build tension? Extract: a wide view of a snowy village, then one house, then a girl at a window, ending as a door handle slowly turns.", "steps": [{"do": "The extract opens with a wide view of a silent, snowy village, which creates a calm, still mood.", "why": "It starts at the beginning, names the focus and gives an effect."}, {"do": "The focus then narrows to one house and then to a girl at its window, which makes the reader curious about why she is watching.", "why": "It names a shift, wide to close, and its effect on the reader."}, {"do": "The extract ends on a close-up of the door handle turning, so the reader waits tensely to see who enters.", "why": "It covers the ending and links the final focus to the named effect, tension."}, {"do": "Overall, the steady zoom inwards from the village to one small detail tightens the reader's attention, so tension builds.", "why": "It sums up the whole structure and returns to the question."}], "answer": "The extract opens with a wide view of a silent, snowy village, which feels calm. The focus narrows to one house, then a girl at the window, making us curious. It ends on a close-up of the door handle turning, so tension peaks as we wait. The zoom inwards tightens attention."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 say the focus moves from the village to one house to the girl?", "o": ["Because naming every word in the passage shows close reading of language", "Because tracking where the focus moves shows how the whole extract is ordered", "Because it retells the plot in order, which earns marks for knowing the story", "Because Q3 only asks about the middle of the extract, not the start"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Commenting on words is language analysis (Q2). Q3 wants the movement of focus, not word choices.", "Right. Q3 rewards tracing how the writer moves your attention across the extract, and saying what that movement does.", "Wrong. Retelling the plot is the commonest trap. The step names a shift in focus and its effect, not just what happens.", "Wrong. Q3 covers the whole extract. The beginning and ending matter as much as the middle."]}, "faded": {"task": "Q3: How does the writer structure the extract to create unease? Extract: a crowded station platform, then one suitcase left alone, ending as its clasp clicks open.", "given": ["The extract opens with a wide view of a crowded, noisy platform, which makes the setting feel ordinary and safe."], "q": "Which sentence should come next in a high-scoring answer?", "o": ["The writer calls the suitcase \"abandoned\", which makes it seem lonely and sad.", "A man arrives, buys a ticket and walks past the suitcase before it later opens.", "The focus then narrows from the crowd to one unattended suitcase, which creates unease.", "The writer uses exciting words and makes the reader want to read on to the end."], "a": 2, "f": ["This comments on a single word, which is language (Q2). The question asks how the whole extract is ordered.", "This retells events. Plot summary does not explain structure or name an effect on the reader.", "Right. It names the shift from wide to close-up, follows the order of the extract and links it to the effect, unease.", "This is vague. It names no part of the extract and no shift of focus, so it does not explain structure."]}, "trap": "Retelling the plot or picking out single words. Instead, move through the extract in order and say where the focus shifts and what each shift does to the reader.", "recap": "Move through the extract in order, name each shift of focus, and say what effect it has on the reader."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p1-q4", "kind": "writing", "why": "Q4 is worth 20 marks, the biggest reading question on Paper 1, and asks you to judge a statement about a later part of the extract.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Read the question closely", "b": "The question names **part of the source**, such as the second half. It then gives a **statement** from a student. Your evidence must come from that part only. Underline the key words in the statement, such as 'frightened' or 'exciting'. They tell you what to write about."}, {"h": "Give a clear judgement", "b": "Say **how far** you agree. You can agree, **partly agree** or disagree. Partly agreeing is allowed and often gives you more to say. Put your view in your first sentence. Keep returning to it as you write."}, {"h": "Evidence plus methods", "b": "Back your view with short **quotations**. Then explain the writer's **methods**, such as word choice, sentence length or structure. Say what effect each method has on the reader. A quote with no comment earns little. Retelling the plot earns even less."}, {"h": "Plan a balanced answer", "b": "Choose three or four moments from the named part. Take them in order. For each, give your view, a short quote, the method and its **effect**. Include at least one point that challenges the statement if you can. End with a short **conclusion** that answers it."}], "example": {"task": "Invented extract: Mara waits alone at a night station. Focus on the second half. Statement: 'The writer makes Mara seem frightened.' How far do you agree?", "steps": [{"do": "I partly agree. The writer makes Mara seem frightened for most of the second half, but the ending shows her becoming braver.", "why": "A clear view in the first sentence, using the statement's key word. Partial agreement is allowed."}, {"do": "In the second half, Mara's fear is physical: 'her fingers clawed at the bench'. The verb 'clawed' suggests a desperate grip, so the reader feels her panic and sees how little control she has.", "why": "A short quotation, a named method (verb choice) and its effect on the reader."}, {"do": "The writer also builds tension through structure: 'A door slammed. Then nothing. Then footsteps.' The short sentences and the pause after 'nothing' make the reader wait nervously alongside Mara.", "why": "A second method, structure, with its effect. New evidence keeps the answer varied."}, {"do": "However, the final line, 'She lifted her chin and stepped towards the light', changes the mood. 'Light' suggests hope, and 'lifted her chin' shows she is choosing courage over fear.", "why": "Weighs evidence against the statement, from the end of the section. This is what 'how far' asks for."}, {"do": "So the writer makes Mara frightened for most of this section, but the ending shows her bravery. I therefore only partly agree.", "why": "The conclusion returns to the question and repeats the judgement."}], "answer": "Partly agree: fear is shown through 'clawed' and the short sentences, but the ending's 'light' and 'lifted her chin' show growing courage, so the statement is only partly true."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer use the final line, which goes against the statement, in step 4?", "o": ["Every Q4 answer must end by disagreeing with the statement", "The ending of an extract is always worth more marks than the middle", "Weighing evidence on both sides shows how far you agree with the statement", "Quoting more lines earns marks even if they do not link to the statement"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. You choose your own view. Here it is 'partly agree', and the ending is evidence for the part that disagrees.", "Wrong. No part of the text is worth more marks. Evidence just has to come from the part named and support your view.", "Right. 'How far' asks for a judgement. Using evidence that challenges the statement, and commenting on it, shows you have weighed it up.", "Wrong. Quotes only earn credit when they are relevant and you comment on the writer's methods. Quantity alone does not help."]}, "faded": {"task": "Invented extract: Tom, dared by classmates, walks onto a frozen pond. Focus on the second half. Statement: 'The writer makes the pond seem dangerous.' How far do you agree?", "given": ["I mostly agree: the pond seems dangerous, though Tom's excitement also stands out.", "In the second half, the writer describes the ice with the line 'the surface groaned beneath him'."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to earn the most marks?", "o": ["This shows that the ice is thin and that Tom keeps walking out onto it.", "The verb 'groaned' makes the ice seem alive, so the reader senses it could break.", "Then Tom walks further out and the ice groans again, making the story exciting.", "The opening calls the pond 'silver and still', so it seems peaceful and safe."], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. This only explains what happens. It names no method and does not say how the writer affects the reader.", "Right. It names the method (the verb 'groaned'), explains the image and states the effect on the reader. It also links back to 'dangerous'.", "Wrong. This retells events and does not comment on a method. 'Exciting' also drifts away from the statement about danger.", "Wrong. The question names the second half. Evidence from the opening loses the focus, even if the comment is sensible."]}, "trap": "Writing only 'I agree' and retelling the plot. Give a clear view, use short quotations from the named part, and comment on the writer's methods and their effect on the reader.", "recap": "Give a clear view on how far you agree, then back it with short quotes and comments on the writer's methods."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p1-q5a", "kind": "writing", "why": "Q5 is worth 40 of Paper 1's 80 marks, half the paper, so it deserves about 45 minutes, including planning.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "How Q5 is marked", "b": "Q5 gives **40 marks**. 24 are for **content and organisation**: your ideas, imagery and structure. 16 are for **technical accuracy**: spelling, punctuation, sentence variety and vocabulary. You choose one of two tasks. Pick the one where you can picture a scene clearly."}, {"h": "Plan one scene", "b": "Spend about five minutes on a **plan**. Pick one place and one **moment**. List three or four paragraph ideas. For a description, move from a wide view to a small detail. For a story opening, stay inside one short event, not the whole plot."}, {"h": "Show, don't tell", "b": "Use the **senses**: sound, touch and smell as well as sight. Choose **imagery**, such as a simile or a precise verb. Show feelings through actions and details instead of naming them. Vary your sentence lengths, and use a short one for impact."}, {"h": "Shape and finish", "b": "Give your writing a clear **structure**. Shift the focus between paragraphs, for example from setting to a person to a small detail. A **cyclical** ending, which returns to an image from your opening, feels planned. Leave two minutes to check punctuation and spelling."}], "example": {"task": "Write a description of a railway platform late on a winter night. (The model answer below is the opening of a response, built in four parts.)", "steps": [{"do": "Frost clung to the platform edge. The lone lamp buzzed like a trapped wasp.", "why": "It starts with precise sensory detail (sight, sound) and a simile. It sets one place and mood at once."}, {"do": "Beneath it, a man counted coins into his palm, again and again.", "why": "The focus zooms in from the setting to one person. This adds interest without starting a new plot."}, {"do": "The wind found his thin coat, yet his eyes never left the empty track.", "why": "It shows his situation through a detail and an action. The reader infers he is cold and waiting."}, {"do": "The lamp buzzed on. Far down the line, a pale light stirred.", "why": "The ending returns to the opening lamp, so the structure feels shaped. It stays in the same moment."}], "answer": "Frost clung to the platform edge. The lone lamp buzzed like a trapped wasp. Beneath it, a man counted coins into his palm, again and again. The wind found his thin coat, yet his eyes never left the empty track. The lamp buzzed on. Far down the line, a pale light stirred."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer move from the lamp to the man counting coins?", "o": ["Every description must include a person to get into the higher mark bands", "It shifts the focus from setting to a detail, which gives structure within one moment", "It starts a new event, so the examiner sees that there is a plot", "It makes the answer longer, and longer answers score more marks"], "a": 1, "f": ["No rule says a description needs a person. Marks reward ideas and structure, and a place alone can be described well.", "Right. Moving from wide view to close detail shapes the writing, and it keeps you inside the same scene.", "Q5 does not reward plot for its own sake. Adding new events is how writers end up rushing through a story.", "Length alone earns nothing. Marks go for the quality of ideas, imagery and organisation, not the number of words."]}, "faded": {"task": "Write the opening of a story in which someone waits for important news.", "given": ["The phone sat on the table, silent and black.", "Mum's tea had gone cold beside it, a skin wrinkling on the top."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to show the tension and stay in the moment?", "o": ["I was nervous and scared, and I kept wondering what the news would be.", "Three years earlier, Dad had lost his job and we had moved to a new house.", "I turned the phone face down, then face up, then pressed my thumb against the table.", "Then it rang, the news was good, and we spent the summer celebrating abroad."], "a": 2, "f": ["This only names the feelings. It tells the reader instead of showing them through actions or details.", "A flashback leaves the moment. The opening should stay on one scene and build detail there.", "Right. Fidgeting actions show the tension without naming it. The sentence also stays in the same moment and keeps the detail going.", "This rushes ahead to the result and later events. Detail on the waiting moment scores better than racing through the plot."]}, "trap": "Cramming a whole story into the time. Choose one moment and build it with sensory detail. Rushing through many events gives you thin ideas and weak structure.", "recap": "Plan one focused scene, show feelings through detail and imagery, shape it with a clear structure, then check your accuracy."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p1-q5b", "kind": "writing", "why": "On Paper 1 Question 5, 16 of the 40 marks are for technical accuracy: varied sentences, precise vocabulary and correct spelling and punctuation.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Vary your sentence types", "b": "A **simple sentence** has one main clause. A **compound sentence** joins two main clauses with a word like and, but or so. A **complex sentence** adds a subordinate clause, often starting with although, when or as. Mix all three. A very short sentence after longer ones adds impact."}, {"h": "Punctuate accurately", "b": "Use capital letters, full stops and commas correctly. A **semicolon** joins two closely linked sentences. A comma on its own cannot: that mistake is a **comma splice**. In speech, the punctuation goes inside the speech marks. Use apostrophes for missing letters or ownership: it’s means it is."}, {"h": "Choose words and spell them", "b": "Choose **precise vocabulary**: “crept” says more than “walked”. Use an ambitious word only if you are sure of its meaning and spelling. Marks reward **accurate spelling**, including the commonly muddled words: their, there, they’re; your, you’re; its, it’s."}], "example": {"task": "Write a description of an empty school corridor just after the final bell. (Model answer, built in four parts.)", "steps": [{"do": "“When the final bell had faded, the corridor seemed to hold its breath.”", "why": "A complex sentence: subordinate clause, then main clause. The comma after the opening clause is correct. “Faded” is precise."}, {"do": "“Lockers hung open like tired mouths; a single blazer lay crumpled beneath the window.”", "why": "The semicolon joins two full, linked sentences correctly. A comma here would be a comma splice. Spelling is accurate throughout."}, {"do": "“Somewhere, a door slammed. Silence.”", "why": "A short simple sentence, then a one-word minor sentence, used on purpose. The contrast with the longer sentences creates variety and tension."}, {"do": "“Nobody’s here,” I murmured, “not even the caretaker.”", "why": "Speech punctuation is correct: comma inside the speech marks, lower-case tag, no capital as the speech continues. The apostrophe in Nobody’s marks a missing letter."}], "answer": "When the final bell had faded, the corridor seemed to hold its breath. Lockers hung open like tired mouths; a single blazer lay crumpled beneath the window. Somewhere, a door slammed. Silence. “Nobody’s here,” I murmured, “not even the caretaker.”"}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer use a semicolon in its second part?", "o": ["It joins a main clause to a subordinate clause, which always needs a semicolon.", "It joins two complete, linked sentences where a comma alone would be a comma splice.", "It replaces the full stop at the end of the paragraph so the writing looks more varied.", "It introduces a list of items, in the same way that a colon would."], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. A subordinate clause cannot stand alone, and a semicolon needs a full sentence on both sides. Here both parts are main clauses.", "Right. Both halves could stand alone as sentences. A semicolon links them correctly, while a comma alone would be a comma splice.", "Wrong. The semicolon sits in the middle of the paragraph, and it is there to link two sentences, not to end one.", "Wrong. This mixes up the semicolon and the colon. Nothing is being listed here, and the second part is a full sentence."]}, "faded": {"task": "Write the opening of a story about someone waiting at a railway station at night.", "given": ["Sentence 1 (compound): “The last train was late, and Sam had stopped pretending otherwise.”", "Next: add a complex sentence, accurately punctuated, to vary the structure."], "q": "Which sentence should come next?", "o": ["The wind scattered litter along the platform, he pulled his coat tighter.", "Because the wind was cold. He pulled his coat tighter.", "Its freezing, he thought, and pulled his coat tighter.", "As the wind scattered litter along the platform, he pulled his coat tighter."], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Two full sentences are joined by only a comma, which is a comma splice. A full stop, semicolon or conjunction is needed.", "Wrong. “Because the wind was cold.” is a fragment, not a sentence. A subordinate clause needs a main clause attached to it.", "Wrong. “Its” should be “It’s” (it is). The sentence also leaves out the complex structure you were asked to add.", "Right. “As the wind scattered litter…” is a subordinate clause, followed by a comma and a main clause. It is accurate and adds variety."]}, "trap": "Joining two full sentences with only a comma: “The bell rang, we left.” Fix it with a full stop, a semicolon or a conjunction. Test each comma: could both sides stand alone?", "recap": "Mix simple, compound and complex sentences, and punctuate each one accurately; a comma alone never joins two full sentences."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p2-q1", "kind": "method", "why": "Q1 opens Paper 2 and is worth 4 marks, and it is quick to get right if you check each statement against the named lines.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What Q1 asks", "b": "You get **eight statements** about a short part of one source. The question names the lines. You choose the **four** that are true. Each correct choice earns one mark, so the question is worth 4 marks in total."}, {"h": "Check every part", "b": "A statement is **true** only if **every part** of it matches the lines. Find the exact words that support it. Watch for changed numbers, names and dates. Also watch for strong words such as \"always\", \"all\" and \"only\"."}, {"h": "Stated, not guessed", "b": "Choose a statement only if the lines **state** it. If it sounds likely but the text does not say it, it is not true for Q1. Do not use outside knowledge. Do not choose a **guess** about what the writer meant."}, {"h": "A quick routine", "b": "Read the **named lines** first. Then take each statement and find the matching words. Mark it true or false. At the end, **count**. You need four. If you have more or fewer, recheck the weakest choices."}], "example": {"task": "Lines 1–3: \"Dev, 16, has volunteered at a food bank since 2021. About 40 families visit each week. He finds it tiring but rewarding.\" Choose the four true statements from A–H (set out in the steps).", "steps": [{"do": "Read the named lines and note the facts: Dev is 16; volunteering since 2021; about 40 families a week; finds it tiring but rewarding.", "why": "Only the named lines count, so your checklist comes from them."}, {"do": "A \"Dev has volunteered since 2021\" matches \"since 2021\", so it is true. B \"Dev volunteers every Saturday\": the lines give no day, so it is false.", "why": "Not stated means not true, even if it sounds likely."}, {"do": "C \"Dev is 16\" matches \"Dev, 16\", so it is true. D \"Dev finds the work easy\": the text says \"tiring\", so it is false.", "why": "A statement that contradicts the text is false."}, {"do": "E \"About 40 families visit each week\" matches, so it is true. F \"About 400 families visit each week\" changes one digit, so it is false.", "why": "Compare exact figures. A small change makes a statement false."}, {"do": "G \"Dev finds the work tiring but rewarding\": both parts match, so it is true. H \"Dev started because his mother asked\": no reason is given, so it is false.", "why": "Every part must match. A reason you guess is not stated."}, {"do": "Count the true statements: A, C, E and G. That is four, as the question asks.", "why": "Q1 has exactly four true statements. Any other count means you recheck."}], "answer": "A, C, E and G. Each one matches the lines exactly. B and H add details the text does not give. D and F change what the text says."}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 2, why is B \"Dev volunteers every Saturday\" false?", "o": ["Food banks are usually closed on Saturdays, so it is unlikely", "The source says that Dev volunteers on Sundays", "The lines give no day, so nothing in the text supports it", "Dev is 16, and 16-year-olds cannot volunteer every week"], "a": 2, "f": ["This uses outside knowledge. Q1 only rewards what the named lines say, not what is usual in real life.", "The source never mentions Sundays. Inventing a detail the text does not contain is a slip.", "Right. The lines never name a day. A statement is true only if the text supports every part of it.", "This is outside knowledge again, and it is not in the text. The lines say nothing about age limits."]}, "faded": {"task": "Lines 1–3: \"Ms Patel has run the village shop for 15 years. It opens at 7 am. She says she may retire next year.\" Check statement B: \"Ms Patel will retire next year.\"", "given": ["Note the key facts: 15 years; opens at 7 am; she \"may\" retire next year.", "Statement A, \"Ms Patel has run the shop for 15 years\", matches exactly: true."], "q": "What should you decide about statement B?", "o": ["True: she may retire next year, so the statement is close enough", "True: she has run the shop for 15 years, so retiring is likely", "False: a shop owner cannot retire while the shop is still open", "False: the text says she \"may\" retire, but B states it as certain"], "a": 3, "f": ["\"Close enough\" is not good enough. A statement must match exactly, and \"may\" is not the same as \"will\".", "This is a guess based on her years in the job. The lines do not say she will retire.", "This is outside knowledge. The text does not say this, and the problem is that B is more certain than the lines.", "Right. The text says \"may\", which is uncertain. B says \"will\", so one part of it does not match."]}, "trap": "Choosing a statement that is mostly right. One wrong detail, such as a changed number or an added reason, makes it false. Check every word against the lines, and do not choose guesses.", "recap": "Choose a statement only if every part is stated in the named lines; if any part is changed or missing, leave it."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p2-q2", "kind": "writing", "why": "Paper 2 Question 2 is worth 8 marks and needs evidence from both sources, so it rewards linked points, not two separate summaries.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Stick to the focus", "b": "The question names a **focus**, such as 'differences in how the writers feel about school'. Write only about that. Check whether it asks for **differences** or similarities, and answer that one. Facts that don't answer the focus earn nothing, even if they are true."}, {"h": "Join the sources together", "b": "Every point should use both sources. Link them with a **connective** such as 'whereas', 'while' or 'in contrast'. This is **synthesis**: one sentence, two sources. Two separate summaries, one after the other, usually score lower."}, {"h": "Quote, then infer", "b": "Use short **quotations**, often one to three words. Then **infer**: say what the words suggest about the writer's feelings or situation. 'Crushed' suggests the writer feels weighed down. Don't just copy the quote, and don't spend time naming techniques."}], "example": {"task": "A: 'The library is my second home; the silence soothes me.' B: 'The library felt like a prison; the silence crushed me.' Summarise how the writers' feelings differ.", "steps": [{"do": "Writer A feels at home in the library, as 'second home' suggests comfort, whereas Writer B feels trapped, as 'a prison' implies confinement.", "why": "It opens on the focus, uses both sources in one sentence, and infers from each quotation."}, {"do": "The silence 'soothes' A, suggesting calm, but it 'crushed' B, implying it felt oppressive.", "why": "A second difference. Again both sources are linked, with a short quote and an inference."}, {"do": "A sees the library as a refuge, whereas B sees it as somewhere to escape from.", "why": "It sums up the overall difference in attitude and stays on the focus."}], "answer": "Writer A feels at home in the library, as 'second home' suggests comfort, whereas Writer B feels trapped, as 'a prison' implies confinement. The silence 'soothes' A, suggesting calm, but it 'crushed' B, implying it felt oppressive. A sees the library as a refuge, whereas B sees it as somewhere to escape from."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer add 'implying it felt oppressive' after the quotation 'crushed'?", "o": ["It names the technique used, which Q2 asks for", "It shows what the word suggests, which is the inference Q2 rewards", "It repeats the quote in other words to make the point longer", "It deals with Source B alone, which keeps the sources apart"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Q2 doesn't ask for techniques. Naming them is Q3 work and takes time away from the inference.", "Right. 'Oppressive' is what 'crushed' suggests about B's feelings. Inferring meaning from short evidence is what Q2 rewards.", "Wrong. It isn't padding. The phrase adds a meaning the quotation only hints at, and that meaning earns the marks.", "Wrong. The sentence is joined to A by 'but'. Keeping the sources apart is a weakness to avoid, not an aim."], "x": null}, "faded": {"task": "A: 'The village shop is the heart of the village; everyone stops to chat.' B: 'The shop is a nuisance; the gossip and queues waste my mornings.' Summarise the differences in attitude.", "given": ["Writer A sees the shop as vital to the village, as 'the heart' suggests, whereas Writer B finds it irritating, as 'a nuisance' implies."], "q": "Which sentence should come next?", "o": ["Both writers mention other people in the shop, so they both see it as a social place.", "A uses a metaphor, 'the heart', which makes the reader see the shop as important.", "A enjoys the chat, implying warmth, whereas B resents the 'queues', implying impatience.", "B says 'gossip and queues' waste the mornings, which is a complaint about the shop."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. This lists a similarity, but the question asks for differences. It also ignores that the writers feel differently about those people.", "Wrong. This analyses language in one source, which is Q3 work. It gives no link to B and no difference.", "Right. It gives a new difference, links both sources with 'whereas', quotes briefly and infers warmth and impatience.", "Wrong. It covers only Source B and just restates the quote. There is no link to A and no inference."]}, "trap": "Writing about Source A, then Source B, with no link, or listing similarities when the question asks for differences. Put both sources in each sentence and join them with 'whereas' or 'while'.", "recap": "Answer the focus asked, put both sources in every point, link them with 'whereas', and follow each short quotation with what it suggests."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p2-q3", "kind": "writing", "why": "Question 3 is worth 12 of the 80 marks on Paper 2 and asks you to analyse the writer's language in part of one source.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What Question 3 asks", "b": "Q3 is on **one source only**, and you are usually given a section of it to focus on. You must explain how the writer uses **language** for **effect**. Look at words, phrases, sentence types and techniques. Do not write about the whole source, and do not compare it with the other one."}, {"h": "Technique, quote, effect", "b": "Make each point in three moves. Name the **technique**, such as a metaphor, a rhetorical question or a list of three. Give a short **quotation**. Then explain the **effect** on the reader: what they think or feel, and why. The effect is where most of the marks are."}, {"h": "Zoom in on words", "b": "Pick out one strong word and explain its **connotations**, the ideas and feelings it carries. Verbs and adjectives often work best. Use careful verbs such as \"suggests\", \"implies\" and \"creates\". **Sentence forms** count too. A short sentence can sound blunt, and a list can build pressure."}, {"h": "Link to the writer's aim", "b": "Finish by connecting your points to the writer's **viewpoint** and **purpose**. What does the writer want you to believe or feel? A few well-explained points beat a long list of techniques with no explanation."}], "example": {"task": "Extract: \"Our high street is dying. Shutters fall, one by one. Who will save it?\" Explain how the writer uses language to show the decline of the high street.", "steps": [{"do": "The writer uses a metaphor in \"Our high street is dying\". A street cannot literally die, so it is presented as a living thing in decline. This makes the reader feel sympathy and a sense of loss.", "why": "Names the technique, quotes it, and explains the effect on the reader."}, {"do": "The verb \"fall\" in \"Shutters fall\" suggests a heavy, sudden collapse, and \"one by one\" implies the closures are steady and cannot be stopped. Together they make the decline seem relentless.", "why": "Zooms in on a single word and its connotations, then links two details."}, {"do": "The rhetorical question \"Who will save it?\" leaves the reader without a clear answer. This suggests nobody is acting, and it makes the reader feel that they should do something.", "why": "Explains what the question does to the reader, not just that it is a question."}, {"do": "Overall, the writer's language creates a worried, urgent tone. The writer wants the reader to care about the high street and to believe it is worth saving.", "why": "Connects the effects to the writer's purpose, showing you understand why these language choices were made."}], "answer": "Each part names a technique, quotes it and explains its effect on the reader. The last part links the language to the writer's viewpoint and purpose."}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 1, why does the answer comment on the word \"dying\" being used about a street?", "o": ["Because \"dying\" is the longest word, and longer words always earn more marks", "Because \"dying\" is a fact, so the writer is just reporting that shops have closed", "Because a street cannot literally die, so the metaphor gives it life and wins sympathy", "Because naming the technique is enough, and the quotation only needs to be copied out"], "a": 2, "f": ["Marks are not given for word length. They are given for explaining what a word suggests and how it affects the reader.", "This treats a metaphor as literal. A street cannot die, so the word is figurative and works on the reader's feelings.", "Right. The mismatch between a street and dying is what makes it a metaphor. Explaining the sympathy it creates is the effect.", "This is the trap of naming and stopping. A quotation must be followed by an explanation of its effect on the reader."]}, "faded": {"task": "Extract: \"Children are glued to screens. Hours vanish. Is this really the childhood we want?\" Explain how the writer uses language to show concern about screen time.", "given": ["The writer uses the metaphor \"glued to screens\", which suggests children are stuck and cannot pull away.", "This makes the reader see screen time as a trap, so they may feel worried for the children."], "q": "Which sentence is the best next step, analysing \"Hours vanish.\"?", "o": ["The writer uses a short sentence here, which is a technique that writers often use.", "\"Hours vanish.\" is blunt; \"vanish\" suggests time lost unnoticed, which alarms the reader.", "This means that children spend many hours on screens and do not notice the time passing.", "The list of three in \"Hours vanish.\" builds the point and makes the reader feel calm."], "a": 1, "f": ["This names a technique and stops. It gives no quotation focus and no effect on the reader, so it earns little.", "Right. It quotes the sentence, comments on a key word's connotations and explains the effect on the reader.", "This retells the meaning in plain words. It does not name a technique or say what the language does to the reader.", "There is no list of three in \"Hours vanish.\", and the effect is wrong. The writer wants the reader alarmed, not calm."]}, "trap": "Naming a technique and stopping, or spotting techniques that are not there. Always quote a few words, then explain what they make the reader think or feel.", "recap": "For every point, name the technique, quote it, then explain what it makes the reader think or feel."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p2-q4", "kind": "writing", "why": "Q4 is worth 16 marks, the most on Paper 2 Section A, and it rewards close comparison of both sources, not a summary of each.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Viewpoint first", "b": "Q4 asks how each writer's **viewpoint** differs and how they put it across. Start by stating each viewpoint in a few words: 'against', 'in favour', 'worried', 'hopeful'. Then name the **methods** that show it: word choice, tone, sentence types and structure."}, {"h": "Compare in every paragraph", "b": "Do not write all of Source A, then all of Source B. Put both writers in every paragraph. Use **comparing words** such as 'whereas', 'in contrast', 'similarly' and 'both'. Each paragraph can **link** the writers through one shared idea or topic."}, {"h": "Quote, method, effect", "b": "Use short **quotations** from both sources. Name the **method**, such as a verb, a list or a question. Then explain the **effect** on the reader and what it shows about the viewpoint. A quotation with no effect earns few marks."}, {"h": "A paragraph pattern", "b": "A reliable pattern has four parts. First, one sentence comparing the two **viewpoints**. Second, quotation, method and effect from A. Third, quotation, method and effect from B. Fourth, a closing **link** between the writers. This keeps the comparison balanced."}], "example": {"task": "Compare how the writers present their viewpoints on school uniform. A: 'Uniform is a pointless daily chore.' B: 'Uniform gives every pupil a fair start.'", "steps": [{"do": "Whereas A dismisses uniform as an annoyance, B presents it as a force for fairness.", "why": "States both viewpoints in one sentence and uses 'whereas', so the comparison starts at once."}, {"do": "A's adjective 'pointless' and the noun 'chore' make uniform seem a wasted, tiring task, so the tone is dismissive and weary.", "why": "Gives a quotation, names the methods, and explains the effect and tone."}, {"do": "In contrast, B's verb 'gives' and the phrase 'fair start' make uniform a gift that levels pupils, so the tone is warm and persuasive.", "why": "Matches the point on A with a quotation, method and effect from B, signalled by 'in contrast'."}, {"do": "Both use short, confident statements, but A's negativity invites the reader to share its irritation, whereas B's positivity aims to reassure.", "why": "Links the writers through a shared method and then separates their purposes."}], "answer": "The paragraph contrasts the viewpoints (dismissive against fair-minded), gives a quotation, method and effect for each writer, and ends by linking their shared sentence style to their different aims."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer open with one sentence about both writers?", "o": ["It shows the examiner you read Source A first and Source B second.", "It sets up the contrast in viewpoints at once, so the paragraph compares.", "It lets you skip quotations because the viewpoints are already clear.", "It proves which writer is more convincing than the other."], "a": 1, "f": ["Order of reading earns nothing. The opening sentence matters because it puts both viewpoints side by side straight away.", "Right. Naming both viewpoints with 'whereas' starts the comparison in the first sentence and gives the rest of the paragraph a focus.", "Quotations are still needed as evidence. Stating the viewpoints does not replace them, and the next steps add them.", "Q4 asks you to compare how viewpoints are presented, not to judge which writer is better. This opening makes no such judgement."]}, "faded": {"task": "Compare how the writers present their viewpoints on homework. A: 'Homework steals evenings from tired teenagers.' B: 'Homework builds habits that last a lifetime.'", "given": ["Whereas A presents homework as a theft of free time, B presents it as a way to build good habits."], "q": "Which sentence should come next?", "o": ["A uses a verb, 'steals', and the sentence is short.", "Homework is bad because A says it takes up evenings, and I agree.", "A's verb 'steals' paints homework as a crime, so the tone is resentful.", "B's verb 'builds' suggests growth, so B has the better argument."], "a": 2, "f": ["It names a method but gives no effect. Always explain what the word makes the reader think or feel.", "Your own opinion earns no marks. Q4 rewards analysis of how the writer presents the viewpoint.", "Right. It quotes A, names the method (verb), and explains the effect and tone, which is the next step in the pattern.", "This moves to B too early and judges the writers. Finish the point on A first and avoid saying who is better."]}, "trap": "Writing about Source A, then Source B, with no comparison. Fix it by putting both writers in every paragraph and joining them with words like 'whereas' or 'in contrast'.", "recap": "In every paragraph, compare both writers' viewpoints, and back each with a short quotation, a named method and its effect."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p2-q5b", "kind": "writing", "why": "Q5 gives 16 of its 40 marks to technical accuracy: sentence variety, vocabulary, spelling, punctuation and grammar, earned or lost in every sentence.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Where the marks are", "b": "Q5 is worth 40 marks. 24 are for content and organisation. 16 are for **technical accuracy**. Examiners look at **sentence structures**, vocabulary, spelling, punctuation and grammar. They reward range and control: varied choices, used correctly. You earn these marks in every sentence, not in one special paragraph."}, {"h": "Vary your sentences", "b": "Mix short and long sentences, and use **compound** and **complex** structures. Add a very short sentence for impact. A **semicolon** joins two linked full sentences: 'Phones distract pupils; they should stay in bags.' Each side must make sense alone. Never put one before a fragment."}, {"h": "Apostrophes of ownership", "b": "Ask: one owner or many? One pupil: **pupil's** book. Many pupils: **pupils'** books. Plurals that do not end in s add 's: children's books. A plain plural never takes an apostrophe: write 'pupils', not 'pupil's', when nothing is owned."}, {"h": "Precise words, safe spelling", "b": "Swap vague words (bad, big, nice) for **precise vocabulary**: harmful, vast, appealing. Match your tone to the form. Use ambitious words only if you can spell them. Check **homophones**: their/there/they're, affect/effect, your/you're."}], "example": {"task": "Write the opening of an article for a newspaper arguing that school should start later in the morning.", "steps": [{"do": "Every morning, thousands of exhausted teenagers drag themselves into school before their brains have woken up.", "why": "Precise words ('exhausted', 'drag') show range. A comma follows the opening phrase, and the complex sentence is controlled."}, {"do": "Pupils' concentration collapses; their teachers' best lessons are wasted.", "why": "The semicolon joins two linked full sentences. In pupils' and teachers' the owners are plural, so the apostrophe follows the s."}, {"do": "This is not laziness.", "why": "A very short simple sentence after longer ones adds emphasis and varies sentence length."}, {"do": "Research suggests that teenagers' body clocks run later, so an early start fights biology.", "why": "Comma plus 'so' joins two clauses correctly, avoiding a comma splice. The plural apostrophe and spellings are accurate."}], "answer": "Every morning, thousands of exhausted teenagers drag themselves into school before their brains have woken up. Pupils' concentration collapses; their teachers' best lessons are wasted. This is not laziness. Research suggests that teenagers' body clocks run later, so an early start fights biology."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the semicolon correct in the second part of the model answer?", "o": ["It replaces a comma, because the sentence is long and needs a pause.", "Semicolons are ambitious punctuation, so examiners reward them wherever they appear.", "Both halves are complete sentences, and the second extends the first.", "The second half is a fragment, and a semicolon lets it lean on the first."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. A semicolon does not just replace a comma for a pause. Using a comma here would create a comma splice.", "Wrong. Marks go for accurate, controlled use, not for the mark itself. A semicolon in the wrong place loses marks.", "Right. 'Pupils' concentration collapses' and 'their teachers' best lessons are wasted' can each stand alone, and they are closely linked.", "Wrong. A semicolon must never be followed by a fragment. Here the second half has its own subject and verb."]}, "faded": {"task": "Speech to your year group: argue that every pupil should do some volunteering. Choose the next sentence that earns technical accuracy marks.", "given": ["Volunteering changes lives, and not only the lives of the people we help."], "q": "Which sentence should come next?", "o": ["Pupils who volunteer gain confidence, they also learn skills that no lesson can teach.", "Pupils who volunteer gain confidence; they also learn skills that no lesson can teach.", "Pupil's who volunteer gain confidence; they also learn skills that no lesson can teach.", "Pupils who volunteer gain confidence; learning skills that no lesson can teach."], "a": 1, "f": ["This is a comma splice. A comma alone cannot join two full sentences. Use a semicolon, or a comma plus a conjunction.", "Right. Both halves are full sentences and closely linked, so the semicolon is correct. 'Pupils' is a plain plural, so it has no apostrophe.", "The semicolon is fine, but 'Pupil's' is wrong. This is a plain plural with nothing owned, so it needs no apostrophe.", "A semicolon cannot come before a fragment. 'Learning skills that no lesson can teach' has no subject and cannot stand alone."]}, "trap": "Writing pupil's for several pupils, or using a semicolon before a fragment. Check that each side of a semicolon could stand alone. For owners, ask: one or many? Many ending in s take the apostrophe after it.", "recap": "Join linked full sentences with a semicolon, put the apostrophe after the s when plural owners end in s, and vary sentence lengths."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-sl1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Your formal talk is reported separately as Pass, Merit or Distinction, not in your GCSE grade, and good planning makes it easier to give.", "hook": {"q": "You listen to a ten-minute talk and feel lost after three minutes. What is the most likely cause?", "o": ["The speaker looked up at the audience quite often.", "The speaker never said what the main points would be.", "The speaker used Standard English throughout.", "The speaker glanced at brief notes now and then."], "a": 1, "f": ["Looking up helps. It keeps listeners involved and shows you are speaking to them, not reading at them.", "Right. Without a clear plan, listeners cannot tell where the talk is going. That is why structure and signposting matter.", "Standard English is expected in a formal talk. It does not make a talk hard to follow.", "Brief notes are a good thing. They help the speaker keep their place and still speak to the audience."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Purpose and audience", "b": "Decide your **purpose** first: to inform, explain, persuade or entertain. Then think about your **audience**: who they are and what they already know. These choices decide your content, examples and tone. A formal talk needs **Standard English**, so avoid slang."}, {"h": "Order the talk", "b": "Give the talk a clear **structure**: an opening, a middle and an ending. In the opening, name your topic and say what points are coming. This is **signposting**. In the middle, take one point at a time with an example. In the ending, sum up and finish firmly."}, {"h": "Use brief notes", "b": "Do not write out every word to read aloud. Use **brief notes**: key words or short prompts on a card. They keep you in order while you look at the audience. Practise aloud and time yourself. Check **pace**, **volume** and clear speech."}, {"h": "After the talk", "b": "Your teacher assesses how well you **present**, how you **respond to questions** and feedback, and your use of **Standard English**. If you cannot answer a question fully, say so honestly. Then give what you do know. Do not make things up."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how you would plan a ten-minute talk called 'Why teenagers need more sleep' for your Year 10 class.", "steps": [{"do": "I would set my purpose: to persuade my classmates that sleep matters and that they should change some habits.", "why": "A clear purpose decides what to include and how strongly to speak."}, {"do": "I would think about my audience: 14-year-olds who know the topic from daily life. I would use examples such as late-night phone use, and keep the language formal Standard English.", "why": "Examples that fit the audience keep them interested. Formal language suits the task."}, {"do": "I would plan the order. The opening names the topic and signposts three points: health, concentration and mood. Each point gets one example. The ending sums up and asks listeners to act.", "why": "A clear structure and signposting help the audience follow the talk."}, {"do": "I would make one small card per point with key words only, such as 'tired in lessons'.", "why": "Brief notes prompt me without a script, so I can look at the audience."}, {"do": "I would practise aloud and time myself, so the talk fits the time limit and I speak clearly.", "why": "Practice shows me if the length, pace and volume work."}], "answer": "I would decide my purpose (to persuade) and think about my audience (classmates). I would open by signposting three points, develop each with an example, then sum up. I would use brief notes on cards, not a script, and practise aloud with a timer."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the plan name the three points in the opening?", "o": ["So the talk lasts longer and fills the time.", "So the talk does not need an ending.", "So the audience knows the route and can follow each point.", "So the teacher can give extra marks for a long introduction."], "a": 2, "f": ["Signposting is about clarity, not length. Padding the opening would not help the listeners.", "The ending is still needed to sum up and finish the talk. Signposting does not replace it.", "Right. Telling listeners the plan at the start lets them follow the structure and see where each point fits.", "The teacher judges how well the talk works for an audience, not how long the introduction is."]}, "faded": {"task": "Plan a talk called 'Why our town needs a youth centre' for a group of school governors.", "given": ["Purpose: to persuade the governors to support a youth centre.", "Audience: adults who will want clear reasons and evidence."], "q": "What should the next step in the plan be?", "o": ["Write the whole talk out and learn to read it word for word.", "Use plenty of slang so the governors see how teenagers speak.", "Add as many facts as possible and say them in any order.", "Open with the topic and three reasons, then give each one with evidence."], "a": 3, "f": ["Reading a full script stops you speaking to the audience. Brief notes are better prompts.", "Governors are a formal audience. Standard English suits them, and slang may weaken the talk.", "Too many facts with no order lose the audience. A clear structure matters more than quantity.", "Right. An opening that signposts the reasons, then evidence for each, gives a clear structure for a formal audience."]}, "trap": "Writing out every word and reading it aloud. You stop speaking to the audience and sound flat. Use brief notes as prompts, and practise until you can speak from them.", "recap": "Plan your purpose, audience and a clear order, then speak to your audience from brief notes, not a script."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-sl2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Answering questions is part of your Spoken Language assessment, reported separately as Pass, Merit or Distinction rather than as part of your 9–1 grade.", "hook": {"q": "A listener asks you a tricky question at the end of your talk. What should you do first?", "o": ["Start talking straight away so there is no silence", "Listen to all of it, then pause briefly to plan your answer", "Ask them to ask you an easier question instead", "Repeat a sentence from your talk to fill the time"], "a": 1, "f": ["Rushing often leads to answering only part of the question, or a different one. A short silence is fine.", "A short pause lets you plan. It looks calm, and it helps you answer the whole question.", "You may ask for a question to be repeated if you missed it. You should not swap it for an easier one.", "Repeating your talk does not answer the question. You need to give new, direct detail."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Listen, then pause", "b": "Listen to the **whole question** before you speak. Many questions have two parts, so note both. Then take a **short pause** to plan. A few seconds of thinking looks calm, not weak. If you missed something, politely ask for the question to be repeated."}, {"h": "Answer directly, with detail", "b": "Start with a **direct answer** to what was asked. Then add a **reason** and an **example**. A one-word reply gives your teacher nothing to assess. Speak clearly and use Standard English where appropriate, as you did in your talk."}, {"h": "Link back and use feedback", "b": "**Link back** to your talk, for example: \"As I said about school meals...\". Then add something new, so you do not just repeat yourself. If you get **feedback**, accept it and use it straight away, such as by adding the missing evidence."}, {"h": "When you are unsure", "b": "If you are not sure, do not stop at \"I don't know\". Say what you **do know**. Then give a careful view: \"I think... because...\". Stay on the question that was asked. Answering a **different question** shows you were not listening."}], "example": {"task": "After your talk \"Everyone should learn to cook\", you are asked: \"Why should cooking be compulsory until Year 11, and how would schools fit it in?\" Explain how to answer well.", "steps": [{"do": "Pause, then say: \"Two good questions. I'll take why it should be compulsory first, then how schools could fit it in.\"", "why": "This shows you heard both parts. It also gives you a plan."}, {"do": "Answer part one directly: \"It is a life skill. Many young adults rely on takeaways, which cost more and are often less healthy.\"", "why": "A direct answer with a reason, not a one-word reply."}, {"do": "Add an example: \"In my own home, a pasta meal for four costs less than one takeaway.\"", "why": "An example makes the reason clear and detailed."}, {"do": "Answer part two: \"Schools could replace one lesson a fortnight in Years 10 and 11 with a cooking class. Some would need extra money for kitchens.\"", "why": "Covers the second part, with a fair point about limits."}, {"do": "Link back: \"As I said in my talk, cooking also builds confidence, so it benefits pupils beyond health.\"", "why": "Connects to the talk and adds a new point."}], "answer": "Pause and name both parts. Answer part one with a reason and an example. Answer part two with a practical idea and a limit. Finish by linking back to your talk. Every part of the question is answered, with detail."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer begin by naming both parts of the question?", "o": ["To fill time so the listener asks you fewer questions", "Because you must repeat the question word for word first", "So you can pick the part you prefer and skip the other", "To show you heard the whole question and to plan your reply"], "a": 3, "f": ["Stalling is not the aim. Naming the parts is useful because it shows careful listening and organises your reply.", "You do not need to repeat the question word for word. A short summary of its parts is enough.", "Skipping a part is the mistake to avoid. Naming both parts helps you answer both.", "Naming both parts shows careful listening and gives your reply a clear order, so neither part is missed."]}, "faded": {"task": "After your talk \"Teenagers should volunteer\", you are asked: \"Why does volunteering matter, and who benefits most?\" You have already started your reply.", "given": ["Pause, then say: \"I'll take why it matters first, then who benefits most.\"", "Give a direct reason: \"It builds skills such as teamwork and confidence.\""], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["Jump to part two: \"Everyone benefits.\" Then stop speaking.", "Say: \"I covered all of this in my talk.\" Then stop.", "Add an example: \"Food bank volunteers learn to work in a team.\"", "Switch to a different topic, such as exam stress"], "a": 2, "f": ["This jumps to part two with a one-line answer. The reason still has no example, so the reply lacks detail.", "Pointing back to the talk without adding anything gives nothing new. Linking back should come with fresh detail.", "An example backs up your reason and makes your answer detailed. Then you can move on to who benefits.", "This answers a different question from the one asked. Stay on what the listener wanted to know."]}, "trap": "Giving a one-word answer, stopping at \"I don't know\", or answering a different question. Pause, answer the exact question, and add a reason and an example. If unsure, say what you do know.", "recap": "Listen to the whole question, pause, answer it directly with a reason and an example, and link back to your talk."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-sl3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Your teacher listens for Standard English in your formal spoken language talk, which is reported separately from your GCSE grade, so it is worth getting right.", "hook": {"q": "You are opening a talk to a panel of teachers. Which opening fits best?", "o": ["Alright, everyone, so, like, I'm gonna chat about the trip.", "Good morning, everyone. Today I am going to talk about the trip.", "Hiya, guys. Right, so my talk's about the trip, yeah?", "Okay, so, basically, the trip was well good, innit."], "a": 1, "f": ["This has slang ('gonna', 'chat') and the filler 'like'. It sounds casual, not formal.", "Right. It greets the audience politely, uses complete sentences and states the topic clearly. This is the formal opening the topic recommends.", "'Hiya, guys' is a casual greeting and 'yeah?' is chatty. A panel expects a formal tone.", "'Basically', 'well good' and 'innit' are fillers and slang. None of them suits a formal setting."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What Standard English is", "b": "**Standard English** is the form of English with agreed grammar and vocabulary. It is not an **accent**. You can keep your own accent and still speak Standard English. What matters is the grammar and words you choose, not how you pronounce them."}, {"h": "Grammar to check", "b": "Watch your **verb agreement**: say \"we were\", not \"we was\". Use the correct past tense: \"I did it\", not \"I done it\". Say \"those books\", not \"them books\". Avoid a **double negative**: say \"I haven't got any\", not \"I haven't got none\"."}, {"h": "Formal words and openings", "b": "A formal setting needs a formal **register**. Leave out **slang** such as \"sick\" or \"chill\". Cut fillers such as \"like\", \"you know\" and \"basically\". Open formally: \"Good morning, everyone. Today I am going to talk about...\"."}, {"h": "Speaking audibly", "b": "**Audibly** means loud enough for everyone, including the person furthest away. Keep a steady **pace**. Do not rush. If you need time to think, pause silently instead of using a filler. A short pause sounds confident."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how you would make a talk to a panel of school governors formal and in Standard English.", "steps": [{"do": "I would open formally, for example: \"Good morning, everyone. Today I am going to talk about...\"", "why": "A formal greeting and a clear topic sentence set the right tone from the start."}, {"do": "I would check my grammar, saying \"we were\" not \"we was\" and \"those books\" not \"them books\".", "why": "Standard English depends on correct grammar, so non-standard forms like these must be corrected."}, {"do": "I would avoid slang and fillers. I would say \"unfair\", not \"well out of order\", and I would not say \"like\".", "why": "Slang and fillers are informal register. They do not suit a formal audience."}, {"do": "I would keep my own accent, but speak clearly, at a steady pace and loudly enough for the back of the room.", "why": "Standard English does not need a changed accent. The audience must still be able to hear you."}], "answer": "I would open formally, use correct grammar such as \"we were\" and \"those books\", and avoid slang and fillers. I would keep my accent but speak audibly at a steady pace, so the governors can hear and understand me."}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 4, why can the speaker keep their accent and still use Standard English?", "o": ["Because panels only mark written work, not how a speaker sounds", "Because accents are allowed only if the speaker talks very slowly", "Because a regional accent counts as slang in a formal setting", "Because Standard English is about grammar and vocabulary, not pronunciation"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Spoken talks are assessed on how you speak. The accent is allowed for a different reason: it does not affect grammar or vocabulary.", "Wrong. Speed is about clarity and pace. It has nothing to do with whether an accent is allowed.", "Wrong. An accent is a way of pronouncing words. Slang is a choice of words. They are different things.", "Right. Standard English is defined by grammar and vocabulary. Accents are pronunciation, so you can keep yours."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how to improve this for a formal talk: \"Me and Sam was, like, dead proud of them posters.\"", "given": ["Point 1: Change \"Me and Sam was\" to \"Sam and I were\". \"I\" is the subject form, and a plural subject needs \"were\".", "Point 2: Change \"them posters\" to \"those posters\". \"Them\" is not Standard English before a noun."], "q": "Which is the best next point?", "o": ["Replace \"dead proud\" with \"well chuffed\", which is friendlier and more formal.", "Say the sentence faster so the slang is less noticeable to the panel.", "Delete the filler \"like\" and change \"dead proud\" to \"extremely proud\".", "Keep \"like\", because a short filler gives the speaker time to think."], "a": 2, "f": ["\"Well chuffed\" is still slang. Swapping one informal phrase for another does not make it formal.", "Speed does not remove slang. Rushing also makes you harder to hear, so it works against audibility.", "Right. \"Like\" is a filler and \"dead proud\" is slang. Removing both leaves a formal sentence.", "Fillers are exactly what a formal talk should avoid. If you need time to think, pause silently."]}, "trap": "Thinking Standard English means hiding your accent, or fixing grammar but leaving slang and fillers such as \"like\". Check three things: grammar, vocabulary and clear delivery. Your accent can stay.", "recap": "Standard English is correct grammar and formal words, not a changed accent; open formally and speak clearly so everyone can hear."}
{"s": "english_lit", "t": "lit-mt-ic", "kind": "writing", "why": "Paper 2 Section A is a 34-mark essay on this play. You need the whole plot, the characters and Priestley's methods ready to build an argument.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Order of the play", "b": "Act One opens with a dinner celebrating the engagement of Sheila and **Gerald Croft**. The Inspector then questions one person at a time: Mr Birling, Sheila, Gerald, Mrs Birling, then Eric. Each is linked to Eva Smith's death. This **structure** builds pressure and keeps the audience guessing."}, {"h": "Why Sheila's guilt matters", "b": "Sheila had Eva sacked from **Milwards** out of **jealousy** and hurt pride. Eva had done nothing wrong. That is why Sheila's guilt matters: a spiteful act cost someone her job. Sheila accepts blame and changes. Her parents do not."}, {"h": "Context: 1945 and 1912", "b": "Priestley wrote in 1945 but set the play in 1912. The audience knows what came next, so Birling's confident predictions show **dramatic irony**. Priestley was a **socialist**. The Inspector's message is **collective responsibility**: \"We are members of one body.\""}, {"h": "Shape of a strong answer", "b": "Examiners reward a clear **argument** across the whole play. Use short quotations. Analyse Priestley's **methods**, such as structure, dramatic irony and characterisation. Add relevant context. Plan three or four paragraphs. Retelling the plot earns little."}], "example": {"task": "How does Priestley present Sheila Birling's attitude to her part in Eva Smith's death? Write about the play as a whole.", "steps": [{"do": "Priestley presents Sheila as the first Birling to accept responsibility, which makes her a symbol of hope for the younger generation.", "why": "A clear argument that answers the question straight away."}, {"do": "At first Sheila seems spoilt. She admits she had Eva sacked from Milwards out of jealousy, because she felt humiliated in the shop.", "why": "Precise reference to the plot, used as evidence rather than retold."}, {"do": "Such a petty motive makes her guilt striking: a small act of spite cost Eva her livelihood. Priestley shows how easily the powerful harm others.", "why": "Analyses the effect of the detail and links it to the play's ideas."}, {"do": "Unlike her parents, who deny blame, Sheila keeps accepting responsibility even when the family doubts the Inspector. This contrast shows her change is real.", "why": "Uses character contrast across the play, not just one scene."}, {"do": "Writing in 1945, Priestley uses Sheila and Eric to show the young can change, supporting the Inspector's warning that \"We are members of one body.\"", "why": "Links to context and Priestley's message, with a short quotation."}], "answer": "Sheila moves from spoilt jealousy to real responsibility. Her petty act makes her guilt striking, her refusal to deny blame contrasts with her parents, and Priestley uses her to give 1945 audiences hope."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer stress that Sheila's motive was petty jealousy?", "o": ["Because jealousy is a serious reason that partly excuses what she did", "Because a trivial motive makes her guilt more striking and serves Priestley's message", "Because the mark scheme requires every answer to mention Milwards", "Because Eva had behaved rudely, so Sheila's anger was fair"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Jealousy and wounded pride are not a serious reason. Treating them as one is the common slip, and it misses why her guilt matters.", "Right. The petty motive shows how a small act of spite can ruin a life. That gives you something to analyse, not just narrate.", "Wrong. No mark scheme demands one named detail. Marks come from argument, analysis and context, not from a compulsory fact.", "Wrong. Eva did nothing wrong. Sheila only imagined an insult, which is why her guilt matters."]}, "faded": {"task": "How does Priestley present Mr Birling as a man who refuses to change? Write about the play as a whole.", "given": ["Priestley presents Mr Birling as a self-satisfied businessman who learns nothing from the Inspector's visit.", "Early on he boasts that the Titanic is \"unsinkable\" and that there will be no war."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to earn the most marks?", "o": ["The Titanic was a ship that sank in 1912 after it struck an iceberg.", "Birling is also Sheila's father and owns a factory called Birling and Company.", "The 1945 audience knew the Titanic sank and war came, so dramatic irony mocks Birling.", "This shows Birling is a clever businessman whom the audience should admire and trust."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. This is a true fact, but it only states what happened. It does not link to Birling or to Priestley's methods.", "Wrong. This is plot detail with no analysis. It does not say what Priestley is doing or why.", "Right. It names a method, dramatic irony, and uses 1945 hindsight to show Birling's views are discredited.", "Wrong. This misreads the play. Priestley wants the audience to distrust Birling, not admire him."]}, "trap": "Retelling the plot, or treating Sheila's reason as serious. She acted from jealousy and wounded pride, so use that pettiness to analyse why her guilt matters. Tie every point to Priestley's methods.", "recap": "Build each paragraph as point, short quotation, analysis of Priestley's method, then context: 1945 hindsight and his message of collective responsibility."}
{"s": "english_lit", "t": "lit-po-pc", "kind": "writing", "why": "Paper 2 Section B prints one Power and Conflict poem and asks you to compare it with another you choose, so you must know your poems and their links.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Compare all the way through", "b": "The question prints one poem. You choose **one other poem** from the cluster to compare it with. Do not write about one poem and then the other. Keep both in view in every paragraph. Use **comparative words** such as 'whereas', 'similarly' and 'by contrast'."}, {"h": "Plan before you write", "b": "Spend a few minutes choosing your partner poem. Then find two or three **links** between the poems, such as the idea, the speaker or the **form**. Write about each link in turn. A plan keeps your answer focused on the question instead of drifting into retelling."}, {"h": "Build each paragraph", "b": "Make a point that answers the question. Support it with a short **quotation**. Name the **method** (language, form or structure) and explain its effect. Then link to the other poem. Where it helps, add **context**: the ideas or events of the poet's time."}, {"h": "Know your poems as pairs", "b": "Learn each poem as **poet plus idea**. Shelley's 'Ozymandias': power fades. Browning's 'My Last Duchess': a controlling duke. Owen's 'Exposure': the weather is the soldiers' greater enemy. Choose a partner poem with a **clear link**, so comparing ideas is easy."}], "example": {"task": "Compare how poets present the power of rulers in 'Ozymandias' and in one other poem from the cluster.", "steps": [{"do": "In 'Ozymandias' Shelley presents power as temporary, whereas in 'My Last Duchess' Browning presents a ruler whose power controls other people in the present.", "why": "It names both poems and both ideas in the first sentence, and compares them straight away."}, {"do": "Shelley mocks the king's pride. The inscription 'Look on my Works, ye Mighty, and despair!' is an imperative, which shows his arrogance, yet the 'lone and level sands' show his empire has gone.", "why": "Short quotations, a named method (imperative) and its effect, and a link to the idea of fading power."}, {"do": "By contrast, Browning's Duke gives himself away in a dramatic monologue. 'I gave commands; / Then all smiles stopped together' suggests he had his wife killed, and his calm tone makes this more chilling.", "why": "'By contrast' links the poems. It names the form, uses a quotation and explains the effect."}, {"do": "Both poets therefore expose the pride of powerful men. Yet Shelley, a Romantic who opposed tyranny, lets time defeat his king, whereas Browning leaves the Duke in control as he speaks, which is perhaps more disturbing.", "why": "Returns to the question with a final comparison, brief context and a judgement, not just a repeat of earlier points."}], "answer": "The answer compares in every part. It opens with a joint point, analyses a method in each poem, links them with 'by contrast', adds brief context, and ends on a final comparison."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 name the method (an imperative) as well as quote the line?", "o": ["Examiners only award marks for naming methods, so quotations are not needed.", "A longer paragraph always earns more marks, whatever it says.", "It shows how Shelley's language creates the effect, which is analysis, not just quoting.", "It tells the examiner who wrote the poem, which is the main skill being tested."], "a": 2, "f": ["Quotations are needed as evidence. Naming a method helps, but the two work together. Skipping the quotation would leave the point unsupported.", "Length does not earn marks. Marks come from the quality of analysis, so padding with extra words does not help.", "Right. Naming the imperative and explaining its effect shows you are analysing the writer's choices. A quotation on its own only repeats the poem.", "The poet's name is not the skill being tested. The marks are for analysing methods and comparing ideas, not for stating facts the examiner already knows."]}, "faded": {"task": "Compare how poets present the suffering of soldiers in 'Exposure' and 'The Charge of the Light Brigade'.", "given": ["In 'Exposure' Owen presents the soldiers' suffering as caused by nature, whereas in 'The Charge of the Light Brigade' Tennyson presents it as caused by people.", "Owen's opening, 'Our brains ache, in the merciless iced east winds that knive us', makes the wind a cruel attacker."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to build the comparison?", "o": ["By contrast, Tennyson's 'Someone had blunder'd' blames human error, not weather.", "Tennyson also wrote about war, and his poem is about a cavalry charge in 1854.", "Owen fought in the war, so his poem is more truthful than Tennyson's.", "Owen repeats 'But nothing happens' to show the waiting wears the soldiers down."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. It moves to Tennyson, uses a short quotation, and contrasts human error with Owen's weather, so it builds the comparison.", "This only gives background on Tennyson's poem. It has no quotation, no method and no link back to Owen, so it does not compare.", "This swaps analysis for a judgement based on the poet's life. It gives no quotation or method from Tennyson's poem.", "A fair point about Owen, but it stays inside one poem. The next step must bring in Tennyson to keep the comparison going."]}, "trap": "Writing about one poem, then the other, or retelling what happens. Keep both poems in every paragraph, and analyse methods and their effects rather than the plot.", "recap": "Compare throughout: make a point, add a short quotation, name the method and its effect, then link to your other poem."}
{"s": "english_lit", "t": "lit-sk1", "kind": "writing", "why": "AO1 is marked in every literature answer you write, on every text, so these habits earn marks in both papers.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Answer the question", "b": "AO1 rewards a **personal response**: your own clear view, aimed at the exact question. Start with a point such as 'Shakespeare presents…'. Do not retell the plot. Examiners want an **argument** they can follow, not a list of events."}, {"h": "Choose short quotations", "b": "Back every point with **evidence**. The best quotations are short, often only a few words, and exact. **Embed** them in your own sentence. If you cannot recall the words, you can refer to a moment in the text instead."}, {"h": "Explain what it shows", "b": "A quotation does nothing alone. Follow it with a **comment**: what does it suggest, and how does that answer the question? Look closely at single words. Phrases like 'could suggest' or 'perhaps' show your own **interpretation**."}, {"h": "Build and extend", "b": "Build each paragraph as point, quotation, explanation. Then **develop** it with a second possible meaning, or a **reference** to another part of the text. This shows you know the whole text and can think for yourself."}], "example": {"task": "Question: How does Shakespeare present Macbeth's ambition? Write part of a paragraph using Act 1 Scene 4, where Duncan names Malcolm as his heir.", "steps": [{"do": "Shakespeare presents Macbeth's ambition as a desire that he knows is wrong and tries to hide.", "why": "A clear, personal point that answers the exact question. It is not a plot summary."}, {"do": "When Duncan names Malcolm his heir, Macbeth asks the stars to 'hide your fires' so that light cannot see his 'black and deep desires'.", "why": "Short, exact quotations built into the sentence, placed in the right moment of the play."}, {"do": "The word 'black' links his ambition to evil, and the plea for darkness suggests he is ashamed of it.", "why": "Zooms in on one word and explains what it suggests about ambition."}, {"do": "It could also suggest he is already imagining murder, as he needs darkness for something he cannot admit even to himself.", "why": "'Could' offers a personal interpretation. A second idea develops the point."}, {"do": "Later he again asks night to cover a deed, 'Come, seeling night', before Banquo is murdered.", "why": "A reference to another part of the play shows knowledge of the whole text and supports the point."}], "answer": "A strong paragraph makes a clear point, embeds short exact quotations, explains single words, offers a tentative second interpretation and links to another part of the play."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 focus on the single word 'black'?", "o": ["Because examiners only give credit for comments on one word at a time", "Because it retells what Macbeth does next in the play", "Because a single well-chosen word can be examined closely for what it suggests", "Because it lets the answer avoid explaining the rest of the quotation"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. You can comment on a phrase or several words. Here one word is useful because it carries a clear meaning.", "Wrong. Retelling events is what AO1 does not reward. This step explains meaning and does not narrate.", "Right. Close focus on one word lets you show exactly what it suggests, here evil and shame, and ties that to ambition.", "Wrong. The comment is meant to explain the quotation's effect, not dodge it. It covers the 'black' desires and the plea for darkness."]}, "faded": {"task": "Question: How does Dickens present Scrooge at the start of A Christmas Carol? Continue this paragraph with the next sentence.", "given": ["Point: Dickens presents Scrooge as a man who chooses to be cut off from others.", "Quotation: He is described as 'secret, and self-contained, and solitary as an oyster'."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to earn the most AO1 credit?", "o": ["Scrooge then refuses to give money to the charity collectors and sends them away.", "Dickens also calls him 'a tight-fisted hand at the grindstone' in the same stave.", "Dickens wrote this in 1843, when poverty was widespread in Victorian Britain.", "The simile suggests a hard shell: he shuts people out, perhaps to protect himself."], "a": 3, "f": ["This retells the plot. It adds an event but does not explain what the quotation shows about Scrooge.", "A second quotation with no comment. The first one has not been explained yet, so no analysis has been added.", "This is context, not a close reading of the quotation. It does not explain what the words suggest.", "Right. It comments on the simile, explains what it suggests about Scrooge and links back to the point, using 'perhaps' for interpretation."]}, "trap": "Retelling the plot, or dropping in a quotation and moving on. Fix: make a point, add a short exact quotation, then explain what it suggests about the idea in the question.", "recap": "Make a clear point that answers the question, back it with a short embedded quotation, then explain what the words suggest."}
{"s": "english_lit", "t": "lit-sk2", "kind": "writing", "why": "AO2 is marked in every literature paper, so you will use it on Macbeth, A Christmas Carol, An Inspector Calls and the poems.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Three things to analyse", "b": "**Language** is word choice, imagery and sound. **Structure** is how a text is organised: the order of events, shifts, beginnings and endings. **Form** is the type of text, such as a play, a novella or a poem. Each one can create meaning."}, {"h": "Technique, quotation, effect", "b": "Naming a technique is not enough. Give a short **quotation**, name the **technique** or word type, then explain the **effect** on the reader. Finish by linking the effect to the writer's ideas. A technique with no effect earns very little."}, {"h": "Zoom in, then zoom out", "b": "**Zoom in** on one or two key words and say exactly what they suggest. Name the word class, such as verb or adjective. Then **zoom out**: how does this fit the whole text? Comment on where it comes, what shifts, or how the text ends."}], "example": {"task": "How does Dickens present Scrooge as greedy in Stave One? Write about the list 'squeezing, wrenching, grasping, scraping, clutching'.", "steps": [{"do": "Dickens presents Scrooge as greedy by describing him with a list of harsh adjectives formed from verbs: 'squeezing, wrenching, grasping, scraping, clutching'.", "why": "Answers the question directly and builds a short quotation into the sentence."}, {"do": "These words suggest violence, as if Scrooge forces money out of people. 'Clutching' and 'grasping' make him seem like a hand that never lets go.", "why": "Names the word class, picks out single words and explains the meanings they create."}, {"do": "The list piles one word on top of another without a break. This suggests that his greed never stops and fills his whole character.", "why": "Comments on how the sentence is built, not only on word meaning. This gives a second effect."}, {"do": "Dickens makes the reader judge Scrooge harshly early in the novella, so his change by the final stave feels more powerful.", "why": "Zooms out to the writer's purpose and the structure of the whole text."}], "answer": "The answer makes a point with a quotation, zooms in on word choice, comments on the sentence's build-up, then links to the novella's structure and Dickens's purpose."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 focus on 'clutching' and 'grasping' instead of copying out the whole list again?", "o": ["Quotations longer than two words earn no marks, so the answer has to cut it down.", "Verbs are the only word class that can be analysed, so the other words are left out.", "Close focus on one or two words lets the answer explain exact meanings and effects.", "Shorter answers score higher, so cutting the quotation saves time and gains marks."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. There is no two-word limit. Longer quotations are allowed, but short, chosen ones let you analyse more closely.", "Wrong. Adjectives, nouns, imagery and more can all be analysed. Step 2 picks these two words because they suggest a tight, greedy grip most clearly.", "Right. Zooming in on single words lets you explain exactly what they suggest. Repeating the whole quotation would not add analysis.", "Wrong. Marks come from the quality of analysis, not from length or brevity. Short quotations are useful because they allow detail."]}, "faded": {"task": "How does Shakespeare create a sense of confusion in Act 1 Scene 1? Write about the line 'Fair is foul, and foul is fair.'", "given": ["Shakespeare creates confusion in the witches' scene through the paradox 'Fair is foul, and foul is fair'.", "The line joins two opposites, so it suggests that good and evil cannot be told apart."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to earn the most AO2 marks?", "o": ["This is effective because it is a strong quotation and shows Shakespeare's skill.", "Its chanting rhythm sounds like a spell, unsettling the audience about who to trust.", "In Scene 3 the witches meet Macbeth and tell him he will become king.", "Paradox is a technique where two opposite ideas are put together in one phrase."], "a": 1, "f": ["This is vague praise. It does not say what the line suggests or how it affects the audience.", "This is right. It comments on sound and rhythm, explains the effect on the audience, and links to the theme of deception.", "This retells the plot instead of analysing the line. It names no technique and gives no effect.", "This only defines the technique, which the answer has already named. It adds no new effect or meaning."]}, "trap": "Mixing up language and structure, and naming techniques with no effect. Structure is how the text is organised, not single words. After every technique, say what it suggests and why the writer chose it.", "recap": "For every quotation, name the technique, then explain its effect on the reader and the idea the writer wants to put across."}
{"s": "english_lit", "t": "lit-sk3", "kind": "writing", "why": "AO3 (context) is worth marks in every essay except unseen poetry, so you need to weave a relevant fact about the time into your analysis, not add it as an extra.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Context must explain meaning", "b": "**Context** means the time, place and ideas around a text. It earns marks only when it helps explain what the writer is doing. A date or fact on its own earns nothing. Always make a **link**: fact, then what it shows in the text, then the effect."}, {"h": "Which context to use", "b": "Use ideas and events that shape the text, not the writer's life story for its own sake. Macbeth: the **divine right of kings**. A Christmas Carol (1843): **Victorian attitudes to the poor**. An Inspector Calls: set in 1912, written in 1945. Power and Conflict: attitudes to war and power."}, {"h": "Weave it into analysis", "b": "Do not write a separate history paragraph. Put context **inside a point**, in one or two sentences. Useful starters: \"Audiences in 1945 would have known...\" or \"This reflects the belief that...\". Then say how the context changes the **effect** on the audience."}], "example": {"task": "How does Priestley present Mr Birling in An Inspector Calls? Build one paragraph with context (AO3) in four parts.", "steps": [{"do": "Priestley presents Mr Birling as a self-satisfied man who is wrong about the future.", "why": "A clear point that answers the question and gives the paragraph a direction."}, {"do": "He boasts that the Titanic is \"unsinkable\" and that war will not come.", "why": "Short, exact evidence that supports the point."}, {"do": "Though set in 1912, the play was written in 1945, when audiences knew both claims were false.", "why": "Relevant context: it uses the two dates to explain why his words sound foolish."}, {"do": "This dramatic irony makes them distrust Birling and reject his selfish ideas, as Priestley intends.", "why": "Links the context to the effect on the audience and to Priestley's purpose."}], "answer": "Priestley presents Mr Birling as a self-satisfied man who is wrong about the future. He boasts that the Titanic is \"unsinkable\" and that war will not come. Though set in 1912, the play was written in 1945, when audiences knew both claims were false. This dramatic irony makes them distrust Birling and reject his selfish ideas, as Priestley intends."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model paragraph mention that audiences in 1945 knew the claims were false?", "o": ["To show the examiner that you know some dates from the play's history", "It explains why Birling's confident words are ironic, which shapes how we judge him", "Because every AO3 sentence must include a date to earn marks", "It tells the reader what happens later in the play to Birling's family"], "a": 1, "f": ["A date shown off for its own sake earns no AO3 marks. Context must explain a meaning in the writing.", "Right. The audience's knowledge turns Birling's boasts into irony, so the context explains the effect on the audience.", "No rule says every sentence needs a date. Context counts only when it is linked to meaning.", "This confuses context with plot. The audience's knowledge of real history is what matters here, not the story."]}, "faded": {"task": "How does Shakespeare present Duncan's murder as a terrible crime in Macbeth? Build a paragraph with context (AO3).", "given": ["Point: Shakespeare presents Duncan's murder as a crime against order itself.", "Evidence: Ross describes darkness in the daytime, and Duncan's horses eating each other."], "q": "What should the next sentence be?", "o": ["Shakespeare was born in 1564 and lived in Stratford-upon-Avon for much of his life.", "The play was first performed around 1606, when James I was on the throne.", "Many Jacobeans believed God chose kings, so killing Duncan seems to upset nature itself.", "Duncan is old and kind, so the audience feel sorry for him after the murder."], "a": 2, "f": ["This is a fact about the writer's life. It does not explain why nature reacts to the murder.", "This is a true fact about the time, but it is not linked to the evidence or to any meaning.", "Right. The divine right of kings explains why a king's murder is shown as unnatural, so the context explains the evidence.", "This comments on character, not context. It uses no idea from the time the play was written."]}, "trap": "Dropping in a date or fact that does not explain the quotation, such as the writer's birthday. Before you write context, ask: which part of the text does this help explain?", "recap": "Context earns marks only when you link a fact about the time to a meaning or effect in the text."}
{"s": "english_lit", "t": "lit-sk4", "kind": "writing", "why": "AO4 gives 4 marks on each of your Macbeth and An Inspector Calls essays, and careless spelling or punctuation slips can cost you them.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What AO4 rewards", "b": "**AO4** is marked on the Shakespeare essay (Macbeth) and the modern text essay (An Inspector Calls). It is not marked on A Christmas Carol or the poetry. Examiners look at **spelling and punctuation**, and at varied **vocabulary and sentence structure**. Accuracy must be consistent, not occasional."}, {"h": "Apostrophes", "b": "Use an apostrophe for **possession** (Macbeth's crown; the witches' prophecy, for several witches) or for **missing letters** (it's = it is). **Its** means belonging to it and has no apostrophe. A plain plural, such as witches, never needs one."}, {"h": "Spelling names and terms", "b": "Copy **names** exactly from your text: Priestley, Birling, Banquo, Lady Macbeth. Learn the words you use often, such as 'responsible' and 'definitely'. Watch **homophones**: their/there/they're, and affect (verb) against effect (usually a noun)."}, {"h": "Vary your sentences", "b": "Use **precise verbs** (suggests, reveals, implies) instead of repeating 'shows'. Mix short and longer sentences. Separate two full sentences with a full stop, or join them with a semicolon or a conjunction. A comma alone makes a **comma splice**, which is an error."}], "example": {"task": "How does Shakespeare present Macbeth's ambition? Build a model opening paragraph that would earn full AO4 marks.", "steps": [{"do": "Shakespeare presents Macbeth's ambition as a force that overpowers his conscience.", "why": "The possessive apostrophe in Macbeth's is correct. 'Presents' is a precise verb that opens a clear point."}, {"do": "In Act 1 Scene 7, he admits that his only spur is 'vaulting ambition'.", "why": "Capitals for Act and Scene, and a short quotation copied exactly inside quotation marks."}, {"do": "The word 'vaulting' suggests a reckless leap; ambition is thrilling but has lost its caution.", "why": "The semicolon joins two full, linked sentences. 'Its' has no apostrophe. 'Suggests' avoids repeating 'shows'."}, {"do": "The witches' prophecy plants the idea, and Lady Macbeth's pressure makes it hard to resist.", "why": "Witches' has its apostrophe after the s, for several witches. Names are spelled correctly. 'And' joins two clauses."}], "answer": "Shakespeare presents Macbeth's ambition as a force that overpowers his conscience. In Act 1 Scene 7, he admits that his only spur is 'vaulting ambition'. The word 'vaulting' suggests a reckless leap; ambition is thrilling but has lost its caution. The witches' prophecy plants the idea, and Lady Macbeth's pressure makes it hard to resist."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 write 'its caution' with no apostrophe?", "o": ["Apostrophes cannot go with the word 'it', because 'it' is not a name.", "A thing like ambition cannot own anything, so no possession is shown.", "'Its' shows the caution belongs to ambition; 'it's' would mean 'it is'.", "Apostrophes of possession only go after words that end in the letter s."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. 'It's' is correct when it means 'it is'. The issue is meaning, not the word 'it'. Check by swapping in 'it is'.", "Wrong. Ideas and objects can have things belonging to them. The caution does belong to ambition, so 'its' is needed.", "Right. 'Its' means belonging to it. Swapping in 'it is' gives 'ambition has lost it is caution', which fails, so no apostrophe.", "Wrong. Possession needs an apostrophe in nouns like Macbeth's. 'Its' is a special case: it never takes one for belonging."]}, "faded": {"task": "Write the next sentence of a paragraph on how Priestley presents Mrs Birling in An Inspector Calls. Choose the one that is fully accurate and builds the point.", "given": ["Priestley presents Mrs Birling as proud and unwilling to accept blame."], "q": "Which sentence should come next?", "o": ["She refuses Eva Smiths appeal for help; this reveals a cold attitude to the poor.", "She refuses Eva Smith's appeal for help, this reveals a cold attitude to the poor.", "She refuses Eva Smith's apeal for help; this reveals a cold attitude to the poor.", "She refuses Eva Smith's appeal for help; this reveals a cold attitude to the poor."], "a": 3, "f": ["Slip: 'Smiths' needs an apostrophe because the appeal belongs to Eva Smith. It should be 'Smith's'.", "Slip: a comma splice. Two full sentences are joined by a comma alone. Use a semicolon, a full stop or a conjunction.", "Slip: 'apeal' is misspelled. It should be 'appeal'. Repeated slips like this lower your AO4 mark.", "Right. The possessive apostrophe, the spelling and the semicolon are all correct, and the sentence gives evidence and a comment on Mrs Birling."]}, "trap": "Adding an apostrophe to a plain plural (witch's for witches), or mixing up its and it's. Test it: swap in 'it is'. If that fits, write it's. If not, write its.", "recap": "AO4 rewards accurate spelling and punctuation with varied vocabulary: proofread for apostrophes, names and comma splices, and keep your sentences varied."}
{"s": "english_lit", "t": "lit-un1", "kind": "writing", "why": "Question 1 is worth 24 of Section C's 32 marks and uses a poem you have never seen, so you need a method that works on any poem.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Read before you write", "b": "Read the poem twice. Notice the **title** first. Ask what the poem is about (its **subject**) and what the speaker feels. Look for a point where the mood or focus changes. Jot down two or three words or images that stand out. Plan before you write."}, {"h": "What Question 1 asks", "b": "The question is usually 'How does the poet present…?' You are rewarded for writing about **language** (word choice and imagery), **form** (the type of poem, stanzas, line length) and **structure** (how it begins, changes and ends). Always link these to the idea."}, {"h": "Quote, then explain effect", "b": "Make a point about the poem's idea. Support it with a short **quotation**. Then explain its **effect**: what it suggests and how it makes the reader feel. Naming a technique earns little by itself. Explain what it does in this poem."}, {"h": "Comment on structure", "b": "Look at how the poem moves. Does the focus **shift** from one thing to another? Do lines get shorter? Does the **ending** stay open or close firmly? Do not just say 'the poem has two stanzas'. Say what the pattern makes the reader notice."}], "example": {"task": "Lines from 'Low Tide': 'The sea drags its blanket from the sand. / I stay, counting the shells it left behind.' How does the poet present loss?", "steps": [{"do": "The poet presents loss as quiet and lingering, as if the speaker is left with only small reminders of what has gone.", "why": "A clear opening answers the question and states the idea and feeling before any detail."}, {"do": "The verb 'drags' makes the sea's leaving seem heavy and unwilling. The metaphor of a 'blanket' suggests the sea once gave comfort, so its removal feels like a loss of warmth.", "why": "It quotes briefly and explains what the words suggest, not just what they are."}, {"do": "'Counting the shells' suggests the speaker clings to tiny things, perhaps to keep the memory alive. 'Left behind' hints at being abandoned.", "why": "A second language point goes deeper into the speaker's feelings and links to the idea of loss."}, {"do": "The first line is about the sea, but the second begins 'I stay'. The focus shifts to the speaker, and the contrast between the sea's movement and the speaker's stillness stresses being left alone.", "why": "It comments on structure, a shift in focus, and links it to meaning."}], "answer": "The poet presents loss as quiet and lingering. 'Drags' makes the sea's leaving seem unwilling, and 'blanket' suggests lost comfort. 'Counting the shells' shows the speaker clinging to small reminders. The shift to 'I stay' contrasts the sea's movement with the speaker's stillness."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer explain what 'drags' suggests, not just call it a verb?", "o": ["Examiners reward naming the word class, so this adds extra marks to the answer.", "Marks are for explaining how a word shapes meaning and feeling, not for labelling it.", "Each quotation must be explained twice before it counts as analysis.", "The poet's life explains the word, so the answer is linking it to biography."], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Naming a word class earns little by itself. The marks come from saying what the word suggests and how it affects the reader.", "Right. The 'drags' sentence explains the effect: heavy, unwilling leaving. That is analysis. A label alone is only identification.", "Wrong. There is no rule about twice. One clear explanation of effect is enough for each quotation.", "Wrong. You are given no facts about the poet and need none. The analysis comes from the words on the page."]}, "faded": {"task": "Lines from 'Last Bus': 'The bus pulls away, its lights shrinking. / I stand holding the wave I did not finish.' How does the poet present parting?", "given": ["Opening: The poet presents parting as sudden, leaving the speaker with feelings still unspoken."], "q": "Which sentence should come next?", "o": ["The poet uses a metaphor here, which shows good use of poetic technique.", "The poet probably lost a friend, which is why the bus scene feels so sad.", "'Holding the wave I did not finish' shows a goodbye cut short, so parting feels sudden.", "The two lines are short and plain, which shows the poem is simple and easy to read."], "a": 2, "f": ["This names a technique but never says what it suggests or how it links to parting. Naming without effect earns few marks.", "This guesses at the poet's life. The poem gives no such facts. Stay with what the words suggest on the page.", "Right. It uses a short quotation and explains its effect: an unfinished wave suggests a rushed goodbye, which links back to the idea of sudden parting.", "This is vague. It comments on length but does not say what the lines make the reader feel about parting."]}, "trap": "Naming techniques without saying what they do. 'The poet uses a metaphor' earns little. Always add: this suggests…, which makes the reader feel…, and link it to the poem's idea.", "recap": "For each short quotation, explain what it suggests and how it builds the poem's idea and feeling, rather than just naming the technique."}
{"s": "english_lit", "t": "lit-un2", "kind": "writing", "why": "Question 2 of the unseen poetry section is worth 8 marks, and you earn them by comparing the poets' methods in both poems.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Compare methods, not stories", "b": "Question 2 asks how the poets present a shared idea. Do not retell either poem. Pick a **method**, such as word choice or imagery. Give a short **quotation**, then explain its **effect** on the reader. Your point should cover both poems."}, {"h": "Link both poems together", "b": "Refer to both poems in every paragraph. Use **linking phrases**: similarly, likewise, whereas, in contrast, however. Pair each point: one method in the first poem, a matching method in the second. Then say how their **effects** differ or match."}, {"h": "Language, structure and form", "b": "**Language** is word choice and imagery, such as personification or a simile. **Structure** is how the poem is organised: line breaks, pauses, shifts, endings. **Form** is its shape: stanzas, rhyme or free verse. Always say what the method makes the reader feel or think."}], "example": {"task": "Compare how the poets present winter. A: 'The frost grips the gate / and the street holds its breath.' B: 'Snow comes like a gift; / the whole town laughs awake.'", "steps": [{"do": "Both poets present winter as changing a place, but A makes it threatening whereas B makes it joyful.", "why": "Opens with a clear comparison of ideas across both poems in one sentence."}, {"do": "In A, the verb 'grips' makes the frost seem like a hand trapping the gate, and 'holds its breath' personifies the street as afraid, creating tension.", "why": "Names a method, quotes briefly and explains the effect on the reader."}, {"do": "In contrast, B uses the simile 'like a gift' to make the snow welcome, and 'laughs awake' personifies the town as happy, so the mood is warm.", "why": "Links back to A. Compares a matching method (personification) with a different effect."}, {"do": "Whereas A's sentence runs on across the line break, like a held breath, B's semicolon creates a pause before the town reacts, like a gift being opened.", "why": "Moves to structure. Compares line endings in both poems and explains the effect."}], "answer": "A full answer links each idea to a matching method in both poems. Each point has a short quotation, an effect and a linking word such as 'whereas' or 'in contrast'."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 begin with 'In contrast'?", "o": ["It shows that B is the better poem, so the answer can ignore A from here", "It signals a difference between B and A, so the answer stays a comparison", "It lets the answer skip the quotation, because the contrast is obvious", "It starts a section that only covers B, as examiners mark each poem separately"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. The phrase marks a difference, not a ranking. Q2 does not ask which poem is better, and A still needs to be linked in.", "Right. The phrase ties B's methods to A's and shows how they differ. That linking is what makes it a comparison.", "Wrong. A method still needs a close reference to the poem to support it. A linking phrase never replaces evidence.", "Wrong. Q2 is marked as one comparison, not two separate answers. Writing about each poem separately is a common way to lose marks."]}, "faded": {"task": "Compare how the poets present a city at night. C: 'The city never rests; / its engines grind the dark.' D: 'The city sleeps at last, / its windows dim as closing eyes.'", "given": ["Both poets give the city human qualities, but C makes it relentless whereas D makes it peaceful.", "In C, the verb 'grind' makes the engines sound harsh, and 'never rests' suggests a tireless, noisy city."], "q": "Which sentence should come next?", "o": ["D also uses a simile, 'dim as closing eyes', which is very effective and clever.", "In contrast, D shows a peaceful city, whereas C shows a noisy one.", "In contrast, D's simile 'dim as closing eyes' makes the windows sleepy and calm.", "Now D: the poet uses a simile in line two and the city sleeps in line one."], "a": 2, "f": ["Names a method but gives only vague praise. It does not say what the simile suggests, and it does not link back to C.", "This compares ideas only. It does not name a method or quote, so it earns little. A comparison of methods is needed.", "Right. It links to C with 'in contrast', names the simile, quotes it and explains the calm effect.", "This spots features and moves to D separately. It gives no effect and no link to C, so it is not a comparison."]}, "trap": "Writing about each poem in turn, or comparing only ideas ('A is sad, B is happy'). Instead, in each paragraph link a method in A with one in B, using a quotation and its effect.", "recap": "Compare methods in both poems in every paragraph: name the method, quote briefly, explain the effect, and link with a word like whereas."}
{"s": "history", "t": "el-2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions on the Elizabethans often ask you to explain why Catholics were seen as a danger to Elizabeth after 1580, and how real that danger was.", "hook": {"q": "In 1570 the Pope declared Elizabeth a heretic and said Catholics need not obey her. What was the main danger for her?", "o": ["Catholics would stop paying taxes to the Church of England", "Some Catholics might now feel they had a religious reason to plot against her", "Every Catholic in England would leave the country at once", "The Pope would take control of the English navy"], "a": 1, "f": ["Taxes were not the main worry. The danger was about loyalty and obedience, not money.", "Right. A ruler is in danger when people are told they need not obey her. Plots and rebellion became more likely.", "Most Catholics stayed in England. The danger came from those who stayed, not from leaving.", "The Pope had no navy to give. His power was over belief, which could still push people to act."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why Catholics worried Elizabeth", "b": "In 1570 the Pope **excommunicated** Elizabeth and told Catholics they need not obey her. Catholic Spain was a strong enemy. From 1574 **seminary priests**, and from 1580 **Jesuits**, all trained abroad, came in secret to keep Catholicism alive. The government saw them as a danger to the queen."}, {"h": "Enforcing the Church settlement", "b": "Elizabeth's Church settlement expected everyone to attend Church of England services. Catholics who refused were **recusants**. A law of 1581 fined them £20 a month. In 1585 it became **treason** for a Catholic priest trained abroad to be in England. Many priests were executed. Even before this law, Edmund Campion was executed in 1581."}, {"h": "Plots, spies and Mary", "b": "Mary, Queen of Scots, a Catholic with a claim to the throne, was held in England from 1568. She became the focus of plots. **Walsingham**'s spies watched her. In 1586 they found letters showing she backed the **Babington Plot** to kill Elizabeth. She was executed in February 1587."}, {"h": "Spain, the Armada and loyalty", "b": "England helped the Dutch rebels against Spain in 1585, and war with **Philip II** followed. Mary's death and Drake's raids angered him. In 1588 his **Armada** sailed to help invade England. It was defeated. Yet most English Catholics stayed **loyal** to Elizabeth, so the threat was real but limited."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why Mary, Queen of Scots was a threat to Elizabeth I.", "steps": [{"do": "Mary had a strong claim to the English throne. She was a great-granddaughter of Henry VII.", "why": "It opens with the root of the threat: a rival with a claim."}, {"do": "Many Catholics saw Elizabeth as illegitimate. They saw Mary as the true queen, so she became the hope of English Catholics.", "why": "It links her claim to religion, which explains why Catholics backed her."}, {"do": "From 1568 Mary was in England, so plotters could reach her by secret letters.", "why": "It shows the threat was practical, not only a claim on paper."}, {"do": "In 1586 she approved the Babington Plot to kill Elizabeth. Walsingham found the letters and she was executed in 1587.", "why": "Specific evidence with correct dates proves the threat was real."}], "answer": "Mary was a threat because she had a claim to the throne and Catholics saw her as the rightful queen. Being in England let plotters reach her. In 1586 she approved the Babington Plot, so Elizabeth had her executed in 1587."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention the Babington letters and the dates in the last step?", "o": ["The question asks for the date of Mary's execution", "Dates alone are enough to prove that Mary was a threat", "Specific, accurate evidence shows the threat was real and supports the point made", "The Babington Plot was the only way Mary threatened Elizabeth"], "a": 2, "f": ["The question asks why Mary was a threat, not for a date. Dates are used as support, not as the answer.", "Dates alone do not explain anything. They matter only because they back up a reason.", "Right. The letters show Mary moving from being a focus for plots to backing one. Accurate detail turns a claim into explained evidence.", "Mary was also a threat through her claim and Catholic hopes. The plot is the clearest evidence, not the only one."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why the Spanish Armada of 1588 was a threat to Elizabeth I.", "given": ["Philip II of Spain was Catholic and ruled the most powerful empire in Europe.", "He was angry at English help for the Dutch rebels (1585) and at Drake's raids on Spanish ships."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to explain why the Armada was a threat?", "o": ["The Armada was beaten at Gravelines, so the threat to England was small.", "Elizabeth made a famous speech to her troops at Tilbury.", "The Armada was to help a Spanish army cross to England, which could end her rule.", "The Armada sailed in 1587 after Drake attacked Cadiz."], "a": 2, "f": ["This describes the outcome. The question asks why it was a threat, which means what it could have done.", "The Tilbury speech is about how Elizabeth led. It does not explain what the danger from Spain was.", "Right. An invasion could remove Elizabeth and restore Catholicism. That is the threat, and it follows from Philip's anger.", "This has the wrong date. Cadiz was in 1587, but the Armada sailed in 1588, and the point does not explain the threat."]}, "trap": "Mixing up dates: Babington Plot 1586, Mary's execution 1587, Armada 1588. Also, do not write as if all Catholics were plotters. Most stayed loyal to Elizabeth.", "recap": "Catholics were a real but limited threat: Jesuits, plots around Mary and Spain's Armada were dangerous, but most English Catholics stayed loyal."}
{"s": "history", "t": "el-3", "kind": "idea", "why": "The exam asks you to describe how Elizabethans lived and explain why poverty grew and how it was tackled, using clear facts linked together.", "hook": {"q": "A town's population grows fast, but the amount of food grown stays much the same. What is likely to happen to food prices?", "o": ["They fall, because more shoppers means lower costs for sellers", "They stay the same, because farmers keep fixed prices", "They rise, because more people compete for the same food", "They fall at first, then stay low for many years"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. More buyers for the same food gives sellers power to charge more, not less. Costs do not fall just because there are more shoppers.", "Wrong. Farmers did not keep fixed prices. Food prices followed supply and demand, so they moved when the population changed.", "Right. When demand rises and supply does not, prices go up. This happened in Elizabethan England and hit the poor hardest.", "Wrong. Prices do not drop when demand rises and supply stays flat. The pressure on food keeps prices high or rising."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three levels of life", "b": "The **rich** (nobles and gentry) lived in large houses with many servants, ate plenty of meat and wore costly clothes. **Middling** people, such as yeomen, merchants and craftsmen, had comfortable homes and some servants. The **poor**, mostly labourers, lived in small cottages and spent most of their money on bread and basic food."}, {"h": "Family life", "b": "The father was head of the **household**, and a wife was under his authority. She ran the home, the food and the servants. Children were taught obedience. Many boys from middling families became **apprentices**, learning a trade. Poor children usually started work young to help the family."}, {"h": "Why poverty grew", "b": "The **population** rose, so there were more people than jobs. **Prices** rose faster than wages, so the poor could buy less. **Bad harvests** in the 1590s caused hunger. Enclosure of land for sheep cut farm work. Soldiers and sailors returning from war also struggled to find jobs."}, {"h": "Dealing with the poor", "b": "Officials divided the poor. The **impotent poor** could not work and were helped. The **idle poor**, fit but refusing work, were punished, for example by whipping. The **1601 Poor Law** made each parish collect a poor rate and appoint overseers, who gave relief to the impotent and work to the able."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why poverty increased in England in the 1590s.", "steps": [{"do": "Point 1: The population rose quickly, so there were more people than jobs.", "why": "It is a long-term cause that put pressure on jobs and food."}, {"do": "Point 2: Prices rose faster than wages, so even working families could buy less food.", "why": "It shows how rising prices made life harder even for people in work."}, {"do": "Point 3: Harvests failed in the 1590s, so grain was scarcer and dearer and some people went hungry.", "why": "It adds a short-term cause that made existing hardship worse."}, {"do": "Point 4: Landowners enclosed fields for sheep, which needed fewer workers, so many labourers lost their jobs.", "why": "It adds a separate cause, unemployment, and ends by linking it to poverty."}], "answer": "Poverty grew because the population rose quickly, so there were more people than jobs. Prices went up faster than wages, so families could buy less food. Bad harvests in the 1590s made food scarcer and dearer. Enclosure for sheep farming also cut farm work, so many labourers lost their jobs."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Point 2 say prices rose faster than wages?", "o": ["The queen cut wages by law to keep prices down", "Wages did not keep up, so even working families could afford less food", "Poor people stopped working when prices rose", "Prices rose only for the rich, who bought more meat"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Wages were not cut to control prices. The problem was that wages rose slowly while prices climbed fast.", "Right. If wages lag behind prices, the same pay buys less. That is why poverty grew even among people who worked.", "Wrong. This repeats the idea that poverty was caused by idleness. Point 2 is about wages failing to keep pace with prices.", "Wrong. Food prices rose for everyone. The poor were hit hardest because most of their money went on basic food."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why the government treated the impotent poor and the idle poor differently.", "given": ["Point 1: The impotent poor, such as the old and sick, could not work, so they were not blamed and were helped."], "q": "Which is the best Point 2?", "o": ["The idle poor were old and sick, so they were given a place in an almshouse.", "The idle poor lost jobs through bad harvests, so the law excused them.", "The idle poor were the same as the impotent poor, so both were whipped.", "The idle poor could work but refused, so they were blamed and punished."], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. This describes the impotent poor, not the idle poor. Old and sick people were helped, not called idle.", "Wrong. Elizabethan law did not excuse people who refused work. It judged the idle poor to be to blame.", "Wrong. This mixes up the two groups. The impotent poor were helped, and only those able to work but refusing were punished.", "Right. The idle poor were able to work but would not, so officials saw them as to blame and punished them. This contrasts with Point 1."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the impotent poor (cannot work, helped) with the idle poor (able but refusing, punished). Ask yourself: could this person work? If not, they were helped. If they refused, they were punished.", "recap": "Poverty grew as population and prices rose; the 1601 Poor Law had parishes help the impotent poor by a poor rate and punish the idle."}
{"s": "history", "t": "el-4", "kind": "idea", "why": "OCR asks you to explain why theatres were attacked or why witches were accused, so you need clear causes backed by specific evidence.", "hook": {"q": "Plague broke out in London in the 1590s. Why might city officials have wanted playhouses shut during an outbreak?", "o": ["Actors were thought to catch plague from the queen", "Plague only affected people who watched plays", "Crowds packed together could spread disease quickly", "Plays were illegal everywhere in England"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. There was no such belief. Officials worried about ordinary crowds, not about the queen or actors.", "Wrong. Plague affected everyone. Officials simply knew that crowds made it spread faster.", "Right. Large crowds stood or sat close together in a playhouse. Officials feared this would spread plague, so they closed theatres in outbreaks.", "Wrong. Plays were legal. Companies needed a noble patron and a licence, but acting was not banned."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why theatres were attacked", "b": "**Puritans** such as Philip Stubbes (*Anatomy of Abuses*, 1583) said plays were **immoral**. Boys played women, and plays showed lies and lust. Critics said people went to plays instead of church and skipped work. City leaders added that crowds spread **plague** and caused disorder."}, {"h": "Control of the stage", "b": "The queen and her court enjoyed plays. Companies needed a noble **patron** and a licence. The **Master of the Revels** checked scripts. The City of London could close theatres inside its walls, so playhouses such as the Rose and the Globe were built on **Bankside**, outside its control."}, {"h": "Puritans and pastimes", "b": "Puritans wanted a strict, godly **Sunday**. They disliked bear-baiting, dancing, gambling and heavy drinking. They said these led to sin and **idleness**. Many people ignored them and carried on. Puritan influence was stronger in some towns and in Parliament than at court."}, {"h": "Witches and their accusers", "b": "The **1563 Witchcraft Act** made killing by witchcraft punishable by death. Lesser harm meant prison. Most accusations came from **neighbours**, not the court. The accused were mostly **poor, older women**. A quarrel, often over refused help, was followed by misfortune, and the woman was blamed."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why Puritans attacked the theatres in Elizabethan England. (Use 3 to 4 linked points.)", "steps": [{"do": "Puritans thought plays were immoral. Stubbes wrote in 1583 that plays taught bad behaviour. Boys dressed as women, which Puritans disliked.", "why": "A belief plus named evidence shows you know the religious motive."}, {"do": "They said playgoing drew people away from church. Crowds went to plays when they should have been at sermons and prayers.", "why": "Links the attack to the Puritan concern with worship."}, {"do": "They also said plays drew people from work. Apprentices and labourers left their jobs for afternoon performances.", "why": "A second, practical cause. City leaders agreed with it."}, {"do": "Finally, crowds could spread plague and cause disorder. Packed playhouses were feared in outbreaks, and riots could start.", "why": "Shows the attack was also about public safety, not only religion."}], "answer": "Puritans like Stubbes thought plays were immoral and lured people from church and work. Crowds in playhouses also risked spreading plague and causing disorder, which is why City leaders joined the attack."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer end by mentioning plague and disorder?", "o": ["It shows the attack had safety reasons as well as religious ones", "It proves plague was caused by watching plays", "It shows the queen wanted all theatres closed", "It repeats the point about boys dressed as women"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Plague and disorder were worries for City officials as well as Puritans. This widens the explanation beyond religion.", "Wrong. Elizabethans did not know what caused plague. The point is that crowds were feared to spread it.", "Wrong. The queen enjoyed plays and protected the companies. Mixing up Puritan and royal views is a common slip.", "Wrong. That was a different point about morals. This step adds a new reason, which earns more marks."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why people in Elizabethan villages accused others of witchcraft.", "given": ["Point 1: Most accused were poor, older women, often widows who begged and had few protectors.", "Point 2: A quarrel, such as refused help, was followed by a death or illness, so the woman was blamed."], "q": "Which point should come next?", "o": ["The queen ordered judges to find witches to protect the court", "Matthew Hopkins toured villages hunting witches for payment", "Convicted witches were burned, which shows how much people feared them", "Belief in the Devil, and the 1563 Act let neighbours take cases to court"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Accusations came from neighbours, not the queen or court. Blaming the court is the commonest trap on this topic.", "Wrong. Hopkins was active in the 1640s, long after Elizabeth. It is the wrong period.", "Wrong. Witches in England were normally hanged, not burned. It also describes a punishment, not a cause of accusations.", "Right. Belief in the Devil made fears seem real. The 1563 Act gave neighbours a legal route to court. This adds a new cause."]}, "trap": "Do not say accusations came from the queen or court. Most came from neighbours in villages, and most accused were poor women. Also remember that English witches were hanged, not burned.", "recap": "Puritans and City leaders attacked theatres over immorality, missed church and work, and plague; witch accusations came from neighbours' quarrels with poor women."}
{"s": "history", "t": "el-5", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions on this topic ask you to explain why England looked overseas, or why Roanoke failed. You need names, dates and reasons ready.", "hook": {"q": "By 1580 Spain was growing rich on silver and gold from its American lands. What would England's leaders most want?", "o": ["To stay out of the Atlantic so that Spain would stay friendly", "To stop all foreign trade, as imported goods were thought harmful", "To win a share of overseas wealth and weaken Spain at the same time", "To buy Spanish silver, because England had no ships of its own"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Staying away would leave Spain's wealth and power growing. England backed captains who challenged Spain's hold on the Atlantic instead.", "Wrong. Merchants wanted more trade, not less. They sought new markets for English cloth and new goods such as silk and spices.", "Right. Wealth and rivalry with Spain drove English ventures overseas. Drake's voyage and the Roanoke colonies both came from these aims.", "Wrong. England did have ships and sailors, as the voyages of Drake and others show. The aim was to win wealth, not just to buy it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why go overseas?", "b": "England was smaller and poorer than **Spain**, which was rich from American silver. Elizabethans wanted **wealth**, new markets for English cloth and a way to weaken Spain. Some hoped to spread Protestant Christianity. Voyages were risky and costly, so merchants and nobles shared the **investment**."}, {"h": "Drake's voyage", "b": "Francis **Drake** sailed the Golden Hind round the world in 1577-80. On the way he raided Spanish ships and ports and came home with huge amounts of treasure. He was a **privateer**: a private captain who attacked enemy ships with the queen's approval. Spain called him a pirate. Elizabeth knighted him in 1581."}, {"h": "Raleigh and Roanoke", "b": "Walter **Raleigh** paid for colonies in North America but did not go himself. The first **Roanoke** colony (1585-86), led by Ralph Lane, left short of food. In 1587 John White led a second group. He sailed home for supplies, but the Armada delayed his return until 1590. He found the settlement deserted, with **CROATOAN** carved on a post."}, {"h": "Trade with the East", "b": "Merchants wanted **silk and spices**. The **Levant Company**, set up in 1592, traded with the Ottoman Empire in the eastern Mediterranean. The **East India Company**, founded in 1600, aimed to reach the East Indies by sea round Africa. Both were trading companies with a royal charter."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the English government backed voyages overseas in the years 1580-1603.", "steps": [{"do": "Wealth: Drake's 1577-80 voyage brought home Spanish treasure, so Elizabeth and investors saw that overseas ventures could pay.", "why": "A named example with dates proves the point."}, {"do": "Rivalry: Raiding Spanish ships hurt England's main enemy, and Raleigh hoped colonies in America would strengthen England against Spain.", "why": "This ties the reason to the conflict with Spain, not just money."}, {"do": "Trade: The Levant Company (1592) and East India Company (1600) sought silk and spices, bringing profit and new markets.", "why": "A second kind of aim, with detail."}, {"do": "Link: Profit from trade and plunder helped pay for ships and sailors, which in turn helped England stand up to Spain.", "why": "Joining reasons together explains. A plain list does not."}], "answer": "The government backed voyages for wealth, trade and rivalry with Spain. Drake's treasure showed they could pay. Roanoke aimed to strengthen England against Spain. New trading companies sought silk and spices. These aims fed each other, as profit built the strength to challenge Spain."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 1 name Drake's voyage and give its dates?", "o": ["Because exam questions only give marks for dates", "Because specific evidence backs up the reason and shows real knowledge", "Because Drake was the only Elizabethan who sailed overseas", "Because it shows that Drake was a better sailor than Raleigh"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Dates help, but marks come from explaining. The dates are there as evidence for the point, not as the aim.", "Right. A reason needs support. Naming the voyage and its dates shows the claim is true and gains marks for accurate knowledge.", "Wrong. Others sailed too, such as Frobisher. Drake is used because his voyage is a clear example of wealth from overseas.", "Wrong. The answer does not compare sailors. It picks Drake because his treasure shows why voyages were backed."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why the Roanoke colonies failed.", "given": ["First colony (1585-86): led by Ralph Lane, it ran short of food and fell out with local people, so the colonists left with Drake in 1586."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to explain the second colony?", "o": ["White returned in 1588 and found the settlers killed by Spanish soldiers.", "Raleigh sailed to Roanoke himself in 1590 and brought the settlers home.", "Drake took the 1587 settlers home, as he had the first colony in 1586.", "In 1587 White left for supplies, but the Armada kept him away until 1590."], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. White did not return in 1588, and he did not find the settlers killed. In 1590 the settlement was deserted and the fate of the settlers unknown.", "Wrong. Raleigh paid for the colonies but did not go to Roanoke. White made the 1590 voyage and found the place empty.", "Wrong. Drake took the first colonists home in 1586. The 1587 group was left behind, which is why White's later return matters.", "Right. White went home for supplies in 1587, but the Armada crisis delayed his return until 1590. By then the settlers had gone."]}, "trap": "Do not say White found the settlers killed, or that Raleigh sailed round the world. Drake did that. Raleigh paid for Roanoke but did not go. Say the settlement was deserted and the fate unknown.", "recap": "England looked overseas for wealth, trade and to weaken Spain: Drake brought back treasure, but Roanoke failed, found deserted with CROATOAN carved there."}
{"s": "history", "t": "hau-1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Site questions ask why Kenilworth was built where it was and how it changed, and marks go to reasons backed by evidence, not description.", "hook": {"q": "You are building a castle in about 1120 and expect to be attacked. Which spot gives you the best chance of holding out?", "o": ["A dry, flat plain far from any river, so enemies cannot hide", "Slightly raised ground with streams close by for fresh water", "A hilltop with no water nearby, so it is hard to reach", "A marshy valley where stone and timber are hard to find"], "a": 1, "f": ["A flat, dry plain gives no natural protection, and a castle needs water. Enemies could approach easily.", "Right. Raised ground helps defence, and streams give water for a long siege. They could later be dammed to make a barrier too.", "Height helps, but without water the garrison would be forced to surrender in a siege. Defence needs supplies as well as walls.", "Marsh may slow attackers, but building stone and timber are needed to build and repair a castle. Resources matter too."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why the site is here", "b": "Lords chose a **location** for reasons. **Defence**: raised ground and natural barriers. Water supply: streams for drinking and, later, the Mere. **Resources**: local stone, timber and farmland. Trade routes: roads helped supplies and control. Name the feature, then say what it did for the castle."}, {"h": "Origins: who and when", "b": "Around 1120, **Geoffrey de Clinton** began Kenilworth. He was an important official of **Henry I**, who rewarded him with land in Warwickshire. He or his son built a strong stone **Great Tower** (keep). It was a stronghold and a sign of the family's power over the area."}, {"h": "Change over time", "b": "The castle changed as owners' needs changed. In the early 1200s King John strengthened it as a **fortress** and created the Mere. In 1266 it held out for about six months before surrendering. John of Gaunt (1300s) and Robert Dudley (1500s) built grand **palace** rooms. After the Civil War, Parliament had it **slighted**."}, {"h": "Showing change with sources", "b": "One source shows one moment. To show **change**, compare sources from different dates, such as a plan, a map and a painting. Say what is different, then give the **reason** for the difference. Change without a reason is only description."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why Kenilworth Castle was built where it was.", "steps": [{"do": "Point: The castle stands on slightly raised ground, which made it harder to attack and gave a good view of anyone approaching.", "why": "This names a feature of the site and says what it did for defence."}, {"do": "Point: Streams ran close by, giving a water supply. In the early 1200s King John had them dammed to make the Mere, which kept attackers back from much of the castle.", "why": "A second reason, linked to evidence: water for the garrison and a defence."}, {"do": "Point: Geoffrey de Clinton, Henry I's official, held land in Warwickshire. A castle here let him control his estates and show his power.", "why": "This links the location to the builder's purpose, not just the landscape."}, {"do": "Link: So the site suited both defence and control of land, which is why a castle was begun here around 1120.", "why": "A final link ties the points together and answers 'why here?'"}], "answer": "Kenilworth was built on raised ground, which was hard to attack. Streams gave water and later formed the Mere as a barrier. Clinton held land here, so a castle let him control his estates and show his power."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does each point in the model answer end by saying what the feature did for the castle?", "o": ["Because examiners count the number of features named, not the reasons", "Because a longer answer always gets a higher mark in this section", "Because the dates of each feature must come before any reasons", "Because 'explain' needs a reason, and naming a feature alone only describes"], "a": 3, "f": ["Naming many features is not enough. Marks go to explaining what each feature did, not to counting them.", "Length does not earn marks by itself. Each sentence must do something useful, such as give a reason.", "Dates help, but they are not what makes an explanation. The reason is the key part.", "Right. 'Explain' means giving reasons. Saying what the feature did (harder to attack, supplied water) turns description into explanation."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why Kenilworth Castle changed in the 1570s.", "given": ["Point: Robert Dudley, Earl of Leicester, added new buildings and gardens."], "q": "Which sentence is the best next step?", "o": ["He needed thicker walls because a siege was expected, so he added stronger defences.", "The new buildings were large and grand, with big windows and fine stonework.", "He wanted a grand home to host Elizabeth I, who visited in 1575, so he built for comfort and show.", "Parliament ordered the work so that no army could use the castle as a base."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong reason. The 1570s were not a time of siege, and Dudley's work was for comfort and show, not defence. Do not assume every change was for defence.", "This only describes the buildings. It gives no reason for the change, so it does not explain anything.", "Right. It gives a reason linked to the point: Dudley wanted to impress the queen and her court, so he built for luxury, not defence.", "This mixes up the periods. Parliament had the castle slighted after the Civil War, around 1650, not in the 1570s."]}, "trap": "Describing the site without explaining why. 'There was a lake' earns little. Say what the lake did and why it mattered. To show change, use sources from different dates, not one.", "recap": "Name a feature or change, say what it did and why, and use evidence from different dates to show change."}
{"s": "history", "t": "hau-2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask who used Kenilworth, what happened there and how typical or important it was, so you need named facts and comparisons.", "hook": {"q": "A castle gets big glass windows, a pleasure garden and guest lodgings. What was its owner most likely using it for?", "o": ["Holding out against a long siege by an army", "Entertaining important guests and showing off wealth", "Storing weapons and supplies for a large army", "Housing the farm workers of a nearby village"], "a": 1, "f": ["Defence needs thick walls and few openings. Big glass windows and gardens make a castle easier to attack, so defence was not the main aim.", "Right. Glass, gardens and guest rooms are for comfort and display. This is how some owners used Kenilworth later, as a residence rather than just a fortress.", "Weapon stores need strong, plain storerooms. Windows, gardens and guest lodgings do not help with that, so this does not fit the features.", "Farm workers would not live in lodgings built for guests. Fine windows and gardens point to wealthy visitors, not farm labour."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "A fortress and a home", "b": "Kenilworth began in the 1120s as a Norman **fortress**. Its keep and water defences made it hard to attack. In 1266 rebels held it against Henry III's army for about six months, one of the longest **sieges** in English medieval history. Later owners also used it as a **residence**, to live in and to entertain."}, {"h": "People and key moments", "b": "**John of Gaunt**, a powerful royal duke, built a grand Great Hall in the late 1300s. **Robert Dudley**, Earl of Leicester, added lodgings and gardens to host Elizabeth I in 1575. After the Civil War, Parliament carried out **slighting**: it damaged the castle on purpose so it could not be a stronghold again."}, {"h": "Typical or important?", "b": "Judging **typical** means comparing Kenilworth with other castles of its type and period. Many began as Norman fortresses and were later altered, so that is typical. A palace-style rebuild for a royal visit is unusual. **Important** is different. It is about significance, such as the 1266 siege. A site can be important but not typical."}, {"h": "Which evidence answers what", "b": "**Written records** from the time, such as accounts and letters, best show who used the castle and why. **Physical remains**, such as walls and windows, show what was built and how it looked. Use both, but go to the records for people and purposes."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why Kenilworth Castle is important as a historic site. Use the people, events and features you have studied.", "steps": [{"do": "Start with an event. Rebels held Kenilworth for about six months in the 1266 siege against Henry III's army.", "why": "A named key moment gives precise knowledge, which earns marks."}, {"do": "Add the people. John of Gaunt built a grand Great Hall, and Robert Dudley entertained Elizabeth I there in 1575.", "why": "Naming who used the site and what they did shows its links to powerful people."}, {"do": "Link to features. The Great Hall and Dudley's lodgings and gardens show the castle changing from fortress to grand residence.", "why": "Tying features to use shows how the site was used over time."}, {"do": "Compare. Few castles were rebuilt on such a scale to host a monarch, so Kenilworth is unusual as well as important.", "why": "Comparing with similar sites is how you judge what is typical or special."}], "answer": "Kenilworth is important for what happened there and who used it. Rebels held it in the 1266 siege. John of Gaunt and Robert Dudley turned it into a grand residence, and Dudley hosted Elizabeth I in 1575. Few castles were rebuilt on this scale, so it is unusual and important."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the last step say few castles were rebuilt on such a scale?", "o": ["It shows Kenilworth is typical, because it matches the other castles", "It proves the castle is important simply because it is old", "It means evidence from the site itself is no longer needed at all", "It compares Kenilworth with similar castles, which is how you judge what is special"], "a": 3, "f": ["This reverses the point. Few castles were rebuilt like this, so Kenilworth is unusual. Typical would mean it matched the others.", "Age does not decide importance. Importance comes from events, people and features. The step makes a comparison, not an argument about age.", "The step adds to the evidence from the site. It does not replace it. Judgements need both the site's facts and a comparison.", "Right. Typical and special are judged by comparing with similar sites of the same type and period. The comparison shows what Kenilworth shares and what is unusual."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the use of Kenilworth Castle changed between the 1200s and the 1600s.", "given": ["Point 1: In 1266 it was a fortress. Rebels held it for about six months against the king's army.", "Point 2: By the late 1300s John of Gaunt had built a Great Hall, so it was also a grand residence."], "q": "Which is the best next point?", "o": ["By 1575 Dudley had strengthened the walls to hold out against a siege by Elizabeth I.", "By 1575 Dudley had slighted the castle so that it could never be used as a fort again.", "By 1575 Dudley had added lodgings and gardens to host Elizabeth I in great style.", "By 1575 Elizabeth I had moved in for good and made Kenilworth her main palace."], "a": 2, "f": ["This gets Dudley's purpose wrong. He was Elizabeth's favourite and built for comfort and display, not to defend against her.", "This confuses Dudley with Parliament. Slighting was done after the Civil War, in the 1600s, not by Dudley in the 1570s.", "Right. This is accurate and continues the story of change from fortress to residence, with a clear reason: hosting the queen in 1575.", "This overstates it. Elizabeth I visited in 1575 and was not based there. Kenilworth was never her main palace."]}, "trap": "Judging a site by looking at it alone. Typical means compared with similar sites. Important means significant for events or people. Kenilworth can be important yet unusual, so compare before you call it typical.", "recap": "Typical means compared with similar sites and important means significant, so name people, events and features and use written records for who and why."}
{"s": "history", "t": "hau-3", "kind": "idea", "why": "The site question in the OCR exam asks what remains show, what they leave unclear, and how far you can trust a reconstruction of the site.", "hook": {"q": "A castle's roof, floors and furniture are long gone. A museum draws a picture of a room inside it. Where do the furniture details most likely come from?", "o": ["The stone walls, which record exactly what stood in the room", "The artist's imagination, with no evidence needed at all", "Written records and finds from similar places, plus some guesswork", "Old photographs of the room taken the day it was finished"], "a": 2, "f": ["Walls show size and layout, not the furniture. Lost items leave little trace in stone, so the detail has to come from elsewhere.", "Artists usually work from evidence. Pure imagination would make the picture worthless to historians.", "Right. Reconstructions combine what survives with written sources and similar sites. Gaps are then filled with informed guesses.", "Photography did not exist when castles were built, so no such photographs can exist."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What remains show", "b": "Physical **remains** such as walls, fireplaces, windows and gardens give clues about **everyday life**. Their size and layout hint at how many people lived there and how the household worked. A huge kitchen suggests a big household. These are clues, not proof."}, {"h": "What they cannot show", "b": "Remains rarely show **who** people were, what they thought, or **why** changes happened. Parts are lost, damaged or rebuilt. Remains also raise **questions**: when was this built, why was this door blocked, who used this room? Answering them needs other sources."}, {"h": "Reconstructions are interpretations", "b": "A **reconstruction** is a drawing, model or rebuilt part showing how a site may have looked. It uses remains, written sources and similar sites. Gaps are filled with **informed guesses**. So it is an **interpretation**, not a photograph of the past."}, {"h": "Pros and cons of visiting", "b": "Visiting lets you judge **scale, layout and setting**, and see real material. But remains are **incomplete**, later changes can mislead, and sites often tell you more about the powerful than ordinary people. Check what you see against written sources."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how the physical remains of a site such as Kenilworth Castle shape reconstructions of how it looked.", "steps": [{"do": "Surviving walls, windows and foundations fix the size and layout, so a reconstruction has to fit them.", "why": "Remains are real evidence. A reconstruction that ignored them would not be believable."}, {"do": "Details such as fireplaces, window shapes and the style of the stonework show how rooms were used and roughly when they were built.", "why": "This links the remains to everyday life and dating, which makes the picture more accurate."}, {"do": "Where roofs, floors or gardens have gone, reconstructions use written sources. The Elizabethan garden at Kenilworth drew on a description written in 1575.", "why": "Remains alone cannot show lost features, so other sources fill the gaps."}, {"do": "Even so, parts are informed guesses, so the reconstruction is an interpretation that may change if new evidence appears.", "why": "This shows you understand its limits, which examiners reward."}], "answer": "Remains fix the size, layout and style of a reconstruction. Written sources fill gaps where features are lost, as with the 1575 garden description. But guesses remain, so a reconstruction is an interpretation that can change."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer bring in a written description of the garden at the third step?", "o": ["Written sources are always more reliable than physical remains", "Remains cannot show lost features, so other sources help fill the gaps", "Physical remains are not real evidence about a castle", "A reconstruction has to be an exact copy of the past"], "a": 1, "f": ["No source is always more reliable. Remains and writing each have limits, and historians use them together.", "Right. Lost features leave little or no trace. Written sources and similar sites give clues to fill the gaps.", "Remains are real evidence. They are incomplete, not worthless. This option confuses incomplete with unusable.", "Nobody can copy the past exactly. Reconstructions include guesses, so they are interpretations, not exact copies."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a reconstruction of a castle kitchen is an interpretation.", "given": ["Large fireplaces and ovens survive, which show where cooking happened.", "But the roof, tables and tools have all gone."], "q": "What should the next point say?", "o": ["So the reconstruction is a photo-like record of how the kitchen looked", "So the missing items prove the kitchen was hardly ever used", "So historians cannot use the kitchen as evidence at all", "So the gaps are filled with informed guesses from other sources"], "a": 3, "f": ["This treats a reconstruction as a photograph. The gaps mean it cannot be an exact record.", "Missing items do not prove little use. Wood and tools rot or get removed, so absence is weak evidence.", "The surviving fireplaces are still useful evidence. Incomplete evidence is not useless evidence.", "Right. Gaps are filled using written sources and similar kitchens. These guesses are why the reconstruction is an interpretation."]}, "trap": "Treating a reconstruction as a photograph of the past. Remains are real but incomplete, so say that gaps are filled with informed guesses, and check them against written sources.", "recap": "Remains give real clues about daily life, but a reconstruction fills the gaps with informed guesses, so it is an interpretation."}
{"s": "history", "t": "ma-1", "kind": "idea", "why": "OCR period study questions ask you to explain causes and consequences, so you need linked reasons for US growth, cotton, slavery and Native removal, 1789–1838.", "hook": {"q": "A new machine cleans seeds from cotton many times faster than hand work. Cleaning was the slowest job. What would planters most likely do?", "o": ["Grow less cotton, because the machine needs fewer workers", "Free their enslaved workers, because the machine replaces them", "Grow more cotton and look for more land and more workers", "Switch to growing wheat, because cotton prices would fall"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Faster cleaning makes cotton cheaper to produce and more profitable, so planters grow more, not less.", "Wrong. The machine only did the cleaning. Fields still needed planting and picking by hand, so workers were still wanted.", "Right. Cheaper, faster cleaning made cotton more profitable. Planters wanted more land to grow it and more labour to work it.", "Wrong. Faster cleaning made cotton more profitable, not less, so there was no reason to switch crops."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Louisiana Purchase, 1803", "b": "In 1803 **France** sold the **Louisiana Territory** to the USA. President Jefferson made the deal. Napoleon needed money for war with Britain. The purchase roughly doubled the size of the USA. It opened huge areas of farmland west of the Mississippi to settlers."}, {"h": "Cotton gin and slavery", "b": "The **cotton gin** (1793) cleaned seeds from cotton far faster than hand work. Cotton became very profitable. Planters spread into new land in the Deep South. Planting and picking still needed many hands, so demand for **enslaved labour** rose. The graph (US census) shows enslaved people rose from under 1 million (1790) to 2.5 million (1840): slavery grew stronger, not weaker.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [1790, 1840], "y": [0, 3], "xstep": 10, "ystep": 0.5, "xlabel": "census year", "ylabel": "enslaved population / millions", "lines": [{"pts": [[1790, 0.7], [1800, 0.9], [1810, 1.2], [1820, 1.5], [1830, 2], [1840, 2.5]], "label": "Enslaved", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1793, 0], [1793, 3]], "label": "Gin 1793", "c": 1}]}}, {"h": "The Indian Removal Act, 1830", "b": "Settlers and planters wanted the land of Native peoples in the South-East, such as the **Cherokee**. The **Indian Removal Act** (1830), backed by President Jackson, let the president swap their eastern land for land west of the Mississippi. The forced Cherokee removal of 1838–39 became the **Trail of Tears**."}, {"h": "One cause links them", "b": "All three developments are linked by **land**. Louisiana added huge areas of land. Cotton made land and enslaved labour more valuable. Settlers then pressed for Native land in the east. In an exam answer, build a **chain**: this led to that, which led to this."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why slavery grew in the southern states after 1793.", "steps": [{"do": "The cotton gin (1793) cleaned seeds from cotton far faster than people could by hand.", "why": "Starts with the cause and says exactly what changed."}, {"do": "This made cotton much more profitable, so planters wanted to grow much more of it.", "why": "Links the invention to a result. Each point must lead to the next."}, {"do": "Planters needed more land, so cotton farming spread west into the Deep South.", "why": "Shows how growth spread, using the land theme."}, {"do": "The gin only cleaned cotton. Planting and picking still needed many workers, so demand for enslaved labour rose.", "why": "This is the key link to slavery."}, {"do": "Enslaved people were sold from older states to the Deep South, so slavery grew and became more firmly rooted.", "why": "Ends on the outcome the question asks about."}], "answer": "The cotton gin (1793) sped up cleaning, so cotton became more profitable and planters spread into new land in the Deep South. The fields still needed many workers, so demand for enslaved labour rose and slavery grew."}, "selfx": {"q": "Step 4 says planting and picking still needed many workers. Why does this matter to the answer?", "o": ["The gin often broke down, so planters had to go back to hand work", "A law of 1793 forced planters to use enslaved workers in the fields", "The gin made cotton harder to pick, so more workers were needed", "The gin only sped up cleaning, so more cotton meant more field labour"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. The gin worked well, and that is the point. Its success made cotton profitable. It did not break down.", "Wrong. No such law existed. Planters chose enslaved labour because cotton was so profitable.", "Wrong. The gin did not affect picking. Picking stayed the same, but there was far more cotton to pick.", "Right. Only the cleaning was faster. More cotton meant more planting and picking, so planters wanted more enslaved labour."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why the Indian Removal Act was passed in 1830.", "given": ["Point 1: Settlers and planters in the South-East wanted the fertile land held by Native peoples such as the Cherokee.", "Point 2: Cotton growing was spreading, so demand for land kept rising."], "q": "Which point should come next to link the chain to the Act itself?", "o": ["The Supreme Court ordered the removal, so Congress had to pass the Act.", "Jackson backed the settlers, so the Act let him swap Native land for land in the west.", "The Cherokee agreed to move freely, so the Act only made their choice legal.", "Jackson wanted to protect Native land, so the Act moved the settlers out west."], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. The Supreme Court did not order removal. In 1832 it sided with the Cherokee, and Jackson did not enforce that ruling.", "Right. This links settler demand to government action. The Act let the president swap eastern Native land for land west of the Mississippi.", "Wrong. Many Native peoples did not agree to move. The Act was used to force removal, as in the Trail of Tears.", "Wrong. The Act did the opposite. It cleared Native peoples from eastern land, helping settlers rather than protecting Native land."]}, "trap": "Thinking the cotton gin made slavery less needed. It cleaned cotton faster, which made cotton more profitable. Planters then wanted more land and more enslaved workers, so demand for enslaved labour rose.", "recap": "Land hunger linked everything: Louisiana added land, cotton made land and slavery more valuable, and the 1830 Act cleared Native peoples from eastern land."}
{"s": "history", "t": "ma-2", "kind": "idea", "why": "OCR often asks you to explain why people moved west or what happened there, so tie each accurate fact to a reason using 'because'.", "hook": {"q": "The Plains were open grassland with few trees. Huge herds of buffalo moved across them with the seasons. Which way of life suited people there best?", "o": ["Building permanent towns beside one river", "Farming one fixed field all year round", "Moving camp to follow the herds", "Living by fishing in the sea"], "a": 2, "f": ["Permanent towns would be left behind when the herds moved. Hunters who relied on buffalo needed to travel with them.", "Some peoples did farm, but groups such as the Sioux relied on hunting herds. A fixed field did not fit that way of life.", "Right. Peoples such as the Sioux were nomadic. They followed the buffalo and used tipis that were easy to move.", "The Plains are far inland, so sea fishing makes no sense. Food came from the buffalo on the grassland."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Plains peoples and the buffalo", "b": "Peoples such as the Sioux, Cheyenne and Comanche were **nomadic**. They followed the **buffalo** herds, hunting on horseback. Almost every part was used: meat for food, hides for **tipis** and clothes, bones for tools. Tipis could be packed up fast when the herds moved. Buffalo also mattered in their beliefs."}, {"h": "Journeys to Oregon and California", "b": "From the 1840s, settlers left Missouri on the **Oregon Trail**, about 2,000 miles by **wagon train**. Many wanted fertile farmland. Some believed in **Manifest Destiny**: that the USA should spread to the Pacific. The trip took four to six months. Disease, rivers and mountains were the main dangers."}, {"h": "Mormons settle Utah", "b": "**Mormons** were driven out of Missouri and Illinois because of their beliefs. After Joseph Smith was killed in 1844, **Brigham Young** led them west. They reached the Great Salt Lake Valley in 1847. They shared the work and used **irrigation** to farm dry land, building a community of their own."}, {"h": "The California Gold Rush", "b": "Gold was found at Sutter's Mill in January 1848. In 1849 thousands of **forty-niners** rushed to California from the USA and abroad. **Mining camps** and San Francisco grew fast, with little law. Native Californians lost land and many lives. California became a state in 1850."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why California changed so quickly between 1848 and 1860.", "steps": [{"do": "Gold was found at Sutter's Mill in January 1848. News spread, and in 1849 thousands of forty-niners set off for California.", "why": "It starts with the trigger, the cause of everything that follows."}, {"do": "Because so many came, the population grew hugely. San Francisco grew from a small settlement into a busy city that supplied the miners.", "why": "It links the trigger to a result with 'because' and adds specific detail."}, {"do": "Mining camps grew faster than government, so there was little law. Crime was common, and some communities formed vigilance committees to punish it themselves.", "why": "It shows a further result of rapid growth: a lack of order."}, {"do": "Native Californians suffered. Miners took their land, and violence and disease killed many.", "why": "It covers a different group affected, which shows range."}, {"do": "The population was so large that California became a state in 1850, without first being a territory.", "why": "It ends on a lasting political result and links size to status."}], "answer": "Gold in 1848 brought thousands of migrants. The population soared and San Francisco boomed. Mining camps had little law, and Native Californians lost land and lives. The population grew so large that California became a state in 1850."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer put the population surge (step 2) before the lack of law (step 3)?", "o": ["The surge caused the lack of law, so the points form a chain of cause and effect", "Examiners require population facts to come before any mention of crime", "Population is always the most important factor in a Gold Rush answer", "The order does not matter because each point stands alone"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Fast growth meant government could not keep up. Linked points like this show explanation, not just a list of facts.", "There is no such rule. The order works because one point causes the next, not because of a set sequence.", "No factor is automatically the most important. The surge comes first here because it leads to the next point.", "Separate points are only a list. Marks go for explanation, so each point should connect to the one before."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why the journey along the Oregon Trail was dangerous.", "given": ["Point 1: Disease. Cholera spread easily in crowded wagon trains, and there were few doctors on the trail."], "q": "Which is the best next point?", "o": ["Attacks by Plains peoples killed most travellers, so the trail was dangerous.", "The railway was crowded and slow, so many travellers fell ill on the way.", "The journey was long and hard, which made it dangerous for the travellers.", "Rivers and mountains: wagons overturned at crossings, and early snow trapped parties."], "a": 3, "f": ["This is a common myth. Disease killed far more travellers than attacks, and many Plains peoples traded with wagon trains.", "This is an anachronism. There was no railway to the West in the 1840s. Travellers went in wagon trains.", "This only restates the question. It gives no specific danger, so it earns little credit.", "Right. It names a new danger with detail: wagons overturned at river crossings, and early snow in the mountains could trap parties."]}, "trap": "Saying Mormons went west for gold or cheap land. Their main reason was religious persecution in Missouri and Illinois. Start any Utah answer with that, then add Joseph Smith's death.", "recap": "On the Plains, peoples followed the buffalo; settlers went west for land, Mormons for religious freedom, and gold made California boom."}
{"s": "history", "t": "ma-3", "kind": "idea", "why": "OCR asks you to explain why the war began and to judge how free Black Americans really were after 1865, and this topic covers both.", "hook": {"q": "Lincoln won the 1860 election opposing the spread of slavery into new territories. How did many Southern leaders react?", "o": ["They accepted him, as he planned to end slavery at once everywhere", "They saw a threat to slavery's future, and seven states left the Union", "They ignored it, since a president had no say over new states", "They asked Britain to take over the Southern states"], "a": 1, "f": ["Lincoln did not promise to end slavery where it already existed. He opposed its spread. Southern leaders still feared it would weaken slavery over time.", "Right. Many Southern leaders feared slavery would be weakened and the South outvoted. Seven states seceded by February 1861.", "The president's views mattered because new states would change the balance of power in Congress. That is why the issue was so tense.", "Britain was not asked to take over. The Southern states formed their own Confederacy instead."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Slavery and the sections", "b": "The South's economy relied on **slavery**, especially on cotton plantations. The North had more industry and growing opposition to slavery. Each new western state raised a question: would it be a **free state** or a **slave state**? The answer shifted the balance of power in Congress."}, {"h": "From election to war", "b": "Abraham Lincoln, a Republican, won the 1860 election. He opposed slavery spreading to new territories. By February 1861, seven Southern states had **seceded** (left the Union) and formed the **Confederacy**. On 12 April 1861 Confederate forces attacked **Fort Sumter**, a Union fort. War began. Four more states then joined."}, {"h": "Freedom in law", "b": "The **Emancipation Proclamation** (1863) freed enslaved people in rebel areas. The **13th Amendment** (1865) ended slavery everywhere. The 14th (1868) gave citizenship and the 15th (1870) gave Black men the vote. The **Freedmen's Bureau** helped with schools and work. Black men were elected to office in the South."}, {"h": "Limits to freedom", "b": "**Black Codes** in 1865–66 limited freed people's rights. The **Ku Klux Klan** used violence to stop Black voting. Many freed people became **sharecroppers**, often tied to landowners by debt. In 1877 federal troops left the South, and white Democrats took back control."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the Civil War began in 1861.", "steps": [{"do": "Slavery divided the country. The Southern economy depended on enslaved labour, while many Northerners wanted slavery limited.", "why": "This is the underlying disagreement. Every later point builds on it."}, {"do": "The dispute grew over new western territories. Each new state would be free or slave, which changed the balance of power in Congress.", "why": "It shows how the disagreement became a political fight over power."}, {"do": "Lincoln, who opposed slavery spreading, won in 1860. Southern leaders feared for slavery, so seven states seceded and formed the Confederacy.", "why": "This links the dispute to the decision to leave the Union."}, {"do": "Lincoln would not accept secession. On 12 April 1861 Confederate forces attacked Fort Sumter, and the war began.", "why": "This is the immediate trigger, the event that turned a crisis into war."}], "answer": "The war began because slavery and its spread divided North and South. Lincoln's 1860 election led seven Southern states to secede. When the Confederates attacked Fort Sumter in April 1861, the crisis became war."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer put the dispute over new territories before Lincoln's election?", "o": ["Because the territories dispute directly caused the attack on Fort Sumter", "Because Lincoln was elected before the territories were settled", "Because that dispute explains why Lincoln's win alarmed the South", "Because the territories dispute mattered more than Lincoln's election"], "a": 2, "f": ["Fort Sumter was triggered by secession and Lincoln's refusal to accept it. The territories dispute was an earlier, long-term cause.", "The point is about linking causes, not dates. The territories dispute had been going on for years before 1860.", "Right. Lincoln's win only alarmed the South because of the earlier fight over slavery in new territories. The order builds a chain of causes.", "The answer does not rank the causes here. The order is there to link them."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why many freed people became sharecroppers after the Civil War.", "given": ["Point 1: Slavery ended in 1865, but freed people had little land or money.", "Point 2: Many Southern landowners had land but little cash to pay wages."], "q": "Which point should come next to link the argument?", "o": ["Landowners paid cash wages, so freed people saved up and bought their own farms", "The Freedmen's Bureau gave each family land, so they rented it out to landowners", "Northern banks lent freed people money, so they did not need to farm for landowners", "Landowners let families farm plots for a share of the crop, meeting both sides' needs"], "a": 3, "f": ["This contradicts Point 2. Landowners lacked cash, and most freed people could not save enough to buy land.", "The Bureau did not give every family land. Most freed people stayed landless, which is why they needed another way to farm.", "There was no wide scheme of Northern bank loans for freed people. Lack of money was the problem.", "Right. Sharecropping let freed people work without money or land, and gave landowners workers without cash wages. It links both given points."]}, "trap": "Treating the 13th Amendment or the Proclamation as full freedom. Freed people had rights on paper but little land, faced Black Codes and violence, and sharecropping often tied them to debt. Write 'freedom, but limited'.", "recap": "Slavery and its spread caused the split that led to Fort Sumter in 1861; after 1865, freedom was real in law but limited in life."}
{"s": "history", "t": "ma-4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to explain why things happened on the Plains, using linked facts on settlers, railroads, ranchers and the Indian Wars.", "hook": {"q": "The Great Plains had almost no trees. In 1865 a homesteader needs a house. What is he most likely to build it from?", "o": ["Timber cut from nearby forests", "Blocks of turf cut from the ground", "Bricks sent in by the new railroad", "Stone quarried from the Black Hills"], "a": 1, "f": ["There were hardly any forests on the Plains. That shortage of wood is the problem the question points to.", "Right. With no wood, homesteaders cut thick turf and stacked it as walls. These were called sod houses.", "Railroads did bring some goods. But bricks were costly and heavy to ship, and most poor homesteaders could not afford them.", "The Black Hills were far away and Sioux land. Most homesteaders could not reach them or afford to haul stone."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Homestead Act and railroads", "b": "The **Homestead Act** (1862) gave 160 acres to settlers who lived on the land and farmed it for five years. Railroads carried settlers west and sold them land. The Union Pacific and Central Pacific lines joined at **Promontory, Utah**, in 1869."}, {"h": "Life as a homesteader", "b": "Few trees grew on the Plains, so many homesteaders built **sod houses** from cut turf. Water was scarce and the weather was extreme. Swarms of **grasshoppers** ate crops in the 1870s. Farming was hard and many families failed. Those who stayed turned grassland into farms."}, {"h": "Ranches and cow towns", "b": "After 1867, Texan **longhorn** cattle were driven north by cowboys along trails such as the Chisholm Trail. They reached **cow towns** like Abilene, Kansas, beside the railroad. There the cattle were sold and sent by train to eastern cities such as Chicago."}, {"h": "The Indian Wars", "b": "Plains Indians such as the **Sioux** followed the buffalo and used the land freely. Settlers, railroads and miners crossed it. Red Cloud's War ended with the **Fort Laramie Treaty** (1868). Gold in the Black Hills in 1874 brought miners in, against the treaty. War followed, including Little Bighorn (1876). The Sioux lost the Black Hills in 1877."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the Plains Indians came into conflict with settlers and the US government between 1861 and 1877.", "steps": [{"do": "The Plains Indians' way of life depended on moving freely across the Plains and hunting the buffalo.", "why": "This shows what they stood to lose when newcomers arrived."}, {"do": "The Homestead Act and the railroads brought settlers who built farms on the land the Plains Indians used.", "why": "This gives the first cause: newcomers took land and disrupted hunting."}, {"do": "In the Fort Laramie Treaty (1868) the government recognised the Black Hills as Sioux land. Gold was found there in 1874 and miners poured in.", "why": "Broken promises explain why the Sioux felt they had to fight."}, {"do": "The Sioux and Cheyenne defeated Custer's troops at Little Bighorn in 1876. The army then defeated them, and in 1877 the government took the Black Hills.", "why": "This shows the conflict's result: the government won the land."}], "answer": "Conflict came because the Plains Indians relied on open land and buffalo, but settlers and railroads took it. The government promised the Sioux the Black Hills in 1868, then miners entered after gold was found in 1874. The Sioux fought at Little Bighorn in 1876 but lost the Black Hills in 1877."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer mention the Fort Laramie Treaty before the 1874 gold rush?", "o": ["Because the treaty ended the gold rush in the Black Hills", "Because it was the first treaty ever signed with Plains Indians", "Because events must always be listed in date order to earn marks", "Because it shows the Sioux had a promise of the land, so miners broke it"], "a": 3, "f": ["The treaty did not stop the gold rush. Miners came anyway, and that is why the treaty matters to the cause.", "It was not the first. An earlier Fort Laramie Treaty was signed in 1851. The answer uses it for what it promised about the Black Hills.", "Date order is not the reason. Marks come from explaining how the treaty links to the cause of conflict.", "Right. The treaty gave the Sioux a promise about the Black Hills. Miners entering after 1874 broke it, which links the gold to the conflict."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why homesteaders found it hard to make a living on the Plains in the 1860s and 1870s.", "given": ["Point 1: There were few trees, so homesteaders built sod houses instead of wooden ones.", "Point 2: Water was scarce and the weather was extreme, so crops often failed."], "q": "Which is the best next point for the answer?", "o": ["The Homestead Act gave each settler 160 acres, which was far too much to farm.", "The railroads joined at Promontory in 1869, so settlers could no longer buy supplies.", "Grasshoppers ate whole crops in the 1870s, so some families lost a year's work and left.", "A treaty gave the whole Plains to the Sioux, so homesteaders could not farm anywhere."], "a": 2, "f": ["On the dry Plains, 160 acres was often too small, not too large, to make a living. This point gets the problem the wrong way round.", "The railroads helped settlers get supplies. The 1869 link made it easier to reach the Plains, not harder.", "Right. Grasshopper swarms destroyed crops in the 1870s. This adds a new reason for failure and links it to families giving up.", "Treaties gave the Sioux certain areas, not the whole Plains. Many homesteaders did farm on the Plains."]}, "trap": "Writing that the land was simply handed over, or that the railroads met at Omaha or Sacramento. Settlers had to live on and farm the land for five years. The lines joined at Promontory, Utah.", "recap": "Settlers, railroads and miners moved onto Plains Indian land, and broken treaties turned that pressure into war."}
{"s": "history", "t": "ma-5", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions on this period study ask you to describe or explain how life changed for these groups, so you need exact laws, dates and reasons ready.", "hook": {"q": "A government splits a tribe's shared land into private family farms and sells any land left over. What happens to the tribe's total land?", "o": ["It stays the same, because only the owners change", "It shrinks, because the leftover land goes to outsiders", "It grows, because farms use land more efficiently", "It is protected, because families now own it"], "a": 1, "f": ["Not quite. Land left over after the farms are shared out is sold, so the tribe's total falls.", "Correct. Leftover land is sold to outsiders, so the tribe holds less. This is what happened under the Dawes Act.", "Farming more efficiently does not add land. The total depends on how much is kept, and here some is sold.", "Private ownership did not protect it. Many plots were later sold, and the leftover land went straight to settlers."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Reservations and the Dawes Act", "b": "Native Americans lived on **reservations**, often on poor land and short of food. The **Dawes Act** (1887) split reservations into individual farms, such as 160 acres for a family head. Leftover land was sold to settlers. The aim was **assimilation**: making Native Americans live like white farmers."}, {"h": "African Americans after 1877", "b": "Reconstruction ended in 1877. Southern states then passed **Jim Crow laws**, which separated Black and white people in schools, trains and public places. Voting tests and taxes kept many Black men from voting. In **Plessy v Ferguson** (1896) the Supreme Court ruled that 'separate but equal' segregation was legal."}, {"h": "The rise of big business", "b": "Industry boomed. Carnegie used **vertical integration**: owning every stage of steel making, from ore to railways. Rockefeller used **horizontal integration**: buying rival oil firms to dominate the market. The government followed **laissez-faire**, meaning it rarely interfered. Owners grew rich, while many workers had low pay and long hours."}, {"h": "Cities and mass migration", "b": "Factory work drew millions to the cities. Many migrants came from Europe, and Chinese migrants came to the West. Cities grew fast, with crowded **tenements** and poor sanitation. **Nativists** feared newcomers. The **Chinese Exclusion Act** (1882) banned Chinese labourers from entering."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the Dawes Act of 1887 weakened Native American tribes.", "steps": [{"do": "The Act aimed to assimilate Native Americans, turning them into farmers like white settlers.", "why": "Opens with the government's purpose, which drives everything that follows."}, {"do": "It split reservation land into individual farms, for example 160 acres for each family head.", "why": "Gives precise detail and shows shared tribal land being broken up."}, {"do": "Land left over was sold to white settlers, so Native Americans held much less land overall.", "why": "Shows the direct loss of land, a clear cause of weakness."}, {"do": "Individual farms replaced shared land and traditional ways of life, so tribal ties and customs weakened.", "why": "Links the land change to its effect on tribal culture."}, {"do": "So an Act meant to assimilate people cut their land and broke up their tribes.", "why": "A short link back to the question completes the explanation."}], "answer": "The Dawes Act aimed to assimilate Native Americans as farmers. It split reservations into individual farms and sold leftover land to settlers. Tribes lost land, and shared tribal life was weakened."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say leftover land was sold to settlers?", "o": ["It shows the Act protected tribal land from settlers", "It explains why Native Americans wanted to become farmers", "It describes the ruling made in Plessy v Ferguson", "It shows how the Act cut the land tribes held, which weakened them"], "a": 3, "f": ["This is the common trap. The sale of leftover land shows the Act cut tribal land and did not protect it.", "The Act was imposed by the government. Selling land to settlers does not explain any wish to farm.", "Plessy v Ferguson (1896) was about segregation. It is a separate topic from the Dawes Act.", "Correct. Selling leftover land reduced the land tribes held. That loss is a key way the Act weakened them."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why life for African Americans in the South became worse after 1877.", "given": ["Point 1: Reconstruction ended in 1877, so federal troops left the South and protection of Black rights weakened."], "q": "Which point should come next?", "o": ["The Dawes Act of 1887 split Black-owned land into individual farms.", "In 1895 Booker T. Washington demanded full equal rights straight away.", "Southern states passed Jim Crow laws separating Black and white people in public places.", "Plessy v Ferguson (1896) ruled that segregation was illegal."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong topic. The Dawes Act affected Native American reservations, not Black land in the South.", "Mixed up. Washington's 1895 speech accepted segregation for now. Demanding rights straight away was Du Bois.", "Correct. After federal protection ended, Southern states passed Jim Crow laws. They segregated daily life and made it harder to vote.", "This reverses the case. Plessy v Ferguson ruled that segregation was legal, which made things worse."]}, "trap": "Thinking the Dawes Act protected tribal land. It cut it, because leftover land was sold to settlers. Likewise, Plessy v Ferguson did not end segregation. It made segregation legal.", "recap": "The Dawes Act cut Native American land to force assimilation, and Plessy v Ferguson (1896) made segregation legal."}
{"s": "history", "t": "nz-1", "kind": "idea", "why": "OCR often asks you to explain how Hitler became a dictator, so you need the key steps in order, with dates and links between them.", "hook": {"q": "In March 1933 the Nazis had no majority in the Reichstag. What would Hitler most need in order to pass any law he wanted?", "o": ["A bigger army than the SA", "The legal power to make laws without a Reichstag vote", "A second general election in the same month", "The support of every newspaper in Germany"], "a": 1, "f": ["The SA was already large. Hitler's problem was legal control of lawmaking, not the number of armed men. The army's loyalty only mattered later.", "Right. Without a majority, every law needed other parties' votes. Power to pass laws on his own would remove that limit. This is what the Enabling Act did.", "A new election was held on 5 March and the Nazis still had no majority (43.9%). Another vote would not guarantee control.", "Newspapers could spread his message, but they could not pass laws. Hitler needed legal power, not only publicity."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The Reichstag fire", "b": "On 27 February 1933 the **Reichstag** building burned. A Dutch Communist, Marinus van der Lubbe, was arrested. Hitler blamed the **Communists** and warned of a revolt. Next day Hindenburg signed a decree suspending **civil rights**. Police and the SA arrested Communists."}, {"h": "The Enabling Act", "b": "In the March 1933 election the Nazis won 43.9%, not a majority. On 23 March the Reichstag voted on the **Enabling Act**. Many Communist deputies were arrested or absent, and SA men stood around the hall. It passed 444 to 94. The government could now **rule by decree** for four years."}, {"h": "Removing rivals", "b": "Hitler used this power to remove opposition. Trade unions were banned in May 1933. In July 1933 Germany became a **one-party state**. In June 1934, in the **Night of the Long Knives**, Hitler had SA leader Ernst Röhm and others killed. The army welcomed this."}, {"h": "Führer, August 1934", "b": "President Hindenburg died on 2 August 1934. Hitler combined the posts of **chancellor** and **president** and called himself **Führer** (leader). The army swore an oath of loyalty to Hitler personally. Nobody was left who could remove him."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why Hitler was able to establish a dictatorship in Germany between January 1933 and August 1934.", "steps": [{"do": "Start with the Reichstag fire. On 27 February 1933 Hitler blamed the Communists. Hindenburg then signed a decree suspending civil rights, and Communists were arrested.", "why": "Shows how fear of Communism was used to remove opponents quickly."}, {"do": "Link to the Enabling Act. With Communist deputies arrested or absent and the SA threatening the others, the Reichstag passed the Act on 23 March 1933. Hitler could make laws without the Reichstag for four years.", "why": "The fire led to the Act. This was the legal route to dictatorship."}, {"do": "Next, say how he used the new power. In May 1933 trade unions were banned, and in July 1933 all parties except the Nazis were banned.", "why": "Shows organised opposition being removed."}, {"do": "Add the Night of the Long Knives. In June 1934 Hitler had Röhm and other SA leaders killed. This ended a threat inside the Nazi movement and pleased the army.", "why": "Shows he dealt with rivals in his own camp too."}, {"do": "Finish with Hindenburg's death. On 2 August 1934 Hitler became both chancellor and president, as Führer, and the army swore an oath to him personally.", "why": "This was the final step. No office or institution could check him."}], "answer": "Hitler used the Reichstag fire to arrest Communists and suspend civil rights. This helped him pass the Enabling Act, so he could rule by decree. He banned unions and parties, had Röhm killed in 1934, and in August 1934 became Führer."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer put the Reichstag fire before the Enabling Act?", "o": ["The fire made Hitler Führer, so the Act could follow", "The fire gave the Nazis a majority in the March 1933 election", "The fire was a law passed by the Reichstag in 1933", "The fire led to Communists being arrested, which helped the Act pass"], "a": 3, "f": ["Hitler did not become Führer until August 1934. The fire came first because it led to arrests, not because it gave him a title.", "The Nazis won 43.9% in March 1933, which was not a majority. The fire helped by removing opponents, not by winning votes.", "The fire was an event, not a law. What followed it was an emergency decree suspending civil rights, signed by Hindenburg.", "Right. Hitler used the fire to arrest Communists. With them removed and others scared, the Act got the votes it needed. The order shows cause and effect."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how Hitler removed other political parties as a threat in 1933.", "given": ["Point 1: After the Enabling Act (23 March 1933), Hitler's government could make laws without the Reichstag.", "Point 2: The Communist Party was already crushed, as its leaders had been arrested after the Reichstag fire."], "q": "Which is the best next point?", "o": ["In June 1934 the Night of the Long Knives removed Röhm, the leader of the SA.", "In August 1934 the army swore an oath of loyalty to Hitler personally.", "In July 1933 a law made the Nazi Party the only legal party, ending party politics.", "In May 1933 the German Labour Front replaced the trade unions."], "a": 2, "f": ["This is true, but Röhm led the SA, which was part of the Nazi movement. The question is about other political parties, and this happened in 1934.", "The army oath came in August 1934 and was about the army's loyalty. It did not remove political parties.", "Right. This continues the point about parties. By July 1933 every other party was banned or had dissolved itself, so Germany became a one-party state.", "Unions were banned in May 1933, but they were not political parties. This answers a different question, so it would not earn marks here."]}, "trap": "Do not mix up the Enabling Act with Hitler becoming Führer. The Act (March 1933) gave him power to make laws. He became Führer in August 1934, when Hindenburg died. Keep the two dates apart.", "recap": "Hitler took legal power in March 1933, removed rivals by June 1934, and became Führer when Hindenburg died in August 1934."}
{"s": "history", "t": "nz-2", "kind": "idea", "why": "You may be asked to explain how the Nazis kept control or why opposition was limited, and good answers link facts to reasons.", "hook": {"q": "A government wants most people to obey without a guard at every door. Which approach is most likely to work?", "o": ["Use only punishment, so that everyone is afraid all the time", "Use only persuasion, because punishment is never needed", "Punish open opponents and persuade everyone else to agree", "Do nothing and let people make up their own minds"], "a": 2, "f": ["Fear alone needs constant policing and breeds resentment. The Nazis did use terror, but they also worked to win people over.", "Persuasion alone leaves determined opponents free to organise. The Nazis did not trust persuasion on its own.", "The Nazis combined both. Terror dealt with opponents, and propaganda shaped what everyone else saw, heard and believed.", "A dictatorship cannot leave opinion to chance. The Nazis controlled information and punished dissent."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Fear and the police state", "b": "The **Gestapo** was the secret state police. It used **informers** and could arrest people without trial. The SS ran the **concentration camps**. Dachau opened in March 1933 and first held mainly communists and other political opponents. Fear made people careful about what they said."}, {"h": "Propaganda and censorship", "b": "**Goebbels** ran the Ministry of Propaganda from 1933. It controlled **censorship** of newspapers, radio and film. Books by banned writers were burned in May 1933. Cheap radios, Nuremberg **rallies** and the 1936 Berlin Olympics spread Nazi messages. People heard little else."}, {"h": "Opposition from the left", "b": "The **KPD** was attacked after the Reichstag Fire of February 1933. The **SPD** was banned in June 1933. **Trade unions** were closed in May 1933. Many leaders were arrested or fled. The left had also been divided before 1933, so it found it hard to resist together."}, {"h": "Churches and young people", "b": "Martin Niemöller helped form the **Confessing Church** in 1934 to resist Nazi control of Protestant teaching. It mostly defended religious freedom rather than challenging Hitler. Some young people, such as the **Edelweiss Pirates** and **Swing Youth**, avoided the Hitler Youth and chose their own music and leisure."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why it was difficult for Germans to oppose the Nazis between 1933 and 1939.", "steps": [{"do": "Start with terror. The Gestapo could arrest people without trial. Opponents could be sent to camps such as Dachau, which was run by the SS.", "why": "This shows the real danger of opposing the Nazis, so it explains why many people did not."}, {"do": "Add informers. The Gestapo was small, so it relied on neighbours and workmates reporting people. Ordinary Germans feared speaking freely, even at home.", "why": "It links the police to everyday life and explains why people stayed silent."}, {"do": "Move to propaganda. Goebbels controlled newspapers, radio and film. Censorship meant opponents could not reach large audiences with their views.", "why": "This explains a second reason: opposition had no public voice."}, {"do": "Finish with the loss of organised groups. In 1933 the SPD was banned, the KPD was attacked and trade unions were closed. Leaders were arrested or fled.", "why": "The strongest groups were destroyed early, so little organised opposition remained."}], "answer": "Opposing the Nazis was hard because of fear and control. The Gestapo, informers and camps made protest dangerous. Goebbels' propaganda and censorship stopped other views being heard. The SPD, KPD and trade unions were crushed in 1933, so organised opposition was removed early."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer move from the Gestapo to informers in the second step?", "o": ["Informers were SA stormtroopers who carried out the arrests", "The Gestapo was small, so its power depended on people reporting others", "Informers wrote propaganda for Goebbels' ministry", "Informers show that most Germans wanted to oppose the Nazis"], "a": 1, "f": ["This confuses the SA with the Gestapo. Informers were ordinary people, not stormtroopers, and the SA did not run the secret police.", "Correct. The Gestapo could not watch everyone itself. Tip-offs from ordinary people spread fear into daily life.", "This mixes up two methods of control. Informers passed on information to the police. Propaganda was the work of Goebbels' ministry.", "This gets it backwards. Informers show that people feared being reported, and some chose to report others. They do not show widespread opposition."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why Niemöller and other pastors set up the Confessing Church in 1934.", "given": ["Point 1: The Nazis wanted control of the Protestant Church. They backed the pro-Nazi 'German Christians'."], "q": "Which is the best next point?", "o": ["Niemöller wanted to overthrow Hitler, so he formed a group to plan a rebellion", "Niemöller was arrested in 1937, so he formed the Confessing Church in 1934", "The Gestapo ordered the Church to split, so the Confessing Church was formed", "Niemöller believed the Church, not the Nazis, should decide its own teaching"], "a": 3, "f": ["This overstates it. The Confessing Church mostly defended religious independence. It did not aim to overthrow the regime.", "The dates are the wrong way round. His arrest in 1937 was a result of his opposition, not the reason for forming the church in 1934.", "This is invented. The Gestapo did not order the split. The pastors chose to separate from the Nazi-backed church.", "Correct. This links the Nazi control in point 1 to the pastors' reason for acting: they wanted the Church to keep its own teaching."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the Gestapo (secret police) with the SA (stormtroopers), or saying the churches tried to overthrow Hitler. Church opposition mostly defended religious independence. Name each group correctly and say what it aimed to do.", "recap": "The Nazis ruled by fear (Gestapo, SS, camps) and persuasion (Goebbels), and opposition from the left, churches and youth was divided and limited."}
{"s": "history", "t": "nz-3", "kind": "idea", "why": "This topic gives you the facts for 'explain' questions on how Nazi rule changed ordinary lives. You need dates and named examples to score full marks.", "hook": {"q": "A dictator wants the next generation to obey without question. What would work best?", "o": ["Leave schools and clubs alone and trust parents to teach loyalty", "Control what schools teach and run youth groups that children must join", "Ban all clubs and let children stay at home with their families", "Close schools and send children to work in factories all day"], "a": 1, "f": ["Parents and churches could teach other values. A dictator wants control, so he cannot leave this to families.", "Right. The Nazis did this. They controlled lessons and built the Hitler Youth, so young people heard one message.", "Banning clubs would remove a tool for shaping children. The Nazis took over youth activity instead.", "Factory work would not teach Nazi ideas. The Nazis wanted children in school and youth groups, absorbing their message."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Work and home", "b": "The **German Labour Front** replaced trade unions in 1933. **Strength Through Joy** gave workers cheap holidays and outings. The Nazis wanted women at home as mothers. Marriage loans began in 1933, and the **Mother's Cross** honoured women with large families from 1938. Watch the graph: official unemployment fell fast (official German statistics, rounded).", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "draw", "pen": true, "read": ["Year", "Millions"], "dp": 1, "ms": 5000, "x": [1932, 1938], "xstep": 1, "y": [0, 6], "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "Year", "ylabel": "Unemployed / millions", "lines": [{"label": "Unemployed", "step": 1, "pts": [[1932, 5.6], [1933, 4.8], [1934, 2.7], [1935, 2.1], [1936, 1.6], [1937, 0.9], [1938, 0.4]], "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [1932, 5.6], "label": "5.6m"}, {"at": [1933, 4.8], "label": "4.8m"}, {"at": [1936, 1.6], "label": "1.6m"}, {"at": [1938, 0.4], "label": "0.4m"}], "cap": "Biggest fall: 1933 to 1934 (4.8m to 2.7m). Official figures left out many, such as Jews and many women, so real unemployment was higher."}}, {"h": "Schools and youth", "b": "Teachers had to join the Nazi Teachers' League. Lessons taught **race science** and Nazi ideas. Boys joined the **Hitler Youth** and girls the League of German Girls. Both stressed obedience, fitness and loyalty to Hitler. Membership was compulsory by 1939."}, {"h": "Jews: 1933 to 1935", "b": "In April 1933 the Nazis held a one-day **boycott** of Jewish shops and banned Jews from the civil service. The **Nuremberg Laws** of 1935 took away Jews' German citizenship and banned marriage between Jews and non-Jews. Jews became second-class citizens."}, {"h": "Kristallnacht, 1938", "b": "The Nazis used the shooting of diplomat Ernst vom Rath in Paris by **Herschel Grynszpan** as an excuse. On 9–10 November, synagogues were burned and Jewish shops smashed. About 30,000 Jewish men were arrested. The Nazis called it public anger, but **Kristallnacht** was organised."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how Nazi policies changed the lives of Jews in Germany between 1933 and 1938.", "steps": [{"do": "In 1933 the Nazis began to push Jews out of work. In April they held a boycott of Jewish shops and banned Jews from the civil service.", "why": "It opens with a date and two facts. It shows persecution starting with jobs."}, {"do": "In 1935 the Nuremberg Laws took away Jews' German citizenship and banned marriage between Jews and non-Jews.", "why": "A named law with its effects. It shows discrimination becoming legal, a step up from 1933."}, {"do": "By 1938 Aryanisation was forcing Jewish owners to sell their businesses cheaply to non-Jews. Many Jews lost their income.", "why": "It links to earlier points. Jews were now shut out of economic life as well as public life."}, {"do": "On 9–10 November 1938, Kristallnacht brought organised violence. Synagogues were burned, shops smashed and about 30,000 Jewish men arrested. Jews were then collectively fined one billion marks.", "why": "It shows the move from laws to open violence. The vom Rath shooting was only an excuse."}, {"do": "So between 1933 and 1938 Jewish life changed from exclusion, to legal discrimination, to violence and loss of property.", "why": "A closing link back to the question. 'Explain' answers earn marks for linking points."}], "answer": "Jews were first excluded from jobs in 1933. The Nuremberg Laws of 1935 made discrimination legal. Aryanisation took their businesses. In November 1938 Kristallnacht showed the Nazis would use organised violence. Each step made Jewish life in Germany harder and more dangerous."}, "selfx": {"q": "Step 2 says the Nuremberg Laws made discrimination 'legal'. Why is that a key point?", "o": ["The laws ended the violence against Jews that had started in 1933", "Jews were ordered to leave Germany as soon as the laws were passed", "The laws were only advice, so Germans could choose whether to follow them", "Persecution now had the force of law, so Jews were officially second-class citizens"], "a": 3, "f": ["The laws did not end violence. They added legal discrimination, and organised violence came later in 1938.", "The Nuremberg Laws removed citizenship and banned marriage. They did not order Jews to leave. That confuses them with later policies.", "The laws were binding state laws, not advice. That is why they mattered so much.", "Right. Making persecution law gave it official backing. Jews lost citizenship and rights, which is why it was a major step."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how Nazi policies changed the lives of young people in Germany between 1933 and 1939.", "given": ["Point 1: Schools changed. Teachers had to join the Nazi Teachers' League, and lessons taught race science."], "q": "Which should be the next point?", "o": ["Young people's lives changed a great deal because the Nazis were very strict with them.", "Women were given marriage loans in 1933 to encourage them to have more children.", "Outside school, the Hitler Youth taught obedience and was compulsory by 1939.", "The Hitler Youth stayed voluntary, so most young people simply ignored it."], "a": 2, "f": ["This is vague. It has no named policy, date or fact, so it earns little credit in an 'explain' answer.", "True, but it is about women and families, not young people. Stay on the question's focus.", "Right. It names a youth group, says what it taught and gives a date. 'Outside school' links it to point 1.", "Wrong. By 1939 membership was compulsory, and many young people joined. This misreads how far the Nazis controlled youth."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the Nuremberg Laws (1935, legal rules) and Kristallnacht (1938, organised violence the Nazis called public anger). Give each a date and one fact, and do not call Kristallnacht spontaneous.", "recap": "Nazis shaped work, home, school and youth; for Jews, persecution moved from exclusion in 1933 to law in 1935 to violence in 1938."}
{"s": "history", "t": "nz-4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions on this topic often ask you to explain why life in Germany changed as the war went on, and why opposition grew.", "hook": {"q": "Before 1942, Nazi leaders tried to keep daily life in Germany as normal as they could. Which reason is most likely?", "o": ["Germany had so much food and fuel that rationing was never needed at all", "The Allies had agreed not to bomb German cities in the early part of the war", "They feared hardship would cause unrest, as it had at the end of the First World War", "Hitler wanted a small war that needed little money, so the economy hardly mattered"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Rationing began in 1939. Leaders kept life steady for other reasons, not because supplies were unlimited.", "Wrong. There was no such agreement. Heavy Allied bombing came later and grew from 1942-43.", "Right. Nazi leaders remembered 1918, when hardship at home led to unrest. They tried to protect living standards until the war turned against them.", "Wrong. Hitler planned for large-scale war. The economy was not fully geared to it early on, but that was not because the war was meant to be small."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Early war, limited change", "b": "In 1939-41 the Nazis tried to keep **living standards** steady. They feared a repeat of 1918, when hardship on the **home front** led to unrest. Quick Blitzkrieg wins and goods taken from occupied lands helped. **Rationing** began in 1939, but women were not made to do war work and the economy was not fully geared to war."}, {"h": "Turning point, 1942-43", "b": "In 1942 **Speer** became Armaments Minister. He made production more efficient and used millions of forced foreign workers. In February 1943 the **Stalingrad** defeat shocked Germany. Within weeks Goebbels asked a Berlin crowd if they wanted **total war**. The aim was every person and every resource for the war."}, {"h": "Total war at home", "b": "Non-essential shops and businesses were closed. More men and women of working age had to register for war work. Goods became scarce. **Allied bombing** destroyed homes in cities such as Hamburg, and many people were evacuated. From late 1944 the **Volkssturm** called up older men and boys to defend Germany."}, {"h": "Opposition grows", "b": "Opposition stayed small because of the Gestapo and fear. It grew as defeat looked likely. The **White Rose** students and the **Edelweiss Pirates** resisted in different ways. Army officers led the **July Plot** of 1944. Hitler survived, and the plotters were arrested and many were executed."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the Nazis introduced total war in 1943.", "steps": [{"do": "Point 1: Germany's 6th Army surrendered at Stalingrad early in 1943. This was a huge defeat that showed the war could be lost.", "why": "This is the trigger. It explains why the change came in 1943."}, {"do": "Point 2: The army had lost vast numbers of men and weapons, but the economy was not yet fully geared to war, so more workers were needed.", "why": "This links the defeat to a real need for resources."}, {"do": "Point 3: Goebbels made a speech in Berlin in February 1943 asking people to back total war and accept sacrifice.", "why": "This shows the regime rallying morale after bad news."}, {"do": "Point 4: New measures were brought in. Non-essential shops closed, more women had to register for work, and Speer's production drive continued.", "why": "This shows how the aim of total war was carried out."}], "answer": "Stalingrad showed that Germany could lose. The army needed men and weapons, so the whole economy had to serve the war. Goebbels' speech rallied support for sacrifice. Shops closed and more people were put to work."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Point 3 mention Goebbels' speech after the Stalingrad defeat?", "o": ["Because the speech was made at Stalingrad, in front of the surviving troops", "Because the speech caused the defeat by giving the Soviet army time to prepare", "Because the speech ended the Allied bombing, which let industry recover", "Because after a big defeat the regime needed people to accept sacrifices"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. It was made in Berlin, weeks after the surrender. The answer links the two events by cause, not by place.", "Wrong. The speech came after the defeat, not before. Getting the order of events wrong breaks the chain of reasons.", "Wrong. Bombing did not stop in 1943. It grew. The speech was about morale and effort, not about ending raids.", "Right. The defeat made harsher demands necessary, and the regime used propaganda to win support for them. The speech is part of the cause-and-effect chain."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why the 20 July 1944 bomb plot did not end Nazi rule.", "given": ["Point 1: By 1944 some army officers believed Germany would lose the war and wanted to remove Hitler.", "Point 2: One officer, Stauffenberg, left a bomb in a briefcase at Hitler's headquarters, and it exploded."], "q": "Which is the best Point 3, to explain why the plot failed?", "o": ["The bomb killed Hitler, but Himmler and the SS quickly took control of Germany", "Hitler survived with only minor injuries, so the plotters could not take control", "Most ordinary Germans backed the plotters, but the army refused to help them", "The Allies refused to make peace, so Germany had to keep on fighting"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. This is the commonest mistake. Hitler was not killed, and the plot failed because he survived.", "Right. Hitler's survival meant the plotters' plan to seize power collapsed. The regime then arrested the plotters, and many were executed.", "Wrong. There was no widespread public backing for the plotters, and the plot did not fail for this reason.", "Wrong. This is about the later war, not the plot. It does not explain why the bomb failed to end Nazi rule."]}, "trap": "Saying the bomb killed Hitler. He survived with minor injuries. The plot failed, and it led to a harsh crackdown rather than the end of the regime.", "recap": "After Stalingrad the Nazis moved to total war, putting people and resources into the war, while opposition grew but never ended Nazi rule."}
{"s": "history", "t": "nz-5", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to explain or describe how the Nazis ruled occupied Europe, how the Holocaust was carried out, and how people responded, from collaboration to resistance.", "hook": {"q": "Nazi racial ideas ranked Slavs as 'inferior' to Germans. Where would that make occupation harshest?", "o": ["Western Europe, because it was closest to Germany", "The same everywhere, because the Nazi system never changed", "Poland and the USSR, because their people were seen as 'inferior'", "Denmark and Norway, because they resisted the most"], "a": 2, "f": ["Distance did not decide it. Western countries were occupied harshly in places, but Nazi racial ideas pushed the worst treatment onto the east.", "Nazi rule was not identical. The Nazis adjusted it to how they ranked each people and what they wanted from the land.", "Right. Nazi racial ideas and the wish for land in the east led to killings, enslavement and destruction in Poland and the USSR.", "Denmark and Norway were treated as closer to 'Aryan' and were not the harshest. Resistance did not set how the Nazis ranked them."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "West and east compared", "b": "In the west (France, the Netherlands, Belgium, Denmark, Norway) the Nazis often kept local officials and police working under German control. Rule was still harsh: arrests, shortages and **forced labour**. In the east (Poland, the USSR) Nazi racial ideas treated Slavs as 'inferior'. The Nazis wanted **Lebensraum**, so they seized land, killed leaders and enslaved or starved civilians."}, {"h": "Vichy France", "b": "After France fell in June 1940, Germany occupied the north and west. The south was run from the town of Vichy by Marshal **Pétain**. The **Vichy** government collaborated: it passed laws against Jews and French police helped arrest them. In November 1942 Germany occupied the south too. **De Gaulle** led the Free French from London."}, {"h": "The Holocaust", "b": "Jews in occupied Poland were forced into **ghettos**. After the invasion of the USSR in June 1941, **Einsatzgruppen** shot Jews in mass killings. At **Wannsee** in January 1942, Heydrich briefed officials on organising the Final Solution. Jews from across Europe were deported to extermination camps in Poland, such as Treblinka and Auschwitz-Birkenau. About six million Jews were murdered."}, {"h": "Responses to occupation", "b": "Responses ran from **collaboration** to **resistance**. Some people collaborated from fear, belief or hope of gain. Many simply kept their heads down. Resisters sabotaged railways, hid Jews and helped Allied airmen. Danes helped most of their Jews reach Sweden in 1943. Resistance risked **reprisals**, such as hostages being shot."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why Nazi occupation was harsher in eastern Europe than in western Europe. (Answer in linked points.)", "steps": [{"do": "Point 1: Nazi racial ideas ranked Slavs, such as Poles and Russians, as 'inferior', while many western peoples were seen as closer to 'Aryan'.", "why": "This gives the root cause: the Nazis treated peoples differently by race."}, {"do": "Point 2: Hitler wanted Lebensraum, living space in the east for Germans, so Poles and others were driven out, enslaved or killed.", "why": "This links the racial idea to a Nazi aim that needed brutal action."}, {"do": "Point 3: In Poland, teachers, priests and officials were murdered, leaving people leaderless and forced to work for Germany.", "why": "This is specific evidence of how harsh rule worked in the east."}, {"do": "Point 4: In the west the Nazis often worked through local officials and police, such as the Vichy regime in southern France, so they needed less direct terror.", "why": "This explains the contrast by showing the different approach in the west."}, {"do": "Point 5: Extermination camps were built in occupied Poland, so the worst killing happened in the east, though Jews from the west were deported there too.", "why": "This ends on the Holocaust, the most extreme result of eastern occupation."}], "answer": "Occupation was harsher in the east because Nazi racial ideas saw Slavs as inferior and the Nazis wanted Lebensraum. Polish leaders were murdered and civilians enslaved. In the west the Nazis often worked through local officials, as at Vichy. The extermination camps were in Poland."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer bring in Lebensraum in Point 2?", "o": ["It was a Nazi aim that explains why they would remove or kill people in the east", "It shows the Nazis planned to take over France before Poland", "It was the plan agreed at Wannsee to organise the Final Solution", "It gives the date when the occupation of eastern Europe began"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Lebensraum was the aim of taking land in the east, and it links the racial idea in Point 1 to the brutal treatment of civilians.", "Lebensraum was about land in the east, not France. Western occupation after 1940 had different causes and methods.", "Wannsee organised the Final Solution in 1942. Lebensraum was a separate, earlier aim. Do not mix the two.", "Lebensraum is an aim, not a date. The step is there to explain why the east was treated so harshly."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why some people in occupied Europe collaborated with the Nazis.", "given": ["Point 1: Fear. Refusing to cooperate risked arrest, and the Nazis punished whole communities with reprisals."], "q": "Which is the best Point 2?", "o": ["Some officials hoped cooperating would keep some local control and protect their people", "Collaborators were forced to join the SS by a law that left them no choice at all", "Collaboration meant hiding Jews and helping Allied airmen escape from the Nazis", "The Free French under Pétain told people to cooperate so that the war would end"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Some leaders and officials, as in Vichy France, believed cooperating would limit the damage. It is a different reason from fear, so it adds a new point.", "Most collaborators were local people, officials and police, not SS members. Collaboration was not a single legal order.", "This describes resistance, not collaboration. Collaboration means working with the occupiers.", "Pétain led Vichy, not the Free French. De Gaulle led the Free French, who fought on against Germany."]}, "trap": "Treating occupation as the same everywhere, or saying Wannsee began the killing. Mass murder had begun in 1941; Wannsee organised it. Pétain led Vichy; de Gaulle led the Free French.", "recap": "Occupation was harshest in the east, and the Holocaust was organised across Europe; people responded in many ways, from collaboration to resistance."}
{"s": "history", "t": "ph-2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask why a government measure worked or failed, and plague orders and the two Gin Acts are the main cases for this period.", "hook": {"q": "A government sets a licence fee for a trade so high that hardly anyone can pay it. What will probably happen?", "o": ["The trade stops completely, because nobody can sell legally", "The trade carries on, but illegally and out of sight", "Prices fall, because sellers compete for the few licences", "The trade grows, because the fee pays for more shops"], "a": 1, "f": ["Not likely. People who can make money from a trade rarely stop just because the legal route is too dear. They find other ways to carry on.", "Right. If few sellers can pay, many sell without a licence. The trade goes underground and the law is hard to enforce. This is what happened with gin in 1736.", "Wrong. Almost nobody can afford a licence, so there is little legal competition. Prices are not what the fee controls.", "Wrong. A fee that few can pay does not fund more shops. It pushes sellers to ignore the law."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Plague orders", "b": "When plague struck, as in 1665, **local** officials enforced the orders. A house with plague was **shut up** for forty days, with the family inside. A red cross was painted on the door and a **watchman** guarded it. Searchers checked bodies. The dead were buried quickly, often at night."}, {"h": "Why orders struggled", "b": "People did not know that **fleas** on rats spread plague. Many blamed bad air. Shutting up a house trapped healthy people with the sick. Some hid cases or bribed watchmen. Rich people fled. Towns had little money and the national government left most health matters to **local councils**. Rubbish stayed in the streets."}, {"h": "The 1736 Gin Act", "b": "In 1690 the government let anyone distil grain spirits, so **gin** became cheap. By the 1730s its heavy use alarmed many. The **1736 Act** put a £50 licence fee on sellers and a tax on gin. It did not ban gin. Hardly anyone paid. Selling went underground and the Act was repealed in 1743."}, {"h": "The 1751 Gin Act", "b": "The **1751 Act** was more **moderate**. Distillers could no longer sell to unlicensed sellers, so the law was **easier to enforce**. Gin sales fell. High grain prices at the time also helped to cut drinking."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how local authorities tried to control the spread of plague in 1665.", "steps": [{"do": "Infected houses were shut up for forty days, with the whole family kept inside.", "why": "This is the main measure. It aimed to cut off the sick from everyone else."}, {"do": "A red cross was painted on the door, with the words 'Lord have mercy upon us'.", "why": "It warned people to keep away and showed officials which houses were infected."}, {"do": "A watchman stood guard at the door to stop anyone leaving.", "why": "Shutting up only works if the order is enforced, and enforcement was local."}, {"do": "Searchers examined bodies to find the cause of death, and the dead were buried quickly in pits, often at night.", "why": "Searchers showed which houses to shut up. Quick burial removed bodies people feared would spread infection."}], "answer": "Local officials shut infected houses up for forty days. They marked the door with a red cross and posted a watchman to stop people leaving. Searchers checked bodies to find plague deaths, and the dead were buried quickly in pits, often at night."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer include the watchman at the door?", "o": ["He treated the sick inside, so shutting up was kinder", "He counted the dead, which was the job of the searchers", "He made sure nobody left, so the shutting up actually worked", "He carried out the king's personal orders in every house"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Watchmen did not treat anyone. They guarded the door and sometimes fetched food, but their job was enforcement.", "Wrong. This mixes up two roles. Searchers examined bodies to find cause of death. The watchman guarded the door.", "Right. Without a guard, people could simply leave. Enforcement by local watchmen is what turned the order into a real quarantine.", "Wrong. The orders were enforced by local officials. The king and court left London in 1665."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why the 1736 Gin Act failed to reduce gin drinking.", "given": ["Point 1: The Act set a £50 licence fee for gin sellers and added a tax on gin."], "q": "Which point should come next in the explanation?", "o": ["The Act banned gin, so sellers closed but then smuggled it in", "Sellers paid the fee, but the money was never spent on health", "Few could pay, so only two licences were bought and selling went underground", "Gin became too dear for the poor, so they switched to beer at once"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. The 1736 Act did not ban gin. It tried to control sales through a very high fee. This is a common slip.", "Wrong. Sellers did not pay it. The problem was that almost nobody did, not how the money was spent.", "Right. The fee was too high, so most sellers ignored it. Illegal sales carried on and the law could not be enforced.", "Wrong. Gin was still sold and drunk widely after 1736. The Act did not make people switch drinks."]}, "trap": "Do not say the 1736 Act banned gin. It put a very high licence fee on sellers. Few paid it, so selling went underground. Say 'high licence fee', not 'ban'.", "recap": "Plague orders failed because the cause was unknown; the 1736 Gin Act failed because it was unenforceable; the 1751 Act worked because it was moderate."}
{"s": "history", "t": "ph-3", "kind": "idea", "why": "People's Health questions often ask why change was slow or fast, and industrial towns, cholera and the Acts of 1848 and 1875 are key evidence.", "hook": {"q": "In 1840 a factory town has no sewers. Waste runs into the river, and people drink from that river. What would you expect to happen?", "o": ["Little change, because dirty water only upsets people for a day", "Disease would spread fast, because waste would reach the drinking water", "Only the rich would fall ill, because they lived nearest the river", "Death rates would fall, because crowded towns have more doctors"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Dirty water carried deadly diseases such as cholera and typhoid. It did far more than cause a short upset.", "Right. Waste in the water supply spread disease quickly through crowded streets. This is the problem that cholera exposed in Britain's towns.", "Wrong. Poor districts were usually the worst hit. They had the most overcrowding, the worst drains and the least clean water.", "Wrong. Fast-growing towns had too few doctors and no health officers. Crowding made disease worse, not better."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why cities were unhealthy", "b": "Factory towns such as Manchester and Leeds grew very fast as people moved for work. Builders put up **back-to-back houses** and cellar homes with no drains. Many families shared one **privy**. **Cesspits** overflowed and waste ran into rivers used for drinking water. No one had to provide clean water or sewers."}, {"h": "Cholera and early responses", "b": "**Cholera** first reached Britain in 1831. It killed quickly, and nobody knew its cause or a cure. Most people believed in **miasma**, meaning disease spread by bad air. Councils set up temporary Boards of Health and tried quarantine. These failed because the disease spread through water. Cholera returned in 1848–49, 1853–54 and 1866."}, {"h": "Chadwick and the 1848 Act", "b": "Chadwick's 1842 report argued that filth caused disease. He said **sewers** and clean water would save money. The **1848 Public Health Act** followed, but it was mostly **voluntary**. Councils had to act only if the death rate was above 23 per 1,000. Many refused to spend ratepayers' money."}, {"h": "Pressure builds, laws toughen", "b": "Snow's 1854 work linked cholera to water. The 1858 **Great Stink** pushed Parliament to act on London's sewers, designed by Bazalgette. The **1875 Public Health Act** was **compulsory**. Councils had to provide clean water, drainage and a medical officer. Reformers such as Chamberlain improved Birmingham's water, drains and slums."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the Public Health Act of 1875 did more to improve towns than the Act of 1848.", "steps": [{"do": "The 1848 Act was mostly voluntary. Councils had to act only where the death rate was above 23 per 1,000, so many towns did nothing.", "why": "This names the weakness of 1848, the point to compare against."}, {"do": "Councils avoided reform because sewers cost money. Ratepayers did not want higher rates, and many people believed government should not interfere.", "why": "It explains why the weakness mattered: reasons behind it."}, {"do": "By 1875 attitudes had shifted. Repeated cholera epidemics, Snow's evidence and the 1858 Great Stink showed that the state had to act.", "why": "It links the change in the law to events, showing the context."}, {"do": "The 1875 Act was compulsory. Councils had to provide clean water, drains and sewers, and appoint a medical officer of health.", "why": "This gives the key feature of 1875 that answers 'did more'."}, {"do": "So towns could no longer ignore the law, and over the following decades death rates began to fall.", "why": "A final link back to the question: the effect of the difference."}], "answer": "1848 was voluntary, so councils ignored it to save ratepayers' money. By 1875, cholera and the Great Stink had changed attitudes. The 1875 Act made clean water, drains and a medical officer compulsory, so towns had to improve."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 of the worked example mention ratepayers?", "o": ["Ratepayers were the only group in towns who could catch cholera", "Ratepayers wrote the 1848 Act and wanted it to stay weak", "Ratepayers paid for reform through rates, so councils feared higher bills", "Ratepayers refused sewers because they already accepted germ theory"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Cholera hit rich and poor. Ratepayers matter because of who paid for reform, not who fell ill.", "Wrong. Parliament wrote the Act. Ratepayers influenced it only by pressuring local councils not to spend.", "Right. Local councils raised money through rates. Reform meant higher bills, so councils avoided it. That is why a voluntary Act achieved little.", "Wrong. Germ theory was not accepted until later. Even then, the issue was cost, not belief in germs."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why John Snow's 1854 findings did not at once change how people tackled cholera.", "given": ["Snow mapped the Soho deaths and found they clustered round the Broad Street pump.", "But he could not show why water spread cholera, because germs had not yet been proved to cause disease."], "q": "Which point should come next in the answer?", "o": ["Snow's map proved that germs caused cholera, so everyone accepted it", "The 1875 Public Health Act had already solved the problem, so nothing more was needed", "Removing the pump handle made the outbreak worse, so people distrusted him", "Many people still believed in miasma, so they doubted that water carried the disease"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Snow showed water spread cholera but not why. Saying he proved germs is the commonest mistake here.", "Wrong. This is anachronistic. The 1875 Act came 21 years after 1854, so it cannot explain the reaction to Snow.", "Wrong. The outbreak was already falling when the handle was removed. It did not get worse, and this does not explain the doubt.", "Right. Miasma was the accepted explanation. Without proof of how water spread disease, many people kept to the older belief."]}, "trap": "Saying Snow proved germs cause cholera. He showed that water spread it, but could not show why, and many still blamed miasma. Write 'linked cholera to water'.", "recap": "The 1848 Act was mostly voluntary, so councils ignored it; the 1875 Act made clean water and drains compulsory."}
{"s": "history", "t": "ph-4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Explain questions on the twentieth century often ask why a government acted as it did, so you need to know what each response was and what caused it.", "hook": {"q": "A new disease appears and scientists do not yet know what causes it. Why is it hard for a government to respond well?", "o": ["People always ignore advice from the government", "Without knowing how it spreads, it cannot give clear advice or treatment", "New diseases are always less serious than old ones", "Governments are only able to act on pollution"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. This assumes people will not listen. The real difficulty is that the government has no reliable advice to give yet.", "Right. Advice and treatment depend on knowing the cause and how it spreads. That is the key difference between 1918 and the 1980s.", "Wrong. Some new diseases are very deadly. Spanish Influenza killed millions worldwide, so this ignores the evidence.", "Wrong. Governments act on disease as well as pollution. The 1980s AIDS campaign is one example of this."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Spanish Influenza, 1918–19", "b": "**Spanish Influenza** killed over 200,000 people in Britain. Scientists could not identify the **virus**, and there was no vaccine or cure. Doctors could only advise rest and hygiene. There was no **Ministry of Health** yet; it was set up in 1919. National action was limited."}, {"h": "AIDS in the 1980s", "b": "Scientists identified **HIV** within a few years of AIDS appearing. They knew it spread through blood and sex, not casual contact. There was no cure. In 1986–87 the government ran **'Don't Die of Ignorance'**: TV adverts and a leaflet to every home. **Needle exchanges** for drug users also began."}, {"h": "Pollution and the Great Smog", "b": "In December 1952 coal smoke and fog formed the **Great Smog** over London. Thousands of people died, mostly from breathing problems. Pressure for action grew. The **Clean Air Act 1956** created **smoke control zones**, where homes had to burn smokeless fuel instead of coal."}, {"h": "Smoking and lifestyle", "b": "From the 1950s, research linked **smoking** to lung cancer. The government acted slowly at first. TV adverts for cigarettes were banned in 1965. In 2007 smoking was banned in **enclosed public places** in England, because **passive smoking** harms non-smokers. Later campaigns also targeted diet and exercise. Watch the graph draw adult smoking rates (ONS, rounded).", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "draw", "pen": true, "read": ["year", "% smoke"], "dp": 0, "ms": 5000, "x": [1970, 2030], "xstep": 10, "y": [0, 50], "ystep": 10, "xlabel": "Year", "ylabel": "Adults who smoke (%)", "lines": [{"label": "% who smoke", "step": 1, "c": 0, "pts": [[1974, 45], [1980, 39], [1990, 30], [2000, 27], [2007, 21], [2010, 20], [2022, 13]]}, {"dash": true, "label": "2007 law", "c": 1, "step": 2, "pts": [[2007, 0], [2007, 50]]}], "cap": "Adult smoking fell from about 45% in 1974 to about 13% in 2022. The dashed line marks the 2007 ban. Source: ONS surveys, rounded."}}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the government's response to AIDS in the 1980s was different from its response to Spanish Influenza in 1918–19.", "steps": [{"do": "In 1918 the cause of Spanish Influenza was unknown and there was no vaccine, so doctors could only advise rest and hygiene.", "why": "This sets out the limits on the 1918 response, which is the starting point of the comparison."}, {"do": "By the 1980s scientists had identified HIV within a few years and knew it spread through blood and sex.", "why": "This is the key contrast. Knowledge of the cause changed what could be done."}, {"do": "This meant the government could give clear, targeted advice, so it ran 'Don't Die of Ignorance' with TV adverts and a leaflet to every home.", "why": "The word 'so' links the knowledge to the action, which is what 'explain' needs."}, {"do": "In 1918 there was no Ministry of Health, which began in 1919. By the 1980s a government health department and the NHS existed to organise national action.", "why": "This adds a second reason: the government's own organisation had changed."}], "answer": "In 1918 the virus was unknown and there was no vaccine or cure, so doctors could only advise rest and hygiene, and there was no Ministry of Health to lead. By the 1980s scientists had identified HIV and knew how it spread, so the government could run a national information campaign with TV adverts and a leaflet to every home."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer mention that HIV was identified within a few years?", "o": ["Because identifying HIV meant a vaccine was ready for the campaign", "Because the government only acts once a disease has a name", "Because knowing how HIV spread let the government give clear, targeted advice", "Because identifying HIV showed AIDS was less serious than flu"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. There was no vaccine and no cure. The campaign was about information, not treatment.", "Wrong. The name is not the point. What matters is that knowing the cause and spread made targeted advice possible.", "Right. Knowing how HIV spread meant the campaign could say how to avoid it. In 1918 that knowledge did not exist.", "Wrong. AIDS was a very serious illness. Identifying HIV did not make it less dangerous."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why the Clean Air Act was passed in 1956.", "given": ["Point 1: In December 1952 a thick smog of coal smoke and fog covered London for several days.", "Point 2: Thousands of people died, mainly from breathing problems, which showed how dangerous the pollution was."], "q": "Which is the best next point to link the problem to the government's action?", "o": ["The Act then banned smoking in pubs and other enclosed public places.", "The Act then sent a leaflet to every home explaining how to avoid smog.", "The Act then banned all coal burning across the whole country straight away.", "The Act then created smoke control zones, where homes had to use smokeless fuel."], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. This mixes the 1956 Act up with the 2007 smoking ban. The Act dealt with coal smoke, not tobacco.", "Wrong. This borrows the 1986–87 AIDS campaign. The Act used legal controls, not a leaflet campaign.", "Wrong. Coal was not banned everywhere at once. The Act worked through local smoke control zones.", "Right. This links the smog deaths to the action taken: smoke control zones where smokeless fuel replaced coal."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the 1956 Clean Air Act with smoking laws. The Act dealt with coal smoke from homes and industry. Tobacco rules came later, such as the 2007 ban in enclosed public places.", "recap": "Government action followed knowledge: little could be done about flu in 1918, but evidence on HIV, smog and smoking led to campaigns and laws."}
{"s": "history", "t": "ph-5", "kind": "writing", "why": "The longer thematic questions ask 'how far' one factor caused change across several periods, so you must compare factors and link them, not just list them.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Five factors to know", "b": "Five **factors** shape change: beliefs, government, science, the growth of towns, and wealth and poverty. Each can **help or block** health. Beliefs such as miasma guided medieval action. Towns created big problems in the 1800s. Money decided what councils and people could afford."}, {"h": "Four periods compared", "b": "Medieval (c.1250–1500): beliefs like **miasma** shaped action, towns made local rules, and central government did little. Early modern (c.1500–1750): towns used quarantine in plague, as in 1665. Industrial (c.1750–1900): growing towns and **cholera** pushed government to act. Modern (c.1900–present): the **NHS** began in 1948."}, {"h": "Factors work together", "b": "Factors rarely act alone. Pasteur's **germ theory** showed microbes cause disease, so clean water had a clear purpose. **Government** turns ideas into action: the 1875 Public Health Act forced councils to provide clean water and sewers. Town growth created the need. Wealth helped pay for it."}, {"h": "Writing a 'how far' answer", "b": "State a **judgement** in your first paragraph. Give one factor per paragraph, with dates and examples from more than one period. Link the factors to each other. End with a **conclusion** that weighs them up and answers 'how far'."}], "example": {"task": "'Government action was the main reason public health improved from c.1250 to the present.' How far do you agree?", "steps": [{"do": "Government became the most important factor from the 1800s, but it rarely worked alone. Beliefs, science and the growth of towns decided when and why it acted.", "why": "A clear judgement at the start gives the whole answer a line of argument."}, {"do": "Before 1800 government did little beyond plague orders, as in 1665, and town rules on waste were often ignored. The 1875 Public Health Act forced councils to provide clean water and sewers, and in 1948 the NHS made the state responsible for care.", "why": "Accurate evidence from different periods shows change over time."}, {"do": "However, government acted because of other factors. Industrial towns grew fast and cholera showed the danger. Pasteur's germ theory in the 1860s then showed microbes cause disease, giving clean water a clear purpose.", "why": "Linking factors shows how they worked together, which is higher-level analysis."}, {"do": "So government was key to turning ideas into action, but it depended on science and on pressure from growing towns. It was important, but not the main factor on its own.", "why": "The conclusion weighs the factors and directly answers 'how far'."}], "answer": "Agrees partly. Government mattered most from the 1800s, through the 1875 Act and the NHS. But towns, cholera and germ theory made it act, so no single factor was the main one."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the third part bring in towns and science after the paragraph on government?", "o": ["Each paragraph must cover a different period, so it moves on to the 1860s", "It switches the answer to disagree completely with the question", "It shows the factors worked together, so government is weighed against other factors", "It adds extra facts, because marks are given for the number of facts"], "a": 2, "f": ["Paragraphs are organised by factor, not by period. The paragraph is there to link factors, not to move through time.", "The answer still partly agrees. It adds other factors to weigh them against government, not to reject the claim.", "Correct. Showing how towns and science made government act lets you judge 'how far' government mattered most.", "Marks reward relevant, linked analysis, not a count of facts. Facts must support the argument."]}, "faded": {"task": "'The growth of towns was the main factor shaping public health c.1750–1900.' How far do you agree?", "given": ["Opening: The growth of towns was very important, because crowded industrial towns created the disease problems that forced change.", "But it needed government and science to turn those problems into solutions."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to start the paragraph on towns?", "o": ["Towns were big and had lots of people, so there were many problems with health.", "Towns like Leeds had slums and poor drains, so cholera spread in the 1830s and 1840s.", "Government was more important, because the 1875 Public Health Act made councils act.", "Jenner's smallpox vaccine in 1796 shows that towns were not important."], "a": 1, "f": ["This is vague. It has no dates, place or detail, so it earns little. Use specific evidence.", "Correct. It gives specific evidence (slums, poor drains, cholera, dates) and links town growth directly to health problems.", "This jumps to another factor before developing towns. The paragraph should support the factor in the question first, then link.", "Jenner's vaccine is a different factor and does not show towns were unimportant. It is off the point."]}, "trap": "Writing about one factor alone, or listing factors one after another. Link them: say how one factor made another work, such as germ theory needing government action.", "recap": "Factors work together: argue how far one factor mattered by showing how beliefs, science, towns, wealth and government combined to cause change."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A1-A3", "kind": "method", "why": "Substituting into expressions and formulae comes up on all three papers, and you use the same skill throughout algebra and in science.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Algebraic notation", "b": "In **algebraic notation**, letters stand for numbers. $3a$ means $3\\times a$. $ab$ means $a\\times b$. $a^2$ means $a\\times a$. $\\dfrac{a}{b}$ means $a\\div b$. In $3a^2$ only the $a$ is squared. Write the number (the **coefficient**) first, then letters in alphabetical order. So $a\\times 4\\times b$ is written $4ab$."}, {"h": "Expression, equation, formula", "b": "An **expression** has no equals sign, such as $3x+2$. You can simplify it but not solve it. An **equation** has an equals sign and is true only for certain values, such as $3x+2=11$. A **formula** is a rule linking two or more quantities, such as $v=u+at$."}, {"h": "Identity and inequality", "b": "An **identity** is true for every value of the letter. It uses the sign $\\equiv$. For example $2(x+1)\\equiv 2x+2$. An **inequality** compares two sides using $<$, $>$, $\\le$ or $\\ge$. For example $x>3$ means $x$ is any number bigger than 3."}, {"h": "Substituting safely", "b": "To **substitute**, replace each letter with its value. Put the value in **brackets**, especially if it is negative. Then follow **BIDMAS**: brackets, indices, divide and multiply, then add and subtract. A bracket around $-3$ means the whole of $-3$ is squared."}], "example": {"task": "Work out the value of $3x^2-4y$ when $x=-2$ and $y=-5$.", "steps": [{"do": "Replace each letter with its value in brackets: $3(-2)^2-4(-5)$.", "why": "Brackets keep the negative signs attached to the numbers."}, {"do": "Do the index first: $(-2)^2=(-2)\\times(-2)=4$. This gives $3\\times4-4(-5)$.", "why": "Indices come before multiplying in BIDMAS."}, {"do": "Do both multiplications: $3\\times4=12$ and $4\\times(-5)=-20$. This gives $12-(-20)$.", "why": "Multiplying comes before the subtraction. A positive times a negative is negative."}, {"do": "Subtract the negative: $12-(-20)=12+20=32$.", "why": "Subtracting a negative number is the same as adding."}], "answer": "$3x^2-4y=32$"}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is $(-2)^2$ worked out as $4$ in step 2, and not as $-4$?", "o": ["Squaring only works on positive numbers, so the minus sign is dropped", "The bracket means all of $-2$ is squared, and $(-2)\\times(-2)=4$", "Multiplying by 3 comes first, and the 3 changes the sign of the answer", "The minus sign stays outside, so only 2 is squared and the answer is negated"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Negative numbers can be squared. The minus sign is not dropped, it multiplies with itself to give a positive.", "Right. The bracket puts the whole of $-2$ under the square. A negative times a negative is positive, so you get 4.", "Wrong. Indices come before multiplying in BIDMAS, and the 3 does not change the sign of $(-2)^2$.", "Wrong. This is the common slip that gives $-4$. With the bracket, the minus sign is inside the square."]}, "faded": {"task": "Work out $5a-b^2$ when $a=-2$ and $b=-3$.", "given": ["Put the values in brackets: $5(-2)-(-3)^2$"], "q": "Which line shows the next step, working out the multiplication and the index?", "o": ["$-10+9$", "$-10+6$", "$-10-9$", "$-10-(-9)$"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. $(-3)^2=9$, but the minus sign in front of $b^2$ is still a subtraction. Here it gives $-10-9$, not $-10+9$.", "Wrong. This treats $(-3)^2$ as $-3\\times2=-6$. An index of 2 means multiply the number by itself.", "Right. $5(-2)=-10$ and $(-3)^2=9$. The subtraction stays, so the line is $-10-9$, which is $-19$.", "Wrong. This takes $(-3)^2$ as $-9$. The square of $-3$ is $+9$, so there is no extra negative to subtract."]}, "trap": "Writing $(-3)^2$ as $-9$, or treating $-x$ as $-3$ when $x=-3$. Put the value in brackets, square the whole bracket, and remember $-x$ becomes $+3$.", "recap": "Substitute values in brackets, then follow BIDMAS, and remember that a negative squared is positive and subtracting a negative adds."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A11-A12", "kind": "method", "why": "Sketching and turning-point questions can appear on any paper, and marks come for the correct shape, the axis crossings and the turning point.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Shape of a quadratic", "b": "Every quadratic graph is a **parabola**. If the $x^2$ term is positive, it is U-shaped and has a **minimum** point. If the $x^2$ term is negative, it is ∩-shaped and has a **maximum** point. The graph is symmetrical about a vertical line through that point."}, {"h": "Crossing the axes", "b": "The graph crosses the $x$-axis where $y=0$. Solve the equation, for example by factorising. The solutions are the **roots**. It crosses the $y$-axis where $x=0$. Substitute $x=0$ to find the **$y$-intercept**. For $y=x^2+bx+c$ this is $c$."}, {"h": "Finding the turning point", "b": "The **turning point** lies on the **line of symmetry**, halfway between the two roots. Find that $x$-value, then substitute it into the equation to get $y$. You can also use completed square form: $y=(x-p)^2+q$ has turning point $(p,q)$."}, {"h": "Cubic and reciprocal graphs", "b": "A **cubic** with a positive $x^3$ term rises from bottom left to top right and can have up to two turning points. The **reciprocal** graph $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ has two separate curves, in the top right and bottom left. It never touches the axes, which are its **asymptotes**. The graph compares both.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [-3, 3], "y": [-4, 4], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"pts": [[-1.5, -3.375], [-1.25, -1.953], [-1, -1], [-0.75, -0.422], [-0.5, -0.125], [-0.25, -0.016], [0, 0], [0.25, 0.016], [0.5, 0.125], [0.75, 0.422], [1, 1], [1.25, 1.953], [1.5, 3.375]], "label": "y = x³", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0.25, 4], [0.3, 3.333], [0.4, 2.5], [0.5, 2], [0.75, 1.333], [1, 1], [1.5, 0.667], [2, 0.5], [3, 0.333]], "label": "y = 1/x", "dash": true, "c": 1}, {"pts": [[-3, -0.333], [-2, -0.5], [-1.5, -0.667], [-1, -1], [-0.75, -1.333], [-0.5, -2], [-0.4, -2.5], [-0.3, -3.333], [-0.25, -4]], "dash": true, "c": 2}]}}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows $y=x^2-4x-5$. Work out where it crosses the axes and the coordinates of its turning point.", "steps": [{"do": "The $x^2$ term is positive, so draw a U-shape with a minimum point.", "why": "The sign of the $x^2$ term decides the shape."}, {"do": "Set $y=0$: $x^2-4x-5=0$, so $(x-5)(x+1)=0$. Then $x=5$ or $x=-1$. It crosses the $x$-axis at $(5,0)$ and $(-1,0)$.", "why": "The graph meets the $x$-axis where $y=0$. Each bracket gives one root."}, {"do": "Put $x=0$: $y=-5$. It crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,-5)$.", "why": "On the $y$-axis, $x=0$. This is the constant term."}, {"do": "The turning point is halfway between the roots: $x=\\frac{-1+5}{2}=2$.", "why": "A parabola is symmetrical, so its turning point is midway between the roots."}, {"do": "Put $x=2$ into the equation: $y=4-8-5=-9$. The turning point is $(2,-9)$.", "why": "Substituting gives the $y$-coordinate. A U-shape crossing the $x$-axis twice has its minimum below the axis, so $y$ should be negative."}, {"do": "Draw a U-shape through $(-1,0)$, $(0,-5)$ and $(5,0)$, with its lowest point at $(2,-9)$.", "why": "Joining the labelled points gives a smooth, symmetrical curve."}], "answer": "U-shaped curve crossing the $x$-axis at $(-1,0)$ and $(5,0)$, crossing the $y$-axis at $(0,-5)$, with minimum turning point $(2,-9)$.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [-1, 5], "y": [-9, 0], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"pts": [[-1.0, 0.0], [-0.9625, -0.223594], [-0.925, -0.444375], [-0.8875, -0.662344], [-0.85, -0.8775], [-0.8125, -1.089844], [-0.775, -1.299375], [-0.7375, -1.506094], [-0.7, -1.71], [-0.6625, -1.911094], [-0.625, -2.109375], [-0.5875, -2.304844], [-0.55, -2.4975], [-0.5125, -2.687344], [-0.475, -2.874375], [-0.4375, -3.058594], [-0.4, -3.24], [-0.3625, -3.418594], [-0.325, -3.594375], [-0.2875, -3.767344], [-0.25, -3.9375], [-0.2125, -4.104844], [-0.175, -4.269375], [-0.1375, -4.431094], [-0.1, -4.59], [-0.0625, -4.746094], [-0.025, -4.899375], [0.0125, -5.049844], [0.05, -5.1975], [0.0875, -5.342344], [0.125, -5.484375], [0.1625, -5.623594], [0.2, -5.76], [0.2375, -5.893594], [0.275, -6.024375], [0.3125, -6.152344], [0.35, -6.2775], [0.3875, -6.399844], [0.425, -6.519375], [0.4625, -6.636094], [0.5, -6.75], [0.5375, -6.861094], [0.575, -6.969375], [0.6125, -7.074844], [0.65, -7.1775], [0.6875, -7.277344], [0.725, -7.374375], [0.7625, -7.468594], [0.8, -7.56], [0.8375, -7.648594], [0.875, -7.734375], [0.9125, -7.817344], [0.95, -7.8975], [0.9875, -7.974844], [1.025, -8.049375], [1.0625, -8.121094], [1.1, -8.19], [1.1375, -8.256094], [1.175, -8.319375], [1.2125, -8.379844], [1.25, -8.4375], [1.2875, -8.492344], [1.325, -8.544375], [1.3625, -8.593594], [1.4, -8.64], [1.4375, -8.683594], [1.475, -8.724375], [1.5125, -8.762344], [1.55, -8.7975], [1.5875, -8.829844], [1.625, -8.859375], [1.6625, -8.886094], [1.7, -8.91], [1.7375, -8.931094], [1.775, -8.949375], [1.8125, -8.964844], [1.85, -8.9775], [1.8875, -8.987344], [1.925, -8.994375], [1.9625, -8.998594], [2.0, -9.0], [2.0375, -8.998594], [2.075, -8.994375], [2.1125, -8.987344], [2.15, -8.9775], [2.1875, -8.964844], [2.225, -8.949375], [2.2625, -8.931094], [2.3, -8.91], [2.3375, -8.886094], [2.375, -8.859375], [2.4125, -8.829844], [2.45, -8.7975], [2.4875, -8.762344], [2.525, -8.724375], [2.5625, -8.683594], [2.6, -8.64], [2.6375, -8.593594], [2.675, -8.544375], [2.7125, -8.492344], [2.75, -8.4375], [2.7875, -8.379844], [2.825, -8.319375], [2.8625, -8.256094], [2.9, -8.19], [2.9375, -8.121094], [2.975, -8.049375], [3.0125, -7.974844], [3.05, -7.8975], [3.0875, -7.817344], [3.125, -7.734375], [3.1625, -7.648594], [3.2, -7.56], [3.2375, -7.468594], [3.275, -7.374375], [3.3125, -7.277344], [3.35, -7.1775], [3.3875, -7.074844], [3.425, -6.969375], [3.4625, -6.861094], [3.5, -6.75], [3.5375, -6.636094], [3.575, -6.519375], [3.6125, -6.399844], [3.65, -6.2775], [3.6875, -6.152344], [3.725, -6.024375], [3.7625, -5.893594], [3.8, -5.76], [3.8375, -5.623594], [3.875, -5.484375], [3.9125, -5.342344], [3.95, -5.1975], [3.9875, -5.049844], [4.025, -4.899375], [4.0625, -4.746094], [4.1, -4.59], [4.1375, -4.431094], [4.175, -4.269375], [4.2125, -4.104844], [4.25, -3.9375], [4.2875, -3.767344], [4.325, -3.594375], [4.3625, -3.418594], [4.4, -3.24], [4.4375, -3.058594], [4.475, -2.874375], [4.5125, -2.687344], [4.55, -2.4975], [4.5875, -2.304844], [4.625, -2.109375], [4.6625, -1.911094], [4.7, -1.71], [4.7375, -1.506094], [4.775, -1.299375], [4.8125, -1.089844], [4.85, -0.8775], [4.8875, -0.662344], [4.925, -0.444375], [4.9625, -0.223594], [5.0, 0.0]], "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [-1, 0], "label": "(-1,0)"}, {"at": [5, 0], "label": "(5,0)"}, {"at": [0, -5], "label": "(0,-5)"}, {"at": [2, -9], "label": "(2,-9)"}]}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why can you find the turning point's $x$-value by halving the sum of the roots?", "o": ["The turning point of a quadratic is always halfway between the two axes", "The curve is symmetrical, so its turning point is on the line midway between the roots", "The roots always add up to the $x$-value of the $y$-intercept", "The turning point is always on the $x$-axis between the two roots"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. The axes meet at the origin, which has nothing to do with the turning point. The point is midway between the roots, not the axes.", "Right. A parabola is a mirror image on each side of its turning point. So that point lies on the line midway between the two roots.", "Wrong. The $y$-intercept comes from putting $x=0$ into the equation. It is not linked to the sum of the roots in this way.", "Wrong. The turning point is on the $x$-axis only if the graph just touches it. Here it is at $y=-9$, well below the axis."]}, "faded": {"task": "Sketch $y=x^2-6x+8$ and find its turning point.", "given": ["The $x^2$ term is positive, so the graph is U-shaped.", "$x^2-6x+8=0$ gives $(x-2)(x-4)=0$, so the roots are $x=2$ and $x=4$."], "q": "What is the next step, and what is the turning point?", "o": ["Halfway between the roots gives $x=3$, then $y=9-18+8=1$, so the point is $(3,1)$", "Halfway between the roots gives $x=-3$, then $y=9+18+8=35$, so the point is $(-3,35)$", "Halfway between the roots gives $x=3$, then $y=9-18+8=-1$, so the point is $(3,-1)$", "Halfway between the roots gives $x=3$, then $y=0$ because it lies on the axis, so $(3,0)$"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. The arithmetic slips: $9-18+8=-1$, not $1$. A minimum between two roots on the axis must be below the axis.", "Wrong. The $x$-value is $\\frac{2+4}{2}=3$, not $-3$. The sign was flipped when finding the midpoint of the roots.", "Right. The midpoint of $2$ and $4$ is $3$. Then $y=3^2-6\\times3+8=-1$, so the turning point is $(3,-1)$.", "Wrong. The turning point is not a root. Only the roots have $y=0$. You must substitute $x=3$ to find its $y$-value."]}, "trap": "Reversing the signs of roots. $(x-5)(x+1)=0$ gives $x=5$ and $x=-1$. Set each bracket to 0 and solve. Check by substituting a root back in: you should get 0.", "recap": "To sketch a quadratic, find the shape, the roots ($y=0$), the $y$-intercept ($x=0$), then the turning point midway between the roots."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A13", "kind": "method", "why": "Higher tier papers often give one turning point on $y=f(x)$ and ask where it ends up after a translation or reflection, or ask for the new equation.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Outside the bracket: y changes", "b": "A change **outside** $f$ affects the y-values. It does what the sign says. $y=f(x)+b$ moves the graph **up** $b$ units. $y=f(x)-b$ moves it down. $y=-f(x)$ is a **reflection in the x-axis**: every y-coordinate changes sign."}, {"h": "Inside the bracket: x changes", "b": "A change **inside** the bracket affects the x-values, and works the opposite way to the sign. $y=f(x-a)$ moves the graph **right** $a$ units. $y=f(x+a)$ moves it left $a$ units. $y=f(-x)$ is a **reflection in the y-axis**: every x-coordinate changes sign. Drag the slider on the graph: at h = 3, $f(x-3)$ is 3 right of $f(x)$. Dashed $f(x)+3$ is 3 up.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [0, 8], "xstep": 1, "y": [-3, 5], "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 1.0], [0.025, 0.9006], [0.05, 0.8025], [0.075, 0.7056], [0.1, 0.61], [0.125, 0.5156], [0.15, 0.4225], [0.175, 0.3306], [0.2, 0.24], [0.225, 0.1506], [0.25, 0.0625], [0.275, -0.0244], [0.3, -0.11], [0.325, -0.1944], [0.35, -0.2775], [0.375, -0.3594], [0.4, -0.44], [0.425, -0.5194], [0.45, -0.5975], [0.475, -0.6744], [0.5, -0.75], [0.525, -0.8244], [0.55, -0.8975], [0.575, -0.9694], [0.6, -1.04], [0.625, -1.1094], [0.65, -1.1775], [0.675, -1.2444], [0.7, -1.31], [0.725, -1.3744], [0.75, -1.4375], [0.775, -1.4994], [0.8, -1.56], [0.825, -1.6194], [0.85, -1.6775], [0.875, -1.7344], [0.9, -1.79], [0.925, -1.8444], [0.95, -1.8975], [0.975, -1.9494], [1.0, -2.0], [1.025, -2.0494], [1.05, -2.0975], [1.075, -2.1444], [1.1, -2.19], [1.125, -2.2344], [1.15, -2.2775], [1.175, -2.3194], [1.2, -2.36], [1.225, -2.3994], [1.25, -2.4375], [1.275, -2.4744], [1.3, -2.51], [1.325, -2.5444], [1.35, -2.5775], [1.375, -2.6094], [1.4, -2.64], [1.425, -