e curve is now y = −f(x−3)+4.", "The minimum (5,−3) went to (5,3), then up 4 to (5,7). The graph is upside down, so it is a maximum at (5,7)."]}}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 2, why does only the y-coordinate change sign?", "o": ["The minus sign is inside the bracket, so it changes the x-values", "Every minus sign in a transformation changes the y-coordinates", "The minus sign is outside $f$, so it multiplies the y-values by $-1$", "The minus sign comes before the $+4$, so it must act on y"], "a": 2, "f": ["It is the other way round. The minus is in front of $f$, outside the bracket, so it acts on y. Inside-bracket changes act on x.", "Not always. $f(-x)$ has the minus inside the bracket and changes the x-coordinates. Position decides, not the minus sign alone.", "Correct. $-f(x-3)$ means the output of $f$ is negated, so each y-value is multiplied by $-1$. That is a reflection in the x-axis.", "Order only tells you when to do it. What makes it act on y is that the minus is outside the bracket."]}, "faded": {"task": "$y=f(x)$ has a maximum at $(-1,6)$. Find the matching point on $y=-f(x+4)-2$.", "given": ["$f(x+4)$ moves 4 left: $(-1,6)\\to(-5,6)$."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Reflect in the y-axis: $(5,6)$", "Reflect in the x-axis: $(-5,-6)$", "Move 2 down: $(-5,4)$", "Move 2 right: $(-3,6)$"], "a": 1, "f": ["The minus is outside $f$, so it changes the y-coordinate, not x. Reflecting in the y-axis would need $f(-x)$.", "Correct. The minus in front of $f$ reflects in the x-axis, so 6 becomes $-6$. Then move down 2 to reach $(-5,-8)$.", "Wrong order. The minus acts on $f(x+4)$ before the $-2$. Moving down first then reflecting gives $(-5,-4)$, not $(-5,-8)$.", "The $-2$ is outside the bracket, so it moves the graph vertically, not horizontally. Inside-bracket changes alone move it sideways."]}, "trap": "Moving $f(x-1)$ one unit left. Inside the bracket, the shift is the opposite of the sign: $x-1$ moves right, $x+1$ moves left. Check with one point.", "recap": "Outside the bracket changes y and does what it says; inside changes x and does the opposite. Apply them to each key point."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A14-A15", "kind": "method", "why": "Real-life graphs turn up across all three papers, often asking you to find a speed, an acceleration or a distance from a graph.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Distance-time graphs", "b": "On a **distance-time graph**, the **gradient** is the speed. Gradient = change in distance ÷ change in time. A flat line means stationary. A steeper line means faster. A line sloping down means coming back towards the start. Average speed = total distance ÷ total time, with rests included."}, {"h": "Speed-time graphs", "b": "On a **speed-time graph**, the gradient is the **acceleration**. A flat line means constant speed. A downward slope means slowing down. The **area under the graph** is the distance travelled. Split the shape into rectangles, triangles or trapeziums, then add their areas. In the graph: 16 + 48 + 8 = 72 m.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 12], "y": [0, 8], "xstep": 2, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "Time (s)", "ylabel": "Speed (m/s)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [4, 8], [10, 8], [12, 0]], "c": 0}, {"pts": [[4, 0], [4, 8]], "dash": true, "c": 1}, {"pts": [[10, 0], [10, 8]], "dash": true, "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [2, 2], "label": "16 m"}, {"at": [8, 4], "label": "48 m"}, {"at": [11, 2], "label": "8 m"}]}}, {"h": "Curves (Higher)", "b": "A curve has a changing gradient. To estimate the gradient at a point, draw a **tangent**: a straight line that just touches the curve at that point. Then find change in y ÷ change in x for the tangent. To estimate an **area under a curve**, split it into strips or trapeziums and add. On the graph, press Next step.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "steps", "x": [0, 4], "y": [0, 16], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 4, "xlabel": "Time (s)", "ylabel": "Speed (m/s)", "lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [0.025, 0.0006], [0.05, 0.0025], [0.075, 0.0056], [0.1, 0.01], [0.125, 0.0156], [0.15, 0.0225], [0.175, 0.0306], [0.2, 0.04], [0.225, 0.0506], [0.25, 0.0625], [0.275, 0.0756], [0.3, 0.09], 