product is $c=-15$ and the sum is $b=2$. Check: $5\\times(-3)=-15$ and $5+(-3)=2$."}, {"do": "Write the brackets: $(x+5)(x-3)=0$.", "why": "Each number goes in a bracket with its own sign."}, {"do": "Set each bracket to 0: $x+5=0$ or $x-3=0$.", "why": "If two factors multiply to give 0, at least one of them must be 0."}, {"do": "Solve each one: $x=-5$ or $x=3$.", "why": "Undo each bracket. Subtracting 5 from $x+5=0$ makes the answer negative."}, {"do": "Check in the original: $(-5)^2+2(-5)=15$ and $3^2+2(3)=15$.", "why": "Both values give 15 on the left, so both solutions work."}], "answer": "$x=-5$ or $x=3$", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [-5, 4], "y": [-16, 12], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 4, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"pts": [[-5.0, 0.0], [-4.94375, -0.446836], [-4.8875, -0.887344], [-4.83125, -1.321523], [-4.775, -1.749375], [-4.71875, -2.170898], [-4.6625, -2.586094], [-4.60625, -2.994961], [-4.55, -3.3975], [-4.49375, -3.793711], [-4.4375, -4.183594], [-4.38125, -4.567148], [-4.325, -4.944375], [-4.26875, -5.315273], [-4.2125, -5.679844], [-4.15625, -6.038086], [-4.1, -6.39], [-4.04375, -6.735586], [-3.9875, -7.074844], [-3.93125, -7.407773], [-3.875, -7.734375], [-3.81875, -8.054648], [-3.7625, -8.368594], [-3.70625, -8.676211], [-3.65, -8.9775], [-3.59375, -9.272461], [-3.5375, -9.561094], [-3.48125, -9.843398], [-3.425, -10.119375], [-3.36875, -10.389023], [-3.3125, -10.652344], [-3.25625, -10.909336], [-3.2, -11.16], [-3.14375, -11.404336], [-3.0875, -11.642344], [-3.03125, -11.874023], [-2.975, -12.099375], [-2.91875, -12.318398], [-2.8625, -12.531094], [-2.80625, -12.737461], [-2.75, -12.9375], [-2.69375, -13.131211], [-2.6375, -13.318594], [-2.58125, -13.499648], [-2.525, -13.674375], [-2.46875, -13.842773], [-2.4125, -14.004844], [-2.35625, -14.160586], [-2.3, -14.31], [-2.24375, -14.453086], [-2.1875, -14.589844], [-2.13125, -14.720273], [-2.075, -14.844375], [-2.01875, -14.962148], [-1.9625, -15.073594], [-1.90625, -15.178711], [-1.85, -15.2775], [-1.79375, -15.369961], [-1.7375, -15.456094], [-1.68125, -15.535898], [-1.625, -15.609375], [-1.56875, -15.676523], [-1.5125, -15.737344], [-1.45625, -15.791836], [-1.4, -15.84], [-1.34375, -15.881836], [-1.2875, -15.917344], [-1.23125, -15.946523], [-1.175, -15.969375], [-1.11875, -15.985898], [-1.0625, -15.996094], [-1.00625, -15.999961], [-0.95, -15.9975], [-0.89375, -15.988711], [-0.8375, -15.973594], [-0.78125, -15.952148], [-0.725, -15.924375], [-0.66875, -15.890273], [-0.6125, -15.849844], [-0.55625, -15.803086], [-0.5, -15.75], [-0.44375, -15.690586], [-0.3875, -15.624844], [-0.33125, -15.552773], [-0.275, -15.474375], [-0.21875, -15.389648], [-0.1625, -15.298594], [-0.10625, -15.201211], [-0.05, -15.0975], [0.00625, -14.987461], [0.0625, -14.871094], [0.11875, -14.748398], [0.175, -14.619375], [0.23125, -14.484023], [0.2875, -14.342344], [0.34375, -14.194336], [0.4, -14.04], [0.45625, -13.879336], [0.5125, -13.712344], [0.56875, -13.539023], [0.625, -13.359375], [0.68125, -13.173398], [0.7375, -12.981094], [0.79375, -12.782461], [0.85, -12.5775], [0.90625, -12.366211], [0.9625, -12.148594], [1.01875, -11.924648], [1.075, -11.694375], [1.13125, -11.457773], [1.1875, -11.214844], [1.24375, -10.965586], [1.3, -10.71], [1.35625, -10.448086], [1.4125, -10.179844], [1.46875, -9.905273], [1.525, -9.624375], [1.58125, -9.337148], [1.6375, -9.043594], [1.69375, -8.743711], [1.75, -8.4375], [1.80625, -8.124961], [1.8625, -7.806094], [1.91875, -7.480898], [1.975, -7.149375], [2.03125, -6.811523], [2.0875, -6.467344], [2.14375, -6.116836], [2.2, -5.76], [2.25625, -5.396836], [2.3125, -5.027344], [2.36875, -4.651523], [2.425, -4.269375], [2.48125, -3.880898], [2.5375, -3.486094], [2.59375, -3.084961], [2.65, -2.6775], [2.70625, -2.263711], [2.7625, -1.843594], [2.81875, -1.417148], [2.875, -0.984375], [2.93125, -0.545273], [2.9875, -0.099844], [3.04375, 0.351914], [3.1, 0.81], [3.15625, 1.274414], [3.2125, 1.745156], [3.26875, 2.222227], [3.325, 2.705625], [3.38125, 3.195352], [3.4375, 3.691406], [3.49375, 4.193789], [3.55, 4.7025], [3.60625, 5.217539], [3.6625, 5.738906], [3.71875, 6.266602], [3.775, 6.800625], [3.83125, 7.340977], [3.8875, 7.887656], [3.94375, 8.440664], [4.0, 9.0]], "c": 0, "label": "y=x²+2x-15"}], "points": [{"at": [-5, 0], "label": "x=-5"}, {"at": [3, 0], "label": "x=3"}]}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 4 split into $x+5=0$ or $x-3=0$?", "o": ["Because the two brackets must add up to 0", "Because each bracket must equal 15, the original right-hand side", "Because two factors that multiply to 0 mean at least one is 0", "Because $x^2$ and $2x$ must each be 0 on their own"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. The brackets are multiplied, not added. The rule comes from the product being 0.", "Wrong. 15 was moved across in step 1. The right-hand side is now 0, so brackets are compared with 0, not 15.", "Right. The product $(x+5)(x-3)$ is 0, so $x+5$ is 0 or $x-3$ is 0 (or both).", "Wrong. Terms need not be 0 individually. $x^2+2x$ equals 15, so only the full expression $x^2+2x-15$ is 0."]}, "faded": {"task": "Solve $x^2-4x=21$.", "given": ["Subtract 21 from both sides: $x^2-4x-21=0$."], "q": "What is the next step? Factorise the left-hand side.", "o": ["$(x+7)(x-3)=0$", "$(x-7)(x+3)=0$", "$(x-7)(x-3)=0$", "$(x+7)(x+3)=0$"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. This multiplies to $-21$ but adds to $+4$. You need the pair to add to $-4$, so the signs are the wrong way round.", "Right. $-7\\times3=-21$ and $-7+3=-4$. Expanding $(x-7)(x+3)$ gives $x^2-4x-21$.", "Wrong. $-7\\times-3=+21$, not $-21$. The numbers must multiply to the constant term, $-21$.", "Wrong. $7\\times3=+21$ and $7+3=+10$. Neither the product nor the sum matches $-21$ and $-4$."]}, "trap": "Forgetting the sign flip or the second answer. $(x+4)(x-1)=0$ gives $x=-4$ and $x=1$. Also never factorise while the right-hand side is not 0.", "recap": "Rearrange a quadratic to equal 0, factorise, set each bracket to 0 and solve. Expect two answers with flipped signs."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A19", "kind": "method", "why": "Simultaneous equations come up in algebra questions on both calculator and non-calculator papers, usually worth 3 or 4 marks, and sometimes hidden in a worded problem.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Match one letter", "b": "Choose a letter to remove. Multiply one or both equations so that letter has the same size of **coefficient** in both. Multiply every term, including the number on the right-hand side. The aim is to **eliminate** one letter."}, {"h": "Add or subtract", "b": "Look at the signs of the matching terms. **Same sign: subtract** the equations. **Different signs: add** them. The letter then cancels, leaving one equation with one letter. Take care when subtracting a negative term."}, {"h": "Substitute and check", "b": "Solve for the first letter. Then **substitute** its value into an original equation to find the other letter. **Check** both values in the other original equation. Always give both answers, such as $x=4$ and $y=1$."}, {"h": "Linear and quadratic (Higher)", "b": "Rearrange the **linear** equation to $y=\\dots$ or $x=\\dots$. **Substitute** it into the **quadratic** equation and solve the new quadratic. Put each solution back into the linear equation. You usually get two pairs of values."}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows the lines $3x+2y=14$ and $5x-4y=16$. Solve these simultaneous equations using algebra. The solution is where the lines cross, at P.", "steps": [{"do": "Label them: (1) $3x+2y=14$ and (2) $5x-4y=16$.", "why": "Labels let you say exactly which equation you are using in each step."}, {"do": "Multiply (1) by 2: (3) $6x+4y=28$.", "why": "This makes the $y$ terms $+4y$ and $-4y$, the same size. Every term is doubled, including 14."}, {"do": "The $y$ terms are $+4y$ and $-4y$, so add (2) and (3): $11x=44$, so $x=4$.", "why": "Different signs means add. The $y$ terms cancel, leaving one equation in $x$."}, {"do": "Substitute $x=4$ into (1): $12+2y=14$, so $2y=2$ and $y=1$.", "why": "Putting $x$ back into an original equation gives an equation in $y$ only."}, {"do": "Check in (2): $5\\times4-4\\times1=20-4=16$. This is correct.", "why": "Using the equation you did not substitute into catches arithmetic slips."}], "answer": "$x=4$ and $y=1$", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 8], "y": [-2, 7], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 7], [6, -2]], "label": "(1)", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1.6, -2], [8, 6]], "label": "(2)", "c": 1}], "points": [{"at": [4, 1], "label": "P"}]}}, "selfx": {"q": "In the worked example, why are the equations added to remove $y$, rather than subtracted?", "o": ["Both $x$ terms are positive, so the equations must be added", "$+4y$ and $-4y$ have opposite signs, so adding makes the $y$ terms cancel", "Adding always gives a larger and easier value for $x$", "Both numbers on the right, 28 and 16, are positive, so you add"], "a": 1, "f": ["The signs of the letter you are removing decide this, not the $x$ terms. Here that letter is $y$.", "Correct. $+4y+(-4y)=0$, so $y$ is eliminated. If the signs matched, you would subtract instead.", "Adding is not chosen for the size of the answer. You choose by whether the terms you want to cancel have the same or different signs.", "The right-hand side numbers do not decide it. Look at the signs of the terms in the letter you want to eliminate."]}, "faded": {"task": "Solve $4x+3y=26$ and $2x+y=12$.", "given": ["Label: (1) $4x+3y=26$ and (2) $2x+y=12$.", "Multiply (2) by 3: (3) $6x+3y=36$."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Add (1) and (3): $10x+6y=62$", "Subtract (1) from (3), but add the $y$ terms: $2x+6y=10$", "Subtract (1) from (3): $2x=10$, so $x=5$", "Subtract (1) from (3): $2x=10$, so $x=10$"], "a": 2, "f": ["The $y$ terms are $+3y$ and $+3y$, the same sign. Adding makes $6y$ instead of cancelling. You must subtract.", "Subtract every term. $3y-3y=0$, so the $y$ terms cancel. You cannot subtract the $x$ terms and add the $y$ terms.", "Correct. Same signs, so subtract: $6x-4x=2x$ and $36-26=10$. Then $x=5$. Next, substitute to find $y$.", "The subtraction $2x=10$ is right, but you must divide both sides by 2. That gives $x=5$, not 10."]}, "trap": "Adding when the matching terms have the same sign, or stopping once you have one letter. Look at the signs of the letter you are removing, and always find and check both values.", "recap": "Match one letter, subtract if the signs are the same and add if they differ, then substitute back to find both values."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A20", "kind": "method", "why": "On Higher papers, iteration is a short calculator task. Each value you write down can earn a mark, so exact digits matter.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What the formula does", "b": "An **iteration formula** such as $x_{n+1}=\\dfrac{x_n^3+1}{4}$ gives the next value from the last one. You are given a **starting value** $x_0$. Put it in to get $x_1$. Put $x_1$ in to get $x_2$. Each answer becomes the next input."}, {"h": "Using the Ans key", "b": "Type $x_0$ and press =. Now type the formula with **Ans** where $x_n$ appears. Press = to get $x_1$. Press = again for $x_2$, and again for $x_3$. The calculator keeps **all the digits**. Do not retype rounded values."}, {"h": "When to stop and round", "b": "Keep going until the values agree to the accuracy asked for. Write down each value you are asked for, to at least 4 decimal places. Round **only the final answer**, to the accuracy the question gives."}, {"h": "Showing a root exists", "b": "A **root** is where $f(x)=0$. If $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ have **opposite signs** and the graph is continuous, a root lies between $a$ and $b$. To prove a root is correct to 1 d.p., test the two values half a step either side of it."}], "example": {"task": "$x_{n+1}=\\dfrac{x_n^3+1}{4}$ and $x_0=0.3$. Press Next step on the graph to see each value. Work out $x_1$, $x_2$ and $x_3$. Give $x_3$ to 3 decimal places.", "steps": [{"do": "Type 0.3 and press =. Then type (Ans³+1)÷4 and press =. $x_1=\\dfrac{0.3^3+1}{4}=0.25675$", "why": "$x_0=0.3$ goes into the formula to give $x_1$."}, {"do": "Press = again. $x_2=\\dfrac{0.25675^3+1}{4}=0.254231\\ldots$", "why": "Ans now holds $x_1$, so the calculator puts $x_1$ into the formula, not $x_0$."}, {"do": "Press = again. $x_3=0.254107\\ldots$", "why": "The same rule gives $x_3$ from $x_2$, still using every stored digit."}, {"do": "Round only $x_3$ to 3 d.p.: $0.254$", "why": "Rounding is the last step. The values are settling near 0.254."}], "answer": "$x_1=0.25675$, $x_2=0.254231\\ldots$, $x_3=0.254107\\ldots$, so $x_3=0.254$ to 3 decimal places.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "steps", "x": [0.24, 0.3], "xstep": 0.01, "y": [0.24, 0.3], "ystep": 0.01, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "equal": true, "lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.24, 0.2535], [0.2404, 0.2535], [0.2407, 0.2535], [0.2411, 0.2535], [0.2415, 0.2535], [0.2419, 0.2535], [0.2422, 0.2536], [0.2426, 0.2536], [0.243, 0.2536], [0.2434, 0.2536], [0.2437, 0.2536], [0.2441, 0.2536], [0.2445, 0.2537], [0.2449, 0.2537], [0.2452, 0.2537], [0.2456, 0.2537], [0.246, 0.2537], [0.2464, 0.2537], [0.2467, 0.2538], [0.2471, 0.2538], [0.2475, 0.2538], [0.2479, 0.2538], [0.2482, 0.2538], [0.2486, 0.2538], [0.249, 0.2539], [0.2494, 0.2539], [0.2497, 0.2539], [0.2501, 0.2539], [0.2505, 0.2539], [0.2509, 0.2539], [0.2512, 0.254], [0.2516, 0.254], [0.252, 0.254], [0.2524, 0.254], [0.2527, 0.254], [0.2531, 0.2541], [0.2535, 0.2541], [0.2539, 0.2541], [0.2542, 0.2541], [0.2546, 0.2541], [0.255, 0.2541], [0.2554, 0.2542], [0.2557, 0.2542], [0.2561, 0.2542], [0.2565, 0.2542], [0.2569, 0.2542], [0.2572, 0.2543], [0.2576, 0.2543], [0.258, 0.2543], [0.2584, 0.2543], [0.2587, 0.2543], [0.2591, 0.2543], [0.2595, 0.2544], [0.2599, 0.2544], [0.2602, 0.2544], [0.2606, 0.2544], [0.261, 0.2544], [0.2614, 0.2545], [0.2617, 0.2545], [0.2621, 0.2545], [0.2625, 0.2545], [0.2629, 0.2545], [0.2632, 0.2546], [0.2636, 0.2546], [0.264, 0.2546], [0.2644, 0.2546], [0.2647, 0.2546], [0.2651, 0.2547], [0.2655, 0.2547], [0.2659, 0.2547], [0.2662, 0.2547], [0.2666, 0.2547], [0.267, 0.2548], [0.2674, 0.2548], [0.2677, 0.2548], [0.2681, 0.2548], [0.2685, 0.2548], [0.2689, 0.2549], [0.2692, 0.2549], [0.2696, 0.2549], [0.27, 0.2549], [0.2704, 0.2549], [0.2707, 0.255], [0.2711, 0.255], [0.2715, 0.255], [0.2719, 0.255], [0.2722, 0.255], [0.2726, 0.2551], [0.273, 0.2551], [0.2734, 0.2551], [0.2737, 0.2551], [0.2741, 0.2551], [0.2745, 0.2552], [0.2749, 0.2552], [0.2752, 0.2552], [0.2756, 0.2552], [0.276, 0.2553], [0.2764, 0.2553], [0.2767, 0.2553], [0.2771, 0.2553], [0.2775, 0.2553], [0.2779, 0.2554], [0.2782, 0.2554], [0.2786, 0.2554], [0.279, 0.2554], [0.2794, 0.2555], [0.2797, 0.2555], [0.2801, 0.2555], [0.2805, 0.2555], [0.2809, 0.2555], [0.2812, 0.2556], [0.2816, 0.2556], [0.282, 0.2556], [0.2824, 0.2556], [0.2828, 0.2557], [0.2831, 0.2557], [0.2835, 0.2557], [0.2839, 0.2557], [0.2843, 0.2557], [0.2846, 0.2558], [0.285, 0.2558], [0.2854, 0.2558], [0.2858, 0.2558], [0.2861, 0.2559], [0.2865, 0.2559], [0.2869, 0.2559], [0.2873, 0.2559], [0.2876, 0.2559], [0.288, 0.256], [0.2884, 0.256], [0.2888, 0.256], [0.2891, 0.256], [0.2895, 0.2561], [0.2899, 0.2561], [0.2903, 0.2561], [0.2906, 0.2561], [0.291, 0.2562], [0.2914, 0.2562], [0.2918, 0.2562], [0.2921, 0.2562], [0.2925, 0.2563], [0.2929, 0.2563], [0.2933, 0.2563], [0.2936, 0.2563], [0.294, 0.2564], [0.2944, 0.2564], [0.2948, 0.2564], [0.2951, 0.2564], [0.2955, 0.2565], [0.2959, 0.2565], [0.2963, 0.2565], [0.2966, 0.2565], [0.297, 0.2565], [0.2974, 0.2566], [0.2978, 0.2566], [0.2981, 0.2566], [0.2985, 0.2566], [0.2989, 0.2567], [0.2993, 0.2567], [0.2996, 0.2567], [0.3, 0.2567]], "label": "y = (x³ + 1)/4"}, {"c": 2, "pts": [[0.24, 0.24], [0.3, 0.3]], "label": "y = x"}, {"c": 1, "step": 1, "pts": [[0.3, 0.24], [0.3, 0.25675], [0.25675, 0.25675]]}, {"c": 3, "step": 2, "pts": [[0.25675, 0.25675], [0.25675, 0.254231], [0.254231, 0.254231]]}, {"c": 1, "step": 3, "pts": [[0.254231, 0.254231], [0.254231, 0.254108], [0.254108, 0.254108]]}], "points": [{"at": [0.3, 0.24], "label": "x₀", "step": 1}, {"at": [0.25675, 0.25675], "label": "x₁", "step": 1}, {"at": [0.254231, 0.254231], "label": "x₂", "step": 2}, {"at": [0.254108, 0.254108], "label": "x₃", "step": 3}], "steps": ["Start at x₀ = 0.3. Go up to the curve. Its height is x₁ = 0.25675. Go across to y = x to carry x₁ over.", "From x₁ = 0.25675, go to the curve. The height is x₂ = 0.254231... Go across to y = x again.", "From x₂, go to the curve. The height is x₃ = 0.254107... The steps get tiny. The values settle near 0.254."]}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 put 0.25675 into the formula and not 0.3?", "o": ["$x_0$ can only be used once, and 0.25675 is closer to the root than 0.3 is", "The formula needs a new starting value each time, which you choose yourself", "Each answer becomes the next input, so $x_2$ is worked out from $x_1$", "$x_1$ must be rounded first, and then it is used in the formula"], "a": 2, "f": ["The rule is not about closeness. $x_{n+1}$ always comes from $x_n$, so $x_2$ comes from $x_1$.", "You do not choose new values. Only $x_0$ is given. Every later value comes from the one before it.", "Correct. The formula takes $x_n$ and gives $x_{n+1}$. So $x_1$ goes in to give $x_2$.", "Rounding early loses accuracy and is not required. Keep all the digits of $x_1$ and round only the final answer."]}, "faded": {"task": "$x_{n+1}=\\dfrac{10}{x_n^2+3}$ and $x_0=1$. Work out $x_2$ to 3 decimal places.", "given": ["$x_1=\\dfrac{10}{1^2+3}=2.5$"], "q": "What is the correct next step?", "o": ["$x_2=\\dfrac{10}{2.5^2+3}=1.081\\ldots\\approx1.081$", "$x_2=\\dfrac{10}{1^2+3}=2.5$", "$x_2=\\dfrac{10}{2.5^2}+3=4.6$", "$x_2=\\dfrac{10}{2.5^2+3}=\\dfrac{10}{8}=1.25$"], "a": 0, "f": ["Correct. Put $x_1=2.5$ in: $2.5^2+3=9.25$ and $10\\div9.25=1.0810\\ldots$, which is 1.081 to 3 d.p.", "This puts $x_0$ in again, which just repeats $x_1$. Each answer becomes the next input, so use $x_1=2.5$.", "This changes the formula. The 3 is inside the denominator, so you divide 10 by $(2.5^2+3)$.", "This slips on $2.5^2$, which is 6.25, not 5. The denominator should be $6.25+3=9.25$."]}, "trap": "Rounding at each step, or putting $x_0$ in again to get $x_2$. Each answer becomes the next input. Keep every digit on the calculator and round only the final answer.", "recap": "Put each answer back into the formula with the Ans key, keep every digit, and round only the final answer."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A21-A22", "kind": "method", "why": "Forming equations and solving inequalities come up across the Edexcel papers, often as 2 to 4 mark questions where clear working earns method marks.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Solve like an equation", "b": "An **inequality** uses <, >, ≤ or ≥. Solve it like an equation: do the same to **both sides**, one step at a time. Adding, subtracting, or multiplying or dividing by a positive number keeps the sign as it is. The answer is a range of values, such as $x<4$."}, {"h": "Negatives reverse the sign", "b": "Multiplying or dividing both sides by a **negative number** reverses the order of numbers. Drag the slider on the graph to k = −1: $2<5$ but $-2>-5$. So you must **reverse the sign**: $-2x\\le 6$ becomes $x\\ge -3$. Or add $2x$ to both sides to keep the $x$ term positive.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [-12, 12], "xstep": 4, "y": [0, 4], "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "position on number line", "ylabel": "top: 2 and 5, bottom: × k", "lines": [{"label": "2 < 5", "c": 0, "pts": [[2, 3], [5, 3]]}], "points": [{"at": [2, 3], "label": "2"}, {"at": [5, 3], "label": "5"}], "frames": [{"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[-4.0, 1.0], [-10.0, 1.0]], "label": "after × k"}], "points": [{"at": [-4.0, 1], "label": "2k"}, {"at": [-10.0, 1], "label": "5k"}], "read": ["2k = −4, 5k = −10", "Gap 5k − 2k = −6; negative means order reversed", "k = −2: order flips only when k < 0"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[-3.0, 1.0], [-7.5, 1.0]], "label": "after × k"}], "points": [{"at": [-3.0, 1], "label": "2k"}, {"at": [-7.5, 1], "label": "5k"}], "read": ["2k = −3, 5k = −7.5", "Gap 5k − 2k = −4.5; negative means order reversed", "k = −1.5: order flips only when k < 0"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[-2.0, 1.0], [-5.0, 1.0]], "label": "after × k"}], "points": [{"at": [-2.0, 1], "label": "2k"}, {"at": [-5.0, 1], "label": "5k"}], "read": ["2k = −2, 5k = −5", "Gap 5k − 2k = −3; negative means order reversed", "k = −1: order flips only when k < 0"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[-1.0, 1.0], [-2.5, 1.0]], "label": "after × k"}], "points": [{"at": [-1.0, 1], "label": "2k"}, {"at": [-2.5, 1], "label": "5k"}], "read": ["2k = −1, 5k = −2.5", "Gap 5k − 2k = −1.5; negative means order reversed", "k = −0.5: order flips only when k < 0"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[0.0, 1.0], [0.0, 1.0]], "label": "after × k"}], "points": [{"at": [0.0, 1], "label": "2k"}, {"at": [0.0, 1], "label": "5k"}], "read": ["2k = 0, 5k = 0", "Gap 5k − 2k = 0; negative means order reversed", "k = 0: order flips only when k < 0"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[1.0, 1.0], [2.5, 1.0]], "label": "after × k"}], "points": [{"at": [1.0, 1], "label": "2k"}, {"at": [2.5, 1], "label": "5k"}], "read": ["2k = 1, 5k = 2.5", "Gap 5k − 2k = 1.5; negative means order reversed", "k = 0.5: order flips only when k < 0"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[2.0, 1.0], [5.0, 1.0]], "label": "after × k"}], "points": [{"at": [2.0, 1], "label": "2k"}, {"at": [5.0, 1], "label": "5k"}], "read": ["2k = 2, 5k = 5", "Gap 5k − 2k = 3; negative means order reversed", "k = 1: order flips only when k < 0"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[3.0, 1.0], [7.5, 1.0]], "label": "after × k"}], "points": [{"at": [3.0, 1], "label": "2k"}, {"at": [7.5, 1], "label": "5k"}], "read": ["2k = 3, 5k = 7.5", "Gap 5k − 2k = 4.5; negative means order reversed", "k = 1.5: order flips only when k < 0"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[4.0, 1.0], [10.0, 1.0]], "label": "after × k"}], "points": [{"at": [4.0, 1], "label": "2k"}, {"at": [10.0, 1], "label": "5k"}], "read": ["2k = 4, 5k = 10", "Gap 5k − 2k = 6; negative means order reversed", "k = 2: order flips only when k < 0"]}], "ctl": {"type": "slider", "label": "k", "vals": ["−2", "−1.5", "−1", "−0.5", "0", "0.5", "1", "1.5", "2"], "start": 2}}}, {"h": "Showing the answer", "b": "On a number line, use an **open circle** for < and >. Use a **closed circle** for ≤ and ≥. For **integer** solutions, list whole numbers only. Include an end value only if the sign is ≤ or ≥. For a double inequality such as $1<2x\\le 8$, do each step to all three parts."}, {"h": "Forming equations", "b": "Let a letter stand for the **unknown**. Write each fact from the problem as an expression. Link them with = or an inequality sign, then solve. Check your answer against the **original problem**, not just your equation. 'At most' means ≤. 'At least' means ≥. 'Fewer than' means <."}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows y = 7 − 3x and y = 19. Solve $7-3x>19$. Then write down the largest integer that satisfies the inequality.", "steps": [{"do": "Subtract 7 from both sides: $-3x>12$.", "why": "You do the same to both sides. Subtracting does not change the sign."}, {"do": "Divide both sides by $-3$ and reverse the sign: $x<-4$.", "why": "You are dividing by a negative number, so $>$ becomes $<$."}, {"do": "Check with $x=-5$: $7-3(-5)=22$, and $22>19$ is true.", "why": "A value from your answer must work in the original inequality. This confirms the direction."}, {"do": "The inequality is strict, so $-4$ is not included. The largest integer is $-5$.", "why": "The sign is $<$, not $\\le$, so the end value is left out."}], "answer": "$x<-4$. The largest integer that satisfies it is $-5$.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [-6, -2], "y": [15, 24], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"pts": [[-5, 22], [-4, 19], [-3, 16]], "label": "y=7-3x", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[-6, 19], [-2, 19]], "label": "y=19", "c": 1}], "points": [{"at": [-4, 19], "label": "x=-4"}]}}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 2, why does $>$ change to $<$?", "o": ["Because 12 is positive while $x$ is negative, so the signs must differ", "Because we subtracted 7 in the step before this one", "Because dividing by $-3$ reverses the order of numbers, so the sign must reverse too", "Because $>$ always changes to $<$ when you solve an inequality"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. The sign of 12 does not decide this. What matters is that you divide by a negative number.", "Wrong. Subtracting 7 does not change the sign. Only multiplying or dividing by a negative number does.", "Right. Dividing by a negative reverses the order of numbers (for example $2<5$ but $-2>-5$), so the sign must reverse.", "Wrong. The sign does not always change. It stays the same when you multiply or divide by a positive number."]}, "faded": {"task": "Solve $3-5x\\ge 18$.", "given": ["Subtract 3 from both sides: $-5x\\ge 15$"], "q": "What is the next step, and the solution?", "o": ["Divide both sides by $-5$: $x\\ge -3$", "Divide both sides by $-5$ and reverse the sign: $x\\le 3$", "Divide both sides by $-5$ and reverse the sign: $x\\le -3$", "Divide both sides by $5$: $x\\ge 3$"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. You divided by a negative number but kept the sign. It must be reversed, giving $x\\le -3$.", "Wrong. The sign is reversed correctly, but $15\\div(-5)=-3$, not 3. Check the sign of the number.", "Right. $15\\div(-5)=-3$, and dividing by a negative reverses $\\ge$ to $\\le$. Check: $x=-3$ gives $3+15=18$, which is allowed.", "Wrong. Dividing by 5 ignores the minus sign on $-5x$. You must divide by $-5$ and reverse the sign."]}, "trap": "Not reversing the sign after dividing by a negative number. $-2x\\le 6$ gives $x\\ge -3$. Avoid it by testing one value from your answer in the original inequality.", "recap": "Solve an inequality like an equation, but reverse the sign whenever you multiply or divide both sides by a negative number."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A23-A25", "kind": "method", "why": "Finding an nth term comes up often in exams, and Higher tier questions on quadratic nth terms are usually worth several marks.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Linear sequences", "b": "In a **linear sequence** the **common difference** $d$ is the same every step. The nth term is $dn + c$. Find $c$ by working out first term $- d$. For 5, 8, 11: $d=3$, $c=5-3=2$, so $3n+2$. Check with $n=1$."}, {"h": "Spotting a quadratic", "b": "Work out the first differences. If they are not all the same, work out the **second differences** (the differences of the differences). If those are constant, the sequence is **quadratic**. Its nth term is $an^2+bn+c$, and $a$ is half the second difference."}, {"h": "Finding the full rule", "b": "Write out the terms of $an^2$ and **subtract** them from the sequence. What is left is a **linear sequence**. Find its nth term, $bn+c$, in the usual way. Then add it to $an^2$. Check with $n=1$ and $n=2$."}, {"h": "Other sequence types", "b": "In a **geometric sequence** you multiply by a **common ratio** each time: 3, 6, 12, 24 has ratio 2. In a **Fibonacci-type sequence** each term is the sum of the two terms before it: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8."}], "example": {"task": "Find the nth term of the sequence 5, 12, 23, 38, 57, ...", "steps": [{"do": "Find the first differences: 7, 11, 15, 19.", "why": "They are not all the same, so the sequence is not linear."}, {"do": "Find the second differences: 4, 4, 4.", "why": "They are constant, so the sequence is quadratic."}, {"do": "Halve the second difference: $a = 4 \\div 2 = 2$. The rule starts $2n^2$.", "why": "For $an^2$ the second difference is $2a$."}, {"do": "Write the terms of $2n^2$: 2, 8, 18, 32, 50. Subtract from the sequence: 3, 4, 5, 6, 7.", "why": "This removes the $n^2$ part and leaves a linear sequence."}, {"do": "The leftover goes up by 1, so it is $n + c$. Here $c = 3 - 1 = 2$, giving $n+2$.", "why": "Use first term $- d$ for the constant, as for any linear sequence."}, {"do": "Add the parts: $2n^2 + n + 2$. Check $n=2$: $8+2+2=12$.", "why": "12 is the 2nd term, so the rule works."}], "answer": "The nth term is $2n^2 + n + 2$."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why do you halve the second difference (4) to get the number in front of $n^2$?", "o": ["Because the power of $n$ is 2, so you always divide by 2", "Because the second differences are split between two lots of terms", "Because the second difference equals the first term divided by 2", "Because for $an^2$ the second difference is always $2a$, so $a$ is half of it"], "a": 3, "f": ["This mixes up the power with a division. With $3n^2$ the second difference is 6, so you halve it because of how the differences work, not because of the power.", "Nothing is shared between terms. The second difference is a fixed number linked directly to $a$, not a total to be split.", "The first term has no link to the second difference. The second difference depends only on $a$, and it is $2a$.", "Correct. Every sequence $an^2+bn+c$ has second difference $2a$. So $a$ is half of the second difference, here $4 \\div 2 = 2$."]}, "faded": {"task": "Find the nth term of 2, 9, 22, 41, 66, ...", "given": ["First differences: 7, 13, 19, 25", "Second differences: 6, 6, 6", "So $a = 3$, and $3n^2$ gives 3, 12, 27, 48, 75"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Subtract $6n^2$ from each term, giving $-4, -15, -32, -55$", "Add $3n^2$ to each term, giving 5, 21, 49, 89", "Subtract $3n^2$ from each term, giving $-1, -3, -5, -7$", "Subtract $3n^2$ from each term, giving 1, 3, 5, 7"], "a": 2, "f": ["You have used the second difference, 6, as $a$. It must be halved first, so $a=3$ and you subtract $3n^2$.", "You must subtract the $an^2$ terms, not add them. Subtracting leaves a linear sequence you can solve.", "Correct. $2-3=-1$, $9-12=-3$, $22-27=-5$, $41-48=-7$. This linear sequence has $d=-2$, which you can now solve.", "The sign is wrong. $2-3=-1$, not 1. Always work out term minus $an^2$, and keep any negative signs."]}, "trap": "Writing the second difference as the number in front of $n^2$ instead of half of it. A second difference of 4 means $2n^2$. Check your final rule with $n=1$ and $n=2$.", "recap": "Quadratic nth term: halve the second difference to get $a$, subtract $an^2$ from the terms, then find the linear rule for what is left."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A5", "kind": "method", "why": "Edexcel papers regularly ask you to make a letter the subject of a formula, and the same skill is used in physics and in many other maths topics.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What 'subject' means", "b": "The **subject** of a formula is the letter on its own on one side. In $v=u+at$, $v$ is the subject. To **make $t$ the subject**, you rearrange until $t$ is alone on one side and everything else is on the other."}, {"h": "Undo in reverse order", "b": "Think how the formula is built from your letter. For $3t-5$: multiply by 3, then subtract 5. To undo it, use the **inverse operations** in **reverse order**: add 5, then divide by 3."}, {"h": "Same to both sides", "b": "Whatever you do, do it to **both sides** so the equation stays **balanced**. Treat each side as a whole. If $3t=4A+5$, dividing by 3 means dividing the entire right side: $t=\\frac{4A+5}{3}$."}, {"h": "Powers and roots", "b": "A square is undone by a **square root**. For $A=\\pi r^2$, first divide by $\\pi$ to get $\\frac{A}{\\pi}=r^2$. Now $r^2$ is **alone**, so take the root: $r=\\sqrt{\\frac{A}{\\pi}}$."}], "example": {"task": "Make $t$ the subject of $A=\\frac{3t-5}{4}$.", "steps": [{"do": "Multiply both sides by 4: $4A=3t-5$.", "why": "The last operation applied to $t$ is ÷4. Undo it first, using ×4 on the whole of each side."}, {"do": "Add 5 to both sides: $4A+5=3t$.", "why": "This undoes the −5 and leaves $3t$ alone on one side. Doing it to both sides keeps the balance."}, {"do": "Divide both sides by 3: $\\frac{4A+5}{3}=t$.", "why": "This undoes the ×3. The whole of $4A+5$ is divided, so it sits over one fraction line."}, {"do": "Write it with $t$ first: $t=\\frac{4A+5}{3}$.", "why": "This is the same equation turned round. Having the subject on the left is the usual layout."}, {"do": "Check with $t=3$: $A=\\frac{9-5}{4}=1$. Then $\\frac{4\\times1+5}{3}=3$.", "why": "Getting back the $t$ you started with shows the rearrangement works."}], "answer": "$t=\\frac{4A+5}{3}$"}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 add 5 to both sides?", "o": ["The 5 must change sign whenever it crosses the equals sign, so it becomes +5", "Adding always comes before multiplying and dividing when you rearrange", "Adding 5 undoes the −5, and doing it to both sides keeps the equation balanced", "It cancels the 4 that was multiplied on the left side in step 1"], "a": 2, "f": ["This is a shortcut that hides the reason. It leads to sign slips. The real reason is that +5 is the inverse of −5.", "There is no fixed order. You undo in the reverse of how the formula was built. Here ÷4 came last, so it went first.", "Correct. +5 is the inverse of −5, which leaves $3t$ alone. Adding it to both sides keeps the equation true.", "The 5 has nothing to do with the 4. The ×4 in step 1 dealt with the 4. Adding 5 cancels the −5."]}, "faded": {"task": "Make $w$ the subject of $P=2(w+3)$.", "given": ["Divide both sides by 2: $\\frac{P}{2}=w+3$"], "q": "What is the next step, giving $w$ as the subject?", "o": ["$w=\\frac{P-3}{2}$", "$w=\\frac{P}{2}-3$", "$w=\\frac{P}{2}+3$", "$w=P-3$"], "a": 1, "f": ["This takes 3 from $P$, but the 3 must come off $\\frac{P}{2}$. $\\frac{P-3}{2}$ is not the same as $\\frac{P}{2}-3$.", "Correct. $+3$ is undone by subtracting 3 from both sides, so $\\frac{P}{2}-3=w$.", "This adds 3. The expression has $+3$, so the inverse is to subtract 3 from both sides.", "This drops the ÷2 from the first step. $\\frac{P}{2}$ is not the same as $P$, so the 2 must stay."]}, "trap": "Dividing or multiplying only part of a side. From $3t=4A+5$, writing $t=4A+\\frac{5}{3}$ is wrong. Divide everything: $t=\\frac{4A+5}{3}$. Use a fraction line or brackets.", "recap": "Undo operations in reverse order, doing exactly the same to the whole of both sides, until the letter you want is alone."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A6-A7", "kind": "method", "why": "'Prove that' questions need full algebra, not number checks, and the same expanding and rearranging skills help you find composite and inverse functions.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Equation or identity?", "b": "An **identity** is true for every value of $x$ and uses the sign $\\equiv$. For example, $2(x+3)\\equiv 2x+6$. An **equation** such as $2x+6=10$ is true only for some values. To test an identity, expand and simplify both sides. Trying one value that works proves nothing."}, {"h": "Writing numbers as algebra", "b": "Let $n$ stand for any integer. An **even** number is $2n$. An **odd** number is $2n+1$. **Consecutive** integers are $n$, $n+1$, $n+2$. A multiple of 5 is $5n$. Two different integers need different letters, such as $m$ and $n$."}, {"h": "Finishing a proof", "b": "Expand and **simplify** the expression. Then show the factor you need, for example $12n=12\\times n$. Finish with a sentence: 'This is a **multiple** of 12 because $n$ is an integer.' The final statement earns a mark, so do not leave it out."}, {"h": "Composite and inverse functions", "b": "A **composite function** $fg(x)$ means do $g$ first, then $f$. The **inverse function** $f^{-1}(x)$ undoes $f$. To find it, write $y=f(x)$, rearrange to make $x$ the subject, then replace $y$ with $x$."}], "example": {"task": "Prove that $(n+3)^2-(n-3)^2$ is a multiple of 12 for every integer $n$.", "steps": [{"do": "Expand the first bracket: $(n+3)^2=n^2+6n+9$.", "why": "Write the bracket twice and multiply all four terms. The middle term is $3n+3n=6n$."}, {"do": "Expand the second bracket: $(n-3)^2=n^2-6n+9$.", "why": "$(-3)\\times(-3)=+9$, and the middle term is $-3n-3n=-6n$."}, {"do": "Subtract: $(n^2+6n+9)-(n^2-6n+9)=n^2+6n+9-n^2+6n-9$.", "why": "The minus sign changes the sign of every term in the second bracket."}, {"do": "Simplify: $n^2-n^2=0$, $6n+6n=12n$, $9-9=0$. The result is $12n$.", "why": "Collect like terms. The $n^2$ terms and the numbers cancel."}, {"do": "Write $12n=12\\times n$. Since $n$ is an integer, $12n$ is a multiple of 12.", "why": "Showing the factor 12 and stating the conclusion completes the proof for every integer $n$."}], "answer": "$(n+3)^2-(n-3)^2\\equiv 12n$. Since $n$ is an integer, $12n$ is 12 times an integer. So the expression is a multiple of 12 for every integer $n$."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the second expansion put in brackets when you subtract in step 3?", "o": ["So that the final answer is positive.", "So that the expression becomes an identity.", "So that the minus sign changes the sign of every term inside.", "So that you only need to expand once."], "a": 2, "f": ["Brackets do not decide whether the answer is positive. This mixes up the brackets with the sign of the result.", "The expression is an identity whether or not you use brackets. Brackets only control the subtraction.", "Correct. Subtracting the whole bracket changes every sign, so $-6n$ becomes $+6n$ and $+9$ becomes $-9$.", "You still expand both brackets separately. Brackets here do not save any expanding."]}, "faded": {"task": "Prove that the sum of any three consecutive integers is a multiple of 3.", "given": ["Let the three integers be $n$, $n+1$ and $n+2$.", "Sum $=n+(n+1)+(n+2)$"], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["Test $n=4$: $4+5+6=15=3\\times5$, so the statement is proved.", "Simplify to $3n+3=3(n+1)$. As $n+1$ is an integer, the sum is a multiple of 3.", "Simplify to $3n+2$, which is close to $3n$, so it is a multiple of 3.", "Simplify to $3n+3$, which is bigger than $3n$, so the sum is a multiple of 3."], "a": 1, "f": ["One example is not a proof. It only shows the statement works for $n=4$, not for every integer.", "Correct. Collecting terms gives $3n+3$. Factorising shows a factor of 3, and $n+1$ is an integer, so the sum is a multiple of 3.", "$1+2=3$, not 2, so the simplification is wrong. $3n+2$ also would not be a multiple of 3.", "$3n+3$ is correct, but being bigger than $3n$ proves nothing. You must factorise to show the factor of 3."]}, "trap": "Testing a few numbers and calling it a proof. Numbers only show the statement works for those cases. Use $n$, $n+1$ and so on, simplify, and state the factor in your conclusion.", "recap": "To prove something for all integers, write them with letters, simplify, show the required factor, and finish with a conclusion sentence."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A8-A10", "kind": "method", "why": "Finding a line's equation is a regular 3 or 4 mark exam question, and the same ideas return in graphs, geometry and problem-solving questions.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Gradient and intercept", "b": "In $y=mx+c$, $m$ is the **gradient**: how far $y$ goes up for each 1 across. $c$ is the **y-intercept**: where the line crosses the $y$-axis. If the equation is not in this form, **rearrange** it first: $2y=6x+4$ becomes $y=3x+2$, drawn in the graph (up 3 for 1 across, crossing the $y$-axis at 2).", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [-1, 3], "y": [-1, 8], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"pts": [[-1, -1], [2, 8]], "label": "y=3x+2", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [1, 2]], "dash": true, "label": "run 1", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[1, 2], [1, 5]], "dash": true, "label": "rise 3", "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [0, 2], "label": "(0, 2)"}, {"at": [1, 5], "label": "(1, 5)"}]}}, {"h": "Gradient from two points", "b": "For points $(x_1,y_1)$ and $(x_2,y_2)$, **gradient** $=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}$. Take the points in the same order on the top and the bottom. A line going up from left to right has a **positive** gradient. A line going down has a negative one."}, {"h": "Finding the equation", "b": "Work out $m$. Write $y=mx+c$. Then **substitute** the $x$ and $y$ of a point on the line and solve for $c$. **Parallel** lines have the same gradient. A line parallel to $y=3x-2$ has $m=3$ but needs its own $c$."}, {"h": "Perpendicular lines (Higher)", "b": "Lines at right angles are **perpendicular**. Their gradients multiply to give $-1$. So the perpendicular gradient is the **negative reciprocal**: flip the fraction and change the sign. A gradient of $2$ becomes $-\\frac{1}{2}$. A gradient of $-\\frac{3}{4}$ becomes $\\frac{4}{3}$. Drag the slider on the graph to change the gradient.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "equal": true, "x": [-4, 4], "xstep": 1, "y": [-4, 4], "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [], "points": [{"at": [0, 0], "label": "O"}], "frames": [{"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[-1.3, 3.9], [1.3, -3.9]], "label": "gradient m"}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[-1.3, -0.433], [1.3, 0.433]], "label": "perpendicular"}], "read": ["Line gradient = −3", "Perpendicular gradient = 0.33", "Product = −1"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[-1.3, 2.6], [1.3, -2.6]], "label": "gradient m"}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[-1.3, -0.65], [1.3, 0.65]], "label": "perpendicular"}], "read": ["Line gradient = −2", "Perpendicular gradient = 0.5", "Product = −1"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[-1.3, 1.3], [1.3, -1.3]], "label": "gradient m"}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[-1.3, -1.3], [1.3, 1.3]], "label": "perpendicular"}], "read": ["Line gradient = −1", "Perpendicular gradient = 1", "Product = −1"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[-1.3, 0.975], [1.3, -0.975]], "label": "gradient m"}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[-1.3, -1.733], [1.3, 1.733]], "label": "perpendicular"}], "read": ["Line gradient = −0.75", "Perpendicular gradient = 1.33", "Product = −1"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[-1.3, 0.65], [1.3, -0.65]], "label": "gradient m"}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[-1.3, -2.6], [1.3, 2.6]], "label": "perpendicular"}], "read": ["Line gradient = −0.5", "Perpendicular gradient = 2", "Product = −1"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[-1.3, -0.65], [1.3, 0.65]], "label": "gradient m"}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[-1.3, 2.6], [1.3, -2.6]], "label": "perpendicular"}], "read": ["Line gradient = 0.5", "Perpendicular gradient = −2", "Product = −1"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[-1.3, -0.975], [1.3, 0.975]], "label": "gradient m"}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[-1.3, 1.733], [1.3, -1.733]], "label": "perpendicular"}], "read": ["Line gradient = 0.75", "Perpendicular gradient = −1.33", "Product = −1"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[-1.3, -1.3], [1.3, 1.3]], "label": "gradient m"}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[-1.3, 1.3], [1.3, -1.3]], "label": "perpendicular"}], "read": ["Line gradient = 1", "Perpendicular gradient = −1", "Product = −1"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[-1.3, -2.6], [1.3, 2.6]], "label": "gradient m"}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[-1.3, 0.65], [1.3, -0.65]], "label": "perpendicular"}], "read": ["Line gradient = 2", "Perpendicular gradient = −0.5", "Product = −1"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[-1.3, -3.9], [1.3, 3.9]], "label": "gradient m"}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[-1.3, 0.433], [1.3, -0.433]], "label": "perpendicular"}], "read": ["Line gradient = 3", "Perpendicular gradient = −0.33", "Product = −1"]}], "ctl": {"type": "slider", "label": "m", "vals": ["−3", "−2", "−1", "−0.75", "−0.5", "0.5", "0.75", "1", "2", "3"], "start": 8}}}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows a straight line passing through $(-1, 2)$ and $(3, 10)$. Find its equation.", "steps": [{"do": "Gradient $=\\frac{10-2}{3-(-1)}=\\frac{8}{4}=2$.", "why": "Subtract the $y$s and the $x$s in the same order. Subtracting $-1$ gives $+1$."}, {"do": "Write $y=2x+c$.", "why": "Use the form $y=mx+c$ with the gradient you have just found."}, {"do": "Substitute $(-1,2)$: $2=2\\times(-1)+c$, so $2=-2+c$.", "why": "The point is on the line, so its coordinates must fit the equation."}, {"do": "Solve: $c=2+2=4$.", "why": "Add 2 to both sides to get $c$ on its own."}, {"do": "Check with the other point $(3,10)$: $2\\times3+4=10$.", "why": "The second point fits too, so the equation is right."}], "answer": "$y=2x+4$", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [-3, 4], "y": [-2, 12], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"pts": [[-3, -2], [4, 12]], "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [-1, 2], "label": "(-1, 2)"}, {"at": [3, 10], "label": "(3, 10)"}]}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer substitute $(-1,2)$ into $y=2x+c$?", "o": ["Because $(-1,2)$ is where the line crosses the $y$-axis, so it gives $c$ directly", "Because substituting a point changes the gradient to the correct value", "Because the point is on the line, so it fits the equation with only $c$ unknown", "Because the gradient formula only works once a point has been substituted"], "a": 2, "f": ["The $y$-intercept has $x=0$. Here $x=-1$, so this point is not the intercept. Substituting is still needed to solve for $c$.", "The gradient was already found from the two points. Substituting a point does not change $m$. It only finds $c$.", "Right. Every point on the line makes the equation true. With $m$ known, putting in $x$ and $y$ leaves one equation in $c$.", "The gradient was worked out first, from two points, without any substitution. Substitution comes afterwards, to find $c$."]}, "faded": {"task": "The graph shows the line $y=-2x+5$ and the point $(3, 2)$. Find the equation of the line parallel to $y=-2x+5$ that passes through $(3, 2)$.", "given": ["Parallel lines have the same gradient, so $m=-2$.", "Write $y=-2x+c$."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Keep $c=5$, so the line is $y=-2x+5$.", "Put $x=3,\\ y=2$ in: $2=-6+c$, so $c=8$.", "Put $x=3,\\ y=2$ in: $2=-6+c$, so $c=-4$.", "Put $x=2,\\ y=3$ in: $3=-4+c$, so $c=7$."], "a": 1, "f": ["This keeps the old intercept. Parallel lines share $m$ only. The new line must pass through $(3,2)$, which $y=-2x+5$ does not.", "Right. $2=-6+c$, so add 6 to both sides to get $c=8$. The line is $y=-2x+8$.", "The substitution is correct, but $c=2-6$ is a sign slip. To remove $-6$, add 6 to both sides, giving $c=8$.", "The coordinates are swapped. A point $(3,2)$ has $x=3$ and $y=2$, so $x$ goes first."], "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [-2, 6], "y": [-6, 8], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"pts": [[-1, 7], [5, -5]], "label": "y=-2x+5", "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [3, 2], "label": "(3, 2)"}]}}, "trap": "Keeping the old intercept. A parallel line has the same $m$ but a different $c$. Always substitute the point it passes through and solve for $c$.", "recap": "Find $m$, write $y=mx+c$, then substitute a point on the line to find $c$."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "G1-G2", "kind": "method", "why": "Construction and loci questions come up regularly, and the marks go for accurate drawing with your construction arcs left visible.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Three loci to know", "b": "A **locus** is the set of points that obey a rule. A fixed distance from a point gives a circle. Equidistant from two points gives the **perpendicular bisector**. Equidistant from two lines that meet gives the **angle bisector**."}, {"h": "Perpendicular bisector", "b": "Open compasses to more than half of $AB$. Draw arcs from $A$ above and below the line. Keep the **same radius** and draw arcs from $B$. Join the two crossing points. This line cuts $AB$ at its **midpoint** and at $90^\\circ$."}, {"h": "Angle bisector", "b": "Put the compass point on the **vertex**. Draw an arc that cuts both lines. From each cut point, draw an arc of the same radius so the two arcs cross inside the angle. Join the vertex to the crossing. This splits the angle into two **equal angles**. Press Next step to build it on the graph.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "steps", "equal": true, "x": [-1, 8], "xstep": 1, "y": [-1, 8], "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "Distance / cm", "ylabel": "Distance / cm", "lines": [{"step": 1, "c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [8, 0]]}, {"step": 1, "c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [0, 8]]}, {"step": 2, "c": 3, "pts": [[3.0, 0.0], [2.9999, 0.0295], [2.9994, 0.0589], [2.9987, 0.0883], [2.9977, 0.1178], [2.9964, 0.1472], [2.9948, 0.1766], [2.9929, 0.206], [2.9908, 0.2354], [2.9883, 0.2647], [2.9856, 0.2941], [2.9825, 0.3233], [2.9792, 0.3526], [2.9756, 0.3818], [2.9717, 0.411], [2.9675, 0.4402], [2.9631, 0.4693], [2.9583, 0.4984], [2.9533, 0.5274], [2.948, 0.5564], [2.9424, 0.5853], [2.9365, 0.6141], [2.9303, 0.6429], [2.9238, 0.6717], [2.9171, 0.7003], [2.9101, 0.7289], [2.9028, 0.7575], [2.8952, 0.7859], [2.8874, 0.8143], [2.8792, 0.8426], [2.8708, 0.8709], [2.8621, 0.899], [2.8532, 0.9271], [2.8439, 0.955], [2.8344, 0.9829], [2.8246, 1.0107], 