-4.8329], [-1.1304, -4.8705], [-0.9778, -4.9035], [-0.8243, -4.9316], [-0.6699, -4.9549], [-0.5149, -4.9734], [-0.3593, -4.9871], [-0.2035, -4.9959], [-0.0474, -4.9998], [0.1087, -4.9988], [0.2648, -4.993], [0.4205, -4.9823], [0.5759, -4.9667], [0.7306, -4.9463], [0.8847, -4.9211], [1.0379, -4.8911], [1.1901, -4.8563], [1.3412, -4.8168], [1.4909, -4.7725], [1.6392, -4.7237], [1.7859, -4.6702], [1.9308, -4.6122], [2.0739, -4.5496], [2.2149, -4.4827], [2.3538, -4.4113], [2.4903, -4.3357], [2.6245, -4.2558], [2.7561, -4.1718], [2.885, -4.0837], [3.0111, -3.9917], [3.1342, -3.8957], [3.2543, -3.796], [3.3713, -3.6925], [3.4849, -3.5854], [3.5952, -3.4749], [3.7019, -3.361], [3.805, -3.2437], [3.9044, -3.1234], [4.0, -2.9999]]}], "points": [{"at": [0, 0], "label": "A"}, {"at": [8, 0], "label": "B"}, {"at": [4, 3], "label": "P", "step": 3}, {"at": [4, -3], "label": "Q", "step": 3}, {"at": [4, 0], "label": "Midpoint", "step": 4}], "steps": ["A and B are 8 cm apart. Draw the line AB with a ruler.", "Compasses at 5 cm, point on A: draw an arc above and below AB. 5 cm is more than half of 8 cm.", "Keep 5 cm, point on B: draw an arc. The arcs cross at P and Q. Each is 5 cm from A and 5 cm from B.", "Join P and Q. This is the perpendicular bisector. It meets AB at 4 cm from A, at 90°.", "Circle, radius 5 cm, centre A. Points inside it are within 5 cm of A.", "Thick arc: the edge of the region. Shade inside the circle on A's side of the bisector (the left): closer to A and within 5 cm."]}}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 2, why must the compasses be set to more than half of AB?", "o": ["So the crossing points are exactly 5 cm from the midpoint of AB", "So the arcs from A and B are long enough to cross each other", "So that the bisector you draw is 5 cm long", "So that the bisector meets AB at $90^\\circ$ rather than another angle"], "a": 1, "f": ["The crossing points are not 5 cm from the midpoint. Their distance depends on the radius and AB. The radius only has to be big enough for the arcs to meet.", "Right. If the radius is not more than half of AB, the arcs cannot cross at two points, so you cannot draw the bisector.", "The length of the bisector depends on how far you draw it. It is a line that goes on forever, and the radius does not set its length.", "The right angle comes from using the same radius at both ends. Any radius over half of AB gives $90^\\circ$, so the size is not what matters here."]}, "faded": {"task": "P and Q are 6 cm apart. Shade the region closer to Q than to P and within 4 cm of Q.", "given": ["Draw PQ, 6 cm long.", "Construct the perpendicular bisector of PQ. It passes 3 cm from each point."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Draw a circle of radius 4 cm centred at P. Shade the part inside it on Q's side.", "Draw a circle of radius 3 cm centred at Q. Shade the part inside it on Q's side.", "Draw a circle of radius 4 cm centred at Q. Shade the part inside it on Q's side.", "Draw a circle of radius 4 cm centred at Q. Shade the part inside it on P's side."], "a": 2, "f": ["The distance rule is about Q, so the circle must be centred at Q. A circle round P gives points within 4 cm of P.", "The radius must be 4 cm, as given. 3 cm is half of PQ, which is where the bisector crosses PQ. Do not mix up the two distances.", "Right. Within 4 cm of Q means inside a circle centred at Q. Closer to Q means Q's side of the bisector. Shade where both hold.", "Q's side of the bisector is the closer-to-Q side. Shading on P's side gives points that are closer to P, which breaks the first rule."]}, "trap": "Mixing up loci. A fixed distance from one point is a circle. Equidistant from two points is a perpendicular bisector. Equidistant from two lines is an angle bisector. Name the rule before you draw.", "recap": "Points equidistant from A and B lie on the perpendicular bisector of AB, which cuts AB at its midpoint at $90^\\circ$."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "G11-G15", "kind": "method", "why": "Bearings can come up on any of the three papers, often in a 2 to 4 mark question, and marks are lost for starting at the wrong point.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Measuring a bearing", "b": "A **bearing** is an angle measured from the north line at the start point. You turn **clockwise**. Write it with **three figures**, so $45^\\circ$ becomes $045^\\circ$. \"The bearing of B from A\" means you stand at A and look towards B."}, {"h": "Back bearings", "b": "The bearing of A from B points the opposite way to the bearing of B from A. That is a half turn, $180^\\circ$. If the bearing is **under $180^\\circ$, add $180^\\circ$**. If it is **$180^\\circ$ or more, subtract $180^\\circ$**. The answer is always less than $360^\\circ$."}, {"h": "Parallel north lines", "b": "North lines at different points are **parallel**. So you can use angle facts. **Co-interior angles** between parallel lines add to $180^\\circ$. That is why the back bearing rule works. Always draw a north line at each point, then mark the angles you know."}], "example": {"task": "The bearing of Q from P is $245^\\circ$. Work out the bearing of P from Q. Press Next step to build the sketch and follow each step.", "steps": [{"do": "Sketch P. Draw a north line at P. Mark Q so the clockwise angle from north is $245^\\circ$. That is past south, so Q is south-west of P.", "why": "A sketch shows the direction, so you can check the answer later."}, {"do": "Draw a north line at Q as well.", "why": "The bearing of P from Q is measured from the north line at Q."}, {"do": "$245^\\circ$ is more than $180^\\circ$, so subtract: $245-180=65$.", "why": "A back bearing is a half turn. Adding $180^\\circ$ would go past $360^\\circ$."}, {"do": "Write the answer in three figures: $065^\\circ$.", "why": "Bearings must have three figures."}, {"do": "Check: P is north-east of Q, and $065^\\circ$ lies between north and east.", "why": "The answer matches the sketch."}], 