], [1.125, 1.285], [1.188, 1.303], [1.25, 1.322], [1.312, 1.34], [1.375, 1.359], [1.438, 1.378], [1.5, 1.398], [1.562, 1.417], [1.625, 1.437], [1.688, 1.457], [1.75, 1.478], [1.812, 1.498], [1.875, 1.52], [1.938, 1.541], [2.0, 1.562], [2.062, 1.584], [2.125, 1.607], [2.188, 1.629], [2.25, 1.652], [2.312, 1.675], [2.375, 1.699], [2.438, 1.723], [2.5, 1.747], [2.562, 1.771], [2.625, 1.796], [2.688, 1.822], [2.75, 1.847], [2.812, 1.873], [2.875, 1.899], [2.938, 1.926], [3.0, 1.953], [3.062, 1.981], [3.125, 2.008], [3.188, 2.037], [3.25, 2.065], [3.312, 2.094], [3.375, 2.124], [3.438, 2.153], [3.5, 2.184], [3.562, 2.214], [3.625, 2.245], [3.688, 2.277], [3.75, 2.309], [3.812, 2.341], [3.875, 2.374], [3.938, 2.408], [4.0, 2.441], [4.062, 2.476], [4.125, 2.51], [4.188, 2.546], [4.25, 2.581], [4.312, 2.618], [4.375, 2.654], [4.438, 2.692], [4.5, 2.73], [4.562, 2.768], [4.625, 2.807], [4.688, 2.846], [4.75, 2.886], [4.812, 2.927], [4.875, 2.968], [4.938, 3.009], [5.0, 3.052]], "label": "Fissions per round"}], "points": [{"at": [0, 1.0]}, {"at": [1, 1.25]}, {"at": [2, 1.5625]}, {"at": [3, 1.9531]}, {"at": [4, 2.4414]}, {"at": [5, 3.0518]}], "read": ["k = neutrons per fission that cause another: 1.25", "Fissions in round 5 = k⁵ = 3.05", "k = 1 steady, k > 1 grows, k < 1 fades away"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[0.0, 1.0], [0.062, 1.026], [0.125, 1.052], [0.188, 1.079], [0.25, 1.107], [0.312, 1.135], [0.375, 1.164], [0.438, 1.194], [0.5, 1.225], [0.562, 1.256], [0.625, 1.288], [0.688, 1.321], [0.75, 1.355], [0.812, 1.39], [0.875, 1.426], [0.938, 1.462], [1.0, 1.5], [1.062, 1.538], [1.125, 1.578], [1.188, 1.618], [1.25, 1.66], [1.312, 1.703], [1.375, 1.746], [1.438, 1.791], [1.5, 1.837], [1.562, 1.884], [1.625, 1.933], [1.688, 1.982], [1.75, 2.033], [1.812, 2.085], [1.875, 2.139], [1.938, 2.194], [2.0, 2.25], [2.062, 2.308], [2.125, 2.367], [2.188, 2.428], [2.25, 2.49], [2.312, 2.554], [2.375, 2.619], [2.438, 2.687], [2.5, 2.756], [2.562, 2.826], [2.625, 2.899], [2.688, 2.973], [2.75, 3.05], [2.812, 3.128], [2.875, 3.208], [2.938, 3.291], [3.0, 3.375], [3.062, 3.462], [3.125, 3.55], [3.188, 3.642], [3.25, 3.735], [3.312, 3.831], [3.375, 3.929], [3.438, 4.03], [3.5, 4.134], [3.562, 4.24], [3.625, 4.348], [3.688, 4.46], [3.75, 4.574], [3.812, 4.692], [3.875, 4.812], [3.938, 4.936], [4.0, 5.062], [4.062, 5.192], [4.125, 5.326], [4.188, 5.462], [4.25, 5.603], [4.312, 5.746], [4.375, 5.894], [4.438, 6.045], [4.5, 6.2], [4.562, 6.359], [4.625, 6.523], [4.688, 6.69], [4.75, 6.862], [4.812, 7.038], [4.875, 7.218], [4.938, 7.404], [5.0, 7.594]], "label": "Fissions per round"}], "points": [{"at": [0, 1.0]}, {"at": [1, 1.5]}, {"at": [2, 2.25]}, {"at": [3, 3.375]}, {"at": [4, 5.0625]}, {"at": [5, 7.5938]}], "read": ["k = neutrons per fission that cause another: 1.5", "Fissions in round 5 = k⁵ = 7.59", "k = 1 steady, k > 1 grows, k < 1 fades away"]}], "ctl": {"type": "slider", "label": "k", "vals": ["0.5", "0.75", "1", "1.25", "1.5"], "start": 2}}}, {"h": "Fusion: joining nuclei", "b": "**Nuclear fusion** is two light nuclei, such as hydrogen nuclei, joining to form one heavier nucleus. Some mass is converted into energy, released as radiation. Nuclei are positively charged, so they repel. They only fuse at very high **temperature** and pressure, as inside stars."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a chain reaction happens in a nuclear reactor and how it is kept under control. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "A neutron is absorbed by a uranium-235 nucleus, which splits into two smaller nuclei.", "why": "This describes fission: a large unstable nucleus splitting, usually after absorbing a neutron."}, {"do": "Energy is released, and the fission products all have kinetic energy.", "why": "Energy is released as the kinetic energy of the products. This is the point of the reactor."}, {"do": "Two or three neutrons are also released in each fission.", "why": "These neutrons are what make a chain reaction possible."}, {"do": "These neutrons can be absorbed by other uranium nuclei and cause more fission. This is a chain reaction.", "why": "Linking neutrons to further fissions is the key idea the mark scheme looks for."}, {"do": "Control rods absorb neutrons, so on average only one neutron per fission causes another fission and the rate stays steady.", "why": "This answers the control part: absorbing neutrons keeps the reaction from growing."}], "answer": "A neutron is absorbed by a uranium-235 nucleus, which splits into two smaller nuclei. Energy is released as kinetic energy of the products. Two or three neutrons are also released. These can split other nuclei, causing a chain reaction. Control rods absorb neutrons, so on average one neutron per fission causes another and the rate stays steady."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say control rods keep it to 'on average one neutron per fission' causing another?", "o": ["Each fission releases exactly one neutron, so no more can ever be absorbed", "More than one would slow the reaction, and fewer than one would speed it up", "Above one, fissions grow out of control; below one, the reaction dies out", "One neutron is the most a uranium nucleus can absorb before it becomes stable"], "a": 2, "f": ["Each fission releases two or three neutrons, not one. The rods absorb the extra ones.", "This is the wrong way round. More than one neutron per fission makes the reaction speed up, not slow down.", "Right. Above one, the number of fissions keeps growing. Below one, it fades away. Exactly one on average keeps it steady.", "The limit is not about a nucleus becoming stable. The aim is to control how many fissions each round causes."], "a_note": null}, "faded": {"task": "Describe how energy is released when two hydrogen nuclei undergo fusion inside a star. (3 marks)", "given": ["Two light hydrogen nuclei need to get very close together.", "Both nuclei are positive, so they repel. The star's very high temperature gives them enough energy to overcome this."], "q": "What is the next point to make?", "o": ["The nuclei split into two smaller nuclei and release neutrons that start a chain reaction", "The heavier nucleus emits an alpha particle, which carries all the energy away", "Control rods absorb the extra neutrons so that the fusion rate stays steady", "The nuclei join into a heavier nucleus, and some mass is converted into energy"], "a": 3, "f": ["This describes fission, not fusion. Fusion joins nuclei; it does not split them.", "Alpha emission is radioactive decay, not fusion. Fusion forms a heavier nucleus and releases energy as radiation.", "Control rods belong to fission reactors. Stars have no control rods, and this point does not describe what fusion does.", "Right. Joining into a heavier nucleus is fusion itself, and the energy comes from some mass being converted."], "a_note": null}, "trap": "Mixing up fission and fusion. Fission splits a large nucleus and releases neutrons that cause chain reactions. Fusion joins light nuclei, as in stars. Control rods absorb neutrons; they do not stop them being released.", "recap": "Fission splits a large nucleus and releases neutrons that can start a chain reaction; fusion joins light nuclei; both release energy."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.5.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 asks you to tell mass from weight, sort scalars from vectors and forces into contact and non-contact, and use $W = mg$.", "hook": {"q": "A 2 kg bag of rocks goes from Earth to the Moon, where gravity is weaker. What happens to the bag?", "o": ["Its mass and its weight both get smaller", "Its mass gets smaller but its weight stays the same", "Its weight gets smaller but its mass stays the same", "Neither its mass nor its weight changes"], "a": 2, "f": ["Only weight falls. Mass is the amount of matter in the bag, and no rocks have been removed.", "Reversed. Mass is the amount of matter, so it stays the same. The weaker pull changes the weight.", "Right. The bag has the same amount of matter, so the mass is unchanged. Gravity is weaker, so the pull on it (its weight) is smaller.", "Weight does change. It is the pull of gravity, and the Moon's pull is weaker than the Earth's."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Scalars and vectors", "b": "A **scalar** has size only. Examples: mass, speed, distance, energy. A **vector** has size and direction. Examples: force, weight, velocity, displacement. A vector is drawn as an arrow. The length shows the size and the arrowhead shows the direction."}, {"h": "Contact and non-contact forces", "b": "**Contact forces** need the objects to touch. Examples: friction, air resistance, tension, the push of a hand. **Non-contact forces** act across a gap. Examples: gravity, magnetic force, electrostatic force. Every force is a vector."}, {"h": "Weight and gravity", "b": "**Weight** is the force on an object caused by gravity. Near the Earth, the **gravitational field** pulls objects towards the Earth's centre. Weight acts as if from one point, the **centre of mass**. A calibrated newtonmeter (spring balance) measures weight."}, {"h": "Using W = mg", "b": "**Mass** is in kg and is the same everywhere. **Weight** is in N and depends on the **gravitational field strength**, g, in N/kg. $W = mg$. At one place, weight is directly proportional to mass: double the mass, double the weight."}], "example": {"task": "Explain the difference between mass and weight. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Mass is the amount of matter in an object. It is measured in kilograms and is a scalar, so it has no direction.", "why": "This defines mass with its unit and says it is a scalar."}, {"do": "Weight is the force on the object due to gravity. It is measured in newtons and is a vector, acting towards the centre of the Earth.", "why": "This defines weight as a force, with its unit and direction."}, {"do": "An object's mass stays the same wherever it is, for example on the Earth or the Moon.", "why": "The amount of matter does not change when the object moves."}, {"do": "Weight depends on the gravitational field strength, because $W = mg$. So the same object weighs less on the Moon, where g is smaller.", "why": "This links weight to g and gives a clear comparison."}], "answer": "Mass is the amount of matter in an object, in kg, and is a scalar. Weight is the force of gravity on the object, in N, and is a vector. Mass is the same everywhere. Weight depends on g, so it is smaller on the Moon."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say mass stays the same on the Moon?", "o": ["Mass is the amount of matter, and moving does not change that", "The Moon has the same gravitational field strength as the Earth", "Mass is a force, and forces do not change from place to place", "Mass is measured in kg, and kg are not affected by gravity"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Mass measures how much matter there is. The object has not gained or lost any, so its mass is unchanged.", "Wrong. The Moon's g is smaller than the Earth's. That is why weight changes, but it is not the reason mass stays the same.", "Wrong. Mass is not a force. Weight is the force. Mass is a scalar and has no direction.", "This is circular. The unit does not explain anything. Mass stays the same because the amount of matter stays the same."]}, "faded": {"task": "A student pushes a box across a rough floor. Describe the forces on the box, saying which are contact and which are non-contact.", "given": ["Push from the student: a contact force, because the hands touch the box.", "Friction: a contact force, because the floor touches the box."], "q": "What is the next point in the answer?", "o": ["Weight: a contact force, because the box presses down on the floor.", "Weight: not a force, because it is measured by mass in kilograms.", "Weight: a non-contact force, but only while the box is moving.", "Weight: a non-contact force, because gravity acts on the box without touching it."], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. The box does press on the floor, but weight is the pull of gravity, not that push. Gravity acts without touching.", "Wrong. Weight is a force, measured in newtons. Mass is the quantity in kilograms.", "Wrong. Gravity pulls on the box whether it is moving or at rest.", "Right. Weight is the force of gravity. It acts on the box across a gap, so it is a non-contact force."]}, "trap": "Treating mass and weight as the same thing, or giving weight in kg. Mass is in kg and is a scalar. Weight is a force in N and is a vector. Name which one you mean.", "recap": "Mass (kg, scalar) is the same everywhere; weight (N, vector) is the pull of gravity, $W = mg$, so it changes with g."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.5.1.4", "kind": "method", "why": "Resultant force questions appear in Paper 2, and you need them for Newton's laws, motion and many calculations that follow.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Free-body diagrams", "b": "A **free-body diagram** shows one object on its own. Draw an arrow for each force, starting on the object. The **length** of the arrow shows the size of the force. The arrow points the way the force acts. Label each arrow with its name and size."}, {"h": "Forces in a line", "b": "Choose one direction as positive. **Add** forces that act the same way. **Subtract** forces that oppose each other. The **resultant force** points the way of the larger total. If the resultant is 0 N, the forces are balanced."}, {"h": "Forces at an angle (Higher)", "b": "Forces that are not in a line cannot simply be added. Use a **scale drawing**. Pick a scale, such as 1 cm = 10 N. Draw the forces **tip to tail**. The line from the start to the end is the resultant. Measure its length and angle."}, {"h": "Resolving a force (Higher)", "b": "A single force can be **resolved** into two **components** at right angles. Together they have the same effect as the original force. Draw the force to scale as the long side of a right-angled triangle. Measure the horizontal and vertical sides. Convert them with the scale."}], "example": {"task": "A drone is pushed by two forces at right angles: 30 N east and 40 N north. Find the resultant force, in size and direction, by scale drawing. Press Next step to build the drawing on the graph.", "steps": [{"do": "Choose a scale: 1 cm = 10 N. So 30 N is drawn as 3 cm and 40 N as 4 cm.", "why": "A scale fits the drawing on the page. You convert lengths back to newtons at the end."}, {"do": "Draw a 3 cm line pointing east. Label it 30 N.", "why": "This is the first force, drawn to scale in its own direction."}, {"do": "From the tip of that line, draw a 4 cm line at 90° pointing north.", "why": "Tip to tail puts the forces in order. The 90° keeps the real angle between them."}, {"do": "Draw a line from the start of the first arrow to the tip of the second. Measure it: 5.0 cm.", "why": "This line is the single force with the same effect as both forces together."}, {"do": "Convert: 5.0 × 10 = 50 N. Measure the angle from the east line with a protractor: about 53°.", "why": "Multiply by the scale to get newtons. The angle gives the direction."}], "answer": "The resultant force is 50 N, at about 53° north of east (measured from the 30 N force).", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "steps", "equal": true, "x": [0, 50], "xstep": 10, "y": [0, 50], "ystep": 10, "xlabel": "Force east / N", "ylabel": "Force north / N", "lines": [{"c": 0, "step": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [30, 0]]}, {"c": 2, "step": 2, "pts": [[30, 0], [30, 40]]}, {"c": 1, "step": 3, "pts": [[0, 0], [30, 40]]}, {"c": 3, "step": 5, "pts": [[10.0, 0.0], [9.9998, 0.058], [9.9993, 0.1159], [9.9985, 0.1739], [9.9973, 0.2318], [9.9958, 0.2897], [9.994, 0.3477], [9.9918, 0.4056], [9.9893, 0.4635], [9.9864, 0.5214], [9.9832, 0.5792], [9.9797, 0.6371], [9.9758, 0.6949], [9.9716, 0.7527], [9.9671, 0.8105], [9.9622, 0.8682], [9.957, 0.926], [9.9515, 0.9837], [9.9456, 1.0413], [9.9394, 1.0989], [9.9329, 1.1565], [9.926, 1.2141], [9.9188, 1.2716], [9.9113, 1.329], [9.9034, 1.3865], [9.8952, 1.4438], [9.8867, 1.5012], [9.8778, 1.5584], [9.8686, 1.6157], [9.8591, 1.6728], [9.8492, 1.7299], [9.839, 1.787], [9.8285, 1.844], [9.8177, 1.9009], [9.8065, 1.9578], [9.795, 2.0146], [9.7831, 2.0713], [9.771, 2.128], [9.7585, 2.1846], [9.7456, 2.2411], [9.7325, 2.2975], [9.719, 2.3539], [9.7052, 2.4102], [9.6911, 2.4664], [9.6766, 2.5225], [9.6618, 2.5786], [9.6467, 2.6345], [9.6313, 2.6904], [9.6155, 2.7462], [9.5995, 2.8018], [9.5831, 2.8574], [9.5663, 2.9129], [9.5493, 2.9683], [9.5319, 3.0236], [9.5143, 3.0788], [9.4962, 3.1339], [9.4779, 3.1889], [9.4593, 3.2437], [9.4403, 3.2985], [9.4211, 3.3532], [9.4015, 3.4077], [9.3816, 3.4621], [9.3613, 3.5165], [9.3408, 3.5707], [9.3199, 3.6247], [9.2988, 3.6787], [9.2773, 3.7325], [9.2555, 3.7862], [9.2334, 3.8398], [9.211, 3.8932], [9.1883, 3.9466], [9.1653, 3.9997], [9.1419, 4.0528], [9.1183, 4.1057], [9.0943, 4.1585], [9.0701, 4.2111], [9.0455, 4.2636], [9.0207, 4.316], [8.9955, 4.3682], [8.97, 4.4202], [8.9443, 4.4722], [8.9182, 4.5239], [8.8918, 4.5755], [8.8652, 4.627], [8.8382, 4.6783], [8.8109, 4.7294], [8.7834, 4.7804], [8.7555, 4.8312], [8.7274, 4.8819], [8.6989, 4.9324], [8.6702, 4.9827], [8.6412, 5.0329], [8.6119, 5.0829], [8.5823, 5.1327], [8.5524, 5.1824], [8.5222, 5.2319], [8.4917, 5.2812], [8.461, 5.3303], [8.4299, 5.3792], [8.3986, 5.428], [8.367, 5.4766], [8.3351, 5.525], [8.303, 5.5732], [8.2705, 5.6212], [8.2378, 5.6691], [8.2048, 5.7167], [8.1716, 5.7642], [8.138, 5.8114], [8.1042, 5.8585], [8.0701, 5.9054], [8.0358, 5.952], [8.0011, 5.9985], [7.9662, 6.0448], [7.9311, 6.0908], [7.8956, 6.1367], [7.8599, 6.1824], [7.824, 6.2278], [7.7877, 6.2731], [7.7512, 6.3181], [7.7145, 6.3629], [7.6775, 6.4075], [7.6402, 6.4519], [7.6027, 6.4961], [7.5649, 6.54], [7.5269, 6.5837], [7.4886, 6.6273], [7.4501, 6.6705], [7.4113, 6.7136], [7.3723, 6.7565], [7.333, 6.7991], [7.2935, 6.8415], [7.2537, 6.8836], [7.2137, 6.9255], [7.1734, 6.9672], [7.1329, 7.0087], [7.0922, 7.0499], [7.0512, 7.0909], [7.01, 7.1316], [6.9685, 7.1721], [6.9268, 7.2124], [6.8849, 7.2524], [6.8428, 7.2922], [6.8004, 7.3317], [6.7578, 7.371], [6.715, 7.4101], [6.6719, 7.4489], [6.6286, 7.4874], [6.5851, 7.5257], [6.5414, 7.5637], [6.4974, 7.6015], [6.4533, 7.6391], [6.4089, 7.6763], [6.3643, 7.7133], [6.3195, 7.7501], [6.2745, 7.7866], [6.2292, 7.8228], [6.1838, 7.8588], [6.1381, 7.8945], [6.0923, 7.9299], [6.0462, 7.9651], [6.0, 8.0]], "label": "53°"}], "points": [{"at": [0, 0], "label": "Start", "step": 1}, {"at": [15, 0], "label": "30 N", "step": 1}, {"at": [30, 20], "label": "40 N", "step": 2}, {"at": [15, 20], "label": "Resultant", "step": 3}, {"at": [30, 40], "label": "5.0 cm = 50 N", "step": 4}], "steps": ["Scale: 1 cm = 10 N, so each square is 1 cm. Draw the 30 N force as 3 cm east from the start.", "From the tip, draw the 40 N force as 4 cm north, at 90°. Tip to tail keeps the forces in order.", "Join the start to the last tip. This line is the resultant: one force with the same effect as both.", "Measure the line: 5.0 cm. Multiply by the scale: 5.0 × 10 = 50 N.", "Measure the angle from the east line: about 53°. The resultant is 50 N at about 53° north of east."]}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the resultant drawn from the start of the first arrow to the tip of the last arrow?", "o": ["The longest side of any drawing is always the resultant", "Forces at right angles must be subtracted, so the line joins the ends", "That line is the single force with the same effect as both forces together", "A protractor only works on a line that starts at the first arrow"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Length is not the reason. The line is the resultant because of how the forces were joined tip to tail, not because it looks longest.", "Wrong. Forces at an angle are not subtracted. Subtracting only works for forces in a line, in opposite directions.", "Right. Drawing forces tip to tail combines them, size and direction. The line from start to end is the one force that does the same job.", "Wrong. A protractor can measure an angle from any line. The reason for this line is what it represents, not how you measure it."]}, "faded": {"task": "Two forces act on a boat at right angles: 12 N north and 16 N east. Find the resultant by scale drawing.", "given": ["Scale: 1 cm = 4 N, so 12 N = 3 cm and 16 N = 4 cm.", "Draw a 4 cm line pointing east."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Continue the same line east for another 3 cm, then measure the whole line", "From the tip of the east line, draw a 3 cm line at 90° pointing north", "Draw a 3 cm line north from the start of the east line, then measure it", "Add 3 cm and 4 cm to get 7 cm, then multiply by 4 N to get 28 N"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Drawing the second force along the first treats them as if they act in the same direction. The 12 N force acts north, at 90° to the east force.", "Right. Tip to tail with the correct 90° angle. Joining the start to the end then gives a 5 cm line, so the resultant is 20 N.", "Wrong. This measures only the 12 N force again. You must draw the second force from the tip of the first, then join the start to the end.", "Wrong. This adds the sizes without regard to direction. Forces at right angles do not add in this way. The resultant is smaller than 28 N."]}, "trap": "Adding the sizes of forces that are not in a line. Here 30 N and 40 N at right angles do not give 70 N. Draw them tip to tail and measure.", "recap": "Add forces in the same direction, subtract opposing ones, and for forces at an angle draw them tip to tail and join the ends."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.5.2", "kind": "method", "why": "Work done is a regular short calculation on Paper 2, often with a unit conversion or a link to kinetic energy or friction heating.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "The work done equation", "b": "**Work done** is the energy transferred when a force moves an object. Use $W = Fs$. W is in joules (J), F in newtons (N) and s in metres (m). Use only the distance moved **in the direction of the force**."}, {"h": "Work done is energy transferred", "b": "The work done by a force equals the **energy transferred**, in joules. Lifting a box at a steady speed raises its **gravitational potential energy** by the work done. Speeding up a trolley raises its **kinetic energy** by the work done on it, if friction is ignored. 1 J = 1 N m."}, {"h": "Work against friction", "b": "When a force moves an object against **friction**, the work done is transferred as **thermal energy**. The surfaces and the surroundings get warmer. Brake blocks heating up is an example. The energy is not lost. It is spread out into the surroundings."}, {"h": "Check units before you start", "b": "Write F in N and s in m before you substitute. **Convert** first: cm ÷ 100 gives m, and km × 1000 gives m. Then use $W = Fs$. To find a distance or force, **rearrange** the equation, for example $s = W / F$."}], "example": {"task": "A worker pushes a crate 250 cm at constant speed across a rough floor with a horizontal force of 60 N. Calculate the work done and say where the energy goes.", "steps": [{"do": "Write the equation: $W = Fs$.", "why": "Work done is force times distance moved in the direction of the force."}, {"do": "Convert the distance: 250 cm ÷ 100 = 2.5 m.", "why": "The answer is only in joules if the distance is in metres."}, {"do": "Substitute: $W = 60 \\times 2.5$.", "why": "The push of 60 N is horizontal, along the movement, so F and s can be used directly."}, {"do": "Calculate: $W = 150$ J.", "why": "Give the unit. Work done is measured in joules."}, {"do": "Link to energy: 150 J is transferred, as thermal energy, to the crate, the floor and the air.", "why": "The speed is constant, so there is no gain in kinetic energy. The work is done against friction, which causes heating."}], "answer": "Work done = 150 J. This energy is transferred by friction to thermal energy, so the crate and floor get slightly warmer."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked example change 250 cm into 2.5 m before using $W = Fs$?", "o": ["Force and distance must always be in the same unit", "One joule is one newton metre, so the distance must be in metres", "The equation only works for distances of more than 1 m", "Metres give a smaller number, which is easier to use"], "a": 1, "f": ["Force and distance have different units. The rule is that N and m together give J, not that the two units match.", "Right. A joule is a newton metre. With F in N and s in m, the answer comes out in J.", "The equation works for any distance. The reason to convert is the unit of the answer, not the size of the distance.", "Easier numbers are not the reason. Using cm would give an answer that is not in joules."]}, "faded": {"task": "A sledge is pulled 0.4 km along level ground by a constant horizontal force of 35 N. Calculate the work done.", "given": ["$W = Fs$", "Convert: $s = 0.4$ km $= 400$ m"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["$W = 35 \\times 0.4 = 14$ J", "$W = 35 \\div 400 = 0.0875$ J", "$W = 35 \\times 400 = 14\\,000$ J", "$W = 35 \\times 400 = 14\\,000$ N"], "a": 2, "f": ["This uses 0.4 km, not the converted 400 m. The distance was converted for a reason, and an answer in joules needs it in metres.", "This divides force by distance. Work done is force multiplied by distance, so the answer would be wrong.", "Right. Substitute the converted distance and multiply: 35 × 400 = 14 000. The unit is joules.", "The number is right but the unit is wrong. Work done is measured in joules (J), not newtons (N)."]}, "trap": "Leaving the distance in cm or km, so the answer is not in joules. Convert to metres first. Also use only the distance moved in the direction of the force.", "recap": "Work done $W = Fs$, with F in newtons and s in metres, equals energy transferred in joules; work against friction heats things up."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.5.3", "kind": "method", "why": "Spring calculations come up often in the exam, and one slip with converting cm to m or squaring e can cost most of the marks.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Extension and compression", "b": "To stretch or compress a spring you need two forces, one at each end. The **extension** is the new length minus the **natural length**. For **compression**, it is the natural length minus the new length. Use metres in the equations."}, {"h": "Force and spring constant", "b": "Within the **limit of proportionality**, force and extension are directly proportional: $F = ke$. On the graph this is the straight line, and its gradient is the **spring constant** k, in N/m. A stiffer spring has a bigger k. Beyond the limit, the graph curves and the equation no longer works.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 0.14], "y": [0, 3], "xstep": 0.02, "ystep": 0.5, "xlabel": "extension in m", "ylabel": "force in N", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [0.08, 2]], "label": "F = ke", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0.08, 2], [0.1, 2.3], [0.12, 2.5], [0.14, 2.6]], "dash": true, "c": 1}], "points": [{"at": [0.08, 2], "label": "limit of proportionality"}]}}, {"h": "Energy stored in a spring", "b": "Stretching or compressing a spring **transfers energy** to it. This is stored as **elastic potential energy**. Within the limit of proportionality, $E_e = \\frac{1}{2}ke^2$. Because e is squared, doubling the extension gives four times the energy."}, {"h": "A method that works", "b": "1. Find e, the change in length. 2. **Convert** cm to m. 3. Choose $F = ke$ or $E_e = \\frac{1}{2}ke^2$. 4. Substitute, and **square** e before you multiply by anything. 5. Give the unit: N or J."}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows force against extension for a spring (k = 25 N/m, natural length 8.0 cm). It is stretched to 14.0 cm (point P), within its limit. Calculate the force applied and the energy stored.", "steps": [{"do": "Extension e = 14.0 − 8.0 = 6.0 cm.", "why": "The equations need the change in length, not the total length."}, {"do": "Convert: e = 6.0 cm = 0.060 m.", "why": "k is in N/m, so e must be in metres."}, {"do": "F = ke = 25 × 0.060 = 1.5 N.", "why": "The spring is within its limit of proportionality, so F = ke applies."}, {"do": "E = ½ke² = ½ × 25 × 0.060² = ½ × 25 × 0.0036 = 0.045 J.", "why": "Square e first (0.0036), then multiply by 25 and by ½."}, {"do": "Check: ½Fe = ½ × 1.5 × 0.060 = 0.045 J.", "why": "The same answer from a second route shows no slip was made."}], "answer": "Force = 1.5 N. Energy stored = 0.045 J.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 0.08], "y": [0, 2], "xstep": 0.01, "ystep": 0.5, "xlabel": "extension in m", "ylabel": "force in N", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [0.08, 2]], "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [0.06, 1.5], "label": "P"}]}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked example use e = 6.0 cm and not 14.0 cm?", "o": ["14.0 cm is beyond the limit of proportionality, so it cannot be used", "The extension is the change from the natural length, not the total length", "The spring constant is only measured over the first 6.0 cm", "14.0 cm would give the energy in the wrong unit"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. The question says the spring stays within its limit. The 14.0 cm is simply the new length, not the extension.", "Right. e is how much longer the spring is than its natural length: 14.0 − 8.0 = 6.0 cm. Using total length is a common slip.", "Wrong. The spring constant is a property of the spring, constant within the limit. It is not tied to a 6.0 cm range.", "Wrong. Using 14.0 cm would still give an answer in J; the problem is that 14.0 cm is the new length, not the extension."]}, "faded": {"task": "A spring with spring constant 80 N/m has natural length 15 cm. It is compressed to 12 cm, within its limit of proportionality. Calculate the energy stored.", "given": ["Compression e = 15 − 12 = 3 cm", "Convert to metres: e = 0.03 m"], "q": "Which is the correct next step?", "o": ["E = ½ × 80 × 0.03 = 1.2 J", "E = 80 × 0.03² = 0.072 J", "E = ½ × 80 × 0.03² = 0.036 J", "E = (½ × 80 × 0.03)² = 1.44 J"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. This forgets to square e. The equation has e², so 0.03 must be squared before multiplying.", "Wrong. This leaves out the ½. The energy equation is ½ke², not ke².", "Right. Square 0.03 to get 0.0009, then multiply by 80 and by ½: 40 × 0.0009 = 0.036 J.", "Wrong. The bracket puts the square on the whole expression. Only e is squared, not ½ × k × e."]}, "trap": "Using cm instead of m, or forgetting to square e. Convert to metres first. Then work out e² on its own before multiplying by ½ and k.", "recap": "Find the extension, convert it to metres, then use F = ke or E = ½ke², squaring e in the energy equation."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.5.4", "kind": "method", "why": "Paper 2 can ask you to find a force or distance on a balanced beam, and to explain how levers and gears make a job easier.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Moment of a force", "b": "A force can make an object turn about a **pivot**. The turning effect is the **moment**: $M = Fd$. M is in newton metres (N m), F in newtons (N) and d in metres (m). d is the **perpendicular distance** from the pivot to the line of action of the force. Convert cm to m first."}, {"h": "Balanced objects", "b": "An object is balanced when the total **clockwise moment** equals the total **anticlockwise moment**. Add up every force turning the same way. Measure each distance from the pivot to that force, never between the forces. A force acting through the pivot has d = 0, so it has no moment."}, {"h": "How levers work", "b": "A **lever** is a rigid bar that turns about a pivot. A small **effort** a long way from the pivot can balance a large **load** close to it, because the moments are equal. The long lever means a smaller force is needed, but the effort has to move through a greater distance."}, {"h": "How gears work", "b": "**Gears** are toothed wheels that mesh together and transmit a turning effect from one to the next. Neighbouring gears turn in opposite directions. A large gear driven by a small gear turns more slowly but with a larger **moment**. A small gear driven by a large one turns faster with a smaller moment."}], "example": {"task": "A light beam is balanced on a pivot. A 12 N force acts down 30 cm to the left of the pivot. Force F acts down 45 cm to the right of the pivot. Calculate F.", "steps": [{"do": "The 12 N force turns the beam anticlockwise. F turns it clockwise.", "why": "You need to know which moments to put on which side of the equation."}, {"do": "Convert the distances: 30 cm = 0.30 m and 45 cm = 0.45 m.", "why": "M = Fd gives N m only if d is in metres."}, {"do": "Anticlockwise moment = 12 × 0.30 = 3.6 N m.", "why": "M = Fd uses the force times its distance from the pivot."}, {"do": "The beam is balanced, so the clockwise moment is also 3.6 N m: F × 0.45 = 3.6.", "why": "Balanced means clockwise moments equal anticlockwise moments."}, {"do": "F = 3.6 ÷ 0.45 = 8.0 N.", "why": "Divide the moment by the distance to find the force."}], "answer": "F = 8.0 N. The anticlockwise moment is 12 × 0.30 = 3.6 N m. Balance needs a clockwise moment of 3.6 N m, so F = 3.6 ÷ 0.45 = 8.0 N."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why can the worked example write F × 0.45 = 3.6 in step 4?", "o": ["The beam is balanced, so clockwise and anticlockwise moments are equal.", "The beam is balanced, so the two forces on it must be equal.", "The beam is light, so its own weight has no moment about the pivot.", "The distance 0.45 m is always multiplied by the moment on the left."], "a": 0, "f": ["Correct. Balanced means the total clockwise moment equals the total anticlockwise moment, so F × 0.45 must equal 12 × 0.30 = 3.6 N m.", "Wrong. Balance needs equal moments, not equal forces. Here the forces are 12 N and 8.0 N, and the distances differ too.", "This is true but does not justify the equation. A light beam means you can ignore its weight. Equal moments come from the beam being balanced.", "Wrong. There is no rule like this. You multiply each force by its own distance from the pivot, then compare the two moments."]}, "faded": {"task": "A light beam balances on a pivot. A 36 N force acts down 0.50 m to the left of the pivot. A 24 N force acts down on the right at distance d from the pivot. Find d.", "given": ["Anticlockwise moment = 36 × 0.50 = 18 N m.", "Balanced, so clockwise moment = 18 N m: 24 × d = 18."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["d = 24 ÷ 18 = 1.3 m", "d = 18 − 24 = −6 m", "d = 18 ÷ 24 = 0.75 m", "d = 18 × 24 = 432 m"], "a": 2, "f": ["You turned the division upside down. d is the unknown being multiplied by 24, so divide the moment by the force: 18 ÷ 24.", "Subtracting does not undo a multiplication. Since 24 × d = 18, divide both sides by 24. A negative distance is a warning sign.", "Correct. 24 × d = 18, so divide both sides by 24. d = 0.75 m.", "You multiplied instead of dividing. To undo 24 × d you must divide by 24. A 432 m beam is also unrealistic."]}, "trap": "Using the distance between the two forces, or measuring from the wrong place. Measure each distance from the pivot to that force's line of action. Also convert cm to m before you multiply.", "recap": "Moment = force × perpendicular distance from the pivot, and a balanced object has clockwise moments equal to anticlockwise moments."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.5.5", "kind": "method", "why": "Paper 2 usually has a short pressure calculation, often with a unit conversion, and sometimes asks why air pressure drops as you climb.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "The pressure equation", "b": "**Pressure** is the force on each unit of area. $p = \\frac{F}{A}$. Force F is in newtons (N) and area A is in m². Pressure p is in **pascals** (Pa), and 1 Pa = 1 N/m². The force must act at right angles to the surface."}, {"h": "Same force, different area", "b": "A bigger **area** spreads the same force out, so the **pressure** is lower. A smaller area gives a higher pressure. A knife edge has a tiny area, so it cuts. Snowshoes have a large area, so they sink less. Doubling the area halves the pressure."}, {"h": "Fluids push at right angles", "b": "A **fluid** is a liquid or a gas. Its particles move about and collide with any surface they touch. Each collision pushes on the surface. The overall push is at **right angles** to the surface, whatever its shape or direction."}, {"h": "Air pressure and height", "b": "The **atmosphere** is a layer of air around the Earth. Air has weight. The pressure at a point comes from the weight of the air above it. Higher up, there is less air above, so **air pressure** is lower."}], "example": {"task": "A block weighs 240 N. It rests on a table on a face measuring 40 cm by 25 cm. Calculate the pressure the block exerts on the table.", "steps": [{"do": "Write the equation: $p = \\frac{F}{A}$.", "why": "Pressure is force divided by area. Writing it first stops you dividing the wrong way round."}, {"do": "The force is the block's weight, F = 240 N.", "why": "The weight pushes straight down, at right angles to the table top."}, {"do": "Change the lengths to metres: 40 cm = 0.40 m and 25 cm = 0.25 m.", "why": "The pascal is N/m², so the area must end up in m²."}, {"do": "Find the area: $A = 0.40 \\times 0.25 = 0.10$ m².", "why": "The face is a rectangle, so area = length × width."}, {"do": "Substitute: $p = \\frac{240}{0.10} = 2400$ Pa.", "why": "Force in N divided by area in m² gives pressure in Pa."}], "answer": "The pressure on the table is 2400 Pa (2.4 kPa)."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer change 40 cm and 25 cm into metres before finding the area?", "o": ["Lengths in centimetres cannot be multiplied together to find an area", "Pressure is always found by dividing, and division only works with metres", "The pascal is N/m², so the area must be in m² to give an answer in Pa", "The block is on a table, and tables are always measured in metres"], "a": 2, "f": ["You can multiply centimetres to get cm². The problem is that cm² would not give the right unit, Pa.", "The equation is the same whatever the units. The unit of pressure, Pa, is what needs m².", "Right. Pa means N/m², so using m² for area gives pressure in Pa. Using cm² would give N/cm², not Pa.", "Where the block sits has no effect on the units. The units come from the definition of the pascal."]}, "faded": {"task": "A girl weighing 500 N stands on both feet. Each foot has an area of 0.025 m². Find the pressure she exerts on the floor.", "given": ["Force on the floor: F = 500 N", "Total area of both feet: A = 2 × 0.025 = 0.050 m²"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["$p = 500 \\times 0.050 = 25$ Pa", "$p = \\frac{500}{0.050} = 10\\,000$ Pa", "$p = \\frac{0.050}{500} = 0.0001$ Pa", "$p = \\frac{500}{0.025} = 20\\,000$ Pa"], "a": 1, "f": ["This multiplies force by area. Pressure is force divided by area, so the answer must be much larger than 25 Pa.", "Correct. Both feet are on the floor, so the total area is 0.050 m². Then 500 ÷ 0.050 = 10 000 Pa.", "This divides area by force, the wrong way round. A person standing does not exert a pressure as tiny as this.", "This uses the area of one foot only. Both feet touch the floor, so the total area of 0.050 m² is needed."]}, "trap": "Dividing area by force, or leaving the area in cm² when the equation needs m². Write $p = \\frac{F}{A}$ first, then check that the force is in N and the area in m² before you divide.", "recap": "Pressure = force ÷ area, with force in N and area in m²; fluids push at right angles, and air pressure falls with height."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.5.5.1.2", "kind": "method", "why": "Higher-tier papers ask you to calculate liquid pressure at a depth, often then a force, and to explain upthrust and floating in a few marks.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Pressure in a liquid", "b": "Pressure at a point in a liquid comes from the weight of liquid above it. Use $p = h\\rho g$. Here p is in pascals (Pa), h is the depth in metres, ρ is the density in kg/m³ and g is in N/kg. Pressure rises with **depth** and with **density**."}, {"h": "Which depth to use", "b": "h is the **vertical depth** below the surface of the liquid. It is not the length of the object or the height of the container. Double the depth and the pressure doubles. To find a force on a surface, use $F = pA$, with the **area** A in m²."}, {"h": "Where upthrust comes from", "b": "A submerged object has liquid above and below it. The bottom is deeper, so the **pressure** there is greater. The upward force on the bottom face is bigger than the downward force on the top face. This difference is the **upthrust**, a resultant force upwards."}, {"h": "Floating and sinking", "b": "If upthrust equals weight, the object **floats** at rest. If its weight is bigger than the upthrust even when fully under the surface, it **sinks**. A denser liquid gives more upthrust for the same submerged volume, so a floating object sits higher."}], "example": {"task": "A hatch of area 0.50 m² is 40 m below the surface of sea water (density 1030 kg/m³). g = 9.8 N/kg. Calculate the force on the hatch due to the water.", "steps": [{"do": "Take h = 40 m, ρ = 1030 kg/m³, g = 9.8 N/kg, A = 0.50 m².", "why": "The hatch is 40 m below the surface, so this is the vertical depth h."}, {"do": "Write $p = h\\rho g$.", "why": "This gives the pressure due to a column of liquid."}, {"do": "Substitute: $p = 40 \\times 1030 \\times 9.8 = 403\\,760$ Pa.", "why": "All three of h, ρ and g must be multiplied together."}, {"do": "Write $F = pA$, so $F = 403\\,760 \\times 0.50 = 201\\,880$ N.", "why": "Pressure is force per unit area, so force is pressure × area."}, {"do": "Round and give the unit: about 200 000 N.", "why": "The least precise data has 2 significant figures, so 2.0 × 10⁵ N is sensible."}], "answer": "The force on the hatch is about 200 000 N (2.0 × 10⁵ N), acting at right angles to the hatch."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 4 multiply the pressure by 0.50 m²?", "o": ["Pressure is force × area, so the area is needed to find the pressure.", "Pressure is force per unit area, so force = pressure × area.", "The area changes the depth, so it must be included in the force.", "Density is measured per square metre, so area must be multiplied in."], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Pressure is force divided by area, not force × area. You already have the pressure, so you multiply by area to get the force.", "Right. $p = F/A$ rearranges to $F = pA$. The pressure is the force on each square metre, and the hatch has 0.50 m².", "Wrong. Area has no effect on depth, and depth was already used in step 3. Area is used because pressure is force per unit area.", "Wrong. Density is mass per cubic metre (kg/m³), not per square metre. Area is used because pressure is force per unit area."]}, "faded": {"task": "A window of area 0.20 m² on a submarine is 25 m below the surface of sea water (1030 kg/m³). g = 9.8 N/kg. Find the force on it due to the water.", "given": ["Depth h = 25 m, ρ = 1030 kg/m³, g = 9.8 N/kg, A = 0.20 m²", "Use $p = h\\rho g = 25 \\times 1030 \\times 9.8$"], "q": "What should you do next?", "o": ["p = 252 350 Pa, so the force is also 252 350 N, as pressure is a force", "p = 252 350 Pa; F = p ÷ A = 252 350 ÷ 0.20 = 1 261 750 N", "p = 252 350 Pa; F = p × A = 252 350 × 0.20 = 50 470 N", "p = 25 750 Pa; F = p × A = 25 750 × 0.20 = 5150 N"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Pressure is force per unit area, not a force. You still need to multiply by the area to find the force.", "Wrong. This divides by the area. Since $p = F/A$, the force is $F = pA$, so you multiply.", "Right. The pressure is 252 350 Pa. Multiplying by the area 0.20 m² gives the force of 50 470 N.", "Wrong. This leaves out g. The pressure must be $25 \\times 1030 \\times 9.8 = 252\\,350$ Pa before you find the force."]}, "trap": "Leaving out one of h, ρ or g, or using the wrong h. Take h as the vertical depth below the surface, not the object's size. Write all three numbers in your substitution.", "recap": "Pressure in a liquid is $p = h\\rho g$ with vertical depth h; upthrust exists because pressure is greater below an object than above it."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.5.6.1", "kind": "method", "why": "Exam questions often give you a journey or a distance-time graph and ask for a speed, so you need to use v = s ÷ t and read gradients correctly.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Speed and velocity", "b": "**Speed** is how fast you move. It has no direction. Use $v = \\frac{s}{t}$, with distance $s$ in m, time $t$ in s and speed $v$ in m/s. **Velocity** is speed in a stated direction. Two cars at 20 m/s, one north and one south, have the same speed but different velocities."}, {"h": "Distance and displacement", "b": "**Distance** is how far you travel along your path. It has no direction. **Displacement** is the straight-line distance from start to finish, in a stated direction. Run one lap of a 400 m track: your distance is 400 m, but your displacement is 0 m."}, {"h": "Reading the graph", "b": "On a distance-time graph, time runs across and distance runs up. A **flat line** means stationary. A straight sloping line means constant speed; steeper means faster. A curve means the speed is changing. Drag the slider to move P along the curve: the tangent at P is steeper than the steady line after 2 s. The **gradient** equals the speed.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [0, 8], "xstep": 1, "y": [0, 64], "ystep": 8, "xlabel": "time / s", "ylabel": "distance / m", "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [0.05, 0.0025], [0.1, 0.01], [0.15, 0.0225], [0.2, 0.04], [0.25, 0.0625], [0.3, 0.09], [0.35, 0.1225], [0.4, 0.16], [0.45, 0.2025], [0.5, 0.25], [0.55, 0.3025], [0.6, 0.36], [0.65, 0.4225], [0.7, 0.49], [0.75, 0.5625], [0.8, 0.64], [0.85, 0.7225], [0.9, 0.81], [0.95, 0.9025], [1.0, 1.0], [1.05, 1.1025], [1.1, 1.21], [1.15, 1.3225], [1.2, 1.44], [1.25, 1.5625], [1.3, 1.69], [1.35, 1.8225], [1.4, 1.96], [1.45, 2.1025], [1.5, 2.25], [1.55, 2.4025], [1.6, 2.56], [1.65, 2.7225], [1.7, 2.89], [1.75, 3.0625], [1.8, 3.24], [1.85, 3.4225], [1.9, 3.61], [1.95, 3.8025], [2.0, 4.0], [2.05, 4.2025], [2.1, 4.41], [2.15, 4.6225], [2.2, 4.84], [2.25, 5.0625], [2.3, 5.29], [2.35, 5.5225], [2.4, 5.76], [2.45, 6.0025], [2.5, 6.25], [2.55, 6.5025], [2.6, 6.76], [2.65, 7.0225], [2.7, 7.29], [2.75, 7.5625], [2.8, 7.84], [2.85, 8.1225], [2.9, 8.41], [2.95, 8.7025], [3.0, 9.0], [3.05, 9.3025], [3.1, 9.61], [3.15, 9.9225], [3.2, 10.24], [3.25, 10.5625], [3.3, 10.89], [3.35, 11.2225], [3.4, 11.56], [3.45, 11.9025], [3.5, 12.25], [3.55, 12.6025], [3.6, 12.96], [3.65, 13.3225], [3.7, 13.69], [3.75, 14.0625], [3.8, 14.44], [3.85, 14.8225], [3.9, 15.21], [3.95, 15.6025], [4.0, 16.0], [4.05, 16.4025], [4.1, 16.81], [4.15, 17.2225], [4.2, 17.64], [4.25, 18.0625], [4.3, 18.49], [4.35, 18.9225], [4.4, 19.36], [4.45, 19.8025], [4.5, 20.25], [4.55, 20.7025], [4.6, 21.16], [4.65, 21.6225], [4.7, 22.09], [4.75, 22.5625], [4.8, 23.04], [4.85, 23.5225], [4.9, 24.01], [4.95, 24.5025], [5.0, 25.0], [5.05, 25.5025], [5.1, 26.01], [5.15, 26.5225], [5.2, 27.04], [5.25, 27.5625], [5.3, 28.09], [5.35, 28.6225], [5.4, 29.16], [5.45, 29.7025], [5.5, 30.25], [5.55, 30.8025], [5.6, 31.36], [5.65, 31.9225], [5.7, 32.49], [5.75, 33.0625], [5.8, 33.64], [5.85, 34.2225], [5.9, 34.81], [5.95, 35.4025], [6.0, 36.0], [6.05, 36.6025], [6.1, 37.21], [6.15, 37.8225], [6.2, 38.44], [6.25, 39.0625], [6.3, 39.69], [6.35, 40.3225], [6.4, 40.96], [6.45, 41.6025], [6.5, 42.25], [6.55, 42.9025], [6.6, 43.56], [6.65, 44.2225], [6.7, 44.89], [6.75, 45.5625], [6.8, 46.24], [6.85, 46.9225], [6.9, 47.61], [6.95, 48.3025], [7.0, 49.0], [7.05, 49.7025], [7.1, 50.41], [7.15, 51.1225], [7.2, 51.84], [7.25, 52.5625], [7.3, 53.29], [7.35, 54.0225], [7.4, 54.76], [7.45, 55.5025], [7.5, 56.25], [7.55, 57.0025], [7.6, 57.76], [7.65, 58.5225], [7.7, 59.29], [7.75, 60.0625], [7.8, 60.84], [7.85, 61.6225], [7.9, 62.41], [7.95, 63.2025], [8.0, 64.0]], "label": "speeding up"}, {"label": "steady", "c": 2, "pts": [[0, 0], [8, 32]]}], "points": [], "frames": [{"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[1.0, 0.0], [3.0, 8.0]], "label": "tangent"}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[2.0, 4.0], [3.0, 4.0]]}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[3.0, 4.0], [3.0, 8.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [2.0, 4.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 2 s, distance = 4 m", "Tangent: rise 4 m ÷ run 1 s = 4 m/s", "Steady line: 32 ÷ 8 = 4 m/s"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[1.5, 1.25], [3.5, 11.25]], "label": "tangent"}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[2.5, 6.25], [3.5, 6.25]]}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[3.5, 6.25], [3.5, 11.25]]}], "points": [{"at": [2.5, 6.25], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 2.5 s, distance = 6.25 m", "Tangent: rise 5 m ÷ run 1 s = 5 m/s", "Steady line: 32 ÷ 8 = 4 m/s"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[2.0, 3.0], [4.0, 15.0]], "label": "tangent"}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[3.0, 9.0], [4.0, 9.0]]}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[4.0, 9.0], [4.0, 15.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [3.0, 9.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 3 s, distance = 9 m", "Tangent: rise 6 m ÷ run 1 s = 6 m/s", "Steady line: 32 ÷ 8 = 4 m/s"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[2.5, 5.25], [4.5, 19.25]], "label": "tangent"}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[3.5, 12.25], [4.5, 12.25]]}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[4.5, 12.25], [4.5, 19.25]]}], "points": [{"at": [3.5, 12.25], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 3.5 s, distance = 12.25 m", "Tangent: rise 7 m ÷ run 1 s = 7 m/s", "Steady line: 32 ÷ 8 = 4 m/s"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[3.0, 8.0], [5.0, 24.0]], "label": "tangent"}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[4.0, 16.0], [5.0, 16.0]]}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[5.0, 16.0], [5.0, 24.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [4.0, 16.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 4 s, distance = 16 m", "Tangent: rise 8 m ÷ run 1 s = 8 m/s", "Steady line: 32 ÷ 8 = 4 m/s"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[3.5, 11.25], [5.5, 29.25]], "label": "tangent"}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[4.5, 20.25], [5.5, 20.25]]}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[5.5, 20.25], [5.5, 29.25]]}], "points": [{"at": [4.5, 20.25], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 4.5 s, distance = 20.25 m", "Tangent: rise 9 m ÷ run 1 s = 9 m/s", "Steady line: 32 ÷ 8 = 4 m/s"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[4.0, 15.0], [6.0, 35.0]], "label": "tangent"}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[5.0, 25.0], [6.0, 25.0]]}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[6.0, 25.0], [6.0, 35.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [5.0, 25.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 5 s, distance = 25 m", "Tangent: rise 10 m ÷ run 1 s = 10 m/s", "Steady line: 32 ÷ 8 = 4 m/s"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[4.5, 19.25], [6.5, 41.25]], "label": "tangent"}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[5.5, 30.25], [6.5, 30.25]]}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[6.5, 30.25], [6.5, 41.25]]}], "points": [{"at": [5.5, 30.25], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 5.5 s, distance = 30.25 m", "Tangent: rise 11 m ÷ run 1 s = 11 m/s", "Steady line: 32 ÷ 8 = 4 m/s"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[5.0, 24.0], [7.0, 48.0]], "label": "tangent"}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[6.0, 36.0], [7.0, 36.0]]}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[7.0, 36.0], [7.0, 48.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [6.0, 36.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 6 s, distance = 36 m", "Tangent: rise 12 m ÷ run 1 s = 12 m/s", "Steady line: 32 ÷ 8 = 4 m/s"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[5.5, 29.25], [7.5, 55.25]], "label": "tangent"}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[6.5, 42.25], [7.5, 42.25]]}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[7.5, 42.25], [7.5, 55.25]]}], "points": [{"at": [6.5, 42.25], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 6.5 s, distance = 42.25 m", "Tangent: rise 13 m ÷ run 1 s = 13 m/s", "Steady line: 32 ÷ 8 = 4 m/s"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[6.0, 35.0], [8.0, 63.0]], "label": "tangent"}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[7.0, 49.0], [8.0, 49.0]]}, {"c": 3, "pts": [[8.0, 49.0], [8.0, 63.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [7.0, 49.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 7 s, distance = 49 m", "Tangent: rise 14 m ÷ run 1 s = 14 m/s", "Steady line: 32 ÷ 8 = 4 m/s"]}], "ctl": {"type": "slider", "label": "p", "vals": ["2", "2.5", "3", "3.5", "4", "4.5", "5", "5.5", "6", "6.5", "7"], "start": 4}}}, {"h": "Finding the gradient", "b": "Choose two points on the straight section, far apart. **Gradient** = change in distance ÷ change in time. Subtract first; do not just use the end values. For a whole journey, **average speed** = total distance ÷ total time, and stops count as time."}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows a cyclist's trip: (0 s, 0 m) to (40 s, 240 m), flat until 100 s, then to (140 s, 360 m). Find the speed in the first 40 s and the average speed for the whole trip.", "steps": [{"do": "The first section is a straight sloping line, so the speed is constant and equals the gradient.", "why": "Gradient of a distance-time graph = speed."}, {"do": "Change in distance = 240 − 0 = 240 m. Change in time = 40 − 0 = 40 s.", "why": "Subtract the two points to get the changes."}, {"do": "Speed = 240 ÷ 40 = 6 m/s.", "why": "Use $v = \\frac{s}{t}$. The units come from m and s."}, {"do": "The flat part from 40 s to 100 s means the cyclist is stationary. The whole trip covers 360 m in 140 s.", "why": "Total distance is the last distance value. The stop still counts as time."}, {"do": "Average speed = 360 ÷ 140 = 2.57 m/s, which is 2.6 m/s to 2 significant figures.", "why": "Average speed = total distance ÷ total time."}], "answer": "Speed in the first 40 s = 6 m/s. Average speed for the whole trip = 360 ÷ 140 = 2.6 m/s (2 s.f.).", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "draw", "pen": true, "read": ["t (s)", "d (m)"], "dp": 0, "ms": 5000, "x": [0, 140], "xstep": 20, "y": [0, 360], "ystep": 60, "xlabel": "time / s", "ylabel": "distance / m", "lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [40, 240], [100, 240], [140, 360]]}], "points": [], "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "cap": "Steep part: 240 m in 40 s = 6 m/s. Flat part, 40 s to 100 s: stopped. Whole trip: 360 m in 140 s."}}, "selfx": {"q": "In the last step, why is the total time 140 s and not 80 s?", "o": ["Only the 80 s spent moving counts, because a stopped cyclist has no speed", "The rest still takes time, so average speed uses the time for the whole trip", "The last time value on the graph is always used for any speed calculation", "Flat sections are at constant speed, so their time is added in"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Average speed is for the whole journey, and the stop is part of it. Leaving it out would give the speed while moving, not the average.", "Right. Average speed = total distance ÷ total time. The 60 s stop is part of the 140 s, so it makes the average lower.", "Wrong. You use changes between two points. The end value is only the total time when you want the whole-trip average.", "Wrong. A flat line means stationary, with speed 0 m/s, not constant speed. Its time still counts because the trip lasts that long."]}, "faded": {"task": "The graph shows a runner's straight sloping line through A (20 s, 100 m) and B (60 s, 500 m). Find the runner's speed.", "given": ["The line is straight, so the speed is constant and equals the gradient.", "Change in distance = 500 − 100 = 400 m."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Divide the end values: speed = 500 ÷ 60 = 8.3 m/s", "Change in time = 60 − 20 = 40 s, so speed = 400 × 40 = 16 000 m/s", "Change in time = 60 − 20 = 40 s, so speed = 400 ÷ 40 = 10 m/s", "Use the end time: speed = 400 ÷ 60 = 6.7 m/s"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. This ignores the change in distance you found and uses the end values. The line does not start at zero here, so this gives the wrong gradient.", "Wrong. The time change is right, but speed is distance divided by time, not multiplied. 16 000 m/s is far too fast for a runner.", "Right. The change in time is 40 s, and speed = change in distance ÷ change in time = 400 ÷ 40 = 10 m/s.", "Wrong. You used the end time of 60 s with a change in distance. Both values must be changes: 60 − 20 = 40 s."], "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 80], "y": [0, 600], "xstep": 10, "ystep": 100, "xlabel": "time / s", "ylabel": "distance / m", "lines": [{"pts": [[20, 100], [60, 500]], "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [20, 100], "label": "A"}, {"at": [60, 500], "label": "B"}], "xnums": true, "ynums": true}}, "trap": "Reading a flat line as constant speed. It means stationary. Also avoid dividing by the end values on the axes. Subtract two points first, then divide the change in distance by the change in time.", "recap": "On a distance-time graph, gradient = speed = change in distance ÷ change in time, and a flat line means stationary."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.5.6.1.5", "kind": "method", "why": "Acceleration calculations and velocity-time graphs come up in Paper 2 forces questions, often as a graph to read followed by a short calculation.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Calculating acceleration", "b": "**Acceleration** is how quickly velocity changes. $a = \\frac{\\Delta v}{t}$, where $\\Delta v = v - u$ (final minus starting velocity) and $t$ is the time in s. The unit is **m/s²**. A negative answer means the object is slowing down."}, {"h": "Reading velocity-time graphs", "b": "Press Next step on the graph. The **gradient** of a velocity-time graph is the acceleration (change in velocity ÷ time). A flat line means constant velocity. The **area under the line** is the distance travelled. Split the area into triangles, ½ × base × height, and rectangles.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "steps", "x": [0, 14], "xstep": 2, "y": [0, 10], "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "Time / s", "ylabel": "Velocity / m/s", "lines": [{"label": "gradient 2", "c": 0, "step": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [4, 8]]}, {"label": "gradient 0", "c": 2, "step": 2, "pts": [[4, 8], [10, 8]]}, {"label": "gradient −2", "c": 1, "step": 3, "pts": [[10, 8], [14, 0]]}, {"dash": true, "c": 3, "step": 4, "pts": [[4, 8], [4, 0]]}, {"dash": true, "c": 3, "step": 4, "pts": [[10, 8], [10, 0]]}], "points": [{"at": [4, 8], "label": "(4, 8)", "step": 1}, {"at": [10, 8], "label": "(10, 8)", "step": 2}, {"at": [14, 0], "label": "(14, 0)", "step": 3}, {"at": [2, 2], "label": "16 m", "step": 5}, {"at": [12, 2], "label": "16 m", "step": 5}, {"at": [8, 4], "label": "48 m", "step": 6}], "steps": ["Velocity rises from 0 to 8 m/s in 4 s. Gradient = 8 ÷ 4 = 2 m/s². That is the acceleration.", "The line is flat from 4 s to 10 s. Velocity stays at 8 m/s, so the gradient and acceleration are 0.", "Velocity falls from 8 m/s to 0 in 4 s. Gradient = −8 ÷ 4 = −2 m/s². The negative sign means slowing down.", "Dashed lines split the area under the graph into a triangle, a rectangle and a second triangle.", "Each triangle: ½ × base × height = ½ × 4 × 8 = 16 m.", "Rectangle: 6 × 8 = 48 m. Total distance = 16 + 48 + 16 = 80 m."]}}, {"h": "The equation without time", "b": "For **uniform acceleration**, $v^2 - u^2 = 2as$. Use it when you know the distance $s$ but not the time. Here $u$ is the starting speed and $v$ the final speed. It gives $v^2$, so finish by taking the **square root**."}], "example": {"task": "A cyclist starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 1.5 m/s² over a distance of 48 m, the area under the graph. Calculate her final speed.", "steps": [{"do": "Write the equation: $v^2 - u^2 = 2as$.", "why": "The question gives acceleration and distance, not time, and the acceleration is uniform."}, {"do": "List the values: $u = 0$ m/s, $a = 1.5$ m/s², $s = 48$ m.", "why": "'Starts from rest' means the starting speed is zero."}, {"do": "Substitute: $v^2 - 0^2 = 2 \\times 1.5 \\times 48$.", "why": "Every value goes in with its own letter, so nothing is swapped."}, {"do": "Calculate: $v^2 = 3 \\times 48 = 144$.", "why": "The right-hand side is worked out first. This gives $v^2$, not $v$."}, {"do": "Take the square root: $v = \\sqrt{144} = 12$ m/s.", "why": "The question asks for speed, so you must undo the squaring. Add the unit."}], "answer": "Her final speed is 12 m/s.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 14], "xstep": 2, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "Time in s", "ylabel": "Velocity in m/s", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [8, 12]], "label": "a = 1.5 m/s²", "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [8, 12], "label": "(8, 12)"}]}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked answer finish by taking a square root?", "o": ["To change the units from m/s² into m/s at the end", "To cancel the 2 left over from the 2as part of the equation", "The calculation gives v², but the question asks for v", "To make sure the speed is smaller than the 48 m distance"], "a": 2, "f": ["Units come from the quantities you put in, not from a square root. The root is needed because the working ends with v².", "The 2 was already multiplied out in 2 × 1.5 × 48. The root has nothing to do with it.", "Correct. 144 is v², not v. Taking the square root gives the speed, 12 m/s.", "Speed and distance are different quantities, so they cannot be compared like this. The root is needed to turn v² into v."]}, "faded": {"task": "A car is moving at 5.0 m/s. It accelerates uniformly at 3.0 m/s² over 24 m. Find its final speed.", "given": ["$v^2 - u^2 = 2as$", "$v^2 - 5.0^2 = 2 \\times 3.0 \\times 24$"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["$v^2 = 144 - 25 = 119$, because $u^2$ is taken away", "$v^2 = 144 + 25 = 169$, because $u^2$ moves to the right-hand side", "$v^2 = 144$, so $v = 12$ m/s, because the $5.0^2$ can be ignored", "$v - 5.0 = 12$, so $v = 17$ m/s, by square-rooting each term"], "a": 1, "f": ["$u^2$ is subtracted on the left, so you add it to both sides. Subtracting it gives the wrong $v^2$.", "Correct. Adding 25 to both sides gives $v^2 = 169$. Then $v = \\sqrt{169} = 13$ m/s.", "The car is already moving, so $u^2$ is not zero. Dropping it ignores the starting speed.", "You cannot square-root the terms separately. Work out $v^2$ first, then take one square root."]}, "trap": "Stopping at $v^2$ and giving it as the speed. Also, in $v^2 = u^2 + 2as$, add $u^2$ before you take the square root, and never root each term separately.", "recap": "Gradient of a velocity-time graph is acceleration, area is distance, and $v^2 - u^2 = 2as$ gives $v^2$, so take the square root."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.5.6.2", "kind": "method", "why": "Paper 2 calculations often ask you to find a resultant force before using F = ma, and third-law questions test whether you can pick out a true pair.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Resultant force", "b": "The **resultant force** is the single force that has the same effect as all the forces acting. For two forces in opposite directions along a line, subtract the smaller from the larger. If it is zero, speed and direction stay the same (**Newton's first law**). If it is not zero, the object accelerates."}, {"h": "Newton's second law", "b": "**$F = ma$**: resultant force (N) = mass (kg) × acceleration (m/s²). Always use the resultant force, not just one force. For the same force, a bigger mass gives a smaller acceleration. Mass is a measure of **inertia**, the resistance to a change in motion."}, {"h": "Newton's third law", "b": "When two objects interact, they exert **equal and opposite** forces on each other. A **third-law pair** is the same type of force and acts on **different objects**. The Earth pulls a book down (weight). The book pulls the Earth up. That is the pair, not the table's push."}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows a 900 kg car accelerating from rest to 20 m/s in 8 s. The resistive forces total 500 N. Calculate the driving force from the engine.", "steps": [{"do": "Find the acceleration: $a = \\dfrac{v-u}{t} = \\dfrac{20-0}{8} = 2.5$ m/s².", "why": "The change in speed divided by the time taken gives the acceleration."}, {"do": "Find the resultant force: $F = ma = 900 \\times 2.5 = 2250$ N.", "why": "F = ma gives the resultant force, not the driving force."}, {"do": "Link to the forces: resultant = driving − resistive. So driving = $2250 + 500$ = 2750 N.", "why": "The engine must also overcome the resistive force, which acts backwards."}, {"do": "Check: 2750 N is bigger than 2250 N, and it acts forwards.", "why": "Driving force must exceed the resultant when resistive forces act."}], "answer": "The driving force from the engine is 2750 N, acting forwards.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 8], "y": [0, 20], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 4, "xlabel": "Time in s", "ylabel": "Velocity in m/s", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [8, 20]], "c": 0}]}}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 3, why do you ADD 500 N to the resultant force of 2250 N?", "o": ["The engine must also cancel the car's weight, which acts downwards.", "Force is always bigger than mass multiplied by acceleration.", "The engine must also overcome the backwards resistive force of 500 N.", "The resultant force is the driving force plus the resistive force."], "a": 2, "f": ["Weight acts vertically and is balanced by the road. It does not affect the forwards calculation here, so it is not added.", "F = ma gives the resultant force exactly. It is not a minimum, so 'always bigger' is not a reason.", "Right. The resultant is driving minus resistive, so you add the resistive force back on to get the driving force.", "The resultant is the difference, not the sum, because the two forces act in opposite directions."]}, "faded": {"task": "The graph shows an 800 kg car slowing from 24 m/s to rest in 6 s. Calculate the braking force, treating it as the resultant force.", "given": ["Change in speed = 24 m/s down to 0, in 6 s.", "Size of acceleration $= 24 \\div 6 = 4$ m/s² (a deceleration)."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["$F = ma = 800 \\times 4 = 3200$ N", "$F = 800 \\div 4 = 200$ N", "$F = 800 \\times 24 = 19\\,200$ N", "$F = 800 \\times 6 = 4800$ N"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Multiply mass by the acceleration of 4 m/s² to get the resultant braking force of 3200 N.", "This divides mass by acceleration. F = ma needs multiplication, so this gives the wrong answer.", "This uses the starting speed in place of acceleration. You must already have used the speed to find a = 4 m/s².", "This uses the time in place of acceleration. The acceleration was already worked out as 4 m/s²."], "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 6], "y": [0, 24], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 4, "xlabel": "Time in s", "ylabel": "Velocity in m/s", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 24], [6, 0]], "c": 0}]}}, "trap": "Putting one force, often the driving force, into F = ma. Use the resultant force: subtract the opposing forces first, then use F = ma.", "recap": "Find the resultant force first, then use F = ma; a third-law pair acts on two different objects."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.5.6.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to link each factor to thinking or braking distance, and to explain why hard braking is dangerous.", "hook": {"q": "A driver's reaction time is 0.7 s. The car's speed doubles from 15 m/s to 30 m/s. What happens to the distance travelled during the reaction time?", "o": ["It stays the same, because the reaction time is the same", "It doubles, because the car covers twice as far in every second", "It goes up four times, because speed is squared", "It halves, because the driver has less time to react"], "a": 1, "f": ["The time is the same, but the car moves faster, so it covers more distance in that time.", "Right. Distance = speed × time. The time is unchanged and the speed doubles, so the distance doubles.", "The squared link is for braking distance (kinetic energy). Thinking distance is just speed × time, so it only doubles.", "Reaction time does not depend on speed. The driver has the same time, but the faster car travels further in it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Two distances", "b": "**Stopping distance** = **thinking distance** + **braking distance**. Thinking distance is how far the car travels while the driver reacts, before the brakes are applied. Braking distance is how far it travels after that, until it stops. Drag the slider to change speed (graph: reaction time 0.7 s, dry road, a model).", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [0, 30], "xstep": 5, "y": [0, 100], "ystep": 20, "xlabel": "speed in m/s", "ylabel": "distance in m", "lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [30.0, 21.0]], "label": "thinking"}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [0.1875, 0.0023], [0.375, 0.0094], [0.5625, 0.0211], [0.75, 0.0375], [0.9375, 0.0586], [1.125, 0.0844], [1.3125, 0.1148], [1.5, 0.15], [1.6875, 0.1898], [1.875, 0.2344], [2.0625, 0.2836], [2.25, 0.3375], [2.4375, 0.3961], [2.625, 0.4594], [2.8125, 0.5273], [3.0, 0.6], [3.1875, 0.6773], [3.375, 0.7594], [3.5625, 0.8461], [3.75, 0.9375], [3.9375, 1.0336], [4.125, 1.1344], [4.3125, 1.2398], [4.5, 1.35], [4.6875, 1.4648], [4.875, 1.5844], [5.0625, 1.7086], [5.25, 1.8375], [5.4375, 1.9711], [5.625, 2.1094], [5.8125, 2.2523], [6.0, 2.4], [6.1875, 2.5523], [6.375, 2.7094], [6.5625, 2.8711], [6.75, 3.0375], [6.9375, 3.2086], [7.125, 3.3844], [7.3125, 3.5648], [7.5, 3.75], [7.6875, 3.9398], [7.875, 4.1344], [8.0625, 4.3336], [8.25, 4.5375], [8.4375, 4.7461], [8.625, 4.9594], [8.8125, 5.1773], [9.0, 5.4], [9.1875, 5.6273], [9.375, 5.8594], [9.5625, 6.0961], [9.75, 6.3375], [9.9375, 6.5836], [10.125, 6.8344], [10.3125, 7.0898], [10.5, 7.35], [10.6875, 7.6148], [10.875, 7.8844], [11.0625, 8.1586], [11.25, 8.4375], [11.4375, 8.7211], [11.625, 9.0094], [11.8125, 9.3023], [12.0, 9.6], [12.1875, 9.9023], [12.375, 10.2094], [12.5625, 10.5211], [12.75, 10.8375], [12.9375, 11.1586], [13.125, 11.4844], [13.3125, 11.8148], [13.5, 12.15], [13.6875, 12.4898], [13.875, 12.8344], [14.0625, 13.1836], [14.25, 13.5375], [14.4375, 13.8961], [14.625, 14.2594], [14.8125, 14.6273], [15.0, 15.0], [15.1875, 15.3773], [15.375, 15.7594], [15.5625, 16.1461], [15.75, 16.5375], [15.9375, 16.9336], [16.125, 17.3344], [16.3125, 17.7398], [16.5, 18.15], [16.6875, 18.5648], [16.875, 18.9844], [17.0625, 19.4086], [17.25, 19.8375], [17.4375, 20.2711], [17.625, 20.7094], [17.8125, 21.1523], [18.0, 21.6], [18.1875, 22.0523], [18.375, 22.5094], [18.5625, 22.9711], [18.75, 23.4375], [18.9375, 23.9086], [19.125, 24.3844], [19.3125, 24.8648], [19.5, 25.35], [19.6875, 25.8398], [19.875, 26.3344], [20.0625, 26.8336], [20.25, 27.3375], [20.4375, 27.8461], [20.625, 28.3594], [20.8125, 28.8773], [21.0, 29.4], [21.1875, 29.9273], [21.375, 30.4594], [21.5625, 30.9961], [21.75, 31.5375], [21.9375, 32.0836], [22.125, 32.6344], [22.3125, 33.1898], [22.5, 33.75], [22.6875, 34.3148], [22.875, 34.8844], [23.0625, 35.4586], [23.25, 36.0375], [23.4375, 36.6211], [23.625, 37.2094], [23.8125, 37.8023], [24.0, 38.4], [24.1875, 39.0023], [24.375, 39.6094], [24.5625, 40.2211],