.6875, 87.979687], [25.875, 89.26875], [26.0625, 90.567188], [26.25, 91.875], [26.4375, 93.192188], [26.625, 94.51875], [26.8125, 95.854687], [27.0, 97.2], [27.1875, 98.554688], [27.375, 99.91875], [27.5625, 101.292187], [27.75, 102.675], [27.9375, 104.067188], [28.125, 105.46875], [28.3125, 106.879688], [28.5, 108.3], [28.6875, 109.729687], [28.875, 111.16875], [29.0625, 112.617188], [29.25, 114.075], [29.4375, 115.542187], [29.625, 117.01875], [29.8125, 118.504688], [30.0, 120.0]], "c": 1, "label": "wet road"}]}}, {"h": "Why big decelerations are dangerous", "b": "Force and acceleration are linked by F = ma. A very large **deceleration** needs a very large force. Friction in the brakes transfers kinetic energy to thermal energy, so the brakes can **overheat**. The tyres may lose grip and the car may **skid**. People in the car can be injured."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a tired driver on a wet road has a longer stopping distance than an alert driver on a dry road, at the same speed.", "steps": [{"do": "State that stopping distance = thinking distance + braking distance, and say that both parts will change here.", "why": "This lets you match each cause to the correct distance."}, {"do": "Tiredness increases the driver's reaction time.", "why": "Reaction time is the delay between seeing the hazard and pressing the brake."}, {"do": "So the car travels further before the brakes are applied, and the thinking distance increases.", "why": "At a steady speed, a longer time means a greater distance."}, {"do": "The wet road reduces the friction between the tyres and the road, so the maximum braking force is smaller.", "why": "Water between tyre and road reduces grip."}, {"do": "A smaller force needs a longer distance to transfer the same kinetic energy, so the braking distance increases.", "why": "Work done = force × distance, so less force means more distance."}], "answer": "Tiredness increases reaction time, so the car travels further before braking starts: thinking distance increases. The wet road reduces friction, so the braking force is smaller. A smaller force needs a longer distance to transfer the same kinetic energy, so braking distance increases. Both parts rise, so stopping distance is longer."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say tiredness changes the thinking distance and not the braking distance?", "o": ["Tired drivers brake more gently, so the car takes longer to slow down", "Tiredness reduces the friction between the tyres and the road", "Thinking distance is the part of stopping distance that depends on the road", "Tiredness lengthens reaction time, which acts before braking starts"], "a": 3, "f": ["Gentle braking is not what tiredness does. It delays the start of braking, which is thinking distance.", "Friction depends on the road and tyres, not on the driver. Mixing this up with wet roads is a common slip.", "The road affects braking distance. Thinking distance depends on the driver's reaction time and the speed.", "Right. Reaction time is the delay before the brakes are applied. That delay is covered by the thinking distance only."]}, "faded": {"task": "A heavily loaded lorry and an empty lorry travel at the same speed on a dry road. The brakes apply the same force. Explain why the loaded lorry has a longer braking distance.", "given": ["Stopping distance = thinking distance + braking distance.", "The load does not change the driver's reaction time, so thinking distance is unchanged. Only braking distance differs."], "q": "Which point should come next?", "o": ["The heavier load lengthens the driver's reaction time, so thinking distance is greater", "The heavier lorry has less kinetic energy, so the brakes take longer to stop it", "At the same speed it has more kinetic energy, so the brakes must do more work to stop it", "Heavier vehicles have less friction with the road, so the braking force is smaller"], "a": 2, "f": ["The given steps already say reaction time is unchanged. A load affects braking distance, not thinking distance.", "Kinetic energy is ½mv². A larger mass at the same speed means more kinetic energy, not less.", "Right. Kinetic energy is ½mv², so a larger mass means more energy to remove. With the same force, work done = force × distance gives a longer distance.", "The question says the brakes apply the same force. The point is about the greater energy to be transferred, not a smaller force."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the two distances. Tiredness, alcohol, drugs and distractions change thinking distance only. Wet roads, worn tyres and worn brakes change braking distance only. Ask: does it act before or after the brakes are applied?", "recap": "Thinking distance depends on speed and reaction time, braking distance on speed, mass, grip and brakes, and large decelerations need dangerously large forces."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.5.7", "kind": "method", "why": "Momentum is Higher tier only and comes up in Paper 2, usually as a calculation on a collision, an explosion or the force in an impact.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Momentum and direction", "b": "**Momentum** is mass times velocity: $p = mv$. Mass is in kg and velocity in m/s, so momentum is in **kg m/s**. Velocity has direction, so momentum does too. Pick one direction as **positive**. Anything moving the opposite way gets a negative sign."}, {"h": "Conservation of momentum", "b": "In a **closed system** no external forces act. Then the **total momentum** before a collision equals the total after. If the second object is at rest and they stick together: $m_1u_1 = (m_1+m_2)v$. In an explosion the total starts at zero, so the parts move off in opposite directions."}, {"h": "Force and momentum", "b": "A force changes momentum. **Force** equals the **rate of change of momentum**: $F = \\dfrac{m\\Delta v}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{mv - mu}{t}$. Force is in N and time in s. The same change in momentum over a longer time needs a smaller force. This is how crumple zones and airbags work."}], "example": {"task": "A 2.0 kg trolley moving right at 3.0 m/s hits a 1.0 kg trolley moving right at 1.0 m/s. Afterwards the 2.0 kg trolley moves right at 2.0 m/s. Find the speed of the 1.0 kg trolley.", "steps": [{"do": "Take right as positive. Both trolleys move right, so all velocities are positive.", "why": "Momentum has direction, so you must fix a positive direction before you start."}, {"do": "Momentum before = 2.0 × 3.0 + 1.0 × 1.0 = 6.0 + 1.0 = 7.0 kg m/s.", "why": "Total momentum is the sum of the momenta of both trolleys."}, {"do": "Momentum after = 2.0 × 2.0 + 1.0 × v = 4.0 + v.", "why": "The trolleys do not stick, so each keeps its own mass and its own velocity."}, {"do": "Total momentum before = total momentum after, so 7.0 = 4.0 + v.", "why": "Momentum is conserved in a closed system."}, {"do": "v = 7.0 − 4.0 = 3.0 m/s, to the right.", "why": "Rearrange for v. The answer is positive, so it moves in the positive direction."}], "answer": "The 1.0 kg trolley moves at 3.0 m/s to the right."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why can you write 'momentum before = momentum after' in step 4?", "o": ["Because the total kinetic energy is the same before and after the collision.", "Because each trolley keeps the same momentum it had before the collision.", "Because no external force acts, so total momentum is conserved in the collision.", "Because the trolleys have different masses, so their momenta must be equal."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Kinetic energy is not conserved here, since some is transferred to other stores. Momentum is conserved for a different reason.", "Wrong. The trolleys exchange momentum. The 2.0 kg one loses some and the 1.0 kg one gains it. Only the total stays the same.", "Right. The system is closed, so there is no external force. The total momentum is therefore the same before and after.", "Wrong. The two momenta are not equal, and they do not need to be. Only the total before and after must match."]}, "faded": {"task": "A 1.5 kg trolley moving at 2.0 m/s hits a stationary 0.50 kg trolley. Afterwards the 1.5 kg trolley moves on at 1.0 m/s in the same direction. Find the speed of the 0.50 kg trolley.", "given": ["Momentum before = 1.5 × 2.0 + 0.50 × 0 = 3.0 kg m/s", "Momentum after = 1.5 × 1.0 + 0.50 × v = 1.5 + 0.50v"], "q": "What is the correct next step?", "o": ["Set 3.0 = 1.5 + 0.50v, so 0.50v = 1.5 and v = 3.0 m/s", "Set 3.0 = 1.5 + 0.50v, so 0.50v = 4.5 and v = 9.0 m/s", "Set 3.0 = (1.5 + 0.50) × v, so v = 1.5 m/s", "Subtract 3.0 − 1.5 = 1.5, so v = 1.5 m/s"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Before equals after, so 3.0 = 1.5 + 0.50v. Subtract 1.5 to get 0.50v = 1.5, then divide by 0.50 to get 3.0 m/s.", "Wrong. You added 1.5 to 3.0 instead of subtracting it from both sides. Moving 1.5 across gives 3.0 − 1.5 = 1.5.", "Wrong. This uses the combined mass, which only works if the trolleys stick together. Here the first trolley keeps moving by itself.", "Wrong. 1.5 is the momentum of the second trolley, not its velocity. You must divide by its mass, 0.50 kg."]}, "trap": "Using only one object's mass after they stick together. Use the combined mass. If objects move in opposite directions, give one a negative velocity before you add momenta.", "recap": "In a closed system, total momentum before equals total momentum after; force equals change in momentum divided by time."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.6.1", "kind": "method", "why": "Wave speed calculations appear in Paper 2, often with the period given instead of the frequency, so you need to know which quantity to find first.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Two types of wave", "b": "In a **transverse wave**, the oscillations are at right angles to the direction the wave travels. Water ripples and light are examples. In a **longitudinal wave**, the oscillations are parallel to the direction of travel. They form compressions and rarefactions. Sound is an example. Waves transfer energy, not matter."}, {"h": "Amplitude and wavelength", "b": "**Amplitude** is the maximum displacement of a point on the wave from its rest position. **Wavelength**, $\\lambda$, is the distance from a point on one wave to the same point on the next, such as crest to crest. Both are lengths, measured in metres."}, {"h": "Frequency and period", "b": "**Frequency** is the number of waves passing a point each second, in hertz (Hz). **Period** is the time for one wave, in seconds. They are linked by $f = \\frac{1}{T}$ and $T = \\frac{1}{f}$. A period of 0.50 s means $f = 2$ Hz."}, {"h": "Wave speed equation", "b": "**Wave speed** is how far a wave travels each second. Use $v = f\\lambda$, with $v$ in m/s, $f$ in Hz and $\\lambda$ in m. If you are given the period, find the **frequency** first. Then multiply by the wavelength."}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows displacement against time for a point on a rope as a wave passes. The wavelength is 1.5 m. Watch it draw, then use crest P to crest Q to find the period, then calculate the wave speed.", "steps": [{"do": "Write down what you know: $T = 0.20$ s and $\\lambda = 1.5$ m.", "why": "The equation $v = f\\lambda$ needs frequency, not period, so you must spot what is missing."}, {"do": "Find the frequency: $f = \\frac{1}{T} = \\frac{1}{0.20} = 5.0$ Hz.", "why": "This turns the period (seconds per wave) into waves per second."}, {"do": "Write the equation: $v = f\\lambda$.", "why": "It links wave speed, frequency and wavelength."}, {"do": "Substitute the values: $v = 5.0 \\times 1.5$.", "why": "Hz times metres gives metres per second."}, {"do": "Calculate: $v = 7.5$ m/s.", "why": "State the answer with its unit."}], "answer": "The wave speed is 7.5 m/s.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "draw", "pen": true, "read": ["t (s)", "d (cm)"], "dp": 2, "ms": 5000, "x": [0, 0.4], "xstep": 0.05, "y": [-2, 2], "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "time / s", "ylabel": "displacement / cm", "lines": [{"c": 0, "step": 1, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [0.0025, 0.1569], [0.005, 0.3129], [0.0075, 0.4669], [0.01, 0.618], [0.0125, 0.7654], [0.015, 0.908], [0.0175, 1.045], [0.02, 1.1756], [0.0225, 1.2989], [0.025, 1.4142], [0.0275, 1.5208], [0.03, 1.618], [0.0325, 1.7053], [0.035, 1.782], [0.0375, 1.8478], [0.04, 1.9021], [0.0425, 1.9447], [0.045, 1.9754], [0.0475, 1.9938], [0.05, 2.0], [0.0525, 1.9938], [0.055, 1.9754], [0.0575, 1.9447], [0.06, 1.9021], [0.0625, 1.8478], [0.065, 1.782], [0.0675, 1.7053], [0.07, 1.618], [0.0725, 1.5208], [0.075, 1.4142], [0.0775, 1.2989], [0.08, 1.1756], [0.0825, 1.045], [0.085, 0.908], [0.0875, 0.7654], [0.09, 0.618], [0.0925, 0.4669], [0.095, 0.3129], [0.0975, 0.1569], [0.1, 0.0], [0.1025, -0.1569], [0.105, -0.3129], [0.1075, -0.4669], [0.11, -0.618], [0.1125, -0.7654], [0.115, -0.908], [0.1175, -1.045], [0.12, -1.1756], [0.1225, -1.2989], [0.125, -1.4142], [0.1275, -1.5208], [0.13, -1.618], [0.1325, -1.7053], [0.135, -1.782], [0.1375, -1.8478], [0.14, -1.9021], [0.1425, -1.9447], [0.145, -1.9754], [0.1475, -1.9938], [0.15, -2.0], [0.1525, -1.9938], [0.155, -1.9754], [0.1575, -1.9447], [0.16, -1.9021], [0.1625, -1.8478], [0.165, -1.782], [0.1675, -1.7053], [0.17, -1.618], [0.1725, -1.5208], [0.175, -1.4142], [0.1775, -1.2989], [0.18, -1.1756], [0.1825, -1.045], [0.185, -0.908], [0.1875, -0.7654], [0.19, -0.618], [0.1925, -0.4669], [0.195, -0.3129], [0.1975, -0.1569], [0.2, -0.0], [0.2025, 0.1569], [0.205, 0.3129], [0.2075, 0.4669], [0.21, 0.618], [0.2125, 0.7654], [0.215, 0.908], [0.2175, 1.045], [0.22, 1.1756], [0.2225, 1.2989], [0.225, 1.4142], [0.2275, 1.5208], [0.23, 1.618], [0.2325, 1.7053], [0.235, 1.782], [0.2375, 1.8478], [0.24, 1.9021], [0.2425, 1.9447], [0.245, 1.9754], [0.2475, 1.9938], [0.25, 2.0], [0.2525, 1.9938], [0.255, 1.9754], [0.2575, 1.9447], [0.26, 1.9021], [0.2625, 1.8478], [0.265, 1.782], [0.2675, 1.7053], [0.27, 1.618], [0.2725, 1.5208], [0.275, 1.4142], [0.2775, 1.2989], [0.28, 1.1756], [0.2825, 1.045], [0.285, 0.908], [0.2875, 0.7654], [0.29, 0.618], [0.2925, 0.4669], [0.295, 0.3129], [0.2975, 0.1569], [0.3, 0.0], [0.3025, -0.1569], [0.305, -0.3129], [0.3075, -0.4669], [0.31, -0.618], [0.3125, -0.7654], [0.315, -0.908], [0.3175, -1.045], [0.32, -1.1756], [0.3225, -1.2989], [0.325, -1.4142], [0.3275, -1.5208], [0.33, -1.618], [0.3325, -1.7053], [0.335, -1.782], [0.3375, -1.8478], [0.34, -1.9021], [0.3425, -1.9447], [0.345, -1.9754], [0.3475, -1.9938], [0.35, -2.0], [0.3525, -1.9938], [0.355, -1.9754], [0.3575, -1.9447], [0.36, -1.9021], [0.3625, -1.8478], [0.365, -1.782], [0.3675, -1.7053], [0.37, -1.618], [0.3725, -1.5208], [0.375, -1.4142], [0.3775, -1.2989], [0.38, -1.1756], [0.3825, -1.045], [0.385, -0.908], [0.3875, -0.7654], [0.39, -0.618], [0.3925, -0.4669], [0.395, -0.3129], [0.3975, -0.1569], [0.4, -0.0]]}, {"dash": true, "c": 1, "label": "P to Q = 1 period", "step": 2, "pts": [[0.05, 2], [0.25, 2]]}], "points": [{"at": [0.05, 2], "label": "P"}, {"at": [0.25, 2], "label": "Q"}], "cap": "One point on the rope moves up and down. Crest P to crest Q is one full wave."}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked example calculate 1 ÷ 0.20 before using $v = f\\lambda$?", "o": ["Because the period is the same as the wavelength, just measured in seconds", "Because the equation $v = f\\lambda$ only works for transverse waves", "Because 1 ÷ 0.20 gives the amplitude, which the equation also needs", "Because the equation needs frequency in hertz, and the period is a time in seconds"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Period is a time and wavelength is a length. They are different quantities and cannot be swapped.", "Wrong. The equation works for all waves, transverse and longitudinal. The step is needed because of what T measures.", "Wrong. Amplitude is a distance and is not in this equation. Taking 1 ÷ T gives the frequency.", "Right. $f = \\frac{1}{T}$ changes seconds per wave into waves per second, which is the frequency the equation needs."]}, "faded": {"task": "A sound wave in air has a period of 0.0020 s and a wavelength of 0.68 m. Calculate its speed.", "given": ["Known: $T = 0.0020$ s and $\\lambda = 0.68$ m.", "$v = f\\lambda$ needs $f$, so find $f$ from $T$ first."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Multiply: $v = 0.0020 \\times 0.68 = 0.00136$ m/s", "Calculate $f = \\frac{1}{0.0020} = 500$ Hz", "Divide: $f = 0.0020 \\div 1 = 0.0020$ Hz", "Calculate $f = \\frac{1}{0.68} = 1.5$ Hz"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. This multiplies the period by the wavelength. Period is not frequency, so convert it using $f = \\frac{1}{T}$ first.", "Right. $f = \\frac{1}{T} = 500$ Hz. Then $v = 500 \\times 0.68 = 340$ m/s.", "Wrong. This divides the wrong way round. Frequency is 1 divided by the period, not the period divided by 1.", "Wrong. This uses the wavelength instead of the period. Frequency comes from the period, using $f = \\frac{1}{T}$."]}, "trap": "Multiplying the period by the wavelength. Period is not frequency. Turn it into frequency with $f = \\frac{1}{T}$ first, then use $v = f\\lambda$.", "recap": "Find the frequency first, using $f = \\frac{1}{T}$ if you are given the period, then use $v = f\\lambda$."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.6.1.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 can ask you to draw ray diagrams and explain refraction. Marks go for measuring angles from the normal and linking bending to a change in speed.", "hook": {"q": "A trolley rolls on smooth floor towards a strip of thick carpet, at an angle. The wheel that reaches the carpet first slows down. What happens to the trolley's path?", "o": ["It carries straight on without changing direction", "It turns towards the line at right angles to the edge", "It turns away from the line at right angles to the edge", "It turns back and bounces off the edge of the carpet"], "a": 1, "f": ["Straight on only happens if both wheels reach the carpet together, head-on. At an angle, one wheel slows before the other.", "The slowed wheel lags behind, so the other wheel swings round. The path turns towards the line at right angles to the edge. Light does the same at a boundary.", "This would need the first wheel to speed up. A wheel that slows lags behind, so the path turns the other way.", "Bouncing back is reflection. Here the trolley keeps going forwards. It only slows, so its direction changes slightly."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three things at a boundary", "b": "When a wave reaches a boundary between two materials, it can be **reflected**, **absorbed** or **transmitted**. Often it is a mixture. Absorbed means the wave's energy is transferred to the material's energy stores. Transmitted means the wave carries on through the material. What happens depends on the wavelength and the materials."}, {"h": "Law of reflection", "b": "The **normal** is a dashed line at 90° to the surface, drawn where the ray hits. The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, both measured from the normal. A smooth surface gives **specular reflection**: parallel rays stay parallel. A rough surface gives **diffuse reflection**: the normal points different ways, so rays scatter."}, {"h": "Why refraction happens", "b": "**Refraction** is a change of direction when a wave crosses a boundary at an angle. It happens because the wave's **speed** changes. The part of the wavefront that arrives first changes speed first, so the wave turns. The frequency stays the same, so the **wavelength** changes with the speed."}, {"h": "Which way does it bend?", "b": "Light is slower in glass or water than in air. Going in, it slows down and bends **towards the normal**. Going out, it speeds up and bends **away from the normal**. A ray travelling along the normal (0°) does not change direction, though it still changes speed."}], "example": {"task": "A ray of light in air hits a rectangular glass block at an angle. Describe and explain what happens to the ray as it enters the block and as it leaves the block.", "steps": [{"do": "At the first surface the ray is refracted. Light travels more slowly in glass than in air, so the light slows down as it enters.", "why": "Refraction is caused by a change in wave speed, so the answer must name the speed change."}, {"do": "The ray bends towards the normal. The angle of refraction in the glass is smaller than the angle of incidence in the air.", "why": "Slowing down means bending towards the normal. Both angles are measured from the normal."}, {"do": "The frequency of the light stays the same, but its wavelength gets shorter inside the glass.", "why": "The source sets the frequency. Since v = fλ, a lower speed with the same f means a smaller λ."}, {"do": "At the second surface the light speeds up as it leaves the glass. It bends away from the normal, so the angle in air is larger than the angle in the glass.", "why": "Speeding up means bending away from the normal. This is the opposite of entering."}, {"do": "The emerging ray is parallel to the original ray, but shifted sideways.", "why": "The two surfaces are parallel, so the second bend exactly undoes the first. The ray ends up with its original direction."}], "answer": "The ray is refracted at both surfaces. In glass the light slows, so it bends towards the normal. Its wavelength shortens but its frequency is unchanged. On leaving it speeds up and bends away from the normal. It emerges parallel to its original path, shifted sideways."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the wavelength gets shorter in the glass?", "o": ["The frequency increases in glass, which squashes the waves together", "The wave loses energy in the glass, so each wave becomes shorter", "Part of the wave is reflected, leaving a shorter wave to be transmitted", "The frequency is fixed by the source, so a lower speed means a smaller wavelength"], "a": 3, "f": ["Frequency does not change at a boundary. The source sets it. Saying it increases is a common misconception.", "Losing energy would affect amplitude, not wavelength. Wavelength changes because the speed changes while the frequency stays fixed.", "Reflection takes some energy away from the transmitted wave. It does not change the wavelength of what is transmitted.", "Correct. v = fλ with f fixed means λ is proportional to v. Light slows in glass, so its wavelength decreases."]}, "faded": {"task": "A ray of light inside a glass block reaches the glass–air boundary at 25° to the normal. It leaves the glass. Explain what happens to the ray.", "given": ["The ray is refracted at the boundary.", "Light travels faster in air than in glass, so it speeds up as it leaves."], "q": "What is the next point in the explanation?", "o": ["It bends towards the normal, so the angle in the air is less than 25°.", "It does not bend, because the light is leaving the glass and not entering it.", "It bends away from the normal, so the angle in the air is greater than 25°.", "It bends away from the normal, so the angle in the air is less than 25°."], "a": 2, "f": ["This is the rule for entering glass. Speeding up on the way out means bending away from the normal, not towards it.", "Light bends at any boundary it meets at an angle. Only a ray along the normal goes straight on.", "Correct. Speeding up means bending away from the normal. The ray moves further from the normal, so the angle in air is greater than 25°.", "The direction is right but the angle is not. Bending away from the normal makes the angle bigger, not smaller."]}, "trap": "Measuring angles from the glass surface instead of the normal, or bending the ray the wrong way. Draw the normal first, then ask: does the light slow down (towards the normal) or speed up (away)?", "recap": "Refraction happens because wave speed changes at a boundary: slower means bending towards the normal, faster means away, and frequency never changes."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.6.1.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to explain how the ear detects sound, and how ultrasound and seismic waves reveal what is hidden inside objects or the Earth.", "hook": {"q": "A drum is hit across a field and you hear it a moment later. What travels from the drum to your ear?", "o": ["Air particles that travel from the drum all the way to your ear.", "Energy that needs no material, like light crossing empty space.", "A pattern of squashed and stretched air that passes energy along.", "Sound particles made by the drum, which then fly into your ear."], "a": 2, "f": ["Air particles only vibrate to and fro about a fixed position. They pass energy on but do not travel from the drum to you.", "Sound is a mechanical wave and needs particles to pass on the vibration. It cannot travel through a vacuum, unlike light.", "Right. Compressions and rarefactions travel through the air, passing energy from particle to particle. The air itself stays roughly where it was.", "There are no special 'sound particles'. Sound is a vibration of the air particles that are already there."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Sound and the eardrum", "b": "Sound is a **longitudinal** wave. A vibrating source makes **compressions** (high pressure) and **rarefactions** (low pressure) in the air. When these reach the eardrum, the changing pressure pushes and pulls it. It vibrates at the same frequency as the sound. The ear then converts these vibrations into electrical signals for the brain."}, {"h": "The range of hearing", "b": "Humans can hear frequencies from about **20 Hz to 20 000 Hz** (20 kHz). The ear can only convert vibrations into electrical signals over this **limited range**. Sound above 20 000 Hz is called **ultrasound**. Younger people usually hear higher frequencies than older adults, whose range shrinks with age, but the exam limit is 20 Hz to 20 kHz."}, {"h": "Using ultrasound", "b": "At a **boundary** between two different materials, some ultrasound is **reflected**. A detector times the **echo**. This is used to scan a fetus, to find cracks in metal and in sonar. Depth is $d = \\frac{vt}{2}$, with a half because the pulse goes there and back."}, {"h": "Seismic waves", "b": "An earthquake sends out seismic waves. **P-waves** are longitudinal and pass through solids and liquids. **S-waves** are transverse and pass through solids only. S-waves are not detected on the far side of the Earth. This shows the **outer core is liquid**. P-waves change direction suddenly at the core boundary."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a sound wave in air makes the eardrum vibrate, and why a sound of frequency 30 kHz cannot be heard by a human.", "steps": [{"do": "The source vibrates and makes compressions and rarefactions in the air. These travel outwards as a longitudinal wave.", "why": "This is how any sound wave in air begins."}, {"do": "Air particles vibrate to and fro and pass energy on. They do not travel from the source to the ear.", "why": "A wave transfers energy, not matter."}, {"do": "A compression pushes the eardrum inwards because the pressure outside is higher. A rarefaction lets it move outwards because the pressure is lower.", "why": "A pressure difference gives a force on the eardrum."}, {"do": "The eardrum vibrates at the same frequency as the sound. The vibrations pass through the ear and are converted into electrical signals.", "why": "This links the wave to what the brain receives."}, {"do": "30 kHz is above 20 kHz, the top of the human range. The ear cannot convert vibrations at this frequency into signals, so it is not heard.", "why": "This uses the limited hearing range as the reason."}], "answer": "The source makes compressions and rarefactions in the air. Particles vibrate and pass energy on. Compressions push the eardrum in and rarefactions let it move out, so it vibrates at the sound's frequency. The vibrations become electrical signals. 30 kHz is above 20 kHz, so the ear cannot convert it."}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 4, why does the eardrum vibrate at the same frequency as the sound?", "o": ["The middle ear amplifies the wave, which raises the frequency that arrives.", "Each wave cycle pushes it in once and lets it move out once.", "Air particles travel from the source and strike it once for each wave.", "The eardrum has a fixed natural frequency of about 20 000 Hz."], "a": 1, "f": ["Amplifying changes the size of a vibration, not its frequency. The frequency stays the same as the source's.", "Right. One compression and one rarefaction arrive per cycle, so the eardrum moves in and out once per cycle. Its frequency matches the wave's.", "Air particles do not travel from the source to the ear. They only vibrate about fixed positions, passing the wave on.", "The eardrum does not vibrate at one fixed frequency. It follows the frequency of whatever sound reaches it."], "a_note": ""}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how ultrasound can be used to find a crack inside a solid metal block.", "given": ["A transmitter on one face sends a pulse of ultrasound into the block.", "The pulse travels through the metal until it meets a boundary between two different materials."], "q": "Which sentence should come next in the answer?", "o": ["The crack absorbs the pulse, so metal particles carry it along to the detector.", "All the pulse passes through the crack, so the crack gives a longer echo time.", "The crack lowers the frequency below 20 kHz, so the operator can hear it.", "Some of the pulse reflects at the crack and returns before the far-face echo."], "a": 3, "f": ["Particles do not travel along the block. A crack is a boundary where some ultrasound is reflected, not a place that carries particles to the detector.", "A crack is a boundary, so some ultrasound is reflected there. Reflection gives an earlier echo, not a longer one.", "Reflection does not change the frequency. The ultrasound stays above 20 kHz and cannot be heard.", "Right. A crack is a boundary, so part of the pulse is reflected. The shorter echo time shows the crack is nearer than the far face."]}, "trap": "Using the full echo time in the depth calculation. The echo time covers the trip down and back, so the answer is double the depth. Halve the time, or halve the distance.", "recap": "Sound is longitudinal and makes the eardrum vibrate; you hear 20 Hz to 20 kHz; ultrasound echoes and seismic P- and S-waves reveal hidden structures."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.6.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Short exam questions ask you to put electromagnetic waves in order or state what they share, and later topics on their uses and dangers depend on knowing this order.", "hook": {"q": "A radio signal and a flash of light are sent from the Moon at the same instant. Which reaches Earth first?", "o": ["The light, because it has a shorter wavelength", "The radio signal, because it has a longer wavelength", "They arrive at the same time", "The light, because we can see it"], "a": 2, "f": ["Shorter wavelength does not mean faster. Wavelength changes along the spectrum, but speed in a vacuum does not.", "Longer wavelength does not mean faster either. Speed in a vacuum is the same for every electromagnetic wave.", "Light and radio are both electromagnetic waves. They travel at the same speed in a vacuum, so they arrive together.", "Being visible has no effect on speed. Whether our eyes detect a wave says nothing about how fast it travels."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The order of the waves", "b": "The **electromagnetic spectrum** is a continuous family of waves. In order of increasing wavelength: gamma, X-ray, ultraviolet, visible, infrared, microwave, radio. The usual list runs the other way, radio to gamma. Along that list, wavelength decreases and **frequency** increases."}, {"h": "What all of them share", "b": "All electromagnetic waves are **transverse**. All transfer energy from a source to an absorber. All travel at the same speed in a **vacuum**, about $3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s. Their speed in air is almost the same."}, {"h": "What changes along it", "b": "The speed stays the same, so $v = f \\lambda$ means a higher **frequency** gives a shorter **wavelength**. Materials also absorb, transmit, reflect or refract the waves differently. Your eyes detect only **visible light**."}], "example": {"task": "Radio waves and gamma rays are both electromagnetic waves. Describe what they have in common and how they differ in wavelength and frequency. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Both are transverse waves. The oscillations are at right angles to the direction the wave transfers energy.", "why": "Every electromagnetic wave is transverse. This is a shared property worth a mark."}, {"do": "Both travel at the same speed in a vacuum, about $3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.", "why": "Same speed in a vacuum is the key shared property, so state it clearly."}, {"do": "Radio waves are at the long-wavelength end of the spectrum. Gamma rays are at the short-wavelength end.", "why": "This places each wave on the spectrum and gives the wavelength difference."}, {"do": "The speed is the same, so gamma rays, with the shorter wavelength, have the higher frequency.", "why": "This links wavelength to frequency through $v = f \\lambda$ and gives the frequency difference."}], "answer": "Both are transverse and travel at the same speed in a vacuum, about 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s. Radio waves have the longest wavelength and gamma rays the shortest. As the speed is the same, gamma rays have the higher frequency."}, "selfx": {"q": "In the last step, why does the answer say gamma rays have the higher frequency?", "o": ["Shorter waves carry less energy, so they vibrate faster", "The speed is the same, so a shorter wavelength means a higher frequency", "Gamma rays travel faster than radio waves, so they vibrate more often", "Frequency and wavelength are always equal in size"], "a": 1, "f": ["Shorter wavelengths actually carry more energy, not less. Also, the frequency link comes from the equal speed, not from energy carried.", "Correct. With speed fixed, $v = f \\lambda$ shows wavelength and frequency are inversely proportional. Shorter wavelength means higher frequency.", "Gamma rays do not travel faster. All electromagnetic waves have the same speed in a vacuum. Higher frequency does not mean higher speed.", "They are not equal. They have different units and values. They are linked by $v = f \\lambda$, so one rises as the other falls."]}, "faded": {"task": "Compare visible light and infrared radiation. Answer in linked points.", "given": ["Both are transverse electromagnetic waves that transfer energy from a source to an absorber.", "Both travel at the same speed in a vacuum."], "q": "Which point should come next?", "o": ["Infrared has a longer wavelength than visible light, so it has a higher frequency.", "Infrared has a shorter wavelength than visible light, so it has a higher frequency.", "Infrared has a longer wavelength than visible light, so it travels more slowly.", "Infrared has a longer wavelength than visible light, so it has a lower frequency."], "a": 3, "f": ["The wavelength is right, but the frequency link is wrong. With the same speed, a longer wavelength means a lower frequency.", "This reverses the order. Infrared lies beyond red light, so its wavelength is longer than that of visible light.", "Speed does not depend on wavelength. The previous point already says both travel at the same speed in a vacuum.", "Correct. Infrared is on the longer-wavelength side of visible light. The speed is equal, so its frequency is lower."]}, "trap": "Reading the list radio, microwave … gamma as increasing wavelength. That order is increasing frequency and decreasing wavelength. Gamma rays have the shortest wavelength, and radio waves the longest.", "recap": "Radio to gamma is falling wavelength and rising frequency, but every electromagnetic wave is transverse and travels at the same speed in a vacuum."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.6.2.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 often asks you to explain how radio waves are made and detected, and why different electromagnetic waves behave differently in the same substance.", "hook": {"q": "A current is a flow of charge. A radio wave reaches a metal aerial. What do you think it causes in the metal?", "o": ["A steady current that flows one way only", "Sound waves that travel along the metal", "A current that keeps changing direction", "A permanent magnet in the metal"], "a": 2, "f": ["A radio wave pushes electrons back and forth, so the current does not stay in one direction.", "Sound is made later by a loudspeaker. The aerial produces an electrical signal, not sound.", "Yes. The wave makes the electrons oscillate, which gives an alternating current. The next screens explain why.", "Radio waves do not magnetise the metal. The effect is on the electrons moving inside it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Four things can happen", "b": "When an electromagnetic wave meets a substance, it can be absorbed, **transmitted**, reflected or refracted. Which happens depends on the substance and on the **wavelength**. Glass transmits visible light. Brick absorbs it. Radio waves pass through brick well enough for a radio indoors to work."}, {"h": "What each one means", "b": "**Absorbed**: the wave's energy is transferred to the substance, which often warms. **Reflected**: the wave bounces off the surface. **Refracted**: the wave changes speed when it enters a new substance, so it can change direction. Its frequency stays the same."}, {"h": "Making radio waves", "b": "Radio waves are produced by **oscillations** of charge in an electrical circuit. A transmitter makes an **alternating current** in its aerial, so electrons move back and forth. The aerial gives out radio waves with the same frequency as the alternating current."}, {"h": "Receiving radio waves", "b": "When a radio wave is **absorbed** by a conductor such as an aerial, its energy is transferred to electrons in it. They oscillate. This makes an **alternating current** with the same frequency as the wave. The radio's circuit uses this signal, and a loudspeaker makes the sound."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a radio signal is produced by a transmitter and detected by the aerial of a radio receiver. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "An alternating current in the transmitter's circuit makes electrons in its aerial oscillate.", "why": "Radio waves are produced by oscillating charges in an electrical circuit."}, {"do": "The oscillating electrons give out radio waves with the same frequency as the alternating current.", "why": "The wave's frequency matches the frequency of the oscillation that made it."}, {"do": "The radio waves travel through the air to the receiver's aerial, carrying energy.", "why": "Waves transfer energy from one place to another."}, {"do": "The aerial is a conductor, so it absorbs the waves. Their energy makes electrons in it oscillate, giving an alternating current of the same frequency as the waves.", "why": "Absorption transfers energy to electrons. This is the key detection point."}, {"do": "The radio's circuit uses this signal, and the loudspeaker then produces sound.", "why": "Shows the aerial gives an electrical signal, not sound itself."}], "answer": "An alternating current in the transmitter makes electrons oscillate, producing radio waves of the same frequency. The waves travel through the air. The receiver's aerial absorbs them, so electrons in it oscillate, giving an alternating current of the same frequency. The radio's circuit and loudspeaker turn this into sound."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does absorbing the radio wave produce an alternating current in the aerial?", "o": ["The wave transfers energy to electrons, so they oscillate back and forth", "The wave is reflected by the metal and pushes electrons one way", "The wave refracts inside the aerial and slows down into a current", "The wave turns into sound inside the metal, which moves the electrons"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Absorbed energy makes the electrons in the conductor oscillate, so the current keeps changing direction.", "Reflection is not what makes the current. Absorption does, and the oscillation gives an alternating current, not one that flows one way.", "Refraction is a change of speed and direction at a boundary. It does not make a current. Absorption does.", "The aerial does not make sound. The wave's energy goes straight to the electrons, and sound is made later by a loudspeaker."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the metal aerial of a TV picks up a signal sent as radio waves from a transmitter. (3 marks)", "given": ["The radio waves from the transmitter reach the metal aerial.", "The aerial is a conductor, so it absorbs the waves. They transfer energy to electrons in it."], "q": "What is the next point that earns the final mark?", "o": ["The electrons flow one way only, making a steady direct current in the aerial", "The electrons are reflected back out, so the aerial sends the waves on to the screen", "The aerial changes the waves into sound waves, which travel to the loudspeaker", "The electrons oscillate, making an alternating current with the same frequency as the waves"], "a": 3, "f": ["The wave makes electrons oscillate, so the current keeps changing direction. It is alternating, not direct.", "The aerial absorbs the waves rather than reflecting them, and the energy goes into the electrons.", "The aerial gives an electrical signal. Sound is made later by a loudspeaker.", "Right. The oscillating electrons form an alternating current of the same frequency as the radio waves."]}, "trap": "Saying the aerial reflects the wave or makes sound directly. It absorbs the wave and produces an electrical signal. A loudspeaker makes the sound later.", "recap": "A conductor absorbing a radio wave gets an alternating current of the same frequency, and an alternating current in a circuit makes radio waves."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.6.2.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often ask you to give a use for a type of wave or describe its hazards, so you need each one matched to the right wave.", "hook": {"q": "You can get sunburn from sunlight but not from a radio signal. Both are electromagnetic waves. What is the main reason?", "o": ["Radio waves cannot travel through air, so they never reach your skin", "Sunlight includes ultraviolet, which has a higher frequency and carries more energy", "Radio waves have more energy but pass straight through skin without harm", "Sunlight is hotter than radio waves, so it burns skin like a flame"], "a": 1, "f": ["Radio waves travel well through air. That is how radio and TV signals reach your home.", "Right. Higher-frequency waves carry more energy, so ultraviolet can damage skin cells and radio waves cannot.", "Radio waves have less energy than ultraviolet, not more. Frequency and energy go up together along the spectrum.", "Sunburn is cell damage, not a flame-type burn. Heat is not the reason; the wave's energy and frequency matter."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Uses: low-frequency end", "b": "**Radio waves**: television and radio broadcasting. **Microwaves**: satellite communication and cooking food. **Infrared**: electrical heaters, cooking food and infrared cameras. Their uses depend on carrying signals or transferring energy as heat."}, {"h": "Uses: high-frequency end", "b": "Visible light: fibre optic communications. **Ultraviolet**: energy-efficient lamps and sun tanning. **X-rays**: medical imaging, for example images of bones. **Gamma rays**: medical treatment, for example killing cancer cells."}, {"h": "Ionising radiation", "b": "X-rays and gamma rays are **ionising radiation**. They carry enough energy to knock electrons off atoms. In body cells this can cause gene **mutations** and **cancer**. Higher frequency means more energy per wave."}, {"h": "Ultraviolet and safety", "b": "Ultraviolet can damage **skin cells**. It makes skin age early and raises the risk of skin cancer. Sun cream reduces the UV reaching your skin. Staff using X-rays or gamma rays limit exposure time and use **shielding**, such as lead."}], "example": {"task": "Gamma rays are used to treat cancer, but they can also cause cancer. Explain how both can be true.", "steps": [{"do": "Gamma rays are ionising radiation. They carry enough energy to remove electrons from atoms and molecules.", "why": "Ionising is the key property behind both the use and the hazard."}, {"do": "When gamma rays hit a cell, they can damage the cell and the genes (DNA) inside it.", "why": "This links the radiation to what happens in the body."}, {"do": "In treatment, a beam is aimed at the tumour. The damage kills the cancer cells.", "why": "This explains the use: cell damage is helpful when it targets cancer."}, {"do": "Healthy cells hit by gamma rays may survive with a mutated gene. A mutated cell can divide out of control and become cancer.", "why": "This explains the hazard: damage that does not kill can cause cancer."}, {"do": "So doctors aim the beam carefully and limit the dose to reduce the risk to healthy cells.", "why": "Linking the hazard to safe use rounds off the answer."}], "answer": "Gamma rays are ionising and damage cells and genes. Aimed at a tumour, this kills cancer cells. In healthy cells, damage can cause mutations and so cancer, so exposure is limited."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the beam is aimed at the tumour?", "o": ["Gamma rays only damage cancer cells, never healthy cells", "Aiming the beam raises its frequency so it becomes ionising", "Most of the damage then falls on the cancer cells, not healthy ones", "Gamma rays are only ionising when they hit a tumour"], "a": 2, "f": ["Gamma rays can damage any cell they hit. That is why healthy cells are still at risk.", "Aiming does not change frequency. Gamma rays are always high frequency and always ionising.", "Right. The beam damages whatever it hits, so aiming it at the tumour keeps most of the damage on cancer cells.", "Gamma rays are ionising wherever they go. Being ionising does not depend on hitting a tumour."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why people are advised to wear sun cream on a very sunny day.", "given": ["Sunlight contains ultraviolet radiation.", "Ultraviolet is absorbed by skin cells and can damage them."], "q": "Which is the best next step to complete the explanation?", "o": ["Damaged cells may mutate and become cancerous. Sun cream cuts the UV reaching skin.", "UV makes the skin radioactive. Sun cream stops skin giving out radiation.", "UV heats organs deep inside the body. Sun cream keeps those organs cool.", "UV passes straight through the body. Sun cream makes the skin thicker."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. It links cell damage to skin cancer and says how sun cream reduces the hazard.", "Radiation does not make anything radioactive. UV damages cells it is absorbed by; it does not make skin give out radiation.", "UV is mostly absorbed at the skin's surface. It does not heat organs deep inside, and sun cream is not a cooling agent.", "UV does not pass through the body; skin absorbs it. Sun cream cuts the UV reaching the skin, not by thickening it."]}, "trap": "Do not pick microwaves or infrared just because they feel hot. Heating is not the same as ionising. The ionising waves are X-rays and gamma rays, at the high-frequency end.", "recap": "Higher-frequency waves carry more energy: ultraviolet can harm skin, and ionising X-rays and gamma rays can cause gene mutations and cancer."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.6.2.5", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 often asks you to explain why an object looks a certain colour in coloured light or through a filter, and to draw ray diagrams for lenses.", "hook": {"q": "You can see your face in a mirror but not in a painted wall. Both reflect light. Why the difference?", "o": ["The wall absorbs all the light that hits it", "The wall does not obey the law of reflection", "The wall scatters light in many directions", "The mirror gives out its own light"], "a": 2, "f": ["Walls do reflect most of the light that hits them, which is how you see them. Absorbing all of it would make the wall black.", "Every surface obeys the law of reflection. A rough wall has many tiny surfaces facing different ways.", "Right. The mirror is smooth, so reflected rays stay organised and form an image. The rough wall sends rays in all directions.", "A mirror only reflects light that already falls on it. It is not a source of light."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Two types of reflection", "b": "A smooth surface gives **specular reflection**. Parallel rays stay parallel, so you see a clear image, as in a mirror. A rough surface gives **diffuse reflection**. Rays scatter in many directions, so there is no clear image. The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection at every point in both cases."}, {"h": "Why objects have colour", "b": "White light is a mixture of all the colours. An opaque object **reflects** some colours and **absorbs** the rest. You see the colours it reflects. A white object reflects all colours. A black object absorbs all of them. In coloured light, an object can only reflect colours that are in that light."}, {"h": "Colour filters", "b": "A **filter** is a see-through coloured material. It **transmits** its own colour and absorbs the others. A red filter in white light lets only red light through. A filter does not add colour to the light. Light of a colour the filter does not transmit is absorbed."}, {"h": "Convex and concave lenses", "b": "A **convex lens** brings parallel rays together at the **principal focus**. A **concave lens** spreads them out, as if from a focus on the incoming side. A ray parallel to the axis bends through the focus (convex) or spreads out as if from the focus (concave). A ray through the centre goes straight. Press Next step on the graph.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "steps", "x": [-6, 6], "xstep": 2, "y": [-3, 3], "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "distance from lens / cm", "ylabel": "height / cm", "lines": [{"c": 3, "pts": [[-6, 0], [6, 0]]}, {"label": "Convex lens", "c": 0, "pts": [[0, -2.5], [0, 2.5]]}, {"label": "Object", "c": 2, "pts": [[-4, 0], [-4, 1]]}, {"c": 1, "step": 1, "pts": [[-4, 1], [0, 1], [2, 0], [4, -1]]}, {"c": 1, "dash": true, "step": 2, "pts": [[-4, 1], [4, -1]]}, {"label": "Image", "c": 2, "step": 3, "pts": [[4, 0], [4, -1]]}], "points": [{"at": [-2, 0], "label": "F"}, {"at": [2, 0], "label": "F"}], "steps": ["Ray 1: from the top of the object, parallel to the axis. At the lens it bends and goes through the principal focus F on the far side.", "Ray 2: from the top of the object, through the centre of the lens. It goes straight on without bending.", "The rays cross 4 cm past the lens. That crossing is the top of the image: real, upside down, and the same size as the object."]}}], "example": {"task": "A green leaf is lit by white light that has passed through a red filter. Explain why the leaf looks black.", "steps": [{"do": "White light contains all the colours of the visible spectrum.", "why": "So the red filter has red light available to transmit."}, {"do": "The red filter transmits red light and absorbs the other colours.", "why": "That is what makes it a red filter: only red passes through."}, {"do": "Only red light reaches the leaf.", "why": "All the other colours were absorbed by the filter before reaching it."}, {"do": "The green leaf reflects green light and absorbs other colours, so it absorbs the red light.", "why": "A green object reflects only green light. Red is not reflected, so it is absorbed."}, {"do": "No light is reflected from the leaf to your eye, so it looks black.", "why": "Black means no light is coming back to the eye."}], "answer": "White light contains all colours. The red filter transmits only red. The green leaf reflects green but absorbs red. So no light is reflected to the eye and the leaf looks black."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the leaf absorbs the red light?", "o": ["Leaves absorb every colour of light that reaches them", "The leaf reflects green, not red, so the red light is absorbed", "Filters make leaves absorb light of the filter's colour", "Red light is too weak to be reflected by any object"], "a": 1, "f": ["That would make every leaf black in white light too. A green leaf reflects green light, which is why it looks green.", "Right. An object reflects only its own colour strongly. A green leaf does not reflect red, so red light hitting it is absorbed.", "The filter acts on the light before it reaches the leaf. It does not change how the leaf behaves.", "Red light is reflected well by red objects. How well a colour is reflected depends on the surface, not on the colour being weak."]}, "faded": {"task": "A white T-shirt is lit by white light that has passed through a blue filter. What colour does it look, and why?", "given": ["White light contains all the colours.", "The blue filter transmits blue light and absorbs the other colours."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["The T-shirt absorbs the blue light, so no light is reflected and it looks black", "The T-shirt reflects all of the white light, so it still looks white", "The blue filter adds blue to the white light, so the T-shirt looks blue-white", "The T-shirt reflects all colours, so it reflects the blue light and looks blue"], "a": 3, "f": ["Black objects absorb all colours, but white objects reflect them. The blue light is reflected, not absorbed.", "Only blue light reaches the T-shirt. There is no white light left to reflect, because the filter absorbed the other colours.", "A filter adds no colour. It only removes colours. The light is blue because the other colours were absorbed.", "Right. White reflects all colours. Only blue reaches it, so blue is reflected to your eye and the T-shirt looks blue."]}, "trap": "Saying a filter 'turns' the light a colour or 'adds' colour. A filter only absorbs the colours it does not transmit. Track which colours are present at each stage.", "recap": "Objects and filters show only the colours they reflect or transmit and absorb the rest, so a colour missing from the light cannot appear."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.6.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to explain why surfaces heat up or cool down, and why the Earth's temperature changes, using infrared emission and absorption.", "hook": {"q": "On a sunny day you can wear a black or a white T-shirt. Which gets warmer, and why?", "o": ["White: it absorbs more of the Sun's radiation", "Black: it reflects more of the Sun's radiation", "Black: it absorbs more of the Sun's radiation", "Both the same, because the sunlight is the same"], "a": 2, "f": ["White surfaces reflect more radiation, so they absorb less. This option swaps the two.", "Black surfaces reflect little. Shiny, light surfaces are the ones that reflect most.", "Matt black surfaces are the best absorbers, so the black T-shirt takes in more energy and warms more.", "The sunlight is the same, but the surfaces absorb different amounts of it. The colour makes a difference."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Everything emits infrared", "b": "Every object **emits** infrared radiation, and every object also **absorbs** it. This happens at any temperature. The hotter an object is, the more infrared it emits each second. Its radiation also shifts towards shorter wavelengths as it gets hotter."}, {"h": "Surfaces matter", "b": "A **matt black** surface is the best absorber and the best emitter of infrared. A shiny, light surface is the worst at both, because it **reflects** most radiation. A **perfect black body** absorbs all the radiation that hits it. Good absorbers are good emitters."}, {"h": "Absorbing versus emitting", "b": "If an object absorbs radiation faster than it emits it, its temperature **rises**. If it emits faster than it absorbs, its temperature **falls**. If the two rates are equal, its temperature stays **constant**."}, {"h": "Earth's temperature", "b": "The Earth's temperature stays roughly **constant** when it absorbs and emits radiation at the same rate. It depends on how much radiation arrives, how much is **reflected** into space (by clouds and ice) and how much the Earth **emits**. Changing any of these changes the temperature."}], "example": {"task": "Two identical cans hold the same amount of water at 90°C. One can is matt black and one is shiny silver. Explain why the water in the black can cools faster.", "steps": [{"do": "Both cans are hotter than the room, so each emits more infrared each second than it absorbs from the room.", "why": "An object hotter than its surroundings emits more than it absorbs, so it loses energy overall."}, {"do": "The matt black surface is a better emitter of infrared than the shiny silver surface.", "why": "Matt black is the best emitter. Shiny surfaces are the worst, because they reflect."}, {"do": "So the black can emits more infrared each second than the silver can.", "why": "This links the surface to the rate of emission, which is what the question is about."}, {"do": "The black can therefore loses energy faster overall, so its water falls in temperature faster.", "why": "A bigger net loss of energy each second means a faster fall in temperature."}], "answer": "Both cans are hotter than the room, so both emit more infrared than they absorb. The matt black can is the better emitter, so it emits more infrared each second. It loses energy faster, so its water cools faster."}, "selfx": {"q": "The black can also absorbs more from the room. Why does it still cool faster?", "o": ["Each can emits more than it absorbs, and the black can's net loss each second is bigger", "Black surfaces cannot absorb any infrared radiation from the cooler room around them", "Absorbing infrared from the room makes the black can lose energy faster than the silver", "The room is hotter than the cans, so the silver can absorbs more energy than the black"], "a": 0, "f": ["The cans are hotter than the room, so emission beats absorption for both. The black can emits more, so its net loss is bigger.", "Matt black is the best absorber, not a non-absorber. It does absorb from the room, but it emits more.", "Absorbing infrared adds energy, so it slows cooling. The black can cools faster because its extra emission outweighs its extra absorption.", "The cans are at 90°C and the room is cooler. Also, shiny silver is a poorer absorber than matt black."]}, "faded": {"task": "The graph shows a metal block at 100°C placed in a room at 20°C. Explain why it cools, and why its temperature then stops falling.", "given": ["The block emits infrared radiation and also absorbs infrared from the room.", "At first it is hotter than the room, so it emits more infrared each second than it absorbs."], "q": "What is the next point in the explanation?", "o": ["So it stops emitting once it has cooled to the same temperature as the room.", "So it cools until it stops absorbing infrared from the cooler room.", "So it cools because the room only emits infrared and the block only absorbs it.", "So it loses energy overall and cools, until it emits and absorbs at the same rate."], "a": 3, "f": ["Objects never stop emitting. At room temperature the block still emits, but it absorbs at the same rate.", "Objects keep absorbing infrared at all times. The temperature stops falling when absorption equals emission.", "Both the block and the room emit and absorb infrared. Neither one does only one of these.", "Net energy loss makes the block cool. Its temperature stops falling when emission and absorption rates are equal."], "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 120], "xstep": 2, "ystep": 20, "xlabel": "time / min", "ylabel": "temperature of block / °C", "lines": [{"pts": [[0.0, 100.0], [0.0625, 97.538659], [0.125, 95.153045], [0.1875, 92.840829], [0.25, 90.599752], [0.3125, 88.427626], [0.375, 86.322329], [0.4375, 84.281806], [0.5, 82.304063], [0.5625, 80.387168], [0.625, 78.52925], [0.6875, 76.728495], [0.75, 74.983142], [0.8125, 73.291489], [0.875, 71.651882], [0.9375, 70.062721], [1.0, 68.522453], [1.0625, 67.029574], [1.125, 65.582626], [1.1875, 64.180196], [1.25, 62.820914], [1.3125, 61.503453], [1.375, 60.226526], [1.4375, 58.988886], [1.5, 57.789324], [1.5625, 56.626669], [1.625, 55.499785], [1.6875, 54.407571], [1.75, 53.348962], [1.8125, 52.322922], [1.875, 51.32845], [1.9375, 50.364575], [2.0, 49.430355], [2.0625, 48.524878], [2.125, 47.64726], [2.1875, 46.796643], [2.25, 45.972197], [2.3125, 45.173117], [2.375, 44.398621], [2.4375, 43.647955], [2.5, 42.920384], [2.5625, 42.215198], [2.625, 41.531708], [2.6875, 40.869247], [2.75, 40.227168], [2.8125, 39.604843], [2.875, 39.001666], [2.9375, 38.417046], [3.0, 37.850413], [3.0625, 37.301213], [3.125, 36.768911], [3.1875, 36.252986], [3.25, 35.752934], [3.3125, 35.268267], [3.375, 34.798512], [3.4375, 34.34321], [3.5, 33.901915], [3.5625, 33.474199], [3.625, 33.059641], [3.6875, 32.657838], [3.75, 32.268397], [3.8125, 31.890938], [3.875, 31.525093], [3.9375, 31.170503], [4.0, 30.826823], [4.0625, 30.493716], [4.125, 30.170859], [4.1875, 29.857934], [4.25, 29.554637], [4.3125, 29.260672], [4.375, 28.975751], [4.4375, 28.699596], [4.5, 28.431938], [4.5625, 28.172515], [4.625, 27.921073], [4.6875, 27.677367], [4.75, 27.441159], [4.8125, 27.212219], [4.875, 26.990322], [4.9375, 26.775252], [5.0, 26.5668], [5.0625, 26.364761], [5.125, 26.168938], [5.1875, 25.979139], [5.25, 25.795181], [5.3125, 25.616882], [5.375, 25.444068], [5.4375, 25.276572], [5.5, 25.114229], [5.5625, 24.956881], [5.625, 24.804373], [5.6875, 24.656558], [5.75, 24.513291], [5.8125, 24.374432], [5.875, 24.239845], [5.9375, 24.109398], [6.0, 23.982965], [6.0625, 23.860423], [6.125, 23.74165], [6.1875, 23.626531], [6.25, 23.514955], [6.3125, 23.406811], [6.375, 23.301994], [6.4375, 23.200403], [6.5, 23.101937], [6.5625, 23.0065], [6.625, 22.914], [6.6875, 22.824345], [6.75, 22.737449], [6.8125, 22.653227], [6.875, 22.571596], [6.9375, 22.492476], [7.0, 22.415791], [7.0625, 22.341465], [7.125, 22.269425], [7.1875, 22.199602], [7.25, 22.131928], [7.3125, 22.066335], [7.375, 22.002761], [7.4375, 21.941142], [7.5, 21.88142], [7.5625, 21.823534], [7.625, 21.76743], [7.6875, 21.713052], [7.75, 21.660347], [7.8125, 21.609264], [7.875, 21.559752], [7.9375, 21.511763], [8.0, 21.465251], [8.0625, 21.42017], [8.125, 21.376476], [8.1875, 21.334126], [8.25, 21.29308], [8.3125, 21.253296], [8.375, 21.214736], [8.4375, 21.177362], [8.5, 21.141139], [8.5625, 21.10603], [8.625, 21.072001], [8.6875, 21.039019], [8.75, 21.007051], [8.8125, 20.976068], [8.875, 20.946037], [8.9375, 20.916931], [9.0, 20.88872], [9.0625, 20.861377], [9.125, 20.834875], [9.1875, 20.809189], [9.25, 20.784292], [9.3125, 20.760162], [9.375, 20.736775], [9.4375, 20.714106], [9.5, 20.692136], [9.5625, 20.670841], [9.625, 20.650201], [9.6875, 20.630197], [9.75, 20.610808], [9.8125, 20.592015], [9.875, 20.573801], [9.9375, 20.556147], [10.0, 20.539036]], "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 20], [10, 20]], "c": 1}], "points": [{"at": [0, 100], "label": "100°C"}]}}, "trap": "Thinking shiny surfaces emit best, or that emitters and absorbers are different surfaces. Matt black is best at both. Shiny, light surfaces are worst at both. Good absorbers are good emitters.", "recap": "All objects emit and absorb infrared; hotter ones emit more, matt black is best at both, and the bigger rate decides heating or cooling."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.7.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Magnetism is examined on Paper 2, usually as short answers: drawing a bar magnet's field, comparing permanent and induced magnets, or explaining what a compass shows about the Earth.", "hook": {"q": "A steel paperclip is not a magnet, yet a magnet picks it up. What do you think happens inside the paperclip?", "o": ["It already has a north pole only, so the magnet pulls it.", "The magnet's field turns it into a magnet while it is near.", "The magnet gives it an electric charge, which then attracts it.", "The magnet changes the paperclip's weight so that it floats up."], "a": 1, "f": ["A paperclip has no poles of its own. A single pole on its own cannot exist. Magnets always have a north and a south pole.", "Right. The field makes the paperclip an induced magnet. That is the idea you are about to meet.", "Charge is an electrical idea. A magnet's pull on iron or steel is magnetic. Charge is not involved.", "Weight does not change. The magnet exerts a magnetic force, which is separate from gravity."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Poles and forces", "b": "Every magnet has two **poles**, a north and a south. The magnetic force is strongest at the poles. Like poles repel. Unlike poles attract. These are **non-contact forces**: the magnets push or pull without touching."}, {"h": "Permanent and induced magnets", "b": "A **permanent magnet** has its own magnetic field all the time. An **induced magnet** is a magnetic material, such as iron, steel, cobalt or nickel, that is magnetic only while in another magnet's field. It is always attracted to the magnet. It quickly loses most of its magnetism when the field is removed."}, {"h": "Magnetic fields", "b": "A **magnetic field** is the region where a magnet or magnetic material feels a force. Outside a bar magnet, **field lines** run from north to south and never cross. They are closest together at the poles, where the field is strongest. The force gets weaker further away."}, {"h": "Compasses and the Earth", "b": "A **compass** has a small bar magnet as its needle. It turns to line up with any magnetic field. Far from other magnets, it still points north. So the Earth has its own magnetic field. This shows the Earth's **core is magnetic**."}], "example": {"task": "A compass needle points north when there are no magnets nearby. Explain what this shows about the Earth's core.", "steps": [{"do": "A compass needle is a small bar magnet, with a north pole and a south pole.", "why": "This links the needle to magnetic ideas, so you can say how it behaves in a field."}, {"do": "A magnet in a magnetic field feels a force that turns it to line up with the field.", "why": "This explains why the needle moves at all. It only turns if a field is present."}, {"do": "With no magnets nearby, the needle still settles in one direction, so the field must come from the Earth.", "why": "Ruling out other magnets shows that the Earth itself is the source."}, {"do": "The Earth's field is like that of a bar magnet, so the core must be magnetic.", "why": "This links the field to its source and answers the question about the core."}], "answer": "The needle is a small magnet that lines up with any magnetic field. It points north even with no magnets nearby, so the field is the Earth's own. The Earth therefore behaves like a giant bar magnet, so its core must be magnetic."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 say 'with no magnets nearby' before concluding the field comes from the Earth?", "o": ["Magnets stop a compass working, so it would give no reading near one.", "A compass needle is only magnetic when it is far from other magnets.", "Only gravity could turn the needle, and a magnet would cancel it out.", "A nearby magnet could also turn the needle, so the cause would be unclear."], "a": 3, "f": ["Magnets do not stop a compass working. They pull the needle round, which is the problem for this argument.", "The needle is a magnet all the time. Being near other magnets changes where it points, not whether it is magnetic.", "Gravity does not turn a compass needle. Its movement is caused by magnetic forces.", "Right. A nearby magnet would also deflect the needle. Removing it leaves the Earth as the only source of the field."]}, "faded": {"task": "An unmagnetised iron nail is held near the north pole of a bar magnet and is attracted. Explain why.", "given": ["Iron is a magnetic material.", "The bar magnet's field makes the nail an induced magnet."], "q": "What is the next point in the explanation?", "o": ["The end of the nail nearest the north pole becomes a north pole, so the two attract.", "The nail becomes a permanent magnet, so it stays magnetic when the magnet is removed.", "The end of the nail nearest the north pole becomes a south pole, so the two attract.", "The nail was already a magnet with a south pole, so it is attracted to the north pole."], "a": 2, "f": ["Like poles repel. If the near end became a north pole, the nail would be pushed away, not attracted.", "An induced magnet loses most of its magnetism quickly once the field is removed. It does not stay a permanent magnet.", "Right. The induced pole nearest the magnet is the opposite pole. Unlike poles attract, so the nail is pulled in.", "The question says the nail is unmagnetised, so it has no poles of its own. The poles are induced by the field."]}, "trap": "Thinking a magnet can repel a piece of iron. A magnet can only repel another magnet. Repulsion proves an object is a permanent magnet. Attraction alone does not, because induced magnets are always attracted.", "recap": "Like poles repel and unlike poles attract, field lines run north to south outside a magnet, and a compass shows the Earth's core is magnetic."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.7.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 often asks you to describe the field around a wire or solenoid, or how to strengthen it, and this leads into the motor effect.", "hook": {"q": "A wire carries a current. You hold a plotting compass right next to it. What do you expect the needle to do?", "o": ["Stay still, because a wire is not a magnet", "Turn, because the current makes a magnetic field around the wire", "Spin round and round, because the current keeps flowing", "Point along the wire, whichever way the current flows"], "a": 1, "f": ["A wire with no current is not magnetic, but a current creates a magnetic field. The compass needle reacts to that field.", "Right. A current produces a magnetic field around the wire. The needle lines up with it, so it turns.", "The field is steady, so the needle settles in one direction. It does not spin.", "The field lines circle the wire at right angles to it. The needle lines up with them, so it does not point along the wire."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Field around a wire", "b": "Any wire carrying a **current** has a **magnetic field** around it. The field lines are circles centred on the wire, at right angles to it. They are closer together near the wire, so the field is stronger there. A bigger current gives a stronger field. Reversing the current reverses the field direction."}, {"h": "Coils and solenoids", "b": "Bend a wire into a coil and the fields from each turn add up inside it. A **solenoid** is a long coil with many turns. Inside, the field is strong and **uniform**: straight, parallel, evenly spaced lines. Outside, the field looks like a bar magnet's, with a pole at each end."}, {"h": "Making it stronger", "b": "Three changes strengthen a solenoid's field: a larger **current**, more **turns** of wire, or a **soft iron core** inside the coil. Iron is a magnetic material. The coil's field magnetises it, and its field adds to the coil's. Copper and plastic are not magnetic, so they do not help."}, {"h": "Electromagnets and poles", "b": "A solenoid with an iron core is an **electromagnet**. Unlike a permanent magnet, it can be switched off. Reversing the current swaps the north and south **poles**, but does not change the strength. Soft iron is used because it loses its magnetism when the current stops."}], "example": {"task": "Describe the magnetic field of a solenoid carrying a current. Explain how you could make the field stronger.", "steps": [{"do": "Outside the solenoid, the field has the same shape as a bar magnet's. One end acts as a north pole and the other end as a south pole.", "why": "Comparing with a bar magnet is the standard way to describe the outside field."}, {"do": "Inside the solenoid, the field is strong and uniform. The field lines are straight, parallel and evenly spaced.", "why": "The inside looks different from the outside, so you describe both."}, {"do": "The field is strong because each turn of wire has its own circular field. Inside the coil, these fields point the same way and add up.", "why": "This links the solenoid's field to the field around a single wire."}, {"do": "To strengthen it, increase the current, for example by turning up the power supply. You could also wind more turns of wire.", "why": "A bigger current gives a stronger field, and more turns add more fields together."}, {"do": "You could also put a soft iron core inside the coil. Iron is magnetic, so it becomes magnetised and adds to the field.", "why": "It names a material that works and gives the reason. Copper or plastic would not work."}], "answer": "Outside, the field is like a bar magnet's, with a north and a south pole. Inside, it is strong and uniform. It is strong because each turn's field adds up. Increase the current, add more turns, or add a soft iron core."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the last step say a soft iron core, rather than a copper core?", "o": ["Copper has more resistance, so it would lower the current", "Iron carries current better, so the coil's current is larger", "Iron is magnetic, so it becomes magnetised and adds to the field", "Copper's own field would cancel out the coil's field"], "a": 2, "f": ["The core is not part of the circuit. The current does not flow through it, so its resistance is not the reason.", "The core does not carry the coil's current. Its job is to be magnetised, not to conduct.", "Right. Iron is a magnetic material. The coil's field magnetises it, and the iron's field adds to the coil's field.", "Copper is not magnetic, so it has no field of its own to cancel anything. It simply has no effect."]}, "faded": {"task": "Describe the magnetic field around a long straight wire carrying a current, and how its strength changes with distance from the wire.", "given": ["The field lines are circles centred on the wire.", "The circles are at right angles to the wire."], "q": "What is the next point to make?", "o": ["The lines are closer together far from the wire, so the field is stronger there.", "The field is the same strength everywhere, so the lines are evenly spaced.", "The lines are straight and point outwards, getting weaker away from the wire.", "The lines are closer together near the wire, so the field is stronger there."], "a": 3, "f": ["This is the wrong way round. The field is strongest near the wire, where the lines are closest together.", "The strength changes with distance. The lines are not evenly spaced, and the field gets weaker further away.", "This contradicts the circles already given. A straight wire has circular field lines, not straight lines pointing outwards.", "Right. Closely spaced lines mean a stronger field. It is strongest near the wire and weakens with distance."]}, "trap": "Choosing a copper or plastic core, or thinking that reversing the current strengthens the field. Only an iron core helps, and reversing the current just swaps the poles.", "recap": "A solenoid's field is like a bar magnet's; increase the current, add turns or insert a soft iron core to strengthen it."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.7.2.2", "kind": "method", "why": "Higher-tier papers often include an F = BIl calculation with the length given in cm, plus a question on how a motor or loudspeaker works.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "The motor effect", "b": "A current-carrying wire in a magnetic field can feel a **force**. This is the **motor effect**. The wire's own field interacts with the magnet's field. The force is largest when the wire is at right angles to the field, and zero when it is parallel. Reversing the current or the field reverses the force."}, {"h": "Fleming's left-hand rule", "b": "Use your **left hand**. Hold the thumb, first finger and second finger at right angles. The **thumb** shows the force (motion). The **first finger** shows the field, from north to south. The second finger shows the current, from + to −. Point two of them correctly and the third gives the direction."}, {"h": "Calculating the force", "b": "For a wire at right angles to the field, $F = BIl$. $F$ is the **force** in newtons (N). $B$ is the **magnetic flux density** in tesla (T). $I$ is the current in amps (A). $l$ is the length of wire in the field, in **metres**. Convert cm to m first."}, {"h": "Motors and loudspeakers", "b": "In a **motor**, the forces on opposite sides of a coil act in opposite directions, so the coil spins. A **split-ring commutator** reverses the current every half turn, so it keeps turning the same way. In a **loudspeaker**, a.c. in a coil makes the force keep reversing. The coil and cone vibrate at the same frequency as the current."}], "example": {"task": "A wire 4.0 cm long carries a current of 1.5 A at right angles to a uniform magnetic field. The force on it is 0.036 N. Calculate the magnetic flux density.", "steps": [{"do": "Write the equation: $F = BIl$.", "why": "The wire is at right angles to the field, so this equation applies."}, {"do": "Convert the length: 4.0 cm = 0.040 m.", "why": "The equation needs length in metres to give B in tesla."}, {"do": "Rearrange to make B the subject: $B = \\dfrac{F}{I l}$.", "why": "B is multiplied by I and l, so divide both sides by I × l."}, {"do": "Substitute: $B = \\dfrac{0.036}{1.5 \\times 0.040} = \\dfrac{0.036}{0.060}$.", "why": "Work out the bottom line first, then divide."}, {"do": "Calculate and add the unit: $B = 0.60$ T.", "why": "Flux density is measured in tesla. Marks are given for the unit."}], "answer": "B = 0.60 T"}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked example change 4.0 cm into 0.040 m before substituting?", "o": ["F = BIl only gives B in tesla when the length is in metres.", "It makes the number bigger, so the force is easier to read.", "The wire is at right angles, so the length must be in metres.", "The current is in amps, so the length must be in the same unit."], "a": 0, "f": ["Correct. Tesla is defined using newtons, amps and metres, so the equation only works with length in metres.", "Changing units does not aim to make numbers bigger. It makes the units match the equation.", "The right angle is needed for the equation to apply. It has nothing to do with the unit of length.", "Amps and metres are different quantities. They never have to be 'the same unit'. The equation just needs SI units."]}, "faded": {"task": "A wire 25 cm long carries 3.0 A at right angles to a field of flux density 0.040 T. Calculate the force on the wire.", "given": ["Equation: $F = BIl$", "Convert the length: 25 cm = 0.25 m"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["$F = 0.040 \\times 3.0 \\times 25 = 3.0$ N", "$F = 0.040 \\div 3.0 \\times 0.25 = 0.0033$ N", "$F = 0.040 \\times 3.0 \\times 0.25 = 0.030$ N", "$F = 0.040 + 3.0 + 0.25 = 3.3$ N"], "a": 2, "f": ["This ignores the conversion that was just done. Using 25 instead of 0.25 makes the force 100 times too big.", "This divides by the current. In F = BIl, all three quantities are multiplied together.", "Correct. Multiply B, I and l in metres: 0.040 × 3.0 × 0.25 = 0.030 N.", "This adds the quantities. The equation multiplies them. Adding also mixes up different units."]}, "trap": "Putting the length in cm, or using the right hand. Convert cm to m before you substitute. For the rule, use your left hand: thumb = force, first finger = field, second finger = current.", "recap": "For a wire at right angles to a field, $F = BIl$ with l in metres, and the left-hand rule gives the force direction."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.7.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "On Paper 2 (Higher), you may be asked to explain how a generator, microphone or transformer works, or to do a transformer calculation.", "hook": {"q": "A bar magnet is pushed into a coil joined to a sensitive voltmeter. Then it is held still inside the coil. What does the voltmeter show?", "o": ["A steady reading the whole time, because the magnet is inside the coil", "A reading only while the magnet moves, and zero when it is still", "Zero all the time, because a magnet cannot make a potential difference", "A reading only while the magnet is still, because the field is strongest then"], "a": 1, "f": ["A steady reading would need a steady supply of energy. A magnet that is not moving gives no changing field, so no pd.", "Right. A pd is induced only while the field through the coil is changing. When the magnet stops, the pd falls to zero.", "A magnet can induce a pd. The field must change, for example by the magnet moving.", "The field is strong when the magnet is still, but a strong steady field induces nothing. The field must change."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Induced potential", "b": "When a conductor moves relative to a magnetic field, or the field around it changes, a **potential difference** is **induced** across its ends. In a complete circuit this makes a current. This is the **generator effect**. A larger pd comes from faster movement, a stronger field or more turns of wire."}, {"h": "Generators and microphones", "b": "In an **a.c. generator**, a coil spins in a magnetic field, cutting field lines, so a pd is induced. It reverses each half turn, giving a.c. Play the graph. A **dynamo** uses a split-ring commutator to give d.c. In a **microphone**, sound moves a diaphragm and coil in a magnet's field, inducing a pd that varies with the sound.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "ms": 200, "x": [0, 360], "xstep": 90, "y": [-1.5, 1.5], "ystep": 0.5, "xlabel": "Angle turned / degrees", "ylabel": "Induced pd / maximum pd", "lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [2.25, 0.0393], [4.5, 0.0785], [6.75, 0.1175], [9.0, 0.1564], [11.25, 0.1951], [13.5, 0.2334], [15.75, 0.2714], [18.0, 0.309], [20.25, 0.3461], [22.5, 0.3827], [24.75, 0.4187], [27.0, 0.454], [29.25, 0.4886], [31.5, 0.5225], [33.75, 0.5556], [36.0, 0.5878], [38.25, 0.6191], [40.5, 0.6494], [42.75, 0.6788], [45.0, 0.7071], [47.25, 0.7343], [49.5, 0.7604], [51.75, 0.7853], [54.0, 0.809], [56.25, 0.8315], [58.5, 0.8526], [60.75, 0.8725], [63.0, 0.891], [65.25, 0.9081], [67.5, 0.9239], [69.75, 0.9382], [72.0, 0.9511], [74.25, 0.9625], [76.5, 0.9724], [78.75, 0.9808], [81.0, 0.9877], [83.25, 0.9931], [85.5, 0.9969], [87.75, 0.9992], [90.0, 1.0], [92.25, 0.9992], [94.5, 0.9969], [96.75, 0.9931], [99.0, 0.9877], [101.25, 0.9808], [103.5, 0.9724], [105.75, 0.9625], [108.0, 0.9511], [110.25, 0.9382], [112.5, 0.9239], [114.75, 0.9081], [117.0, 0.891], [119.25, 0.8725], [121.5, 0.8526], [123.75, 0.8315], [126.0, 0.809], [128.25, 0.7853], [130.5, 0.7604], [132.75, 0.7343], [135.0, 0.7071], [137.25, 0.6788], [139.5, 0.6494], [141.75, 0.6191], [144.0, 0.5878], [146.25, 0.5556], [148.5, 0.5225], [150.75, 0.4886], [153.0, 0.454], [155.25, 0.4187], [157.5, 0.3827], [159.75, 0.3461], [162.0, 0.309], [164.25, 0.2714], [166.5, 0.2334], [168.75, 0.1951], [171.0, 0.1564], [173.25, 0.1175], [175.5, 0.0785], [177.75, 0.0393], [180.0, 0.0], [182.25, -0.0393], [184.5, -0.0785], [186.75, -0.1175], [189.0, -0.1564], [191.25, -0.1951], [193.5, -0.2334], [195.75, -0.2714], [198.0, -0.309], [200.25, -0.3461], [202.5, -0.3827], [204.75, -0.4187], [207.0, -0.454], [209.25, -0.4886], [211.5, -0.5225], [213.75, -0.5556], [216.0, -0.5878], [218.25, -0.6191], [220.5, -0.6494], [222.75, -0.6788], [225.0, -0.7071], [227.25, -0.7343], [229.5, -0.7604], [231.75, -0.7853], [234.0, -0.809], [236.25, -0.8315], [238.5, -0.8526], [240.75, -0.8725], [243.0, -0.891], [245.25, -0.9081], [247.5, -0.9239], [249.75, -0.9382], [252.0, -0.9511], [254.25, -0.9625], [256.5, -0.9724], [258.75, -0.9808], [261.0, -0.9877], [263.25, -0.9931], [265.5, -0.9969], [267.75, -0.9992], [270.0, -1.0], [272.25, -0.9992], [274.5, -0.9969], [276.75, -0.9931], [279.0, -0.9877], [281.25, -0.9808], [283.5, -0.9724], [285.75, -0.9625], [288.0, -0.9511], [290.25, -0.9382], [292.5, -0.9239], [294.75, -0.9081], [297.0, -0.891], [299.25, -0.8725], [301.5, -0.8526], [303.75, -0.8315], [306.0, -0.809], [308.25, -0.7853], [310.5, -0.7604], [312.75, -0.7343], [315.0, -0.7071], [317.25, -0.6788], [319.5, -0.6494], [321.75, -0.6191], [324.0, -0.5878], [326.25, -0.5556], [328.5, -0.5225], [330.75, -0.4886], [333.0, -0.454], [335.25, -0.4187], [337.5, -0.3827], [339.75, -0.3461], [342.0, -0.309], [344.25, -0.2714], [346.5, -0.2334], [348.75, -0.1951], [351.0, -0.1564], [353.25, -0.1175], [355.5, -0.0785], [357.75, -0.0393], [360.0, -0.0]], "label": "induced pd"}], "points": [], "frames": [{"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [0.0, 0.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [0.0, 0.0], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 0° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[15.0, 0.0], [15.0, 0.259]]}], "points": [{"at": [15.0, 0.2588], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 15° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0.26 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[30.0, 0.0], [30.0, 0.5]]}], "points": [{"at": [30.0, 0.5], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 30° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0.5 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[45.0, 0.0], [45.0, 0.707]]}], "points": [{"at": [45.0, 0.7071], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 45° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0.71 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[60.0, 0.0], [60.0, 0.866]]}], "points": [{"at": [60.0, 0.866], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 60° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0.87 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[75.0, 0.0], [75.0, 0.966]]}], "points": [{"at": [75.0, 0.9659], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 75° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0.97 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[90.0, 0.0], [90.0, 1.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [90.0, 1.0], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 90° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 1 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[105.0, 0.0], [105.0, 0.966]]}], "points": [{"at": [105.0, 0.9659], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 105° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0.97 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[120.0, 0.0], [120.0, 0.866]]}], "points": [{"at": [120.0, 0.866], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 120° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0.87 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[135.0, 0.0], [135.0, 0.707]]}], "points": [{"at": [135.0, 0.7071], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 135° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0.71 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[150.0, 0.0], [150.0, 0.5]]}], "points": [{"at": [150.0, 0.5], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 150° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0.5 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[165.0, 0.0], [165.0, 0.259]]}], "points": [{"at": [165.0, 0.2588], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 165° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0.26 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[180.0, 0.0], [180.0, 0.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [180.0, 0.0], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 180° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[195.0, -0.0], [195.0, -0.259]]}], "points": [{"at": [195.0, -0.2588], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 195° from sides moving along the field", "pd = −0.26 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[210.0, -0.0], [210.0, -0.5]]}], "points": [{"at": [210.0, -0.5], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 210° from sides moving along the field", "pd = −0.5 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[225.0, -0.0], [225.0, -0.707]]}], "points": [{"at": [225.0, -0.7071], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 225° from sides moving along the field", "pd = −0.71 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[240.0, -0.0], [240.0, -0.866]]}], "points": [{"at": [240.0, -0.866], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 240° from sides moving along the field", "pd = −0.87 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[255.0, -0.0], [255.0, -0.966]]}], "points": [{"at": [255.0, -0.9659], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 255° from sides moving along the field", "pd = −0.97 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[270.0, -0.0], [270.0, -1.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [270.0, -1.0], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 270° from sides moving along the field", "pd = −1 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[285.0, -0.0], [285.0, -0.966]]}], "points": [{"at": [285.0, -0.9659], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 285° from sides moving along the field", "pd = −0.97 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[300.0, -0.0], [300.0, -0.866]]}], "points": [{"at": [300.0, -0.866], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 300° from sides moving along the field", "pd = −0.87 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[315.0, -0.0], [315.0, -0.707]]}], "points": [{"at": [315.0, -0.7071], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 315° from sides moving along the field", "pd = −0.71 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[330.0, -0.0], [330.0, -0.5]]}], "points": [{"at": [330.0, -0.5], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 330° from sides moving along the field", "pd = −0.5 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[345.0, -0.0], [345.0, -0.259]]}], "points": [{"at": [345.0, -0.2588], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 345° from sides moving along the field", "pd = −0.26 × the maximum"]}, {"lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 1, "pts": [[360.0, -0.0], [360.0, -0.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [360.0, -0.0], "label": "now"}], "read": ["Turned: 360° from sides moving along the field", "pd = 0 × the maximum"]}], "ctl": {"type": "play", "label": "a", "vals": ["0", "15", "30", "45", "60", "75", "90", "105", "120", "135", "150", "165", "180", "195", "210", "225", "240", "255", "270", "285", "300", "315", "330", "345", "360"], "start": 0}}}, {"h": "How a transformer works", "b": "A transformer has two coils wound on one **iron core**. An alternating pd across the **primary coil** gives a changing magnetic field in the core. This changing field passes through the **secondary coil** and induces an alternating pd in it. Direct current would give a steady field and no induced pd."}, {"h": "Transformer equations", "b": "For an ideal transformer, $\\frac{V_p}{V_s} = \\frac{n_p}{n_s}$. More turns on the secondary makes a **step-up** transformer. Fewer makes a **step-down** one. An ideal transformer wastes no energy, so power in equals power out: $V_p I_p = V_s I_s$. If the pd goes up, the current goes down."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a step-down transformer changes 230 V mains to 23 V, and why the current in its secondary coil is larger than in its primary coil.", "steps": [{"do": "The alternating pd across the primary coil makes an alternating current in it.", "why": "Only a changing current makes a changing magnetic field."}, {"do": "This current creates a changing magnetic field, which the iron core carries to the secondary coil.", "why": "Iron is easily magnetised, so it links the two coils."}, {"do": "The changing field through the secondary coil induces an alternating pd across it.", "why": "Inducing a pd needs a changing field. This is the generator effect."}, {"do": "The secondary has fewer turns than the primary, so the pd is smaller: $\\frac{V_p}{V_s} = \\frac{n_p}{n_s}$.", "why": "The pd across each coil is in proportion to its number of turns."}, {"do": "For an ideal transformer $V_p I_p = V_s I_s$. A smaller $V_s$ means a larger $I_s$.", "why": "No energy is lost, so power is unchanged. If pd falls, current must rise."}], "answer": "An alternating current in the primary makes a changing field in the iron core. This induces a pd in the secondary. Fewer secondary turns give a smaller pd. Power is the same, so the secondary current is larger."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 1 stress that the current in the primary coil is alternating?", "o": ["Direct current would overheat the primary coil and stop the transformer working", "Alternating current has a higher pd than direct current, so the secondary gets more", "The iron core only works if the current keeps changing direction to stay magnetised", "Only a changing current makes a changing field, which is what induces the secondary pd"], "a": 3, "f": ["Heating is not the reason. The problem is that a steady current gives a steady field, so no pd is induced.", "a.c. and d.c. can have the same pd. What matters is that a.c. changes, so the field changes.", "A steady current would magnetise the iron too. The real point is that the field must keep changing to induce a pd.", "Right. A steady field induces no pd. The current must change so the field through the secondary changes."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a microphone changes sound into an electrical signal.", "given": ["Sound waves hit the diaphragm and make it vibrate.", "The diaphragm is joined to a coil, so the coil moves back and forth in the field of a magnet."], "q": "Which statement is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["A current in the coil makes it feel a force, which pushes the diaphragm in and out.", "The moving coil gains charge from the magnet, so a steady direct current flows.", "The coil cuts the magnet's field lines, inducing a pd that varies with the sound.", "Sound energy heats the coil, and the heat pushes charge round the circuit."], "a": 2, "f": ["This describes a loudspeaker, which uses the motor effect. A microphone works the other way round, using the generator effect.", "The magnet does not give charge to the coil. Moving a coil in a field induces a pd, and as the motion varies, so does the pd.", "Right. Movement of the coil in the field induces a pd. It changes with the vibrations, so it matches the sound.", "Heating is not how the signal forms. The pd comes from the coil cutting field lines, not from heat."]}, "trap": "Thinking current falls when voltage falls. For an ideal transformer $V_p I_p = V_s I_s$, so in a step-down transformer the secondary current is larger than the primary current. Smaller pd means larger current.", "recap": "A changing magnetic field induces a pd, so transformers need a.c., and for an ideal one $V_p I_p = V_s I_s$."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.8.1.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 often asks you to describe the Solar System or how it formed, and knowing the order of events earns quick marks.", "hook": {"q": "A huge cloud of dust and gas floats in space. Which force could pull it together into a clump?", "o": ["Friction between the particles", "Gravity between the particles", "Magnetism between the particles", "Air pressure from outside the cloud"], "a": 1, "f": ["Friction needs surfaces rubbing together. It cannot pull a cloud together from far away.", "Right. Every particle has mass, so every particle attracts the others by gravity. Over a long time this pulls the cloud inwards.", "Dust and gas are not magnets, and magnetism is not what pulls a cloud together. Gravity is the force that attracts all masses.", "Space is almost empty, so there is no air outside the cloud to push it inwards."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The Sun and the planets", "b": "Our **Sun** is a star at the centre of the Solar System. It is one of billions of stars in a **galaxy** called the Milky Way. Eight **planets** orbit the Sun. The Sun's gravity keeps them in orbit."}, {"h": "Moons and smaller objects", "b": "A **moon** is a **natural satellite**. It orbits a planet, not the Sun directly. **Dwarf planets**, such as Pluto, orbit the Sun but are smaller than the eight planets. Asteroids and comets also orbit the Sun."}, {"h": "Birth from a nebula", "b": "The Solar System began as a huge cloud of dust and gas called a **nebula**. **Gravity** pulled the material together. As it fell inwards, the particles sped up and collided more often, so the temperature rose. The hot, dense centre became a **protostar**."}, {"h": "The Sun and planets form", "b": "When the centre was hot enough, hydrogen nuclei began to join to make helium. This is **nuclear fusion**, and it releases energy. The Sun was born. The dust and gas left over, further out, were pulled into clumps by gravity. These clumps became the **planets**."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how the Solar System formed. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "The Solar System formed from a huge cloud of dust and gas called a nebula.", "why": "This names the starting material. It also shows you are not describing the Big Bang."}, {"do": "Gravity pulled the dust and gas together, and the centre became denser and hotter.", "why": "Gravity is the cause. Saying the temperature rose shows what happened at the centre."}, {"do": "The centre became a protostar. When it was hot enough, hydrogen nuclei fused to form helium nuclei and released energy, so the Sun formed.", "why": "This names fusion as the process that made the Sun a star."}, {"do": "The dust and gas left over further out were pulled together by gravity and formed the planets.", "why": "This says where the planets came from: the leftover material."}], "answer": "A nebula of dust and gas was pulled together by gravity. The centre became hotter and denser, forming a protostar. When it was hot enough, hydrogen nuclei fused into helium, releasing energy, and the Sun formed. Leftover material clumped together by gravity to form the planets."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 of the answer mention gravity?", "o": ["Gravity is the energy released when hydrogen nuclei join in the centre", "Gravity pushes the cloud outwards, so the dust spreads into planets", "Gravity pulls the particles inwards, so the centre heats up", "Gravity was left over from the Big Bang and held the planets in place"], "a": 2, "f": ["This confuses gravity with fusion. Gravity is a force. Fusion is the process that releases energy, and it starts later.", "Gravity pulls inwards. It does not push the cloud outwards. Planets form from material pulled into clumps.", "Right. Gravity pulls the particles inwards. They speed up and collide more often, so the centre heats up.", "The Big Bang is a different event. It does not give the Solar System its gravity, and it is not part of the formation story."]}, "faded": {"task": "Describe what the Solar System contains and how the objects move. (4 marks)", "given": ["The Sun is a star at the centre of the Solar System.", "Eight planets orbit the Sun."], "q": "Which statement should come next to add a correct point about moons?", "o": ["Moons are small stars that orbit the planets and give out their own light.", "Moons are natural satellites. Each one orbits a planet.", "Moons are dwarf planets that orbit the Sun between the planets.", "Moons orbit the Sun, and the planets orbit the moons."], "a": 1, "f": ["Moons are not stars. They do not make their own light. They reflect light from the Sun.", "Right. A moon is a natural satellite, and it orbits a planet.", "Dwarf planets, such as Pluto, orbit the Sun. Moons orbit planets. They are different kinds of object.", "This gets the orbits the wrong way round. Planets orbit the Sun, and moons orbit planets."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the Big Bang with the formation of the Solar System. The Big Bang started the universe. The Solar System formed much later, from a nebula of dust and gas pulled together by gravity.", "recap": "Gravity pulled a nebula of dust and gas together to form the Sun, and the leftover material formed the planets."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.8.1.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 questions ask you to describe a star's life cycle in the right order and explain what happens at each stage.", "hook": {"q": "A huge cloud of gas and dust floats in space. Which force pulls it together to start forming a star?", "o": ["Magnetism between the dust grains", "Friction between the gas particles", "Gravity between the particles", "The nuclear force from fusion"], "a": 2, "f": ["Magnetism is not what pulls a star's material together. The pull between all the particles is gravity.", "Friction needs surfaces to rub against each other. It does not pull a whole cloud together.", "Correct. Every particle attracts every other through gravity, so the cloud slowly collapses inwards.", "Fusion cannot be the cause. It only starts after the cloud has been squashed and heated by gravity."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Birth of a star", "b": "A star starts as a **nebula**: a huge cloud of dust and gas. **Gravity** pulls the cloud into clumps. As a clump shrinks, its particles collide more often, so its temperature rises. This hot, dense ball is a **protostar**. When it is hot enough, hydrogen nuclei fuse and a star is born."}, {"h": "Stable main sequence", "b": "In a **main sequence star**, hydrogen nuclei fuse to make helium and release energy. The star stays the same size because two effects are balanced. **Gravity** pulls inwards. The outward **pressure** from the fusion energy pushes outwards. This stable stage lasts billions of years."}, {"h": "Sun-sized stars end", "b": "When the hydrogen in a Sun-sized star runs low, the star swells into a **red giant**. It then shrinks into a **white dwarf**, which is small, hot and dim. Over time the white dwarf cools and fades into a **black dwarf**, which gives out no light."}, {"h": "Massive stars and heavy elements", "b": "Stars much more massive than the Sun swell into **red supergiants**, then explode as a **supernova**. The core left behind is a neutron star or a black hole. Fusion makes elements only up to iron. A supernova makes elements **heavier than iron** and scatters them into space."}], "example": {"task": "Describe what happens to a star much more massive than the Sun after its main sequence stage, and how this can lead to new stars and planets containing heavy elements.", "steps": [{"do": "When the hydrogen runs low, the star swells into a red supergiant.", "why": "A star far more massive than the Sun becomes a supergiant, not an ordinary red giant."}, {"do": "Fusion inside the star makes elements up to iron.", "why": "Stars make all the elements up to iron by fusion. Heavier ones need something more violent."}, {"do": "The star then explodes as a supernova, and elements heavier than iron are made in the explosion.", "why": "For AQA, elements heavier than iron are made in a supernova. This is a key mark."}, {"do": "The core left behind becomes a neutron star or, if massive enough, a black hole.", "why": "This is the end state of a massive star. A white dwarf is the Sun-sized route, so it is wrong here."}, {"do": "The explosion scatters the elements into space, where they join a nebula. New stars and planets form from it.", "why": "This links the supernova to the heavy elements found in later stars, planets and us."}], "answer": "The star becomes a red supergiant, then explodes as a supernova. Elements up to iron come from fusion, and heavier ones form in the explosion. A neutron star or black hole is left. The scattered elements join a new nebula and form new stars and planets."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer end with the material being scattered into space?", "o": ["Because the star must lose mass before it can become a black hole", "Because heavy elements must be spread into a new nebula to reach new stars and planets", "Because the scattered material is what makes the neutron star", "Because fusion only produces iron once the material has left the star"], "a": 1, "f": ["Losing mass is not the point of this step. The step explains how heavy elements end up in new stars and planets.", "Correct. The elements are made inside or by the dying star, so they must be thrown out to join a nebula that forms new stars and planets.", "The neutron star is made from the core, which stays behind. The scattered material is the outer part.", "Fusion in the star makes elements up to iron while the star is still in one piece. Scattering is not needed for that."]}, "faded": {"task": "Describe the life cycle of a star the same size as the Sun.", "given": ["A nebula, a cloud of dust and gas, is pulled together by gravity.", "Its particles collide more as it shrinks, so it heats up and becomes a protostar."], "q": "What is the next stage to write?", "o": ["It swells into a red giant as its hydrogen runs out.", "It explodes as a supernova and leaves a neutron star.", "Hydrogen nuclei split apart, releasing energy, and it becomes a white dwarf.", "It gets hot enough for hydrogen to fuse into helium, becoming a main sequence star."], "a": 3, "f": ["This skips a stage. The protostar must first become a main sequence star, which is the longest stage.", "A supernova belongs to much more massive stars. A Sun-sized star never explodes this way.", "Stars release energy by fusion, where nuclei join. They do not split. A white dwarf also comes much later.", "Correct. When the protostar is hot enough, hydrogen nuclei fuse to make helium. This releases energy and starts the main sequence stage."]}, "trap": "Sending a Sun-sized star to a supernova. The Sun ends as a red giant, then a white dwarf, then a black dwarf. Supernovae, neutron stars and black holes belong to much more massive stars.", "recap": "A Sun-sized star fades to a white then black dwarf; a much more massive star explodes as a supernova, making elements heavier than iron."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.8.1.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Space physics is on Paper 2, and orbit questions often ask you to explain how speed, velocity, radius and gravity are linked, in the right order.", "hook": {"q": "A satellite is orbiting Earth. Suddenly Earth's gravity switches off. What does the satellite do?", "o": ["It stops dead where it is", "It moves off in a straight line at constant speed", "It falls straight down to Earth", "It carries on moving in the same circle"], "a": 1, "f": ["Nothing is acting to stop it. With no force, an object keeps moving at its current speed and direction.", "Right. With no force it keeps its velocity, so it moves in a straight line. Gravity is what bends its path into an orbit.", "Falling towards Earth needs gravity pulling it. With gravity off, there is no force to make it fall.", "A circular path needs a force towards the centre all the time. Without gravity, nothing provides it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Gravity points to the centre", "b": "A planet, moon or satellite stays in orbit because **gravity** pulls it towards the centre of its orbit. The Sun's gravity acts on the planets. A planet's gravity acts on its moons and on **artificial satellites**. Without this **force**, the object would leave in a straight line."}, {"h": "Constant speed, changing velocity", "b": "In a circular orbit the **speed** stays constant. But **velocity** is speed in a given direction, and the direction keeps changing. So the velocity changes all the time. A changing velocity means the object is accelerating, and that needs a resultant force. That force is gravity."}, {"h": "Radius and orbital speed", "b": "Gravity is stronger closer to the object it orbits. So at a smaller **radius** the pull is greater, and a stable orbit needs a greater **orbital speed**. At a larger radius the pull is weaker, so the speed is lower. If an orbiting object's speed changes, its radius must change too."}, {"h": "Smaller orbit, shorter period", "b": "The **time period** is the time for **one full orbit**. A smaller orbit is a shorter path, and the object travels faster along it. Both effects shorten the time period. So planets closer to the Sun have shorter years than planets further away."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a satellite in a circular orbit has a constant speed but a changing velocity, and why a satellite in a lower orbit travels faster.", "steps": [{"do": "Earth's gravity pulls the satellite towards the centre of its orbit.", "why": "Gravity is the force that keeps any orbiting object in orbit."}, {"do": "This force acts at right angles to the motion, so it changes the satellite's direction but not its speed.", "why": "The pull is sideways on to the path, so it does not speed the satellite up or slow it down."}, {"do": "Velocity has direction as well as size. The direction keeps changing, so the velocity changes even though the speed is constant.", "why": "This is the link between constant speed and changing velocity that the mark scheme looks for."}, {"do": "At a lower orbit the satellite is closer to Earth, so gravity is stronger.", "why": "Gravitational pull gets greater as the distance gets smaller."}, {"do": "A stronger pull needs a greater speed to keep a stable orbit at that radius. This also gives a shorter time period.", "why": "Smaller radius means greater speed, so one orbit takes less time."}], "answer": "Gravity pulls the satellite towards Earth's centre. It changes direction, not speed, so speed is constant but velocity changes. A lower orbit has stronger gravity, so the satellite needs a greater speed to stay in a stable orbit."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the velocity changes when the speed is constant?", "o": ["Because gravity slowly increases the satellite's speed on each orbit", "Because velocity depends on the satellite's mass, which keeps changing", "Because velocity includes direction, and the direction keeps changing", "Because speed and velocity mean the same, so the speed must change"], "a": 2, "f": ["Gravity here changes direction only. If the speed rose each orbit, the orbit would not stay stable.", "Velocity does not depend on mass, and the satellite's mass stays the same. Direction is the key point.", "Correct. Velocity is speed in a given direction. The direction changes all the time, so the velocity does too.", "Speed and velocity are different. Speed has size only, but velocity also has direction."]}, "faded": {"task": "Planet P orbits the Sun at a larger radius than planet Q. Both orbits are circular. Compare their orbital speeds and time periods.", "given": ["The Sun's gravity provides the force towards the centre for both planets.", "At P's larger radius, the Sun's gravity is weaker than at Q's radius."], "q": "What is the next step in the answer?", "o": ["So P needs a smaller speed than Q, and it has a longer time period.", "So P needs a greater speed than Q, or the Sun would pull it inwards.", "So P and Q have the same speed, because the same Sun pulls on both.", "So P needs a greater speed than Q, because its orbit is a longer path."], "a": 0, "f": ["Correct. A weaker pull means a lower speed gives a stable orbit. A lower speed on a longer path gives a longer time period.", "This reverses the link. A weaker pull needs less speed, not more. A greater speed belongs to the smaller orbit.", "Orbital speed depends on the radius, not only on which body is pulling. Different radii need different speeds.", "A longer path does not make the orbit faster. The speed is set by the strength of gravity at that radius."]}, "trap": "Thinking a satellite closer to Earth moves slower. Gravity is stronger at a smaller radius, so a stable orbit there needs a greater speed. Remember: smaller radius, greater speed, shorter time period.", "recap": "Gravity provides the force towards the centre; a smaller orbit needs a greater speed and has a shorter time period."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.8.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Red-shift often appears in Paper 2 as an 'explain' question linking what astronomers observe to an expanding universe and the Big Bang.", "hook": {"q": "An ambulance siren sounds lower in pitch once it has passed you and is driving away. What has happened to the sound waves reaching you?", "o": ["They have a shorter wavelength and a higher frequency", "They have a longer wavelength and a lower frequency", "They travel more slowly through the air", "They have a longer wavelength and a higher frequency"], "a": 1, "f": ["Shorter wavelength and higher frequency is what you hear as the ambulance comes towards you, not away.", "Right. A source moving away stretches the waves. The wavelength gets longer and the frequency gets lower, so the pitch drops.", "The speed of sound in air stays the same. Only the wavelength and frequency change because the source is moving.", "Wavelength and frequency go opposite ways when speed is fixed. A longer wavelength means a lower frequency, not a higher one."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What red-shift is", "b": "Light from a galaxy moving away from us has a **longer wavelength** than the same light made in a lab. Its spectrum is shifted towards the red end. This is **red-shift**. The frequency is lower. It is the same kind of effect as the siren moving away."}, {"h": "Further means faster", "b": "Light from almost all galaxies is red-shifted, so they are moving **away** from us. Light from more distant galaxies has a **bigger red-shift**. A bigger shift means a **faster** speed of recession. So the further away a galaxy is, the faster it moves away."}, {"h": "An expanding universe", "b": "Galaxies are **moving apart** in every direction, and the furthest move fastest. The best explanation is that the **universe is expanding**. This is not one galaxy moving off. It is the whole universe getting bigger."}, {"h": "The Big Bang", "b": "If the universe is expanding now, it was smaller in the past. The **Big Bang** theory says everything began in one **very small region** that was extremely **hot and dense**, and has been expanding ever since. Red-shift is evidence for this theory."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how the red-shift of light from distant galaxies is evidence that the universe is expanding and that it began with the Big Bang.", "steps": [{"do": "The light from distant galaxies has a longer wavelength than the same light measured in a lab. It is shifted towards the red end of the spectrum.", "why": "This is the observation. You must state it first, as the rest of the answer depends on it."}, {"do": "This shows that the galaxies are moving away from us.", "why": "A source moving away stretches its waves, like the siren, so a longer wavelength means moving away."}, {"do": "Light from more distant galaxies shows a bigger red-shift, so more distant galaxies are moving away faster.", "why": "This is the pattern in the data. It links distance to speed."}, {"do": "Galaxies are moving apart in all directions, so the universe is expanding.", "why": "Only an expanding universe explains every distant galaxy receding, with the furthest fastest."}, {"do": "If it is expanding now, it must have been smaller in the past. This supports the Big Bang: the universe began from a very small, hot, dense region.", "why": "Running the expansion backwards leads to the Big Bang theory."}], "answer": "Light from distant galaxies has a longer wavelength than expected (red-shift), so they are moving away. More distant galaxies have bigger red-shifts, so they move away faster. This shows the universe is expanding. It must once have been smaller, supporting the Big Bang."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer say that more distant galaxies have a bigger red-shift?", "o": ["It shows distant galaxies are closer to us than the near ones", "It shows that distant galaxies emit light with shorter wavelengths", "It shows that the Earth is at the centre of the universe", "It shows speed increases with distance, so the whole universe is expanding"], "a": 3, "f": ["Bigger red-shift does not mean closer. The pattern links larger shift to greater distance and greater speed.", "Red-shift means longer wavelengths, not shorter. Shorter wavelengths would be a shift the other way, towards us.", "Every galaxy moves away from every other, so no place is special. The pattern shows expansion, not that Earth is central.", "Right. A bigger shift means a faster recession. Speed rising with distance fits the whole universe expanding, not just one galaxy moving."]}, "faded": {"task": "A spectral line is 486 nm in a lab. From a nearby galaxy it is 490 nm; from a distant galaxy it is 510 nm. Explain what this shows about the universe and its past.", "given": ["Both wavelengths are longer than 486 nm, so both galaxies are red-shifted and moving away from us.", "The distant galaxy's shift (24 nm) is bigger than the nearby galaxy's (4 nm), so the distant galaxy is moving away faster."], "q": "What is the next point that links this evidence to the Big Bang?", "o": ["So the universe is expanding, and was once a tiny, hot, dense region.", "So the universe is shrinking, because the light waves are stretched on the way.", "So the universe has always been the same size, and the galaxies just drift about.", "So the Earth must be at the centre, because everything moves away from us."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Expansion now means a smaller universe in the past, leading back to a very small, hot, dense start: the Big Bang.", "Stretched waves and galaxies moving apart show expansion, not shrinking. A shrinking universe would show blue-shift.", "A universe of constant size does not fit galaxies receding, with the furthest moving fastest. The evidence points to expansion.", "Every galaxy would see the others moving away from it. The pattern does not make Earth special, and it does not link to the Big Bang."]}, "trap": "Mixing up directions. Red-shift means a longer wavelength, a lower frequency and a galaxy moving away from us. Never write 'towards', and never say the wavelength gets shorter.", "recap": "Distant galaxies show red-shift, and the further away they are, the bigger it is, so the universe is expanding, supporting the Big Bang."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.1.2.1b", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 1 often asks you to explain Christian beliefs about Jesus and salvation, such as in 4-mark 'explain two beliefs' questions, so you need the key terms.", "hook": {"q": "Christians say Jesus 'saves' people. What do you think they believe he saves them from?", "o": ["From Roman rule over their country", "From ever having to die a physical death", "From sin and the way it separates people from God", "From having to follow any religious laws"], "a": 2, "f": ["Some people in Jesus' time hoped for a political rescuer. Christian teaching on salvation is about sin and God, not freedom from Rome.", "Christians believe people still die physically. Salvation is about forgiveness and eternal life with God, not avoiding death.", "Right. Christians believe sin damages the relationship between people and God. Salvation means being saved from sin and its consequences.", "Jesus taught that people should follow God's commands. Christians do not believe salvation means having no laws to follow."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Jesus: God made human", "b": "Christians believe Jesus is the **Son of God**: God the Son, the second person of the Trinity. He became a human being. This is the **incarnation**, meaning 'made flesh'. Christians believe he was fully God and fully human, so he can bring God and people together."}, {"h": "Sin and salvation", "b": "**Sin** is acting against God's will. Many Christians believe everyone inherits a tendency to sin from Adam and Eve's disobedience. This is **original sin**. Sin spoils people's relationship with God. **Salvation** means being saved from sin and its consequences, so that the relationship is restored."}, {"h": "Death and resurrection", "b": "Christians believe Jesus was crucified and that his death was a sacrifice for human sin. This brings **atonement**: people are made 'at one' with God. On the third day, Easter Sunday, Jesus rose from the dead. This is the **resurrection**. Christians believe it shows death is defeated and promises eternal life."}, {"h": "Ascension and grace", "b": "Christians believe that 40 days after Easter, Jesus returned to heaven. This is the **ascension**. Salvation is God's free gift, called **grace**. Lutherans say people receive it through **faith** alone, not good works. Catholics also teach salvation by grace, but stress faith shown in good works."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the death and resurrection of Jesus matter to Christians' beliefs about salvation.", "steps": [{"do": "Christians believe all people sin, and sin separates them from God.", "why": "It states the problem. Salvation only makes sense if there is something people need saving from."}, {"do": "Jesus, God's Son made human, died on the cross as a sacrifice for human sin.", "why": "It links the incarnation to the crucifixion and says what the death was for."}, {"do": "This is atonement: it puts people right with God, making them 'at one' with him again.", "why": "It uses the key term and explains what it means, rather than just naming it."}, {"do": "Christians believe his resurrection shows that death is defeated, so believers can have eternal life.", "why": "It shows the outcome. Christians see the resurrection as a sign that the sacrifice worked."}], "answer": "Christians believe all people sin, and sin separates them from God. Jesus, God's Son made human, died on the cross as a sacrifice for sin. This is atonement: it puts people right with God. Christians believe his resurrection shows that death is defeated, so believers can have eternal life."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer start with sin before it mentions Jesus' death?", "o": ["Sin is the problem Jesus' death solves, so it shows why people need salvation.", "Sin is the result of the crucifixion, so it comes first in the story.", "Every exam answer on salvation must begin with a definition of sin.", "Christians believe only people who have sinned badly need salvation."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. A good explanation starts with the problem. Christians believe sin separates people from God, and the death of Jesus is the answer to it.", "This reverses cause and effect. Christians believe sin came before the crucifixion, and the crucifixion was God's response to it.", "No exam rule says this. Sin goes first because it makes the later points make sense, and you earn marks for linking points.", "Christians believe everyone sins and needs salvation, because of original sin. It is not only for people who have sinned badly."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why the ascension of Jesus is important to Christians.", "given": ["Christians believe that 40 days after Easter, Jesus returned to heaven. This is the ascension.", "The question asks why it matters, so the next point must say what the ascension means for Christians."], "q": "Which point should come next?", "o": ["It is when Jesus rose from the dead on Easter Sunday, proving he is God's Son.", "It shows Jesus' work ended on earth, so he now has no part in people's salvation.", "It is when God became human in Jesus, so Christians can be forgiven.", "Christians believe Jesus is with God in heaven and will come again at the end of time."], "a": 3, "f": ["This describes the resurrection, not the ascension. The resurrection happened on Easter Sunday. The ascension came 40 days later.", "Christians do not believe this. They believe Jesus is still with God in heaven, so the ascension does not end his part in salvation.", "This describes the incarnation, when God became human. It comes at the start of Jesus' life, not at its end.", "Right. Christians believe Jesus is now with God in heaven and will return. This explains why the ascension matters."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the Ascension with the Resurrection. The Resurrection is rising from death at Easter. The Ascension is going up to heaven 40 days later. Also, Lutherans believe people are saved by grace through faith, not good works.", "recap": "Christians believe Jesus, God made human, died to atone for sin, rose again and ascended, so people are saved by God's grace."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.1.2.2a", "kind": "idea", "why": "Worship, sacraments, pilgrimage and festivals are tested on the Christianity paper, often as 'Explain two…' questions worth 4 marks, so you need facts you can develop.", "hook": {"q": "Water is used in baptism to welcome someone into the Church. What do you think it stands for?", "o": ["Water is cheap and easy to find, so any church can use it", "Water washes things clean, so it is a sign of a fresh start", "Water is needed to make the bread and wine for communion", "Water shows that the person has paid to join the Church"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Churches use water because of what it symbolises, not because it is convenient. Think about what water does to dirty things.", "Right. Washing makes things clean, so water is a natural sign of a fresh start and of welcome into the Church.", "Wrong. This mixes baptism up with the Eucharist, which uses bread and wine. Baptism uses water as a sign in itself.", "Wrong. Nobody pays to be baptised. Baptism is a welcome into the Church, and water is a symbol of cleansing, not a fee."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Worship and prayer", "b": "**Liturgical worship** follows a set order and set words, as in a Catholic Mass. **Non-liturgical worship** has no fixed script. It may use songs, a sermon and spontaneous prayer. Private worship happens alone, at home. Prayer can use **set prayers**, such as the Lord's Prayer, or the worshipper's own words."}, {"h": "The sacraments", "b": "A **sacrament** is an outward sign of God's grace. In **baptism**, water welcomes a person into the Church and signals a new start. Catholics and Anglicans usually baptise babies. Baptists baptise believers who choose it themselves. In the **Eucharist**, bread and wine recall Jesus' Last Supper."}, {"h": "Pilgrimage: Lourdes and Iona", "b": "A **pilgrimage** is a journey to a holy place for religious reasons. At **Lourdes** in France, Catholics believe Mary appeared to Bernadette in 1858. Pilgrims seek healing and stronger faith. **Iona** is a Scottish island linked to St Columba, who founded a monastery there in 563. Visitors go for prayer, quiet and community."}, {"h": "Christmas and Easter", "b": "**Christmas** celebrates the birth of Jesus. Christians prepare in Advent and often attend Midnight Mass or carol services. **Easter** remembers the crucifixion and resurrection. Holy Week includes Maundy Thursday and Good Friday, and Easter Sunday is the day of joy. Many Christians give things up in **Lent** beforehand."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Catholics go on pilgrimage to Lourdes. [4 marks]", "steps": [{"do": "Reason 1: Many Catholics go to Lourdes to seek healing.", "why": "States a clear reason. This earns the first mark."}, {"do": "They bathe in or drink water from the spring linked to Bernadette's visions in 1858. They pray for a cure or for strength to cope with illness.", "why": "Develops the reason with what pilgrims do and why it matters. This earns the second mark."}, {"do": "Reason 2: Pilgrims go to strengthen their faith.", "why": "A different reason from the first, so it can earn its own marks."}, {"do": "Joining prayers, the rosary and candlelit processions with thousands of others helps them feel closer to God and Mary.", "why": "Develops the second reason with a practice and its effect on belief. This earns the fourth mark."}], "answer": "Many Catholics go to Lourdes to seek healing. They bathe in or drink water from the spring linked to Bernadette's visions in 1858 and pray for a cure or strength to cope with illness. Also, pilgrims go to strengthen their faith. Prayers and candlelit processions with thousands of others help them feel closer to God and Mary."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 mention the spring and what pilgrims do there?", "o": ["It is a second, separate reason, so it earns a mark of its own", "Exam answers need as many facts and dates as possible to score", "It develops the reason by saying what pilgrims do and why, earning the extra mark", "It repeats the first sentence so that the answer looks longer"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Step 2 is not a new reason. It extends Reason 1. The second reason does not start until step 3.", "Wrong. Marks go to facts that explain the reason, not to piling up details. Each detail must link back to the point.", "Right. A 4-mark 'explain two' answer gives each reason a development. Step 2 shows what pilgrims do and why it matters.", "Wrong. Step 2 adds new information about the spring and prayer. Repeating a point in other words earns no extra mark."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Christians celebrate Christmas. [4 marks]", "given": ["Reason 1: It celebrates the birth of Jesus, the Son of God."], "q": "Which sentence best develops Reason 1?", "o": ["Christmas is a bank holiday, so families have time off to celebrate together.", "This means Christians celebrate the birth of Jesus every year at Christmas time.", "Jesus rose from the dead on this day, so it is the most joyful festival.", "Christians believe God became human in Jesus, so it shows God's love for the world."], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. A bank holiday is not a religious reason. Development must explain why the festival matters to Christians' beliefs.", "Wrong. This only repeats the point in other words. A development must add why the birth matters, not restate it.", "Wrong. This mixes up Christmas with Easter, which remembers the resurrection. The facts must match the festival.", "Right. It links the birth to the Christian belief in the incarnation and shows why it matters, which is what earns the development mark."]}, "trap": "Stopping after naming a point. 'They want healing' earns one mark. Add a development: what Christians do and why it matters to their faith. Do not mix up festivals or sacraments.", "recap": "In a 4-mark 'explain two' answer, state each point, then develop it with what Christians do and why it matters to their faith."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.1.2.2b", "kind": "idea", "why": "This topic comes up in your Christianity exam as short 'explain' questions, where you earn marks for clear reasons and a Bible teaching to back them up.", "hook": {"q": "A church opens a food bank for anyone in the town, whatever they believe. What is the most likely reason?", "o": ["It is the only way a church can raise money for repairs to its building", "The law says every church must run a food bank in its area", "Christians believe that caring for people in need is part of following Jesus", "People must join the church before they are allowed to take any food"], "a": 2, "f": ["Food banks give food away. They are not a fundraising scheme. The motive is Christian teaching about caring for others.", "No law forces churches to run food banks. They choose to, because of their beliefs.", "Right. Christians believe Jesus taught them to love their neighbour and to help those in need. Food banks put that teaching into practice.", "Churches do not make people join before they get help. Food banks are open to everyone, so this confuses help with pressure."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Local church work", "b": "Many churches work for their **local community**. Some run **food banks** that give food to people who cannot afford it. **Street Pastors** patrol town centres at night to help vulnerable people. Christians do this because they believe Jesus taught them to love their neighbour. In Matthew 25 he says helping the needy is helping him."}, {"h": "Mission and evangelism", "b": "**Mission** is the work of spreading Christian beliefs and caring for others. **Evangelism** is sharing the Christian faith to bring others to it. In the **Great Commission** Jesus says, 'Go and make disciples of all nations' (Matthew 28). Many Christians believe this is a duty. It also helps the Church to grow."}, {"h": "Reconciliation and persecution", "b": "**Reconciliation** means bringing together people who are in conflict. Christians believe Jesus' death reconciled people with God, so they try to make peace between others. They also believe the worldwide Church is one body. They pray for, speak up for and support Christians facing **persecution**, which means cruel treatment for their faith."}, {"h": "Charities ending poverty", "b": "**Christian Aid**, **CAFOD** and **Tearfund** are Christian charities that work to end world poverty. CAFOD is the official aid agency of the Catholic Church in England and Wales. They give emergency help after disasters. They also fund long-term projects, such as clean water and farming."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two reasons why evangelism is important to Christians. Refer to a source of wisdom in your answer. (5 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Reason 1: Evangelism is important because Jesus told his followers to spread the faith.", "why": "A clear first reason in one short sentence."}, {"do": "In the Great Commission, Jesus says, 'Go and make disciples of all nations' (Matthew 28).", "why": "This is the source of wisdom the question asks for. It is named and backs up reason 1."}, {"do": "Reason 2: Many Christians believe that faith in Jesus leads to salvation, so they want others to have it.", "why": "A different reason, not a repeat of reason 1."}, {"do": "They share their faith out of love for other people, because they want everyone to receive what they see as the greatest gift.", "why": "This develops the reason. Developed reasons earn more marks than bare statements."}], "answer": "Evangelism is important because Jesus told his followers to spread the faith. In the Great Commission he says, 'Go and make disciples of all nations.' It is also important because many Christians believe faith in Jesus leads to salvation, so they share it out of love for others."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 quote Matthew 28 after stating the first reason?", "o": ["The question asks for a source of wisdom, and the quotation backs up the reason", "A quotation on its own earns the marks, so no reason is needed", "It is the only Bible passage that mentions spreading the faith", "It shows that evangelism is the same thing as helping the poor"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. The question says to refer to a source of wisdom. The quotation is linked to the reason, so it supports it.", "A quotation alone does not explain anything. You still need a reason, and the source should support it.", "Other passages could be used too. Nothing in the question requires this one, so 'the only one' is wrong.", "Evangelism is sharing the faith. Helping the poor is a separate kind of work. Mixing them up loses marks."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Christians support work to end world poverty. Refer to a source of wisdom. (5 marks)", "given": ["Reason 1: Christians believe they must love their neighbour as themselves.", "Source: Jesus told the parable of the Good Samaritan, who helped a stranger in need."], "q": "Which is the best way to start the second reason?", "o": ["Christian Aid, CAFOD and Tearfund are large charities that work in many countries.", "Christians support Oxfam because it is the biggest charity fighting world poverty.", "The Bible says the poor will always be with us, so Christians think nothing can change.", "Reason 2: Jesus taught that helping those in need is helping him, so it serves God."], "a": 3, "f": ["This only describes the charities. It gives no reason why Christians support them, so it earns no explanation marks.", "Oxfam is not a Christian charity. This also gives no Christian reason, only a description.", "This twists a saying into a reason to do nothing. It does not explain why Christians act, and it clashes with their teaching to help.", "Right. It is a new reason, different from loving your neighbour. It can be followed by a source such as Matthew 25."]}, "trap": "Naming Oxfam, or another non-Christian charity, as a Christian one. Learn the three main examples: Christian Aid, CAFOD and Tearfund. Do not mix up evangelism (sharing faith) with charity work.", "recap": "Christians help the needy because Jesus said it is helping him; evangelism shares the faith; Christian Aid, CAFOD and Tearfund tackle poverty."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.1.5.1b", "kind": "idea", "why": "AQA asks short questions on prophets, holy books and Imams, and you can use them as evidence in 12-mark answers about Muslim authority.", "hook": {"q": "You must pass an important message to people you will never meet. What best makes them trust it came from you?", "o": ["A trusted messenger, and a written record that stays the same", "Many people retelling it in their own words over the years", "A messenger who adds ideas of their own to improve it", "A short summary passed on with no named sender"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. A trusted messenger and an unchanged record give a message authority. Muslims use the same logic for prophets and the Qur'an.", "Retelling in new words lets the message drift. Authority depends on the message staying the same.", "Added ideas change the message. A messenger earns trust by passing it on faithfully.", "With no named sender, nobody can check where the message came from. That weakens its authority."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Prophets bring Allah's guidance", "b": "Muslims believe Allah sent **prophets** to every people. This is **risalah**. Prophets include Adam, Ibrahim, Musa, Dawud, Isa and Muhammad. They teach that Allah is one and show people how to live. Their authority comes from Allah, not from themselves. Muslims believe they were human, not divine."}, {"h": "Muhammad: the final prophet", "b": "Muslims believe Muhammad is the **Seal of the Prophets**. He was the last prophet, so no prophet comes after him. Allah's message is complete. Muslims believe **Jibril** (Gabriel) revealed the Qur'an to him over about 23 years. His life is an example to follow."}, {"h": "The holy books", "b": "Muslims believe Allah sent books through prophets. The Sahifah (Scrolls) went to Ibrahim, the **Tawrat** to Musa, the **Zabur** to Dawud, the **Injil** to Isa and the Qur'an to Muhammad. Muslims believe the earlier books were changed or lost. They believe the Qur'an is unchanged, so it has the highest authority."}, {"h": "Shi'a belief: the Imams", "b": "Shi'a Muslims believe Allah appointed **Imams** to lead the community after Muhammad, starting with **Ali**. They believe the Imams are **sinless** and guide Muslims in understanding Islam. Imams are not prophets. Muhammad is still the final prophet. Sunni Muslims do not believe leaders were appointed by God in this way."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Muhammad has authority for Muslims. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "First reason: Muslims believe Allah chose Muhammad as a prophet, and Jibril revealed the Qur'an to him.", "why": "A simple, accurate reason earns the first mark."}, {"do": "This means he passed on Allah's own words, not his own ideas, so his teaching carries Allah's authority.", "why": "Explaining the first reason further earns the second mark."}, {"do": "Second reason: Muslims believe Muhammad is the Seal of the Prophets, the last prophet Allah sent.", "why": "A second, different reason earns the third mark."}, {"do": "So no prophet comes after him. Allah's message is complete, and Muslims follow his example (Sunnah) as final guidance.", "why": "Developing the second reason earns the fourth mark."}], "answer": "Muslims believe Allah chose Muhammad as a prophet and Jibril revealed the Qur'an to him, so he passed on Allah's own words. Also, he is the Seal of the Prophets, the last prophet, so Allah's message is complete and Muslims follow his example as final guidance."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention that Muhammad was the Seal of the Prophets?", "o": ["It shows he was more important than Isa and Musa", "It shows his message is final, so no later prophet is needed", "It shows he wrote the Qur'an himself", "It shows he was the first prophet Allah sent"], "a": 1, "f": ["Muslims honour all prophets. 'Seal' is about being last, not about ranking him above the others.", "Right. 'Seal' means the end of the line. Muslims believe Allah's message is complete, so his guidance is final.", "Muslims believe the Qur'an is Allah's words revealed through Jibril, not Muhammad's own writing.", "The first prophet in Islam was Adam. 'Seal' means last, not first."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Shi'a Muslims give authority to the Imams. (4 marks)", "given": ["First reason: Shi'a Muslims believe Allah appointed the Imams, starting with Ali, to lead after Muhammad.", "This means their leadership comes from Allah, not from a choice made by people."], "q": "Which sentence best gives the second reason?", "o": ["They believe the Imams are prophets who bring new revelation after Muhammad.", "They believe the Imams were chosen by a vote of the community.", "They believe the Imams are sinless, so their guidance on Islam can be trusted.", "They believe the Imams replaced the Qur'an as the main holy book."], "a": 2, "f": ["This is the trap. Muhammad is the final prophet, and Shi'a do not see Imams as prophets.", "Choosing leaders by community agreement is the Sunni view of early leadership. Shi'a believe Allah appointed the Imams.", "Right. Being sinless is why Shi'a trust the Imams to guide the community and explain Islam.", "Shi'a Muslims still treat the Qur'an as Allah's word. Imams guide people in understanding it."]}, "trap": "Calling Imams prophets, or mixing up the books. Learn the pairs: Sahifah to Ibrahim, Tawrat to Musa, Zabur to Dawud, Injil to Isa, Qur'an to Muhammad. Muhammad is the final prophet.", "recap": "Prophets bring Allah's guidance, the Qur'an is his final unchanged book, and Shi'a believe sinless Imams, starting with Ali, guide the community."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.1.5.2a", "kind": "idea", "why": "AQA asks you to describe and explain Muslim practices in short 2 to 5 mark questions, so you need both the Sunni and Shi'a lists.", "hook": {"q": "Muslims all over the world pray facing the same place, the Kaaba in Makkah. What is the most likely reason?", "o": ["Prayer is only allowed to be performed inside Makkah itself", "It shows they are united in worshipping one God, wherever they are", "Each country's government tells its Muslims which way to face", "The sun always rises in the direction of Makkah and the Kaaba"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Muslims pray all over the world, not only in Makkah. The direction (qiblah) matters, not the place.", "Right. Facing the Kaaba together shows unity: one God, one direction. This links to the idea of the worldwide Muslim community.", "Wrong. The direction comes from Islamic teaching, not from governments. It is the same for every Muslim.", "Wrong. The sun always rises in the east, but Makkah lies in different directions from different places. The qiblah has nothing to do with the sun."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Two lists of duties", "b": "Sunni Muslims follow the **Five Pillars**: Shahadah, Salah, Zakat, Sawm and Hajj. Shi'a Muslims follow the **Ten Obligatory Acts**. Salah, Sawm, Zakat and Hajj are in both lists. Shi'a also declare the Shahadah, but it is not one of their Ten."}, {"h": "The Shahadah", "b": "The **Shahadah** is the declaration of faith: 'There is no god but Allah, and Muhammad is the messenger of Allah.' Saying it sincerely makes a person a Muslim. It is heard in the call to prayer and in Salah. Many Shi'a add that Ali is the **wali** (friend) of Allah."}, {"h": "Salah: daily prayer", "b": "**Salah** is prayer five times a day: Fajr, Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha. Muslims first do **wudu** (ritual washing) to be clean. They then face the **qiblah**, the direction of the Kaaba in Makkah. Many Shi'a combine prayers, so they pray at three times of day."}, {"h": "The extra Shi'a acts", "b": "Shi'a add six acts to the shared four: **Khums** (a fifth of yearly surplus income given), **Jihad** (struggle in Allah's way), Amr-bil-Maroof (encouraging good), Nahi Anil Munkar (discouraging evil), Tawalla (loyalty to Allah and the Imams) and Tabarra (turning from Allah's enemies)."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why Salah is important to Muslims. [4 marks]", "steps": [{"do": "Salah is important because it is a duty from Allah. It is a Pillar for Sunni Muslims and an Obligatory Act for Shi'a Muslims.", "why": "First reason, stated clearly. Naming both traditions shows accurate knowledge."}, {"do": "Muslims pray at set times (Fajr, Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha), so Allah is remembered throughout the day.", "why": "This develops the reason by saying what the duty means in daily life."}, {"do": "Salah is also important because it unites Muslims. They all face the qiblah, towards the Kaaba in Makkah.", "why": "Second, different reason, linked with 'also'. The qiblah is a precise detail."}, {"do": "Praying together, for example at Friday prayers in the mosque, makes Muslims feel part of one worldwide community.", "why": "This develops the second reason by showing the effect on Muslims."}], "answer": "Salah is a duty from Allah, a Pillar for Sunni and an Obligatory Act for Shi'a. Praying at set times keeps Allah in mind all day. It also unites Muslims, who face the qiblah and pray together as one community."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 1 mention both 'Pillar' and 'Obligatory Act'?", "o": ["Because Shi'a Muslims do not need to pray five times a day", "Because Salah matters only to Sunni and is optional for Shi'a", "To show Salah is a required duty in both Sunni and Shi'a Islam", "Because Salah is the only duty that appears in both lists"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Many Shi'a combine prayers into three times, but Salah is still compulsory and the five prayers still matter.", "Wrong. Salah is one of the Ten Obligatory Acts, so it is compulsory for Shi'a too, not optional.", "Right. Pillar is the Sunni term and Obligatory Act is the Shi'a term. Naming both shows Salah is required across Islam.", "Wrong. Four duties are in both lists: Salah, Sawm, Zakat and Hajj. Salah is not the only one."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why the Shahadah is important to Muslims. [4 marks]", "given": ["The Shahadah is the first Pillar. It states the key Muslim belief: 'There is no god but Allah, and Muhammad is the messenger of Allah.'", "Saying it sincerely is what makes a person a Muslim."], "q": "Which sentence is the best next step to develop the answer?", "o": ["It is one of the Ten Obligatory Acts, so Shi'a Muslims must say it five times a day.", "Shi'a Muslims do not say it, because they add only Khums and Jihad to the Sunni list.", "It is said in the call to prayer and in Salah, so Muslims renew their belief in one God daily.", "It requires Muslims to give a fifth of their yearly surplus income to those in need."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. The Shahadah is not one of the Ten Obligatory Acts. Shi'a do say it, for example in the call to prayer and in Salah.", "Wrong. Shi'a do say the Shahadah, and many add that Ali is the friend (wali) of Allah. Khums and Jihad are not the whole difference.", "Right. This develops the point with a precise detail: where the Shahadah is said and what it does for belief each day.", "Wrong. This describes Khums, a Shi'a act, not the Shahadah. The Shahadah is a statement of belief, not a payment."]}, "trap": "Naming Salah, Sawm, Zakat or Hajj as the extra Shi'a acts. These are in both lists. The extras are Khums, Jihad, Amr-bil-Maroof, Nahi Anil Munkar, Tawalla and Tabarra.", "recap": "Sunni follow five Pillars, Shi'a follow ten Obligatory Acts; four are shared, and Salah and the Shahadah are central to daily Muslim life."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.1.5.2b", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions here ask you to explain reasons or describe practices, often for 4 or 5 marks, so you need accurate detail and clear links.", "hook": {"q": "A Muslim goes without food and drink from dawn to sunset. What might this help them understand?", "o": ["That wealth matters more than faith", "What life is like for people who often go hungry", "That prayer is no longer needed that month", "That they have now completed their pilgrimage"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Fasting is meant to put faith first and loosen the grip of comfort and wealth, not to raise the importance of money.", "Right. Feeling hunger helps Muslims sympathise with the poor. This is one reason given for fasting in Ramadan.", "Wrong. Prayer continues in Ramadan, and many Muslims pray more. Fasting is added to Salah, never a replacement for it.", "Wrong. Pilgrimage is Hajj, which happens in a different month. Fasting is Sawm, a separate duty."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Sawm and Zakah", "b": "**Sawm** is fasting from dawn to sunset in Ramadan, the ninth month. Muslims give up food and drink in daylight. It builds self-discipline, obedience to Allah and sympathy for the poor. **Zakah** is giving 2.5% of savings each year to those in need. It purifies wealth and supports the community."}, {"h": "Hajj and jihad", "b": "**Hajj** is the pilgrimage to Makkah in Dhul Hijjah. Muslims must go once if they are healthy and can afford it. Rituals include **Tawaf**, walking seven times anticlockwise around the Ka'bah, and Sa'i, walking between Safa and Marwa. Greater jihad is the inner struggle to live as Allah wants. Lesser jihad is the outer struggle to defend Islam."}, {"h": "The two Ids", "b": "**Id-ul-Fitr** ends Ramadan, on the first day of Shawwal. Muslims pray together, give charity, wear new clothes and share meals. **Id-ul-Adha** is on 10 Dhul Hijjah, during Hajj season. It remembers Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son. Many families sacrifice an animal and share the meat."}, {"h": "Ashura", "b": "**Ashura** is on 10 Muharram, the first month of the Islamic year. Shi'a Muslims mourn the martyrdom of **Husayn**, grandson of Muhammad, at Karbala. They hold processions and listen to retellings of the story. For them it is a day of mourning, not celebration. Many Sunni Muslims fast on this day to remember Allah saving Musa from Pharaoh."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Muslims fast during Ramadan. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Muslims fast to obey Allah, because Sawm is one of the Five Pillars of Islam.", "why": "A clear first reason, stated in a full sentence. This earns the first mark."}, {"do": "Fasting in Ramadan is commanded in the Qur'an, so keeping it shows obedience to Allah.", "why": "The 'so' develops the reason by explaining it. This earns the second mark."}, {"do": "Fasting also builds sympathy for the poor.", "why": "A different second reason, not a repeat of obedience. This earns the third mark."}, {"do": "Feeling hunger from dawn to sunset helps Muslims understand those who go hungry, so they are more willing to give Zakah.", "why": "The development links the practice to the reason. This earns the fourth mark."}], "answer": "Muslims fast to obey Allah, because Sawm is one of the Five Pillars. The Qur'an commands fasting in Ramadan, so keeping it shows obedience. Fasting also builds sympathy for the poor. Feeling hunger from dawn to sunset helps Muslims understand those who go hungry, so they are more willing to give Zakah."}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 2, why does the answer add that the Qur'an commands fasting?", "o": ["It adds a third reason, which gains extra marks", "It copies the Qur'an word for word, as the mark scheme requires", "It develops reason one by explaining how fasting shows obedience", "It replaces the need for a second reason in the answer"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. You are asked for two reasons only. Extra reasons earn nothing. This sentence develops the first reason; it is not a new one.", "Wrong. You do not need to quote word for word. The sentence explains the reason in your own words.", "Right. It builds on the first reason and explains how it works. Development like this earns the second mark for that reason.", "Wrong. Two reasons are asked for, and each needs its own development. One developed reason alone cannot earn full marks."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Hajj is important for Muslims. (4 marks)", "given": ["Hajj is a Pillar of Islam, so Muslims must go once in their life if they are able.", "Completing it shows obedience to Allah and is part of living as a Muslim."], "q": "Which is the best next step, giving a different second reason?", "o": ["It shows Muslim unity: pilgrims of every race and nation worship together in Makkah.", "It remembers the end of Ramadan, so pilgrims wear new clothes and share a feast.", "Pilgrims walk seven times around the Ka'bah and then between Safa and Marwa.", "It is a Pillar of Islam, so Muslims must go once in their life if they are able."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. This is a new reason, and the second half explains it. Unity is a commonly given reason for Hajj's importance.", "Wrong. This mixes Hajj up with Id-ul-Fitr, which ends Ramadan. Hajj happens in Dhul Hijjah.", "Wrong. This describes rituals but gives no reason why Hajj matters. Explain questions need a reason and a link.", "Wrong. This repeats the first reason in other words. A repeat earns no new marks. You need a different reason."]}, "trap": "Mixing up look-alikes. For Shi'a Muslims, Ashura is mourning for Husayn, not a celebration like Id-ul-Fitr. Tawaf circles the Ka'bah; Sa'i goes between Safa and Marwa. Lesser jihad means outer defence, not 'smaller'.", "recap": "For each duty or festival, know what Muslims do and why, and join the two with 'because' or 'so'."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.2.1.1a", "kind": "idea", "why": "Theme A is in Paper 2. Questions ask you to explain beliefs about marriage and divorce, so you need the exact terms and a source of authority.", "hook": {"q": "A religion teaches that marriage is a promise made before God, not only to each other. What would you expect it to say about divorce?", "o": ["Divorce is as easy to accept as ending any other agreement", "Divorce is a serious matter, so it is discouraged or not recognised", "Divorce is encouraged so that people can find new partners quickly", "Divorce is never mentioned, because marriage is a private matter"], "a": 1, "f": ["If the promise is made before God, it is more than an ordinary agreement. This option treats marriage as a simple contract, which ignores the religious meaning.", "Right. A promise before God makes ending a marriage serious. Religions that see marriage this way discourage divorce, or, like Catholics, do not recognise it.", "Religions that value marriage do not encourage divorce. Encouraging it would go against the idea that the promise is meant to last.", "Religions have clear teachings on marriage and divorce. This option ignores the teachings you will be asked about."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The Catholic view", "b": "Catholics believe marriage is a **sacrament**: a lifelong **covenant** made before God. Sex belongs inside marriage, for love and for having children. The Church does not recognise divorce. It can grant an **annulment**, which declares that a valid marriage never existed. That is not the same as divorce."}, {"h": "Other Christian views", "b": "The Church of England also teaches that marriage is for life. Yet it accepts that some marriages break down. Since 2002 a divorced person may sometimes **remarry** in church, but the priest can refuse. Some Protestant Churches are more accepting of **divorce**. Christians read Jesus' teaching on divorce in Mark 10 in different ways."}, {"h": "The Muslim view", "b": "Muslims see marriage (**nikah**) as a contract. Sex is right only inside marriage, and sex outside it (**zina**) is forbidden. Divorce is allowed but strongly discouraged. Couples first try reconciliation, often with help from family. A husband can use talaq and a wife can seek khula. Remarriage is allowed."}, {"h": "The Humanist view", "b": "**Humanists** have no religious rules. Most judge sex, marriage and divorce by **consent**, love and **harm**. Sex between consenting adults in a loving relationship is acceptable, in or out of marriage. Divorce is accepted when a relationship has broken down, and remarriage is fine."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Roman Catholics do not recognise divorce. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "First reason: Catholics believe marriage is a sacrament, a lifelong covenant made before God.", "why": "A clear belief in the right term earns the first mark."}, {"do": "So the couple's promise is made to God as well as to each other, and no human authority, such as a court, can break that bond while both partners live.", "why": "This develops the belief by saying what it means, which earns the second mark."}, {"do": "Second reason: Jesus taught, 'What God has joined together, let no one separate' (Mark 10:9).", "why": "A source of authority gives a second, different reason."}, {"do": "So a civil divorce ends only the legal marriage. In the Church's eyes the couple are still married and cannot remarry in church while both live.", "why": "This develops the source by showing what follows from it."}], "answer": "Catholics believe marriage is a sacrament, a covenant made before God, so no court can end it while both partners live. They also follow Jesus' words in Mark 10:9, so a civil divorce does not end the marriage in the Church's eyes."}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 2, why does the answer say a court cannot end the marriage?", "o": ["Because Catholics think a legal contract matters more than a promise", "Because an annulment is just the Church's name for divorce", "Because a sacrament is a covenant made before God, which a civil court cannot undo", "Because Catholics believe marriage ends once a couple stop living together"], "a": 2, "f": ["This reverses the belief. Catholics see marriage as a sacred promise before God, not as a mere legal contract.", "An annulment says no valid marriage ever existed. It is not divorce, and it has nothing to do with why a court cannot end a sacrament.", "Right. The promise is made before God, so Catholics believe no court can undo it. A court can end the legal marriage, not the sacrament.", "Catholics do not say marriage ends when a couple separate. It is a lifelong covenant, whatever the couple's living arrangements."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain two reasons why many Muslims accept divorce only as a last resort. (4 marks)", "given": ["Reason 1: Muslims see marriage (nikah) as a contract that gives families stability.", "So ending it affects children and relatives, not just the couple."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to give a second, correct reason?", "o": ["Reason 2: Islam forbids divorce completely, so a Muslim marriage can never end.", "Reason 2: Marriage is a sacrament, so only God can end it.", "Reason 2: Only a husband may divorce, and he needs no reason, so it is not serious.", "Reason 2: The Qur'an urges couples to try reconciliation, using family arbiters."], "a": 3, "f": ["Islam does not forbid divorce. It allows it as a last resort. Saying it is banned is the commonest mistake on this topic.", "'Sacrament' is a Catholic belief. Muslims see marriage as a contract, so this answer uses the wrong religion's teaching.", "Wives can also end a marriage, for example by khula. Saying a husband needs no reason also contradicts the idea of divorce as a last resort.", "Right. The Qur'an tells couples to try reconciliation, with an arbiter from each family, before divorce. This is a second, different reason that fits 'last resort'."]}, "trap": "Writing 'Islam bans divorce' or 'an annulment is a Catholic divorce'. Say Islam allows divorce as a last resort. Say an annulment declares that no valid marriage ever existed.", "recap": "Catholics see marriage as a lifelong sacrament and do not recognise divorce; Islam allows divorce as a last resort; Humanists judge by consent and harm."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.2.1.1b", "kind": "idea", "why": "Theme A questions ask you to explain teachings on family and gender for 4 or 5 marks; in 5-mark answers, a sacred text earns a mark.", "hook": {"q": "Two people are described as 'equal in worth'. What does that most plainly mean?", "o": ["They must do exactly the same jobs", "One is more important, but both are needed", "They have the same value, even if they do different things", "They must be treated identically in every situation"], "a": 2, "f": ["This mixes up 'equal' with 'the same'. Worth is about value, not about doing identical jobs.", "This is wrong. If one is more important, they are not equal in worth.", "Right. Equal worth means equal value. Whether roles are shared or different is a separate question, and religious views differ on it.", "Equal worth does not mean identical treatment in every case. People can be valued equally and still have different roles or needs."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Purposes of the family", "b": "Christians believe the family is part of God's plan. Its main purposes are to **have and raise children**, to give **security** and love, and to **teach the faith**. Parents often bring children to church and pray with them. Genesis 1:28 tells humans to 'be fruitful and increase in number'."}, {"h": "Christians and equality", "b": "Genesis 1:27 says God made humans, male and female, in his **image**. Many Christians conclude that men and women have equal worth. Galatians 3:28 says 'there is neither male nor female' in Christ. Catholics teach **equal dignity**, but only men can be priests. Many Protestant churches **ordain** women. Some Christians read Ephesians 5 as giving the husband headship."}, {"h": "Muslim views on roles", "b": "Many Muslims believe men and women are **equal before Allah**. Surah 33:35 promises the same reward to believing men and believing women. Many also teach **different roles**: the husband as **provider** and protector, the wife caring for the home and children and keeping her own earnings. Views vary, and many Muslim families share tasks."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two reasons why many Christians believe men and women are equal. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. (5 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Reason 1: Many Christians believe men and women are equal because both are made in the image of God.", "why": "This states a clear reason, which earns the first mark."}, {"do": "Source: Genesis 1:27 says God created humans, male and female, in his image.", "why": "A relevant sacred text earns its own mark. It also backs up the reason."}, {"do": "Link: So both have the same value to God, and neither is of lesser worth.", "why": "This develops the reason by tying the source to equality. Developed points earn the extra mark."}, {"do": "Reason 2: Many Christians believe that in Christ all are united. Galatians 3:28 says there is neither male nor female, for all are one in Christ.", "why": "This is a different reason with its own support, so it is not a repeat of reason 1."}, {"do": "Link: So many Christians believe men and women share the same status in the Church, and many Protestant churches ordain women.", "why": "This develops reason 2 and gives a real example of the belief in practice."}], "answer": "Many Christians believe men and women are equal because both are made in God's image. Genesis 1:27 says God created them male and female in his image, so neither is of lesser worth. Many also believe all are united in Christ. Galatians 3:28 says there is neither male nor female, and many Protestant churches ordain women."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer add 'neither is of lesser worth' after quoting Genesis 1:27?", "o": ["It links the source to equality, which develops the reason and earns the extra mark", "A quotation on its own always earns full marks, so this is just extra", "It shows that all Christians agree on the roles of men and women", "It proves that men and women must do identical jobs in church"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. A source alone only supports the reason. Explaining what it means for equality develops the point, and that is what earns the extra mark.", "A quotation alone does not earn full marks. You must explain how it supports the reason.", "The link is about equal worth. It does not claim all Christians agree, and Christians differ on roles, such as priesthood.", "The link is about equal worth, not identical jobs. Equal and the same are different ideas."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain two reasons why many Christians believe the family is important. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. (5 marks)", "given": ["Reason 1: The family is where children are brought up to know and love God."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to develop reason 1?", "o": ["This matters because children need to be brought up to know and love God.", "A second reason is that the family gives its members security and love.", "The Bible says the family exists so that fathers can rule over everyone.", "At infant baptism, parents promise to bring their child up in the faith at home."], "a": 3, "f": ["This only repeats reason 1 in other words. Development needs new detail, such as an example or a link to a belief.", "This is a fine second reason, but it moves on too soon. Reason 1 has not been developed yet.", "This is a false claim. Christians do not teach that the family exists for fathers to rule. It exists for love, security and faith.", "Right. It adds a Christian practice, the baptism promise, showing that parents take on the duty of teaching the faith. That develops the reason."]}, "trap": "Writing that 'equal' means 'the same'. Many Muslims and Catholics teach equal worth with different roles. State the equal value first, then name the roles, and write 'many', not 'all'.", "recap": "Families raise children, give security and pass on faith; many Christians and Muslims teach equal worth, though some see different roles."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.2.1.2a", "kind": "idea", "why": "Theme B exam questions ask you to explain religious and scientific ideas about creation, and why believers care for the environment and animals.", "hook": {"q": "A friend goes on holiday and leaves you in charge of their pet. Which best describes your role?", "o": ["You own the pet, so you can treat it however you like", "You look after it for its owner and must answer to them", "You can ignore it, because it is not really yours", "You must never touch it, in case you harm it"], "a": 1, "f": ["Looking after something is not the same as owning it. You are acting for the owner, so you cannot do whatever you like.", "Correct. You care for something that belongs to someone else, and you must answer to them. This is the idea behind stewardship.", "Being trusted with something brings a duty to care for it. Ignoring it would let your friend down.", "Caring means looking after the pet properly, which includes feeding and handling it. It does not mean keeping away from it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The Big Bang theory", "b": "Scientists say the universe began about 13.8 billion years ago. It started from a tiny, hot, dense point. This point expanded very quickly and is still expanding. Stars and galaxies formed later. This is the **Big Bang** theory. It is a **scientific** explanation of **how** the universe began."}, {"h": "Religious accounts of creation", "b": "Genesis 1 says God created the universe in six days. **Creationists** take this literally. Other Christians read it as symbolic and may accept the Big Bang, with God as its cause. Muslims believe **Allah** created the universe. Some accept the Big Bang as the way he did it."}, {"h": "Stewardship and khalifah", "b": "Christians believe God made humans **stewards**: trusted carers of creation. Genesis says humans have **dominion**, which most Christians read as responsible rule, not a right to use nature as they like. Muslims believe humans are **khalifah**, Allah's agents on earth. Both are answerable to God for how they treat the world."}, {"h": "Treatment of animals", "b": "Christians and Muslims believe animals are part of God's creation and have **value**. Many believe humans may use animals for food and work, but must avoid **cruelty**. Views differ within each religion, for example on animal testing. Some accept it if it saves human lives. Others reject it."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Christians believe they should care for the environment. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Christians believe God created the world and saw that it was good.", "why": "A clear first reason: the world has value because God made it."}, {"do": "So damaging the environment disrespects God's creation, and Christians should protect it.", "why": "Developing the reason earns the second mark for it."}, {"do": "Christians also believe God made humans stewards, trusted to look after creation for him.", "why": "A different second reason, using the key term stewardship."}, {"do": "They are answerable to God, so they must not waste resources or leave a damaged world for future generations.", "why": "Develops the reason by saying what stewardship means in practice."}], "answer": "Christians believe God created the world and called it good, so damaging it disrespects his creation. They also believe humans are stewards, trusted by God to care for creation. They are answerable to God, so they should not waste resources."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 4 say Christians are 'answerable to God' for the environment?", "o": ["Dominion gives humans the right to use nature however they choose", "Stewards own the world, so they decide what happens to it", "Stewards look after what belongs to God and must account to him for it", "Christians must leave nature alone and never use its resources"], "a": 2, "f": ["This misreads dominion as permission to use nature freely. Christians read it as responsible care on God's behalf.", "Stewards do not own what they look after. The world belongs to God, which is why they are answerable to him.", "Correct. A steward looks after something that belongs to someone else and must account to the owner for how it was treated.", "Stewardship does not mean never using resources. It means using them responsibly and with care."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Muslims believe they should care for the environment. (4 marks)", "given": ["Reason 1: Muslims believe Allah created the earth, so it belongs to him."], "q": "Which sentence best develops Reason 1?", "o": ["So Muslims should respect the earth and not waste it, as misuse dishonours Allah.", "Reason 2: Muslims believe humans own the earth, so they may use it as they wish.", "Reason 2: Muslims are khalifah, so they must look after the earth for Allah.", "The Big Bang shows the earth is billions of years old, so Muslims must protect it."], "a": 0, "f": ["Correct. It builds on the reason by saying what follows from the earth belonging to Allah, which earns the development mark.", "This starts a new reason, so it does not develop Reason 1. It is also wrong, since Muslims believe the earth belongs to Allah, not to humans.", "Khalifah is a good second reason, but this sentence starts a new reason. The question asks you to develop Reason 1.", "This brings in the Big Bang, which is not Reason 1 and is not a Muslim reason for caring for the environment."]}, "trap": "Reading 'dominion' as a right to use nature however we like. Stewardship means caring for creation on God's behalf. Always say who the world belongs to and who humans answer to.", "recap": "The Big Bang explains how the universe began; Christians and Muslims believe God created it, and humans are stewards or khalifah, answerable to God."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.2.1.2b", "kind": "idea", "why": "Theme B questions often ask you to explain religious and non-religious views on sanctity of life, abortion and euthanasia, so you need the key beliefs ready and explained.", "hook": {"q": "Someone says a human life is 'sacred'. What do they most likely mean?", "o": ["It is holy and precious, so it deserves great respect", "It is only worth living if it is mostly pleasant", "It is the same thing as how happy a person feels", "It can be ended whenever the person chooses"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. 'Sacred' means holy and precious. That idea sits behind the sanctity of life, which you will use throughout this topic.", "This is quality of life, not sacredness. A sacred life is valued whether or not it is pleasant.", "Happiness is part of quality of life. Sacred means holy, which does not depend on how a person feels.", "This is about personal choice, which Humanists stress. Calling life sacred usually means it should be protected."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Sanctity of life", "b": "**Sanctity of life** means life is holy and precious. Many Christians believe life is a **gift from God** and that humans are made in God's image (Genesis 1:27). Many Muslims believe Allah gives life and chooses when it ends. So many believers say life should be protected, whatever its quality."}, {"h": "Quality of life", "b": "**Quality of life** means how good a life is: pain, independence, dignity and happiness. **Humanists** do not believe in God or an afterlife. They stress this life and personal choice, so many support voluntary euthanasia. Religious believers also care about quality of life, but most say it does not outweigh sanctity."}, {"h": "Euthanasia", "b": "**Euthanasia** means ending a life to end suffering. It is **voluntary** if the patient asks, non-voluntary if they cannot ask, and involuntary if it is against their wishes. Active euthanasia is illegal in the UK. Most Christians and Muslims oppose it and support **hospice** care, which eases pain."}, {"h": "Abortion", "b": "In Great Britain, abortion is generally legal up to 24 weeks, with two doctors' agreement. **Catholics** oppose it strongly because they believe life begins at conception. Some other Christians accept it in certain cases, such as a risk to the mother's life. Many Humanists support a woman's **right to choose**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two reasons why many Christians oppose euthanasia. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "State reason one: many Christians believe life is sacred, a gift from God.", "why": "A clear belief gives the first mark. It names the group and the idea."}, {"do": "Develop it: so only God has the right to end a life, and euthanasia takes away what God has given.", "why": "The second mark is for linking the belief to the conclusion about euthanasia."}, {"do": "State reason two: Christians believe humans are made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27), so every life has value, even when ill.", "why": "A different belief gives a separate reason. Naming the source adds precision."}, {"do": "Develop it: so suffering people should be cared for, for example in hospices, not helped to die.", "why": "It links the belief to what Christians do instead. This earns the development mark."}], "answer": "Many Christians believe life is sacred, a gift from God. So only God has the right to end it, and euthanasia takes away that gift. Christians also believe humans are made in God's image, so every life has value. Suffering people should be cared for in hospices, not helped to die."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer add 'only God has the right to end a life' after saying life is sacred?", "o": ["It gives a second, separate reason, so it earns a second mark", "It links the belief to opposing euthanasia, earning the development mark", "It shows that all Christians agree about euthanasia, so it is stronger", "It repeats the question, so the examiner knows the topic"], "a": 1, "f": ["The sentence develops reason one. A separate reason needs a different belief, as in the second half of the answer.", "Right. A development explains how the belief leads to the conclusion. Without it, you have only stated a belief.", "The answer says 'many Christians', not all. Some Christians accept euthanasia in extreme cases, so avoid claiming agreement.", "Repeating the question earns nothing. Development must add an explanation."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Humanists support voluntary euthanasia. (4 marks)", "given": ["Reason 1: Humanists believe quality of life matters, not just being alive."], "q": "Which sentence best develops reason 1?", "o": ["Life is a gift from God, so it should be ended gently and kindly", "Sanctity of life means it must be protected whatever the pain", "Constant pain with no hope of recovery lowers quality, so ending life can be kinder", "Voluntary euthanasia is legal in the UK, so Humanists support it"], "a": 2, "f": ["Humanists do not believe in God. This mixes up a religious belief with a Humanist one.", "This is the religious view that Humanists reject. It would support the opposite conclusion.", "Right. It links quality of life to the case for euthanasia: a life without quality, and ending it as the kinder choice.", "Voluntary euthanasia is illegal in the UK, so this is false. A law is also not a reason for a belief."]}, "trap": "Writing 'all Christians' hold one view. Catholics strongly oppose abortion, but some other Christians accept it in certain cases, such as a risk to the mother's life. Write 'many' or 'some' and name the group.", "recap": "Many religious believers say life is sacred, a gift from God, so it should be protected; Humanists stress quality of life and choice."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.2.1.4a", "kind": "idea", "why": "Theme D questions ask you to explain religious teachings on war and peace with sources of wisdom, then weigh those views in the 12-mark question.", "hook": {"q": "A country invades its neighbour to seize its oil. Most people say this war is wrong. What is the best reason?", "o": ["Only religious leaders are allowed to declare a war", "It lacks a just cause, because it is fought out of greed", "Wars are only wrong when the invaded country is smaller", "It is wrong because the war would cost too much money"], "a": 1, "f": ["Religious leaders do not have to declare wars. Just war ideas ask for legitimate authority, usually a lawful government, not only religious leaders.", "Correct. A just war needs a just cause, such as defending people from attack. Greed and gain are not just causes.", "Size is not the test. A war can be wrong whatever the size of the countries. What matters is the cause and the way it is fought.", "Cost may matter to governments, but it is not a religious reason. Just war ideas judge the cause and the intention."], "a_note": null}, "chunks": [{"h": "Pacifism", "b": "**Pacifists** believe war and violence are always wrong. They think disputes should be settled by talking and **peaceful** means. Many Christian pacifists, such as Quakers, point to Jesus' teaching to love enemies and his words 'Blessed are the peacemakers' (Matthew 5:9). Pacifists may still help others, for example as medics."}, {"h": "Just war", "b": "The **just war** theory says war can be right if strict conditions are met. These include a **just cause**, **legitimate authority** (a lawful government) and last resort, after peaceful ways have failed. Others are right intention, proportionality and a reasonable chance of success. Many Christians use these conditions."}, {"h": "Holy war and jihad", "b": "A **holy war** is fought for a religious cause, led by a religious authority. In Islam, **lesser jihad** is armed struggle to defend the faith or resist oppression, under strict rules. **Greater jihad** is the inner struggle to resist temptation and live as a good Muslim. Many Muslims see greater jihad as more important."}, {"h": "Peace and forgiveness", "b": "**Peace** means more than no fighting. It includes justice and harmony. **Reconciliation** means restoring a broken relationship. Christians point to Jesus' teaching on **forgiveness**. Muslims believe Allah is merciful and forgiving. Most Christians and Muslims condemn terrorism, because it harms innocent people."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two reasons why some Christians are pacifists. Refer to a source of wisdom in your answer. (5 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "One reason is that Jesus taught Christians to love their enemies.", "why": "This states a clear belief as a reason. It is the first point."}, {"do": "In the Sermon on the Mount he said 'Love your enemies' (Matthew 5:44), so many Christians think fighting them cannot be right.", "why": "This quotes a source of wisdom and links it to pacifism. That develops the reason."}, {"do": "A second reason is that Christians believe all human life is sacred.", "why": "This is a different reason from the first. Repeating the first would not earn extra marks."}, {"do": "Humans are made in God's image, and pacifists read the commandment against killing (Exodus 20:13) as covering war, so they refuse to fight.", "why": "This explains why sacred life leads to pacifism. It also adds a second source."}], "answer": "Some Christians are pacifists because Jesus taught love for enemies. In Matthew 5:44 he said 'Love your enemies', so they believe fighting cannot be right. Also, they believe life is sacred, as humans are made in God's image. Pacifists read the commandment against killing (Exodus 20:13) as covering war."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 quote Matthew 5:44 and then link it to fighting?", "o": ["A quotation on its own earns full marks without any explanation", "Sources of wisdom only count if they come from the Old Testament", "The task needs a source, and the link shows how it supports the reason", "It makes the answer longer, and length alone gains the extra marks"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. A bare quotation shows recall, but explanation is needed to develop the reason and earn the full marks.", "Wrong. Matthew is in the New Testament. Sources of wisdom include the Bible, the Qur'an and the teachings of religious leaders.", "Correct. The question asks for a source. Linking 'Love your enemies' to refusing to fight shows how the source supports pacifism.", "Wrong. Marks are for content that answers the question, not for length. Padding earns nothing."], "a_note": null}, "faded": {"task": "Explain two reasons why some Muslims accept lesser jihad. Refer to a source of wisdom in your answer. (5 marks)", "given": ["One reason is that Muslims believe they may defend their faith and community when attacked."], "q": "What should the next sentence be to develop this reason with a source?", "o": ["Muslims believe they can fight any non-Muslim, as the Qur'an 2:190 commands this.", "Lesser jihad is the inner struggle to resist temptation, so it is not about fighting.", "Muslims can declare war whenever a leader decides, since the Qur'an sets no limits.", "Qur'an 2:190 allows fighting those who fight Muslims, but forbids going beyond limits."], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. This is a common distortion. Qur'an 2:190 refers to fighting those who fight Muslims, not everyone who is not a Muslim.", "Wrong. This describes the greater jihad. The question is about lesser jihad, which is armed struggle with strict rules.", "Wrong. Lesser jihad has strict conditions, including legitimate authority and defence. It is not open-ended permission for war.", "Correct. It uses a real source and links it to the reason of defence. It also shows that lesser jihad has limits."], "a_note": null}, "trap": "Mixing up the two jihads. Lesser jihad is armed defence with strict rules, not permission for any aggression. Greater jihad is the inner struggle to live as a good Muslim.", "recap": "Pacifists reject all violence; just war allows it only under strict conditions; lesser jihad is armed defence, greater jihad is inner struggle."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.2.1.4b", "kind": "idea", "why": "Theme D questions often ask you to explain two reasons or beliefs, and the 12-mark question asks you to weigh up views on issues such as nuclear weapons.", "hook": {"q": "Two neighbouring countries each own weapons that could destroy the other. Neither has ever attacked. What is the most likely reason?", "o": ["They have agreed that the weapons can never be used", "Their weapons are too old to work", "Each knows an attack would bring a devastating reply", "Both want to get rid of all weapons"], "a": 2, "f": ["Agreements may exist, but the usual reason is fear of what the other side could do back, not a promise.", "There is no sign the weapons are old. Deterrence depends on weapons that work and could be used.", "Right. Fear of a devastating reply stops an attack. This is the idea of deterrence.", "Wanting to get rid of weapons is disarmament. Here both countries still keep them."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Religion and conflict", "b": "Religion can add to conflict when groups hold **different beliefs** or claim the same **holy places**. **Extremists** may use religion to justify violence. But religion is rarely the only cause. Land, power and politics matter too. Most followers reject violence, and many religions teach peace."}, {"h": "Nuclear deterrence", "b": "**Nuclear deterrence** means holding nuclear weapons so enemies do not attack, for fear of **retaliation**. Some Christians accept it as a way to keep peace. Many say it breaks **just war** rules: the harm is not proportionate, and the weapons kill civilians. Quakers reject all war."}, {"h": "Religious peace-making", "b": "Many believers see **peace-making** as a duty. Christians recall Jesus' words, 'Blessed are the peacemakers'. After apartheid, Archbishop Desmond Tutu chaired South Africa's **Truth and Reconciliation Commission**. It encouraged people to tell the truth and seek **forgiveness**. Religious leaders also bring opposing groups together for talks."}, {"h": "Helping victims of war", "b": "Religious organisations help **victims of war**, such as refugees. **CAFOD**, Christian Aid and Muslim Aid give food, water, shelter and medical care. They help people of any faith or none. Christians point to the Good Samaritan and loving your neighbour. Muslims point to the duty of charity, **zakat**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two reasons why some Christians reject nuclear deterrence. Refer to religious teaching in your answer. [5 marks]", "steps": [{"do": "Some Christians reject it because nuclear weapons break the just war rule that force must be proportionate.", "why": "States one clear reason, based on Christian just war thinking."}, {"do": "The harm they cause is far greater than any good they could achieve, so no use of them could be proportionate.", "why": "Develops the reason by explaining what the rule means here."}, {"do": "Another reason is that Christians believe human life is sacred, because people are made in God's image (Genesis 1:27).", "why": "A second, different reason, with a named source of teaching as asked."}, {"do": "Nuclear weapons would kill huge numbers of civilians, and deterrence only works if a state is ready to use them.", "why": "Develops the belief by linking it to how deterrence works."}], "answer": "Some Christians say nuclear weapons break the just war rule of proportion, because the harm is far greater than any good. They also believe life is sacred, as people are made in God's image (Genesis 1:27). Deterrence needs a readiness to kill huge numbers of civilians."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 4 say deterrence only works if a state is ready to use the weapons?", "o": ["Because deterrence means actually using the weapons", "It links the Christian objection to the threat itself, not only to using them", "Because Christians say a state must disarm before it defends itself", "It shows that nuclear weapons are never used in practice"], "a": 1, "f": ["This is the common slip. Deterrence means threatening to use the weapons, not using them.", "Right. The threat only works if it is believable. So the objection to harming civilians applies even if the weapons are never fired.", "The step makes no claim about disarming first. It explains why the threat itself is a problem.", "Deterrence is not about whether the weapons are used. The step makes the point that the threat must be real to work."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain one way a Christian organisation such as CAFOD helps victims of war. Refer to religious teaching.", "given": ["Point: CAFOD gives refugees emergency food, clean water and shelter."], "q": "What should the next step be, to add the religious teaching?", "o": ["CAFOD was founded to negotiate peace treaties, so it works only with governments.", "Christians believe aid should go first to fellow Christians, so churches receive it.", "Just war theory says aid is only allowed if it is proportionate to the harm done.", "Christians follow 'love your neighbour', as in the Good Samaritan, so they help anyone."], "a": 3, "f": ["This changes the point. CAFOD's work in the given step is practical aid, not treaty negotiation.", "Christian teaching on helping others does not limit aid to Christians. CAFOD helps people of any faith or none.", "Just war theory is about when and how to fight, not about giving aid. This links to the wrong teaching.", "Right. It links the aid to a Christian teaching on helping others, and explains why the organisation helps people whatever their faith."]}, "trap": "Deterrence means holding weapons to threaten retaliation, not using them. Do not write that religion is the only cause of conflict. Desmond Tutu, not Nelson Mandela, chaired the Truth and Reconciliation Commission.", "recap": "Deterrence means holding nuclear weapons to prevent attack; many Christians reject it as disproportionate and harmful to civilians, and value peace-making."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.2.1.5a", "kind": "idea", "why": "Theme E questions ask why people commit crime and what religions teach about murder, theft and hate crime, and good answers back each view with a teaching.", "hook": {"q": "Two people are assaulted. One is hit in a row over a parking space. The other is hit after insults about their faith. Which is a hate crime?", "o": ["Only the parking space assault", "Both, because both involved violence", "Neither, because both are just assault", "Only the assault linked to the person's faith"], "a": 3, "f": ["The parking row was about the space, not about who the person was. There is no hostility towards a group, so it is not a hate crime.", "Violence alone does not make a hate crime. The motive must be hostility towards a group or identity.", "Both are assaults, but one was also motivated by hostility to a faith. That motive makes it a hate crime.", "Right. The attacker targeted the person because of their religion. Hostility towards a group or identity is what makes it a hate crime."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Good and evil actions", "b": "Many religious people see **good actions** as those that follow God's teaching and help others. They see **evil actions** as those that go against God's will and harm people. A **crime** is different: it breaks the law of the land. Something can be evil without being a crime."}, {"h": "Why people commit crime", "b": "Causes of crime include **poverty**, **upbringing**, **mental illness**, addiction, greed and hatred. Some people break laws they believe are unjust, as a protest. Others act from anger or peer pressure. Religious believers may add that people can choose wrongly, but they can also choose to change."}, {"h": "Religious views on lawbreakers", "b": "Many Christians cite **Romans 13**: believers should obey the authorities, which God has put in place. Christians also stress **justice** and **forgiveness**. Many Muslims stress justice too, and teach that crime goes against Allah's law. Both religions want wrongdoing dealt with fairly."}, {"h": "Hate crime, theft and murder", "b": "A **hate crime** targets someone for their group or identity. Christians and Muslims believe God created everyone, so all deserve respect. Theft is forbidden: Exodus 20:15 says 'You shall not steal'. Murder breaks the belief that life is **sacred**. Qur'an 5:32 says killing one innocent is like killing all humanity."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Christians believe murder is wrong. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Christians believe murder is wrong because the Bible forbids it. The Ten Commandments say 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13).", "why": "This gives reason one and names its source of authority."}, {"do": "Christians see the commandments as God's own instructions, so murdering someone disobeys God.", "why": "This develops reason one by explaining why the teaching matters to believers."}, {"do": "Christians also believe life is sacred, because humans are made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27).", "why": "This is a different reason, so it earns separate marks."}, {"do": "So killing a person destroys something God has made and values, which Christians see as an attack on God's creation.", "why": "This develops reason two by linking the teaching to why murder is wrong."}], "answer": "Christians believe murder is wrong because the Ten Commandments say 'You shall not murder'. They see these as God's instructions, so murder disobeys God. They also believe life is sacred because humans are made in God's image, so killing destroys what God values."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer add 'so murdering someone disobeys God' after the Exodus quotation?", "o": ["The quotation alone would already earn full marks", "It makes the answer longer, and length earns marks", "It starts a third reason that the question did not ask for", "It develops the reason by showing why the teaching matters to Christians"], "a": 3, "f": ["A quotation on its own only states the reason. Explain questions reward development of it.", "Marks are for developed points, not length. Extra words that explain nothing earn no marks.", "It is still reason one. A new reason would need a different belief, such as the sanctity of life.", "Right. The 'so...' sentence explains the link between the teaching and the belief. That development earns the second mark for the reason."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Christians oppose hate crime. (4 marks)", "given": ["Reason one: Christians believe all humans are made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27)."], "q": "Which sentence best develops this first reason?", "o": ["Hate crime is against the law, so Christians must obey the police.", "Hate crimes are common and the number has risen in recent years.", "So attacking someone for their race or faith disrespects a person God made.", "Many Christians go to church each week and read the Bible."], "a": 2, "f": ["This jumps to a different idea (obeying authority). It does not develop the image-of-God reason.", "This is a fact about crime figures, not a Christian belief. It does not explain the reason.", "Right. It links the teaching to hate crime: harming someone for who they are disrespects God's creation.", "This is true of some Christians but does not explain why they oppose hate crime."]}, "trap": "Calling any violent crime a hate crime. The motive must be hostility towards someone's group or identity. In explain answers, never leave a quotation bare: add 'so...' to show why it matters.", "recap": "For every religious view, name the belief, give its source, then explain why it matters: life is sacred because humans are made in God's image."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.2.1.5b", "kind": "idea", "why": "Punishment questions ask you to explain religious beliefs for 4 or 5 marks and weigh them up for 12, so you need clear aims and sources.", "hook": {"q": "A school gives a detention to a pupil who shouted out. The head hopes other pupils will now stay quiet too. Which aim is this?", "o": ["Retribution, because the pupil is paying for what they did", "Deterrence, because the punishment puts others off", "Reformation, because the pupil is being changed for the better", "Protection, because the pupil is kept away from others"], "a": 1, "f": ["Retribution is payback for the offender. The head's hope here is about other pupils, so that is not the main aim.", "Right. The punishment is meant to put people off by fear of the same thing happening to them. That is deterrence.", "Reformation aims to change the offender. A detention that is meant to warn others is not mainly about changing this pupil.", "Protection keeps people safe by removing a danger. A detention does not do that, and the aim here is to influence others."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Aims of punishment", "b": "**Retribution** is payback: the punishment fits the harm done. **Deterrence** puts people off crime through fear of punishment. **Reformation** changes the offender for the better. Protection keeps the public safe, for example by locking up dangerous people."}, {"h": "Christians and the death penalty", "b": "Catholics teach that the death penalty is **inadmissible**, because every person has dignity as made in God's image. Quakers oppose it because there is **that of God** in everyone. Christian views differ: some accept it for the worst crimes, but many oppose it."}, {"h": "Muslim views on punishment", "b": "Many Muslims accept the death penalty for crimes such as murder. They believe Shari'ah law allows **qisas**, a punishment that matches the crime. The Qur'an also encourages the victim's family to **forgive**, and they may accept compensation. Muslims differ on when it should be used."}, {"h": "Forgiveness and offenders", "b": "Christians follow Jesus' teaching on **forgiveness**. The Lord's Prayer asks God to forgive us as we forgive others. Jesus also said, \"I was in prison and you visited me\" (Matthew 25). **Elizabeth Fry**, a Quaker, campaigned for better conditions for women in Newgate prison."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two reasons why many Christians oppose the death penalty. Refer to a source of wisdom or authority in your answer. (5 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Many Christians oppose the death penalty because they believe human life is sacred, as people are made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27).", "why": "This gives reason one and names a source of wisdom."}, {"do": "This means only God has the right to end a life. The Catholic Church teaches that the death penalty is inadmissible because it attacks a person's dignity.", "why": "This develops the reason by saying what it means for the death penalty."}, {"do": "A second reason is that Christians believe in forgiveness, as Jesus taught.", "why": "This is a separate, clear second reason."}, {"do": "Execution ends any chance for offenders to repent and change. Quakers believe there is 'that of God' in everyone, so people can be reformed.", "why": "This develops reason two by showing what execution takes away."}, {"do": "So these Christians believe punishment should aim to reform offenders, not to kill them.", "why": "This links back to the question and finishes the point."}], "answer": "Many Christians believe life is sacred because people are made in God's image (Genesis 1:27), so only God should end it. The Catholic Church calls the death penalty inadmissible. Christians also believe in forgiveness. Quakers see 'that of God' in everyone, so offenders can change, but execution ends any chance to repent."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 4 say that execution ends any chance for offenders to repent and change?", "o": ["It shows that the death penalty cannot work as a deterrent", "It repeats reason one so that the answer looks longer", "It develops the forgiveness reason by showing what execution takes away", "It proves that all Christians agree about punishment"], "a": 2, "f": ["Deterrence is a different aim. This step is about forgiveness and the chance to change, not about putting people off crime.", "Reason one was about life being sacred. This step develops reason two, so repeating would earn nothing new.", "Right. A developed reason explains why the belief leads to a view. Forgiveness matters because execution removes the chance of repentance.", "The step explains one view and does not claim every Christian agrees. Christians do differ, so this claim would also be inaccurate."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain one reason why many Muslims accept the death penalty for murder. Refer to a source of wisdom or authority.", "given": ["Many Muslims accept the death penalty for murder because Shari'ah law teaches qisas, a punishment matching the crime.", "The Qur'an (2:178) sets out qisas for those who kill."], "q": "Which sentence is the best next step to develop this reason?", "o": ["So the death penalty is wrong, because the Qur'an says every life is sacred.", "So the aim is reformation, because every murderer can always be changed.", "So this matches the Catholic teaching that the death penalty is inadmissible.", "So many Muslims see this as retribution: the punishment must fit the harm."], "a": 3, "f": ["This contradicts the reason already given. The answer is meant to explain why many Muslims accept the death penalty, not reject it.", "Reformation is a different aim from qisas. Qisas is about matching the punishment to the crime, which is retribution.", "This is a Catholic Christian view and the opposite of the Muslim view in the answer. Do not mix the religions.", "Right. Qisas means a punishment that fits the harm done. Naming this as retribution develops the reason by linking it to an aim."]}, "trap": "Mixing up the aims. Deterrence puts people off future crime. Retribution is payback for what was done. Reformation changes the offender. Ask: is the punishment about others, the past or the offender's future?", "recap": "Learn the aims of punishment, then use them to explain why Christians and Muslims hold different views on the death penalty and forgiveness."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.5.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Keeping conditions like temperature and blood glucose steady lets enzymes and cells work properly, and exams often ask you to name each part of a control system.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What homeostasis means", "b": "**Homeostasis** is the regulation of conditions inside your body so they stay at the **optimum** level. Examples are body temperature, blood glucose concentration and water levels. Enzymes and other cell functions work best at optimum conditions. So the body keeps conditions steady, even when things change inside or outside it."}, {"h": "Parts of a control system", "b": "Every control system has three parts, in this order. **Receptors** are cells that detect a change (a stimulus). A **coordination centre** receives and processes the information. It can be the brain, spinal cord or pancreas. **Effectors** carry out the response. Effectors are muscles or glands."}, {"h": "Nervous or chemical", "b": "Control systems are **automatic**. You do not decide to use them. Some use **nervous responses**, where electrical impulses carry the information. Others use **chemical responses**, where hormones carry it in the blood. Either way, the response brings the condition back to its normal level."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how the body's control system brings body temperature back to normal when you get too hot.", "steps": [{"do": "Temperature receptors detect that body temperature has risen above 37 °C.", "why": "Every control system starts with receptors detecting a change."}, {"do": "The receptors send nerve impulses to the brain, which processes the information.", "why": "The brain is the coordination centre. It receives and processes the information."}, {"do": "The brain sends signals to the sweat glands, which release sweat.", "why": "Sweat glands are the effectors. Glands and muscles carry out the response."}, {"do": "The sweat evaporates and cools the skin, so body temperature falls back towards 37 °C.", "why": "The response returns the condition to normal, so enzymes keep working at their optimum."}], "answer": "Temperature receptors detect that body temperature is above 37 °C. They send nerve impulses to the brain, the coordination centre, which processes the information. The brain signals the sweat glands, the effectors, to release sweat. Sweat evaporates and cools the skin, so temperature falls back to normal and enzymes keep working."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer name the sweat glands as the effectors, not the receptors?", "o": ["They carry out the response that cools the body, while receptors only detect the change", "They detect the rise in temperature and pass the information on to the brain", "They process the information from the receptors and decide what response is needed", "They are part of the brain, so they take the place of the coordination centre"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Effectors are muscles or glands that carry out the response. Receptors detect the stimulus. Sweat glands release the sweat, so they are effectors.", "Wrong. Detecting the change is the job of the receptors. Mixing up detecting and responding is the commonest slip on this topic.", "Wrong. Processing information is done by the coordination centre, here the brain. Sweat glands do not decide anything. They respond to signals.", "Wrong. Sweat glands are in the skin, not the brain. The brain is the coordination centre, and the glands are separate effectors."], "f_note": null}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the body's control system responds when body temperature falls on a cold day.", "given": ["Temperature receptors detect that body temperature is below 37 °C.", "Nerve impulses carry the information to the brain, which processes it."], "q": "What is the next step in the answer?", "o": ["The receptors in the skin contract rapidly, which is shivering, and this warms the body", "The brain acts as the effector and releases sweat to warm the body", "The brain signals muscles (effectors) to contract rapidly, so you shiver and warm up", "The brain signals the sweat glands, the effectors, which release sweat to warm the body"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Receptors only detect changes. They cannot contract. Muscles are the effectors that shiver.", "Wrong. The brain is the coordination centre, not an effector. Sweating also cools the body, so it does not help when you are cold.", "Right. The brain signals the muscles, which are effectors. Shivering is rapid contraction, and respiration in the muscles releases energy that warms the body.", "Wrong. Sweat glands are effectors, but sweating cools the body. When you are cold, the response is shivering by muscles."]}, "trap": "Saying the effector detects the change. Receptors detect, the coordination centre processes, and effectors respond. Learn the order: receptor, coordination centre, effector. Match them to detect, process, respond.", "recap": "Receptors detect the change, a coordination centre processes it, and effectors (muscles or glands) respond to bring the condition back to normal."}
